 was particularly affected by the\n1056\tdimensions of the fortune which the\n1057\tdecedent had amassed All through the\n1058\tfunereal function he continuously muir\n1059\tINJURED: where did he get it? Where\n1060\tdid he get it?' and when his ceremonial\n1061\tobligations were discharged he departed\n1062\tstill repeating increasingly: Where did\n1063\the get it? Where did he get it2'\n1064\t\n1065\t\n1066\tTraffic managers of great lines have\n1067\tbeen observed to prosper in ratio\n1068\twholly disproportionate to their salaries\n1069\tIn fact. very many OF them have become\n1070\texceedingly rich, matter which has\n1071\tnot altogether escaped criticism. Some\n1072\tof them have made money by going\n1073\tlong or short Of the stock OF the cor\n1074\tporation employing them, accordingly\n1075\tas their information and Judgment\n1076\tprompted and IN respect of THIS practice\n1077\tthe public censuro has never been severe\n1078\t1t is not unlikely that the course which\n1079\tMr.. ROOSEVELT has pursued toward the\n1080\tmanagement OF tine railroads may pro-\n1081\twoke a more minute and attentive scru-\n1082\ttinv of the practices of their officials,\n1083\tin which case it may appear that the\n1084\tsources of their wealth were far more\n1085\tquestionable.\n1086\t\n1087\t\n1088\tWe venture to think that Mr.. RoosE-\n1089\tWELT * BY this time satisfied that the\n1090\treally grave charge against the rail-\n1091\troads of the country is not the high\n1092\trates charged for transportation but\n1093\tthe giving of secret and criminal re-\n1094\tbates We have never INDUCED the\n1095\tbelief that rate cutting was inspired\n1096\tsolely by desire for increased tonnage.\n1097\tThe rebates and cut rates enjoyed by\n1098\tthe Standard Oil Company cannot be\n1099\texplained solely by the competition\n1100\tbetween rival roads for the oil COM\n1101\tpany's traffic. No one who is conversant\n1102\twith the railroad business could be hood\n1103\twinked into such a belief. There were\n1104\tcertain phenomena connected with the\n1105\tgrowth of that company which are,\n1106\tindeed. inscparableSfr the history Of\n1107\tall great corporations during the last\n1108\tquarter OF a century, but which have\n1109\tescaped general notice and have never\n1110\tbeen brought out into the light.\n1111\t\n1112\t\n1113\tIf President ROOSEVELT persists in\n1114\tthe direction which has led to the late\n1115\trevelations touching the criminal man\n1116\tagement of the Atchison, Topeka and\n1117\tSanta Fe Railroad, some of the phenom-\n1118\tena will see the light beyond a doubt and\n1119\tif. in consequence, some Of our leading\n1120\trailroad men find themselves in the penl-\n1121\tTertiary they will have only themselves\n1122\t10 blame, and the public may fifty con-\n1123\tgraduate itself. Then and in that\n1124\tevent there will no longer remain any\n1125\tillusions Us to the nature and causes Of\n1126\trailroad acknowledgement, and they will\n1127\tbe found to have been at least as pref\n1128\tjudicial to the interests Of the people who\n1129\tactually own the railroads as they have\n1130\tbeen detrimental TO The public welfare.\n1131\tThe profit and advantage of the secret\n1132\trebate have not accrued solely 10 the\n1133\tCORPORATION for whose benefit it was\n1134\tapparently created.\n1135\t\n1136\t\n1137\tThe late Mr. JAMES AICHENRY often\n1138\tcomplained 10 US that what he described\n1139\tas ghost trains were run upon the Erie\n1140\tRailroad, trains which performed their\n1141\tdue function upon the operating sched-\n1142\tules OF the road, but of which the results\n1143\tnever penetrated into the traffic returns.\n1144\tHad he lived longer and become ac-\n1145\touainted with these later revelations of\n1146\tthe methods Of railroad management\n1147\twe believe he would have hit upon a\n1148\tmore practical and adequate hypothesis.\n1149\tThere are two sides to the question, and\n1150\tboth are of grave Importance; and the\n1151\tplundering of the public has proceeded\n1152\tpar passu with the defraudng Of the\n1153\trailroad investor We are informed that\n1154\tone railroad which freed itself from the\n1155\trate cutting and rebate system showed\n1156\tan increase of earnings IN the first twelve\n1157\tmonths of its emancipation of something\n1158\tlike SIC. 600. $50 Certain Industries which\n1159\twere involved were less profitably con-\n1160\tQuoted. perhaps. while ertainindividuals\n1161\tconcerned found their personal emolp-\n1162\tments subjected 10 a severe shrinkage.\n1163\t\n1164\t\n1165\tWe hope the Atchison scandal will be-\n1166\turobed TO its uttermost depths and that\n1167\tthe President will let no guilty mar\n1168\tescape. We could wish that the duty\n1169\tof doing the necessary work dcvolvec\n1170\tupon a body Of better repute thar\n1171\tthe Interstate Commerce Commission\n1172\twhich for incompetence and peryersitJ\n1173\tis without a peer and which enjoys\n1174\tin an equal degree the contempt ot\n1175\tthe courts and the indifference Of thu\n1176\trailroads Perhaps, too, the President\n1177\twill be convinced that in the pursuit\n1178\tof his amb ition he has found a weapon\n1179\tmore effective than Congress and less\n1180\tdoubtful than an appeal from the Con\n1181\tstitution. We mean public opinion. II,\n1182\thas certainly enlisted it as it never wa.\n1183\tenlisted before, and we think that viii\n1184\tits aid anything that is desirable can b'\n1185\taccomplished. 1t were better far thai\n1186\tthe public continued to suffer for a time\n1187\tand that railroad owners were robbed %\n1188\tall they possess than that the principle\n1189\tand spirit of our theory OF govcramen\n1190\t\n1191\t\n1192\t5000 fro,, Vur., ,-reDus lun\n1193\t\n1194\t=== 1_1896-07-06_p14_sn85066387_0017503741A_1896070601_0113.json\n1195\tThe San Francisco call. · 1896-07-06\n1196\tcrops: None\n1197\theadline: She Wants to Join Her Husband\n1198\tin the Spirit Land When\n1199\t\n1200\t\n1201\tUnder Them.\n1202\t\n1203\tIS NOW IN A PADDED CELL\n1204\t\n1205\tThe Doctors at the Receiving Hos.\n1206\tpital Can See No Signs of In.\n1207\t\n1208\t\n1209\tsanity in Her.\n1210\t---\n1211\tThe case of Mrs. Allie Gender, widow\n1212\t53 years of age, will form subject for in\n1213\tvestigation by the Insanity Commission\n1214\ters this morning.\n1215\t\n1216\t\n1217\tShe is in padded cell in the Receiving\n1218\tHospital charged with insanity, but any\n1219\tone who talks with her cannot see the\n1220\tslightest sign OF mental alteration in hel\n1221\tconduct or speech. Yet her friends say\n1222\tshe is crazy, and too dangerous to be al\n1223\tlarge.\n1224\t\n1225\t\n1226\tTwelve years ago her husband was killed\n1227\tin railroad accident. He had an accl-\n1228\tdent policy for $500, and this sum was\n1229\tpaid to the widow. or rather to Albert\n1230\tShainwald, her brother-in-law, living at\n1231\t207 Geary street, who was administrator Of\n1232\ther husbands estate.\n1233\t\n1234\t\n1235\tThe widow was left with three young\n1236\tchildren, boy and two girls. They were\n1237\tput in home and the mother worked\n1238\thard to support herself and them. The\n1239\t$500 was placed on deposit in the San\n1240\tFrancisco Savings Union bank and with\n1241\tinterest it has reached the sum of $2100. OF\n1242\tthis amount $500 has been withdrawn and\n1243\tloaned to G. Petrie, printer in Alameda.\n1244\t\n1245\t\n1246\tMrs. Gender about two years ago went\n1247\tto live with her brother-in-law, but three\n1248\tweeks ago she removed to 238 Sixth street,\n1249\twhere she engaged a room. According TO\n1250\tthe landlady, Mrs. George, she showed\n1251\tsymptoms of insanity shortly after being\n1252\tin her house and on Saturday she became\n1253\tviolent and threatened to take her life, so\n1254\tDR.. Bohm was sent for and he ordered\n1255\tthat she be sent to the Receiving Hospital.\n1256\t\n1257\t\n1258\tHer chief hallucination is that her hus-\n1259\tbands spirit is constantly with her.\n1260\tWhen walking on the street she imagined\n1261\that har hnos do J., II\n1262\tverse with him as if he were in the flesh\n1263\tat night she would alarm every one in\n1264\tthe house by going from room to room\n1265\tsearching for her husband and when she\n1266\tcould not find him she became violent and\n1267\thad to be placed under restraint.\n1268\t\n1269\t\n1270\tWhen she became calm she would talk\n1271\tnationally and say she wanted to Join her\n1272\thusband in the spirit land and if the\n1273\tmoney was only divided among her chill\n1274\tdren she would soon be with him.\n1275\t\n1276\t\n1277\tThe Oldest, young man % years of age,\n1278\tis in Mendocino County, where he has\n1279\tbought some Government land. One of\n1280\tthe girls is married and the other is a\n1281\tservant with family on Union street.\n1282\tApparently the money is not to be di-\n1283\tvided till the youngest. who is now 20\n1284\t| years of age. attains the age of 2i.\n1285\t| Speaking of her arrest Mrs. Gender said\n1286\tyesterday: sl think it is all a Joke to\n1287\tkeep me here. was in my room vester.\n1288\tday when two men wearing blue uniforms\n1289\tcame and asked me to put on my shoe.\n1290\tand come with them. Then they put me\n1291\tin patrol wagon and brought me here\n1292\t| But | don't know why. l am not crazy\n1293\tand | cannot see why l should be kept\n1294\there\"\n1295\t\n1296\t\n1297\tThe doctors at the hospital who have\n1298\tconversed with her say that she does not\n1299\texhibit the slightest symptoms of in.\n1300\tsanity, but the landlord and landlady o.\n1301\tthe lodging-house at 238 Sixth Street, DR.\n1302\tBohm and others say that she has spells\n1303\tand when under them she is violently\n1304\t/ insane.\n1305\t\n1306\t=== 30_1946-07-13_p2_sn83045462_00280604781_1946071301_0507.json\n1307\tEvening star. · 1946-07-13\n1308\tcrops: None\n1309\theadline: 4o Seek Ghost Job\n1310\t---\n1311\tPROVIDENCE, R. I. (P).-Just 40\n1312\tmen passed civil service examina-\n1313\tton for motor vehicle inspector.\n1314\tbut they're holding onto their pres\n1315\tent jobs-if they have any. For\n1316\tthere is not single vacancy for\n1317\tthe post.\n1318\t\n1319\t=== 6_1914-07-25_pna_sn86061215_00295875410_1914072501_0441.json\n1320\tOttumwa tri-weekly courier. [volume] (Ottumwa, Iowa) 1903-1916 · 1914-07-25\n1321\tcrops: None\n1322\theadline: ,cr1Aprrp xv-(Concluded.)\n1323\t---\n1324\tfCHApTER xv.-(Concluded)\n1325\t\n1326\t\n1327\tBut only for a minute; then they\n1328\tcaved. crumpled up, and took their\n1329\tmoney-perhaps because couple OF\n1330\tDAYS before they had been watching\n1331\tme tossing tomato cans into the ali\n1332\tnd,bouring them with my pistol, with\n1333\tfewer misses than looked good, to\n1334\tthem.\n1335\t\n1336\t\n1337\tThree months did fir the Double\n1338\tCircla. Pacif1ed, there was no further\n1339\tneed of my services. My happy little\n1340\touting was over, and it was trail back\n1341\tto the home: back, with the scent OF\n1342\tlife In my nostrils and the thrill of\n1343\taction in my blood, into the scent or\n1344\tthe grave and the asphyxiating atmos-\n1345\tphere Of the mentally feeble and mort\n1346\tbund.\n1347\t\n1348\t\n1349\tBut. happily, the Double Circla Incl\n1350\tdent proved the dawning OF the bright\n1351\test period Of my life, a dawning whose\n1352\tshining rays were shortly to lead me\n1353\tinto a harbor Of peace and Joy; OF con-\n1354\ttentment and bliss, that I had never\n1355\tdreamed could be mine.\n1356\t\n1357\t\n1358\tDown OF the home one day came\n1359\tfrom the redwood lumber camps OF\n1360\tnorth California an old soldier whom\n1361\t/ had known as a lad in Ionia County.\n1362\t\n1363\t\n1364\tThrough the war he had served IN\n1365\tMichigan regiment. Only twice since\n1366\thad he revisited the old home. but he\n1367\thad continued correspondence with his\n1368\tfamily.\n1369\t\n1370\t\n1371\tFrom him I learned-oh, Joy! the\n1372\tgreatest that could come to me-not\n1373\tonly that Tom Harper had recovered\n1374\tof the wound that had left me regard\n1375\tinR myself for half a century as an\n1376\toutlaw. not only that my mischievous\n1377\tBessie McVicker still lived, but that\n1378\tthrough all the weary years OF my ap'\n1379\tsence she had remained single!\n1380\t\n1381\t\n1382\tGould It be that she had withheld\n1383\ther heart and hand through all those\n1384\tyears for memory Of me? No. That\n1385\twere indeed folly Of the widest.\n1386\t\n1387\t\n1388\tsha wait for me? She, who had\n1389\tnever passed me so much as kindly\n1390\tword. who was ever making me the\n1391\tsubject Of cruel jests! She, above all,\n1392\twho had run to Toms side and taken\n1393\this head In her lap when, fallen under\n1394\tmy blow he lay so still In the snow!\n1395\t\n1396\t\n1397\tNonsense. Doubtless instead she\n1398\thad forgotten the fact had ever ex-\n1399\tlisted. But would know; must\n1400\tknow\n1401\t\n1402\t\n1403\tPromptly her answer came. Fol\n1404\tlowed a correspondence in which she\n1405\towned she had loved me from the first;\n1406\tthat she we'll deserved the sousing IN\n1407\tthe sap-trough had given her; and\n1408\tthat she had only run to Tom IN fear\n1409\tmy blow had killed him. and In the\n1410\thope she might revive him and save\n1411\tme the grave consequences that must\n1412\tensue in case he died at my hands.\n1413\t\n1414\t\n1415\tBut oh! the grief intermingled 4\n1416\tour 10y! She remained as poor as |;\n1417\tcould no more come to me than could\n1418\t70 to her She was shackled by her\n1419\tpoverty in the sugar-bush where had\n1420\tleft her, I by mine in the Soldiers\n1421\tHome. We were like two disembodied\n1422\tspirits reunited, denied the pressure OF\n1423\tdearly loved lips. the clasp Of quick\n1424\tpulsing hands.\n1425\t\n1426\t\n1427\tBut, ali! the pride and happiness our\n1428\tletter intercourse brought me! Bessie\n1429\thad stayed true to me as to her. And\n1430\ttome it was all the dearer that mine\n1431\thad been a silent alteration had never\n1432\tdared express, and yet so strong she\n1433\tmust have sensed it, to have responded\n1434\tq plainly she had, by lifetime's de-\n1435\tvotion.\n1436\t\n1437\t\n1438\tTime passed-much time-until we\n1439\t\n1440\t=== 1_1897-06-30_p4_sn82015104_00280768765_1897063001_0257.json\n1441\tThe record-union. · 1897-06-30\n1442\tcrops: None\n1443\theadline: FATHER AND SON.\n1444\t\n1445\tThey Are Having Trouble Over the\n1446\t\n1447\t\n1448\tLatter's Earnings.\n1449\t---\n1450\tWhat will probably prove an inter\n1451\testing case will be tried before a Po-\n1452\tlice Court jury next Saturday morn\n1453\ting.\n1454\t\n1455\t\n1456\tComplaint was made at police head\n1457\tquarters on Saturday last, by the\n1458\tfather and brother of Pearley Chap\n1459\tpell, that that young man had fallen\n1460\tunder the influence of an alleged spir-\n1461\titualist, named Mrs. Quarles, and at\n1462\ther suggestion had refused to return\n1463\tto the paternal roof\n1464\t\n1465\t\n1466\tThe boys story is that his father\n1467\tand brother interfered to prevent him\n1468\tfrom drawing his salary and that he\n1469\thas not been allowed fair share of\n1470\this earnings.\n1471\t\n1472\t\n1473\t1t was at first the intention of the\n1474\telder Chappell to have his son Pear\n1475\tley, who is about IS years old, ar\n1476\trested, but acting on subsequent ad-\n1477\tvice concluded to bring a charge of\n1478\tdisturbing the peace against Mrs.\n1479\tQuarles.\n1480\t\n1481\t\n1482\tYoung Chappell's brother, who is en-\n1483\tployed in Lavensons shoe store, has\n1484\talso caused the arrest of Mrs. Clough,\n1485\ta respectable woman who keeps a 1odg-\n1486\t"}]}}
