{"parentUuid":"ff8f9eae-bd9f-4d59-944a-ce6859614dbb","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"775e54ef-6487-4f79-8598-643421b87718","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RSuaC9umHAjodTaKsJy5BE","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t=== 71_1893-09-12_pna_sn86092518_00414186749_1893091201_0065.json\n2\tWheeling register. [volume] (Wheeling, W. Va.) 1878-1935 · 1893-09-12\n3\tcrops: cached\n4\theadline: ADJUSTMENTS.\n5\t\n6\tTHE THREE GUARDsMEN\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t\n10\t\n11\tFor vigor and originality Alexander\n12\tSalivni probably stands without an\n13\tequal on the stage, as the large audi\n14\tence that witnessed his personation OF\n15\tD'Argtanan In THE Three Guardsmen\"\n16\tat the Opera House last evening will at\n17\ttest. Every element Of the hero OF the\n18\tromantic drama Is embodied In him.\n19\tYoung, handsome, powerful and\n20\tgraceful, q complete master\n21\t''stage business\" and anactor of consu-\n22\tmate skill and delicate sensibility, Mr..\n23\tSalvini divides fully the histrionic\n24\thonors with his famous father, though\n25\ttheir roles are different. Young Salvini\n26\tpossesses fortune in his remarkable\n27\tvigor and earnestness, which to-day are\n28\trare qualities on the stage. His portrayal\n29\tof Dumas vivacious, daring and heroic\n30\tyoung Gascon soldier was as strong\n31\tand consistent throughout as IL was pol\n32\tished and artistic. The appreciation OF\n33\tthe audience was evidenced by the re-\n34\tcalls which Mr.. Salvini received at the\n35\tclose of each act. His support was ex-\n36\tcellent and the stage setting good. 1t\n37\twas theatrical treat, and Wheeling\n38\ttheatre-goers will be glad to welcome\n39\tthe brilliant young Italian actor again.\n40\t\n41\t\n42\tDEVILS MINE\"\n43\t\n44\t\n45\tThe Devils Mine Company will be\n46\tthe attraction at the Grand Thursday,\n47\tFriday and Saturday. This beautiful\n48\twestern drama has been at the Grand\n49\tseveral times In the last four seasons,\n50\tand no doubt will be welcomed back\n51\twith a packed house at each perform\n52\tance. The play is faithful picture OF\n53\tlife in the Rockies. There are many\n54\tpretty and interesting scenes In the\n55\tplay, among them beautiful cataract\n56\tscene with real water rushing over the\n57\trocks. Reserved seats now on sale at\n58\tthe Grand box office.\n59\t\n60\t\n61\tTHE MUSER.\n62\t\n63\t\n64\tA large audience thronged the Musee\n65\tTheater last night to see the curios and\n66\tlisten TO the wonderful. \"Pama Dasi-\n67\tka\" made all sorts of gyrations with her\n68\tpet alligators, lizards and snakes.\n69\tSongs aud dances, and Harry Boy, the\n70\tspiritualistic medium, and other attrac-\n71\toil ,noAa uacs m mani\n72\t\n73\t=== 13_1893-08-14_pna_sn94052989_00175040262_1893081401_0525.json\n74\tThe morning call. [volume] (San Francisco [Calif.]) 1878-1895 · 1893-08-14\n75\tcrops: cached\n76\theadline: At the Camp=Meeting of\n77\t\n78\t\n79\tColored People.\n80\t\n81\tHOW THE BOY ENJOYED 1T.\n82\t\n83\tLed Away by His Mother to a Pos\n84\tSible SeanCe Behind the\n85\t\n86\t\n87\tWOodshed.\n88\t---\n89\tne Jumped so high, he hit the SKY.\n90\t\n91\t\n92\tAnu never came down till the Fourth OF July.\n93\t\n94\t\n95\tvery small boy. with very big buzz\n96\tsaw hat aud blue sailor blouse, sat on\n97\t\n98\t\n99\thigh-5acked red bench at Trestle Glenlyes-\n100\tterday afternoon, and kept repeating the\n101\twords for the benefit of his mother. who\n102\tmildly eXpostulated with him from time to\n103\ttime. From the canvass-waled pavilion\n104\tnear at hand the strains of II Need Thee\n105\tEvery Hour\" and 1.-The Fountain Lies\n106\tOpen\" Game splashing out through the\n107\topenings. and the mother invnluntarily\n108\tfollowed the refrain in half audible tones.\n109\tUnmiudful of the music. the boy kept say\n110\ting, II asked my mamma. etc\"\n111\t\n112\t\n113\tInside the pavilion there was that which\n114\treminded the spectators OF the White\n115\tCity at Chicago, because IL was so differ\n116\temit 1t was. in fact, the opening day of\n117\tthe camp meeting and revy 4 To be held\n118\tfor two weeks under the auspices OF the\n119\tcolored Beth-Eden Baptist Church. of\n120\twhich Rev. r. A. McGuinn is pastor, in\n121\tthe work of which he is assisted by the\n122\tState evangelist. Rev. j. Fl. Van Leu.\n123\tThe Rev. Mr.. McGuinn Is d tall gen tie\n124\tman. with dignltied expression of coun-\n125\ttenance and a pair of natiy-ooking side\n126\tWhlSkerS. Ht IS also possessed Of a muSl-\n127\tcal bass voice. and has at his tongues end\n128\tnearly all the good, old-fashioned camp\n129\tmeeting tunes that were known u the\n130\tancestors of the present generation. In IL\n131\tthere Is the dr0nlng suggestion of The\n132\tplantation melodies of Dixie, and this was\n133\tthe voice hat attracted the attention of the\n134\tmother OF the boy whose mind was\n135\tfastened upon the elephant that jun ped so\n136\thigh.\n137\t\n138\t\n139\tThere were services in the morning ser\n140\tvices in the afternoon and services IN The\n141\tevening. all of which were well attended\n142\tby the cnlored folk from around the DAY,\n143\tand every one OF them sang as If his or\n144\ther entire soul was wrapped up in the\n145\tsongs.\n146\t\n147\t\n148\tAfter the services Rev. Mr.. Quinn said:\n149\texpect an increase with every day. The\n150\tfact of the matter is that this NEE ing was\n151\tarranged in hurry, and We had no chance\n152\tto advertise 1t so that all our people would\n153\tknow aboutit. Considering everything as\n154\tii Is we are more than pleased with the\n155\toutlook. and | am OF tile belief that before\n156\tthe two weeks are over We will have big\n157\tcrowd and will be able to do a splendid\n158\twork. Then, too. the white people seem\n159\tlake 4 great deal Of intere st in our meet\n160\tings, and they treat US with the greatest\n161\tkindness. They come in large numbers\n162\tto all our services and listen with the most\n163\trespectful attention. They know that we\n164\tare in earnest, and give US credit for our\n165\tefforts\n166\t\n167\t\n168\tThen as the crowd slowly dispersed the\n169\tboy with buZZ-saw hat took his mothers\n170\thand and said Il asked my mamma for\n171\tIs cents\" and as he disappeared over the\n172\tELL hi, mother looked at him with a\n173\tgrieved expression of face in which there\n174\twas just an Intimaton OF threat.\n175\t\n176\t=== 18_1932-04-14_p8_sn82014085_00414183499_1932041401_0661.json\n177\tThe Waterbury Democrat. · 1932-04-14\n178\tcrops: cached\n179\theadline: BRISTOL.\n180\t\n181\tHigh School Nine\n182\t\n183\t To Meet Clubsters\n184\t\n185\tCross Bats Saturday Afternoon at Muzzy Field\n186\t\n187\t Forty Hours Devotion at St Matthew's\n188\t---\n189\tMir and Mrs A. Recchio of 105 Jacob street have announced the marriage of their daughter, Sarah Dolores to Arthur Martin, son of Mir and Mrs A. Martin of Tulip street. The marriage took pace in :t Anthony's church April 9 with Rev Louis Becarris, pastor of the church officiating.\n190\t\n191\t Nionahanmcn Meet Ciubsters\n192\t\n193\t The Bristol high school baseball charges of Coach Tommy Mona ian will face the Bristol Boys dub nine Saturday afternoon at .tuzzy field. The maroo'i and white ossers will present theil strongest ..ncup in quest of a victory over heir arch rivals.\n194\t\n195\t Coach Monahan will start Albie .;urske upon the mound in the ,,l st game with Andy Palau on the receiving end of the losses. The in- pe.d which includes three sopho- lrcrc; will find Walt Sonstroem at tne initial sack, Pete Nodarski at TiidNay. Wal,y Utke digging 'em f, at short and Hennie kwiatkow- sst. at third base. Joe Marc. Cap n Roy Cur.iss. Mix. Pavelchak, M pari,y\" Di Giovanni and Jackie .ys,fgrove will make the outfeld.\n196\t\n197\t sings Over WTIC\n198\t\n199\t Mi..s Winifred Barnfcld of this <. ty. well Known cont,.alto soloist .feadt asted spring time pro- E Iran, over Station WTIC at Hart ef ferd | his morning at