t that was the inquiry\n231\tOf cabin dwellers in Indiana, put to\n232\tthose who knocked at their doors,\n233\to'nights, in times of danger This is\n234\tjust as correct as the statement that\n235\tSaratoga is composed Of the word Sara\n236\tand of the Latin word toga which\n237\tmeans n cloak. Both theories Of derlv-\n238\talton are phonetically fine, lingually\n239\tgrammatical, audibly impressive, typo\n240\tgraphically taking and amusingly apo\n241\tcryphal.\n242\t\n243\t=== 33_1906-09-12_p1_sn86058242_00271745734_1906091201_0480.json\n244\tThe Lake County times. [volume] (Hammond, Ind.) 1906-1933 · 1906-09-12\n245\tcrops: cached\n246\theadline: TRIPS TO GHOSTLAND\n247\t\n248\tHAUNTED HOUSES INVESTIGATED BY\n249\t\n250\t\n251\tLOMBROSO.\n252\t---\n253\t\n254\t\n255\t\n256\t10 BE Beyond Solution by Any Of\n257\tthe known Laws OF Physical Sci\n258\teace-A PusxIed Physicist.\n259\t\n260\t\n261\tProfessor Lombroso, the italian phys\n262\t.cist contributed to the Annales des\n263\tSciences Psyehiques an account OF two\n264\tpsychic phenomena which he investi-\n265\tgated purely in the interests of mate\n266\trial science. The article is entitled\n267\tHaunted Houses Which | Have Stud\n268\tled\" and the professor confessed that\n269\tbe set about his investigations with\n270\tnothing to be desired in the complete\n271\tness of his skepticism.\n272\t\n273\t\n274\tThe phenomena in question, how\n275\tever, proved to be beyond solution by\n276\tany known laws of physical science.\n277\tFrankly, the great criminologist adj\n278\tmitted that for the first time in his\n279\tlife he was in the presence of the in\n280\ttangible and that he was worsted in\n281\this attempt to grapple with the unsub-\n282\tstantial\n283\t\n284\t\n285\tII was asked in November, 1900, to\n286\tinvestigate some strange occurrences\n287\twhich had taken place at the house Of\n288\tSignor Pavarino by way Of Pescatori,\n289\tTurin. One phenomenon was asked\n290\tto explain was the mysterious over\n291\tturning of cups and jugs, which were\n292\tprecipitated to the ground by some\n293\tunseen agency.\n294\t\n295\t\n296\t''These occurrences were accompa-\n297\tMED by loud knockings on the walls\n298\taud the pulling of bells in various\n299\tparts Of the house. The youngest\n300\tdaughter was awakened on several oc-\n301\tcasions by blows aimed at her by some\n302\tunseen hand, and her body on exami-\n303\tnation was found to be covered with\n304\tbruises. the result of severe pinchings.\n305\tHer sister, who slept in the same bed,\n306\tfelt nothing and was not disturbed In\n307\tany way.\n308\t\n309\t\n310\t1.-THE clash of rapiers was heard in\n311\tthe house at another time, accompa-\n312\tMED by cries of anger and groans of\n313\tanguish. Lights were also seen pass\n314\ting to and fro in unlighted rooms. An\n315\tAlpine hat was continually moving\n316\tabout the room, and even when it was\n317\t4 deliberately nailed to g chair an invis-\n318\t| ible hand precipitated it to the ground.\n319\t| All these mysterious happenings went\n320\ton for eight months, and in regard to\n321\t/ them l took the evidence of reliable\n322\tpersons, who testified that they had\n323\tbeen eyewitnesses of the phenomena.\n324\t! Even when the bell ropes were removed\n325\tfrom the bells the tinkling continued\n326\tat all hours OF the day and night.\n327\t\n328\t\n329\t1.-THE scene changed to a house In the\n330\tnext street, by way of Bava, adjoining\n331\tby way of Pescatori, where the news\n332\tpapers of Turin began to chronicle mys-\n333\tFERROUS occurrences of almost a similar\n334\tnature to these which occurred in by\n335\tway of Pescatori, cups and saucers,\n336\tpans and kettles being juggled about\n337\tby an unseen agency and causing at\n338\tthe time a most Internal row.\n339\t\n340\t\n341\tTN particular, one circumstance was\n342\tmost noteworthy. In the cellar filed\n343\twith full and empty bottles, much loss\n344\tto the proprietor was caused by the\n345\tsmashing of the TASKS by some unseen\n346\thand, but with every trace of method\n347\tand willfulness in the way in which the\n348\tbreakage occurred. On my entrance\n349\tinto the cellar l lighted five candles\n350\tthinking that a supernatural spirit\n351\twould avoid the light. On the con\n352\ttrary, l saw three empty bottles roll\n353\tabout as If pushed by a finger and\n354\tbreak close by me. l made every pos\n355\tsible investigation to discover bidder\n356\ttrick, but to no avail. Several bottles\n357\twere taken up in the air as if lifted by\n358\tthe hand and dashed to the ground.\n359\t\n360\t\n361\tTHE wife of the proprietor left abou\n362\tthat time for visit to her father\n363\tWhile she was away the occurrences\n364\tceased, only to break out again imme\n365\tdiately on her return by way of Bava\n366\tThe waiter of the house was then re\n367\tmoved, and the occurrences ceased al\n368\ttogether, leaving it to be supposed\n369\tsince no other hypothesis is probable\n370\tthat he was the medium through which\n371\t: the phenomena were rendered possible\n372\t\n373\t\n374\tI'm May, 1903, / was asked TO inves\n375\ttigate another mysterious affair which\n376\thappened in the family of a printe\n377\tnamed Mignotti, also in Turin. 1t wa\n378\tlittle boy, went TO bed at night louo\n379\tknoekings began to be heard on thu\n380\twall next to which he lay. 4 doctor\n381\tthe police and myself were called in\n382\tWe examined the walls and the room\n383\twith great care.\n384\t\n385\t\n386\tGAS soon as the boy was placed Il\n387\tbis bed the knocking began. 1t wa\n388\tevidently conducted In an inteiligen\n389\tmanner, and it was possible to estah\n390\tlish some sort of communication. l\n391\twas only, however, when the boy la\n392\tawake chat the phenomenon occurred\n393\tAS soon as he went TO sleep the knock\n394\ting became very vague and indistinci\n395\tHe was clearly the immediate cause\n396\tthough beyond high fever, when tn\n397\tphenomenon was most frequent, b\n398\twas in other respects a normal child\n399\t! ! state these facts clearly and absC\n400\tlutely as / witnessed them and comics\n401\tmy total inability to explain them\n402\tPublic Opinion.\n403\t\n404\t=== 8_1932-10-28_p17_sn82014085_00393347715_1932102801_0807.json\n405\tThe Waterbury Democrat. · 1932-10-28\n406\tcrops: None\n407\theadline: you go to market.\n408\t\n409\t HOb-Goblin Gingerbread\n410\t---\n411\tyou go to market.\n412\t\n413\t Hob- Goblin Gingerbread\n414\t\n415\t Make gingerbread with your usual recipe, and bake in a sheet. While it is baking, make your hob goblin filling as follows: Mash 2 packages cream cheese G oz) and to it add package pasteurized dates (sliced), cup chopped nut meats and 1-2 teaspoon salt. Add enough cream so mixture will spread. When gingerbread is done. split, spread with this filling and replace top layer. Serve warm. Delicious! If you prefer, add the 1-2 package of, dates right to your gingerbread batter and bake as usual. Then frost with orange frosting.,\n416\t\n417\t Witches Brew\n418\t\n419\t 1 can grapefruit\n420\t\n421\t=== 4_1906-11-09_p1_sn83045366_00206538442_1906110901_0197.json\n422\tThe Minneapolis journal. [volume] (Minneapolis, Minn.) 1888-1939 · 1906-11-09\n423\tcrops: None\n424\theadline: Problem of the Phantom Auto,\n425\t---\n426\tJacques Futrelle, and. that wonder\n427\tfull Thinking Machine in which he con.\n428\t6des, relates in the Sunday Magazine\n429\tOf next Sundays Journal the \"Prob-\n430\tlem Of the Phantom Auto.' Yarbor-\n431\tough was a wide 'expanse Of country\n432\testates and superbly kept roads. The\n433\tTrap was singularly tempting, a per-\n434\tfectly maca damized road lying betw cen\n435\tthe two tall walls, with only enough\n436\tOf a sinuous twist in it to make each\n437\tend invisible from the center. The\n438\tTrap was an ideal spot for curbing the\n439\tambitions of Marlborough residents for\n440\teclipsing all speed records in autos.\n441\tWith man at each end of the Trap,\n442\twith telephone communication between,\n443\tthe Marlborough constables had reason\n444\tto Congratulate themselves, But the\n445\tPhantom Auto changed all this. One\n446\tnight it came into the Trap at sixty\n447\tmi les an hour. The man at the other\n448\tend waited twenty minutes and failed\n449\tto. see it. The next night the same\n450\tthing. happened. The problem wassstill\n451\tunsolved when Hutchinson Hatch, the\n452\treporter, laid it before Professor Van\n453\tDusen, the Thinking Machine. His\n454\tlogic easily solved the problem that\n455\tseemed impossible to detectives: It\n456\twas all so simple thai. it set everybody\n457\ta-laughing when the phantom auto was\n458\tno longer mystery ,r =\n459\t\n460\t=== 10_1921-05-24_p16_sn85038485_00340583036_1921052401_0507.json\n461\tThe Birmingham age-herald. [volume] (Birmingham, Ala.) 1902-1950 · 1921-05-24\n462\tcrops: None\n463\theadline: THE BIRMINGHAM AGEHERALD TUESDAY, MAY a. 1921\n464\t---\n465\trare pianist who makes them laugh and\n466\tlisten. His partner plays well on the\n467\txyliphone. An enthusiastic applause re-\n468\twarded the act.\n469\t\n470\t\n471\tWith piano jazz and fun over the piano,\n472\tfunny remarks. songs and Jig, the\n473\tWidowed Pair act went over Dig, and\n474\tsecond only to the headliner. Clayton is\n475\tcomedian with the droll appeal, his\n476\tpartner is pretty and attractive and jgs\n477\tgracefully.\n478\t\n479\t\n480\tThe mysticism Of seance is used for\n481\tartistic purposes by Edna Dreon in her\n482\t''Vaudevill Seance\" She recalls the girl\n483\tof the past, brings down girl from\n484\tMars and asks the ouija questions in a\n485\touija song that took well with the\n486\taudience.\n487\t\n488\t\n489\tTwo other acts completed the pro-\n490\tgramme. One is a target shooting act\n491\twith pretty settings, and some daring\n492\tWorkmanship. The other and closing\n493\tact is two modern gynnasts. They are\n494\tfine and attractive personally, giving\n495\tone of the best acts Of the kind in\n496\tlong time.\n497\t\n498\t=== 5_1920-11-12_p2_sn85033000_0041421213A_1920111201_0919.json\n499\tThe log cabin Democrat. · 1920-11-12\n500\tcrops: None\n501\theadline: OUIJA BOARD MEDIUM\n502\t\n503\t OF LOW BROWS' ONLY\n504\t---\n505\tChicago, Nov. Elf you would mingle with the elite of departed spirits, do not toy with the shades who use the ouija boards as means of communication, because, under ordi- nary circumstances, only the riffraff of spiritdom speak during sessions of the great American parlor game was the warning given today by Miss Gail Wilson, trustee of the American sec tion of the theosophical society. She stands high in the councils of the theosophical world\n506\t\n507\t Only the low type of soul would make itself manifest on the ouiia board said Miss Wilson, who has given the subject much research. \"A high type of spirit would not use such a crude and dangerous method Why should highly evolved spirit want to mingle among earth beings when they are on a much higher plane\n508\t\n509\t Speaking n4the slangy languages of the earth, most of the spirits we get connected with on the ouija lie like circus posters.\n510\t\n511\t I'll you are looking for advice, it is far better to take it from a good living person than a bad dead one No spirit can tell the future better than an ordinary earth-being. It's rare that a respectable spirit raps on the library table, either\"\n512\t\n513\t Miss Wilson holds that there are just few cases on record of high. class spirits talking via the ouiia board route, but these cases are so rare that they may be discounted.\n514\t\n515\t=== 15_1898-06-12_p1_sn83030214_00175036866_1898061201_0236.json\n516\tNew-York tribune. [volume] (New York [N.Y.]) 1866-1924 · 1898-06-12\n517\tcrops: None\n518\theadline: CONGRESS SPRINGS.\n519\t\n520\tTHE VIRTUES OF THE WATER BOTTLED FOR\n521\t\n522\t\n523\tEXPORT.\n524\t---\n525\t\n526\t\n527\t\n528\tEXPORT\n529\t\n530\t\n531\tCongress Syring water has PROB