entions in his interview that if there is graft in\n1170\tthe police department he doesn't know anything\n1171\tshout it. The coppers ought to feel gratfied toward\n1172\ttheir boss for the inuendo supplied in the statement\n1173\t\n1174\t\n1175\tCongressman johnson doesn't feel that he can\n1176\tleave Washington to do a little fenee-budng IN his\n1177\tdistrict All of which goes to show that johnson has\n1178\tmore sense than believed he had The money\n1179\tthat he would spend on the trip and campaign will\n1180\tcome IN handy when be retires from congress next\n1181\tspring\n1182\t\n1183\t\n1184\tarmy EngIand's to get $4,000 dozen American eggs\n1185\tat pc the Dozen\n1186\t\n1187\t=== 5_1920-02-11_p1_sn87057262_00295875276_1920021101_0053.json\n1188\tThe daily Gate City and constitution-Democrat. (Keokuk, Iowa) 1916-1922 · 1920-02-11\n1189\tcrops: None\n1190\theadline: CHALLENGE To\n1191\tSPIRIT MEDIUM\n1192\tIf Mrs. Curran Can Prove Her\n1193\tPowers, She Will be Given\n1194\t\n1195\t\n1196\tthe Reward Of\n1197\t\n1198\t\n1199\t$4,000.\n1200\t\n1201\tOPERATES OUIJA BOARD\n1202\t\n1203\tTest Proposed In Effort to Receive\n1204\tActual Message from Person\n1205\t\n1206\t\n1207\tIn tho Next\n1208\t\n1209\t\n1210\tWorld.\n1211\t---\n1212\tUNITED Press Leased Wire Service ]\n1213\t\n1214\t\n1215\tNEW YORK, Feb. 11.-Joseph F.\n1216\tRim today explained on what condi-\n1217\ttons Mrs. John H Curren Of St Louis,\n1218\tmedium for Patience Worth\" can win\n1219\tthe $1,000 he has offered for proof OF\n1220\tspiritual communication.\n1221\t\n1222\t\n1223\t\"The conditions l will make In or\n1224\tder to be convinced. are these:\n1225\t\n1226\t\n1227\tArrangement of letters on Mrs.\n1228\tCurran's ouija board must be chang\n1229\ted. 4-, a\n1230\t\n1231\t\n1232\t\"The board must be covered with\n1233\tpaper, upon which a number will be\n1234\tplaced corresponding to the letter be-\n1235\tmeath. ~ ....\n1236\t\n1237\t\n1238\tTHE key to the board will be plac-\n1239\ted on table behind Mrs. Curran Sc\n1240\ther spirit friends can see 1t.\n1241\t\n1242\t\n1243\t\"If under these conditions. she can\n1244\tproduce understandable communica-\n1245\ttons from a person or persons in the\n1246\tnext world, will admit she has some\n1247\tpsychic or telepathic power and she\n1248\twins the $4,000 Until then, will con-\n1249\ttinue to believe the Patience Worth\n1250\tpoems and writings are the result OF\n1251\ther own personality\"\n1252\t\n1253\t=== 5_1913-06-21_p1_sn85066387_00280768601_1913062101_0113.json\n1254\tThe San Francisco call. [volume] (San Francisco [Calif.]) 1895-1913 · 1913-06-21\n1255\tcrops: None\n1256\theadline: \"sP0OK' Or PLUMBING,\n1257\t\n1258\t\n1259\tLISTENING To NOISES\n1260\t\n1261\t\n1262\tMEANS 81 EACH NIGHT\n1263\t\n1264\tPalo Alto House Shivers\n1265\tWith Nocturnal Groans\n1266\t\n1267\t\n1268\tand Chain Clankings\n1269\t\n1270\twANTED--Watehma for ghosts; must\n1271\t\n1272\t\n1273\tbe temperate and steady; hours 8\n1274\ty. m. 10 sunrise. every third night;\n1275\t\n1276\t\n1277\t81 per night. Apply JOHN ARATA.\n1278\tHamilton avenue extension, Palo Alto.\n1279\t---\n1280\tSpecial Dispatch to The CalD\n1281\t\n1282\t\n1283\tPALO ALTO, June 2O.-Every third\n1284\tnight a ghost stalks in John Arata's\n1285\thome here. The house wobbles and\n1286\tthen shakes nightly weird echoes Of\n1287\tclanking chains. mysterious means and\n1288\tghostIy groans strike terror in the\n1289\tArata family.\n1290\t\n1291\t\n1292\tFor two weeks Morris Quinn. heavily\n1293\tarmed, had the job of watchman Four\n1294\ttimes he heard the ghost, but was un-\n1295\tdaunted, collecting his ST at sunrise\n1296\teach morning Wednesday night he\n1297\tsaw a shadowy white form OF the heavy\n1298\tlines of Charles H. Firebaugh, who\n1299\tdied in the house several years ago.\n1300\tquinn forgot all about the dollar and\n1301\tran\n1302\t\n1303\t\n1304\tMr.. Arata has been shooting at the\n1305\tghost\" for several weeks. but so far\n1306\thas not slain it Neighbors complained\n1307\tand Mr. Arata was forced to stop shoot\n1308\ting. Yesterday the wholenArata fam\n1309\tiiy-quite a flock Of children, too-vis-\n1310\ttied Constable Fred simpson and de-\n1311\tnanded that deputy watch the house\n1312\tand stay the ghost by the arm OF\n1313\tthe law.\n1314\t\n1315\t\n1316\tsimpson spent last night at the Arata\n1317\thome. Hearing weird noises in the\n1318\tbasement. Simpson bravely descended,\n1319\tbut could not discover anything.\n1320\t\n1321\t\n1322\tConstable simpson today tried to\n1323\tascriDe the noises to the plumbing. A\n1324\tplumber viii be sent to the Arata house\n1325\tnext week to see whether he can find\n1326\tq sspool1.\"\n1327\t"}]},"uuid":"4e475f81-8ed7-4ec4-8867-1c25db0e527d","timestamp":"2026-10-31T07:37:36.278Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/c1/g02.txt","content":"=== 71_1893-09-12_pna_sn86092518_00414186749_1893091201_0065.json\nWheeling register. [volume] (Wheeling, W. Va.) 1878-1935 · 1893-09-12\ncrops: cached\nheadline: ADJUSTMENTS.\n\nTHE THREE GUARDsMEN\n---\n\n\n\nFor vigor and originality Alexander\nSalivni probably stands without an\nequal on the stage, as the large audi\nence that witnessed his personation OF\nD'Argtanan In THE Three Guardsmen\"\nat the Opera House last evening will at\ntest. Every element Of the hero OF the\nromantic drama Is embodied In him.\nYoung, handsome, powerful and\ngraceful, q complete master\n''stage business\" and anactor of consu-\nmate skill and delicate sensibility, Mr..\nSalvini divides fully the histrionic\nhonors with his famous father, though\ntheir roles are different. Young Salvini\npossesses fortune in his remarkable\nvigor and earnestness, which to-day are\nrare qualities on the stage. His portrayal\nof Dumas vivacious, daring and heroic\nyoung Gascon soldier was as strong\nand consistent throughout as IL was pol\nished and artistic. The appreciation OF\nthe audience was evidenced by the re-\ncalls which Mr.. Salvini received at the\nclose of each act. His support was ex-\ncellent and the stage setting good. 1t\nwas theatrical treat, and Wheeling\ntheatre-goers will be glad to welcome\nthe brilliant young Italian actor again.\n\n\nDEVILS MINE\"\n\n\nThe Devils Mine Company will be\nthe attraction at the Grand Thursday,\nFriday and Saturday. This beautiful\nwestern drama has been at the Grand\nseveral times In the last four seasons,\nand no doubt will be welcomed back\nwith a packed house at each perform\nance. The play is faithful picture OF\nlife in the Rockies. There are many\npretty and interesting scenes In the\nplay, among them beautiful cataract\nscene with real water rushing over the\nrocks. Reserved seats now on sale at\nthe Grand box office.\n\n\nTHE MUSER.\n\n\nA large audience thronged the Musee\nTheater last night to see the curios and\nlisten TO the wonderful. \"Pama Dasi-\nka\" made all sorts of gyrations with her\npet alligators, lizards and snakes.\nSongs aud dances, and Harry Boy, the\nspiritualistic medium, and other attrac-\noil ,noAa uacs m mani\n\n=== 13_1893-08-14_pna_sn94052989_00175040262_1893081401_0525.json\nThe morning call. [volume] (San Francisco [Calif.]) 1878-1895 · 1893-08-14\ncrops: cached\nheadline: At the Camp=Meeting of\n\n\nColored People.\n\nHOW THE BOY ENJOYED 1T.\n\nLed Away by His Mother to a Pos\nSible SeanCe Behind the\n\n\nWOodshed.\n---\nne Jumped so high, he hit the SKY.\n\n\nAnu never came down till the Fourth OF July.\n\n\nvery small boy. with very big buzz\nsaw hat aud blue sailor blouse, sat on\n\n\nhigh-5acked red bench at Trestle Glenlyes-\nterday afternoon, and kept repeating the\nwords for the benefit of his mother. who\nmildly eXpostulated with him from time to\ntime. From the canvass-waled pavilion\nnear at hand the strains of II Need Thee\nEvery Hour\" and 1.-The Fountain Lies\nOpen\" Game splashing out through the\nopenings. and the mother invnluntarily\nfollowed the refrain in half audible tones.\nUnmiudful of the music. the boy kept say\ning, II asked my mamma. etc\"\n\n\nInside the pavilion there was that which\nreminded the spectators OF the White\nCity at Chicago, because IL was so differ\nemit 1t was. in fact, the opening day of\nthe camp meeting and revy 4 To be held\nfor two weeks under the auspices OF the\ncolored Beth-Eden Baptist Church. of\nwhich Rev. r. A. McGuinn is pastor, in\nthe work of which he is assisted by the\nState evangelist. Rev. j. Fl. Van Leu.\nThe Rev. Mr.. McGuinn Is d tall gen tie\nman. with dignltied expression of coun-\ntenance and a pair of natiy-ooking side\nWhlSkerS. Ht IS also possessed Of a muSl-\ncal bass voice. and has at his tongues end\nnearly all the good, old-fashioned camp\nmeeting tunes that were known u the\nancestors of the present generation. In IL\nthere Is the dr0nlng suggestion of The\nplantation melodies of Dixie, and this was\nthe voice hat attracted the attention of the\nmother OF the boy whose mind was\nfastened upon the elephant that jun ped so\nhigh.\n\n\nThere were services in the morning ser\nvices in the afternoon and services IN The\nevening. all of which were well attended\nby the cnlored folk from around the DAY,\nand every one OF them sang as If his or\nher entire soul was wrapped up in the\nsongs.\n\n\nAfter the services Rev. Mr.. Quinn said:\nexpect an increase with every day. The\nfact of the matter is that this NEE ing was\narranged in hurry, and We had no chance\nto advertise 1t so that all our people would\nknow aboutit. Considering everything as\nii Is we are more than pleased with the\noutlook. and | am OF tile belief that before\nthe two weeks are over We will have big\ncrowd and will be able to do a splendid\nwork. Then, too. the white people seem\nlake 4 great deal Of intere st in our meet\nings, and they treat US with the greatest\nkindness. They come in large numbers\nto all our services and listen with the most\nrespectful attention. They know that we\nare in earnest, and give US credit for our\nefforts\n\n\nThen as the crowd slowly dispersed the\nboy with buZZ-saw hat took his mothers\nhand and said Il asked my mamma for\nIs cents\" and as he disappeared over the\nELL hi, mother looked at him with a\ngrieved expression of face in which there\nwas just an Intimaton OF threat.\n\n=== 18_1932-04-14_p8_sn82014085_00414183499_1932041401_0661.json\nThe Waterbury Democrat. · 1932-04-14\ncrops: cached\nheadline: BRISTOL.\n\nHigh School Nine\n\n To Meet Clubsters\n\nCross Bats Saturday Afternoon at Muzzy Field\n\n Forty Hours Devotion at St Matthew's\n---\nMir and Mrs A. Recchio of 105 Jacob street have announced the marriage of their daughter, Sarah Dolores to Arthur Martin, son of Mir and Mrs A. Martin of Tulip street. The marriage took pace in :t Anthony's church April 9 with Rev Louis Becarris, pastor of the church officiating.\n\n Nionahanmcn Meet Ciubsters\n\n The Bristol high school baseball charges of Coach Tommy Mona ian will face the Bristol Boys dub nine Saturday afternoon at .tuzzy field. The maroo'i and white ossers will present theil strongest ..ncup in quest of a victory over heir arch rivals.\n\n Coach Monahan will start Albie .;urske upon the mound in the ,,l st game with Andy Palau on the receiving end of the losses. The in- pe.d which includes three sopho- lrcrc; will find Walt Sonstroem at tne initial sack, Pete Nodarski at TiidNay. Wal,y Utke digging 'em f, at short and Hennie kwiatkow- sst. at third base. Joe Marc. Cap n Roy Cur.iss. Mix. Pavelchak, M pari,y\" Di Giovanni and Jackie .ys,fgrove will make the outfeld.\n\n sings Over WTIC\n\n Mi..s Winifred Barnfcld of this <. ty. well Known cont,.alto soloist .feadt asted spring time pro- E Iran, over Station WTIC at Hart ef ferd | his morning at 10:15 o'clock. Miss Barnfield was assisetd at the pi;tno by Miss Gladys Hodgkinson, pianist.\n\n Card Party\n\n The Parent Teachers assocIa- tty1ns of the. Park street and South sss de schools will hold card party sffiis evening at the Park street .ci.oo, auditoriunl. Whist, auction s {, d centi.cct bridge will be in play. Hiss Mary Comerford. teacher at she South $ de school is in charge ul the ticket arrongements.\n\n Recent Bir.hs\n\n % The following births have been E corG ed at the office Of City Clerk -,'hon,as E. Steele. recert,y.\n\n A son. Frank Clifton April 4, lu Mir and Mrs Francis Clifford ihurber of II Summer street.\n\n A san, Edward Lcwellyn. Jr. 10 MT and Mrs Edgar Lcwellyn at the s>hcron hospital.\n\n A Q1uSH lAdDctIl IhAi iS, April 6. to MT ard Mrs John Dolan uf 29 Gridley street.\n\n No Baseball\n\n { Unless amateur teams desiring tto use the Greene-Hill school grounds within the future are without a permit from Principal Harry D. Williamson, they will . probably be brought into the juve- rifle court and will be charged with t.espass:ng on private property. it vas learned by the principal of the torestville school to-day.\n\n Cards Face PhaEtOms\n\n The Cardinal baseball team of ] il'orestville will face the Phantoms of Bristol a. the Greene-Hill school grounds Sunday afternoon at 3 O. Clock. Francis Hop\" Murphy and i. Martin Stenstrom will form the 'tattcry for the Red Birds. while Tony Zagryn will be assigned to toss 'em up for the Bristolites with benson on the receiving end of the slitches. It will mark the second game for the Forestviile contin-\n\n=== 16_1900-11-16_pna_sn89058013_00296020333_1900111601_0374.json\nThe Camden chronicle. (Camden, Tenn.) 1890-current · 1900-11-16\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nMaurice Thompson says that the\nword \"HOosler\" comes from who's\nyer| and that that was the inquiry\nOf cabin dwellers in Indiana, put to\nthose who knocked at their doors,\no'nights, in times of danger This is\njust as correct as the statement that\nSaratoga is composed Of the word Sara\nand of the Latin word toga which\nmeans n cloak. Both theories Of derlv-\nalton are phonetically fine, lingually\ngrammatical, audibly impressive, typo\ngraphically taking and amusingly apo\ncryphal.\n\n=== 33_1906-09-12_p1_sn86058242_00271745734_1906091201_0480.json\nThe Lake County times. [volume] (Hammond, Ind.) 1906-1933 · 1906-09-12\ncrops: cached\nheadline: TRIPS TO GHOSTLAND\n\nHAUNTED HOUSES INVESTIGATED BY\n\n\nLOMBROSO.\n---\n\n\n\n10 BE Beyond Solution by Any Of\nthe known Laws OF Physical Sci\neace-A PusxIed Physicist.\n\n\nProfessor Lombroso, the italian phys\n.cist contributed to the Annales des\nSciences Psyehiques an account OF two\npsychic phenomena which he investi-\ngated purely in the interests of mate\nrial science. The article is entitled\nHaunted Houses Which | Have Stud\nled\" and the professor confessed that\nbe set about his investigations with\nnothing to be desired in the complete\nness of his skepticism.\n\n\nThe phenomena in question, how\never, proved to be beyond solution by\nany known laws of physical science.\nFrankly, the great criminologist adj\nmitted that for the first time in his\nlife he was in the presence of the in\ntangible and that he was worsted in\nhis attempt to grapple with the unsub-\nstantial\n\n\nII was asked in November, 1900, to\ninvestigate some strange occurrences\nwhich had taken place at the house Of\nSignor Pavarino by way Of Pescatori,\nTurin. One phenomenon was asked\nto explain was the mysterious over\nturning of cups and jugs, which were\nprecipitated to the ground by some\nunseen agency.\n\n\n''These occurrences were accompa-\nMED by loud knockings on the walls\naud the pulling of bells in various\nparts Of the house. The youngest\ndaughter was awakened on several oc-\ncasions by blows aimed at her by some\nunseen hand, and her body on exami-\nnation was found to be covered with\nbruises. the result of severe pinchings.\nHer sister, who slept in the same bed,\nfelt nothing and was not disturbed In\nany way.\n\n\n1.-THE clash of rapiers was heard in\nthe house at another time, accompa-\nMED by cries of anger and groans of\nanguish. Lights were also seen pass\ning to and fro in unlighted rooms. An\nAlpine hat was continually moving\nabout the room, and even when it was\n4 deliberately nailed to g chair an invis-\n| ible hand precipitated it to the ground.\n| All these mysterious happenings went\non for eight months, and in regard to\n/ them l took the evidence of reliable\npersons, who testified that they had\nbeen eyewitnesses of the phenomena.\n! Even when the bell ropes were removed\nfrom the bells the tinkling continued\nat all hours OF the day and night.\n\n\n1.-THE scene changed to a house In the\nnext street, by way of Bava, adjoining\nby way of Pescatori, where the news\npapers of Turin began to chronicle mys-\nFERROUS occurrences of almost a similar\nnature to these which occurred in by\nway of Pescatori, cups and saucers,\npans and kettles being juggled about\nby an unseen agency and causing at\nthe time a most Internal row.\n\n\nTN particular, one circumstance was\nmost noteworthy. In the cellar filed\nwith full and empty bottles, much loss\nto the proprietor was caused by the\nsmashing of the TASKS by some unseen\nhand, but with every trace of method\nand willfulness in the way in which the\nbreakage occurred. On my entrance\ninto the cellar l lighted five candles\nthinking that a supernatural spirit\nwould avoid the light. On the con\ntrary, l saw three empty bottles roll\nabout as If pushed by a finger and\nbreak close by me. l made every pos\nsible investigation to discover bidder\ntrick, but to no avail. Several bottles\nwere taken up in the air as if lifted by\nthe hand and dashed to the ground.\n\n\nTHE wife of the proprietor left abou\nthat time for visit to her father\nWhile she was away the occurrences\nceased, only to break out again imme\ndiately on her return by way of Bava\nThe waiter of the house was then re\nmoved, and the occurrences ceased al\ntogether, leaving it to be supposed\nsince no other hypothesis is probable\nthat he was the medium through which\n: the phenomena were rendered possible\n\n\nI'm May, 1903, / was asked TO inves\ntigate another mysterious affair which\nhappened in the family of a printe\nnamed Mignotti, also in Turin. 1t wa\nlittle boy, went TO bed at night louo\nknoekings began to be heard on thu\nwall next to which he lay. 4 doctor\nthe police and myself were called in\nWe examined the walls and the room\nwith great care.\n\n\nGAS soon as the boy was placed Il\nbis bed the knocking began. 1t wa\nevidently conducted In an inteiligen\nmanner, and it was possible to estah\nlish some sort of communication. l\nwas only, however, when the boy la\nawake chat the phenomenon occurred\nAS soon as he went TO sleep the knock\ning became very vague and indistinci\nHe was clearly the immediate cause\nthough beyond high fever, when tn\nphenomenon was most frequent, b\nwas in other respects a normal child\n! ! state these facts clearly and absC\nlutely as / witnessed them and comics\nmy total inability to explain them\nPublic Opinion.\n\n=== 8_1932-10-28_p17_sn82014085_00393347715_1932102801_0807.json\nThe Waterbury Democrat. · 1932-10-28\ncrops: None\nheadline: you go to market.\n\n HOb-Goblin Gingerbread\n---\nyou go to market.\n\n Hob- Goblin Gingerbread\n\n Make gingerbread with your usual recipe, and bake in a sheet. While it is baking, make your hob goblin filling as follows: Mash 2 packages cream cheese G oz) and to it add package pasteurized dates (sliced), cup chopped nut meats and 1-2 teaspoon salt. Add enough cream so mixture will spread. When gingerbread is done. split, spread with this filling and replace top layer. Serve warm. Delicious! If you prefer, add the 1-2 package of, dates right to your gingerbread batter and bake as usual. Then frost with orange frosting.,\n\n Witches Brew\n\n 1 can grapefruit\n\n=== 4_1906-11-09_p1_sn83045366_00206538442_1906110901_0197.json\nThe Minneapolis journal. [volume] (Minneapolis, Minn.) 1888-1939 · 1906-11-09\ncrops: None\nheadline: Problem of the Phantom Auto,\n---\nJacques Futrelle, and. that wonder\nfull Thinking Machine in which he con.\n6des, relates in the Sunday Magazine\nOf next Sundays Journal the \"Prob-\nlem Of the Phantom Auto.' Yarbor-\nough was a wide 'expanse Of country\nestates and superbly kept roads. The\nTrap was singularly tempting, a per-\nfectly maca damized road lying betw cen\nthe two tall walls, with only enough\nOf a sinuous twist in it to make each\nend invisible from the center. The\nTrap was an ideal spot for curbing the\nambitions of Marlborough residents for\neclipsing all speed records in autos.\nWith man at each end of the Trap,\nwith telephone communication between,\nthe Marlborough constables had reason\nto Congratulate themselves, But the\nPhantom Auto changed all this. One\nnight it came into the Trap at sixty\nmi les an hour. The man at the other\nend waited twenty minutes and failed\nto. see it. The next night the same\nthing. happened. The problem wassstill\nunsolved when Hutchinson Hatch, the\nreporter, laid it before Professor Van\nDusen, the Thinking Machine. His\nlogic easily solved the problem that\nseemed impossible to detectives: It\nwas all so simple thai. it set everybody\na-laughing when the phantom auto was\nno longer mystery ,r =\n\n=== 10_1921-05-24_p16_sn85038485_00340583036_1921052401_0507.json\nThe Birmingham age-herald. [volume] (Birmingham, Ala.) 1902-1950 · 1921-05-24\ncrops: None\nheadline: THE BIRMINGHAM AGEHERALD TUESDAY, MAY a. 1921\n---\nrare pianist who makes them laugh and\nlisten. His partner plays well on the\nxyliphone. An enthusiastic applause re-\nwarded the act.\n\n\nWith piano jazz and fun over the piano,\nfunny remarks. songs and Jig, the\nWidowed Pair act went over Dig, and\nsecond only to the headliner. Clayton is\ncomedian with the droll appeal, his\npartner is pretty and attractive and jgs\ngracefully.\n\n\nThe mysticism Of seance is used for\nartistic purposes by Edna Dreon in her\n''Vaudevill Seance\" She recalls the girl\nof the past, brings down girl from\nMars and asks the ouija questions in a\nouija song that took well with the\naudience.\n\n\nTwo other acts completed the pro-\ngramme. One is a target shooting act\nwith pretty settings, and some daring\nWorkmanship. The other and closing\nact is two modern gynnasts. They are\nfine and attractive personally, giving\none of the best acts Of the kind in\nlong time.\n\n=== 5_1920-11-12_p2_sn85033000_0041421213A_1920111201_0919.json\nThe log cabin Democrat. · 1920-11-12\ncrops: None\nheadline: OUIJA BOARD MEDIUM\n\n OF LOW BROWS' ONLY\n---\nChicago, Nov. Elf you would mingle with the elite of departed spirits, do not toy with the shades who use the ouija boards as means of communication, because, under ordi- nary circumstances, only the riffraff of spiritdom speak during sessions of the great American parlor game was the warning given today by Miss Gail Wilson, trustee of the American sec tion of the theosophical society. She stands high in the councils of the theosophical world\n\n Only the low type of soul would make itself manifest on the ouiia board said Miss Wilson, who has given the subject much research. \"A high type of spirit would not use such a crude and dangerous method Why should highly evolved spirit want to mingle among earth beings when they are on a much higher plane\n\n Speaking n4the slangy languages of the earth, most of the spirits we get connected with on the ouija lie like circus posters.\n\n I'll you are looking for advice, it is far better to take it from a good living person than a bad dead one No spirit can tell the future better than an ordinary earth-being. It's rare that a respectable spirit raps on the library table, either\"\n\n Miss Wilson holds that there are just few cases on record of high. class spirits talking via the ouiia board route, but these cases are so rare that they may be discounted.\n\n=== 15_1898-06-12_p1_sn83030214_00175036866_1898061201_0236.json\nNew-York tribune. [volume] (New York [N.Y.]) 1866-1924 · 1898-06-12\ncrops: None\nheadline: CONGRESS SPRINGS.\n\nTHE VIRTUES OF THE WATER BOTTLED FOR\n\n\nEXPORT.\n---\n\n\n\nEXPORT\n\n\nCongress Syring water has PROBABLY done more\n10 make Saratoga famous than any other ming\nThe people who came in such numbers that III\"\nsmall ,averns and hotels had 10 make way for the\nnew large houses would probably have gone else\nwhere had they not known of the Congress water.\nThrough all the changes that have taken place In\nSaratoga since IL became n summer health resort\nCongress water has maintained its place a. the\nfirst attraction. and has gone 10 all parts OF me\nworld in bottles for the Of those who cannot\ncome 10 the springs\n\n\nThe spring was discovered in 1872 Its owners\n\n\n\nThis famous water being now (by most elaoo-\nrate retubng) restored to all It's former strength\nand excellence. the great mneral-water drinking\npublic might have been sees the last season a,\nSaratoga hurrying to Stake their thirst AZ 'h's\nhealthful fountain\n\n\n\"While the water is now as strongly caiharic as\nat any period since It's dscovery-over one hundred\nwears ago-it still retains the delicious flavor and\nsmooth cathartic action that has always been\ncharacteristic of this famous water.\n\n\nby its efficacy purity and acknowledged sani-\ntary properties. and the happy proportions OF It's\nvarious alkaline salts. the Congress water stands\nunrivalled by all. either foreign or domestic.\n\n\nGuests at Saratoga usually make their hrst WISH\nto the Congress Park where the Congress water\npushes forth and Is handed. sparkling ana ener\nTescent 10 the waiting crowds. There was time\nwhen one had 10 go TO the little springhouse In\nSaratoga 70 obtain the water Bottling it. that\nlis virtues could be preserved and exerted away\nfrom the fountain. where II brok\" tIIou' lne\nrock was an unknown art. But the denanD rom\ndistant places soon became so great That orange\nments had 10 