th the food and the albuininods. and Is\nthe medium through which nature holds in solution\nthe lime separated from the blood, to strengthen\nthe bones Of the body.\n\n=== 30_1888-01-14_pna_sn83016025_00414210582_1888011401_0216.json\nThe Portland daily press. [volume] (Portland, Me.) 1862-1921 · 1888-01-14\ncrops: None\nheadline: The Haunted wyona.\n---\nCapt. Albert H. Ray of North 0rrington,\nhas sold his splendid coasting vessel, the\nWyona, to Captain Nathaniel S. Paine for\nrather a low price considering her excellence,\nbut the low figure is probably owing to her\nbeing haunted, as Capt. Ray at is said, firm\nlv belieyes.\n\n=== 18_1891-06-26_pna_sn85025407_00279522126_1891062601_0289.json\nMaryland independent. [volume] (Port Tobacco, Md.) 1874-current · 1891-06-26\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\n1 ~NAAN+t'9 ttAAh AAlIb \"a EEtAtts\nsweet, old face smiled sadly at cher\nboy\" lie remembered her dreams\nTor him; he realized for the first time\nhow far lie had fallen from her ideals.\n\n\nNow he fully understood what She\nlad once said 10 him: \"Expcricncc is\nsorrow. Oaly is life happy as we\nlive it for others\"\n\n\nThe face was a mans now; hand\nsome still, but an eager look had come\nINTO the eyes, and the mouth was\nmore firmly set. lie was starting out\nINTO tho wOrl'l anxious for knowledge\nOF 1t; determined TO fight and con-\nqucr Clearly the night he left home\ncame back TO him. She of tho laugh\ning eyes was with him, but they\nlaughed 10 longer. Tears were brim.\nming over and the little lips too trem-\nuIous for words. lie held her hand\nand mighty struggle went on within\nhim. lie knew what the tears meant\nand lc longcd to take her in his arms;\nbut pride and caution whispered:\n\n\n\"Wait! You lave the world before\nyou\n\n\nAud SO he spoke no word, but left\nher.\n\n\nlie had never really meant to give\nher up. He iutcHdcd in the near\nfuture to 90 back for her; but, first,\nhe was too poor to take the time, and\naficrward the business world claimed\nhim as its slavc-its slave when most\nhe felt himsclfit lis master.\n\n\nSO the days passed by and lie never\nwent back.\n\n\nThen he heard that another had\nwon her, and for moment j. Wee,\ndon bradshaw admitted to himself\nthat he hal possibly made mistake\nIN life after all.\n\n\nIn course of time hc married his\npartners daughter. lie paid her bills,\ntreated her with, respectful considcra-\nton, and when sic died regretted the\nunfortunate circumstance. But her\nface had no place on the window. The\nyouth of the past was crying to the\nman of to-day for rcparation and\nwould not be silcnced.\n\n\n''Why did you treat ms SOP he\ncried. \"Why did you stno my love,\nteaching me this terrible absolute ill.,\ndifference TO everything good or bad?\nWhy did you train me to think that\nmoney and what money could buy\nwas the best in life and nothing else\ni1attcrcd? What have you given me in\nfcturn for youth, love, aud libcrty?\"\n\n\nAnd Weldon Bradshaw bowed his\nhead in silence. Dead Sea fruit.\n\n\nClearly he saw now, as in the light\nof noonuay, the life ho had missed.\nThe life of love and higher aspiration,\nthe abncgation of self that leads 10\nthe \"largcr heart, the kindlicr hand\"\n\n\nAll this he saw, and groancd in\nspirit.\n\n\nOn through the night rushed the\nexpress; but side by side with it kept\nthe phantom train filed with the\nghosts of Weldon Bradshaw's past.\n\n\nThe Young bride had gone TO sleep\nwith her head on her husbands shoul-\nder and the other passengers were in\nvarious stages of unconsc:ousucss.\n\n\nSuddenly violent, shuddering jerk\nthroughout the train-a mighty crash\nand hcaving, and then silence more\nterrible. ;, My.r ~....-.--..-.~-~-...>\n\n\nSilence f5r moments space only,\nhowever; thon cries, questions, excla-\nnations wild confusion of tongues.\n\n\nThe engine had jumped the track on\na down grade and half dragged the\nbaggage car with it; but the passen-\nger coaches were only badly shaken.\n1t would have been terrible disaster\nbut for the qu!ckncss a\"d nerve of the\nenginc%--hc stopped the train in its\nown length, but bis life was the price.\n\n\nThe crowd grew silent as they stood\nabout the wreck and that motionless\nobject now stretched upon the road\nside.\n\n\nThey pecrcd into each others pale\nfaces, scarcely visible by the flickering\nlight of a few lanterns carried in\nnervous hands.\n\n\nThank God! they were all safe\nbut one man had given ur his life for\nthem.\n\n\n''lIe did his duty nobly,\" they said,\nand then began TO think how they\ncould continue their journey.\n\n\n1t was no; that they were unfeelng\nonly \"practicap; there was moth\ning to be done and they were in a\nhurry.\n\n\nOnly Bradshaw remained standing\nby the body-he felt shaken, un-\nncrvcd, strangely Old. Those silent\nlips seemed bidding him stay. A\nv6feo was speaking 10 him ihro0gls\nthem UNDECIDED by other cars Even\nas am wilt thou be. ~ Frank Les\nlies Illustrated.\n\n\nThe Human Figure.\n\n\nThe proportions of the human Go-\n\n=== 11_1891-06-28_p16_sn90059522_00175033579_1891062801_0574.json\nSt. Paul daily globe. · 1891-06-28\ncrops: None\nheadline: PERSONALS.\n\nATTENTION. ATTENTION.\nKNOWLEDGE IS POWER.\nFIRST TIME IN ST. PAUL\n\n\nOF MRS. DIR. DALE,\n\n\nTRANCE CLAIRVOYANT\n\nAND SPIRIT MEDIUM.\n---\n\n\n\nBorn with a double veil and wonderful\nprophetic gift of second sight; has the power\nof any two mediums you ever met; this she\nis prepared to prove. Tells your entire life,\npast, present and future, in dead trance;\nevery hidden mystery revealed. Tells the\nfull name of future husband or wife, with\nage and date of marriage, and tells whether\nthe one you love is true or false tells all\nyour business affairs with utmost truth, gives\nadvice on divorce. contested wills specula-\ntons, etc., tells your life from the cradle to\nthe grave; positively no imposition; reveals\neverything; understands the science of the\nPersian and Hindoo magic\" or ancient\ncharming, andprepares Egyptian talismans,\nwhich will overcome your enemies, remove\nfamily troubles, restore lost affections, make\nmarriage with the one you love, no failure,\nremove evil influences. bad habits. Cures\nwitchery, rheumatism. all female complaints,\ndrunkenness. opium and morphine habits,\nfits and all loDg-standing and mysterious\ndiseases.\n\n\nBusiness strictly private and confidential.\n\n\nCONSULTATION FREEt\n\n\nSend stamp for circular.\n\n\nCut this out and call early, for this adver-\ntisment will not appear every day.\n\n\nOffice hours, 9 a. m. to p. m. daily. Ad-\ndress,\n\n=== 3_1925-09-10_pna_sn85040310_0051415885A_1925091001_0171.json\nThe Telegraph-courier. [volume] (Kenosha, Wis.) 1888-1946 · 1925-09-10\ncrops: None\nheadline: Wins SANDER Case\n\n\nBy Proving Illusion\n---\nBerlin. - Professor Albert\nMoll, one of 'Berlin's foremost psyche\nphysicists, was victorious recently in\nSANDER suit brought against him\nby Frau Rudolf, spiritualistic medi-\nUNI, for criticizing one of her per-\nformances. He had said that one could\nspeak of a spiritualistic phenomenon\nonly then, if every step of the\n\"seance\" could be carefully watched\nand controlled by some licensed state\ninstitution, such as the Techno-Physi-\ncal institute.\n\n\nAS the trial progressed Professor\nMoll proposed that a seance be held\nthen and there in the courtroom, and\nthat Frau Rudolf be requested to\nshow her tricks\" She strongly OBJ\njected, however stating that the at\nmosphere of a courtroom was not con-\nducive to summoning spiritual phe-\nnomena. Professor Moll then played\nthe part of the spiritualist without\ndarkening the courtroom and request\ned the judge to be the medium\" The\nlatter's eyes were covered and the\nprofessor placed both points of a\ndrawing compass upon his hand, then\nremoved one point. The judge failed\nto notice this, and maintained both\npoints were still resting on his hand.\nSeveral other experiments of a SINH\nlar nature were made by DR.. Moll to\nprove that many things that to the\noccultist may seem like something\nsupernatural have quite rational ex-\nPLANTATION\n\n\nThe court found DR.. Moll not guilty.\n\n=== 47_1880-05-08_pna_sn86088181_0020029693A_1880050801_0845.json\nSan Marcos free press. [volume] (San Marcos, Tex.) 1877-1892 · 1880-05-08\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nTHE following books, lately ordered\nare now ready: My Mother and l\nThe Phantom Ship; Memoirs Of\nPhysicisna Mysteries of Paris; a\nCaptain at Fifteen.\n\n\nJE@7. G. R. JULIAN.\n\n=== 34_1914-01-19_pna_sn86063952_00295864163_1914011901_0109.json\nThe Guthrie daily leader. [volume] (Guthrie, Okla.) 1893-1996 · 1914-01-19\ncrops: None\nheadline: |SOONERs\nNAME CLARK\n\n\nCAPTAIN\nSTAR TACKLE WINS SPIRIT\nED RACE FOR 1914\n\n\nLEADER\n---\nNorman, Okla, Jan. l9.-The task\nIof leading Oklahoma through most\nlstrenucus schedule of gridiron games\nnext fall must be carried by billie\nClark, tackle, for two years a regular\nafsity player The alection which\ndid not come until the football sea-\nson was many weeks past was the\n|cimax to hotly contested race be-\ntween Clark and Trim Capshaw. The\nnew captain received II votes to four\nfor Capshaw.\n\n\nThe race was somewhat uniquei\nsince both Clark and Capshaw were\nthe yognger brothers Of Sooner mel\nwho had previously piloted the des-\ntinies of the sooners. Fred Capshaw\ncaptaned Oklahoma in 1911 when\nCoach Ben Owen turned out his first\nall-vctorions team. Glenn Clark fol-\nlowed as The 1912 leader, The two\nYounger brothers, as well as Fred and\nGlenn, are stars Of the first rank.\nTrim Capshaws work at halfback\nwas a sensation throughout the sea\nson Just closed\n\n=== 3_1856-06-02_pna_sn82015672_0029602188A_1856060201_0542.json\nThe Evansville daily journal. [volume] (Evansville, Ia. [i.e. Ind.]) 1848-1862 · 1856-06-02\ncrops: None\nheadline: LOCAL DEPARTMENT.\n\nUP TOWN ITEMS.\n---\nBusiness HoURs-CLERKs.-In connection with the ar\ntcle on the Library and Reading Rooms, published a\nday 