cognizant\n| in he min\nrves the mechanic has thrown aside his TOOLS\nhas 'knOched of\" two hours ago and called it a day\nthe hoarse DreathiDgs of the engine have been\nsilenced metioness stands the OF machinery in\nthe factory. The brute slave has almost forgotten\nabors the day. and half dreaming under recuper\natng, influences of well supplied epgastrium.\nvery little whether he will have hay.\nbreakfast in the morning, places of amusement, and\nReading Rooms well filed, and floods of living\nlight stream from hundred fame crested burners as\nif to douby intensity if possible the delights the\nsocial reunion, wherever there is ocus of pleasure\nand friendship Still at the stake stands the patient\nheretic still at the tugs the galley slave. What mat\nters it, he's but salesmara clerk; he has need of\nmental culture--no need of exercise he's hired to be\nmechine, at starving salary and what right has he\ncomplain if he's made machine Of\n\n\nWE'LL at last its O'Clock and the retiring world\nwith afectionate good night perhaps kiss so all\n/\nthere may yet be single tardy purchaser and poor\n\n\nittiebaI expected to keep OPEN until expectation\nAt last he closes, aud the prisoner is fee-not\nnot yeti pass along that way about hour hence the\ndoors closed and shutter up, but there's gimmer\nof light under the door, along the SILL through key\nhole, where there's or granny he is do\n\n\ning his last duty arranging the shelves for the DAY\na.or and when spirits of the night are revealing\nin their orgies, when silent Keeping their\nalmost midnight vigs, he may sally forth replensh\nhis family burniu damp with oil. whether there\nreally is thing in life worth living OF course at\nthat hour he finds only the Animal d either be\ncomes animal himself returns sorrows gto his lone\n~ CELL satisfied that it is well to be machine\n\n\nbrute Now for eep, its past midnight turn in; what\nwithout reading page? Certainly, the body must\nbe supported; if the mind does become imbecice and\nbesdes he's only clerk salesman Bnt for\ngot that we writing only newspaper column,\nin which prolixity neglect if exclusion. bid\nspace and your patience permit, would like follow\nthis class of young men, through a sabbath and show\nthe necessary result of their weekly routine and show\nwhere lies the possibility Of nine tenths of sabbath\nDissertation.\n\n\n4 In other cities Employers have the evil alluded\nabove, and recognized the necessity granting\nemployees respite from labor sufficient for all phys\n\n\ncal and intellectual demands; and their right re\n\n\nSpite and they have with onesaccord discontinued nigh\nbusiness after early hour. The young men OF Ev-\nansvlle much entitled that respite TO the\nbenefits of Reading Rooms and social circles without\ninfringing the hours needed for repose ae the\nyoung of any other city where this RI hit las been\nconceded and we hope they will wi demand\na movement among sorekeepers in their fay, r, al\nthen hope that merchants will follow the exam\npie of other cities, and grant their request\n\n=== 22_1914-08-24_pna_sn88085187_00211108381_1914082401_0591.json\nThe Tacoma times. [volume] (Tacoma, Wash.) 1903-1949 · 1914-08-24\ncrops: None\nheadline: The Parental School\n---\nAn inspection of the Parental School, supervised\nby the local school board, forcefuly impresses the\nvisitor that the 20-odd lads confined therein are\nas matter of fact, enjoying vastly better home in\nfluenee than perhaps one-half the boys who live\nwith their parents Mr.. and Mrs. John c. Greene,\nIn charge of the school, young folk. Greene who\nis head -master, farm boss, companion and robust\nvithal is young enough to have the boys viewpoint.\nPersonally If boy again, we would be\nstrongly tempted to hook jack and thrust out an i'm\npudent tongue at the truant officer and devise other\ndevilish means, to enjoy few months at the Parent\nal school, it is conducted in Tacoma. Once boy\nhas been judge \"ineorrigble,' the best way, sane\nauthorities agree, to improve him is to strengthen\nhis spirit, not to break it, 'tIncorrigbty' in boys\nIs another and more awsesome term for BOYS who\nnot milksops. We hazard the guess that Huclxleber\nry Finn, and Teddy Roosevelt and Henry Ford and\nsuch, were \"incorrigibles' in their day.\n\n\n\n\n\nThe direct primary is great advertising medium\nSome of these county candidates may not get any\nvotes, except from their family, but they had\ntheir pictures every fence and tree in the city for\nweeks.\n\n\nTacomas police force is getting desperate 1t be-\ngan Saturday night to arrest motorcycles and auto\nmobiles. Wonder why we don't hear of 'em pinch\ning some of the dives that Mavor Faweett has ex-\nposed and reported to Mills and Loomisg\n\n\nChief of Police Ardella p LOOMS openly defies\nthe mayor to prove charges of graft But he ceverlv\nmentions in his interview that if there is graft in\nthe police department he doesn't know anything\nshout it. The coppers ought to feel gratfied toward\ntheir boss for the inuendo supplied in the statement\n\n\nCongressman johnson doesn't feel that he can\nleave Washington to do a little fenee-budng IN his\ndistrict All of which goes to show that johnson has\nmore sense than believed he had The money\nthat he would spend on the trip and campaign will\ncome IN handy when be retires from congress next\nspring\n\n\narmy EngIand's to get $4,000 dozen American eggs\nat pc the Dozen\n\n=== 5_1920-02-11_p1_sn87057262_00295875276_1920021101_0053.json\nThe daily Gate City and constitution-Democrat. (Keokuk, Iowa) 1916-1922 · 1920-02-11\ncrops: None\nheadline: CHALLENGE To\nSPIRIT MEDIUM\nIf Mrs. Curran Can Prove Her\nPowers, She Will be Given\n\n\nthe Reward Of\n\n\n$4,000.\n\nOPERATES OUIJA BOARD\n\nTest Proposed In Effort to Receive\nActual Message from Person\n\n\nIn tho Next\n\n\nWorld.\n---\nUNITED Press Leased Wire Service ]\n\n\nNEW YORK, Feb. 11.-Joseph F.\nRim today explained on what condi-\ntons Mrs. John H Curren Of St Louis,\nmedium for Patience Worth\" can win\nthe $1,000 he has offered for proof OF\nspiritual communication.\n\n\n\"The conditions l will make In or\nder to be convinced. are these:\n\n\nArrangement of letters on Mrs.\nCurran's ouija board must be chang\ned. 4-, a\n\n\n\"The board must be covered with\npaper, upon which a number will be\nplaced corresponding to the letter be-\nmeath. ~ ....\n\n\nTHE key to the board will be plac-\ned on table behind Mrs. Curran Sc\nher spirit friends can see 1t.\n\n\n\"If under these conditions. she can\nproduce understandable communica-\ntons from a person or persons in the\nnext world, will admit she has some\npsychic or telepathic power and she\nwins the $4,000 Until then, will con-\ntinue to believe the Patience Worth\npoems and writings are the result OF\nher own personality\"\n\n=== 5_1913-06-21_p1_sn85066387_00280768601_1913062101_0113.json\nThe San Francisco call. [volume] (San Francisco [Calif.]) 1895-1913 · 1913-06-21\ncrops: None\nheadline: \"sP0OK' Or PLUMBING,\n\n\nLISTENING To NOISES\n\n\nMEANS 81 EACH NIGHT\n\nPalo Alto House Shivers\nWith Nocturnal Groans\n\n\nand Chain Clankings\n\nwANTED--Watehma for ghosts; must\n\n\nbe temperate and steady; hours 8\ny. m. 10 sunrise. every third night;\n\n\n81 per night. Apply JOHN ARATA.\nHamilton avenue extension, Palo Alto.\n---\nSpecial Dispatch to The CalD\n\n\nPALO ALTO, June 2O.-Every third\nnight a ghost stalks in John Arata's\nhome here. The house wobbles and\nthen shakes nightly weird echoes Of\nclanking chains. mysterious means and\nghostIy groans strike terror in the\nArata family.\n\n\nFor two weeks Morris Quinn. heavily\narmed, had the job of watchman Four\ntimes he heard the ghost, but was un-\ndaunted, collecting his ST at sunrise\neach morning Wednesday night he\nsaw a shadowy white form OF the heavy\nlines of Charles H. Firebaugh, who\ndied in the house several years ago.\nquinn forgot all about the dollar and\nran\n\n\nMr.. Arata has been shooting at the\nghost\" for several weeks. but so far\nhas not slain it Neighbors complained\nand Mr. Arata was forced to stop shoot\ning. Yesterday the wholenArata fam\niiy-quite a flock Of children, too-vis-\ntied Constable Fred simpson and de-\nnanded that deputy watch the house\nand stay the ghost by the arm OF\nthe law.\n\n\nsimpson spent last night at the Arata\nhome. Hearing weird noises in the\nbasement. Simpson bravely descended,\nbut could not discover anything.\n\n\nConstable simpson today tried to\nascriDe the noises to the plumbing. A\nplumber viii be sent to the Arata house\nnext week to see whether he can find\nq sspool1.\"\n","numLines":1327,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1327}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ff8f9eae-bd9f-4d59-944a-ce6859614dbb","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"other"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2c-reader","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
