{"parentUuid":"39c4c8fb-8386-42ea-8c15-4814748fdb2a","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"775e54ef-6487-4f79-8598-643421b87718","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018TfAjm787VUufQNBZiBNVx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t=== 40_1879-01-04_p1_sn85042459_00280769009_1879010401_0005.json\n2\tDaily Los Angeles herald. · 1879-01-04\n3\tcrops: None\n4\theadline: Joo Jefferson.\n5\t---\n6\tOne wonders that the man is not\n7\tentirely subservient 20 the actor,\n8\twrites a correspondent about Jeffer-\n9\tson. Indeed, in private life, the\n10\tsame tones, and even at times the\n11\tbroken accent, are noticeable. That\n12\the has good reason to be attached\n13\tto his part, even \"a man of success\"\n14\twould admit, for it has brought\n15\thim, so I am told by an intimate\n16\tfriend, over $1,nOO,0O0.\n17\t\n18\t\n19\tIn private lifo he is very jolly,\n20\talways in good spirits, and in man\n21\tners, while inclining to the brusque,\n22\tthose of a gentleman. / had never\n23\tseen him off the stage until last\n24\tweek, and it must be confessed that\n25\tno woman was ever so completely\n26\tmetamorphosed by \"make up\" as\n27\tour venerable jefferson. A little\n28\tover the medium height, with q\n29\twell-knit frame and tendency to\n30\tstoutness, regular and rather small\n31\tfeatures, smooth face, and, in spite\n32\tof his fifty years, thick, dark hair\n33\twithout a thread of silver. In\n34\tdress. very plain, and wearing no\n35\tJCW anu 40 L lClll al htt pclOIt,\n36\tin voice and accent \"Rir,\" and,\n37\tthat indeed, was the only recogniz.\n38\table point.\n39\t\n40\t\n41\tWhile In Chicago he makes his\n42\thome at his fatber-in-law's, who\n43\tresides In comfortable style on\n44\tMichigan Avenue. 1t will be re-\n45\tmembered that eleven years ego\n46\tthe great actor married Miss\n47\tWarren, of Buffalo, a lady who,\n48\tthough not a member of the pro-\n49\tfession, comes from an old and\n50\thighly respectable theatrical fami.\n51\tly, and is u niece of ex-Mayor frice\n52\tof this city. A blonde, aud very\n53\t| graceful, she ia intellectual and\n54\taccomplished, and has won many\n55\tfriends through her charming\n56\tmanners and generous heart.\n57\t\n58\t\n59\tMr.. and Mrs. Jefferson have two\n60\tbright little children, q son and\n61\t\n62\t=== 12_1841-08-03_p1_sn82015015_00415661265_1841080301_0822.json\n63\tThe Madisonian. [volume] (Washington City [i.e. Washington, D.C.]) 1837-1845 · 1841-08-03\n64\tcrops: None\n65\theadline: Nem Fork Correspondence.\n66\t---\n67\tNEW DIRK, August i 1911.\n68\tThe news from Washington continues TO\n69\tgladden. the hearts Of all Wings in this region,\n70\tand those who unwaveringly resisted the over\n71\twhelming power Of misrule through the long\n72\tnight of darkness, are more and more rejolced\n73\tthat they have lived 10 see this day. The con\n74\tfidently anticipated passage Of the BANK Bill\n75\tand the REVENUE Bill, in addition TO the excel\n76\tlent measures Of National defence and financial\n77\tintegrity already enacted, have dissipated all\n78\tgloom in this quarter. Every one feels that the\n79\tcountry has now the very Congress that she has\n80\tlong needed, and that all necessary and proper\n81\tmeasures of relief TO the People will now be\n82\tperfected But fearful will be the reyulsion,\n83\tbitter the disappointment, if the BANKRUPT Bill\n84\tdoes not become law before the adjournment.\n85\tFriends at Washington would you but realize\n86\thow many crushed, despairing, helpless fellow\n87\tcreatures are pining in hopeless and useless\n88\twretchedness for the passage of this act, you\n89\tcould not, you DARE not go home without passing\n90\til! The spectres of the ruined and heart-broken\n91\tof beggared families and blasted hopes-woud\n92\tglare upon you from every glade and thicket by\n93\tthe way-side. Be entreated, friends in Con-\n94\tgress, if your understandings be not blinded TO\n95\tpolicy and your hearts dead to humanity, to\n96\tsignalize this glorious extra session by letting\n97\tthe hopelessly, uselessly oppressed GO FREE =\n98\tDo SO, and millions will rise up To call you\n99\tblessed, while you will have contributed i'm\n100\tmensely to restore prosperity and business ac\n101\tuvily to our depressed country.\n102\t\n103\t\n104\tThe Loco-foco gathering in the Park on\n105\tThursday evening was quite large one.\n106\tthink from four to six thousand persons present.\n107\tYou will have seen that the resolutions de-\n108\tnounced every prominent measure of the session\n109\tearept the Bankrupt Law, which was left en-\n110\ttirely untouched. 1t was first intended to de-\n111\tnounce the passage of any Bankrupt Bill with\n112\tout including corporations but some of the best\n113\tworking politicians of the party went to the\n114\tleaders and told them that they would not stand\n115\tt-that any measure calculated to impede the\n116\tpassage Of the Bankrupt bill would create an\n117\texplosion. SO it was given up. The direct as\n118\tsertion that the Bank Charter shall be repealed\n119\tat the first opportunity threat made under the\n120\tsanction of the best names in the party here\n121\twill show the country what it has to expect from\n122\tthe return of Loco -focoism to power.\n123\t\n124\t\n125\tOur Markets wear healthy appearance\n126\tBest Flour (Genesee) is quick at IS 81 1-2,\n127\tand the small stock on hand is held at So\n128\tGeorgetown IS rs; Ohio S. rs 81 l-2; Mi\n129\tchigan Sc 62 S. rs. North Carolina Wheat,\n130\tfirst quality, 81 23 a IS : inferior do. 81 a.\n131\tRye 81 2 a 63c; Corn roc Oats 43c Ashes\n132\tSIS so. Cotton, no change. N. c. Turpentine\n133\t81 25; Tar s. 81. Coffee has advanced la.\n134\tTea is also looking up.\n135\t\n136\t\n137\tStocks droop U. S. Bank is down TO l6 Sd.\n138\tIndiana Bonds SO ga So. New York Five\n139\tand Halls, 90 l-2; City Elves SO.\n140\t\n141\t\n142\tSterling bills 8 a 8 \"a; on Paris 5.25 $50.\n143\t\n144\t\n145\t\"A terrible murder has been committed at Ho-\n146\tboken. On Sunday morning last, Mary Cece\n147\tliza Rogers, young woman who formerly\n148\ttended Anderson's tobacco store, and was thence\n149\tstyled the beautiful Cigar Girl left her mo-\n150\ttheirs house, 1876 Nassau street, and was joined\n151\tat the corner of Theatre Alley by young man.\n152\twith whom she proceeded to Hoboken. This\n153\twas the last that her friends have seen of her in\n154\tlife. She did not return at night; her anxious\n155\trelatives advertised for her on Tuesday, and on\n156\tWednesday her body was found in the Hudson,\n157\toff Hoboken, bearing marks of horrible outrage\n158\tand violent death cannot learn that any trace\n159\tof her murderers has yet been discovered. |\n160\tam assured that the girls character was ire\n161\tproachablc\n162\t\n163\t\n164\tWe had cold storm from Friday evening 9\n165\tO'Clock till this morning The weather is still\n166\tcool.\n167\t\n168\t\n169\tYours, HAROLD\n170\t\n171\t=== 6_1920-04-02_p1_sn84024808_00212477497_1920040201_0122.json\n172\tGreat Falls daily tribune. · 1920-04-02\n173\tcrops: None\n174\theadline: Ouija Board for Him if\n175\t\n176\t\n177\tHoover and Barnes Ever\n178\t\n179\t\n180\tAgain Boss Grain Prices\n181\t---\n182\t\"But you did rely on Hoover's and\n183\tBarnes statements?\" asked Represen-\n184\ttative Mclaughlin. Republican, Nebraska.\n185\t\n186\t\n187\tI've took them to be men CT their\n188\tword\" replied Clements.\n189\t\n190\t\n191\tLee G. Metcaff. representing the Hi\n192\tnois Grain Dealers association. said if\n193\tthere was ever another gain corporation\n194\twhen he was buying grain he would get\n195\tan ouija board\"\n196\t\n197\t\n198\tr. T. Smiley, representing Kansas and\n199\tNebraska dealers, declared Mr.. Hoover's\n200\tannouncement in August, 1917, of ne\n201\tcessity to reduce the price of bread\n202\tgrains was Infamous\n203\t\n204\t=== 18_1939-07-11_pna_sn88085187_00200291633_1939071101_0136.json\n205\tThe Tacoma times. [vo