al trees came down with the slide.\n1296\t\n1297\t\n1298\tThe first melt was run out at the Lantern\n1299\tGlobe Company's works yesterday. One pot\n1300\twas broken during the week, but the start, every\n1301\tthing considered, was q succeEs. Next week with\n1302\tno bad luck. everything will be on In full.\n1303\t\n1304\t\n1305\tThe candidates are husIling. trading on other\n1306\tcandidates, and the ticket on Monday will be the\n1307\tworst scratched ticket ever voted here. Under\n1308\thand work is claimed 10 have been done, and\n1309\twork at the pools on Monday will be very excit.\n1310\tIng.\n1311\t\n1312\t=== 5_1889-10-11_pna_sn90059522_00175033439_1889101101_0381.json\n1313\tSt. Paul daily globe. [volume] (Saint Paul, Minn.) 1884-1896 · 1889-10-11\n1314\tcrops: None\n1315\theadline: THE OPERA HOUSE DEAL.\n1316\t\n1317\tAn Old Ghost of Uncertainty\n1318\tWhich Disturbs the Sleep of\n1319\tTheateP-GOerS.\n1320\t---\n1321\tAn Old Ghost of Uncertainty\n1322\t\n1323\t\n1324\tWhich Disturbs the Sleep of\n1325\t\n1326\t\n1327\tTheater-GOers.\n1328\t\n1329\t\n1330\tRumors of \"sure things\" in the way\n1331\tof gorgeously beautiful play houses in\n1332\tst. Paul have become so frequent of\n1333\tlate that any mention of a possibly sue\n1334\tcessfuI theater combination is regarded\n1335\tby the general public as the yariest\n1336\tchestnut. And perhaps the public is\n1337\tJustified in its opinion. Certain it is\n1338\tthat the city's amusement future is\n1339\tabout as uncertain as DR.. Days lights\n1340\tin the court house tower. The day that\n1341\thas not developed a new theater-On\n1342\tpaper-has invariably been cold one.\n1343\tYesterday being fair the new\n1344\tscheme did not fail to material\n1345\tize. The Barnes-Poupeny-Litt COM\n1346\tbination were its projectors, and the\n1347\tscheme was an entirely new one, hay\n1348\ting no connection with the Panorama\n1349\tbuilding. or the dozen other schemes\n1350\tthat these gentlemen have been charged\n1351\twith. Mr.. Barnes returned from Du-\n1352\tluth yesterday morning. ana lie was\n1353\tsoon afterwards credited with having\n1354\tstated that, in connection with Aaron\n1355\tPoupeny and Manager Jacob Lift, he\n1356\thad secured tease OF property OH East\n1357\tSeventh street, between Robert and\n1358\tJackson streets. Mr.. Barnes departed\n1359\tfor Duluth again before sunset, Mr.\n1360\tPOupeny is out of the city, and MIR. Lift\n1361\tis in Milwaukee, so that the could neither\n1362\tbe affirmed or dedied. They may have\n1363\tsecured a lease or an option, but it is\n1364\tdoubted that it is in the district men-\n1365\ttoned. 1t would be impractical to re-\n1366\tmodel the Ryan annex. and the rumor\n1367\tmust look to the other side of the street\n1368\tfor support. There. either corner is\n1369\tout of the question, and the only really\n1370\tavailable property is in the center of\n1371\tthe block, owned by William Dawson.\n1372\tA part of this was very recently leased\n1373\tfor ten years. and the site seems to be\n1374\tout of the question. There may, how\n1375\tever, be more in the story than appears\n1376\ton its surface, and it is more than pos\n1377\t\n1378\t=== 61_1898-01-12_pna_sn84026409_0023728804A_1898011201_0043.json\n1379\tThe Somerset herald. [volume] (Somerset, Pa.) 1870-1936 · 1898-01-12\n1380\tcrops: None\n1381\theadline: HE LIKED TO\n1382\t\n1383\t\n1384\tCHANGE GHOSTS.\n1385\tTHIS WAS THE REASON ALFRED\n1386\tALLEE KILLED EIGHT OF\n1387\t\n1388\t\n1389\tHIS FELLOWS.\n1390\t---\n1391\this hands thrust deep into his pockets\n1392\tand a queer look on his ugly face, he\n1393\tmade answer:\n1394\t\n1395\t\n1396\tbecause l like to change ghosts\"\n1397\t\n1398\t\n1399\tHe is a ghost himself now. \\ year\n1400\tago, in Laredo, three Mexicans who\n1401\thated him found him in his shirt\n1402\tsleeves, weap0nless and intoxicated.\n1403\tThey set to work on him with knives.\n1404\tHe was incapable Of resistance, and\n1405\tsaid nothing more than:\n1406\t\n1407\t\n1408\tDon't do that You don't know\n1409\twhat you are doing. Don't do that.'\n1410\tHe had eight wounds on him when\n1411\tpicked up, and every one Of them went\n1412\tto the hollow. He was a thoroughly\n1413\tbutchered man. Each of the knife\n1414\tstabs represented q life that he had ta-\n1415\tken. Alice was an accurate shot, re-\n1416\tmarkably swift with his pistol and ore\n1417\tof the most coolly desperate men the\n1418\tRio Grande frontier has known. The\n1419\tpassion for slaughter seemed to grow\n1420\ton him, and in the last decade Of his\n1421\tlife he killed four men in ati'rays. For\n1422\teach Of these killings he stood trial in\n1423\tthe courts and was acquitted, sometimes\n1424\ton the plea Of self-defense and sOnle-\n1425\ttimes for no reason at all that any one\n1426\tcould see. Occasionally he was drunk\n1427\twhen making his record, but not al\n1428\tways. He was a mercilcss man at all\n1429\ttimes, and whisky did not make him\n1430\tany mere or any less muTderOus.\n1431\t\n1432\t\n1433\tAlfred Alice was born in Southwest\n1434\tern Texas and followed the business Of |\n1435\tall natives. That is to say, he was n\n1436\tranchman. He had only common\n1437\tschool education and early developed\n1438\ttraits of wildness. He consorted hab- |\n1439\titualIy with the rustler part Of the pop\n1440\tulatiOn and was a frequent figure at /\n1441\tdances, horse races and cock fights He /\n1442\twas desperate gambler when once en- /\n1443\tgagged, and those who dealt for him\n1444\twere careful to deal square\" More /\n1445\tthan once he was accused of standing |\n1446\tin with the many gangs of cattle thieves /\n1447\twith which the country was infested, |\n1448\tbut this vas never proved. Its certain |\n1449\tthat he was a rigidly upright man in\n1450\tbusiness and his word was as good as\n1451\this bond.\n1452\t\n1453\t\n1454\tThe first four murders with which he I\n1455\twas charged were ordinary affairs the |\n1456\tranch, the cow camp and the cattle I\n1457\ttrail The victims were Mexicans or\n1458\tcowboys of no prominence, and they /\n1459\tbrought their slayer no great amount Of I\n1460\treputation. He was known as a man /.\n1461\twho would shoot quickly and well, and /\n1462\tone upon whom it was hard to get the /\n1463\tdrop, but he was not classed with the /\n1464\tgilt-edged killers, of whom something\n1465\tlike a dozen owned the Rio GraHde !\n1466\tcountry. Alice WAS not brought into\n1467\timmediate contact with any one Of\n1468\tthem, which was perhaps good thing\n1469\tf T them, and he went along the even\n1470\ttenor Of his way, killing an obscure\n1471\tperson now and then, but exciting no\n1472\tspecial comment or remark.\n1473\t\n1474\t\n1475\tAbout this time he moved to Pearsall,\n1476\tthe county seat of Frio county, and en-\n1477\tgagged in shipping cattle. 1t was at the\n1478\theight of the \"nesterp excitement.\n1479\tPasture fences were cut nightly, cattle\n1480\twere stolen by the hundred head and\n1481\ttSe rough element was in the ascendant.\n1482\tThe Rsnehmen Of FriO county formed\n1483\tthe Cattltmen's Protective Association\n1484\tand made a business Of running down\n1485\tthe thieves. Some Of them were tried\n1486\tand sent to the peniteniiary, but good\n1487\tmany more quietly disappeared. 1t\n1488\twas given out that they had fled the\n1489\tcountry, and no one made particular\n1490\tinquiry for them. All through the\n1491\tmonths Of October and November, 1885,\n1492\thowever, it was noticed that the Mex\n1493\tlean vultures were plentiful about the\n1494\tmesquite grown pastures Of FTiO county.\n1495\tNow and then a corpse with a good\n1496\tmany bullet holes in it floated down the\n1497\tFriO river. simultaneously the cattle\n1498\tbusiness began to pay larger dividends\n1499\t\n1500\tIi was not known then, but it subse-\n1501\tquently developed that Alice was one\n1502\tOf the most trusted and effective agents\n1503\tof the Cattlemen's Protective AssOcia-\n1504\tton. His former intimacy with the\n1505\trustler clement gave him exceptional\n1506\tadvantages, and be utilized them. \\\n1507\tchoice was offered to him, and he wise\n1508\tly decided to range himself on tie de\n1509\tOf law and order.\n1510\t\n1511\t\n1512\tRe-established in the good graces Of\n1513\tthe wealthy men Of that section and\n1514\twell paid by them, his desire to kill\n1515\tsomebody not a rustler got the beltsr\n1516\tof him. The affair brought him Into\n1517\tState prominence. The victim was\n1518\tnamed Rhodes. He was shot on the\n1519\tpublic square Of Pearsall. He reached\n1520\tfor his pistol, but Alice had him cover\n1521\tcred before his fingers touched the butt\n1522\tand be went dead in his tracks.\n1523\tRhodes was well connected in the low\n1524\ter counties and his slayer was bitterly\n1525\tprosecuted. Self fdefense was the plea\n1526\tand justifiable homicide the verdict.\n1527\tThis affair cost Alice all the money he\n1528\thad made from the cattlemen and he\n1529\twent to drinking.\n1530\t\n1531\t\n1532\tBoarding an international and Great\n1533\tNorthern passenger train at COtulla,\n1534\t\n1535\t=== 19_1913-02-21_pna_sn83016589_00393340927_1913022101_0070.json\n1536\tThe Mankato free press. [volume] (Mankato, Blue Earth County, Minn.) 1879-1924 · 1913-02-21\n1537\tcrops: None\n1538\theadline: IS SPIRiT!SM\n1539\t\n1540\t\n1541\tONLY DEM0NISM?\n1542\t\n1543\tAre Mediums EaCaived as Well\n1544\t\n1545\t\n1546\tas Their Followers\n1547\t\n1548\tTO THE BIBLE?\n1549\t---\n1550\tdiums Come Not From Dead HUI\n1551\tmans, but From the Fallen Angels,\n1552\tSaws Pastor Russel-in the Bible He\n1553\tTraces Their Histcry-Satan Him\n1554\tself Poses as an Angel of Lisht-The\n1555\tBible Is the Torch Of Liberty and In\n1556\ttellsence, the Foe to Superstition,\n1557\tignorance and Demonism.\n1558\t\n1559\t=== 5_1910-04-08_p10_sn85066387_00175046586_1910040801_0175.json\n1560\tThe San Francisco call. · 1910-04-08\n1561\tcrops: None\n1562\theadline: