; the brave fellows\n651\tpaused moment. as this new and unex.\n652\tpected obstacle crossed their path, but at\n653\tthe stern forward of their leaders. thew\n654\t/ boldly plunged in, and without drum or\n655\t! bugie note to cheer their steady courage.\n656\tmoved on In dead silence straight on the\n657\tpalisades. The noise had now alarmed\n658\tthe sentinels, and the rapid discharge of\n659\ttheir muskets through the gloom was fol-\n660\tlowed by lights moving swiftly about Ola\n661\tthe ramparts, and hurried shouts of so\n662\tarms! TO Grasp and the fierce roll of\n663\tdrums, rousing up the garrison from its\n664\tdream of security. The next moment\n665\tthat dark rock was one mass Of fame, as\n666\tthe artillery and musketiy opened along\n667\tits sides, shedding Iurid light on the\n668\tcountenances of the men below and 'Ad.\n669\tunnce! AdrGncep rung in startling accents\n670\talong the ranks.\n671\t\n672\t\n673\t\"the ramparts were alive with SOL\n674\tdiers, and amid shouts and hurried words\n675\tOF command, the fiery torrents from the\n676\tsummit kept on these devoted men. The\n677\twater around them was driven into spray\n678\tby the grape shot and balls thai fell in an\n679\tincessant shower, while the Kissing, bur\n680\t\n681\t\n682\tsting shells traversing the air in every d.\n683\t\n684\t\n685\trectlon, added inconceivable terror 10 the\n686\tscene. Yet these fororn hopes toiled\n687\tvigorously on, and heaved away OF the\n688\tGd6GttaS to open gap for the column,\n689\tthat, without returning a shot, stood and\n690\tcrumbIed under the fire, waiting with\n691\tfixed bayonets to rush to the assault. At\n692\tthe head of one of these was Wayne,\n693\tchatting like lion in the tolls, at the OBJ\n694\tstacies that arrested his progress. The\n695\ttorlorn hope in front of him, worked\n696\tsteadily Oh in the very blaze of the bat\n697\tteries, and the rapid blows of th sir axes\n698\twere heard in the intervals ofthe thu der\n699\tOF artillery, that shook the midnight air,\n700\twhile one after another dropped dead in\n701\this footsteps, till out of the twenty that\n702\tstarted, only three stood up unharmed.-\n703\tYet still their axes fell steady and strong\n704\tuntil an opening was made through\n705\twhich the columns could pass, and then\n706\tthe shout of Wayne was heard above the\n707\tdin and tuInult, summoning his followers\n708\ton. With fixed bayonets they marched\n709\tsterniy through the portals made at such\n710\tnoble sacrifice and pressed furiously for\n711\tward--through the morass-over every\n712\tobstacie-up TO the very mouths of the\n713\tcannon, and up the rocky activity, they\n714\tstormed on crushing everything in their\n715\tpassage. Towering al the head of his\n716\tshattered column, pointing still onward\n717\tand upward, with his glittering blade, and\n718\tndiDg his thrilling shout back over his\n719\tfollowers, Wayne strobe steadily up the\n720\theight, till at length struck in the head by\n721\tmusket ball, he fell backward amid\n722\tthe ranks. Instantly rising on one knee\n723\the cried ost-'March on! carry me into\n724\tthe fort, for will die at the head of my\n725\tcolumn! And those heroes put their\n726\tbrave arms around him and bore him on.\n727\tNot shot was fired, but taking the rap\n728\tid volleys on their unshrinking breasts,\n729\ttheir bayonets glittering in the flash oil\n730\tthe enemy,s guns. they kept on over the\n731\tliving and the dead, shifting down the\n732\tveteran ranks that threw themselves in\n733\tvain valor before them, till they reached\n734\tthe centre ofthe fort. where they met the\n735\tother column, which, over the same OBJ.\n736\tstacles, had achieved the same triumph\n737\tAt the sight of each other, one loud shout\n738\tshook the heights and rolled down the\n739\tbleeding line-was again sent back till\n740\tthe Heavens rung with the wild huzzas,\n741\tand then the flag of freedom went up and\n742\tHaunted away on the midnight air. The\n743\tthick volumes of smoke that lay around |\n744\tthat rock, slowly lifted and rolled up the\n745\tHudson, the stars appeared once more in\n746\tthe sky, and all was over. the lordly\n747\triver went sweeping by as it had done\n748\tduring the deadly strife that cast such\n749\tbaIeful light on its bosom, and darkness,\n750\tand death. like silence. shrouded the\n751\tshores. Mournfully and slow those for\n752\tlorn- hopes and their brave companions\n753\twho had fallen in the assault, were bro't\n754\tup from their CORY beds and conveyed to\n755\tthe grave.\n756\t\n757\t\n758\t\"Wayne's wound proved not to be se-\n759\tvere-the ball only having grazed the\n760\tskull for two inches. and he lived TO wear\n761\tthe laurels grateful nation placed on his\n762\tbrow. The country rung with his name\n763\tand Congress presented him with gold\n764\tmedal. The whole plan of the assault\n765\twas most skillfully laid, and the bearing\n766\tOF Wayre, throughout was gallantin the\n767\textreme. He chose the post of danger\n768\tat the head of his column. and led his men\n769\twhere even the bravest might shrink to\n770\tfollow, and when struck and apparently\n771\tdying, heroically demanded to be carried\n772\tforward, that he might die in the arms of\n773\tvictory, or be left where the last stand\n774\twas made. His troops were worthy Of\n775\tsuch leader, and more gallant officers\n776\t\n777\t=== 27_1897-06-26_pna_sn78000395_0021247969A_1897062601_0027.json\n778\tThe labor world. [volume] (Duluth, Minn.) 1896-current · 1897-06-26\n779\tcrops: None\n780\theadline: PUMpHOUSE SPECTER\n781\t\n782\tCised. Who\n783\t\n784\t\n785\t,This Time?\n786\t., Investigate.\n787\t\n788\tlater Works Purchase Question\n789\tComes Up.-Extra Session.\n790\t\n791\t\n792\tMcGiIvray's Bond.\n793\t---\n794\tnight started out insufferably\n795\tdull but wound up with an en-\n796\tpertaining burst OF excitement.\n797\tAldermen Hansen and Shannon\n798\twere absent, and as neither side\n799\twas able to muster on the I'M\n800\tportant issues q majority of the\n801\tentire membership OF the council\n802\tlittle progress was made.\n803\t\n804\t\n805\tThe mayor submitted the APP\n806\tpointments of Christian Johnson\n807\tas spokesman at West Duluth and\n808\tHerman Vending to be pound\n809\tmaster at Woodland. The\n810\tformer after a lengthy discussion\n811\twas turned down, the latter re-\n812\tferred, and the former appoint\n813\tment of D. D. Mcdonald special\n814\tpoliceman without pay at the\n815\tinstance OF the Cranberry Lun-\n816\tDer company was confirmed.\n817\t\n818\t\n819\tThe fire commissioners sub\n820\tn1itted a report of the salvage\n821\tCORPS from the board OF fire\n822\tUnderwriters. The report gives\n823\ta list of the stockholders and a\n824\tfinancial statement which shows\n825\tthe total receipts up to June IS,\n826\t1897, to be So, 538 .46, of which\n827\tthere remains a balance OF\n828\t$3,14O.S5, all of which will be\n829\tneeded to keep up the service\n830\tuntil the 1897 tax is due from\n831\tthe insurance companies.\n832\t\n833\t\n834\tThe city attorney reported that\n835\tMr.. Patton would still be city\n836\tengineer until his successors\n837\tbond had been approved. Notice\n838\twas ordered given that the city\n839\twould no longer require Great\n840\tEastern hall for armory pur-\n841\tposes Estimates were allowed\n842\tto the Carroll Porter Company\n843\tfor $15,s5t:24 and to T. w.\n844\tHugo for $93.92.\n845\t\n846\t\n847\tThe board of public works,\n848\tprotested against dispensing\n849\twith the superintendent of\n850\tstreet sprinkling; the bond of\n851\tFred Voss for 100000 was\n852\tapproved; the final estimate to\n853\tG. J. Frederickson was held up; a\n854\tstring of miscellaneous bills was\n855\tallowed, during the roll call on\n856\twhich Alderman Jefferson ex-\n857\tplanned that while, voting for the\n858\tmotion to allow, he did not ap-\n859\tprove OF the Hartman com\n860\tmission allowance.\n861\t\n862\t\n863\tA proposition to let a contract\n864\tfor the steel work connected with\n865\tthe pumphouse was tabled until\n866\tthe new city engineer has quail\n867\tflied; the uniform charter bill for\n868\t$63.22 was allowed, after it had\n869\tbeen explained that the bill\n870\tcould not be collected by law,\n871\tbut that the council had a right\n872\tto pay it if it was deemed a just\n873\tdebt.\n874\t\n875\t\n876\tThe St. Paul & Duluth rail-\n877\troad was instructed to either\n878\tkeep a Waterman rput in gates\n879\tat the crossings Of 57th, 59th\n880\tand 63rd avenues west over\n881\ttheir tracks. And after much\n882\tdiscussion a proposition to dis\n883\tperse with one of the fire\n884\twardens was tabled.\n885\t\n886\t\n887\tAt this point things began to\n888\twarm up and the audience grew\n889\tinterested. Alderman Burg\n890\twanted a committee of two citi-\n891\tzens from each ward to be ap-\n892\tpointed by the president to act\n893\tin conjunction with the water\n894\tworks committee relative to the\n895\tpurchase of the water plant.\n896\tA motion to lay on the table\n897\tfailed, the roll call standing as\n898\tfollows:\n899\t\n900\t\n901\tYeas-Dahl, Duren, Jefferson,\n902\tSimpson, Stevens and Trevll-\n903\tliOn.--6.\n904\t\n905\t\n906\tNays - Burg, Crassweller,\n907\tHarker, Richardson, Rowley,\n908\tSang and Tischer.-7.\n909\t\n910\t\n911\tPresident Cochrane, who had\n912\tcalled Alderman Rowley to the\n913\t\n914\t=== 43_1868-11-27_pna_sn84024738_00271741959_1868112701_0524.json\n915\tThe daily dispatch. [volume] (Richmond [Va.]) 1850-1884 · 1868-11-27\n916\tcrops: None\n917\theadline: \n918\t---\n919\t\n920\tLeaC@Dark working common to good, ssasA;\n921\tane a wrapping. Sl4 soGs7. 50. sun-cursd CON- 1\n922\tmon to medium, $40 sO6Sl4; good to fine, slsN go.\n923\tYellow wrappers. common to medium, s29GsaS;\n924\tgood to extra (fancy), s\"@ss5.\n925\t\n926\t\n927\tRichmond Prices-Current.\n928\t\n929\t\n930\tTHURSDAY, November %, 1883.\n931\tAlcohoL-sa9s4.s so q gallon.\n9