22\tnot otherwise, and ne concludea WIN lhe\n1823\tremark that when he had time to go fool.\n1824\tIng ArOunu Will Spli AVo, 'ASJ sSOoA>< ~ W\n1825\tthat character that exhilierate rather than\n1826\tdepress, and of whose origin and genuine\n1827\tness the label would be sufficient guar:\n1828\tantec.\n1829\t\n1830\t\n1831\tThe Colonel has erected\n1832\t\n1833\t\n1834\tA CAB1NET\n1835\t\n1836\t\n1837\tand expects that Madame will soon be\n1838\table to materialize the friends who have\n1839\tcmployed her as medium of communica-\n1840\tton with this world. Beyond the changing\n1841\tof the letters and the discolDration OF the\n1842\twater spoken of in last weeks issue, there\n1843\thave been no physical manifestations, but\n1844\tMadame has been rapidly developing up\n1845\tto the peint when she will be able to pro-\n1846\tduce in person those with whom she now\n1847\tconverScs.\n1848\t\n1849\t\n1850\tCol Sweet has concluded 70 discontinue\n1851\tthe public receptions for while, and con-\n1852\tfine his efforts to private seances, or \"de.\n1853\tveloping circles,' as they are called, until\n1854\the can present Madame in less nervous\n1855\tstate and insure the success of all her un-\n1856\tdertakings.\n1857\t\n1858\t=== 12_1896-11-01_p1_sn83030272_00175042908_1896110101_0014.json\n1859\tThe sun. [volume] (New York [N.Y.]) 1833-1916 · 1896-11-01\n1860\tcrops: None\n1861\theadline: VAR ON SOCIAL TICE.\n1862\t\n1863\tDisreputabie Women In London Driven\n1864\t\n1865\t\n1866\tfrom Ono Haunt to Another.\n1867\t---\n1868\tLONDON. Oct. 31.-The disreputable woman In\n1869\tLondon is being haunted out OF her accustomed\n1870\thaunts to congregate in others. Several attempts\n1871\thave been made by tho local authorities to deal\n1872\twith this evil. The latest originated with the\n1873\tPlccadllly vestry. which summoned a general\n1874\tconference of London ~vestries. which was held\n1875\ton Oct. Ia. 1t was agreed that all the London\n1876\tlocal authorities take concerted action for the\n1877\ttotal suppression OF the systematic vice which\n1878\tinfested the streets.\n1879\t\n1880\t\n1881\tThis concerted actor\" has since been In\n1882\toperation. with the result that tho local authori-\n1883\tties. after a weeks trial, have in certain dis\n1884\ttricts ordered tho police to resort to former\n1885\tmethods of dealing with disorderly people. The\n1886\tfooltnG around OF purity vigilance societies has\n1887\thas been found especially obnoxious to the\n1888\tpolice management Of the districts.\n1889\t"}]},"uuid":"ca1e4fa8-f31b-4d8a-a46c-a968d1300a12","timestamp":"2026-10-31T07:41:58.365Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/c1/g04.txt","content":"=== 12_1912-08-02_p8_sn86069873_00280763093_1912080201_0084.json\nThe Bourbon news. · 1912-08-02\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Superstitious italians.\n---\nPerhaps of all countries Italy is the\nmost rife with superstition in all it's\nstrange and imaginative varieties. Ital-\nians believe in the \"evil eye\" They\nhave a false spirit\" which is given\nto practical jokes on poor mortals, and\na darker hobgoblin, the ''Fata Nor\ngana,\" who draws youth beneath the\nwaves of the Straits of Messina to\ndrown.\n\n=== 10_1885-10-10_pna_sn86091095_00279550912_1885101001_0013.json\nThe Ketchum keystone. [volume] (Ketchum, Idaho) 1885-1899 · 1885-10-10\ncrops: cached\nheadline: NOT \"A GHOST.\n---\n! / had just returned from Tor\n\n\nontO-whither l had gone to esti.\n\n\nmate the cost of a public work\n\n\nwith an amusing party of friends.\n\n\nThe train was delayed a few hours\n\n\nnear Vandrull. When / left the\ntumble-down Bonaventure station\n\n\ntie night was very late, the weather\n\n\nwet and windy; the streets, long\n\n\nseries of little puddles, flickering\n\n\nunder the gas. Hurrying along, l\n\n\ncrossed street corners diagonally,\n\n\nkicking up about the curb-stones\n\n\nheavy masses of ncw-fallen leaves.\n\n\nAt the street door | stood for n\n\n\nfew moments, fumbling with my\n\n\nlatch-key. Were impressionablc\n\n\n| might have thought, SO strangely\n\n\ndid the wind act, that unseen fng-\n\n\ncrs clutchcd my garments, and that\n\n\nforms scarcely palpable interposed\n\n\nbetween me and the dark hall\n\n\nwithin.\n\n\nWith some aid from a match l\n\n\nfelt my way up two pairs of stairs\n\n\nand stood within my room. Light\n\n\ning the gas l looked around.\n\n\nThough nothing was unfamiliar or\nchanged a singular air of dcscrtion\nprevailed the place. l shivcrcd\nslightly, being strangely chilled,\nand for moment thought of\nabandoning the intention to take a\nbath before bed. But to turn in\nwith that grimmy feeling which\ncomes from long railway journey\nwas out of the question. Nor was\nl sleepy. On the contrary, my\nsenses seemed preternaturally ac-\ncute and my head delightfully.\"- clear\nand capable. Go to bed? No.\nThere were some intricate calcula-\ntiOns of strains required next day,\nand to which l determined to devote\nmyself after bathing. 1t was not\nthat / did not wish to 90 to bed,\nbut that | knew could not sleep,\nand that / dreaded the horrors of\ninsomnia, from which l had once\nsuffered.\n\n\nDown to the bathroom l went.\nand, turning on the water, rcgained |\nmy room. Certainly there was not\nthe least unusual sound either in\ndescending or ascending the stairs.\nIs it possible to believe that my\nnervous condition can have changed\ngreatly, changed without cause,\nduring the short time after going\nback to my room, while l was\nundressing?\n\n\nAs all in the house had long been\nin bed there was no danger of meet\ning anyone on the stairs in my\ncondition, so l threw off\nmy coat, waistcoat and white shirt,\nput off my boots and thrust my\nfeet into slippers. Then l wound\nmy watch, laid it under my pillow,\nbrushed my teeth, and read page\n,r two Of Lord Ralcigh's address\nbefore the British association.\nThese particulars are mentioned\nhat all may understand how per-\nectly / remember every occurrence\n'r a night on which | was perfectly\n,alm and obscrvant.\n\n\nWith towels, matches, fesh-brush\nmd sponge On arm or in hand !\nattempted to open my door. For !\nmoment it refused to open, being !\nleld in some unexpected fashion {\nhen it came to my hand with a\nush. The gaslight violently bent t\nmd swayed with quick draft of\nir through the open windows. f\nlet some force acting in contrary I\nErection sharply closed the door I\nehind me.\n\n\nCropping to the head of the stair f\nray, l paused in the absolute t\narkness. From the water falling 8\nn the bathroom there was faint, '\nroar. Suddenly a /\nhild's voice broke in on the gloom c\nq o wild, but not loud, tone Of t\n\n=== 21_1900-10-25_pna_sn83045604_00202199720_1900102501_0398.json\nThe Seattle post-intelligencer. [volume] (Seattle, Wash. Terr. [Wash.]) 1888-1914 · 1900-10-25\ncrops: None\nheadline: sEArrLE, THURSDAY, OCTOBER rs\n\nTurner'S Speech.\n---\nThe speech that senator turner IL ue-\nlivering throughout the state may be con-\nsidered fairly representative of the man\nand the party in this campaign. As such\nIl requires attention that It's merits could\nnot command For. though often dexter\nously phrassd. and designed to play upon\nall the impulses that move men. It Is. at\nhest. but mosaic OF arguments and as\nsartions that crumbis to nothing at the\ntouch Of truth. The wonder Is that man\nwhose mental qualities have not always\nbeen below contempt can offer such adul-\nterated food a. this for the public con-\nsumption.\n\n\nTho appeals TO Lincoln and the pretend\nCd citations from his works that contrib\nute 10 largely to the Turner speech ere\nintroduced to obtain the sanction OF that\ngreat name for the policies Of the fusion\nrarty today. Perhaps there 10 no other\noffence OF which II has been guilty that\ndeserves or will receive from the people\nso stern rsbuks a. It's defamation OF tho\niilustrious dead. Passages from Ilncoin's\nspeeches are twisted away from their in\ntended reference and loaded with impos-\nsshle meanings and dishonorabie infer\nences. We need say no more on this\npoint than that Senator Turner is stili\nquoting TO all his audience. as genuine\nutterance Of Lincoln the preposterous\neoncoction Of the medium. Cora Hatch.\ndelivered a. . message from the spirit\nworld. n Il a fsting finale 70 work\nOf fraud.\n\n\nHis financial discussion starts from the\nqualitative theory Of money; that ''the\nvolume OF money. it, sxchangeabilify for\nother products OF labor. depends at\nany particular timc upon the quantity OF\nIl which may be in existence\" This he\nderives from . novel source, He says\nthat principles como from Almighty God.\nand that this financial law Is ono OF those\nprinciples. Inasmuch as. In tho shape stat\n\"a. u II rcjeoted by every thinking man\nand every political economist Of stand\nIng. Senator Turner IA not complimentary\n70 the Almighty. ''Tha iaboring man IL\ntho debtor OF the worid.\" he says on\nthe contrary. the laeorins man Is the\nworlds great creditor: witness tho as-\nsregate Of savings banks deposits The\nsaid dollar \"strlSes down half the money\n81 tho worid\"s sex Senator Turner cannot\npoint 70 one coin % silver that has beer\nretired from circulation because az the\nTala standard The trath Ia that the tree\nsslser people ska;e o. thin ice these days.\nsaa no reconciliation % their claims IN\nISIS with existing facts is possible\n\n\nfaksns 20 the srasts MT Tarner 10.\neenes the mere aemsgogue. holding that\na.l trusts \"rnaat be Destroyed\" This ia\nsae language Of Mr., Bryan. and II Is\nbadly sishonsst. The trus, q today is\nq corporation. rt.e oniy aircrruss Is tn\ntae amount ~ capitalization How e one\ncorporation 20 b. destroyed hy Law\nwt,nest testTussns am The sMsofai Gle.\nmsns e Eaesspcsy ept the distinction Is\n.tos arAsn hy peHoersts, who psnasr 70\niSmeraues 