 Monday\n828\tMr.. Gilbert Kahn, while using\n829\tthe saw in the work shop had\n830\tthe misfortune to cut the end Of\n831\this forefinger off. On the same\n832\tday Mr.. DeBlanc, picking up an\n833\tiron bar at the forge works, was\n834\tvery seriously burned; the bar\n835\thad been heated at the wrong\n836\tend by some careless student,\n837\tand the young gentleman being\n838\tignorant of the fact suf-\n839\tfered a painful accident.\n840\t\n841\t\n842\tAfter being visited by a spell\n843\tIF the measels, Mr.. Mumps now\n844\tboldly knocks at the dormitory\n845\tloor; poor Miss Guidry is his\n846\tirst victim; we trust that she |\n847\twill soon recover and that the |\n848\twill soon be free from\n849\t\n850\t=== 3_1857-01-16_pna_sn86092535_00202190595_1857011601_0057.json\n851\tWheeling daily intelligencer. [volume] (Wheeling, Va. [W. Va.]) 1852-1859 · 1857-01-16\n852\tcrops: cached\n853\theadline: \n854\t---\n855\tTHEsE STAYING TmEs.-Yes, slay is the word.\n856\tFor instance, a team started up street yesterday\n857\tlike mad cap, and in its course came in collision\n858\twith dray. The dray was so standing that a\n859\tvery slight jar to the tail of it, would give the\n860\tStats very forcible side motion And so it\n861\thappened, and down came the horse almost\n862\tbrvathlcss. This is but one of the Staying ac-\n863\teidents we see every day. Yesterday evening\n864\tan elderly gentleman had his pedestals suddenly\n865\tKnocked from under him by the sled of reck-\n866\tless urchin, who came tearing down the hill on\n867\tUnion street, at a:4O rate. Last, but very far\n868\tfrom least, this department had the wind raised\n869\taround its head by huge snow ball, which one\n870\tyoung scavcnger hurled at another in prOmis-\n871\tcuous crowd. This all comes under the head of\n872\t''Gying; and so might we say of the slips and\n873\tfalls which we witness at every corner. We\n874\ttee every time VIC go out as if it was the swim\n875\tter of our discomfort\" and our nerves, what\n876\tform our own mishaps and sympathy with 9th\n877\terS, will be in utter arrangement by the first of\n878\tApril\n879\t\n880\t=== 21_1912-08-01_p1_sn88076432_00271740785_1912080101_0260.json\n881\tCourier Democrat. (Langdon, N.D.) 1891-1920 · 1912-08-01\n882\tcrops: None\n883\theadline: ASailor Yam\n884\t---\n885\tWell, mebby dey is. but ghost ships\n886\tamt\"\n887\t\n888\t\n889\tThey were at the Sailors Snug Har-\n890\tDor, sitting on benches in the sun.\n891\tSome of the younger ~ones-young fell\n892\tlows of s~eventy-had imbibed modern\n893\tnotions concerning apparitions. The\n894\tman who demurred, Bob Barclay, an\n895\told tub some eighty-six years old,\n896\twas not minded to have the traditions\n897\tOf a lifetime thus demolished.\n898\t\n899\t\n900\twhat de y know about ghost\n901\t| ships?' asked one Of the sunshine\n902\tbathers.\n903\t\n904\t\n905\tWhat do 1 know about ghost ships?\n906\t| Well, hist. mates. and I'll spin y a yarn\n907\tabout one. and when l get through /\n908\tdon't want none O y to say 'twan't no\n909\tSIGH thing. I'm goin' to tell y what l\n910\tseen with my own two eyes,\"\n911\t\n912\t\n913\tThe old fellow was shrunken like a\n914\tbladder with the wind gone out of it,\n915\t| and his voice was husky as a cracked\n916\tbell. But he believed the yarn he told,\n917\tas any one could see by the earnestness\n918\twith which he told it.\n919\t\n920\t\n921\tat was this way, mates: I'd left the\n922\tsea and had gone to live with my\n923\t| darter in a seaport town on the south\n924\tAtlantic coast. The apple O. my eye\n925\twas my grandfather, Molly Sewell,\n926\tas pretty a little craft as ye eve see.\n927\t| Most young uns hain't got no use for\n928\t| old buffers, but Molly had a lot O. use\n929\t| for me, and there was nothin' she\n930\t| wouldn't do for me. The only thing l\n931\t| could do for her was to make fancy\n932\tbaskets for her, as I'd learned to do\n933\taboard ship, and spin yarns for her\n934\t| about the sea. She'd inherited a love\n935\t| for a sailors life from me and a lot\n936\tO. other granddads before me-we was\n937\tall sailors and she couldn't get\n938\tenough yarns about sea life out O. me\n939\tto satisfy her.\n940\t\n941\t\n942\tzone day I see her walkin' with a\n943\tyoung sailor, Jack Barston, OF the\n944\tbeach, and when she come in I said,\n945\t| Molly. air We goin' to sail in that\n946\tship?'\n947\t\n948\t\n949\t\"'Nhat ship, grandpa she asked,\n950\t| colorin'. She knew well enough what\n951\tI meant.\n952\t\n953\t\n954\t\"'The ship 1 saw skuddin' before the\n955\twind on the beach this afternoon\n956\t\n957\t\n958\t\"She jist dropped her hand down on\n959\tmy shoulder. put her arms around me\n960\tand didn't say nothin'.\n961\t\n962\t\n963\t\"It didn't make no difference to me\n964\ther havin' a sweetheart. She was jist\n965\tas lovin' to me as before. Barston\n966\tvas as fine a young mar as ever pull\n967\ted a rope, and none Of us had any OBJ\n968\tlection to him. But he had to save\n969\tsome money to put a gal he'd marry\n970\tinto a cot. and that would take him\n971\tsome time. He made several voyages\n972\tafter he got engaged to Molly, and\n973\tevery time he come back he'd doubled\n974\this pile. At last he said he'd make\n975\tone more voyage and when he come\n976\tback from that he'd get married and\n977\tstay ashore. He'd have enough to\n978\tbuild his cot and buy him a good boat\n979\tfor odd jobs by which he could make\n980\ta Twin.\n981\t\n982\t\n983\tcarter he'd sailed away on this last\n984\tcruise on the President Adams Molly\n985\tcame to me for comfort. She allus\n986\trelied on me for that when her boy\n987\twent away, because I'd let her talk\n988\tabout him and nothin' else. And she\n989\tsays to 'grandp',' says she. Somehow\n990\tl got an idee that Jack amt never\n991\tcomm back from this voyage I jist\n992\tTUX her in my arms and tried to smoth-\n993\ter it out of her. but I couldn't. But,\n994\tgrandp',' she went on. he promised\n995\tme that if he lost his life by bein'\n996\twrecked or dyin' of fever or anything\n997\tlike that he'd send me some token to\n998\tlet me know that he died thinking of\n999\tme-.\n1000\t\n1001\t\n1002\tII disremember what year it was\n1003\tthat Jack was due. but it was in the\n1004\tspring. Molly got a letter from him\n1005\tfrom Singapore figurin' out the date of\n1006\tthe ships reachin' the home port with.\n1007\tin a week. At the beginnin' O. that\n1008\tweek Molly was very narvous. She\n1009\tnever acted before about Jacks com.\n1010\tin home as she did this time. She KEEP\n1011\tsayin': 'Grandp'. he won't come. l know\n1012\the won't come The first day Of the\n1013\tweek Jack allowed the ship would\n1014\tlcdSu lOIl pdSSeu. anu lllC SGCUllu dllU\n1015\tso on to the sixth. and no ship Adams\n1016\t\n1017\t\n1018\tION the morning of the seventh and\n1019\tlast day a thick fog was rollin' il\n1020\tfrom the ocean. Molly and l sat quiet\n1021\tly on a dune. l holdin' her hand te\n1022\tkeep up her courage. waitin' for the\n1023\tfog to lift and hopin' the Adams would\n1024\tbe in the ofing. AFTER awhile there\n1025\twas a break in the mist, and then\n1026\twith all her sails filled, came a shiI\n1027\tI knew to onct was the Adams. Blu\n1028\ta chill run owe me. for I knowed there\n1029\twasn't any wind.\n1030\t\n1031\t\n1032\tMOLLY looked and clutched her hos\n1033\tON with her hands.\n1034\t\n1035\t\n1036\t'\"Then the fog slowly closed in OF\n1037\tthe ship, and we couldn't see no NOR\n1038\tOf her. We waited. and l knowed i\n1039\tshe was a real ship is soon as the TO:\n1040\tcleared away we'd see her ridin' a\n1041\tanchor in the harbor. But when i\n1042\tcleared up she wasn't there.\n1043\t\n1044\t\n1045\t\"Molly and l went home. both know\n1046\tin she hadn't been there. Jack Bars\n1047\tton had sent his ghost to tell Moll,\n1048\t4laA H. Aal -a..l Ill ii Z\n1049\t\n1050\t=== 19_1893-02-03_pna_sn84026918_00295862099_1893020302_0658.json\n1051\tThe weekly Union times. [volume] (Union C.H., South Carolina) 1871-1894 · 1893-02-03\n1052\tcrops: None\n1053\theadline: in THE HEART OF BRAZIL.\n1054\t\n1055\tDESCRIPTION OF A TYPICAL IN\n1056\tOla RUBBER CAMP.\n1057\t\n1058\ta Source of Profit to Many, But tho\n1059\t:, Poor Indian Gets Practically\n1060\t\n1061\t\n1062\tNothing for His Perllous Labor.\n1063\t---\n1064\tthese Amazonian towns, where every\n1065\tthing reeks of india rubber--wbere it is\n1066\ttho one enSrossing industry Of the\n1067\tpeople, the one topic Of conversation,\n1068\tthe one thing constantly seen, smellcd\n1069\tand tastcd-you are sure to be seized by\n1070\tthe spirit of discovery, sooner or later,\n1071\tand burning desire to turn hunter\n1072\tyourself and become a habitante, il for\n1073\tever so brief a period, of n genuine\n1074\trubber camp in the wilderness. And\n1075\tnothing Is easier than to crafty that\n1076\tlaudahlc ambition, especially if you are\n1077\t\"takcn\" with it at Manaos-a thousand\n1078\tmiles up the mighty river, iu the heart\n1079\tof the greatest rubber producing section\n1080\tCt the globe.\n1081\t\n1082\t\n1083\tIn our case it was particularly easy,\n1084\tfor our host is a rubber merchant (as are\n1085\tmost Of the substantial citiz,as OF the\n1086\tplace., who keeps from fifty to one\n1087\thundred Indians constantly cmp'oyel as\n1088\tcollectors, under tho leadership of a\n1089\t\"captaiu,\" or head collector, of their\n1090\town choosing.\n1091\t\n1092\t\n1093\tThe following facts. stated briefly a,\n1094\tpossible, arc summary Of the informt-\n1095\tton VIC have picked up in Brazil, from\n1096\tdivers sources, relative to tho rubber\n1097\ttrade. Like other industries in various\n1098\tParts of the world, it appears 10 be con.\n1099\ttrol!el hy capital on the Scriptural plan\n1100\toi \"to him that hath shall he givcu,\"\n1101\tWhile the actual toilcrs are very poorly\n1102\tpaid for their labor. Hcreabouts it it\n1103\tcustomary for wealthy man to obtain {\n1104\tgrant OF land of greater or less extent,\n1105\taccording to his political influence aud\n1106\tfinancial powers Of persuasion, with the\n1107\texclusive righJ of gathering rubber\n1108\tthereon for Ii stated number OF years.\n1109\tThe entire river trade Of the Amazon is\n1110\trun Oh the credit system. The rubber\n1111\thunters, who are mostly Indiups, arr\n1112\tfitted out by their employers muci as\n1113\tAmerican miners used to be supplied\n1114\twith Hgrub stake\" in their search for\n1115\tprecious metals. Each hunter, before\n1116\the sets forth on a fresh journey, is pro-\n1117\tvided with gun, u quantity of ammu-\n1118\tmilton, blanket, hammock, and enough\n1119\tprovisions to sustain him from two to\n1120\tsix months; all Of which is charged to\n1121\this account at the highest market price,\n1122\tan,i i'll return he stipulates to sell to this\n1123\tsame accommodating dealer, at some\n1124\tfixed sum per pound, all the rubber he\n1125\tmay collect during his trip, after paying\n1126\twhat lie owes for the outfit.\n1127\t\n1128\t\n1129\tBut he seldom gets the debt paid up,\n1130\tand, according to the laws OF Brazil, as\n1131\tlong as he owes penny the man to\n1132\twhom he is indebted eau claim him for\n1133\twork, holidays aud all, III bondage\n1134\takin to slavery. They are engaged in\n1135\tthe first place for a term of years, aud\n1136\tthe majority of hunters, in consequence\n1137\tOf their debts and propensity to drink\n1138\tup all available cash in the form Of\n1139\tchicha, are practically never released\n1140\tfrom tho contract.\n1141\t\n1142\t\n1143\tThe employers, in their turn, arc\n1144\tbound to be in debt to the small traders\n1145\tin the river towns, to whom they sell the\n1146\trubber. They pay absurily high prices\n1147\tfor inferior goods and get little for the\n1148\tproduct Of the enterprise as compared to\n1149\tthe price of rubber when it gets out Of\n1150\tthe clutches of the \"middle-men, whilo\n1151\tthose who do all the actual work aud\n1152\tendure the risks aud hardships get next\n1153\tto nothing. The small trader, likewise,\n1154\tis in debt to the wholesale dealer in\n1155\tPara; and the wholesaler is even more\n1156\tdeeply indebted to the New York, Balti-\n1157\tmore or London firm which furnishes\n1158\thim with supplies aud finally secures the\n1159\trubber. Some body makes heavy profits\n1160\tout Of every transaction, but it is not\n1161\ttho poor Indian, upon whose courage\n1162\tard powers Of endurance all depends, nor\n1163\tyet the smaller traders.\n1164\t\n1165\t\n1166\tThe annual expectation Of India rub\n1167\tber from Para is said to be upward OF\n1168\tgo, 600, 900 pounds, worth from So, O00,-\n1169\tcoo to $4,000, 900. Tnc rubber tree of\n1170\tBrazil (siphonia elastica, near relative\n1171\tof the iicus elastlca of the East Indies\n1172\tand the urccola elastica Of Asia) is\n1173\treally giant species Of milk weed. 1t be-\n1174\tgins TO yield when about fifteen years\n1175\told, and the Government has repeatedly\n1176\tsuggested plans for cultivating it by\n1177\tplanting large areas with trees and con-\n1178\tQuoting the business like that of coffee\n1179\tand sugar plantations. But Brazilians\n1180\tseem to be peculiarly devoid of the\n1181\tpower to take ga long look ahead\" aud\n1182\tSO far nobody has been found willing to\n1183\twait fifteen years for the first returns on\n1184\tan investment.\n1185\t\n1186\t\n1187\tHcreabouts the rubber hunters are\n1188\tcalled Siringuerios, as in Central Amer\n1189\tlea they are known as Yularoes. They\n1190\tusually go out in small parties, having\n1191\tchosen one Of their own number as \"Cap-\n1192\ttain\" and as soon as tile rubber swamps\n1193\tare reached they select a suitable spot\n1194\t= Tor INC pasc or opcravrons aud proceed TO\n1195\tconstruct rude camp, which serves as\n1196\tthe general rendezvous. They then wan.\n1197\tder of, slngly and in couples, searching\n1198\tall day for new trees and returning to\n1199\theadquarters at nlghtfall. The scamp\"\n1200\tconsists of a central hut, built on stilt-\n1201\tlike supports to elevate it above the ma\n1202\tlaria haunted norass in which rubber\n1203\ttrees most abound, with palm matched\n1204\troof, projecting all around like that Of a\n1205\tSwiss chalet.\n1206\t\n1207\t\n1208\tThere arc neither windows NJ: door\n1209\tto this rude home, nor any interior\n1210\tfurnishings except hooks upol which\n1211\thammocks may be suspended wlen rains\n1212\tdrive their owners inside. Ordinarily\n1213\t'al outdoors serves we'll for kitchen,\n1214\tand the men prefer to suspend their\n1215\tsleeping paraphernalia from tho trees\n1216\taround the central fire, built for the\n1217\ttriple purpose of frightening away\n1218\tDrOwlinO bpasts counteractin o. the effects\n1219\t\n1220\t=== 1_1920-10-07_pna_sn85040437_00414215397_1920100701_0117.json\n1221\tThe Washburn times. [volume] (Washburn, Wis.) 1896-1976 · 1920-10-07\n1222\tcrops: None\n1223\theadline: THE HAUNTED POOL.\n1224\t---\n1225\tSY0pSis. - DIR. John Nicholson,\n1226\tJust beginning his career, becomes\n1227\tresident physician and companion\n1228\tOF Homer Sidney at Hartley house.\n1229\tMr.. Sidney is an American, semi\n1230\tinvalid, old and rich and very de-\n1231\tsIrous to live. Mrs. Sidney is\n1232\tSpanish woman, dignified and reti-\n1233\tcent. Jed, the butler, acts like\n1234\tprivileged member OF the family.\n1235\tHartley house is a line old isolated\n1236\tcountry place, with a murder story,\n1237\tq haunted pSol.\" and many watch\n1238\tdogs. and an atmosphere OF mys-\n1239\tterry. The haunted pool is where\n1240\tRichard Dobson, son of former\n1241\towner of Hartley house, had killed\n1242\this brother, Arthur Dobson. JED\n1243\tbegins operations by locking the\n1244\tdoctor i'm his room the very first\n1245\tnight. Doctor John fixes his door\n1246\tso he can't be locked in. He meets\n1247\tIaobel. daughter of the house, anc\n1248\tfalls I'm love at first sight. In the\n1249\tnight he finds the butler drunk and\n1250\tholding Mrs. Sidney by the wrist.\n1251\tHe interferes. Mrs. Sidney makes\n1252\tlight Of it. John buys a revolver.\n1253\tJohn overhears Jed telling Mrs.\n1254\tSidney he will have his way. In\n1255\treply she says she will not hesitate\n1256\tto kill him. Mrs. Sidney asks John\n1257\tto consent to the announcement of\n1258\this engagement to Isobel. The\n1259\tyoung people consent to the make\n1260\tbelieve engagement. Later they\n1261\tfind it is to head off Jed. who\n1262\twould marry Isobel. Jed tries to\n1263\tkill John, but the matter is\n1264\tsmoothed over. John, though \"en-\n1265\tgagged to Isobel, conceals his love.\n1266\tMr.. Sidney visits a nearby prison\n1267\tand has Dobson, the murderer,\n1268\tpointed out Queer stories of the\n1269\t''haunted pool are told.\n1270\t\n1271\t=== 13_1874-06-12_p1_sn85034076_00200292364_1874061201_0609.json\n1272\tThe Andrew County Republican. [volume] (Savannah, Mo.) 1871-1876 · 1874-06-12\n1273\tcrops: None\n1274\theadline: \n1275\t---\n1276\t1ouT ulSuuesS, 1Aao 40 iiV'iC AAioUy,\n1277\tshe loves the ~failies I have seen her\n1278\tlooking for us many time\"\n1279\t\n1280\t\n1281\t\"She is welcome\" said the princess.\n1282\t\"Form a ring, my trends let us\n1283\tdance\"\n1284\t\n1285\t\n1286\tIn a moment they had formed a circle,\n1287\tand danced round and round the tree till\n1288\tKitty was almost dizzy watching them.\n1289\tThen they stopped and broke into little\n1290\tgroups and chatted to each other.\n1291\t\n1292\t\n1293\tChimes are changed\" said one fairy.\n1294\tOnce the children often came to\n1295\tdances and were glad to seek us out, but\n1296\tnow they care nothing about u.s.\"\n1297\t\n1298\t\n1299\t\"Not quite so bad as that\" said the\n1300\ttall fairy who had recognized Kitty.\n1301\t81 see more mortals than almostany n\n1302\tof us, unless it may be Ivy here, and I\n1303\tcan tell you there are some children,\n1304\tyes, and grown people, too, who care\n1305\tfor u.s.\"\n1306\t\n1307\t\n1308\t\"Ah, yes\" said graceful Fuchsia,\n1309\t\"I know how they look for us and\n1310\twhisper their wishes to u.s.\"\n1311\t\n1312\t\n1313\tEYES,\" said Ivy, for all old Scarlet\n1314\thas been among mortals so long, I go to\n1315\ta great many of their haunts that he\n1316\tdoes not, and I will tell you how it is.\n1317\tChildren love fairies as they love oil\n1318\tbright and pretty things, but a great\n1319\tmany children have q sort Of fairyland\n1320\tof their own /; not like ours, you know,\n1321\tfor their beautiful dresses and toys tear\n1322\tand soil and break, while ours are al\n1323\tways the same ; but when theirs are\n1324\tare fresh they arc very lovely, and they\n1325\tare SO taken up with them that they\n1326\thaven't time to care for us. Then they\n1327\thave so many parties of their W that\n1328\tthey don't care for our dances, and\n1329\tsometimes their mothers give so much\n1330\tattention to all these fine things that\n1331\tthey never think to tell them of the\n1332\tfairies, and so they. even hear of\n1333\tthem. Scarlet's friend, little Kitty,\n1334\tdoesn't think so much of fine things\n1335\tbut that she can love the flowers and\n1336\tfairies\"\n1337\t\n1338\t\n1339\tZone thing more\" said a tall, beau\n1340\ttiful fairy, the loveliest OF them all,\n1341\twith pure white robes and pale golden\n1342\thair-she looked like the beautiful\n1343\tangel in the church window, Kitty\n1344\tthought. cit is only good and gentle\n1345\tlittle children who can see MS; children\n1346\twho love each other and are good to\n1347\ttheir mammas. We couldn't have i'll\n1348\ttempered, greedy or rude children find\n1349\tus-we should vanish away instantly\"\n1350\t\n1351\t\n1352\t\"Halloo - cried Joe, flinging open\n1353\tthe door with a bang. What are You\n1354\tdoing there. you little stupid?\"\n1355\t\n1356\t\n1357\t\"Hush !\" said Kitty. \"Just see the\n1358\tfairies!\"\n1359\t\n1360\t\n1361\t\"Fairies, indeed, Iguess so; where 2\n1362\t\n1363\t\n1364\tKitty pointed to the jar, but the\n1365\tfairies had vanished.\n1366\t\n1367\t\n1368\t\"There is nothing there, you\n1369\tlittle goosel You've been dreaming.\n1370\tThere don't cry, I don't mean tc\n1371\tplague you,\" for the tears started tc\n1372\tKitty's eyes, and Joe wasa k~ind-hearted\n1373\tboy, in spite of his roughness. The lit\n1374\ttie old man Just peeped ont from the\n1375\tmaple boughs, and nodded as Joe put\n1376\this arm around the little girl, in a shy,\n1377\tshame-faced way.\n1378\t\n1379\t\n1380\tKitty pointed eagerly. \"See, Joe !\n1381\t\n1382\t\n1383\tels it a butterfly ?\" said Joe. \"II\n1384\tcatch it for you\"\n1385\t\n1386\t\n1387\tHe hastily stretched out his hand\n1388\tbut the fairy vanished.\n1389\t\n1390\t\n1391\t\"It was a fairy, Joe did you reall.\n1392\tsee it :\"\n1393\t\n1394\t\n1395\tJoe looked puzzled.\n1396\t\n1397\t\n1398\t81 saw some wings futtering, Tut\n1399\tcan't find anything. Are there reall..\n1400\tfairies after all ?\"-ILedrH and Home\n1401\t\n1402\t=== 4_1941-07-27_p1_sn88063294_00414187511_1941072701_0147.json\n1403\tDetroit evening times. (Detroit, Mich) 1921-1958 · 1941-07-27\n1404\tcrops: None\n1405\theadline: \n1406\t---\n1407\t'Ccet uuc Don toirc utcrcrs1ute\" ne said\n1408\t-But / stI can't piece 30 together The one\n1409\twhite man on the Islands rs Encst Biddell, the\n1410\tHudsons Bay Company representative there\n1411\tII wag he who sent out the story by messenger\n1412\tand dog team one hundred miles to the Great\n1413\tWhale River post on The mainland, where they\n1414\thave tranamitter. l just had the post on\n1415\tthe air\n1416\t\n1417\t\n1418\tBut AN / could make out was a hodge\n1419\tpodgo about the Twenty fourth chapter of st.\n1420\tNattlD The World Wars Ghost ship\n1421\tan amazing display Of Northern lights. men,\n1422\twomen and children going out naked TO \"A hill\n1423\ttop In twenty degrees below zero. \"A falling\n14