w. -\n\n\nMr.. Stephens says he has conversed with\nspirits in the spirit land. and they have\nadvised the establishment of this church.\nII ia expected that church building Will\nbc- erect cd next wear\n\n=== 3_1926-11-23_p3_sn82014519_00414219731_1926112301_0419.json\nNew Britain herald. · 1926-11-23\ncrops: None\nheadline: gRS. HOUD|NI HAS\n\n NOT G0T MESSAGE\n\n40 Spirit Communications From\n\n Her Husband\n---\nNew York. Nov. 23 R-Harry Houdini, magician. who died on October 31, had three compacts by which he planned to try to get mes sages. over from the spirit world, his wife said yesterday.\n\n The magician. who during 35 years of activity, did much to ex- pose fraudulent practices of fake mediums, composed a quotation of ten words of which his wife alone shared the knowledge he took to bis grave.\n\n Many \"Messages\"\n\n Of the dozens and dozens of messages\" which she said today that mediums had given her as coming from her departed husband, Mrs. Beatrice Houdini. said that not one held the key words that would identify it as Houdini's.\n\n Claims Contact\"\n\n The Scientific American today released for publication a message received by medium in Attleboro, Mass, on November 2, which she submitted as the attempt of Hou- dini 10 get in touch with this world. The medium. who asked\n\n=== 7_1894-05-31_pna_sn85033255_00514152536_1894053101_0085.json\nRiver Falls journal. [volume] (River Falls, Pierce County, Wis.) 1872-2019 · 1894-05-31\ncrops: None\nheadline: JACkALING A JAY.\n\nHow the Ambitious Young Dramatist\nfrom the Country is Fleeced.\n---\nHow the Ambitious Young Dramatist\n\n\nfrom the Country is FIeeced.\n\n\nDO you know, for instance, what\n''iackaling\" is in New York? A jackal\nis a man generally of good address and\ncapable of display of good-feilowship,\ncombined with much knowledge of\nliterature and art, and vast and in\ntinate acquaintance with writer's,\nmusicians and managers. He makes\nit his business to haunt hotels, the\natrical agencies and managers offices,\nand to know whenever, in his language,\n\"a new jay comes to town\"\n\n\nThe jay he is after is some man gem\norally from the smaller provincial cities\nwho has artistic or theatrical aspira-\ntions and pocketful of money. 1t is\nthe jaclSal's mission to turn this jaw\ninto an ''angel.\" Has the gentleman\nfrom LOclSport come with the score of\na comic opera under his arm ard two\nthousand dollars in his pocket? Two\nthousand dollars will not go far 10\nward the production of a comic opera\nin these days, and the jay finds that\nout later. but not until after the\njackal has made himself intimate\ndciudihtcu WIN very gen\ntlemanly and experienced mana\nger, who thinks that it can\nbe done for that price with strict\neconomy. Has the young man of pro-\nnounced theatrical talent arrived\nfrom Keokuk with gold and thirst\nfor fame? The jackal knows just the\ndramatist who will write him the play\nthat he ought to star in. Does the\nwealthy and important person from\nPodunk desire to back something alb\nsolutely safe and sure in the line of\ntheatrical speculation? The jaclxal has\nthe very thing for which he is looking.\nAnd in all these, and in all similar con-\ntingencies, it is n poor jackal who does\nnot get his commissions at both ends. =\nSrrihner\n\n=== 3_1883-11-08_p1_sn85025620_00212479330_1883110801_0175.json\nThe Worthington advance. [volume] (Worthington, Minn.) 1874-1908 · 1883-11-08\ncrops: None\nheadline: HER HUSBAND THINKS SHE rs\n\n\nIYTY'r1\n---\na Georgia lady recently became de.\nranged While attending Il revival meet\ning in Worth county. She went ravi,lR\nabout the house and yard with Il bucket\nOf water. baptizing everything in her\npathway. She baptized her husband\nand children, ami while doing it sang\nsongs she had heard but once or twice.\nAlthough All unlettered woman she\nreads any chapter in the, Bible, pro-\nnonncing correctly and distinctly, pays\ning attention to punctuation points,\netc. She preachesnearly all the while\nuses the choicest words, and displays\ngreat knowledge in the handling Of\ndifferent subjects. Although not d\nMason, she Knows all the mysteries Of\nems OF Mnsons have gone to see her and\nthey have All gone away dumbfounded.\nShe neither eats nor sleeps. Her bus;\nband has come to the conclusion sha\nis Il witci.-North Georgia Gazette. i\n\n\nHer husband is an ignorant priest\nled man. All that this woman does\nand much more is done every day by\nmediums, and the facts are perfectlE,\nfamiliar to thousands uf people who\nknow --t!t;st the persons are neither\nwitches nor insane. It is Il simple,\nlnatterof spirit control and exactly\nlike 1.-THE gift of tongues\" Of ''prsph-\necy\" ETC., mentioned as occurring in\n\n\nBible times. Yet in the full light of\nthe 18th century people who are sus-\nceptlble to this spirit control are treat\ned 10y ignorant doctors. preachers, and\nbig@tS as insane or as fraunds. A gen\ntleman III Ohio some time since had\nhis wife seat to the insane ASYLUM. at\n\n\nNewburg. heca!Se she w:sy subject to\nspirit control at times. There the poor\nwoman must stay in the hands Of iynO-\nrant physicians, who will not study the\nlaws of psychology and Of spirit con.\ntroll, because a materialistic church on\nthe one hand ana an egotlstical science\non the other. condemn such invpstiga-\ntions as ''ot the devil'Or us unworthy\nthe lord'y pretensions OF se-called\nscientists ts.-Worthington ADVANCE.\n\n\n1--WE are glad to recognize in A. p.\nMiller, editor Of the ADVANCE. Il\nbrother indeed who is not afraid to\nspeak out the plain. simple truth which\nso readily takes all tie mystery out of\nmany Of these otherwise wonderful\noccurrences. Just use Il little common\nsense in the light Of known and well\nfacts, and the case Of the\nGeorgia lady is simple and not very\nstrange. When will our physicians\nlearn wisdom and use good common\nsense in the treatment of so-callea in.\nsane ?-1ndependent Age.\n","numLines":1610,"startLine":1,"