{"parentUuid":"c64e2733-9d4e-4b69-b57f-60629123ab94","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"775e54ef-6487-4f79-8598-643421b87718","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018o8x4xTkjL1BVAHHs1nYfM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t=== 37_1856-06-13_pna_sn84024443_00280779209_1856061301_0558.json\n2\tThe Athens post. [volume] (Athens, Tenn.) 1848-1917 · 1856-06-13\n3\tcrops: cached\n4\theadline: \n5\t---\n6\tSPIRirUALrsM.-The following is set forth in\n7\tone of our exchanges as the creed of the\n8\t'Spirtualsts.\" The reader will admit, il\n9\tthis is all, that there is nothing very mon.\n10\tstrous or blasphemous about it:\n11\t\n12\t\n13\tThey all believe in and teach the exis-\n14\ttence of GOD, the great eternal and first\n15\tcause, who rules and superrules all things\n16\tthat Jesus Christ was the spiritual son of the\n17\tMost High, more fully developed than any\n18\tbeing before or since, empowered and dele\n19\tgated by the Father to perform the mission\n20\tand accomplish the work assigned him-that\n21\tman has within him an immortal principle\n22\twhich must, in accordance with the laws If\n23\this creation, progress in holiness purity and\n24\tvirtue throughout the ceaseess ages Of eter-\n25\tnity-that death is an improvement in knew\n26\tledge and progress, and every one when born\n27\tinto the spirit land, enters that state of ex\n28\tistence exactly as they leave the earth form\n29\tthat the whole human family are an unit,\n30\tand that the happiness of each depends upon\n31\tthe happiness Of the whole, and vice versa-\n32\tthat il is the first great duty of man to love\n33\tGod, and one another, and that no opportu-\n34\tnifty should be omitted to do good to our fell\n35\tlows, more particularly the lost and down\n36\ttrodden of the land-that sin is violation\n37\tof the law of God, and in no case can the\n38\tsinner go unpunshed-that sin debases those\n39\twho indulge therein and cannot, according\n40\tto the immutable laws of God, produce any\n41\tthing but contumely and disgrace, and in the\n42\tend debasement and ruin. These are a few\n43\tof the general outlines of the teachings of\n44\tthe Spiritualist.\"\n45\t\n46\t=== 28_1898-09-19_p1_sn88053055_00271741224_1898091901_0287.json\n47\tThe Wilmington daily Republican. (Wilmington, Del.) 1890-1902 · 1898-09-19\n48\tcrops: cached\n49\theadline: The Ghost Soon Walked.\n50\t---\n51\t1 The new managers Of the Bijou, Greves\n52\tami Harris have come to grief They\n53\topened this hoodooed place of Amusement\n54\ttwo weeks ago and their represen tative\n55\tcame to tho REPUBLICAN Office this day\n56\tweek and wanted large adveI tsement\n57\tinserted In Its columns, but as this paper\n58\thad had one apple out Of that bag, the ad.\n59\tvertlsement was refused, without the noe\n60\tsssary cash being handed In at the same\n61\ttime. The press representative said the\n62\tnew managers had money to burn, but 1t\n63\tonly took two weeks of bad business to\n64\tburn up their pile and they have left Will\n65\tmlngton sadder but wiser men, after hav-\n66\tIng sunk some fifteen hundred dollars,\n67\tSheriff Finn called on them saturday\n68\tnight with claim If $50 when the NJ ost\n69\twalked. The theatre will not be open I'd\n70\tthis evening\n71\t\n72\t=== 7_1874-10-09_pna_sn84024653_00393349517_1874100901_0359.json\n73\tStaunton vindicator. [volume] (Staunton, Va.) 1860-1896 · 1874-10-09\n74\tcrops: cached\n75\theadline: \n76\t---\n77\tSINGULAR COINCIDENCE.-A most sim\n78\tguiar incident in connection with the death\n79\tof Bishop Lee, of Iowa, has come to light\n80\tA private letter from Davenport, Iowa,\n81\tcontains the follow ng: 1--WE have seen\n82\tvery anxious the last two weeks over the\n83\tIllness of Bishop Lee, which terminated in\n84\this death ON Saturday morning. The whole\n85\tcommunity are saddened by the event.\n86\tSome two months ago he got up IN the night\n87\tand took Il bath, and. on returning to his\n88\troom he made mistake and stepped OIL IL\n89\tlong flight OF stairs, and landed III lie loot\n90\twith u tremendous crash, as he was very\n91\theavy, weighing over two hundred pounds\n92\t1t aroused the whole family, and Mrs. Lee\n93\tmd Carrie sprang from their beds, ana,\n94\tlighting each i candle, went to see what had\n95\thappened, and found the Bishop lying on\n96\tthe floor Of the entry. He got up, however,\n97\twithout aid, and seemed to have received\n98\tno injury, except few slight bruises,\n99\tthough his right hand was little lamed.\n100\t\n101\t\n102\t\"Mr f. and myself called on him two\n103\tdays after, and while telling us the circum-\n104\tstance of the fall he mentioned this coin\n105\tcidence He had Il letter in his hand, which\n106\the had just received from his son, Henry,\n107\tliving at Kansas City. His son wrote:\n108\tAre you well; for last night had q dream\n109\tthat troubles me. 1 heard crash, and,\n110\tstanding up, said to my wife, Did you hear\n111\tthat crash ? | dreamed that father had a\n112\tfall and was dead l got up and looked at\n113\tmy watch, and it was O'Clock. | coald\n114\tnot sleep again, so vivid was the dream,\n115\tand it made him anXionsto hear from home.\n116\t\n117\t\n118\tThe Bishop said he was not supersti-\n119\ttious but he thought it remarkable that\n120\tHenry should have had the dream at the\n121\tvery hour of d the same night on which\n122\t| the accident occurred. The difference iii\n123\tthe time there and here is just fifteen min\n124\tutes, and it was 2:15 by his watch. making\n125\tit ut the same moment. 1t was as If he had\n126\tactually heard the fall And the fall finally\n127\tcaused the Bishops death. His hand be-\n128\tcame intensely lainful, and gangrene set\n129\tin, which, after two weeks Of Suffering,\n130\tterminated his life. We are none Of us\n131\tspiritualists, us you know, but surely facts\n132\tlike this must go far to make us realize\n133\tthat there is a basis Of truth for their by\n134\t| pothesis of spiritual faculties resident in\n135\tman. ILow did Henry Lee become cogni-\n136\t! zant Of the accident to his father ?''-\n137\t| }VueEju1Ion (ti;r0,icfS\n138\t\n139\t=== 2_1853-01-15_pNone_sn83045784_00415661605_1853011501_0480.json\n140\tWeekly national intelligencer. · 1853-01-15\n141\tcrops: cached\n142\theadline: RAPPINGs, INSANITY, AND SUICIDE.\n143\t---\n144\tEVENING MIRROR.\n145\t\n146\t\n147\tOur morning contemporaries largely occupied with\n148\tthe testimony taken before the Coroner Jury yesterday\n149\t\n150\t\n151\tin the of Martin Langdon, whose death by suicide\n152\tMonday week. has been already chroncled The prin-\n153\tcpal witness the wife the deceased who testified\n154\tthat he had not been well for three four weeks pre-\n155\tvousy his illness arising chiefly from pain and disorder\n156\tin the head, brought by mental excitement first\n157\tcaused and afterwards kept up by his introduction and\n158\tconnc1on with certain circles Of rappers in this city\n159\t\n160\t\n161\tThe deceased printer by profession and up the\n162\ttime he became deuded by the roppers was strong\n163\tminded He had, prior to his acquaintance with these\n164\trappng humbugs lost child circumstance which ap-\n165\tto have been turned to great by the opera\n166\ttors his creduity. We extract paragraph from Mrs\n167\tLangdon's testimony\n168\t\n169\t\n170\tFor the last ten months the deceased has been con-\n171\tstany reading what he called spiritual papers he read\n172\tthe Spiritual Telegraph, and also the Mountain Cove\n173\tJournals the first paper is published in this city, the\n174\tJournal is published believe in Lafayette county vir\n175\tgnia he did belong to the Methodist church. but since\n176\the began to read these particular papers his mind seemed\n177\twholly absorbed in them he in the habit OF going\n178\tattend the spiritual circles in New York for the last\n179\tmonths for three four weeks previous to his decease\n180\the the circles very frequently during 