f several of the Northern States\n281\tin direct conflict with the Constitution of the United\n282\tStates, and ought to be repealed if continued upon\n283\tthe statute books after public attention has been\n284\tdirected to the subject the injured States will be\n285\tjustified in Secession\" As these personal liberty\n286\tacts still remain in force, the Southern States may\n287\tplauby plead the authority of the late head of the\n288\tExecutive, in Justification Of Secession But there\n289\tis still To be found even q higher authority than\n290\tMr.. Buchanan, in favour of the course which the\n291\tSouth has pursued, and that authority is the late\n292\teminent senator, John G. Calhoun. Thirty years\n293\tago. Mr.. Calhoun declared that - the Constitution\n294\tof the United States is q compact to which the\n295\tStates were parties in their sovereign capacity;\n296\tnow, whenever compact is entered into by parties\n297\t\n298\t\n299\t\n300\titv to decide in the last resort each of them has\n301\tthe right to decide itself in relation to the nature\n302\textent and obligations of the instrument\" Mr..\n303\tEverett himself appears have been long in doubt\n304\tabout this very question, for he states candidly\n305\tthat, % while this ill-starred movement con-\n306\tfined the States of the extreme South, and they\n307\tabstained from further aggression, was willing\n308\tthey should go in peace. This course, thought,\n309\twould retain the Border States, and bring back\n310\tthe seceders in year or two, and such understood\n311\tto have been in substance the programme OF the\n312\tAdministration Well, this programme and these\n313\tdoctrines of Mr.. Everett seem to belong to the\n314\tcategory of very strange - notions. Ii the w ash\n315\tngton authorities sincerely believed that Secession\n316\twas rebellion, their duty clearly was to put 1T\n317\tdown. or at least to make the attempt to do so. If\n318\tthey believed that the South had right to secede,\n319\thowever incOnvenient to. or subyersive OF, the\n320\tnational dignity and power, they can have no right\n321\teven now to prevent it. There can be no medium\n322\tbetween these two extremes and whatever may\n323\thave been the duty of the Washington Cabinets in\n324\tthe circumstances it is certain they have hitherto\n325\tfailed to do it.\n326\t\n327\t\n328\t1t is pitiable to witness the Government of q\n329\tgreat nation in the hands OF politicians who could\n330\tfold their hands in helpless incapacity, and specu\n331\tlate on the probability of retaining part OF the\n332\tcountry in allegiance by sacrificing another part to\n333\tthe convenience of rebellion. What would we have\n334\tthought of an English statesman had any such pro-\n335\tposed, In 1910, 10 allow lreldIlu W 70 III pclCcg\n336\tif she ubstained from further aggression, in order\n337\tthat the Government might continue to hold peace\n338\table possession of Scotland. The idea is too alb\n339\tsurd for serious argument; and how Mr.: Everett\n340\tcould reconcile such policy with common sense\n341\twe are at a loss to conceive. What contrast in\n342\tpractical energy does the conduct Of the British\n343\tGovernment present in dealing with the Indian re-\n344\tbellIon to that of the American Government in\n345\tdealing with this Secession movement. In the\n346\tformer case the action of the Executive was prompt\n347\tand decided there neither doubt nor vacllla-\n348\ttion exhibited and no speculative maunderings\n349\tabout the rights of the King of Delhi were al\n350\tlowed to interfere with the embarkatiOn Of troops\n351\tand munitions of war and a like spirit animated\n352\tthe Indian Government and all its subordinates,\n353\tfrom the Hooghly to the Himalayas. General\n354\tScott is busy mastering great army round Wash-\n355\tington with remarkable patience; and he is wise\n356\tto exercise the \" better part of yalor \" for the con-\n357\thict with nine millions of Anglo-SaxOns in the\n358\tSouth, exasperated to the last degree against the\n359\tNorth must be one of the most dreadful character.\n360\tBut had the North shown, at an earlier date.\n361\ttithe of the spirit and energy which it now dis\n362\tplays this Secession movement, which has at length\n363\tattained such great force, might have been checked\n364\tin the bud.\n365\t\n366\t\n367\tDr. Motley, the accomplished author OF - The\n368\tHistory of the United Netherlands has also en-\n369\ttered upon this ' tournament of talk like a\n370\tcond disinherited knight, for the purpose of up\n371\tholding the honor oil the Lady Union against all\n372\tcorners. In pamphlet on the Secession movement,\n373\tDIR. Motley occupies twenty-nine and half pages\n374\twith quotations, arguments, and illustrations, for\n375\tthe purpose of showing that the Southern States\n376\thad no right to secede, while he dismisses the de\n377\tfacio question with ten and half lines. Now, we\n378\tcannot help thinking that all such labors, in the\n379\tface of an undoubted Secession, are idle as the\n380\twinds. 1t is lamenting over \"spilled milk, or\n381\tlocking the stable door after the steed is stolen,\"\n382\tand then sitting down patiently to convince oneself\n383\tthat the thief had right to steal away the horse\n384\tThere are facts in connection with this Secession\n385\tmovement which are patent and indisputable, viz:\n386\tthe Southern States have constituted themselves\n387\tpower; and the North has done little or nothing,\n388\tfor more than six months, to prevent them. While\n389\tthese facts remain palpabe realities, writers and\n390\tspeechifers may rave and scribble till doomsday;\n391\tand the fruits Of their labOrs will be somewhat the\n392\tsame as that Of the countryman who heeced bis\n393\tpig. and was rewarded with great cry, but very\n394\tlittle wool. Whether or not the South had right\n395\tto secede is a matter of little consequence.\n396\tThe question is, how is Secession, which is q\n397\tfat accOmpI, to be dealt with The great military\n398\tforce at length set on foot BY the North must an\n399\tswer this question.\n400\t\n401\t\n402\t= Professor johnson, of Middleton University,\n403\twas one day lecturing before the students of min\n404\teralOgy. He had before him quite number of\n405\t\n406\t=== 10_1915-04-29_p1_sn99021999_00280779076_1915042901_0626.json\n407\tOmaha daily bee. · 1915-04-29\n408\tcrops: cached\n409\theadline: pespollers OF Nature.\n410\t---\n411\t\n412\t\n413\t\n414\tOMAHA, April 2s--To the Editor OF The\n415\tBee: / was very glad to see the article\n416\ton \"wildflowers and Picnickers\" by M\n417\tL.\" In Sundays Bee and only wish that\n418\tII might had even more prominent space\n419\t30 that all might see\n420\t\n421\t\n422\tl. too. am keenly interested in the virgin\n423\twoodland about. us and as have not the\n424\tprivilege Of possessing any OF It myself\n425\t/ cannot possibly be criticized for mer-\n426\tcenary motives. Therefore l feel free to\n427\tdiscuss the criticism in that letter which\n428\twas as Just as the invitation was gem\n429\tsrous.\n430\t\n431\t\n432\tl do not know SO much about the region\n433\tnorth of Florence because my ''Haunts::\n434\tare for the most part along the Missouri.\n435\tdown toward Bellevue-thst most de-\n436\tiishtful childs Point region amongst\n437\tthe hills and dales and virgin forest\n438\twhich DO not doubt those splendid men\n439\tcomposing the Fontenelle Forest associa-\n440\tton contemplate some day acquiring and\n441\tpreserving for the public.\n442\t\n443\t\n444\tTime and again have strolled along\n445\tthe paths 10 ana from that region. SO\n446\tmasnificiently and iuxuriously favored\n447\tby Dame Nature. only TO have the pleas\n448\trlre and Joy OF the scene blotted by the\n449\tfrequent sight OF immense punches OF\n450\twithered wild flowers which q few hours\n451\tearlier had been ruthlessly and greedily\n452\tplueked by thoughtlets youth OF spend\n453\tthrift maturity, and as quick