ll career, collegiate\n1761\tand professional. It occurred when\n1762\tTrait n. ex-Notre Dame star and\n1763\tcenter for the Bears, ran into him\n1764\tin a race for a forward pass.\n1765\t\n1766\t\n1767\tFunny in this pro game\" Red\n1768\tpursued his horizontal reflections\n1769\t''These fellows who were all Amer.\n1770\tleans in college aren't half 50 tough\n1771\tas some of these birds from small\n1772\tcolleges that you never heard Of.\n1773\t\n1774\t\n1775\tAge Doesn't Count\n1776\t\n1777\t\n1778\t\"Another thing-age doesn't seen\n1779\tto count. There are a lot of fel.\n1780\tlows of IS and SO years old playing\n1781\tfootball in this league that we'd b\n1782\tmighty glad to get on the Yankees\n1783\t"}]},"uuid":"bb4e878b-d214-48fe-9a16-b64817781300","timestamp":"2026-10-31T07:52:21.298Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/c1/g08.txt","content":"=== 37_1856-06-13_pna_sn84024443_00280779209_1856061301_0558.json\nThe Athens post. [volume] (Athens, Tenn.) 1848-1917 · 1856-06-13\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nSPIRirUALrsM.-The following is set forth in\none of our exchanges as the creed of the\n'Spirtualsts.\" The reader will admit, il\nthis is all, that there is nothing very mon.\nstrous or blasphemous about it:\n\n\nThey all believe in and teach the exis-\ntence of GOD, the great eternal and first\ncause, who rules and superrules all things\nthat Jesus Christ was the spiritual son of the\nMost High, more fully developed than any\nbeing before or since, empowered and dele\ngated by the Father to perform the mission\nand accomplish the work assigned him-that\nman has within him an immortal principle\nwhich must, in accordance with the laws If\nhis creation, progress in holiness purity and\nvirtue throughout the ceaseess ages Of eter-\nnity-that death is an improvement in knew\nledge and progress, and every one when born\ninto the spirit land, enters that state of ex\nistence exactly as they leave the earth form\nthat the whole human family are an unit,\nand that the happiness of each depends upon\nthe happiness Of the whole, and vice versa-\nthat il is the first great duty of man to love\nGod, and one another, and that no opportu-\nnifty should be omitted to do good to our fell\nlows, more particularly the lost and down\ntrodden of the land-that sin is violation\nof the law of God, and in no case can the\nsinner go unpunshed-that sin debases those\nwho indulge therein and cannot, according\nto the immutable laws of God, produce any\nthing but contumely and disgrace, and in the\nend debasement and ruin. These are a few\nof the general outlines of the teachings of\nthe Spiritualist.\"\n\n=== 28_1898-09-19_p1_sn88053055_00271741224_1898091901_0287.json\nThe Wilmington daily Republican. (Wilmington, Del.) 1890-1902 · 1898-09-19\ncrops: cached\nheadline: The Ghost Soon Walked.\n---\n1 The new managers Of the Bijou, Greves\nami Harris have come to grief They\nopened this hoodooed place of Amusement\ntwo weeks ago and their represen tative\ncame to tho REPUBLICAN Office this day\nweek and wanted large adveI tsement\ninserted In Its columns, but as this paper\nhad had one apple out Of that bag, the ad.\nvertlsement was refused, without the noe\nsssary cash being handed In at the same\ntime. The press representative said the\nnew managers had money to burn, but 1t\nonly took two weeks of bad business to\nburn up their pile and they have left Will\nmlngton sadder but wiser men, after hav-\nIng sunk some fifteen hundred dollars,\nSheriff Finn called on them saturday\nnight with claim If $50 when the NJ ost\nwalked. The theatre will not be open I'd\nthis evening\n\n=== 7_1874-10-09_pna_sn84024653_00393349517_1874100901_0359.json\nStaunton vindicator. [volume] (Staunton, Va.) 1860-1896 · 1874-10-09\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nSINGULAR COINCIDENCE.-A most sim\nguiar incident in connection with the death\nof Bishop Lee, of Iowa, has come to light\nA private letter from Davenport, Iowa,\ncontains the follow ng: 1--WE have seen\nvery anxious the last two weeks over the\nIllness of Bishop Lee, which terminated in\nhis death ON Saturday morning. The whole\ncommunity are saddened by the event.\nSome two months ago he got up IN the night\nand took Il bath, and. on returning to his\nroom he made mistake and stepped OIL IL\nlong flight OF stairs, and landed III lie loot\nwith u tremendous crash, as he was very\nheavy, weighing over two hundred pounds\n1t aroused the whole family, and Mrs. Lee\nmd Carrie sprang from their beds, ana,\nlighting each i candle, went to see what had\nhappened, and found the Bishop lying on\nthe floor Of the entry. He got up, however,\nwithout aid, and seemed to have received\nno injury, except few slight bruises,\nthough his right hand was little lamed.\n\n\n\"Mr f. and myself called on him two\ndays after, and while telling us the circum-\nstance of the fall he mentioned this coin\ncidence He had Il letter in his hand, which\nhe had just received from his son, Henry,\nliving at Kansas City. His son wrote:\nAre you well; for last night had q dream\nthat troubles me. 1 heard crash, and,\nstanding up, said to my wife, Did you hear\nthat crash ? | dreamed that father had a\nfall and was dead l got up and looked at\nmy watch, and it was O'Clock. | coald\nnot sleep again, so vivid was the dream,\nand it made him anXionsto hear from home.\n\n\nThe Bishop said he was not supersti-\ntious but he thought it remarkable that\nHenry should have had the dream at the\nvery hour of d the same night on which\n| the accident occurred. The difference iii\nthe time there and here is just fifteen min\nutes, and it was 2:15 by his watch. making\nit ut the same moment. 1t was as If he had\nactually heard the fall And the fall finally\ncaused the Bishops death. His hand be-\ncame intensely lainful, and gangrene set\nin, which, after two weeks Of Suffering,\nterminated his life. We are none Of us\nspiritualists, us you know, but surely facts\nlike this must go far to make us realize\nthat there is a basis Of truth for their by\n| pothesis of spiritual faculties resident in\nman. ILow did Henry Lee become cogni-\n! zant Of the accident to his father ?''-\n| }VueEju1Ion (ti;r0,icfS\n\n=== 2_1853-01-15_pNone_sn83045784_00415661605_1853011501_0480.json\nWeekly national intelligencer. · 1853-01-15\ncrops: cached\nheadline: RAPPINGs, INSANITY, AND SUICIDE.\n---\nEVENING MIRROR.\n\n\nOur morning contemporaries largely occupied with\nthe testimony taken before the Coroner Jury yesterday\n\n\nin the of Martin Langdon, whose death by suicide\nMonday week. has been already chroncled The prin-\ncpal witness the wife the deceased who testified\nthat he had not been well for three four weeks pre-\nvousy his illness arising chiefly from pain and disorder\nin the head, brought by mental excitement first\ncaused and afterwards kept up by his introduction and\nconnc1on with certain circles Of rappers in this city\n\n\nThe deceased printer by profession and up the\ntime he became deuded by the roppers was strong\nminded He had, prior to his acquaintance with these\nrappng humbugs lost child circumstance which ap-\nto have been turned to great by the opera\ntors his creduity. We extract paragraph from Mrs\nLangdon's testimony\n\n\nFor the last ten months the deceased has been con-\nstany reading what he called spiritual papers he read\nthe Spiritual Telegraph, and also the Mountain Cove\nJournals the first paper is published in this city, the\nJournal is published believe in Lafayette county vir\ngnia he did belong to the Methodist church. but since\nhe began to read these particular papers his mind seemed\nwholly absorbed in them he in the habit OF going\nattend the spiritual circles in New York for the last\nmonths for three four weeks previous to his decease\nhe the circles very frequently during this latter\nperiod he visited them twice week circle meeting\nheld at Mrs Fishs in Seventh avenue, another\nat Mrs Clacklsys in Sixth avenue another Mrs\nRikers in Tenth avenue; all of these ladies have hus\nbands living; my husband told me that at Mrs. Fishs\ncircle each person attending the meeting paid one dollar\n\n\n= n\neach night Of meeting\n\n\nMrs. Langdon goes on to say that she induced to\nattend of these rappng meetings where she\nhusband magnetised, and adds:\n\n\n81 then away, for frightened; after got into\nthe street. he he deccased said his head felt very bad\nmade afraid that Mrs. Cackley said there were so\nspirits in the she said that when her hand moved\nthe spirits that moved it, straight home\nCsckley told the company that she medium and\nthe spirits manifested themselves through her. About\nweeks previous this meeting the told that Mrs.\nCackley and Mrs Riker said he should st\nut his own house night and this would prepare him\nbecome seeing medium he did nearly night\nhe would not allow speak to him during this time did\nnot do but minded my work. he pursued this until\nwithin three four days Of the time which he his\nthroat\"\n\n\nTO the stand is but continuation of the picture Of deusos\nand nfatuaton by which victim has been hurried\nviolently from the world TO bear witness trust In\nsoberer realm, against the and cdesroyng\n\n\nlist Of unarcs suicides caused by the rappng\nhundreds the victims being in the main persons Of peculiar\nand i'll balanced organizations with cracked brains and\ntroubled with disorders the sort Of people\nwho commissions at mpmeengs and be\nverted every week in the year by ranting revival\n\n\nIn deciding that his death consequence\nOf these appings the cooner jury recommended that the\nwhich he had attended be end to by the grand\njury.\n\n\nwe doubt If this is the way reach the evil Beyond\nquestion the internal rappngs make lunacs and do\nbut the thing largely done BY various religious excite\nments here and there During the reign Of Merism our\nasyums eemng with craxed by ridiculous\n\n\nidea hundreds Of might be cited year OF\nsettled and suicide in consequences OF the\nmind on religious or spiritual topics,\n\n\nThe death Of THOMAS s. HAnBLrs, the proprietor Of\nthe Bowery Theatre, New York. his residence\nabout hapast 10 o'clock Saturday night from brain\nfever which attacked him on Monday. The announce\nmen of the event made from the stre of the theatre\nduring the performance, when the curtain st once\n\n=== 11_1861-09-21_pna_sn82015408_00212471379_1861092101_0290.json\nPolynesian. [volume] (Honolulu [Oahu], Hawaii) 1844-1864 · 1861-09-21\ncrops: cached\nheadline: No. 2I.\n---\n\n\n\nand vet she had to surrender that right and after\nwards recognise their independence. No doubt,\nthe Americans morally In the right in resisting\nthe imposition of taxes levied upon them by Leg\nisature in which they were not represented but\nthe legality of that right was urged by British\nstatesmen it was admitted by many amongst the\nAmericans themselves. and when they rose In\narmed resistance they cast to the winds the oaths\nthey had previously taken to the paramount Power.\n\n\n1t is by no means so clear as Mr.. Everett supposes\nthat the Southern States had not some kind Of\nright to secede. He thinks otherwise, and his\nopinion is supported by many eminent authorities;\nbut still 1t is an open question. Mr.. Buchanan,\nthe late President, declared that 90 TIE personal\nliberty acts of several of the Northern States\nin direct conflict with the Constitution of the United\nStates, and ought to be repealed if continued upon\nthe statute books after public attention has been\ndirected to the subject the injured States will be\njustified in Secession\" As these personal liberty\nacts still remain in force, the Southern States may\nplauby plead the authority of the late head of the\nExecutive, in Justification Of Secession But there\nis still To be found even q higher authority than\nMr.. Buchanan, in favour of the course which the\nSouth has pursued, and that authority is the late\neminent senator, John G. Calhoun. Thirty years\nago. Mr.. Calhoun declared that - the Constitution\nof the United States is q compact to which the\nStates were parties in their sovereign capacity;\nnow, whenever compact is entered into by parties\n\n\n\nitv to decide in the last resort each of them has\nthe right to decide itself in relation to the nature\nextent and obligations of the instrument\" Mr..\nEverett himself appears have been long in doubt\nabout this very question, for he states candidly\nthat, % while this ill-starred movement con-\nfined the States of the extreme South, and they\nabstained from further aggression, was willing\nthey should go in peace. This course, thought,\nwould retain the Border States, and bring back\nthe seceders in year or two, and such understood\nto have been in substance the programme OF the\nAdministration Well, this programme and these\ndoctrines of Mr.. Everett seem to belong to the\ncategory of very strange - notions. Ii the w ash\nngton authorities sincerely believed that Secession\nwas rebellion, their duty clearly was to put 1T\ndown. or at least to make the attempt to do so. If\nthey believed that the South had right to secede,\nhowever incOnvenient to. or subyersive OF, the\nnational dignity and power, they can have no right\neven now to prevent it. There can be no medium\nbetween these two extremes and whatever may\nhave been the duty of the Washington Cabinets in\nthe circumstances it is certain they have hitherto\nfailed to do it.\n\n\n1t is pitiable to witness the Government of q\ngreat nation in the hands OF politicians who could\nfold their hands in helpless incapacity, and specu\nlate on the probability of retaining part OF the\ncountry in allegiance by sacrificing another part to\nthe convenience of rebellion. What would we have\nthought of an English statesman had any such pro-\nposed, In 1910, 10 allow lreldIlu W 70 III pclCcg\nif she ubstained from further aggression, in order\nthat the Government might continue to hold peace\nable possession of Scotland. The idea is too alb\nsurd for serious argument; and how Mr.: Everett\ncould reconcile such policy with common sense\nwe are at a loss to conceive. What contrast in\npractical energy does the conduct Of the British\nGovernment present in dealing with the Indian re-\nbellIon to that of the American Government in\ndealing with this Secession movement. In the\nformer case the action of the Executive was prompt\nand decided there neither doubt nor vacllla-\ntion exhibited and no speculative maunderings\nabout the rights of the King of Delhi were al\nlowed to interfere with the embarkatiOn Of troops\nand munitions of war and a like spirit animated\nthe Indian Government and all its subordinates,\nfrom the Hooghly to the Himalayas. General\nScott is busy mastering great army round Wash-\nington with remarkable patience; and he is wise\nto exercise the \" better part of yalor \" for the con-\nhict with nine millions of Anglo-SaxOns in the\nSouth, exasperated to the last degree against the\nNorth must be one of the most dreadful character.\nBut had the North shown, at an earlier date.\ntithe of the spirit and energy which it now dis\nplays this Secession movement, which has at length\nattained such great force, might have been checked\nin the bud.\n\n\nDr. Motley, the accomplished author OF - The\nHistory of the United Netherlands has also en-\ntered upon this ' tournament of talk like a\ncond disinherited knight, for the purpose of up\nholding the honor oil the Lady Union against all\ncorners. In pamphlet on the Secession movement,\nDIR. Motley occupies twenty-nine and half pages\nwith quotations, arguments, and illustrations, for\nthe purpose of showing that the Southern States\nhad no right to secede, while he dismisses the de\nfacio question with ten and half lines. Now, we\ncannot help thinking that all such labors, in the\nface of an undoubted Secession, are idle as the\nwinds. 1t is lamenting over \"spilled milk, or\nlocking the stable door after the steed is stolen,\"\nand then sitting down patiently to convince oneself\nthat the thief had right to steal away the horse\nThere are facts in connection with this Secession\nmovement which are patent and indisputable, viz:\nthe Southern States have constituted themselves\npower; and the North has done little or nothing,\nfor more than six months, to prevent them. While\nthese facts remain palpabe realities, writers and\nspeechifers may rave and scribble till doomsday;\nand the fruits Of their labOrs will be somewhat the\nsame as that Of the countryman who heeced bis\npig. and was rewarded with great cry, but very\nlittle wool. Whether or not the South had right\nto secede is a matter of little consequence.\nThe question is, how is Secession, which is q\nfat accOmpI, to be dealt with The great military\nforce at length set on foot BY the North must an\nswer this question.\n\n\n= Professor johnson, of Middleton University,\nwas one day lecturing before the students of min\neralOgy. He had before him quite number of\n\n=== 10_1915-04-29_p1_sn99021999_00280779076_1915042901_0626.json\nOmaha daily bee. · 1915-04-29\ncrops: cached\nheadline: pespollers OF Nature.\n---\n\n\n\nOMAHA, April 2s--To the Editor OF The\nBee: / was very glad to see the article\non \"wildflowers and Picnickers\" by M\nL.\" In Sundays Bee and only wish that\nII might had even more prominent space\n30 that all might see\n\n\nl. too. am keenly interested in the virgin\nwoodland about. us and as have not the\nprivilege Of possessing any OF It myself\n/ cannot possibly be criticized for mer-\ncenary motives. Therefore l feel free to\ndiscuss the criticism in that letter which\nwas as Just as the invitation was gem\nsrous.\n\n\nl do not know SO much about the region\nnorth of Florence because my ''Haunts::\nare for the most part along the Missouri.\ndown toward Bellevue-thst most de-\niishtful childs Point region amongst\nthe hills and dales and virgin forest\nwhich DO not doubt those splendid men\ncomposing the Fontenelle Forest associa-\nton contemplate some day acquiring and\npreserving for the public.\n\n\nTime and again have strolled along\nthe paths 10 ana from that region. SO\nmasnificiently and iuxuriously favored\nby Dame Nature. only TO have the pleas\nrlre and Joy OF the scene blotted by the\nfrequent sight OF immense punches OF\nwithered wild flowers which q few hours\nearlier had been ruthlessly and greedily\nplueked by thoughtlets youth OF spend\nthrift maturity, and as quickly tired or\nand thrown by the wayside,\n\n\ndo not. and hardly think that NJ L.\nwould. object to the bona fide plucking\nOf spray for a -button.hols bouquet or\neven. when they are plentiful. to the\ncareful digging up OF root for trans\nplanting at home: but the ruthless, sell\nfish mania for wholesale plumber is most\nemphatIcally 10 be diBcountenanced The\nprobability OF nature Is source OF in\nSEPARATION when Contemplated I'm place;\nas socn as severed It quickly becomes\ndross. And. in passing, II may very APP\npropriately be stated that the birds are\na. much a fixture in the landscape as\nthe flowers and when ruthlesy killed\nare but food for worms\" and thence\nforth some life\" Is .inexpiicably absent\nfrom the canvas.\n\n\nSO It Is regrettable that in this. as in\nother matters. people cannot practise\ntemperance but must needs have total\nprohibition forced upon them As Is the\ncase In every tourist region where even\nthe plueking Of twig n penalized.\n\n\nsuppose, however that Il is idle to\ndwell upon the subject IL seems that\nthere always. have Deen, and probably\nVIII continue 10 be. some people to whom\nthe love OF Personal Liberty and the\nwinning of a race In feverish CONNER\neislism is the acme Of true satisfaction.\nA. W. ANDERSON.\n\n=== 23_1915-10-27_pna_sn83045389_00296020151_1915102701_0509.json\nRichmond times-dispatch. [volume] (Richmond, Va.) 1914-current · 1915-10-27\ncrops: cached\nheadline: AGED sPECTER ARISES\n\n\nTO PLAGUE THEIR Minds\n\nJurors Are Puzzling Over Application\nOF Decision in Missouri Case to\n\n\nNow Haven Trial.\n\nQUERY OF GUILTY KNOWLEDGE\n\nWere Directors Absolvcd From\nknowing Their Acts We're Illegal,\nor Knowing 1t, Did They Delibcr-\n\n\nately Continue Illegal Conspiracy?\n---\n\n\n\n\n\n\nNEW YORK. October 26.-The spec\nter of the oft-quotcd Missouri freight\nratio decision. by which the United\nstates Supreme Court In 1897 first APP\npiled the Sherman antitrust law to\nrailroads. arose In the trial OF the\neleven New York. New Haven and\nHartford Railroad directors to-day, to\nplague the minds of the jurors.\n\n\nThe question put before them to de-\nc!dc was whether the decision absolved\nthe New Haven directors from know\ning that they were committing illegal\nacts prior to that date and therefore\ncould not have intended the conspiracy\nwith which they are charged. that of\nmonopolizing the traffic of New Eng\nland; or whether. knowing they had\nbeen committing illegal acts. they con-\ntinued TO commit them in spite of the\ndecision.\n\n\nOBJECTION Is MADE TO\n\n\nINTRODuCING MINUTES\nThe question came up when the\ngovernment proposed to Introduce min\nutes of the New Haven board dated\nMay /. 1897. in which il was stated\nthat the directors had been called to-\ngether to consider what policy should\nbe pursued By this company. and what\nadvice should be given the New York\nand New England Railroad Compan, in\nregard TO freight matters in view OF\nthe recent decision Of the Supreme\nCourt Of the United states in the\ntrans-Missouri freight case.\n\n\nControl OF the New England road\nIt's chief competitor. had just been ac-\nCURED by the New Haven previous TO\nthis, II had been shown. ana T. V.\nLindabury. of counsel for the defense.\ninquired upon what theory tho minutes\nwere introduced. Government, Attorney\nSwackcr explained that IT was To show\nthat the New Haven directors knew Of\nthe existence Of this decision. but\nnevertheless disregardcd it. TO this\nend defendants attorney replied that\nup TO that time the law had never\nbeen applied TO railroads: that the Mis-\nsouri, rate decision was the reversal! OF\ndecisions Of two lower courts absoly-\ning II from application TO the railroads\nand that therefore the directors of the\nNew Haven could not know they We're\ncum-i!tting illegal acts\n\n\nThomas Thachcr. also Of counsel for\nthe defense, followed with \"a similar\nargument. and il vas apparent that\non\" II the important points for the\ndefense was being pressed\n\n\nNo action was taken by the directors\nIN regard To the question. the minutes\nstated. and after they were in the\nrecord. Attorney Swackcr began the\n\n\n\n\n\nshow that no action having been taken\nthe alleged conspiracy further TO\nifonopoiizc the transportation traffic Of\nNew England Was continued\nTESTIMONY COMPLETED\n\n\nUp no TIF: YEAR 1907\nPrior testimony to-day completed the\ntestimony OF the government up TO the\nyear 1897. The New Haven at tina;\ntime had virtually acquired all the\nrailroads south OF the Boston and AL\nbany. and had fought that road 10 :.\nstandsti!l\n\n\nTIE mastcrful power OF the late J.\nT. Morgan WAS emphasized by Mr.\nlcilcn as harn!ouiziug factor in :hc\nh.story of the trouble between the\nt\"o roads AS presented by the gov\nernments testimony. Mr.. Morgan was\nthen director OF both the New Haven\nand the New York Central. which con\ntroilcd the Boston and Albany, and at\nmeeting on Morgan's yacht the Cor\nsair. Mr. Mcllcn testified tho traffic\narrangement between III. New Haven\nana the Boston and Albany was entered\ninto.\n\n\nThe mass Of technical detail col\nlected in the many letters put into the\nrecord caused Judge Hun; :\" sugg.s.\nthat Only their salient points lie read\n10 the jury.\n\n\n1T has taken US ten days ALREADY\nto reach the 1895 lines\" he remarked\nCounsel FOR the defense insisted how\never mat their full text be read AL\nthough X. Y. Linda bury. in protest.\nIng against the reading OF one letter\npredicted that ii the government did\nnot cease to cumbcr the record with\nirrelevant material. We cannot hope TO\nconclude this case before July I had\nhoped that We could finish by Christ\nmas\"\n\n=== 15_1917-09-27_pNone_sn99021999_00279551187_1917092701_0371.json\nOmaha daily bee. · 1917-09-27\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Wanted to Celebrate\n\n\nBefore Going to Dry State\n---\nThe wish to get good and hilarious\nbefore they went to dry state,\nbrought All Haunting, 4l88 Cass street,\nand Harry Lever, Jefferson. Ia, into\nSouth Side police station \"Wednesday\nmorning. Police Judge Madden fined\nthem each $40 and costs.\n\n\nThey were arrested Tuesday night\nby Officer Mcclure. They were plan\nming on leaving Nebraska for the dry\nstate of Iowa.\n\n=== 14_1900-09-08_pna_sn86091092_00295869367_1900090801_0143.json\nThe Caldwell tribune. [volume] (Caldwell, Idaho Territory [Idaho]) 1883-1928 · 1900-09-08\ncrops: cached\nheadline: THE GHOSTS.\n---\nFl\n\n\nIf Bob IflgersoH had survived tiil-\ntil this time and had withheld the\nwriting Of his book, The Ghosts, I\nhe would probably have dedicated\nit 10 Democratic politics instead Of\nthe Hebrew religion.\n\n\nOf all the ghosts that religious\nbigotry has ever conjured tip, none\nwere quite 50 ttnsubstantial as the\nghosts which the Democratic party\nhas manufactured for the purposes\nOf this campaign. The chief ghost\nis called Imperialism and is fright\nfull mainly Oh account of its form\nliable name. Te understand how\nlittle substance there is in this APP\npartition, one needs only to exam\ninc. his own mind. Imperialism,\nwithin the meaning placed upon\nthe term by the Democratic party,\ncontemplates the autocratic rule Of\nan emperor, reigning over numer-\nous disconnected states, provinces\nand dependencies; It means the sub\njugation Of all the people within\nthe imperial realm to the will or\nwhim of one man. To bring about\na government of that character in\nthese United States requires, first,\nthe absolute subjugation Of the peo'\npie of the United States; it demands\nthat the people themselves are will\ning to forge tie chains that will\nbind them in subjugation; it means\nthat they will raise vast armies to\nhold them in that condition.; it\nmeans that they are ready to yield\ntheir liberties, their sovereign pow\ner and all that Americanis1n stands\nfor to the keeping OF one person;\nand this is the thing which our\nDemocratic friends tell us the Re\npublican party is contemplating.\nNow we would like to find in all\nthis broad land, one single individ-\nual so degraded and abject as to\nconsider such proposition for one\nmoment. If that individual can be\nfound, it may be conceded that\nthere is something substantial in\nthe imperial ghost.\n\n\nThe next Democratic ghost in\norder is the \"Trust.\" They tell\nus that the Republican party is pre-\npairing to enthrone capita1istic com\nbinations, for the express purpose\nof enabling these combinations to\ncontrol the commercial and indus\ntrial destinies of The nation. They\ntell us that the Republicans, with\nfull knowledge that they will be\ntraYpled upon and driven to indus\ntrial slavery, are now engaged in\nbuilding up, spreading and encour-\naging trusts; and more than that,\nthey insist that we are doing it for\nno purpose on earth except to graf\niGy vindictive passion against hon\nest people and legitimate enterprise.\n\n\nThe next Democratic ghost is\nthe Gold Bug\" This ghost has\nbeen obliged to take a third or\nfourth rate seat III the congregation\nof ghosts. 1t is not nearly as terri\nBILE a spectre as it was four years\nago, i'll fact, SO little is it dreaded,\nthat Mr.. Bryan, himself, in his\ngreat speech at Indianapolis, actual\nly forgot to mention its existence;\nbut in this section Of the country,\nevery possible effort is being made\nto deck it,outin the frightftl1 habili-\nments i'll which it was arrayed in\n1896. The Honorable Chas. A.\nTowne is in our State for the ex-\npress purpose of injecting new life\ninto this moribund apparition, but\nup to the present time the ghost\nseems much disposed to shuttle off\nwhatever of animation it once had\nand go quietly to rest in the tomb\nof its ancestors.\n\n\nThe fourth ghost in the catalogue\nis Government by Injunction\"\nbut in this State, this once potent\ngoblin has ceased to be of much\nservice, the Democrats themselves\nhaving destroyed its influence by\n\n\n\n\n=== 12_1914-06-26_p2_sn94050056_00414185216_1914062601_0444.json\nThe Ketchikan miner. · 1914-06-26\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Ghost Plant of Oregon.\n---\nThe ghost plant was known and much praised by the Indians of Oregon in times past and is sometimes known as the Indian pipe plant. It is said the Indians believed that 1t had great rem edial qualities and made from it To- ton which they considered curative for diseases of the eye. It is tall. waxlike plant about eight inches high. and it's bloom resembles a waxen cup Portland Oregonian.\n\n=== 18_1896-02-05_pna_sn85066387_00175037342_1896020501_0090.json\nThe San Francisco call. [volume] (San Francisco [Calif.]) 1895-1913 · 1896-02-05\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Bought a Haunted House.\n---\nOAKLAND, CAL, Feb. 4.-George A.\nCakes, the weli-known and popular Native\nSon and journalist of Hay wards, has pur-\nchased the John Meig her place, about one\nmile out of that city. The estate has been\nIn court some eleven years and was orig\ninally appraised at $3.50, which included\nsome barnyard fowls and other articles\nwhich have long gone to decay or waste.\n1t has been the source of much gossip and\nis claimed to be haunted. His bid of $3.50\nwas affirmed blythe court to-day.\n\n=== 30_1920-08-25_pna_sn86071593_00415628511_1920082501_0379.json\nThe Brattleboro daily reformer. (Brattleboro, Vt.) 1913-1955 · 1920-08-25\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nA department store advertises a Won\nderful new game guaranteed to tell\none s age correctly also the weekly sal\nairy, family facts and other things.\nThis seems to be going the ouija board\none better.\n\n=== 8_1913-11-29_pna_sn85042344_0020029550A_1913112901_0306.json\nTulsa daily world. [volume] (Tulsa, Indian Territory [Okla.]) 1905-1919 · 1913-11-29\ncrops: cached\nheadline: THERE ARE NO GHOSTS\n---\nThere are no ghosts, the skcptics say.\nDead is the rose Of yesterday.\nThe friends we love return no more\nhy winding stair or secret door\nWhere little children blithcly play.\nAt twilight when the world Is prav.\nAnd shadows rise in dim array,\n\n\nThere are no ghosts\nyet once ] dreamed. from FAR away.\nJ heard her baby footsteps stray\nSoftly across the nursery floor.\nEach football touched the hearts\n\n\ndeep core\n\n\nII love could bring her back l-but nav,\n\n\nThere are no ghosts\n\n\nr. f. Parr. I'm London sphere.\n\n=== 25_1891-11-23_p1_sn92053945_00212471690_1891112301_0812.json\nRock Island daily Argus. (Rock Island, Ill.) 1886-1893 · 1891-11-23\ncrops: cached\nheadline: ILLINOIS POLITICS,\n\nThe Field Of the Sucker State\n\n\nViewed by a Correspondent\n\nThe Bright Outlook Fer the l,emo-\ncracy In 0s-Other Matters-ip.\neidenis and Nouas-A\n\n\nPoetcaI Tramp.\n---\no\"u no'e nau the opportunity to sound\nthe nee corner and grocery store poll\nticlans on their views of the coming cam,\npaign The results of my investigations\nIN TINS line are anything but flattering to\nthe republican party, and if Illinois fails\n10 sdorn the democratic column. after\nthe battle of 90. it will not be the fault of\nthe farmers in this section The repubu-\ncan who solaoes his soul with the idea\nthat the farmer landslide of a wear 500\nhas been recovered is hugging a ehasily\noeiusion. There is one and only one\ncandidate who could bring back into the\nrepublican fold portion of the disaffec.\ntea ones, and that is Blaine, and il ia\npretty generally conceded by the tinplste\nwiseacres that he will again forbid the\nuse OF his name at the convention. The\nhorny handed tiller of the soil is no\nlonger the conservative element of the\ncountry and reference to the c1nserva-\ntive administration of President Harrison\nis greeted with discontinued grunt.\nBlaine with his reciprocity scheme in\nwhich the farmers recognize democratic\nlow tariff. sugar coated TO delude monop.\nolIsts, might bring bsck some of the old\ntime enthusiasm. The farmers utterly\nrefuse TO be reconciled TO the Mckinley\nbill, and the Australian ballot system has\ncut OF the purchase of foaters, and\nmakes Indiana surely democratic and\nIllinois as surely doubtful.\n\n\nThe farmer dissffection is not confined\nto national issues. They heve their\nknives out and ready whetted to slaugh~\nter Joe Fifer, should be be the nominee\nfor governor. They have neither forgot\nten or forgiven Private Joes' action in\nthe matter of the railroad and warehouse\nboard. The governor sighted and ig-\nnoreG the grsnsers in appointing Tanner\nagainst the expressed wishes of the\nfarmers and they have not forgotten it.\nFifer and Tanner have fallen out\nand it is said the latter sspires to the\ntreasurership. The farmers never did\nlike John R, and will \"swot\" his head\nwherever he pokes it up. Farmer, disaf-\nlection is not tie only obstacle that con-\nfronts the republicans in the coming cam\npaign. The leaders of the party sre all\nsplit up into factions and are at war\namong themselves. The Hank Evans\nclique have already made up their state\nfor The state no nlnees. and will endeavor\nTO force it upon the convention, in spite\nof farmer protests. II is rumored that\nEvans wants to be treasurer himself, and\nwith Tanner as the next most probable\ncar didate the convention will probably\n| ALLEVIATE the few remaining farmer adj\nherents of the party.\n\n\nDuring the recent convention of the\nYoung Peoples Society of Christian En-\ndesvor in this city, visiting divine told\nme good story bearing on politics. |\nhave already related it in letter to an\nother paper, but it has not as yet had time\nto start on the rounds and will be new to\nARGUS readers. The minister in ques-\nton wss called. during the late campaign\nin Ohio, to administer consolation TO a\ndying democrat Among other things he\nread the incident of SIMPSONS Staying\nthousands of Philistines with the jaw\nbone of an ass. Daring the reading the\nsick man remained in an apparently I'M\nconscious condition, but at the close of\nthe story suddenly asked:\n\n\nwho told that story?'\n\n\nat is from The bible solemnly re-\nsponded the preacher.\n\n\n\"Well, hang me if didn't think it was\nan extract from one of Bill McKirley's\ntariff speeches\" and so saying, the demo-\ncrst turned his face TO the wall and gave\nup the ghost.\n\n\nID dJ DltSCal IL plOSICSo III lAAS\ncircuit court case that will interest the\nentire state. About year ago Chicago\nwas much wrought up over a report that\ncitizens of that burg were eating the meat\nof cattle affected with lumpy jaw or &C.\ntinomy COSTS. There was a lively news\npaper discussion over the matter, and\nwar of words between the Chicago meat\ninspectors and the state board of live\nA eho ha,r, ,~fY ha\ndiscussion the whisky trust shipped from\nPeoria to Chicago 1875 fat cattle. Upon\ntheir arrival in 1icsgo the meat iuspec-\ntors culled out 10 bead as afflicted with\nlumpy jaw and permitted The\nboard of live stock com missinners stepped\nin, condemned the entire consignment and\nGent them to the refuse tank. The pres\nent suit is brought by The whiskey trust\nto recover damages from the state veter-\nmarian and live stock commissioners for\nthe 1875 cattle de destroyed. The town is\nswarming with both theoretical and prac\ntical witnesses. and the trust will en-\ndeavor to show that lumpy jaw is not an\ninfectious or con lagious disease. and that\nthe meat of cattle affected with the disease\nis fit for human food with the exception\nof the head. They will endeavor to\nestab ish the fact that the disease is pure\nly local and not systemic.\n\n\nFrom diseased meat to diseased brains\nmay be but ste D and reminds me OF an\nother incident Of the past week. An\nidiotic but harmless tramp was given\nlodgings at the police station the other\nnight. and before he was consigned to\nbed the desk sergeant searched him and\nfound among 9th er things roll OF man\nuscript poetry evidently home made. The\nsergeant permitted me to copy the verses\nwhich, though outrsging all common\nsense and coherency. have all the Jingle\nof Gilbert g. Sullivan comic opera The\ncrack Drained poet starts out by address,\ning his best girl ia the following style:\n\n\nssTwInkle. twinkle, little girl,\n\n\nHow thy nose : out of curl.\n\n\nUp above thy chin so h'gh,\n\n\nLike a lamp post in the sky.\n\n=== 6_1844-05-30_pna_sn84038585_00414186440_1844053001_0469.json\nWheeling times and advertiser. [volume] (Wheeling, Va. [W. Va.]) 1839-1849 · 1844-05-30\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nthni IN very qulet season NOW.\nSui do those waters roll nud Temp and roaf,\n\n\nAnd tumble, oil DAY long\nSlll are lie ruInnows spanning rhem\n\n\nA hundred feel below\nSUI When The sun is GA them Do HEY slime\n\n\nAnd glow like molten gold\nsui When the day Is elo1my do they fall\nLike sno\", or seem 10 rfumhIe away,\nLike lie FROM Of great chalk elf.\nOr roll down the rock like leuse while .nekr\nBa always does The mighty stream appear\n\n\nII DE ns II comes down.\nAnd always from The unialhnmahle grave\nArse thai tremendous ghnsl uf spfav\nAID rn'si which Is never land.\n\n\nWhich has haunted lbis place\n\n\nWIN The same dread iolennlfy.\n\n\nSince darkness brooded oa The deem\nAnd lhai inst flood before The Delupe-Llpht\nTine rushing on creation al The word Of God\nTo any one WHO reads his we need not ,ay that\nhui lhfee lines IN II vary al all from the closest\nreqntsluons oiar Iamblc movement The measure\nis precisely Of The KIND winch Mr. Soulhe. sn often\nused Fur The readers convenience. We eopy\nirom Ihalaba his well remembered line. on n,ghr.ns\nan instance.\n\n\nHow beautiful is Night\n40 ms, oescutes nor cloud. nor speck, nor slain\n\n\nBreaks The serene OF heaven\n\n\nIn fall ofbed glory yonder Moon divine\nRolls through the dark blue depths\n\n\nBeneath her steady ray\n\n\nThe desert ctce sPreads\n\n\nLike The rUurd ocean. glfdleI with the sky\n\n\nHow beautiful Is night\"\nFrom the An euean Notes We extinct the follow.\ning passage. only changing lis form. as IN the line,\non Niagara.\n\n\n'Canl as We may. SAYS Mr. Dickens prd osue\nshali To The end OF all things II Is very much botd.\n\"f 'ne poor 10 he vftnon' lhan the rlch; and the\nsoo1 '\"a' t'ln 'hem shines the better Tor IT, And\nIII another strain he continues. IN prose pr,nled\nverse f\nIN many noble mansion lives man\n\n\nlie best OF husband, and Of fathers.\nHhose private worth ia both capacities\n\n\nIs justly landed 10 The SKIES\nnu' ot'hs nun here, upon lhls crowded deck;\nAhc Je\"eIs,-unblnd her br,,d.ih.,.\n\n\nea'y wrinkles on her brow\n\n\nPinch her pale check\n\n\n'a'e ana much pusalon,-\n\n\narray her lated form\n\n\nIa roarseiy patched am.\n- l,e' 'Hefe be nolaIng bur hls Ioy.\no'e' her forth aud deck her nm,\n'''1 you Sia'l pat 1t 10 The proof indeed\n% change his station IN he yo,1\n\n\nThai he shall see\n\n\n>\"o'e young ihlrgs who climb oh.,,, -,\nu t'\"' records Of hs wealth and nan us Knee\n'''.'o \"'e'Ilets with blm 70, L,s IL,\n\n\n.\"',P P\"aelef' On his .canlv ms sI'} dread.\n~ ~ many ualts 10 DIVIDE \"'1'\nr'e'efy sun ofcopC,,\nanu iHihet u reduce II. .u,Ii\n\n=== 28_1909-02-08_p9_sn85042462_00175035758_1909020801_0159.json\nLos Angeles herald. · 1909-02-08\ncrops: cached\nheadline: DR.. WORCESTER\nTELLS OF WORK\nFOUNDER OF THE EMMANUEL\nMOVEMENT breaches\nCRUSADE HAS EXTENDED OVER\n\n\nENTIRE WORLD\nBegan with Fight in Boston Against\ntuberculosis and Was Confined\n\n\nto Pastors Special\n\n\nParish\n---\nSdllOu THAT IctASQ LIlS SSolllIl5 ScpdSleS y\nto the utmost.\n\n\nLong before the hour of service peo-\npie began to gather. The parishioners\ncame in through the choir entrance\nbut the general public was not ad-\nnitted until ten minutes before the\nopening Of the service. Many stood\nduring the entire service.\n\n\nDR.. Worcester, who is medium\nsized man with a face full of good will\nspoke in an easy, convincing, CONVEX\nsational tone. with no attempt what\never at oratorical effects and with few\nif any, gestures He described how\nhe had started and carried on his work\ngiving a personal touch to the whole\naddress.\n\n\n\"I am much cheered at ethe interest\nmanifested in our work\" said DR.\nWorcester. 'It has been matter of\nsurprise that in this distant state\nthere has been as much interest taken\nas in any of the other states.\n\n\nThere is today a very interesting\nreligious movement. The interest in\nour work is simply astonishing. This\nwork has been carried on in the east\nfor about two years. When we began\nwe had no idea of anything more than\nanother form of church work in our\nown parish We had been interested\nin the work of fighting tuberculosis\nfor two years and we thought it pos\nsib1e to cooperate. but had no idea of\ntreating others than our own parish\ntoner's.\n\n\n1.-The idea was to treat our parish\ntoner's for nervous disorders. We have\nmade no effort to extend our work\nalong this line.\n\n\nWork Is Worldwide\n\n\n''All over this country and in Eng\nland, Ireland, the Philippines. Hawaii,\nCentral Africa and South Africa are\ngroups of people engaged in the same\nform of service. Not long ago re-\nceived a letter from Japanese pro-\nfe@sor in Toxic, saying he had a class\nof 800 Japanese.\n\n\nct is a sign of the times. 1t would\nhave been impossible to gain this\nwidespread interest by advertising.\nWhen the time is ready for the idea\nit is only necessary to enunciate it\nand it will spread by its own vitality.\n\n\n\"This is one of the most interesting\nreligious phenomena that has ever\npassed over the country, and has been\nsaid to be compared only with the\n. first years of Christianity.\n\n\n''It seems to be a movement of the\nspirit of God and is spreading largely\noutside of the churches. Wherever we\ngo we find people who are dissatisfied\nand are trying to carry out the Chris\ntian religion.\n\n\n'It is our attempt to carry out the\nprinciples of the gospel of Christ. We\nbelieve that is all necessary for men.\nWe do not want to form a new sect\nor a new cult. but to go back to the\nold religion. This religion of ours, we\ndo not get much good from it because\nwe do not apply it to our lives. This\nsounds strange to say, but it is true.\n\n\n\"In religions behind which there is\nonly one personality, as in our own\nreligion and Buddhism, there comes a\ntime when the founder is forgotten.\nThen it is necessary to go back to the\nfounder and study his life and princi-\npies. Something of that kind came at\nthe time of the Protestant reformation,\nand, unless all signs fail, this is an\n'other reformation in the hearts of the\npeople. The hearts of the people are\nstirred, and there is a vital movement\nat present.\n\n\nScientific Movement\n\n\nalong with this there is an interest\ning scientific and psychological move\nment. We have learned through psy-\nchology many things. We have learned\nto draw on a source of power and to\ngain great physical endurance. Today\nit is possible and easy for me to do\ntwice the work that I did ten years\nago.\n\n\nThere is an ability in a man to\ndraw on a power iii excess of ordinary\nwants. What the nature of that power\nis I cannot say. 1t is spiritual and\nis found within US. I believe the soul\nof man and the spirit of God inSepara-\nBILE. We are parts of God in his spir-\nitual nature. In saying the kingdom\nof God is within u.s., Jesus meant the\nspirit Of God is within you.\n\n\nLet US consider how to tap this\nreservoir. Take the terrible event OF\nthe San Francisco earthquake. That\nshows it in two ways. in the individual\nand in the community. | was per\nfectly amazed at the recuperation OF\nthat city. So much of the city has\nbeen. rebuilt, but if they had simply\nl~-nented that city would be a waste\nplace today. I was talking with a\ndoctor who came east soon after the\ndisaster. He had lost all, home, H.\nDrary, instruments and his patients\nHe simply said it would be his joy tC\nbegin again. Fate has no power over\nsuch people.\n\n\nIl have talked with many people\nwho were in that disaster and have\nbeen told how they were on their feet\nal. day and did not feel fatigued. What\nwas the cause? 1t was an unusual\ncircumstance that enabled the people\nto draw on a power which they are\nordinarily not able to reach.\n\n\nIn many events~of life, in the un-\nusual, we are able to draw on some\nsource of power and do what otherwise\nwould be impossible. If we could only\nlearn how to make use of this at al\n4 times, how different it would be! Il\nwe could make our life in Gods life\nwhat blessing it would be to US!\n\n\nRare Moments in Life\n\n\n1--WE owe what moral and spiritua\nstrength we have through life to those\nrare moments when God spoke to us\nand our hearts spoke to God. Wher\nwe turn to Jesus Christ we see the\ndifference. Christ's life in God was\nnot an intermittent thing. In the Ser\nmon OH the Mount, we see how Jesus\nsought to find God in every event OF\nlife. The only stand that he was will\ning to propose to his disciples was\nBE ye therefore perfect, even as your\nFather in heaven is perfect\n\n\nLet me show what the realization OF\nGod in our lives would mean, anC\nwhat it would do for US. First, it would\n\n=== 13_1836-05-06_pna_sn84024735_00414184091_1836050601_0452.json\nRichmond enquirer. [volume] (Richmond, Va.) 1815-1867 · 1836-05-06\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nand that the movement intended is advisable.\n\n\n(SgnedD f. THORN,\n\n\nri. RAGGED,\n\n\nj. ROBERTS,\n\n\nc. \". SIMMS,\n\n\nCounter signcd) A. HOTC1IKiSS.\nNew O