 Denied. T.e Editor of the DEMOCRAT:\n1476\t\n1477\t A sensational article in yesterdays Globe stated that the occupants of my house on Baldwin street were in a state of excitement over the alleged appearance of a ghost. The article is false in every particular. None of the six families who live in the house know anything about the matter. Mrs Florence Sullivan. men, toned Tn the story. denies ever having re ceived a supernatural visit or having seen or heavd anything unusual in the house The statement that remained on watch and heard the noises is untrue. Indeed. never watched in the house. In short ths whole story is malicious and without tns least shadow of truth.\n1478\t\n1479\t DANIEL O'CoSsoR.\n1480\t\n1481\t=== 8_1887-11-19_pna_sn88064537_00295876190_1887111901_0785.json\n1482\tSt. Landry democrat. (Opelousas, La.) 1878-1894 · 1887-11-19\n1483\tcrops: cached\n1484\theadline: AN EVENING WITH THE SPIRITS\n1485\t\n1486\tSome Specimen Communications Obtained\n1487\t\n1488\t\n1489\tThrough Gifted Medium.\n1490\t---\n1491\tSome Specimen Communications Obtained\n1492\t\n1493\t\n1494\tThrough Gifted Medium.\n1495\t\n1496\t\n1497\tSince attending a seance few nights ago\n1498\tam firm believer that spiritualism is a big\n1499\tthing.\n1500\t\n1501\t\n1502\tThe female who had the ghost in charge\n1503\t(the visitors were charged. too) was a\n1504\tmedium sized woman, of the Queen Anne\n1505\torder of architecture, with cross eyes and a\n1506\ttemper to match.\n1507\t\n1508\t\n1509\tShe said the conditions were favorable, and\n1510\tthat a choice assortment of spirits had\n1511\tarrived, and were prepared to do their best.\n1512\tThe way the spirits were put through their\n1513\ttraces was this:\n1514\t\n1515\t\n1516\tThe medium sat on one side of the table\n1517\tand the thirster after spiritual lore occupied\n1518\talow-lying stool on the other. Both clasped\n1519\tslate which lay on the table, and on this\n1520\tslate the spirits displayed their chirographi-\n1521\tcal ability, or rather their inability.\n1522\t\n1523\t\n1524\t1t was necessary to write a question on a\n1525\tslip of paper, roll it up in the shape of sugar\n1526\tcoated pill, and let the medium touch it\n1527\twith her cold, clammy finger. Whereupon\n1528\tthe spirit, if he understood his business,\n1529\twould place his answer ON the slate.\n1530\t\n1531\t\n1532\tA few of the questions and answers are ap-\n1533\tpended:\n1534\t\n1535\t\n1536\t\"Mr. Bacon-Esteemed lord: Did you\n1537\twrite Shakspeares plays?\"\n1538\t\n1539\t\n1540\tAnswer-\"Yes, | wrote them plays after\n1541\tnight, when my other work was DONE Bill\n1542\tpromised to pay me three pun ten apiece\n1543\tfor 'em, and he hain't paid me yet.-BAcoN\"\n1544\tcivilian Shakespeare, Esq.-Dear Bill:\n1545\tRapnn him &c\n1546\tthe plays he wrote for you. How is this?''\n1547\tAnswer\" Bacon is a bass. Didn't\n1548\tpromise him nothing for writing no plays.\n1549\twrote 'em myself on typewriter. and /\n1550\tcan lick the man who says l didn't. Il.\n1551\tDonnelly is crank and will come to some\n1552\tbad end-WM. SHAKESPEARE\"\n1553\t\n1554\t\n1555\tCaptain Kidd - Respected mariner\n1556\tWhere did you bury your treasurer\"\n1557\t\n1558\t\n1559\tAnswer\" Never buried any treasure\n1560\tHad none to bury: Just before died l\n1561\tpaid a plumbers bill.-KIDD'\n1562\t\n1563\t\n1564\t\"Mr. Franklin-Dear BENJAMIN: Is it\n1565\ttrue that you did not discover lightning I'M\n1566\ttil after you were married?\"\n1567\t\n1568\t\n1569\tAnswer-\"Rats BENZ. FRANKLIN\"\n1570\t\n1571\t\n1572\tBenedict Arnold-Sir: How is the traitor\n1573\tindustry? Is it hot enough for you2''\n1574\t\n1575\t\n1576\tAnswer-\"Go to Tophet.-B. A.\"\n1577\t\n1578\t\n1579\tWhile these questions were being an\n1580\tswered in such a satisfactory manner it\n1581\tseemed that mob of spirits, not specially\n1582\tasked were hustling each other around the\n1583\ttable. Some of them were evidently afraid\n1584\tthat no questions would be asked them, and\n1585\tso they kindly volunteered some choice bits\n1586\tof information, from which the following\n1587\tare selected:\n1588\t\n1589\t\n1590\teye man who invented We little Ratchet\n1591\tstory is liar, and We truth is not in him.\n1592\tG. WASHINGTON\"\n1593\t\n1594\t\n1595\tI'VE 10 very happy here.-L1NDLEY MUIR\n1596\tRAY\"\n1597\t\n1598\t\n1599\tII am lerning tn spel.-JosH BILLINGS\n1600\tThe medium said it was very seldom SO\n1601\tlarge company Of spirits could be induced\n1602\tto talk at once, and it really seemed as\n1603\tthough the seance was a success.\n1604\t\n1605\t=== 15_1920-06-27_pna_sn85042345_00237284331_1920062701_0491.json\n1606\tThe Morning Tulsa daily world. [volume] (Tulsa, Okla.) 1919-1927 · 1920-06-27\n1607\tcrops: cached\n1608\theadline: \n1609\t---\n1610\tFIRST UNIVERsAIl--slxth and\n1611\tBoulder, county courtroom, at 7:t5.\n1612\tTrance lecture by Mrs. Amanda Mof-\n1613\tfit, spirit messages by Marie Hutch\n1614\terson. Laura Perryman and G. I.\n1615\tMartin. Ladies' auxiliary meets\n1616\tWednesday with Mrs. Perrymsn, 214\n1617\tSouth Guthrie,\n1618\t"}]},"uuid":"3f6e2d2a-46af-456c-9865-4e66bd0d7a77","timestamp":"2026-10-31T07:54:20.412Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/c1/g09.txt","content":"=== 5_1898-02-02_p1_sn86063034_00414182239_1898020201_0340.json\nThe morning news. [volume] (Savannah, Ga.) 1887-1900 · 1898-02-02\ncrops: cached\nheadline: LowIands of Georgia.\n---\nMiles and long miles of the raggedest\ngrasses,\nScraggy palmetto scrub, staring white\n\n\nsand,\nSleepy black pools and gray sedgy NOR\n\n\nraises:\nWould you not think this desolate land?\nAll, but it is not! A stranger might view\n\n\nit so,\nCareless, cold eyes of tourist pursue it\nBut if you tramped it, and loved it, and\n\n\nknew it, so\nClose as a friends face or own firesde\nThen you would know that whatever be-\n\n\ntide.\nLowlands of Georgia, these Lowlands of\n\n\nGeorgia.\nLiving, would haunt you, and after you've\ndied\n\n\nYour heart would turn back to the Low\n\n\nlands.\nClimb to the crown of the stateliest noun\n\n\ntain\nWrapped in the ermine such kings alone\n\n\nwear,\nFeast fun your soul at sublimitys foun-\n\n\ntain,\nWEAKNESS follows this over-rich fare.\nThen you'll remember them, hunger for\n\n\nweary for,\nLike \"a lost eaglet his lone, lofty eyrie for.\nSigning, 20 Lowlands, again to be near\nyer' for\nCalm through the pine-tree aisles breathe\nthe soft breeze,\nLow laps the pool round the big cypress\nknees.\nLowlands of Georgia, o Lowlands Of Geor-\ngia!\nHeart-rest is wrapped in such memories\nAs rest in the heart of the Lowlands.\nBrown in the shadow and bronze in the\nsun lie\nLong lengths of pine needles, have you yet\nseen\nCarpets more rich or more radiant spun by\nLooms of the Orient for tread of a Queen?\nO, for the reaches of sun-lighted dis\ntances!\nWhere the eye sees, and the eager ears list\nfancies,\nWhile in soft, slow fluctuations the mist\ndances\nOver the rice fields of silver and green.\nGreen over silver. you know the soft sheen.\nLowlands of Georgia, o, Lowlands of\nGeorgia!\nAll your true lovers know all it doth mean\no, true throbbing heart of the Lowlands!\nYet, dear Lowlands, We need not this\npoor song,\nThough from fond heart in exile it\nflows,\nMeody's masterpiece, no less is your\nsons,\nSung by the mockblrd where jessamine\nblows.\no, for the jessamine vine, and the fra\ngrances\nFolding us round through a summer davis\nvagrances,\no, for day of the bloom thieving fla\ngrancies\nStealing the gold from the jessamine vine.\nRdthlessly robbing these treasures of\nthine\nLowlands of Georgia. o. Lowlands of\nGeorgia.\nOnce we have known We, what wonder we\npine\n\n\nAgain to be with you o Lowiandsi\n\n\nMinnie Mosher jackson.\n\n=== 36_1869-12-25_pna_sn82014296_00296022172_1869122501_0143.json\nThe Evansville journal. [volume] (Evansville, Ind.) 1866-1870 · 1869-12-25\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nSEANcEs.-The Won Bomelsweitzen\nBrothers will give two \"Seances\nDiabolique\" at their hall, on Main\nStreet, this afternoon and tonight.\nThey propose to do more wonderful\nthings to day than at previous seaDe es.\nThe sudden changing of coats, STOW\ning of hands. and the playing of SIX\ninstruments all at the same time, as\nperformed by these brothers, is really\nwonderful, They will also allow the\ncommittee to sit in the cabinet with\nthem during the seance. See the no\ntice in local column.\n\n=== 10_1921-11-17_p1_sn82014248_00513683017_1921111701_0499.json\nDaily Kennebec journal. [microfilm reel] (Augusta, Me.) 1870-1975 · 1921-11-17\ncrops: cached\nheadline: The Phantom Fleet\n---\n(Ncw York Times\n\n\nIn Homer's account in the Iliad are\ndrawn up on the enemy shore-ships\nby number with black ribs, or dark\nhulls, or scarlet prows. But list\nthat would better deserve to be re-\nmembered by future history and epic\nverse would be the roster of ships\ndragged out of the deep and de-\nstroyed or kept from launching as a\nresult of this conference.-the\n'scrapped.\" the unborn. or the I'M\nchristened ships-the Phantom Fleet\nthat will help to keep the peace Of\nthe world.\n\n=== 10_1906-08-09_p1_sn83030272_10048125A_1906080901_0592.json\nThe sun. [volume] (New York [N.Y.]) 1833-1916 · 1906-08-09\ncrops: cached\nheadline: suuer,or Virtue Of America.\n---\n70 rRR EDTros or TEE SuN-Sir: Answer\ning Moses oppenheimer. I am glad 10 say\nthat something has -established among us\na higher standard Of purity aud virtue a oom-\npared, for instance. with those Of Germany.\nfrance or Italy\"-and I may d England.\nIn America obscenity does not Haunt itself.\nUnspeakable photographs are not peddled on\nthe streets. Art students themselves do not\nrun naked through the city. Here II q vtsitor\nwants nasdness he must hunt for It; u 10 not\nobtruded.\n\n\nII Mr.. Comstock ls neasuYably responsible\nfor the difference he 10 entitled TO the thanks\nand encouragement Of the American people.\n\n\nII may be that the Law under which he\noperates has In some instances been used\noppressively or for the purposes Of black\nmail. Almost every law can be perverted.\nA wise law must be wisely administered, Or.\nlike ~vaulting ambition. It oerisaps itself and\nfAlls on tho other side.\n\n\n! make n comment On Mr.. CoDetooks\nrecent raid, bue ] cay generally that thsre u\nd deal moro cant about tho saeredness Of Art\nthan 10 justified.\n\n\nII Il true, Of oourss. that 10 ths pure IN heart\nall things sre pure: but purity OF heart de-\nponds upon purity Of thought. and Thoughts.\nunfortunately. depend upon whatsver cause\navakens them. =.\n\n\nNEW York. Au gust 7.\n\n=== 4_1886-03-11_p1_sn83045239_00295876608_1886031101_0234.json\nEcho de l&#39;ouest. [volume] (Minneapolis, Minn.) 1883-1929 · 1886-03-11\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Extrait dune correspondence\nd. Ottawa adress6e a PElecteur de\nQuebec.\n---\nExtrait dune correspondence\nd'Ottawa adress6e q IElectew de\nQuebec.\n\n\nSamedi soir a Gu lieu au S6nat\nlie grand lever que lie gouverneur\ndome q l'ou,erture de chaque SEES\nsion. Un DIES 6v6nements de CO\nlever a etc l'apparition de Lady\nMacdonald, ayec une parure en-\niiamants que Went de lui bonner\nSir George Stephens. On dit que\ncette parure a codt6 q Londres trois\nmile louis sterling ($l5,000) ct\nellie parait les valolr. Lady Mac\ndonald portrait aussi une broche\nform6e de la derniere fche plantee\nsur le Pacifque. Cette fche est\nen- or orn6 de brillants. Inutile de\ndire que, tout en- admirant cette\nparure dune richesse toute orien-\ntale, les autres danes regrettaient\nde n'avoir pas pour maris DIES hon.\nmes en- etat de donner cent millions\nde pi%astres q ceux qui reconnais-\nsent 81 replacement les faveurs qui\nleur ont etc faites a meme la caisse\npublique. Je Grains beaucoup que\nIa fche de Lady Macdonald, toute\nMAGNITUDE qu'elle so