ume] (Elko, Nev.) 188?-1915 · 1912-01-24\ncrops: cached\nheadline: BEST PLAY EVER\n\n\nBOOKED IN ELKO\n---\nThe continental musical success\n\"Alma Where DO You Liver which\nscored such a great hit iE Paris,\nBerlin and other European cities.\nwill be seen for the first time at\nthe Bradley Opera House. Thurs\nday, Feb. LIST. The book is by Paul\nHerve, the music by Jean Briquet,\nand the English translation is by\nGeorge v. Hobart. The comedy\nis said to be of the most whole\nsome variety. the melodies are of\nthe haunting kind. The \"Alma\"\nwaltz which runs all through the\nplay is said to outrival THE Mer-\nry Widow\" waltz. Nannette Flack,\nCharles A. Murray, Aubrey Yates,\nand spendid cast of players will\nbe seen in the different roles.\n\n\nEvery musical number in\nalma\" is a hit in itself. Some of\nthe titles are \"Alma\" which is the\nrecurrent waltz song of the\npiece; \"Girlies\" Never More;\"\n\"Boo-Hoo-Hoo;\" Childhood Days\"\n\"B ogie Boo,\" 1.-THE Land of\nBeautiful Dreams\" \"Sail Home\"\nkiss Me-\" My Love-\" and Love\nMe. THE lyrics have all been done\ninto English by George V. Hobart,\nthe lIbrettist of the American\nversion.\n\n=== 29_1894-09-24_p4_sn94053256_00271762963_1894092401_0306.json\nWaterbury evening Democrat. · 1894-09-24\ncrops: cached\nheadline: LETTERS FROM THEPEoPGE\n\nA Sensational Ghost Story. Printed h\n\n Sunday Paper, Denied. Is\n---\n. Sunday Paper. Denied. T.e Editor of the DEMOCRAT:\n\n A sensational article in yesterdays Globe stated that the occupants of my house on Baldwin street were in a state of excitement over the alleged appearance of a ghost. The article is false in every particular. None of the six families who live in the house know anything about the matter. Mrs Florence Sullivan. men, toned Tn the story. denies ever having re ceived a supernatural visit or having seen or heavd anything unusual in the house The statement that remained on watch and heard the noises is untrue. Indeed. never watched in the house. In short ths whole story is malicious and without tns least shadow of truth.\n\n DANIEL O'CoSsoR.\n\n=== 8_1887-11-19_pna_sn88064537_00295876190_1887111901_0785.json\nSt. Landry democrat. (Opelousas, La.) 1878-1894 · 1887-11-19\ncrops: cached\nheadline: AN EVENING WITH THE SPIRITS\n\nSome Specimen Communications Obtained\n\n\nThrough Gifted Medium.\n---\nSome Specimen Communications Obtained\n\n\nThrough Gifted Medium.\n\n\nSince attending a seance few nights ago\nam firm believer that spiritualism is a big\nthing.\n\n\nThe female who had the ghost in charge\n(the visitors were charged. too) was a\nmedium sized woman, of the Queen Anne\norder of architecture, with cross eyes and a\ntemper to match.\n\n\nShe said the conditions were favorable, and\nthat a choice assortment of spirits had\narrived, and were prepared to do their best.\nThe way the spirits were put through their\ntraces was this:\n\n\nThe medium sat on one side of the table\nand the thirster after spiritual lore occupied\nalow-lying stool on the other. Both clasped\nslate which lay on the table, and on this\nslate the spirits displayed their chirographi-\ncal ability, or rather their inability.\n\n\n1t was necessary to write a question on a\nslip of paper, roll it up in the shape of sugar\ncoated pill, and let the medium touch it\nwith her cold, clammy finger. Whereupon\nthe spirit, if he understood his business,\nwould place his answer ON the slate.\n\n\nA few of the questions and answers are ap-\npended:\n\n\n\"Mr. Bacon-Esteemed lord: Did you\nwrite Shakspeares plays?\"\n\n\nAnswer-\"Yes, | wrote them plays after\nnight, when my other work was DONE Bill\npromised to pay me three pun ten apiece\nfor 'em, and he hain't paid me yet.-BAcoN\"\ncivilian Shakespeare, Esq.-Dear Bill:\nRapnn him &c\nthe plays he wrote for you. How is this?''\nAnswer\" Bacon is a bass. Didn't\npromise him nothing for writing no plays.\nwrote 'em myself on typewriter. and /\ncan lick the man who says l didn't. Il.\nDonnelly is crank and will come to some\nbad end-WM. SHAKESPEARE\"\n\n\nCaptain Kidd - Respected mariner\nWhere did you bury your treasurer\"\n\n\nAnswer\" Never buried any treasure\nHad none to bury: Just before died l\npaid a plumbers bill.-KIDD'\n\n\n\"Mr. Franklin-Dear BENJAMIN: Is it\ntrue that you did not discover lightning I'M\ntil after you were married?\"\n\n\nAnswer-\"Rats BENZ. FRANKLIN\"\n\n\nBenedict Arnold-Sir: How is the traitor\nindustry? Is it hot enough for you2''\n\n\nAnswer-\"Go to Tophet.-B. A.\"\n\n\nWhile these questions were being an\nswered in such a satisfactory manner it\nseemed that mob of spirits, not specially\nasked were hustling each other around the\ntable. Some of them were evidently afraid\nthat no questions would be asked them, and\nso they kindly volunteered some choice bits\nof information, from which the following\nare selected:\n\n\neye man who invented We little Ratchet\nstory is liar, and We truth is not in him.\nG. WASHINGTON\"\n\n\nI'VE 10 very happy here.-L1NDLEY MUIR\nRAY\"\n\n\nII am lerning tn spel.-JosH BILLINGS\nThe medium said it was very seldom SO\nlarge company Of spirits could be induced\nto talk at once, and it really seemed as\nthough the seance was a success.\n\n=== 15_1920-06-27_pna_sn85042345_00237284331_1920062701_0491.json\nThe Morning Tulsa daily world. [volume] (Tulsa, Okla.) 1919-1927 · 1920-06-27\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nFIRST UNIVERsAIl--slxth and\nBoulder, county courtroom, at 7:t5.\nTrance lecture by Mrs. Amanda Mof-\nfit, spirit messages by Marie Hutch\nerson. Laura Perryman and G. I.\nMartin. Ladies' auxiliary meets\nWednesday with Mrs. Perrymsn, 214\nSouth Guthrie,\n","numLines":1618,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1618}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2e8d1df6-7301-4d15-b489-cf8134ce05b7","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"other"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId"