le servant OF The\n1333\tcommon veal, let commerce be fosiered and\n1334\tprotected; but let not the independence of\n1335\tAmerica be sacrificed in mercantile specu-\n1336\tlation /; let not her distinguished immuni\n1337\tties and ineStimable liberties be the booked\n1338\tand legered items in the account of foreign\n1339\ttrade.\n1340\t\n1341\t\n1342\tThe progressive Improvement of domes\n1343\tTIC manufactures is Cause OF present ga\n1344\ttulation and rejoicing. Numerous hills\n1345\twritten with f1eeces; unnumoered values la\n1346\tDor with plenty the plains wave with lux\n1347\turiant harves. $; the teeming earth disc. SEES\n1348\ther secret treasures; universal abundance\n1349\tiHvites the artist and the arts. b, remuv\n1350\ting the necessity of resnrtng to foreign\n1351\tchimes for supplies of the conveniences If\n1352\tlite, domestic manufactures promote the\n1353\t\n1354\t\n1355\t\n1356\t\n1357\t\n1358\tstrengthen the bonds of UNION between\n1359\tthe confederate states. by establishing inter\n1360\tnal commerce and increasing their mutual\n1361\treliance.\n1362\t\n1363\t\n1364\tWhile the tempest of war has desolaJ ed\n1365\tthe natiors while the whirlwind of destruc\n1366\tton has wasted the Kingdoms, and overturn\n1367\ted the thrones of Europe while the sword\n1368\thas consumed the people. and a deluge OF\n1369\tblood has drenched their fields. and over\n1370\tflowed with polluted streams our native\n1371\tskies have continued serene and fair Peace\n1372\thas inhabited our borders, and security\n1373\tdwelt in our towns. Our civil privileges\n1374\twhich were achieved by valor. have been\n1375\tpreserved by wisdom. Our comforts which\n1376\twere gained by enterprise, have been IN\n1377\tcreased by industry. Our institutions which\n1378\twere founded by hberali y. have been fos\n1379\ttered by munihcence. The principles Of our\n1380\tconstitution of government rsmain uncaang\n1381\ted !; and so long as they shall endure, and\n1382\tthe integrity OF the federal union sh al CONT\n1383\tnue. unimpaired, Americans may rejoice in\n1384\tthe freedom, sovereignty & independence Of\n1385\tthe u 5. They shall endure forever. BY\n1386\tour brave fathers memories, by the awful\n1387\tshades of revolutionary martyrs, swear,\n1388\tthey shall endure forever,-f for, . though\n1389\tIndividuals may perish, truth is eternal.\n1390\tThe rude blasts or tyranny may blow from\n1391\tevery quarter, out freedom is marcy plant,\n1392\tthat viii survive the tempest, and strike\n1393\tan everlasting root in the most unfavorable\n1394\tsoil''\n1395\t\n1396\t\n1397\tColumbia, favorite of heaven. and hope OF\n1398\tthe world Rejoice Nature and Art unite\n1399\tto adorn thee! Wealth is thy handmade\n1400\tand wisdom and honor attend thy steps\n1401\tThe eagle will SOAR to the stars, and build her\n1402\teyry, where danger cannot climb. Nations\n1403\twill seek shelter under the covert of her\n1404\twings. The corners of the earth will bound\n1405\tthy power, and the confines of eternity limit\n1406\tthy glory\n1407\t"}]},"uuid":"51b71d37-9930-4bba-856b-64766753328b","timestamp":"2026-10-31T07:56:20.505Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/c1/g10.txt","content":"=== 12_1848-05-06_p1_sn83016957_00200292443_1848050601_0502.json\nBoon&#39;s Lick times. [volume] (Fayette, Mo.) 1840-1848 · 1848-05-06\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Vol. D.\n\nTO THE EVENING ZEPHYR.\n\nBY MISS ALICE CAREY.\n---\n| sit where the wild bee i, humming,\nAnd listen in vain for thy song;\nI've waited before for thy coming,\nBut never, oh never so long\nHow Oft with the blue sky above us.\n\n\nAnd waves breaking light on the shore,\nThou, knowing they would not reprove us,\nHast kissed me thousand times Ger\nSo sweet were thy dewy embraces,\n\n\nThy falsity who could believe\nSome phantom thy fondness effaces-\n\n\nThou could'st not have aimed to deceived\nThou toldest thy love for me never,\n\n\nBut all the brigh: stars in the skies,\nthough striving TO do so forever,\n\n\nCould scarcely have numbered thy signs.\nAlone in the gathering shadows\n\n\nStill waiting sweet Zephyr, for the.\nlook for the waves OF the meadows.\n\n\nAnd dimples to blot the blue sea.\nThe blossoms tina. waited TO greet thee\n\n\nWith the heat Of the noontide oppres,,\nNow futter SO lightly to meet thee,\n\n\nThou'r. coming. know, from the West.\nAlas! if thou fndest me pouting,\n\n\nIT'S only my love that alarms\nForgive, then, pray thee, my doubling,\n\n\nAnd take me once more to thy arms!\n\n=== 20_1883-01-06_p1_sn83016348_00415624578_1883010601_0367.json\nThe Cecil Whig. · 1883-01-06\ncrops: cached\nheadline: MATERIAL ApPARITlONS.\n---\nMhlEKlAL ApPARlTlONS\n\n\nEnvelopes were first used in 1899.\n\n\nAnathesia was discovered in 1874.\n\n\nThe first steel pen was made in 1890\n\n\nThe first air pump Was made in 1854,\n\n\nThe first luclfer match was made in\n1898.\n\n\nMohammed WAS born at Mecca about\n$40.\n\n\nThe first iron steamship was built in\n1890.\n\n\nThe first balloon ascent was made in\n1898.\n\n\nThe first advertisement appeared in\n1862.\n\n\nCoaches were first used in England in\n1863.\n\n\nThe first steel plate was discovered in\n1880.\n\n\nThe first horse railroad was built in\n1861.\n\n\nThe Francscaus arrived in England in\n1924.\n\n\nThe first steamboat piled the Hudson\nin 1907.\n\n\nThe entire Hebrew Bible was printed\nin 14Ss.\n\n\nShips were first copper bottomed -\nin 1873.\n\n\nGold was first discovered in Cali fornia\nin lS4s.\n\n\nIA\" Hrst telescope was used in England\nin 160S\n\n\nChristianity was introduced into Japan\nin 1919.\n\n\nThe first watches were made at Nuren-\nburg in i4111.\n\n\nFirst almanac printed by Geo. Won Fur\nbach in l4G0\n\n\nomnibuses We're first introduced in New\nYork in 1880.\n\n\nPercussion arms were used in the L. s.\nArmy in 1920.\n\n\nThe first copper cent was coined in New\nHaven in 1851.\n\n\nKerosene was first used for lighting\npurposes in 1896\n\n\nFirst saw makers anvil was brought TO\nAmerica in 1919.\n\n\nThe first use of a locomotive in this\ncountry was in 1929.\n\n\nThe first glass factory in the United\nStates was built in 1880.\n\n\nThe first printing press in the United\nStates was worked in 1920.\n\n\nGlass windows were first introduced\nnto England in the eighth century.\n\n\nThe first steam engine on this continent\nvas brought from England in 1853.\n\n\nThe first complete sewing machine was\n'atented by Elias Howe. Jr., in 1916.\n\n\nThe first sociery for the promotion of\nhrlstian knowledge was organized in\n;G9S.\n\n\nThe 'irst attempt to manufacture pins\nthis country was made soon after the\nvar of 1912.\n\n\nThe first prayer book of Edward vi.\n'ame into use by authority of Parliament\nn Whitsunday 1549.\n\n\nThe first temperance society in this\nouDtry was organized in Saratoga county\n\\. Y, in March, 1908.\n\n\nThe first coach in Scotland was brought\n'hither in 1861, when Queen Mary came\nrom France. 1t belong to Alexander\nLord Seaton.\n\n\nThe first daily newspaper appeared in\nl102. The first newspaper printed in the\nUnited States was published in Boston\nOh September %, 1890.\n\n\nThe manufacture of porcelain was in\ntroduced into the providence of Helm,\nJapan, from China, in 1913, and Helm\nware still bears Chinese marks.\n\n\nThe first society for the exclusive pur-\npose of circulating the Bible was organ\nzed in 1905, under the name of the\nBritish and Foreign Bible Society.\nThe first telegraphic instrument was\nsuccessfully operated by S. F. Z. Morse,\nthe inventor, in 1885, though it's utility\nwas no. demonstrated to the world until\n1912,\n\n\nThe first union flag was unfurIed on\nhe lst of January, 1916, over the camp\nCambridge. 1t had thirteen stripes of\nwhite and red, and retained the English\nross in one corner.\n\n\nWhen Captain Cook first visited Tahiti\noone, shell and stone. When they saw\nron nails they fancied them to be shoots\n# very hard wood and, desirous of Sc\n'uring such valuable commodity, they\nplanted them in their gardens\n\n=== 6_1881-11-05_p1_sn85026455_00202198041_1881110501_0155.json\nNorthern tribune. [volume] (Cheboygan, Mich.) 1875-1885 · 1881-11-05\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nsaddle on has broke loose. Will some\nbrother head the animal for no sister\ncan walk home this hot dayp\"\n\n\nQuiet being restored he continued:\n\"Well, my brethren, will now try to\nsay what allowed to about the Presby-\nterians.\n\n\n\"As | said before, they raise their\nchildren heap better than we do. They\nbehave better in church, and keep Sun\nday better, and read the Bible and larn\nthe Catechism better than ours do.\ndeclare, my brethren, their children are\ntaught that Westminster Catechism by\nthe time they began to talk plain.\n\n\n1T amt three weeks since was out\ncattle huntin'-for two of my yearlin's\nhad strayed Off-and l stopped at old\nBrother Harkey's, on Mud creek, and\ntook dinner. He's deacon in the Pres\nbyterian church over thar. Well, as\ntrue as stand here. brethren, Sister\nHarkey had her little gal standin'\nright before her, with toes just even\nwith the crack o. the floor, and her hands\nwas hangin' down by her side, and\nher mouth turned up like chicken\nwhen it drinks; and she was putin\nthis question to her out O. that Cate\nchism: & A ~\n\n\n\"'What are the benefits which in this\nlife do either accompany or flow from\njustification, adoption and sanctiaca-\ntiOn.'\n\n\n\"Now, the question itself was enough\nto break the child down. But when she\nhad to begin and say that question all\nover fOr that's the way it was in the\nbook and then hitch the answer to it,\nand which all put together made this:\n\"The benefits which in this life do either\naccompany or flow from justification,\nadoption and sanctifcatiOn are peace of\nconscience, joy in the Holy Ghost, in\ncrease of grace, and perseverance there\nin to the end'-I thought the child was\nthe greatest wonder I'd ever seen in all\nmy life. She tuck it right through, too,\nwithout balkin' or misSin' the first word.\nAnd she spoke so sweet and she looked so\nlike little angel that before l knOwed\nit the tears were runnin' down my\ncheeks as big as buckshot. I've seen the\nday when l could have mauled and split\nthOusan' rails quicker and easier than\n] could have larnt that thing and said\n1t off like she did.\n\n\n''NOw, my brethren, that child didn't\nunderstand or know the meanin' of one\nword O. that. 1t put me up to all knew\nto take it in myself. But just let that\nPresbyterian young one grow up, and\nevery word of that catecism will come\nback to her, and her character will stiff\nfen up under it, and she'll have the\nbackbone of the matter in her for life.\n\n\n\"NOw, / can't put things into nay chill\ndren that way. NOthin' don't stay, any\nhow. It's like drivin' nail into a rot\nten log\"\n\n\nThis last remark l never forget. For\n80 years afterward, as would stand at\nthe blackboard trying to fix rules and\nprinciples in the mind of the dull pupil,\nthis remark would come back to me\nwith its peculiar pertinenency.\n\n=== 8_1903-04-09_pna_sn82016014_00295871088_1903040901_0082.json\nThe Topeka state journal. [volume] (Topeka, Kansas) 1892-1980 · 1903-04-09\ncrops: cached\nheadline: FUNK WANTS A BOUNCER.\n\nBesieged by Tons of Letters About\n\n\nthe Beecher Ghost.\n---\nNew York, April 9.-Rev. DR. Isaac\nK. Funk. head of the publishing firm Of\nFunk & Wagnalls, No 80 Lafayette\nplace. wants a bouncer and a secretary\ndue to his interesting interviews with\nthe ghost of Henry Ward Beecher.\n\n\nDr. Funk said yesterday 1T will\nprobably be necessary for me to en-\nsage an additional secretary just to at\ntend to the tons of letters from pro\nnoters and people with troubles which\nhave poured in upon me since I told of\nthe remarkable demonstration in which\nthe spirit of Mr.. Beecher reminded me\nof Professor Vests widows mite The\nriphtful owner Of the coin is. Mr.. West\nclaims the coin as executor of his faith\ner's estate. I am not sure that he IS\nentitled to it. The purchaser of the\ncollection. which Mr.. West sold for\nST. 600 after his fathers death, may\nhave paid that amount with the belief\nthat the mite was still in it.\n\n=== 16_1912-08-22_pna_sn84037844_00415664928_1912082201_0672.json\nThe Clarksburg telegram. [volume] (Clarksburg, W. Va.) 1874-1926 · 1912-08-22\ncrops: cached\nheadline: sunny JIM\"\n---\nUUWlvl UIM\n\n\nCONTINUED from page one.)\n\n\n\n\n\na spirit of self abnegation never wit\nmessed since the charge of the Light\nBrigade at Balaklava-a Theirs not\nto reason why, theirs but to do and\ndie sort of exaltation,-led by the\nGrand Young Man from Indiana. de-\nvout but tuneful, the assembled vas\nsails proclaimed their joyous intellec-\ntual surr(nder to the feudal lord in\nthe following hymn of driveling\nresponsibility:\n\n\nFollow, follow\n\n\nWe will follow Roosevelt,\n\n\nAnywhere, everywhere\n\n\nWe will follow him.\n\n\nFollow, follow,\n\n\nWe will follow Roosevelt,\n\n\nAnywhere he leads US,\n\n\nWe will follow on.\n\n\nAll of which being chanted to the\nravishing air Of that stirring ditty,\nentitled We don't know where we're\ngoing, but we're on the way brought\nthe multitude into such a state of\nblind and benighted ldolatry that au-\nthentic information to the effect that\nthe colonel had just waylaid per-\nfectly respectable minister of the gos-\npei and robbed him of his last months\ndonations would have brought forth\nenthusiastic cheers for the colonel\nand vote of stern condemnation for\nthe man of God as the representative\nof dangerous and Ubiquitous pluto\ncracy.\n\n\n\"In form two thousand delegates,\nmore or less, gathered in the Col\niseum; in reality Mr.. Roosevelt met\nin convention at Chicago, made a\nconfession of faith, gave his hand to\nthe colored brother from the North\nand his foot to the colored brother\nfrom the South, adopted a platform,\nnominated himself and Brother john\nson, and adjourned with the ease Of\na thoroughly trained thimblerigger\nplying his vocation among the rural\nvisitors to the midway plaisance.\n\n\nTHE campaign upon which we are\nabout, to enter presents issues OF\nmore serious moment to the Ameri-\ncan people than any they have con-\nfronted since the grave questions\nwhich Immediately preceded and ac-\ncompanicd the Civil War. The over\nshadowing question then was whether\nthe Union, under the Constitution,\ncould be perpetu]ted; that which\nconfronts us today is whether the\nConstitution itself, and the govern\nment which the Constitution estab-\nlished, shall be preserved-a ques-\nton of equal if not greater gravity,\nsince it would be of little avail to\nhave preserved the Union from the\nchaos of disintegration if the govern\nment Of the Union is to be given over\nto the chaos of disorganation.\n\n\n1.-The party to which we belong, Mr..\nVice President, stands in the supreme\ncontest for the independence aud in\ntegrity of the judicial tribunals of the\nland, without which the guaranty of\nlife, liberty, and property would be a\nmeaningless platitude. 1t stands for\nthe settled rule of impersonal gov\neminent, as opposed to the shifty OPP\nportunism of personal manipulation;\nfor the liberty and order of general\nlaw, as against the tyranny of spec\nial edicts of changing men. 1t plants\nitself upon the impregnable ramparts\nof the Constitution, and, solemnly\nprotesting against any subverslon of\nthe terms of that great compact by\nthe arrogant and revolutionary pro-\nces of amendment by misconstructlon.\nappeals from the midsummer madness\nof that portion of the people which\ncan be fooled all the time to the sober\nsecond thought of the great body of\nthe American electorate who will\nrender judgment in November\n\n\n\n\n=== 14_1907-09-05_p1_sn89067273_00200292662_1907090501_0301.json\nMexico Missouri message. · 1907-09-05\ncrops: cached\nheadline: stopped HseeIna rhInnn..\n---\nEnthusiastic Nature Lover Ito Re.\nformed Tramp)-Ah, my friend, how\nwell you must know the face of ma.\nture, and know It in all its moods.\nHave you ever seen the sun sinking\nin such a glare OF glory that 1t swal-\nlows up the whole horizon with its\npassionate flre7 Have you seen the\nmist gliding like specter down the\nshrinking hillside. or the pale moon\nstruggling to shake OF the grip Of the\nragged storm cloud?\n\n\nReformed Tramp--No, sir; not\nsince signed the pledge.\n\n=== 15_1925-10-25_p1_sn83045462_00280659319_1925102501_0480.json\nEvening star. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1854-1972 · 1925-10-25\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Scene from\nlie PHANTOM otfHe\nOPERA\n\nFamous Model With\n\"Mayhowers.\"\n---\nX ago TORTONI, a famous model\n\n\nfor newspaper story illustrations.\nwhose figure has been reproduced and\nsyndicated in probably more news\npapers and magazines than that of\nany other girl in the world, will make\nher stage debut as a dancer in the\nforthcoming StanleySawyer musical\nplay. \"Mayflowers.\"\n\n\nMiss Tortoni is an American girl\nwith the distinction of having been\nborn in Toxic, raised and educated in\nAthens, and resident of New York\nthese past four years.\n\n=== 4_1906-04-12_pna_sn85047084_00294557477_1906041201_0435.json\nThe Pacific commercial advertiser. [volume] (Honolulu, Hawaiian Islands) 1885-1921 · 1906-04-12\ncrops: cached\nheadline: AcHI's GHOST DANCE.\n---\nAchi, indeed, is the scarecrow of the\nwhole situation-and, for dead one,\nseems to be able to show more life than\nthe whole bunch put together. 1t may\nbe ghost dance that he is doing. to\nbe sure. It's effect in a harvest of ter\nror is about the same, anyhow.\n\n\nThe Fishmarket, these DAYS. fairly\nhuns with Achi and his doings and\nsayings. The gossips cannot follow him\nat all, and so they talk the faster\nThey know that the wicked one is\nabroad. but they do not know where to\nhave him. and. in the meantime. there is\nalways the danger that he will slip up\nIn. the night and catch some of them\nwhen they amt a-noticing. That is\nnot a restful state of mind for men to\nbe in who have long campaign ahead\nof them. and problems to solve that\nwould give even Nagaran Fernandez a\nbad case of brain flag.\n\n=== 13_1838-01-06_pna_sn83016942_00295878204_1838010601_0607.json\nLiberty advocate. [volume] (Liberty, Miss.) 1835-1866 · 1838-01-06\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nP~SP'S'\n\n\nHuMANE.-Among the sufferers by the\nrecent fire at New Haven, was an indus\ntrious mechanic. Wm. G. Baldwin. At\nter the fire, one Isaiah Gale, who rejoices\nin the possession of soul, attached the\nsmall remnant Of Mr., Baldwin's property\nto secure debt of fifty-nine dollars. Such\nman ought to be buried where the sun\nnever rises.-BoSiNa transcript.\n\n\nLet's pass him around and get the opin-\nion of the press concerning him. We\nthink he ought to be made get his living\nby chewing paper rags Of the dirtiest sort.\nEastern Argus.\n\n\nWe think Isaiah ought TO experience a\ngale five hundred miles at sea, IN an In\ndams canoe; without compass or compan-\nion or any thing to eat except what our\nfriend of the Argus is disposed to give\nhim. Traveller.\nHis raiment should be sack cloth and\nashes-the latter from the remains Of his\nvictim. EUening Star.\n\n\nThe first paragraph has been in the sun\nbefore-but we re-pubish to record our\n\"opinion.\"s He should be sent where no ex-\ntinguishment of the fire will leave him\nspace to put an attachment.-N. Y. Sun.\nHe ought to be attached to an Arabian\ncourser, and carried into the depth of a\nforest where the eye of man shall never\nsee him. baltimore Express.\nHis head should be applied to grind\nstone, making three revolutions an hour,\nand ground down till it is as small as his\nsoul is. Hartford Times.\n\n\nHe has none. He should be doubled up,\nhis feet put into his mouth, and then COM\nPEELED to swallow himself whole, with cow\nhide shoes on, which would at least put a\nsole into his body. Kick him along.\n\n\nHartford Courier.\n\n\nHe should have his teeth drawn one by\none, in King Johns style, until he refund\ned the amount with interest and damages.\n\n\nM. advertiser.\nSend the soULLEss \"monster\" south and\nwe will have him honorably escorted thro'\nour principal towns marching to the tune\nOf \"sETTING ON A RAIL.\"-Greea. Noun\n| tGineer.\n\n\nStart him out without cent in his pock-\nct, on blind poney, to collect newspaper\nsubscription Of over one years standing\nand over one hundred miles distance, made\nup exclusively ofthe accounts of those We\nry considerate gentlemen who tax pub\nfishers with postage, and transpose their\nterms, requesting them first to discontin-\nue their papers and then send the account.\nIf he lives at that, send him around again\nand we will give him another kick.\n\n\nJacksonville Republican.\n\n\nLet's take up for Isaiah little. Per-\nhaps poverty by unseen fortune justified\nhis levy on Mr.. Baldwin's property to keep\nhis soul and body together. Give him his\nDUE Southern Register.\n\n\nThe next gale that blows after recor-\nding our opinion, should wait him on his\nwings of \"thunder and lightning\" to the\n\"White River Indians\" who would ''aca-\nbash him\" in the handsomest manner\n\n\nCourier.\n\n\nMay curse, worse than that of Cain,\nbe stamped upon his forehead, in living\ncharacters. May he wander through the\nworld, an outcast from society, with no\nfriend to console him, in the hour of af-\nFiction, or sooth his sorrows, or soften, in\nany degree, the asperities OF his dishonor-\ned life--to sum up all, may he be cursed\n} in his eating, his drinking, his sleeping\ncursed by men, devils, and angels to the\nend of time. Vernon Visitor.\n\n\nAs the general opinion ofthe press is e.\nlicited in regard to this hoggish trick Of\nIsaiah, we will give him sly dabb way\n} of slant. The man who would thus take\nthe advantage of another placed in such\nan unfortunate condition, is not jot bet\nter than he who stole the cold pone from\nnegroes saddle-bags, and he ought to\nhave his fingers whitfield down to the Gilt\ntie end Of nothing\" his head shaved, and\nfed on 'saw-dust pudding the remainder OF\nhis days. Veray Tunes\n\n\nHe ought to be haunted with duns, ours\ned with scolding wife, forced to sleep in\nhog stye, and at last be buried in the\ndepth Of tan vat.--BrOwnSville Banner.\nThe scoundreI should have his nose\npulled.\n\n=== 8_1859-11-26_pna_sn83021205_00517172042_1859112601_1107.json\nWorcester daily spy. [volume] (Worcester [Mass.]) 1850-1888 · 1859-11-26\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Wise still Believes in Ghosts.\n---\nWASHINGTON, Nov. 25.-According to the\nreported conversation with Gov. Wise, recent\nly, in this city, he has no doubt of the exist\nence of secret societies in various parts of Ohio\nand elsewhere, the members of which are\nbound by horrid oaths, not only to rescue\nBrown, but to take vengeance on those who\nwere instrumental in the conviction of him\nand his associates for the offences at Harper's\nFerry. The letters which the Governor has\nreceived upon the subject are from men in\nwhose word he places the fullest confidence\nbut their names will not be revealed by him.\n1t was doubtless the belief of the existence of\nsuch societies, numbering, it was reported,\nthousands of members. for the purposes stated,\nthat led him to call for an additional number\nof troops\n\n=== 1_1917-01-24_pna_sn86063147_00340585987_1917012401_0900.json\nThe Lamar register. [volume] (Lamar, Colo.) 1889-1952 · 1917-01-24\ncrops: cached\nheadline: SAW DEATH ANGEL\n\nApparition That Appeared to\n\n\nRoyal Children.\n\nStory Related by Governess Of Rus.\nsian Prlneesses-czar and czar-\nIna Believe Supernatural Fig.\n\n\nure Really Was Visible.\n---\nGrand Duke Ernest of Hesse had n\nvery pretty little daughter by his first\nwife, Princess Victoria Melitu Of Great\nBritain and Coburg, now married To\nGrand Duke Cyril of Russia. This lit\ntie girls name was Elizabeth. and on\naccount of her beauty and sprightly\ncleverness she was universal favor\nlite and the only tie between her par.\nents after the estrangement. f.. Gun.\nlife Owen writes In the New York\nWorld\n\n\nWhile staying with her uncle and\naunt, the present ear and czarinn. at\ntheir picturesque country seat in 20\nland, she succumbed when seven years\nold To poisOn-ptomaine poison. nc.\ncordlng to some. but according to 9th.\ners drugs conveyed into food OF\ndrink by the Nihilists for the purpose\nOF taking the life Of Emperor Mich.\nolas.\n\n\nA remarkable account Of the affair\nIs given by an English woman of the\nname OF Miss Eager who. after spend\nIng II number of years in the service\nOF the emperor and empress of Rus\nSTA as the nursery governess of their\nyoung children, published on her re-\nturn to England, with the full author\nity and approval OF their majesties. a\nvolume entitled six Years at the\nCourt of Russia\"\n\n\nAccording to her, little Princess\nElizabeth, or ELLA\" of Hesse was\ntaken IN one afternoon or night and\n\n=== 13_1907-05-08_p7_sn85042354_00383343252_1907050801_0982.json\nEvening journal. · 1907-05-08\ncrops: cached\nheadline: GHOST ARRESTS\n\n\nCRAP SHOOTERS\n---\nCARMI, Ill. May 9-Chief of Police\nLampp las learned new way OF catch\ning crap shooters who iniest the fields\naround here\n\n\nI'VE couldn't catch them\" he said.\nlin the ordinary ways. SO ] decided to\nparalyze them and pick them up while\ndazed Just at sundown 1 wrapped a\nwhite sheet about me and suddenly\nappeared in front of the lookout\n\n\nHE fell to the ground helpless, and\nI handcumed him and tied him 10 a\ntree. Then I stole on down by the old\ngraveyard and suddenly stood before a\ndozen negroes happy i'm game.\nSeven fell fiat. and I nailed them.\nThe others ran. guess they're stin\nrunning\"\n\n=== 31_1872-02-23_pna_sn82016419_00212472967_1872022301_0373.json\nThe Emporia news. [volume] (Emporia, Kan.) 1859-1881 · 1872-02-23\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nPractical JoKEs.-They had a\nterrifying sen sation in a boarding\nhouse in Davenport, Iowa, the other\nevening, the result of the penchant\nof lady for little fun. This lady\nput on a ghostly mask, donned a\ncoat of her husbands, and, creeping\nsoftly to the door of another lady,\npushed it ajar, and, with a groan,\nput her head i'll the opening. The\noccupant turned, and looked, and\ngave Il shrek, and fell to the floor\nin spasns. The fun maker saw the\nresult of her sport, and swooned\naway. The noise made by the vic\ntim caused rush of other boarders\nto the scene, who appreciated the\nsituation at once. 1t was with\ngreat difficulty that the frightened\nlady was restored to consciousness,\nand after that she was in crazed\nstate of mind for several hours.\nMeantime the other lady was taken\nto her room, where Testoratiyes\nbrought her to her senses. She in\nquired as to the condition of her\nfriend, and, when some one told her\nthe facts, she tainted again, remain\ning in an alarming condition so long\nthat her husband and all about her\nwere filled with the direst appre-\ntensions. Both ladies were unable\nto leaYe their rooms the next day.\n\n=== 5_1855-10-26_pna_sn84023127_00280776981_1855102601_0388.json\nBurlington free press. [volume] (Burlington, Vt.) 1827-1865 · 1855-10-26\ncrops: cached\nheadline: CATHARTIC PILLS\n---\nOPERATE by their powerful influence on the\n\" internal yiscera to purify the blood and stinu-\nlate it into healthy action. They remove the\ndistractions Of the stomach, bowels, liver, and other\norgans Of the body, and by restoring their irregular\naction TO health correct. wherever they exist such\narrangements are the first causes of disease.\nAn extensive trial Of their virtues by Professors,\nPhysicians, and Patients, has shown cures Of dan\ngcrous diseases almost beyond belief, were they not\nsubstantiatcd by persons Of such exalted position\nand character as to for