d. and quite able to\n584\tDO her own thinking\n585\t\n586\t\n587\tYou wouldn't believe all the\n588\tdreadful things I believe about Ham\n589\tilton Certcis.\" said at last. $50\n590\twhy should I mention them\n591\t\n592\t\n593\tII wouldn't doubt your word. my\n594\tdear. hut I might insist on drawing\n595\tmy own conclusions from your facts.\n596\twhatever they are\" she said.\n597\t\n598\t\n599\tHE isn't an American citizen\" |\n600\treminded her. as II that dcnunciation\n601\tcovered \"A multitude OF sins\n602\t\n603\t\n604\tmo. II he were. he wouldn't be\n605\tgoing TO take possession OF n small\n606\tsection OF Spain. And | couldn't go\n607\twith him. Jane. dear. why shouldn't\n608\t| go In for my portion OF this worlds\n609\tYou've had your share\n610\tunless Katherine Miller provides\n611\tYOU with more. Why shouldn't l\n612\tclaim my bitF\"\n613\t\n614\t\n615\t\"Oh. la la! ! always knew YOU\n616\tbelieved that ouija board story about\n617\tCcrtcis and his royal ancestors\"\n618\t\n619\t\n620\tIto be Cuntlnued)\n621\t\n622\t=== 8_1846-08-26_pna_sn85026944_00202194278_1846082601_0085.json\n623\tThe banner. [volume] (Abbeville, S.C.) 1844-1847 · 1846-08-26\n624\tcrops: cached\n625\theadline: FOR THE BANNER.\n626\tWHO is TRULY GREAT.\n627\t---\n628\tmore pleasure than the study of man,\n629\tand the peculiar features which mark\n630\tthe leading propensities of his character,\n631\tand disposition. BY contemplation, 1t is\n632\teasily observed that there are certain\n633\tprinciples in human nature, which\n634\tteach, that every man is created with\n635\tvarious and dissimilar forms of desire,\n636\twhich move to active and persevering\n637\texertion. That some are moved to ac-\n638\tton from pure and laudable principles\n639\tof philanthropy, while others are inAa-\n640\tmed by the jealous pride of power, and\n641\tdazzled by the vain glories of aspiring\n642\tdemagogues. And that also the seeds\n643\tof true and real virtue, planted by ma\n644\tture, early springing up, wields at plea\n645\tsure the evil propensities of the one, and\n646\tmakes him look into his own heart, and\n647\tfrom himself learn not alone, the weak\n648\tness of human nature, but to bound and\n649\tset limits to its natural wants and era\n650\twings. Whilst the inordinate love of\n651\tfame fires the breast of the other. arou-\n652\tses his slumbering energies, paralyzcs\n653\this nobler faculties, and prepares HIM\n654\tfor the horrors and bloody scenes\n655\tof unholy strife, and to grasp with\n656\tIl hand goary with the blood of in\n657\tnocency the wreath Of ,ransitory fame.\n658\t\n659\t\n660\tBut how fleeting are its phantoms.\n661\tLike star on the verge Of the horizon,\n662\tthey dazzle in the gaze of the world for\n663\tmoment and then sink into the abyss\n664\tOF darkness, \"even the renowned Ene-\n665\tas, the terror of the Greeks, and whose\n666\tmagnanimity of soul drove him amid\n667\tthe flaming towers Of hoas,cd Troy\"\n668\tbearing upon his back his old father\n669\tANcnIsEs,\" was made to wander even\n670\tan exile from the land he loved and is\n671\tonly remembered in the songs\" and fic-\n672\ttions \"of Virgil\" No conqucror has\n673\tever yet shook the world by arms, or\n674\tmade nations bow suppliant a. his feet\n675\twho was not indeed doomed to 60 down\n676\tin the great vortex Of revolution. His\n677\tfame is low and poor compared with the\n678\tmagnanimity of virtue\" 1t vanlshes\n679\tbefore the greatness Of principle\" The\n680\tmartyr, the patriot, and the phlanthro-\n681\tpist. \"The unshrinking adhcrcnt to\n682\tdespair, Of deserted truth and religion\"\n683\twithout a smiling compassionate friend,\n684\tto extend to him the balm Of consolation.\n685\t\"nor variety Of objects to draw his\n686\tthoughts from himself, with no cheering\n687\tvoice to arouse and nourish energy but\n688\tyields calmly to the most powerful sour\n689\tgings of fortune, and perhaps to the\n690\tmost excrucia:ing pain which one word\n691\tretracted would remove\" is as far supes\n692\trior to the bloody conqueror, Gas heaven\n693\tabove is superior to all beneath\"\n694\t\n695\t\n696\tGod then, the maker Of heaven and\n697\tearth, and ali things else is alone truly\n698\tgreat. And he who aspires 10 great\n699\tness, should with the rest of his conduct,\n700\tpractice christian purity and holiness\n701\tThat greatness is not true, which is\n702\tgained by the sword of an ambitious\n703\tand relentless unfeeling con queror, or\n704\tby the artful intrigues of the aspiring\n705\tpolitician. But he is great, and truly\n706\tgreat too, whose generous heart exuIts\n707\twith proud delight, and swells at the\n708\tsametime, with gratitude and benevo-\n709\tlence to humanity, for the high distinc-\n710\tton to which he may have promoted\n711\thimself\n712\t\n713\t\n714\tTrue greatness is not then, what the\n715\tworld has always supposed it to be-.\n716\tBut what is it2 and in what does it con.\n717\t- So Il. n\n718\t\n719\t=== 1_1898-05-31_p7_sn86072192_00340583322_1898053101_0498.json\n720\tThe age-herald. · 1898-05-31\n721\tcrops: cached\n722\theadline: \n723\t---\n724\tbeat the sheaves and pins of the blocks to drive the devil out of the gear, and the noise they make is a horrid din. rms IS said to have originated in an order2 given which resulted in disaster A certain Spanish squadron that had been anehoreo some time was surprised by the enemy and could not make sail and escape. as all the blocks were rusted and useiess\n725\t\n726\t The Spanish sailor has prejudice against starting out on a voyage on Fri day, that being the day on which CO., lumbus started on his voyage of discovery. English and American sailors will not sail on that day if they can help it. They would consider the whole trip hoodooed by such beginning.\n727\t\n728\t A sneeze at sea would hooddoo the ship unless it happened to turn to the right, when it brings good luck. Sailors have faith in odd numbers and naval gunners carry out this belief in the number of times they fire a salute\n729\t\n730\t WhistliHg for wind is another favorite superstition of the man before the mast. This is a trick when ships are becalmed, as \"Whtles rash tempests roar\" IA \"eapful of wind\" or a \"a bagfu'' ussl- ally defines the quantity.\n731\t\n732\t The phantom ship.so often seen by nayigators. is not purely the apparition of romance, but mirage, something al- most as wonderful; but not of the super natural order.\n733\t\n734\t The horseshoe is still a popular contri- tion by sailors to the fetish of good luck. A distinguished precedent is given for this, Lord Nelson believed in the luck of the horseshoe, and had one nailed to the mast of his ship Victory\n735\t\n736\t A peculiar superstition is that if women are taken on board a voyaging ship some disaster will follow. This may be the req tn carry women passengers. Sailors, too, pre- fer ship that has a masculine name, or is called after some man, or Is geographical A line of steamers will often employ a cer- tain selection of names to the exclusion or all others. The Cunard steamers end all their names with \"la, feminine terminal, but one that has been the reverse of dis astrous, since that line has mover lost a ship. Captains are quite as superstitious as their crews, being of bettetr education, l, and that may account for the names Of sailing vessels being identical with that of wife or. sweetheart. Certain names are known in the history of nautical nomen- cature as hoodoos. They have been paint ed over and other names substituted, but bad luck attended the ship, and not even reconstruction saved it. Then, if the name must be feminine, the crew want some thing they are familiar with One old tar who was told the name of new ship was the Aurora said:\n737\t\n738\t \"Why don't they leave poetry to the navy folk and stick to the Susannahs and Mary Anns and Jemimas of my day? Aurora! How is a plain able seaman to answer a hail with such a mouthful as that behind his teeth?\"\n739\t\n740\t Sailors cling to their supersttions with a tenacity worthy of better cause. Possibly it is more from custom rather than from an actual belief in their efficacyI The man at the wheel will tell you that he can not steer straight if there is a cross-eyed passenger on board, and he repeats a charm of words to annul the evil: St. Peter! St. Peter, pray give us charm, Against the bad eye that would do us a\n741\t\n742\t harm.\n743\t\n744\t=== 4_1900-01-25_p1_sn88076270_00212475944_1900012501_0701.json\n745\tWilliston graphic. (Williston, Williams County, N.D.) 1895-1919 · 1900-01-25\n746\tcrops: cached\n747\theadline: 'POSSUM TALKS.\n748\tZcb White Tells About Mountain Ghost.\n749\t\n750\t\n751\tBv M.\n752\t---\n753\tOne evening, when night had fallen\n754\tdark and grim over the peaks of the\n755\tCum berland, and had brought with it\n756\tsuch strange stiffness that one could\n757\thear the opening of the chestnut\n758\tburrs and the fall of the nuts on the\n759\tshriveled leaves below, l asked the old\n760\t'possum hunter if he had never met\n761\twith anything weird and mysterious\n762\tup there on the rugged slopes.\n763\t\n764\t\n765\teyes, I've met up with some quare\n766\tthings in my time,\" he replied after a\n767\tminute, land I've always sorter reck-\n768\toned that ghost or speerit once\n769\thelped me out O. q powerful tight\n770\tplace.\"\n771\t\n772\t\n773\tBeing pressed for the story, he\n774\tstirred up the logs on the fire, lighted\n775\this pipe and continued:\n776\t\n777\t\n778\tII reckon 1 have told yo' why went\n779\tinto moonshine whisky. 1t was the\n780\tsame way with all the rest. We could\n781\tturn our con into money that way,\n782\tand in no other. Nobody ever did\n783\tmake any money at it, and every man\n784\tabout a still run the risk of bein' sent\n785\toff to state prison most any day in the\n786\tweek. I did heap o thinllin' befo' l\n787\twent into the bizness. The ole woman\n788\twas agin it from the start, and many a\n789\tnight she woke me up as she cried\n790\tout: G.\n791\t\n792\t\n793\t\"'Zeb White, I've bin dreamin' that\n794\tthem revenoo fellers had cotched yo\n795\tand sent yo' to the penitentiary fur\n796\ttwo y'ars!'\n797\t\n798\t\n799\t\"Bimeby,\" continued the old man, ~\n800\tdecided to go into it. We had to pack\n801\tthe still 10 miles over the hills on oul\n802\t\n803\t=== 7_1898-07-10_pna_sn85042461_00280769800_1898071001_0138.json\n804\tThe herald. [microfilm reel] (Los Angeles [Calif.]) 1893-1900 · 1898-07-10\n805\tcrops: cached\n806\theadline: \n807\t---\n808\tthe wall that Delaigne was knocked\n809\tdown.\n810\t\n811\t\n812\tSomething wrong here\" cried the\n813\trider, leaping from his horse.\n814\t\n815\t\n816\tHe fell squarely upon Delairne.\n817\tThere was a short, fierce struggle in\n818\tthe dark, and at the end, overpowered\n819\tby numbers. for several horsemen came\n820\tto the help of their comrade. the\n821\tAmerican found himself in the hands\n822\tOf Spanish soldiers, a prisoner.\n823\t\n824\t\n825\t(TO BE CONCLUDED - [\n826\t\n827\t=== 2_1914-04-01_p1_sn83016689_00279552003_1914040102_0910.json\n828\tThe Detroit times. · 1914-04-01\n829\tcrops: cached\n830\theadline: GETS JUDGMENT FOR\n831\t\n832\t\n833\tWATCH GIVEN AS PLEDGE\n834\t---\n835\t1t was beautiful dress.\n836\t\n837\t\n838\tFor days II had haunted Its grace.\n839\tfull curves. its snowy laces, its sneen\n840\tof silk and melting shades before the\n841\teyes Of Mrs. Anna Watson. She\n842\tlonged for that dress. she dreamed OF\n843\tIT, saw 1t In her waking hours. felt\n844\tits softness. pictured Its drape on her\n845\tfigure.\n846\t\n847\t\n848\tBut she lacked IS of having enough\n849\tto buy il. She turned and twisted\n850\tand planned and figured and wore out\n851\tpencils without number. trying to\n852\tfigure the answer. Then one day she\n853\tsaw her husbands watch on the\n854\tdresser.\n855\t\n856\t\n857\tThat night she had the dress and\n858\tSteinberg Bros, No. 213 Michigan\n859\tave.. had the watch as security for\n860\tthe payment of the balance on the\n861\tdress. A few days later fire swept\n862\tthe place and when Mrs. Watson\n863\twent down to redeem the watch, II\n864\thad gone up In smoke. Wednesday\n865\tshe brought suit and based her claims\n866\ton the fact that as the watch was\n867\tnot hers, the company must return II\n868\tor Its value to the original owner, her\n869\thusband.\n870\t\n871\t\n872\tJustice DeGaw found for the wom-\n873\tan, and the attorney for the defen-\n874\tdant will appeal.\n875\t\n876\t=== 52_1905-02-25_pna_sn86090528_00295864448_1905022501_0362.json\n877\tThe Chickasha daily express. (Chickasha, Indian Territory [Okla.]) 1899-current · 1905-02-25\n878\tcrops: cached\n879\theadline: PHANTOM SHIp\n880\tOR\n881\tThe Flying Dutchmon.\n882\t\n883\t\n884\t~~s-T cAsEAN MARRYAr\n885\t\n886\t\n887\t---\n888\tCTFAPTERXXVII- Continued\n889\t\n890\t\n891\tI'VE are Hollanders.\" replied Philip\n892\t\n893\t\n894\t\"A part of the crew of the vesse\n895\tEhich was wrecked?\" inquired he\n896\t\n897\t\n898\t\"Yes!\"\n899\t\n900\t\n901\tYou have nothing to fear; you ar\n902\t\n903\t\n904\tnemies to the Portuguese, and so ar,\n905\twe. We belong to the island OF Ter\n906\tnate-our king is at war with tn,\n907\tPortuguese, who are villains. Wher,\n908\tare your companions? on which isl\n909\tRnd?\"\n910\t\n911\t\n912\t\"They are all dead\" replied Philip\n913\tMAY l ask you whether you have fall\n914\ten- in with woman who was adrift or\n915\tpart Of the raft by herself; or have\n916\tyou heard of herr\"\n917\t\n918\t\n919\t\"We have heard that a woman was\n920\tpicked up on the beach to the south\n921\tward, and carried away by the Tidore\n922\tpeople to the Portuguese settlement on\n923\tthe supposition that she was Portu-\n924\tguese.\"\n925\t\n926\t\n927\t\"Then God be thanked, she is saved, \"\n928\tcried Philip. \"Merciful Heavenl accept\n929\tmy thanks. To Tidore you said?\"\n930\t\n931\t\n932\tEyes;