have given our faith of the\n1124\tsupernatural Ghost stories, which have\n1125\tnot lost their secret charm for the\n1126\twisest as well as the simplest OF\n1127\tcourse, in the light of science and of\n1128\treason, all these things, are nonsense:\n1129\tbut after all, it is a nonsense for which\n1130\twe cannot help keeping Il soft spot III\n1131\tour hearts\"\n1132\t\n1133\t\n1134\t| think il was Charles Lamb who\n1135\treasoned that science had established\n1136\tthe fact that the subject of ghosts was\n1137\tnot theory, or anything of that indeti-\n1138\tmite sort, and he worked it out\" III\n1139\tthis way:\n1140\t\n1141\t\n1142\tcit is now admitted to be a scientific\n1143\ttruth, that at the end Of every seventh\n1144\tyear of every MAS life, or every wom-\n1145\tans life, not particle of physical hui\n1146\tmainly remains in their system which\n1147\twas IN 1T seven years before\"\n1148\t\n1149\t\n1150\tEvery seven years, therefore, a man\n1151\tor woman Is \"not that man > or woman,\n1152\tbut another man or woman. Every\n1153\tseven years there has been peeled off\n1154\tfrom each concentrlc ring, as from\n1155\tthe concentric ring of an onion, shad-\n1156\towy representative of the individual\n1157\tperson, which is wandering In space\n1158\tincreasing by one at the end OF every\n1159\tseven years. And this was Lambs the\n1160\tDRY of ghosts. Every seven years a\n1161\tmans ghost left him, to appear after\n1162\tward anywhere, and at any time, and\n1163\t''no questions asked, Of a cold night\n1164\tas IN the poor buried King OF Den\n1165\tmark S case. Now, reader, has not this\n1166\tthought, s. forcibly expressed by\n1167\tJoanna Bailey, sometimes entered your\n1168\tmind?\n1169\t\n1170\t\n1171\tq shall the departed gaze thee again?\n1172\t\n1173\t\n1174\tShall glide past thee in the noontide hour,\n1175\t\n1176\t\n1177\tAnd thou perceive il not? think'st perhaps\n1178\t\n1179\t\n1180\tlis but the mourtul breeze that passes by -\n1181\t\n1182\t\n1183\tSitting beside winter fire of an\n1184\tevening Ill a roundabout chair upon a\n1185\tsolitary hearth, in a house where the\n1186\tunwonted sound of foOtstep is to be\n1187\theard only after the summons of q bell,\n1188\thave you never encountered those\n1189\t\"ministering spirits2' [ think YOU\n1190\thave, / know / have.\n1191\t\n1192\t\n1193\tThe fact is, that there is not one fam\n1194\tlily In ten but has its traditionarv ghost\n1195\tstory, which, when narrated to the\n1196\tgroup that gathers round the winter\n1197\ttireside, excites, according to the age\n1198\tand character of the listeners, terror\n1199\tsympathy, doubt, incredality and ridi-\n1200\tME al or 81,\n1201\tand cherished in after life; for it per\n1202\ttains to that world of untried being,\n1203\twhich approaches toward us with its\n1204\tslow and nolseless but irresistible and\n1205\toverwhelming movement.\n1206\t\n1207\t\n1208\tThe incident narrated below, by an\n1209\told friend and correspondent of mine.\n1210\twas told first to me at his own most\n1211\thospitable hearth, and afterward, at NV\n1212\tearnest request, reduced to writing,\n1213\tfrom which the present narrative is\n1214\tgreatly reduced, vet contains in com\n1215\tpact form, all that is necessary for the\n1216\tfull understanding of the reader. The\n1217\timportant point made by Lamb, as to\n1218\tthe eccentric theory of escaped ghosts,\n1219\tI'm rings, every seven years, in a wood\n1220\ten- leg, which does not expand, and\n1221\tbOurgeon, and fade, and vanish, in\n1222\tshadow with the human svstem-\" that\n1223\tis the question: The very remarkable\n1224\tincident took place in the Island of Do\n1225\tm'nca, IN relation to which there is at\n1226\tpresent some national agitation, which\n1227\tpromises to increase as time goes on.\n1228\t\n1229\t\n1230\tDuring Il period of considerable ex-\n1231\tcitement, arising from a season of great\n1232\tmortality among the inhabitants of the\n1233\tisland, IN the year -, Il veteran Scot\n1234\tt Sh regiment was stationed upon the\n1235\thigh blum Of land which forms one\n1236\tpoint Of crescentular bay, and over\n1237\tlooks the town and harbor. Inland. to-\n1238\tward the east, small plain extends it\n1239\tself, while, on the west and north.\n1240\twhich is nearest the shore, and almost\n1241\toverhangIng 1t, are several low, one\n1242\tstory buildings, hastily erected, Of\n1243\twood, Tor the accommodation of the\n1244\tofficers OF the corps, and consisting all\n1245\tof three or four rooms In each end, with\n1246\tpiazza on the side toward the sea, cx-\n1247\ttending the whole length OF the struc-\n1248\tture, and forming shaded and agreea-\n1249\tbic promenade during the earlier part\n1250\tOF the day. The rooms opened upon\n1251\tthe piazza and communicated with each\n1252\tother by means of u side-door, which\n1253\twas occasionally kept open for the freer\n1254\tcirculation of air\n1255\t\n1256\t\n1257\tIn one of these barracks were quar-\n1258\ttered three officers of the regiment\n1259\tMajor Hamilton, Capt. Gordon. and a\n1260\tthird, whose name [ cannot at this mo-\n1261\tment recall.\n1262\t\n1263\t\n1264\tMajor Hamilton's apartment was in\n1265\tthe center. He had lost a leg in the\n1266\tservice; and usually wore a wooden pin\n1267\tq stick shod with iron, and being an\n1268\talert man, fond of exercise, used TO\n1269\twalk mp and down this piazza for hours\n1270\ttogether, stopping occasionally at Cor\n1271\tdon's door or window, and sometimes\n1272\tlooking In at that of the other officer.\n1273\texchanging q cheerful word with them,\n1274\trs they sat, each in his apartment. en-\n1275\tdeavorlng to begule the time with\n1276\tdressing, reading, writing, thoughts OF\n1277\tpromotion, of home, and of a speedy\n1278\tAID happy return to Britain\n1279\t\n1280\t\n1281\tThe sound of the Majors step was\n1282\tpeculiar. 1t Was only the blow given\n1283\tby the iron ferule nc the end of his\n1284\twooden leg that was heard; for, altho'\n1285\tstout man, he trod lightly with the\n1286\tremaining foot, and heavily only with\n1287\tthe wooden substitute, which gave forth\n1288\tIt's note at short intervals, as he paced\n1289\tto and fro so regularly that there was\n1290\ta certain pleasure in listening TO it.\n1291\tSounds that strike the ear in this\n1292\tmeasured way affect Us more than 9th\n1293\ters. The attention becomes engaged,\n1294\taud they grow emphatic rs We listen.\n1295\t\n1296\t\n1297\tThe calker's hammer stroke, as it flies\n1298\tfrom the dock-yard of the busy port\n1299\t\n1300\t=== 74_1881-03-12_p3_sn82015483_00271764170_1881031201_0246.json\n1301\tMorning journal and courier. · 1881-03-12\n1302\tcrops: cached\n1303\theadline: The Grim Spectre Follows Vaccination.\n1304\t\n1305\t BRooKLYN, March 11.-Chief Engineer\n1306\t---\n1307\tJames W. Whitaker, of the United States navy, died yesterday in this city from an at- tack of erysipe1as, resulting from recent vac cination. He was born in New Jersey in 1886, and was appointed to the navy in 1857. He at one time was on special duty as inspec- tor in the Portsmouth, N. H., navy yard.\n1308\t"}]},"uuid":"3e951369-50cb-4cac-b153-ec509af8d01a","timestamp":"2026-10-31T07:58:32.484Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/c1/g11.txt","content":"=== 14_1898-03-18_p1_sn85042104_00332892162_1898031801_0577.json\nThe news &amp; observer. [volume] (Raleigh, N.C.) 1894-current · 1898-03-18\ncrops: cached\nheadline: POPULIST RESOLUTIONS.\n\na Correct Copy of the Resolutions\n\n\nAdopted by the Conference.\n---\n\n\n\nAdopted by the Conference.\nBY an inadvertence one line was left\nout of the resolutions adopted by the\nPopulist Conference. leaving out that\nthey endorsed the address by Chairman\nJones. rs we'll as that by Chairman But\nTer and Chairman Townp. The resolu.\ntlons were not obtained until after four\nO'Clock in the morning, and in tho hurry\nOf making up the forms, the omission\nwas not noted. The resolutions as\nadopted were as follows:\n\n\n\"Resolved. That the spirit of this con-\nference warrants the assertion that any\ndifferences which may have existed in\nthe Peoples party of North Carolina.\nare eliminated, and the sense of this\nConference is that any effort to create\ndiscord hereafter, will be an object Of\n\n=== 8_1899-10-26_p1_sn85029677_00212473108_1899102601_0124.json\nPhillipsburg herald. [volume] (Phillipsburg, Kan.) 1882-1905 · 1899-10-26\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Bitter Fight Among spiritualists.\n---\nChicago, Oct. 2O.-Expansion, impe-\nrialism and adherence to the declara-\nton of independence were the rocks\nupon which the National Spiritualist\nassociation came dangerously near\nlaundering at yesterdays session.\nLack of patriotism. disrespect of the\nconstitution and disloyalty to the flag\nwere some of the charges made against\nmembers. So bitter did the fight be-\ncome that it was only the firm ruling\nof President Barre that prevented a\nrupture.\n\n=== 14_1867-10-10_p1_sn84027398_00279529418_1867101001_0386.json\nThe Tipton advertiser. [volume] (Tipton, Cedar Co., Iowa) 1856-1962 · 1867-10-10\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nThe c .inese are spiritualists, and they\ncredit each person with six spirits. The\nnext world With them must be pretty\nthiclsiy inhabited. In addition to the\nsix spirits every Chlnaman has three\nSouig\n\n=== 20_1902-09-05_p1_sn85049554_00416159427_1902090501_0045.json\nEvening times-Republican. [volume] (Marshalltown, Iowa) 1890-1923 · 1902-09-05\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nDo you smoke the Middle West?\"\n\n\nDR.. Nichols, over Mcbride & Willis.\n\n\nDIR. Burkart, osteopatll, 302 West Mair\n\n\n'.rhe heavenworth Case a lawyers\n\n\nSTORY, at Liebig's.\n\n\nFive hundred young mems suits al\n\n\nprices that make them so some. O'Brien\n\n\n& Co.\n\n\nSend for copy of Dudley Business\nCollege Journal and get ready to enter\nnext Monday.\n\n\nWe have good supply of split wood\nI and kindling for sale. Brown Fuel and\nLime Company.\n\n\n| Haviland china with each so cent. 81,\nS1.50 and 50 cash purchase at Sin\nclair's tomorrow.\n\n\nTomorrow, Saturday, we will sell\ngraphophone records for 50 cents. Adj\namson Music House\n\n\nPrivate stock Val Blatz beer is the\npurest and best for table use. Order q\n\n\n\nMax Hotfman's test seance Saturday\nafternoon at 3 o'clock at the Spiritual\nlists camp. Specially for the ladies.\nTomorrow, Saturday, you can have\ntwo pieces of the Harlan sheet music\nfor 5 cents. Adamson Music House.\nCenterville Block or Empire Illi-\nnois Goals are excellent for furnace or\nrange use. Brown Fuel and Lime Com\npany.\n\n\nBoys % cent Iron Clad hose tomor-\nrow for 10 cents a pair; not more than\na pair to a customer. Lee & Bene-\ndict.\n\n\nOne hundred mems negligee and stiff\nbosom shirts Former price, 81 and up\nto s1.50; Saturday, IS cents. O'Brien\n& Co.\n\n\nOctober number of the Delineator and\nall other leading fashion books. c..\nDeuell's News Depot, West Main\nstreet.\n\n\nIi you have not seen those handsome\nrugs at Cards you should do so. Any\nsized rug you want at prices that will\nsurprise you.\n\n\nOne hundred dozen mems Royal Rib\npure wool underwear. Former price,\ns1.25; Saturday, 20 cents; SO for two\nsuits. O'Brien & Co.\n\n\nThe Melbourne Record says that I.\nNJ. BARLEY and family. of this city, have\nmoved to Melbourne. Mr.. BARLEY takes\ncharge of the Pearson hotel property.\n\n\nGeorge B. Speers, of Eldora, has sold\nthe old Wallace farm, in Bangor and\nMinerva townships. for $13,4S7. Charles\nMcKeen Guren, of Hardin county, is the\npurchaser.\n\n\nA word on trouserings. The patterns\nfor the coming season embrace many\nnew effects that are extremely new.\nHopkins will take pleasure insshowing\nyou the best.\n\n\nWhy do you pay the fancy prices for\nspices that many ask for them? We\nsell you pure spices for cents for\nquarter pound, that you pay 10 and Is\ncents for quarter pound. Bee Hive.\n\n\nA beautiful piece of Haviland &\nCo.'S china with each so cent, 81, $3.50,\nand 81 cash purchase, al Sinclair's to.\nmorrow. Remember these are the same\nthree decorations you have been get\nting.\n\n\n+. NJ. White has purchased the White\nmarket on East Main street, and open\ned up for business this morning. He\nwill keep a complete stock of fresh and\nsalt meats, and deliver all orders\n\n\n\nThe Bee Hive has displayed in their\nwest window some very big values in\nsilk laces ana velvets. Your choice for\nso cents yard former price up 20 81\na yard: this will interest you as they\nare positive bargain.\n\n\nMr. Carl Thornquist, who recently\naccepted the position of chief clerk to\nsuperintendent j. a. Davis, of the Cen\ntrail, at Oskaloosa, arrived in the city\nevening and this morning\nwent to Oskaloosa, where he will as\nsumo the duties of his new work.\n\n\nWord received by Master Mechanic\nGeorge d. Brooke. of the Central, is to\nthe effect that two of the company's /\nnew Schenectady consolidated engines,\nNos. so and 81. left the works Wednes-\nday. 1t is expected that they will ar\nrive on the line Sunday or Monday.\n\n\nContractor George Sewer went to\nthis morning to look into the\nmatter of brick. The work of laying\nnew sidewalks, for which Mr.. Sewer\nhas the contract, has been greatly de-\nlayed owing to his inability to secure\nbrick from the manufacturers in Oska- |\nloosa.\n\n\nOne of the Central's Pittsburg ten\nwheel engines, No 101. which has been\nin the shops for the past two months\nbeing rebuilt, was brought out this\npractically as new as it was\nthe day it left the locomotive works /\nThe engine will be sent to Oskaloosa to-\nbe used in the time freight service.\n\n\nThe Cleveland Plain Dealer speaks |\nvery complimentary of the work of Mrs.\nEmma II. Weidel, formerly of this city I\nbut now of Sioux City, in reference to\nher work in the Cuyahoga teachers in\nstitute. Mrs Weidel has been doing |\nspecial work with the Speer method\nOf arithmetic and has pleased the Ohio\nteachers immensely\n\n\nThe young women of the Marshall\nCompany surprised Super\nntendent w. G. Bowe at his home, 609\nWest State street, \"Wednesday evening,\n\n=== 54_1885-12-29_pna_sn90059522_00175035357_1885122901_0617.json\nSt. Paul daily globe. [volume] (Saint Paul, Minn.) 1884-1896 · 1885-12-29\ncrops: cached\nheadline: GOTHAM SWELLDOM.\n\nNew York Girls and Something Of Their\n\n\nClothes, Shoes and Limps.\n\nThe Vaccination Rage Among the\n\n\nBelles-Opera Fashions.\n\nSpecimen of the New York Swell\n\n\nGotham Glimpses.\n---\nNew York Correspondence:\n\n\nFrom the windows of the Union league\nthe New Yorker may be studied in his or her\npeculiarities.\n\n\nIi you use the latter pronoun change the\npeculiarities into idiocies. Not that the\nmale bird is free from features that attract\nyour wondering attention, but that the lady\nbird presents to the strangers eye features\nSO extraordinary and so striking that your\nattention is diverted from her more COM\nmonplace companion.\n\n\nThe men of New York are well worth\nlooking at OF course. The \"doosid\" good\nfellow club man, you know, is always well\ngroomed, lives in bachelor apartments any\nwhere from Washington square up to the\nSixty streets, is, above all else, English in\nproclivities and taste, and in his moments\nOF leisure land their name is legion), if it\ndoesn't bore him too much, does the heavy\nagreeable to the fair opponents of social\nart.\n\n\nHe is man, tits true, who doesn't con-\ntribute anything Of Importance toward the\nprogress of the world, but then he fills, and\nfills handsomely, you must admit, niche\nthat would look empty without his pleasing\nform. He is inoffensive, and you are rather\nglad that he is there.\n\n\nThe younger man, the dude. who lives\nbut to become something that he can\nnever be, who is always aping somebody or\nsomething, whose appearance is at times\ngrotesQue, and always contemptible-he\ndeserves but little notice from you. You\nsheer a sheer as he passes, and say short\nmass for the repose of his soul.\n\n\nThese male birds. as l say, catch your\neye as they go by, and you size them up in\na moment A passing pleasure, as it were,\nand soon forgotten.\n\n\nBut in the notice and study of the female\nspecies, vast fields Of inquiry open out be-\nfore you. Young society girls, young mar\nried women, stout countess dowagers. all\npresent points of interest that are worth\nprolonged notice and consideration. And\nyet. strange to say, the one most noticeable\nfeature of the younger members Of this\nmore interesting species, is the almost I'M\nqualified and servile imitation of some of\nthe features of the other less interesting\nspecies.\n\n\nTHE NEW YORK GIRL\n\n\nis nothing if not manly. In many of her\nhabits, and especially in her dress, she\ntries to look like the men, don't you know.\nRedfern & Overall, and the other ladies'\ntailors, make the clothes for the swell\nyoung woman. Her tailor-made suits cost\nher $125; are fitted by men, and are made\nto look as much like mems clothes as waists\nand skirts can be made to resemble waist\ncoats and trousers.\n\n\nHer shoes are this fall exactly like mems\nshoes. They have thick soles, square box\ntoes, and are made to lace up in front.\nShod with these ''stogies.\" encased like an\numbrella in a tight-ftting ulster, which fits\nlike paper on the wall over a huge hustle\nprojecting far into the rear; mans hat\non her head, and a small, big-handled\numbrella in hand, my lady strides up Fifth\navenue-one of many, but flying private\nsignals in the way of details of dress, and\nmaking her ''the worst YETI until the next\none comes along.\n\n\nStrides, however, is hardly the word to\nbe used. She minces, and she makes this\nmincing noticeable by limp. You ask if\nshe is lame. By no means. They all\nlimp, and surely they can't all be lame. 1t\nis simply the fashion to limp. The Princess\nof Wales limps, and for time it was the\nfashion for court ladies to limp also. 1t\nis the swell thing to do here, but for a\ndifferent reason. They have all been vac\ncinated. Not to be vaccinated is not to be\none Of us girls, and, therefore. whether OF\nno, we limp, l heard a dudish young doc\ntor, the other day at an afternoon tea, say\nthat he had just come from a vaccinating\nseance, where he had just scratched the\nsymmetrical or otherwise calves of some\nhalf-dozen society ''girls.\" One Of the\nnumber was actually so timid and modest,\nyou know, that she cut little round hole\nin her stocking, Just large enough for the\nlance rather than to expose to the profess\nsional eye the soft, white eXpanse of her\nshapely leg. That is, 1 dare say it was\nsoft and white, and shapely.\n\n\nNow, these same charming New York\ngirls are guilty of conspicuousness ON the\nstreet. What would you say if you saw a\nsumner come booming up the avenue. a\ntailor-made suit on, and in the way of a\nwrap, tight-ftting jacket of bright red\ncloth, military-looking fastenings stretch\ning from one side to the other and involv-\ning a tremendous amount of cord. don't\nknow the scientific name for these fasten-\nings, but they are effective and numerous.\nThe edges of this red, danger-signal sort of\ncoat must be trimmed-that is if you are\nto do the exceedingly swell thing-with\nastrakhan. No, there is no compromise.\nIT MUST BE ASTRAKHAN,\nand of course if you insist, you can put on\nother kinds of fur, such as the vulgar seal\nskin, sable OF otter, but then you will be\nwithout the pale of swelldom, and to be\ncorrect, to be dressed unexceptionably, you\nmust have astrakhan and plenty of it. Your\ncoat does not offer space enough for it's dis\nplay. You must also trim the edges of\nyour hat with it. The hat must be of red\ncloth, of small cone shape, and broad\nband of astrakhan around the BASE And\nyou mustn't stop there. If you can't very\nwe'll get any more on your dress and coat,\nand you already have muff made of it,\nwhy, then have coat made of it for your\npet dog. He'll look awfully swell in it.\nactually saw that on Madison avenue yes\nterday, hound tottering along under COW\nering of astrakhan.\n\n\nwent to the Metropolitan the opening\nnight this season. l didn't expect to be\nable to tell what the ladies wore. because\nl knew that not more than from the waist\nupward would be exposed to view from the\nparquette. But 1 was surprised, not to say\ndisappointed.\n\n\nIn the decollete STYLE there were many,\nbut not so many as in previous seasons.\nSquare necks and corsages en- VS. abounded.\nNow, square-neck dress is all right for a\nquiet little dinner, or progressive euchre\nparty, or, say, for church society, but fol\nbox at the opera there is nothing like the\nno-waist dress, commonly called low neck\nand short sleeve. Before the masculine eye\nlooking through acknowledgements, say\nonly moderately-powerful opera glasses, a\ncostume of that kind looms up grandly.\nThe effect is superior. Lines are drawn out\nand soft curves are brought into relief in a\nway that is impossible with the vulgar\nhigh-neck dress, whose principal office\nseems to be to hide that which was meant\n\n=== 5_1910-11-27_pna_sn83045462_00280657827_1910112701_0086.json\nEvening star. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1854-1972 · 1910-11-27\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\n\"But of course you won't\" pursued Miss Payton.\n\"You mustn't! This your life, and it is unworthy.\nDon't let me put any such ideas into your head\n\n\nIl think have learned the value of sound nerves.\nhe said.\n\n\nMiss Payton reflected. At times he seemed almost\nperson of education.\n\n\nA BRIGHT idea occurred to her. At the mansion\nbungalow of the Payton ranch was certain highly\nornamental stand of rosewood, with many supple\nmentary cylinders resembling black lacquer; silent\nFrom this apparatus the operator could produce at will\nghost voices of very live singers. honeyed melodies\ncaught in maze of little craven lines Why not\nganize party and bring this preserved music to Miles\n\n=== 23_1918-10-19_pna_sn84020438_0051368499A_1918101901_0886.json\nPalisadian. [volume] (Cliffside Park, N.J.) 1906-current · 1918-10-19\ncrops: cached\nheadline: White Ghosts-Uqh!\n---\nMany white ghosts appeared in West\nPalisade the past few nights, but they\nwere the coverings for plants too\nlarge to bring indoors and out again.\nThe effect was rather startling for\nstrangers.\n\n=== 20_1902-11-05_pna_sn89053684_00414182380_1902110501_0443.json\nThe Savannah morning news. (Savannah, Ga.) 1900-current · 1902-11-05\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\ndull; medium size vessels. Rosin-\nCork, for ORDERS Is cd per barrel of\n30 pounds. and per cent. primage.\nSpirits-3s Sd per 40 gallons gross, and\n5 per cent. primage. Large vessels,\nrosin. rs Sd; spirits, Is Sd. Steam, lie\nper 100 pounds on rosin; 211z on spir-\nits, Savannah to Boston, and 91sc en-\nrosin. and 19c on spirits to New York\n\n=== 4_1919-06-03_pna_sn87093407_00200290793_1919060301_0213.json\nThe Seattle star. [volume] (Seattle, Wash.) 1899-1947 · 1919-06-03\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\npicions about Certels' pro German\nactivities.\n\n\nNeither could l dictate TO the Sc\neret service men. or. by hurryinE\ntheir operations. cause the immc\ndate arrest Of cerieia. and prevent\nhis marriage 10 chrys.\n\n\nIn fact. tho siowness with which\nthe federal agents proceeded against\nCcrteis puzzled me Sometimes\nwas almost convinced that he was\nnot a spy. but rather. what he claim.\ncd \" be. and that ar secret sery.\nice man he was actually tracking\nand triclSinR tho Boishevikl.\n\n\nTommy and were pledged not to\nbetray the secrets OF the sub cellars\ncouldn't tell chrys what had\nseen there. and indeed. all had lived\nthru In ccrtrls' house often assumed\nthe unrcaiity OF a nightmare OF a\ntime Ions gone.\n\n\nBob--and his fate--and my second\nwooing by my very own husband\nand the successful plotting OF my\nlovely rival absorbed my thoughts\nand made tho rest OF my life mere\nmirage.\n\n\nThen thru the vaguencsa and in.\ndefinitencrs OF other happenings.\ncame the sharp personality OF chrys\nIn my turn | regarded her thru her\nhaze OF blue smoke. she was alto.\ngether \"A project specimen OF an\nAmerican girl. physically superb\nmentally splendid. and quite able to\nDO her own thinking\n\n\nYou wouldn't believe all the\ndreadful things I believe about Ham\nilton Certcis.\" said at last. $50\nwhy should I mention them\n\n\nII wouldn't doubt your word. my\ndear. hut I might insist on drawing\nmy own conclusions from your facts.\nwhatever they are\" she said.\n\n\nHE isn't an American citizen\" |\nreminded her. as II that dcnunciation\ncovered \"A multitude OF sins\n\n\nmo. II he were. he wouldn't be\ngoing TO take possession OF n small\nsection OF Spain. And | couldn't go\nwith him. Jane. dear. why shouldn't\n| go In for my portion OF this worlds\nYou've had your share\nunless Katherine Miller provides\nYOU with more. Why shouldn't l\nclaim my bitF\"\n\n\n\"Oh. la la! ! always knew YOU\nbelieved that ouija board story about\nCcrtcis and his royal ancestors\"\n\n\nIto be Cuntlnued)\n\n=== 8_1846-08-26_pna_sn85026944_00202194278_1846082601_0085.json\nThe banner. [volume] (Abbeville, S.C.) 1844-1847 · 1846-08-26\ncrops: cached\nheadline: FOR THE BANNER.\nWHO is TRULY GREAT.\n---\nmore pleasure than the study of man,\nand the peculiar features which mark\nthe leading propensities of his character,\nand disposition. BY contemplation, 1t is\neasily observed that there are certain\nprinciples in human nature, which\nteach, that every man is created with\nvarious and dissimilar forms of desire,\nwhich move to active and persevering\nexertion. That some are moved to ac-\nton from pure and laudable principles\nof philanthropy, while others are inAa-\nmed by the jealous pride of power, and\ndazzled by the vain glories of aspiring\ndemagogues. And that also the seeds\nof true and real virtue, planted by ma\nture, early springing up, wields at plea\nsure the evil propensities of the one, and\nmakes him look into his own heart, and\nfrom himself learn not alone, the weak\nness of human nature, but to bound and\nset limits to its natural wants and era\nwings. Whilst the inordinate love of\nfame fires the breast of the other. arou-\nses his slumbering energies, paralyzcs\nhis nobler faculties, and prepares HIM\nfor the horrors and bloody scenes\nof unholy strife, and to grasp with\nIl hand goary with the blood of in\nnocency the wreath Of ,ransitory fame.\n\n\nBut how fleeting are its phantoms.\nLike star on the verge Of the horizon,\nthey dazzle in the gaze of the world for\nmoment and then sink into the abyss\nOF darkness, \"even the renowned Ene-\nas, the terror of the Greeks, and whose\nmagnanimity of soul drove him amid\nthe flaming towers Of hoas,cd Troy\"\nbearing upon his back his old father\nANcnIsEs,\" was made to wander even\nan exile from the land he loved and is\nonly remembered in the songs\" and fic-\ntions \"of Virgil\" No conqucror has\never yet shook the world by arms, or\nmade nations bow suppliant a. his feet\nwho was not indeed doomed to 60 down\nin the great vortex Of revolution. His\nfame is low and poor compared with the\nmagnanimity of virtue\" 1t vanlshes\nbefore the greatness Of principle\" The\nmartyr, the patriot, and the phlanthro-\npist. \"The unshrinking adhcrcnt to\ndespair, Of deserted truth and religion\"\nwithout a smiling compassionate friend,\nto extend to him the balm Of consolation.\n\"nor variety Of objects to draw his\nthoughts from himself, with no cheering\nvoice to arouse and nourish energy but\nyields calmly to the most powerful sour\ngings of fortune, and perhaps to the\nmost excrucia:ing pain which one word\nretracted would remove\" is as far supes\nrior to the bloody conqueror, Gas heaven\nabove is superior to all beneath\"\n\n\nGod then, the maker Of heaven and\nearth, and ali things else is alone truly\ngreat. And he who aspires 10 great\nness, should with the rest of his conduct,\npractice christian purity and holiness\nThat greatness is not true, which is\ngained by the sword of an ambitious\nand relentless unfeeling con queror, or\nby the artful intrigues of the aspiring\npolitician. But he is great, and truly\ngreat too, whose generous heart exuIts\nwith proud delight, and swells at the\nsametime, with gratitude and benevo-\nlence to humanity, for the high distinc-\nton to which he may have promoted\nhimself\n\n\nTrue greatness is not then, what the\nworld has always supposed it to be-.\nBut what is it2 and in what does it con.\n- So Il. n\n\n=== 1_1898-05-31_p7_sn86072192_00340583322_1898053101_0498.json\nThe age-herald. · 1898-05-31\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nbeat the sheaves and pins of the blocks to drive the devil out of the gear, and the noise they make is a horrid din. rms IS said to have originated in an order2 given which resulted in disaster A certain Spanish squadron that had been anehoreo some time was surprised by the enemy and could not make sail and escape. as all the blocks were rusted and useiess\n\n The Spanish sailor has prejudice against starting out on a voyage on Fri day, that being the day on which CO., lumbus started on his voyage of discovery. English and American sailors will not sail on that day if they can help it. They would consider the whole trip hoodooed by such beginning.\n\n A sneeze at sea would hooddoo the ship unless it happened to turn to the right, when it brings good luck. Sailors have faith in odd numbers and naval gunners carry out this belief in the number of times they fire a salute\n\n WhistliHg for wind is another favorite superstition of the man before the mast. This is a trick when ships are becalmed, as \"Whtles rash tempests roar\" IA \"eapful of wind\" or a \"a bagfu'' ussl- ally defines the quantity.\n\n The phantom ship.so often seen by nayigators. is not purely the apparition of romance, but mirage, something al- most as wonderful; but not of the super natural order.\n\n The horseshoe is still a popular contri- tion by sailors to the fetish of good luck. A distinguished precedent is given for this, Lord Nelson believed in the luck of the horseshoe, and had one nailed to the mast of his ship Victory\n\n A peculiar superstition is that if women are taken on board a voyaging ship some disaster will follow. This may be the req tn carry women passengers. Sailors, too, pre- fer ship that has a masculine name, or is called after some man, or Is geographical A line of steamers will often employ a cer- tain selection of names to the exclusion or all others. The Cunard steamers end all their names with \"la, feminine terminal, but one that has been the reverse of dis astrous, since that line has mover lost a ship. Captains are quite as superstitious as their crews, being of bettetr education, l, and that may account for the names Of sailing vessels being identical with that of wife or. sweetheart. Certain names are known in the history of nautical nomen- cature as hoodoos. They have been paint ed over and other names substituted, but bad luck attended the ship, and not even reconstruction saved it. Then, if the name must be feminine, the crew want some thing they are familiar with One old tar who was told the name of new ship was the Aurora said:\n\n \"Why don't they leave poetry to the navy folk and stick to the Susannahs and Mary Anns and Jemimas of my day? Aurora! How is a plain able seaman to answer a hail with such a mouthful as that behind his teeth?\"\n\n Sailors cling to their supersttions with a tenacity worthy of better cause. Possibly it is more from custom rather than from an actual belief in their efficacyI The man at the wheel will tell you that he can not steer straight if there is a cross-eyed passenger on board, and he repeats a charm of words to annul the evil: St. Peter! St. Peter, pray give us charm, Against the bad eye that would do us a\n\n harm.\n\n=== 4_1900-01-25_p1_sn88076270_00212475944_1900012501_0701.json\nWilliston graphic. (Williston, Williams County, N.D.) 1895-1919 · 1900-01-25\ncrops: cached\nheadline: 'POSSUM TALKS.\nZcb White Tells About Mountain Ghost.\n\n\nBv M.\n---\nOne evening, when night had fallen\ndark and grim over the peaks of the\nCum berland, and had brought with it\nsuch strange stiffness that one could\nhear the opening of the chestnut\nburrs and the fall of the nuts on the\nshriveled leaves below, l asked the old\n'possum hunter if he had never met\nwith anything weird and mysterious\nup there on the rugged slopes.\n\n\neyes, I've met up with some quare\nthings in my time,\" he replied after a\nminute, land I've always sorter reck-\noned that ghost or speerit once\nhelped me out O. q powerful tight\nplace.\"\n\n\nBeing pressed for the story, he\nstirred up the logs on the fire, lighted\nhis pipe and continued:\n\n\nII reckon 1 have told yo' why went\ninto moonshine whisky. 1t was the\nsame way with all the rest. We could\nturn our con into money that way,\nand in no other. Nobody ever did\nmake any money at it, and every man\nabout a still run the risk of bein' sent\noff to state prison most any day in the\nweek. I did heap o thinllin' befo' l\nwent into the bizness. The ole woman\nwas agin it from the start, and many a\nnight she woke me up as she cried\nout: G.\n\n\n\"'Zeb White, I've bin dreamin' that\nthem revenoo fellers had cotched yo\nand sent yo' to the penitentiary fur\ntwo y'ars!'\n\n\n\"Bimeby,\" continued the old man, ~\ndecided to go into it. We had to pack\nthe still 10 miles over the hills on oul\n\n=== 7_1898-07-10_pna_sn85042461_00280769800_1898071001_0138.json\nThe herald. [microfilm reel] (Los Angeles [Calif.]) 1893-1900 · 1898-07-10\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nthe wall that Delaigne was knocked\ndown.\n\n\nSomething wrong here\" cried the\nrider, leaping from his horse.\n\n\nHe fell squarely upon Delairne.\nThere was a short, fierce struggle in\nthe dark, and at the end, overpowered\nby numbers. for several horsemen came\nto the help of their comrade. the\nAmerican found himself in the hands\nOf Spanish soldiers, a prisoner.\n\n\n(TO BE CONCLUDED - [\n\n=== 2_1914-04-01_p1_sn83016689_00279552003_1914040102_0910.json\nThe Detroit times. · 1914-04-01\ncrops: cached\nheadline: GETS JUDGMENT FOR\n\n\nWATCH GIVEN AS PLEDGE\n---\n1t was beautiful dress.\n\n\nFor days II had haunted Its grace.\nfull curves. its snowy laces, its sneen\nof silk and melting shades before the\neyes Of Mrs. Anna Watson. She\nlonged for that dress. she dreamed OF\nIT, saw 1t In her waking hours. felt\nits softness. pictured Its drape on her\nfigure.\n\n\nBut she lacked IS of having enough\nto buy il. She turned and twisted\nand planned and figured and wore out\npencils without number. trying to\nfigure the answer. Then one day she\nsaw her husbands watch on the\ndresser.\n\n\nThat night she had the dress and\nSteinberg Bros, No. 213 Michigan\nave.. had the watch as security for\nthe payment of the balance on the\ndress. A few days later fire swept\nthe place and when Mrs. Watson\nwent down to redeem the watch, II\nhad gone up In smoke. Wednesday\nshe brought suit and based her claims\non the fact that as the watch was\nnot hers, the company must return II\nor Its value to the original owner, her\nhusband.\n\n\nJustice DeGaw found for the wom-\nan, and the attorney for the defen-\ndant will appeal.\n\n=== 52_1905-02-25_pna_sn86090528_00295864448_1905022501_0362.json\nThe Chickasha daily express. (Chickasha, Indian Territory [Okla.]) 1899-current · 1905-02-25\ncrops: cached\nheadline: PHANTOM SHIp\nOR\nThe Flying Dutchmon.\n\n\n~~s-T cAsEAN MARRYAr\n\n\n---\nCTFAPTERXXVII- Continued\n\n\nI'VE are Hollanders.\" replied Philip\n\n\n\"A part of the crew of the vesse\nEhich was wrecked?\" inquired he\n\n\n\"Yes!\"\n\n\nYou have nothing to fear; you ar\n\n\nnemies to the Portuguese, and so ar,\nwe. We belong to the island OF Ter\nnate-our king is at war with tn,\nPortuguese, who are villains. Wher,\nare your companions? on which isl\nRnd?\"\n\n\n\"They are all dead\" replied Philip\nMAY l ask you whether you have fall\nen- in with woman who was adrift or\npart Of the raft by herself; or have\nyou heard of herr\"\n\n\n\"We have heard that a woman was\npicked up on the beach to the south\nward, and carried away by the Tidore\npeople to the Portuguese settlement on\nthe supposition that she was Portu-\nguese.\"\n\n\n\"Then God be thanked, she is saved, \"\ncried Philip. \"Merciful Heavenl accept\nmy thanks. To Tidore you said?\"\n\n\nEyes; we are at war with the Por-\nkuguese, we cannot take you there\"\n\n\nmol but we shall meet again\"\n\n\nThe person who accosted them was\nevidently Of consequence. His dress\nwas, to certain degree, Mohammedan,\nbut mixed up with Malay; he carried\narms in his girdle and spear in his\nhand; his turban was Of printed\nchintz; and his department, like most\npersons Of rank in that country, was\ncourteous and dignified.\n\n\n1--WE are now returning to Ternate\nand will take you with us. Our king\nwill be pleased to receive any Hol-\nlanders, especially as you are enemies\nto the Portuguese dogs, forgot to\ntell you that we have one of your\ncompanions with us in the boat; we\npicked him up at sea much exhausted,\nbut he is now doing we'll\"\n\n\nwho can it be?\" observed Krantz;\nut must be some Que belonging to\nsome other vessel\"\n\n\nNO.\" replied Philip shuddering, at\nmust be Schriften.\"\n\n\n'\"Then my eyes must behold him be-\nfore | believe It,\" replied Krantz\n\n\nThen believe your eyes, replied\nPhilip, pointing to the form of Schrif-\nten, who was now walking toward\nthem.\n\n\n\"Mynheer Vanderdecken, glad to see\nyou. Mynheer Krantz, hope you are\nwell. How lucky that we should all be\nsaved. Hel he!\"\n\n\nTHE ocean has then, indeed, given\nap its dead, as requested\" thought\nPhilip.\n\n\nIn the meantime Schriften, without\nmaking any reference to the way in\nwhich they had so unceremoniously\nparted company, addressed Krantz\nwith apparent good humor and some\nslight tinge of sarcasm.\n\n\nWhat do you think Of him.\nKrantg?\"\n\n\nTHAT he is a part of the whole, and\nhas his destiny to fulfill as we'll as\nyou. He has his part to play in this\nwondrous mystery. and will remain\nUntil 1t is finished. Think not of him.\naecollect, your Anime is safe\"\n\n\nTrue\" replied Philip, \"the wretch\nS not worth thought; we have now\nto do but to embark with\nhese people; hereafter we may rid\nourselves of him and strive then to re-\nDIN my dearest Anime\"\n\n=== 10_1858-05-08_pna_sn85038518_00200293253_1858050801_0436.json\nNashville union and American. [volume] (Nashville, Tenn.) 1853-1862 · 1858-05-08\ncrops: cached\nheadline: NEW BOOKS.\n---\nTbs Life and Work Of Isaac VISITS.\nms Llfs Ct Sir Henry Hanl0sk, K OBJ ~ =\nThs CoEmnaiou Tabls By Dr. Cumming\nThs Infant SalvanOn By Dr. Cummihg,\nIN . Baptismal F0unt by DR. Cumming.\nChrist0ur Passover bT DE Cumming So -~-r\nThoughts On he lnf0snce Of The Holy Spirit b. DR.\n\n\nCumming.\n\n\nThe Finger q God By Dr. Cumming. - s..\nThe Great SscTsE by Dr. Cumming ---\nTIE PrOInate signs Of The eoss0f this Dispsnaiion\n\n\nBy Dr. Cumming. gg av7si -frr\nSigns Of th8Times 81 Or Cumming. 40 eos\nPhilosophy Of The Plan Of SalvhtiOn--WsIkef. o nl\nPhilosophy Of skeptieism and UltFaism-Wslkar,\nAfEander Fletchers Sermons--eomplsts\nsigns q the Tines b. Bunsan.\nThe Methodist Preacher.\n\n\nHere and Hereafter BY Athsrn.\nThe Prince Of the House Of David. -\nDR. Cummings Daily Family DsVotlon.\nLife Of Dr. Kane by Elder\n\n\nDr. Kenss Arctic kxplorstonE\n\n\nDr LIVing5tOne's Reseaicaes IN Africa\nDiscoveries m North and Central Africa bV Barb\nPartons Lite Of Aaron Burr\n\n\nAnnual Of Scientific scovsry for 1855.\nHand ok OF Household Sciencs-Youmau\nLiterary Attractions Of tbe Bibe-Halsey.\nWOrthsR's Cyelopedia Of d aEing.\nThe Pitts-StTest Chmch Lectures\nFor ale by JOHN YORK 5 co.\nmaya No IS Union street.\n\n\n=== 44_1911-05-13_pna_sn82016014_00295870680_1911051301_0502.json\nThe Topeka state journal. [volume] (Topeka, Kansas) 1892-1980 · 1911-05-13\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nThe Spiritualists of Topeka will hold\nservices at the Lincoln Post hall on\nSixth avenue on Sunday evening, May\nIA, at o'clock sharp. Rev. Charles\nH Moody, state president Kansas\nSpiritualists association, will deliver\na lecture on Spiritualism. Gertrude\nc. Aber will assist in the services and\nWilliam w. Aber will follow with his\npsychic messages.\n\n=== 96_1895-10-08_pNone_sn85042461_00280769630_1895100801_0464.json\nThe herald. · 1895-10-08\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nDRS. J. B. AND MRS. LEVET, CLAIR\n\n\nvoyants, test mediums, mental and\nmagnetic bealers; all diseases diagnosed\nclairvoyantly free; we invite the invalids\nto come and test oar power; sittings daily;\ndeveloping circle Tuesday night; seance\nevery Friday night. The Menlo, 400 S.\nMain ST.. rooms 4 and 5. l1-4\n\n=== 18_1871-03-04_pna_sn85040344_00271769593_1871030401_0094.json\nThe Superior times. [volume] (Superior, Wis.) 1870-1912 · 1871-03-04\ncrops: cached\nheadline: A WOODEN LEGGED GHOST.\n\nA Real True ry.''--Spirituality\n\n\nand its Agents.\n---\nwith the remark, that\n\n\nMost people turn willing ear to\nevery revelation of the marvellous\nSuperstition and credulity can scarce\n\" be regarded as salient features of our\nnineteenth century civilization, and yet\nfew of us would like to confess how,\nafter all we have given our faith of the\nsupernatural Ghost stories, which have\nnot lost their secret charm for the\nwisest as well as the simplest OF\ncourse, in the light of science and of\nreason, all these things, are nonsense:\nbut after all, it is a nonsense for which\nwe cannot help keeping Il soft spot III\nour hearts\"\n\n\n| think il was Charles Lamb who\nreasoned that science had established\nthe fact that the subject of ghosts was\nnot theory, or anything of that indeti-\nmite sort, and he worked it out\" III\nthis way:\n\n\ncit is now admitted to be a scientific\ntruth, that at the end Of every seventh\nyear of every MAS life, or every wom-\nans life, not particle of physical hui\nmainly remains in their system which\nwas IN 1T seven years before\"\n\n\nEvery seven years, therefore, a man\nor woman Is \"not that man > or woman,\nbut another man or woman. Every\nseven years there has been peeled off\nfrom each concentrlc ring, as from\nthe concentric ring of an onion, shad-\nowy representative of the individual\nperson, which is wandering In space\nincreasing by one at the end OF every\nseven years. And this was Lambs the\nDRY of ghosts. Every seven years a\nmans ghost left him, to appear after\nward anywhere, and at any time, and\n''no questions asked, Of a cold night\nas IN the poor buried King OF Den\nmark S case. Now, reader, has not this\nthought, s. forcibly expressed by\nJoanna Bailey, sometimes entered your\nmind?\n\n\nq shall the departed gaze thee again?\n\n\nShall glide past thee in the noontide hour,\n\n\nAnd thou perceive il not? think'st perhaps\n\n\nlis but the mourtul breeze that passes by -\n\n\nSitting beside winter fire of an\nevening Ill a roundabout chair upon a\nsolitary hearth, in a house where the\nunwonted sound of foOtstep is to be\nheard only after the summons of q bell,\nhave you never encountered those\n\"ministering spirits2' [ think YOU\nhave, / know / have.\n\n\nThe fact is, that there is not one fam\nlily In ten but has its traditionarv ghost\nstory, which, when narrated to the\ngroup that gathers round the winter\ntireside, excites, according to the age\nand character of the listeners, terror\nsympathy, doubt, incredality and ridi-\nME al or 81,\nand cherished in after life; for it per\ntains to that world of untried being,\nwhich approaches toward us with its\nslow and nolseless but irresistible and\noverwhelming movement.\n\n\nThe incident narrated below, by an\nold friend and correspondent of mine.\nwas told first to me at his own most\nhospitable hearth, and afterward, at NV\nearnest request, reduced to writing,\nfrom which the present narrative is\ngreatly reduced, vet contains in com\npact form, all that is necessary for the\nfull understanding of the reader. The\nimportant point made by Lamb, as to\nthe eccentric theory of escaped ghosts,\nI'm rings, every seven years, in a wood\nen- leg, which does not expand, and\nbOurgeon, and fade, and vanish, in\nshadow with the human svstem-\" that\nis the question: The very remarkable\nincident took place in the Island of Do\nm'nca, IN relation to which there is at\npresent some national agitation, which\npromises to increase as time goes on.\n\n\nDuring Il period of considerable ex-\ncitement, arising from a season of great\nmortality among the inhabitants of the\nisland, IN the year -, Il veteran Scot\nt Sh regiment was stationed upon the\nhigh blum Of land which forms one\npoint Of crescentular bay, and over\nlooks the town and harbor. Inland. to-\nward the east, small plain extends it\nself, while, on the west and north.\nwhich is nearest the shore, and almost\noverhangIng 1t, are several low, one\nstory buildings, hastily erected, Of\nwood, Tor the accommodation of the\nofficers OF the corps, and consisting all\nof three or four rooms In each end, with\npiazza on the side toward the sea, cx-\ntending the whole length OF the struc-\nture, and forming shaded and agreea-\nbic promenade during the earlier part\nOF the day. The rooms opened upon\nthe piazza and communicated with each\nother by means of u side-door, which\nwas occasionally kept open for the freer\ncirculation of air\n\n\nIn one of these barracks were quar-\ntered three officers of the regiment\nMajor Hamilton, Capt. Gordon. and a\nthird, whose name [ cannot at this mo-\nment recall.\n\n\nMajor Hamilton's apartment was in\nthe center. He had lost a leg in the\nservice; and usually wore a wooden pin\nq stick shod with iron, and being an\nalert man, fond of exercise, used TO\nwalk mp and down this piazza for hours\ntogether, stopping occasionally at Cor\ndon's door or window, and sometimes\nlooking In at that of the other officer.\nexchanging q cheerful word with them,\nrs they sat, each in his apartment. en-\ndeavorlng to begule the time with\ndressing, reading, writing, thoughts OF\npromotion, of home, and of a speedy\nAID happy return to Britain\n\n\nThe sound of the Majors step was\npeculiar. 1t Was only the blow given\nby the iron ferule nc the end of his\nwooden leg that was heard; for, altho'\nstout man, he trod lightly with the\nremaining foot, and heavily only with\nthe wooden substitute, which gave forth\nIt's note at short intervals, as he paced\nto and fro so regularly that there was\na certain pleasure in listening TO it.\nSounds that strike the ear in this\nmeasured way affect Us more than 9th\ners. The attention becomes engaged,\naud they grow emphatic rs We listen.\n\n\nThe calker's hammer stroke, as it flies\nfrom the dock-yard of the busy port\n\n=== 74_1881-03-12_p3_sn82015483_00271764170_1881031201_0246.json\nMorning journal and courier. · 1881-03-12\ncrops: cached\nheadline: The Grim Spectre Follows Vaccination.\n\n BRooKLYN, March 11.-Chief Engineer\n---\nJames W. Whitaker, of the United States navy, died yesterday in this city from an at- tack of erysipe1as, resulting from recent vac cination. He was born in New Jersey in 1886, and was appointed to the navy in 1857. He at one time was on special duty as inspec- tor in the Portsmouth, N. H., navy yard.\n","numLines":1308,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1308}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"cdfb9e4b-6525-49f4-9fd9-06588faad86b","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"other"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","git