things which they do not 1Oih. But there are\n264\ttwo ailings in the discovery Of the great Doctor\n265\tbodies.\n266\t\n267\t\n268\tl. Grant it true, that we have no moral power\n269\tand it is only the worse for the STAKEHOLDER. There\n270\tis sympathy getting up among us like steam. II\n271\tmust have vent. If it cannot get at the slavehold\n272\ters through the moralchannel, they must look to il\n273\tthat it do not some day or other, come down upon\n274\tthem in the physical.\n275\t\n276\t\n277\t2. The very power which they tell us we have\n278\tnot, is ut aOorh through the appointed consti\n279\ttutional grooves-and tits too late to stopit. On\n280\tthis subject we must quote the eloquent Herald oil\n281\tFreedom, (versus Dr. Wayland) to which we\n282\tcounsel all aOeak abohtonists occasionally to resort\n283\tfor dose of cayene and quinine. We will say\n284\tthat we need not get vote from congress against\n285\tslavery in order to its abolition, there and every\n286\twhere. Congress? what is it? The mere dregs\n287\tand precipitationS; the settings ahd sediments Of\n288\tthe nation. 1t is as soulless as corporation. Ii\n289\thas no soul, no mind, no principle, no opinion.\n290\t1t is an echo, and that not always a true one. 1t\n291\tis mere catastrophe-an upshot. 1t will only\n292\tnutter the word abolition, after it has become an\n293\told story through the country. We have struck\n294\tslavery its death blow already. We need not con-\n295\ttend with the DR.. aboAt power. One thing you\n296\thave DONE said an eminent Judge to us, you have\n297\tdriven the South to come out and declare directly\n298\tin favour of slavery. Heretofore, they have pre-\n299\ttended to lament it. as an evil. Now they decl are\n300\tit is blessing, and righteous institution. Have\n301\twe not, said we, driven them to join the issue, be-\n302\tfore the world, in favor of savcholdng? You\n303\thave said the Judge Must they not maintain it\n304\tbefore the world, said we, to save the