| Pool he muttered between his clencbed teeth,\n783\t| II have you now have you now\"\n784\t\n785\t\n786\tCHAPTER III.\n787\t| The Burglars Nest was well named.\n788\tSituated in one OF the loneliest and most mac\n789\tcessible parts Of Pennsylvania it, existence was\n790\thardly known; few travellers stopped there, and\n791\t\n792\t\n793\tONE Of those that did were never head from\n794\tagain, so little could DC lNid.\n795\tRugged and steep was the way that led TO it,\n796\twith here and there frightfu rayine TO break the\n797\tgloomy expanse Of giant trees and tangled shrub\n798\tberry that lined the road for mses.\n799\tTlse structure itself was venerable wood build\n800\t\n801\t\n802\tng, evidently built during the Revolutionary war,\n803\tthough fer Whist purpose could hardly be con.\n804\tcoived in so Wild and unfreQuented portion OF\n805\tThe country. Twenty-Five years ago the tourist,\n806\tIa his woodland raWbles, might have often met\n807\twith mysterious looking haoitations. long I'M\n808\ttenanted which 10 the country people had .\n809\tghostly prestige; where spectral lights could be\n810\tseen at night, and unearthly noises were heard\n811\tduring the day; Of which variety Of dark\n812\tstories could be told, terrible 10 childrens ears,\n813\tand listened 10 even by 'hc stranger with vague\n814\thorror.\n815\t\n816\t\n817\tThese old abodes were perhaps some former\n818\ttavern, where dreadful murders we're said 20 have\n819\tbeen perpetrated, still haunted by the troubled\n820\tspirits Of the victims; the sign swinging mourn\n821\tfully in the wind. and the door broken from its\n822\thinges, from which issued the reptile and it's COM\n823\tpanion, the owl, screeching its melancholy song\n824\tduring the night\n825\t\n826\t\n827\tway houses and Boar Hesds, Of olden time.\n828\tbut the Buresrs Nest hcssa q Haa9oesfa sA a.\n829\tthen something dark and tragcsi would take\n830\tplace, but there were no ghosts TO vex the actors\n831\tin the bloody scene, sa