relating to Pliny, Archi-\n1191\tmodes, Marine and Sir Isaac Newton are\n1192\tillustrations of passive eccentricity, the\n1193\treverie of genius. They were, it is we'll\n1194\tknown, conspicuous for the absence Of the\n1195\tcommon modes and courtesies of life, but\n1196\tthis forgotten In those whose works will\n1197\tlive in glory, long after their eccentrici-\n1198\tties are obliterated. Many brilliant scars\n1199\tof literature are endowed with the so often\n1200\tfatal gift Of genius, in whom intellect not\n1201\tonly begins to grow, but to burst forth\n1202\tinto blossom while yet it's organ Is still\n1203\tin the bud. So many gentuses are devel-\n1204\toped often in infancy, and frequently the\n1205\tso-called infant prodigy who does not ul-\n1206\ttimately become a genius, will stop half\n1207\tway becoming insane. Insanity is good\n1208\thaving well passed its confines, will in all\n1209\tprobability develop into a genius;a but,\n1210\talas, many fail to pass this barrier, and,\n1211\tconsequently our institutions are full Of\n1212\tbrilliant inteNects cut short in the pre-\n1213\tcocity Of their youth, especially if any I'M\n1214\t\n1215\t\n1216\tdue excitement be administered or en-\n1217\tcouraged in the Inflated subject of pre-\n1218\tcocity. The genius Of Sir Walter. Scott\n1219\tended in imbecility. He first became con-\n1220\tscious Of his owncondition by partial loss\n1221\tOf memeory, and a want OF recognition,\n1222\teven OF his own sonnets. The early COM\n1223\tposition Of Scott was wonderful, but in\n1224\tglancing at the later efforts, there was\n1225\tevidence to the psychologist Of that faced\n1226\tdegeneration OF tubular neurine which\n1227\tprobably began with his reverses and kept\n1228\tpace with his wonderful toll to liquidate\n1229\this pecuniary debts; and the effort to do\n1230\tthis was SO great that his mental state\n1231\tbecame unhinged. ~\n1232\t\n1233\t\n1234\t\". .sTlis wretched brain gave way,\n1235\t\n1236\t\n1237\tAnd became a wreck at random driven,\n1238\tWithout one glimpse of reason or Of\n1239\t\n1240\t\n1241\tb. Heaven =\n1242\t\n1243\t\n1244\tThe cerebral condition of Scott resem-\n1245\tbled in many ways the many sided genius\n1246\tOf the immortal Bard of Avon, and he\n1247\twould probably, under happier ctrcum-\n1248\tstances, have reached a good old age.\n1249\tdied in the meridian Of his\n1250\tsplendor Of foolish excess. We have it\n1251\tin the diary of Mr.. Ward, who was init\n1252\tmate with Shakespeare, and who wrote his\n1253\tdiary which is preserved in the records Of\n1254\tthe Medical Society Of London. In this\n1255\twe read that Shakespeare. Drayton and\n1256\tBen Johnson had merry meeting, and, it\n1257\tseems, drank too hard, for Shakespeare\n1258\tdied of fever there contracted\"\n1259\t\n1260\t\n1261\tThe insanity of genius is itself psyche\n1262\tlogical problem, and comes before us with\n1263\tmost awful Contrasts spectral life in\n1264\tdeath Illusion is a prominent charac-\n1265\tteristic of genius; this is not wondered\n1266\tat when we consider that the workings of\n1267\tan lima Curative mind are one continued\n1268\tand protracted course of ideal creation.\n1269\tOF this, illusion when the mind is con-\n1270\tscious does not constitute insanity any\n1271\tmore than it can be termed disease, for\n1272\tmental wandering may be under the con-\n1273\ttroll Of the will, whilst the systematic\n1274\tfunctions and the concomitant organs are\n1275\tin perfectly healthy condition.\n1276\t\n1277\t\n1278\t\" Space is not at my disposal to enter\n1279\tfully into details respecting all the\n1280\tgeniuses who have become admittedly\n1281\tthe victims OF insanity, but might emu\n1282\tmerate briefly some Of the poetical ones\n1283\twhose minds ultimately gave way but\n1284\twhose works live after them. a\n1285\t\n1286\t\n1287\t1914. TorQuato Tasso suffered from\n1288\tHmania periodique.' He was, unfor-\n1289\ttlirately, a victim to the literary envy OF\n1290\tt