 the commercial Instinct\"\n1395\tsaid Oscar s. Straus. \"Hume tells us\n1396\tthat under Richard l. 500 Jews sought\n1397\tsafety in the castle Of York, where, not\n1398\tbeing strong enough for defense, they\n1399\tkilled their wives and children, threw\n1400\tthe bodies over the walls into the mob\n1401\tbelow and, setting fire to the castle,\n1402\twere themselves burned alive. The\n1403\tgentry owing money to the Jews has\n1404\ttened to the cathedral, where their\n1405\tbonds or promissory notes were kept,\n1406\tand made bonfire before the altar.\n1407\tThe compiler Of the Annals Of Waiver\n1408\tley, in relating the event. Hume says,\n1409\t'blesses the Almighty for thus deliver\n1410\ting over this impious race to destruc-\n1411\ttion.' History blazes with such stories\n1412\tOf self sacrifice and barbarity. The\n1413\tcommercial instinct in this particular\n1414\tcase appears to have been on the other\n1415\tside.\n1416\t\n1417\t\n1418\t\"Instead Of the Jew being natural\n1419\tbarterer, he Is the most spiritual man\n1420\talive. He has been pounded through\n1421\tthe ages, robbed and massacred, but he\n1422\tyields neither his traditions nor his\n1423\tideals. Had he been materialistic he\n1424\twould have sold out long ago and\n1425\tbought peace. If he would join the\n1426\tGreek church he would be safe in Rus\n1427\tsia Rather than do that he will walk\n1428\tinto the jaws of hell. Therefore say\n1429\tthat he is spiritualistic or idealistic\n1430\trather than practical or material. Were\n1431\tI to choose a family that would live\n1432\twould have it meet hardships and per-\n1433\tsecutions. Were to choose one to die\n1434\tI would give it pleasure and luxury.\n1435\tThe Jew, denied his civil rights, de-\n1436\tspoiled Of his property, scourged and\n1437\tmurdered, has only been made tougher\n1438\tand longer lived. His enemies have\n1439\tgiven him strength and trained him\n1440\tmost admirably for the battle Of life,\n1441\tno matter in what part of the world\n1442\tfortune may lead him.\" New York\n1443\tWorld .\n1444\t\n1445\t=== 14_1907-12-10_p4_sn84024827_0021110689A_1907121001_0747.json\n1446\tBisbee daily review. · 1907-12-10\n1447\tcrops: cached\n1448\theadline: TRUE GHOST STORY.\n1449\t\n1450\tDead Wife Calls Husband to the\n1451\t\n1452\t Spirit World.\n1453\t\n1454\tJEALOUS OF HER SUCCESSOR.\n1455\t---\n1456\tDying. She Told Her Mate That She\n1457\t\n1458\t Would Come Back if He Ever Mar\n1459\t\n1460\t ried Again, and She Kept Her Word\n1461\t\n1462\t With Fatal Effect.\n1463\t\n1464\t The following true ghost story is told by correspondent of the St Lou Is Republic. who declares the facts came partly under his own notice and were in part told to him by eyewit- nesses:\n1465\t\n1466\t Philip Hartmann was big. easy going, good natured. lazy fellow. We called him Elephant at school. Early\n1467\t\n1468\t=== 3_1897-10-23_p1_sn83016810_00280768005_1897102301_0135.json\n1469\tThe Appeal. [volume] (Saint Paul, Minn. ;) 1889-19?? · 1897-10-23\n1470\tcrops: cached\n1471\theadline: NASHVILLE, TENN.\n1472\t\n1473\tA FEWlTEMS FROM THE EXPO\n1474\t\n1475\t\n1476\tSlTION CITY\n1477\t\n1478\tPrescription Declared to be no Greater\n1479\tThan In Mianesota-Rev. George w.\n1480\tGaines Gets a Mild Roast Some Mime\n1481\tsotians Cotuplimented.\n1482\t---\n1483\tThe many attractions at the Centennial\n1484\t\n1485\t\n1486\tExposition this week. are above the\n1487\t| ordinary and general ruu of am easements\n1488\t\n1489\t\n1490\tand inducements offered by similar Elk\n1491\tpositions. Great care is m anifeated b y\n1492\tthe c )mmittee on amusements as to the\n1493\t\n1494\t\n1495\tvariety and diversity in the entertain\n1496\tmenta provided for the visitors, and no\n1497\teffort is left undone to make the visitors\n1498\t\n1499\t\n1500\tto feel welcome. In fact several special\n1501\t\n1502\t\n1503\tdays were set apart, on which all Afro\n1504\t\n1505\t\n1506\tAmericans vera admitted at half p1 ics\n1507\t\n1508\t\