earted heathenism to take,\nHim. on whose brow, 1.-THE pure baptismal dew\nWas shed which marked him of the fold of\n\n\nChrist?\nEven now, though rollSt above him, with the\n\n\nplay\nOfall thy crested waves, mocking the trust\nWhich from the footing of the firm, green earth\nHe drew. TO place on thee. Thou to mis- eye\nDid'st hold fair pictures ofthe snowy sail\nSwelling in beauty, the foreign port\nRepleie with wealth, and of the glad return.\nHow hast thou kept thy promise, faithless main\nWe ask thee, holc he died?\n\n\nThe storm was high,\nAnd mid deep midnight, on the slippery shrouds\nHe missed his footing. Loose he swung, and\n\n\nwide\nOver the boiling surge, single rope\nGrasping convulsively, and on the blast\nPouring wild cries for help. The vesscllurchd\nThe strong surge swept the deck, and from the\n\n\nbreast\nOF the cold pillows. rose voice of prayer,\nUnto redeeming love.\n\n\nA rope was cast.\nHe failed TO reach its aid; boat was lowered,\n1t could not live within that Tasirg sea,\nThe cheering no of his comrades shout\nSank meath the tumult OF the thunder Hast,\nAnd soon deaih-silence settled where ne strovc\nAwhile, with painting breast\nRelentless deepl\n1t doth no; srievr thee, that broken heart\nLies hcary in mothers breast, for him,\nOr that pale brow'd maiden counts the hours\nBy sound Of dropping tears.\n\n\nBut there shall come\nblast of trumpet, and Thy startled depths\nAll The ref spoil of earth shall render back\nAtom BY atom.\n\n\nThen may'st thou arise,\nIn glorious beauty, Sailor Boy, and hear\nHis welcome voice, whose name was on thy lips\nWhen the deaih-wave broke over thee, and find\nA blessed home ofeverlasiing peace,\nWhere there is no more sea.\n\n=== 11_1868-04-09_p1_sn83045646_00279528621_1868040901_0515.json\nDaily Davenport Democrat. [volume] (Davenport, Iowa) 1864-1874 · 1868-04-09\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nKIT Carson passed through Montana on\nFriday last The editor Of the Democrat\nSAY him, and says: /\" Poor Kit-eld. thin,\nattenuatsd in the last stages Of consump-\nton, DYING day by day, will soon be sum\nmopsd to the hunting ground in the spirit\nland -\"\n\n=== 5_1840-06-16_pna_sn85040303_00514151313_1840061601_0002.json\nSouthport telegraph. [volume] (Southport, W.T. [i.e. Kenosha, Wis.]) 1840-1850 · 1840-06-16\ncrops: cached\nheadline: RICHMOND PALACE--TfIE FATE\nOF KINGS AND OITEENS\n---\nOF KINGS AND Queens.\n\n\nFrom Rambles among the Rivers, IA Bcntley's Mis-\n\n\nceany.\n\n\nOF thc ancient palace OF England, which\nstood on the Richmond green, little is left\nnow except the gateway, and that little Of\ners nothing TO satisfy the gaze of any but\nhe mere antiquay. 1t does not look old\nand venerable enough for the lover oil the\npicture Squc, being so patched up, by and\nweGgcd in between surrounding houses, as\n10 have almost lost its distinctive character\nSeveral kings and queens Of England lived\nand died upon this spot. Edward1 and Ii.\nresided here, and Edward LII. died here,\ndeserted in that last hour'5y ali the hatter-\ncrs and parasites who had faitencd upon\nhis bounty; even Alice Pierce the mistress\nof his bosom, flying from his side, and\nleaving him to die with no more attendance\nthan il he had been bcggar, givngup the\nghost in a ditch. Richard II. the next\nking, passed much Of his time at this ma\nnor; in whose days, at Sheen, rs we arc\ninformed by that vcracious chronicler.\nStowe, \"therc was a great fighting an ong\ntho gnais! They were 50 thick gathered\"\nsays he, \"that the air was darkcncd, and\nthey fought and made a great battle. Two\nparts of them being slain, fell down 10 the\nground, the third part having got the vic\ntory, flew away, no man knew whthcr--\nThe number Of the dead was such that they\nmight be swept up with Bosom, and bush\nels filled with them\" With what a gnsto\ndoes the old historian describe the battle!\nhow persuaded he seems Of lis truth and\nwith what a relish for the marvellous, and\nexpectation to find the same in his reader,\ndoes he note every circumstance! Many\nof the battles between the rival houses OF\nYork and Lancaster, arc dismissed by him\nwith hardly more notice.\n\n\nAnne, the Queen Of Richard IT,, died in\nn is building. She was so tcndcrly hco-\nwe'd by her husband that be cursed the place\nwhere she died, and would never after\nwards nhabit it. The very sight Of the\nbuilding so moved him with grief, that he\ngive directions that it should be pulled\ndown. The order was only partially exc\ncuted, and the building remained in a ru-\nnous condition until the time Of Henry v.\nwho repaired it, and founded three religious\nhouses near it. |; was destroyed by fire\nIN the reign of Henry VII. who built it up\nagain more magnificcntIy than before, and\nfirst altered tho name Of the village from\nSheen to Richmond, which it has ever\nsince borne. Henry Will, also resided\nhere in the early part Of his reign, and\nonce instituted a grand tournament on the\ngreen, at which he fought in disguise, -.\nlie afterwards exchanged it with Wolscy\nCor the more magnificent palace Of IIamp-\nrton Court; but after the fall, aad death OF\nNhOt minister, the palace again reverted TO\nthe crown Elizabeth was confined in il\nfor short time, during the reign Of her\nsister, and here she died broken hearted,\nfor the death Of the Earl Of Essex. Du-\nring the disscnsion Of tho revolution, this\nfpalacc met some rough treatment from the\nthanis of the republicans, and tho greater.\npart of il was pulled down. 1t has never,\nsince held up its head in The world, but has\ngradually pinned away TO its present condi-\nnon.\n\n=== 4_1839-04-06_pna_sn84022687_00202199240_1839040601_0047.json\nThe voice of freedom. [volume] (Montpelier, Vt.) 1839-1848 · 1839-04-06\ncrops: cached\nheadline: The Power Question.\n---\nThe Power Question.\n\n\nIn the Greek case no body discovered that W.\ncould render no aid to the side of liberty, 6ec0aS\n2ce mere nat Turks. Those who could do no more\nclapped Henry Clay's speech heartily, on the pnn\ncple that even W5nd is matter and every TITLE helps\nBut, in the American Slave case, the Rabble\nhave discovered that northern people, millions\nupon millions enough to tie the Shareholders all Vi\nin one sack, and toss them into Symmes' hole, hav\nno manner of responsibility for the existence o\nSlavery, nor power for its abolition! What do they\nmean? Do they mean We haven't the physical\nthe blanket-tossing power? Probably not. They\nmean, rather, that we have, legally and constitution\nally, no tongue and type power-none OF mat pow\nor which sets all other powers golng-Whlch per-\nSuGdeS men to a0iSh to do many things, and to III\nsome things which they do not 1Oih. But there are\ntwo ailings in the discovery Of the great Doctor\nbodies.\n\n\nl. Grant it true, that we have no moral power\nand it is only the worse for the STAKEHOLDER. There\nis sympathy getting up among us like steam. II\nmust have vent. If it cannot get at the slavehold\ners through the moralchannel, they must look to il\nthat it do not some day or other, come down upon\nthem in the physical.\n\n\n2. The very power which they tell us we have\nnot, is ut aOorh through the appointed consti\ntutional grooves-and tits too late to stopit. On\nthis subject we must quote the eloquent Herald oil\nFreedom, (versus Dr. Wayland) to which we\ncounsel all aOeak abohtonists occasionally to resort\nfor dose of cayene and quinine. We will say\nthat we need not get vote from congress against\nslavery in order to its abolition, there and every\nwhere. Congress? what is it? The mere dregs\nand precipitationS; the settings ahd sediments Of\nthe nation. 1t is as soulless as corporation. Ii\nhas no soul, no mind, no principle, no opinion.\n1t is an echo, and that not always a true one. 1t\nis mere catastrophe-an upshot. 1t will only\nnutter the word abolition, after it has become an\nold story through the country. We have struck\nslavery its death blow already. We need not con-\ntend with the DR.. aboAt power. One thing you\nhave DONE said an eminent Judge to us, you have\ndriven the South to come out and declare directly\nin favour of slavery. Heretofore, they have pre-\ntended to lament it. as an evil. Now they decl are\nit is blessing, and righteous institution. Have\nwe not, said we, driven them to join the issue, be-\nfore the world, in favor of savcholdng? You\nhave said the Judge Must they not maintain it\nbefore the world, said we, to save the institution\nfrom going down? They must he replied\nCan they maintain it said we. No, said he,-\nand yet the Judge is not an abolitionst.\"\n\n\nWe need not contend with this Wayland and\nwayward President, for the power citizens or as\nmen, to beat down southern slaveholding-We\nhave erercized the poover already, and the South\nknows it. We have walked the nation to discuss\nthe dement's of the system and the question or tue\nnegro mans humanIty,-and they are discussing\n1t, and amid the flash and fervor Of the agitation,\nthe foul system dies. 1t can no more endure it,\nthan owls can noon, or bats sunshine, or ghosts\nday-break. While Wayand is gropeing about in\nhis metaphysics to get hold of some puzzle to en-\nbarrass US about the power, we will have exercised\nIt to the full, and cleared the land of slavery.\nThen where will the Doctor find market for his\n\" limitations z\" Slavery is a dead man already\nunless orator Rhett, and professor Dew, and\ncolonel! Nc Dufie, and general Hamilton, and\ndoctor this, that and the other one, can MAINTAIN\nthe precious creature in the argument, and get the\nverdict of an enlightned and purged christianity\nin its favor. TO this conclusion it has already\ncome. The question is stated-the issue joined\nthe pleadings closed-al demurring and abateing\nmd delaying past by. And now for the trial.\nNow slavery, hold thy own. The Doctors ques-\nhon of our having the power comes too late.-Hn-\nman Rights.\n\n=== 14_1883-11-16_pna_sn89064525_00212479214_