hand that bestowed the wherewithal to start\nanew--it waa his brothers hooe aud courage\nTHAI warded of desnair, and saved him\nfrom suicidal grave. Mora tiiau this\nit was his brothers. voice that turned\nhim, late though it was from the paths Of\ngreed, ibis brothers. influence that sowed in\nthe long neglected garden of his soul the seeds\nthat make for loving kindaess, peace and good\nwill to men. He lives to-day,-a chll Il ss man\nstilI.-but every youugstr In the village calls\nhim \"Uncle Cyrus.\"\"-and no man or woman\nis afraid to seek favor at his hands\n\n=== 9_1892-02-17_pna_sn84026853_00237287368_1892021701_0092.json\nThe Abbeville press and banner. [volume] (Abbeville, S.C.) 1869-1924 · 1892-02-17\ncrops: cached\nheadline: THE TRIBUTE OF SILENCE.\n---\nA post read his verses. and Of two\nWho listened, one spake nought but open\n\n\npraise;\n\n\nThe other held his peace. but all his face\nWas frightened by the inner joy he knew.\nTwo friends, long absent, met; and one had\n\n\nborne\n\n\nThe awful stroke and seattle of binding\n\n\nloss.\n\n\nHand fell in hand; so knit they, like a\n\n\ncross;\nWith no word uttered, heart to heart was\n\n\nsworn.\nA mother looked into her baby's eyes,\nAS blue as heav'n and deep as neither sea.\nBy what dim prescience, spirit-wise knew\n\n\nshe\nSuch souls exchanges never more wocld\n\n\nrise?\n% deep is silence-deep as human souls,\nAye, deep as life, beyond all lead and line,\nAnd words are but the broken shells that\n\n\nshine\nAlong the shore by which the ocean rolls.\n\". Buckham, in New England Magazine.\n\n=== 7_1860-12-29_pna_sn83030313_00271743361_1860122901_1190.json\nThe New York herald. [volume] (New York [N.Y.]) 1840-1920 · 1860-12-29\ncrops: cached\nheadline: LITER nature.\n---\n\n\n\nthen recovering his selfpossession, he said, with\nfeeble smile\n\n\nII am needlessly depriving you Of your rest\"\nsaid the detective.\n\n\nmo nc, = replied the preacher, = am better\n\n\nnow, and many thanks for your sympathy. ID is\nnot often meet with one kind yourself; hard\n| fare and bad treatment have not made me insensi\n| bic to attentions\n\n\nWhere do you propose going tomorrow 1N in,\n| il is |. long distance to the nearest town\"\n1.-THE Lord will be my guide said the preacher;\n\n\ncrossing his hands with resignation.\nNever mind rejoined Mr.. Duffy, kindly; lyon\nshall go with me. have horse and ohaise; IL is\n\n\nlarge enough for las both.\n| II accept the offer with gratitude said the\n\n\npreacher; your generosity affects me; you go in\n| the morning.\"\n\n\n\"As early as dawn\" replied Duffy; ~ must be\n| off as soon az possible brief pause ensued.\n| \"This is a lonely place y remarked the preacher,\n\n\nstirring the fire, for its expiring embers were\n\n\nalready casting deep shadows around the apart\n/ ment 9A..\n| Lonely enough such place as ] fancy might\n\n\nbe the rendezvous of vany,\" replied the old\n\n\nman thoughtiully. \"Do you know, continued he,\n\n\nlowering his voice TO a whisper, THAT there is\n\n\nsomething about this house and its inmates that\n| do not ike\" .E\n\n\nIndeed exclaimed the preacher, with sur\n| PRISE\n\n\n! Yes, wait one moment and + will tell you,''--\n\n\nthe detective arose and cautiously bolted the door;\n\n\nafter listening few seconds, TO assure himself the\n\n\nmovement was unnoticed outside, he returned to\n/ his seat II believe,n said he, speaking low and\n\n\nearnestly, that this is o den of some kind--the\n\n\ninkecper is honest looking enough, but as was\n\\ passing to this room\"\n\n\nWhisper lower or we may be overheard\" in\n\n\nterrupted the other excitedy, and in atone so diff\n| ferent that the detective involuntarily paused.\n| \"Ha.' whispered Duffy, never mind me; you\n| see a. was passing 70 this room, caught sight\n| through little creyice, which the deuse himself\n4\n| looking scoundrels, one of whom am certain\n| have met before--don't be alarmed, may be\n| mistaken in their character\ndo you not feel afraid, y asked the preacher.\nmo, am used 10 these things rejoined the\ndetective, with q smile. \"See,' he added, pulling\nout two revolvers, these are modern inventions;\ninvaluable as they are, their is not generally\nknown or appreciated. There will be time when\neven scoundrels will know their yalue. With\nthem,n continued the detective, ~ am more than\nmatch for q scoro Of desperadoes.\"\n\n\n\"Yoi; think there is danger, them\" observed the\npreacher.\n\n\n\"No immediate danger, ] apprehend,' replied\nthe other as he returned the pistols to their hid\ning place. IL have made arrangements to remain\nin this room, preferrng to sleep on bench rather\nthan trust myself in a chamber that ! know\nnothing about. The door, as you see, has good\nbolt and think you bad better remain also. Iou\nwill be safe enough\n\n\nyou think me 2 Coward said the preacher,\nsmiling.\nPrudence is not cowardice,'' remarked the\nother There are times when the bravest quail;\nour nerves have more to do with that than you\nimagine; but surely, you do not intend TO risk\nyourself anywhere elsey'\n\n\nIl do\" said the preacher, quietly; \"the inn\nkeeper pointed out my room, and by this time it is\nready. ! should judge it safe.\"\nVery well, if you will co. observed the detec-\ntive, examine your apartment carefully before\nyou retire; ascertain whether 1t has good, strong\n| bolt; make sure there is nobody under your bed,\nand look out especially for trapdoors and sliding\n| panels\n\n\nII viii take a careful look, and il anything JUS\ntifes suspicion l shall return,' rejoned the\n| preacher. Good night\n| He extended his hand, and IL was warmly\n| grasped by the detective.\nII will see you in the morning said he; re\n\n\nmember, my vehicle is at your service a. Tar as\n| go. Coed night\"\n\n\n| The preacher again expressed his grateful ac-\n| acknowledgements for the kindness Of his friend, and\n| with another cordial press Of Us hand, gathered\n\n\nup his saddlebags and cloak. and slently retired.\n! A. the door closed on himself, The Tomut flor 1t\n| was be) gave a low derisive Laugh.\n| Pool he muttered between his clencbed teeth,\n| II have you now have you now\"\n\n\nCHAPTER III.\n| The Burglars Nest was well named.\nSituated in one OF the loneliest and most mac\ncessible parts Of Pennsylvania it, existence was\nhardly known; few travellers stopped there, and\n\n\nONE Of those that did were never head from\nagain, so little could DC lNid.\nRugged and steep was the way that led TO it,\nwith here and there frightfu rayine TO break the\ngloomy expanse Of giant trees and tangled shrub\nberry that lined the road for mses.\nTlse structure itself was venerable wood build\n\n\nng, evidently built during the Revolutionary war,\nthough fer Whist purpose could hardly be con.\ncoived in so Wild and unfreQuented portion OF\nThe country. Twenty-Five years ago the tourist,\nIa his woodland raWbles, might have often met\nwith mysterious looking haoitations. long I'M\ntenanted which 10 the country people had .\nghostly prestige; where spectral lights could be\nseen at night, and unearthly noises were heard\nduring the day; Of which variety Of dark\nstories could be told, terrible 10 childrens ears,\nand listened 10 even by 'hc stranger with vague\nhorror.\n\n\nThese old abodes were perhaps some former\ntavern, where dreadful murders we're said 20 have\nbeen perpetrated, still haunted by the troubled\nspirits Of the victims; the sign swinging mourn\nfully in the wind. and the door broken from its\nhinges, from which issued the reptile and it's COM\npanion, the owl, screeching its melancholy song\nduring the night\n\n\nway houses and Boar Hesds, Of olden time.\nbut the Buresrs Nest hcssa q Haa9oesfa sA a.\nthen something dark and tragcsi would take\nplace, but there were no ghosts TO vex the actors\nin the bloody scene, save their own disordered\nthere everything was social and lively enough.\nand, a. Mr.. Beg opined, little more 50 than was\nnecessary.\n\n\nThe former occupants of the Nest were an old\ncouple, who, having mysteriously disappeared,\nleft the Tomtit 70 take quiet possession without\nthe trouble and expense OF questions asked, aud\nmonthly rent 10 pay. The place was fitted up a.\na tavern, according to the suggestion Of the Bur-\nglar Chief and Mr.. Beg obtained the appointment\nOf custodian Of the same receiving a. . recon-\npensc for hs services share in the yearly profits\n\n\nOf the trade, which, a. that gentleman sGgaciously\nobserved. was q very good thing when ii paid\"\n\n\nbut a. the old novels say let usresumc the thread\n| Of our story.\n\n\nwell, It's mean, that's vot IL Isl\" calculated\n| Dick lat On with an oath, the moment his leader\n\n\nhad left the room, a. narrated In Introductory\n| chapter ''Nunce wos Their time ther Tointit would\n\n\nave broken 4 ed afore he'd let sich same alp,\n\n\nbut blast my peppers, he's gettin' dummy\"\nThere was general grunt OF approval from the\n\n\nBurglars az this expression 81 Ar. Hatton's views,\nwho rendered them more emphatic By bringing\n\n\nhis bra wny fat on the tabIe with treme udous yo-\n\n=== 20_1896-08-29_p10_sn85042461_0028076968A_1896082901_0688.json\nThe herald. · 1896-08-29\ncrops: cached\nheadline: POOR, BUT A REAL LADY\nMoves the Soft Hearted\n\n\nPhilanthropists\nA LADY WITH NUMEROUS Aliases\nMrs. Orr Poses as a Lone\n\n\nWidow\nWITH A DYING DAUGHTER\nAs Mrs. Barron She Summons Spirits\n...Not Of the Liquid Variety\nThe Spooks Keep Her and Family 10 High\n\n\nGlover for a Time\nAnd Then the Spring Street Fortune Teller\nBlossms Out as Q French Hadame on\n\n\n\n\nWITH A DYING DAUGHTER\n\nAs Mrs. Barron She Summons Spirits\n\n\n...Not of the Liquid Variety\n\nThe Spooks Keep Her and Family 10 High\n\n\nClover for a Time\n\nAnd Then the Spring Street Fortune Teller\nBlossms Out as . French 7adame on\nWall street, Where the Game\n\n\nCoutinues-A Queer Case\n---\nfact that the confirmed criminal, what\never his specially may be, possesses a\ncertain pride in the neat results of his\nmisdirected genius, just as any man In\nthe more orthodox walks of life, takes\npride in mastering and carrying into ex-\necution the varied details of their respec-\ntive avocations.\n\n\nBetween these two classes. however.\ncript fakirs who contrive to keep within\nthe limits of the law, and yet whose\nmethods are essentially criminal, albeit\nthey are not classified as criminals.\nNeedless to say, perhaps, they are even\nmore keenly repudiated by the respecta-\nBILE portion of society.\n\n\nThere has again emerged from her re-\ntirement a lady-for so she proclaims\nherself-who belongs to this intermedi-\nate class, who appears to make very\nhandsome living by preying upon So\nciety at large. She has husband and\ngrown-up daughter who is quite young\nlady. \"They toll not, neither do they\nspin,\" but while, apparently, they pos\nsess all of the necessaries Of life they\ncontrive also to gain many of the lux\nuries made impossible to the hard work\ning mechanic or the average woman\nwho works her heart out day by day just\nfor the mere privilege of living.\n\n\nAnd this is made possible by the inge-\nnuity of the mother. She is lady\" of\nmany aliases. She has been known as\nMrs. Orr and Mrs. Barron and then she\nrepudiated her country and blossomed\nforth as Nine. but this\nflower of humanity changed it's name,\nthe rankness Of fraud and deception con-\ntinued to be its distinguishing character\nistic.\n\n\nAS Mrs Orr many city residents hav-\ning philanthropic tendencies, contribu-\nted to the support of this united family.\nWith modest and somewhat engaging\ndemeanor Mrs. Orr told how her husband\nhad recently died leaving her with\nfamily of small children in totally desti\ntute circumstances. Though not strong\nshe was willing to fight for the little\nones, however, remembering that the\nLord tempers the wind to the shorn\nlamb\" and she was trying to sell few\narticles, made by herself, in order to\nmake bare living.\n\n\nThe articles in question. bought\nwholesale for a nominal sum, sold like\nhot cakes and Mrs. Orr (?) must have\nchuckled when she spoke of the \"shorn\nlamb\" She fleeced the folks every time\nthrough working on their sympathies.\n\n\nAt home on San Julian street, not\nlong ago, she rang in a pitiful story of\nyoung daughter wasting away with\nconsumption. With tears in her eyes she\ntold of her fight with poverty since her\nhusbands death and how her daughter\nwas dropping for the comforts and at\ntention that her condition required. The\nlady to whom she poured out her woes\nfound from her that she lived at Six\nteenth street and Maple avenue and\nafter buying hei entire stock in trade,\ngot some of her hearted friends in\nterested and the same afternoon loaded\nup buggy with good things for the\ndying daughter, as well as the mother,\nand drove out to the address given. The\nimposition was then discovered for the\naddress was a fake\" one.\n\n\nThe Associated Charities had been\nworked at an early stage of the game\nbut Mrs. Orr, to use her most common\ncognomen, had been so skillful in ex\ntorting sympathy from people, at so\nmany dollars per head, that the police\nbegan to investigate. Chief Glass re-\nports her as being one of the very clev-\ncrest of those fakirs who work daringly\nalong the danger line without actually\ninfringing upon it.\n\n\nScenting the presence of the police\nMrs. Orr developed into Mrs. Barron and\ntook rooms on South Spring street,\nwhere a bright band of spirits also took\nup their abode and assisted her in telling\n| fortunes. Fortunes were read off with\nall the ease of creased lightning at 81\nper head for gentlemen, half price for\nladies' For a time the spiritualistic\n! dodge worked to a charm but recently\n| Mrs. Barron saw fit to skip out from her\n| Spring street habitation, it being under\n| stood she was going to make the grand\n| tour\" that spiritual mediums frequent\n| ly make, for the purpose of giving other\n| cities the benefit of the band of spirits\n| surrounding her.\n\n\nThe grand tour\" really meant the\n! blossoming forth on Wall street as\nMadame So and So, where she has con-\n\n\ntinued to pump spiritual thunder at sc\ncents and 81 per clap.\n\n\nBut spiritualistic mediums, In COM\nmon with other businesses, fake and\notherwise, have found the financial\n! grass growing rather short. Madame\n! has therefore again taken to the high\n| way and is holding up soft-hearted\nmothers having children of their own\n| at the point of a well sharpened tongue\n| and basketful of trumpery articles\n| Complaints are beginning to come into\n\n\nthe office of the Associated Charities, as\n| we'll as the police, and it may be well for\nmadame to again take to the fortune\n! telling business. 1t, too, is a fraud but\nnot in all cases illegal, albeit 1t IS diff\n! cult to separate the sheep from the\ngoats.\n\n\nAS for the ordinary householder II\n\n\nmay, perhaps, be well If he or she, as the\n\n\ncase NAV be- will think a long time be-\n\n=== 3_1896-07-08_pna_sn84026907_00295862373_1896070801_0058.json\nThe Lexington dispatch. [volume] (Lexington, South Carolina) 1870-1917 · 1896-07-08\ncrops: cached\nheadline: OBITUARY.\n---\nGeorge Violet, infant daughter OF\nGeorge A.*nlAgaes R. Hal:iwanger.\nwas born on the 22d day OF Sept.\n1895, and died June 9th, 1896,\naged nine mouths and five days.\nOnly few months ago, her father\npreceded her to the spirit land and\nonly q mother snl wife with one\nlittle girl is left Of the once charmed\nfamily circle. A bud plucked from\nearth to bloom in heaven. We gently\nlaid her to rest in the cemetery OF\nCaper's Chapel, by the side of her\nfather. IS,\n\n=== 24_1945-06-25_p1_sn83045462_00280604549_1945062501_0589.json\nEvening star. · 1945-06-25\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Charles Town Results\n---\nFIRST RACE-Purse. 5000: 3-year-oldi\nand up; claiming; about q 4k furlongs\nHutoka fEdensb $50 32O go\nTen Oaks (Grant) 42O 3S0\nTime Burner (BakhSh) 4S}0\nTime, 0:50A.\n\n\n: Also ran--Jo Beth Bum. Over Gold\nBonnie Ina, Morbrook, Dunemall.\n\n\nSECOND RACE-Purse, 5000: 3-year.\nolds and up; allowances; about Az fur\nlongs.\nFlying West tCarillo) 1920 $50 400\nPatty Boy fEdens) 44O 400\nBig Talk (Dufford) $50\nTime, O.50.\n\n\nAlso ran-Ghosts, Peg O'Sullivan, Led\nAstray and Chat Hopkins\n\n\nDAILY Double paid 81 2nl\n\n=== 21_1895-09-14_p1_sn90059959_00271742848_1895091401_0568.json\nThe Irish standard. · 1895-09-14\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nKosnis Ind since 1915 and nov\n\n\nKoEhic MED. CO.. Chicago, 1ll. a\n10 s.. Franklin Street. Il\n\n\nsold 81 DRUGGISTS at 81 per Bottle. 6Nro6 .!\n\n\nLarge size, Sl.7B. Bottles for 80. ~\n\n\nmarked by eccentric behavior Samuel\njohnson said: Madness itself merely\ndiscovers itself by unnecessary deviations\nfrom the uual modes Of the world En-\nthusiasm has often been styled insanity.\nMichael Angelo was called by Richards on\nt.The Divine Mailman\" whilst Oliver Gold\nsmith was designated An Inspired Idiot. =\nTurner, whose works, strange as they may\nappear at first sight, as a number Of In\nccngruous masses Of paint used indiscrim-\ninately, but who produced such wonder\nfull results with gamboge, vermillion,\nwhite lead and verdigris, was considered\nby many at the time to be hardly respon-\nsible for his actions. The mind is often\ntoo absorbed with the intensity OF Its Gre\n\n\natiOns, that the Impressions Of a sense\n\n\ndoes not impart such impressions upon\nthe sensoriuIn. Genius is often fatal gift,\nlike beauty, and burst into blossom when\nthe organism is still in its bloom. Cen\nius, as is often seen, is seldom combined\nwith common sene. The irritability Of\ngenius which is SO common is the first\nlink in that chain of psychical maladies 50\nwhen melancholy marks the martyr Of\nthought and genius as its own.\n\n\nThe stories relating to Pliny, Archi-\nmodes, Marine and Sir Isaac Newton are\nillustrations of passive eccentricity, the\nreverie of genius. They were, it is we'll\nknown, conspicuous for the absence Of the\ncommon modes and courtesies of life, but\nthis forgotten In those whose works will\nlive in glory, long after their eccentrici-\nties are obliterated. Many brilliant scars\nof literature are endowed with the so often\nfatal gift Of genius, in whom intellect not\nonly begins to grow, but to burst forth\ninto blossom while yet it's organ Is still\nin the bud. So many gentuses are devel-\noped often in infancy, and frequently the\nso-called infant prodigy who does not ul-\ntimately become a genius, will stop half\nway becoming insane. Insanity is good\nhaving well passed its confines, will in all\nprobability develop into a genius;a but,\nalas, many fail to pass this barrier, and,\nconsequently our institutions are full Of\nbrilliant inteNects cut short in the pre-\ncocity Of their youth, especially if any I'M\n\n\ndue excitement be administered or en-\ncouraged in the Inflated subject of pre-\ncocity. The genius Of Sir Walter. Scott\nended in imbecility. He first became con-\nscious Of his owncondition by partial loss\nOf memeory, and a want OF recognition,\neven OF his own sonnets. The early COM\nposition Of Scott was wonderful, but in\nglancing at the later efforts, there was\nevidence to the psychologist Of that faced\ndegeneration OF tubular neurine which\nprobably began with his reverses and kept\npace with his wonderful toll to liquidate\nhis pecuniary debts; and the effort to do\nthis was SO great that his mental state\nbecame unhinged. ~\n\n\n\". .sTlis wretched brain gave way,\n\n\nAnd became a wreck at random driven,\nWithout one glimpse of reason or Of\n\n\nb. Heaven =\n\n\nThe cerebral condition of Scott resem-\nbled in many ways the many sided genius\nOf the immortal Bard of Avon, and he\nwould probably, under happier ctrcum-\nstances, have reached a good old age.\ndied in the meridian Of his\nsplendor Of foolish excess. We have it\nin the diary of Mr.. Ward, who was init\nmate with Shakespeare, and who wrote his\ndiary which is preserved in the records Of\nthe Medical Society Of London. In this\nwe read that Shakespeare. Drayton and\nBen Johnson had merry meeting, and, it\nseems, drank too hard, for Shakespeare\ndied of fever there contracted\"\n\n\nThe insanity of genius is itself psyche\nlogical problem, and comes before us with\nmost awful Contrasts spectral life in\ndeath Illusion is a prominent charac-\nteristic of genius; this is not wondered\nat when we consider that the workings of\nan lima Curative mind are one continued\nand protracted course of ideal creation.\nOF this, illusion when the mind is con-\nscious does not constitute insanity any\nmore than it can be termed disease, for\nmental wandering may be under the con-\ntroll Of the will, whilst the systematic\nfunctions and the concomitant organs are\nin perfectly healthy condition.\n\n\n\" Space is not at my disposal to enter\nfully into details respecting all the\ngeniuses who have become admittedly\nthe victims OF insanity, but might emu\nmerate briefly some Of the poetical ones\nwhose minds ultimately gave way but\nwhose works live after them. a\n\n\n1914. TorQuato Tasso suffered from\nHmania periodique.' He was, unfor-\ntlirately, a victim to the literary envy OF\nthe sovereign. He was regarded by some\nmore a martyr than as an insane person.\nHe suffered from we'll marked aricular\ndelusions and phantasmag CORA. The. APP\nparation was, to him no fancy in his i'm\nagination,, but a real human being, and\none Of substance. He would converse elo-\nquently with his imaginary familiar spir-\n1t, who, according to his statement, paid\nhim various visits. The discourses with\nthis spirit were eloquent and so sublime\nthat it was difficult to realize that he was\nsimply talking to q phantom OF his own\ninvention. 5\n\n\n# The hallucinations Of Tasso differed\nfrom the creations of Shakespeare, and\nyet, doubtless, Shakespeare saw the illu-\nsion in the minds eye as clearly as Tas-\nso. did, but at the same time knew them\nto be phantoms. It is a very dangerous\n\n\nthing to indulge to any extent in phanta-\nsy, as the impression may become pern1a-\nnent, and what was imag: native may be-\ncome reality Abnormal cerebral circnla-.\nton Is the supposed physical cause for\nthese states Of 5phant gorfa, which we\nread Of as occurring III so many pOetical\ngeniuses. One of the leading characters\ntics of genius is .rritability, showing 8\nhighly strung nervous system; the conds-\ntfOn OF the mind Is often acutely sensi-s\ntive. sinking like Inimosa from the breath\nof criticism and when such a state is fed\ny4thesWear and tear of the brain. thee\nfeet may lie peri ous and destru ctiye. 3s\n\n\nit lis q Facf. ssss\nThat Minneapol is hiss the largest barber\nshop III Ineric the Metropo Titan t,y\n\n=== 15_1906-01-27_pna_sn86086499_00414214514_1906012701_0425.json\nWauwatosa news. [volume] (Wauwatosa, Wis.) 1900-1948 · 1906-01-27\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Pecnliar customs Of he Batonga\n---\nThe Batunga tribe inhabit the regions\nadjoining the IambeSi river. Oh both\nbanks from tb~ confluence of the Gwani\nriver to that If the Sengwe. OF their\nearly history and origin no authentic in\nformation can he obtained. When the\nMatuhele entered what is now Rhatlesis.\nthe Batonga accepted the rule of this\nWArilke nation without resistance\n\n\nAmong the peculiar customs attaching\n10 the tribe is that by which the success\nsion to an estate or a chieftainshu, is not\n20 the eldest son of the deceased man,\nbut 10 the eldest son of his eldest sister.\nAll adults are buried in front of the door\nOf the hut occupied during lifetime. and\nchildren under the caves.\n\n\n\\ characteristic of the tribe il ria.\npractice Of knocking out the four front\nteeth of the upper jaw in both sexes on\nthe arrival at the age OF puberty Tho\nnearest approach To religious belief Is a\nwe'll established faith in the existence If\nspirits of the dead ard in their capacity\n10 work evil on their surviving relations\nIn former times iron ore was treat,,\naud manufactured into hoes. asSagais\nand axes, while raw cotton was woven\ninto cloth; but both these industries hav,,\ndisappeared since the advent Of Euro\npean metal work and fabrics. -Suuta\nAfrica\n\n=== 12_1906-09-28_pna_sn85000631_00199918266_1906092801_0720.json\nThe Washburn leader. [volume] (Washburn, McLean County, N.D.) 1890-1986 · 1906-09-28\ncrops: cached\nheadline: THE PROSECUTED JEW.\n\nHe IL, 1t IL Claimed, the Most Spiritual\n\n\nMan Alive.\n---\nHe IL, 1t IL Claimed, the Most Spiritual\n\n\nMan Alive.\n\n\n'\"The Jew is said to have what Is\nknown as the commercial Instinct\"\nsaid Oscar s. Straus. \"Hume tells us\nthat under Richard l. 500 Jews sought\nsafety in the castle Of York, where, not\nbeing strong enough for defense, they\nkilled their wives and children, threw\nthe bodies over the walls into the mob\nbelow and, setting fire to the castle,\nwere themselves burned alive. The\ngentry owing money to the Jews has\ntened to the cathedral, where their\nbonds or promissory notes were kept,\nand made bonfire before the altar.\nThe compiler Of the Annals Of Waiver\nley, in relating the event. Hume says,\n'blesses the Almighty for thus deliver\ning over this impious race to destruc-\ntion.' History blazes with such stories\nOf self sacrifice and barbarity. The\ncommercial instinct in this particular\ncase appears to have been on the other\nside.\n\n\n\"Instead Of the Jew being natural\nbarterer, he Is the most spiritual man\nalive. He has been pounded through\nthe ages, robbed and massacred, but he\nyields neither his traditions nor his\nideals. Had he been materialistic he\nwould have sold out long ago and\nbought peace. If he would join the\nGreek church he would be safe in Rus\nsia Rather than do that he will walk\ninto the jaws of hell. Therefore say\nthat he is spiritualistic or idealistic\nrather than practical or material. Were\nI to choose a family that would live\nwould have it meet hardships and per-\nsecutions. Were to choose one to die\nI would give it pleasure and luxury.\nThe Jew, denied his civil rights, de-\nspoiled Of his property, scourged and\nmurdered, has only been made tougher\nand longer lived. His enemies have\ngiven him strength and trained him\nmost admirably for the battle Of life,\nno matter in what part of the world\nfortune may lead him.\" New York\nWorld .\n\n=== 14_1907-12-10_p4_sn84024827_0021110689A_1907121001_0747.json\nBisbee daily review. · 1907-12-10\ncrops: cached\nheadline: TRUE GHOST STORY.\n\nDead Wife Calls Husband to the\n\n Spirit World.\n\nJEALOUS OF HER SUCCESSOR.\n---\nDying. She Told Her Mate That She\n\n Would Come Back if He Ever Mar\n\n ried Again, and She Kept Her Word\n\n With Fatal Effect.\n\n The following true ghost story is told by correspondent of the St Lou Is Republic. who declares the facts came partly under his own notice and were in part told to him by eyewit- nesses:\n\n Philip Hartmann was big. easy going, good natured. lazy fellow. We called him Elephant at school. Early\n\n=== 3_1897-10-23_p1_sn83016810_00280768005_1897102301_0135.json\nThe Appeal. [volume] (Saint Paul, Minn. ;) 1889-19?? · 1897-10-23\ncrops: cached\nheadline: NASHVILLE, TENN.\n\nA FEWlTEMS FROM THE EXPO\n\n\nSlTION CITY\n\nPrescription Declared to be no Greater\nThan In Mianesota-Rev. George w.\nGaines Gets a Mild Roast Some Mime\nsotians Cotuplimented.\n---\nThe many attractions at the Centennial\n\n\nExposition this week. are above the\n| ordinary and general ruu of am easements\n\n\nand inducements offered by similar Elk\npositions. Great care is m anifeated b y\nthe c )mmittee on amusements as to the\n\n\nvariety and diversity in the entertain\nmenta provided for the visitors, and no\neffort is left undone to make the visitors\n\n\nto feel welcome. In fact several special\n\n\ndays were set apart, on which all Afro\n\n\nAmericans vera admitted at half p1 ics\n\n\nwith the intantion Of showing feeling\nof goo ! will toward than. Every per-\nson who can should avail himself of the\nopportunity Of visiting this Exposition,\nit tends to briax the representative\npeople 10 ciose: contact with representa-\ntive white people which necessarily\nproduces good results. The white man\nsees the Af1o-American: as education\nand association has develop I'D him aid\ncome !, conclusion the Afr o-Ameri\ncan is not such beast or savage after\nall. The effect upon the Afro Ameri-\ncan is also good. he finds that tie South\nern white man is MOI 80 bad as painted,\nand thus it goes.\n\n\nOftimes educated people are prone to\nconjure up creatures of straw one to b Q\nknocked down by sober reason judge\nment, as WIS clearly demonstrated in\nthe case of REV. Geo. w. Gaines of Chi |\ncego I'll. This eminent divine came all\nthe way from Chicago to Nashville pre-\nsumable on a business trip, but would !!\n\n=== 20_1892-02-25_pna_sn84028296_00296029166_1892022501_0703.json\nDemocratic Northwest. [volume] (Napoleon, Ohio) 1869-1894 · 1892-02-25\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Man or Woman. Ghost or Human.\n---\nWe can not say what will cure ghosts,\nbut many men and many women who\nlook like ghosts rather than human be-\nings, throuph sickness, would regain\nhealth and happiness, II they would\ntry the virtue of the world-renowned\nremedy, DIR. Pierce's Golden Medical\nDiscovery. Torpid liver, or eruptions,\nbilliousness, impure blood, skindiseas-\nes serofulous sores and swellings,\nConsumption (which is scrofula\nOf the lungs, all yield to this wonder\nful medicine. 1t is both tonic and\nstrength-restoring, and Alternative or\nblood-cleansing.\n\n=== 27_1888-12-23_pna_sn85058130_00206535787_1888122301_0503.json\nThe Salt Lake herald. [volume] (Salt Lake City [Utah]) 1870-1909 · 1888-12-23\ncrops: cached\nheadline: THE LAND OF. PALMS.\n\nPanama and It's Fever\n\n\nHaunted Regions.\n\nDESIRABLE PLACE FOR REST\n---\nCelombIs's Tnde btedRess Taking\nLife Easy-Vhe Ladies' Pets\n\n\nRow Monkeys are Captured.\n\n\nAsPINWALL, Panama, Dec. 1. 1885,\nSpecial CORES 2ondence The HERALD I\nSO many changes have occurred in\nthis chaotic portion of the world\nwithin the last quarter of a century\nthat in roaming about the Isthmus one\nfinds it hard to keep track of himself\nand to remember under which govern\nment he is living. Though apparently\nnorth OF the dividing line between Cen\ntrail and South America, this State OF\nPanama-which includes the isthmus of\nthe same Numerically belongs to South\nAmerica, as part of 1.-The United States\nof Colombia a.\" and its western frontier\nadjoining Costa RIGA. and its eastern,\nbounded by the surveye intar-oceanic\nroute from Caledonia Bay on the north\nto the Gulfo de San Miguel on the Pa\ncific side.\n\n\nItcontains the cantons, or provinces,\nof Panama, Azuers, Chirsiyui and\nVirszua-a total area of %. 756 square\nmiles, and a population of about\n$50 600.\n\n\nAN INSIGNIFICANT sTRIFf\n\n\nThere seems to have been more\n1 nj ~\n| of country than its burning sands and\nfever-haunted swamps, its deadly eli\nmate and the unstable character of its\nnatives would seem to warrant. And\nright here it may be well to insert a\nscrap of history.\n\n\nEARLY as the year 1919. the name\nColombia was adopted by all the\n| northern countries of South America,\nwhen Venezuela and New Grenada (the\n| latter then included the Isthmus) united\nin establishing one central government\n| for the purpose of resisting Spain. Ten\nwears later Venezuela retired from this\nunion and constituted herself a sep\ncrate republic. After the resigna-\nton of Bolivia (1S30) she again\nJoined herself to New Grenada; but the\nunion lasted only few months. In\nNovember, 1881, another separation\ntook place, and at the same time it was\ndecided that the former province of\nQuito should constitute q separate\ngovernment, under the name of Ecua\ndor, thus dividing the former unwieldy\n\"Co!ombia\" into three distinct and In\ndependent republics, viz: Venezuela,\nEcuador and New Granada.\n\n\nANOTHER CHANGE.\n\n\nIn 1861 another radical change oc.\nCured, since which date the United\nStates of Colombia\" has been the\nofficial title Of what was before known\nas New Granada. Its government\nextends extraordin&gy inducements to\nforeigners and especially to citizens OF\nthe North American republics, to per-\nsuade them to make homes here.\nGrants of some OF the finest and on the\nface ofR the earth are offered for their\nacceptance free of cost, providing only\nthat they ayow an intention to settle\npermanently in Colombia.\n\n\nNew Granada, with its skies of\ncloudless blue, its luscious fruits ana\nbeautiful flowers, its picturesque val\nley and the grandeur Of It's mountains,\nsottened by eternsl summer, would De\nfor the emigrant one OF the most de-\nsirable places on the globe-the ultima\nthule of his desires-Were it not for The\nchina e influences. The unhealthy IA\nture of the coast presents an obstacle\nformidable, if not unS9rmOunGable, to\ncolonization by the northern races.\nEspecially on the Pacific side. the whole\ncountry is a network Of muddy creeks\nand sWampy islands, worse, if possible,\nthan the western shores of Africa.\nThere are nitric valleys healthy a\nany in the world, and p ateous and ex-\ntensive tracts half way up the noun\ntain sides where Paradise might be\nlooated-bUt hemming them in IS the\nfever-haunted coast.\n\n\nTHE RATIONAL DEBT.\n\n\nColomLia's inde btedness to England\nalone is over $4,000 900, and there is\nnot the slightest prospect Of its ever\nbeing paid Since the emancipation of\ntheir slaves, in 1852, labor cannot be\ncontrolled in any way, not even by\nmoney-fOr why should man exert\nhimself when he has no ambition to\ncrafty, no aspirations to attain and 70\nneeds to suppiy2 In a land where the\nbanana and the black man flourish side\nby SIDE, both without effort Or aultiva-\nton. is no congenial place for Anglo\nSaxons.\n\n\nNature having provided so bounti-\nfully in these parts for almost every\nwant OF mar, the ignorant nali7es are\nwholly without energy, thrift or enter\nprise, With them every thing is DIananG\nthe tomorrow\" thai never comes.\nThey live with no thought or care for\nthe future, the \"eVen teaor\" of their\nsouls disturbed By neither doubts nor\nfears, nor vsulting ambitions; and\nbeing devout Catholics every one. even\ndeath has for them'no terrors, but they\nare quite ready to shuttle off this NOR\ntai coil at any moment, as indiferently\nas they have lived.\n\n\nCOMMENTED PEOPLE.\n\n\nSo strongly marked are the lines of\ncaste, and so impossible the gulf be-\ntween the few partisans and the many\nparvonues that the latter never dream\nof attempting TO bridge it by any\nearnest effort. If man has been born\nin the lower ranks, he accepts his lot\nwith the utmost contentment. A few\nstalks Of wild ha m hOO Driuht\n\n=== 1_1864-03-25_p1_sn84038105_00271765253_1864032501_0167.json\nThe Union. [volume] (Georgetown, Del.) 1863-1866 · 1864-03-25\ncrops: cached\nheadline: ChiIdren,s Ioys nnd sorroMa.\n---\n| can endure melancholy man, but not\nmelancholy child; the former, in whatever\nslough he may sink, can raise his eyes either\nto the kingdom of reasons or or hope; but the\nlittle child is entirely absorbed and weighed\ndown by one black poison-drop of the pres\nent. Think Of a child led to the scaffold,\nthink of Cupid in oofin; or watch butter\nfly, after its four wings have been torn off,\ncreeping like worm, and you will feel what\nl mean. But wherefore? The first has been\nalready given the beast, only knows purcst,\nthough shortest sorrow; !; one which has no\npast and no future; one such as the sick man\nreceives from without the dreamer from him\nself into his asthenie brain; finally, one with\nthe consciousness not of guilt, but Of inno-\ncence. Certainly, all the sorrows of children\nare but shortest nights, as their joys are but\nlongest days; and, indeed both so much So\nthat is the latter, often cloudless and starless\ntime Of life, matured man only longingly re-\nmembers his old childhood's pleasures while\nhe seems altogether to have forgotten his\nchildhood's grief. This weak remembrance\nis strangely contrasted with the opposing one\nin dreams and fevers in this respect, that in\nthe two last it is always the cruel sorrows of\nchildhood wkich return; the dream this\nmoek-sun of childhood-and the fevers, its\ndistorting glass-both drawn forth from dark\ncorners the fears Of defenceess childhood,\nwhich press and cut with iron fangs into\nthe PROSTATE soul. The fair scenes of dreams\nmostly play on an after-stage, whereas the\nfrightful ones choose for theirs the cradle and\nthe nursery. Moreover, in fever, the ice\nhands Of the fear of ghosts, the striking one\nof the teachers and parents, and every claw\nwith which fate has passed the young heart,\nthemselves out to catch the wandering man.\nParents, consider then that every childhoo's\nRupert--the name given in Germany to the\n\n\nfictitious being employed to frighten children\ninto obedience-ev though it has lain chained\nfor tens of years, yet breaks loose and gains\nmastery over the man so soon as it finds him\non sick-bed. The first fright is more dan\ngerous the sooner it happens; as the man\ngrows older, he is less easily frightened; the\nlittle cradle Of the child is more easily quite\ndarkened than the starry heaven of the man.\n\n=== 52_1919-10-31_p9_sn83045433_0028076405A_1919103101_0491.json\nThe Washington herald. · 1919-10-31\ncrops: cached\nheadline: SPIRITUALISTS.\n\nWashing;on's Favorite Adviser\nPROF. MARCUS La MAR\n---\nMost remarkable reader Of human destiny OF\nthe present. age. tells your name In full also full\nnames Of husband wife Or sweetheart; reveals\nyour tnnermost secrets. helps you to gain your\nevery desire; brings about marriage with the Que\nyou love. and gives never failing help and ad-\nvice on business. law suits or any affair Of life.\nCall today; he will surprise YOU -\n","numLin