ise, as was aware of the extreme pride\n54\tBritish officer has in his sword and his re-\n55\tluctance to lay it beyond his reach.\n56\t\n57\t\n58\tHard upon these details came three others\n59\tof different nature. saw at once that the\n60\tman was intoxicated, though not beyond his\n61\town control; that in his left hand he had an\n62\topen paper, while his right, which still hung\n63\tby his side, held cooked pistol, though this\n64\tI would not have seen but for the sudden\n65\tCLEAN of flash of lightning. 1t gave me a\n66\tquick realization of the fact that / was in\n67\tgreat danger and without the means of COM\n68\tbatting it, for my sword was in the closet,\n69\twhile both pistols and rife lay unloaded in\n70\ta corner.\n71\t\n72\t\n73\tThe crash of thunder following the flash\n74\tESQ H. ]\n75\taction was equally out of the question, for\n76\twas casting searching glances about the room\n77\tfor some means of defense, he raised the\n78\tweapon and leveled it at me.\n79\t\n80\t\n81\t/ could fathom no motive for his thus at\n82\tlacking ME, save the heat in his rum-mad.\n83\tdened brain, and, were he impelled by drink,\n84\tmy position was doubly critical. Had there\n85\tbeen ghost of chance to act in the ag-\n86\tgressive, in this desperate moment / should\n87\thave availed myself of it; but not even that\n88\treally formidable weapon and guard, chair,\n89\twas nearer than the table, and to have made\n90\tmove toward it felt meant certain de-\n91\tBtruction. A sudden attack on him was out\n92\tIF my power, for betwixt US interviewed the\n93\tbroad table itself, which made it impossible\n94\tTO avail myself of the tactics had used with\n95\tLounsbury. There was naught for me to do\n96\tbut stand as was and await the terrible\n97\tissue.\n98\t\n99\t\n100\tHowever, as he remained silent, man.\n101\tnged to find my voice, and, as the thunder\n102\t| rolled in the distance, ! shouted:\n103\t\n104\t\n105\tcapt. Scanned, are you SO demented or\n106\t% cowardly and 30 lost to honor that you\n107\twill murder the man you challenged to tight\n108\tin the feld?\"\n109\t\n110\t\n111\tWithout swervs of the arm, he answered\n112\tstsrnly:\n113\t\n114\t\n115\t\"Sit DOWN.\"\n116\t\n117\t\n118\tEven thus beset, it irked me to obey him\n119\tlike . schoolboy, but realized that, armed,\n120\tunder the existing circumstances he was far\n121\tstronger than with all my sinews. Wisely,\n122\tthen, l swallowed my ire, and, approaching\n123\tthe table, seated myself with the hope thai\n124\the would come within reach.\n125\t\n126\t\n127\tHe was not allow in following, for he\n128\tstepped forward and carefully slid into the\n129\tchair opposite, though not for second un-\n130\tcovering me with the muzzle of his pistol, or,\n131\tfor that matter, taking his eye from mine.\n132\tAnd here we sat while flash and crash fol-\n133\tlowed each other in quick succession. Ny\n134\tbrain worked rapidly enough, but as the sec\n135\tonds sped no way could 1 find to rid me of the\n136\tshining ring of steel confronting me or turn\n137\tfor moment the equally brilliant CLINT of\n138\this eye, each of which acquired clearness\n139\twith the fitful lighting of the room.\n140\t\n141\t\n142\tThe table was too broad for an onslaught,\n143\tand now that we were closer, could see\n144\tthe nerves of the man were at full tension,\n145\tand thnt on the slightest hostile move on my\n146\tFart he would pull the trigger.\n147\t\n148\t\n149\tI hoped lightning would strike the house\n150\tand put an end to the situation, which at\n151\tlast began to have its effect on me; and I\n152\ttake it that no man can long stand the scowl\n153\tOf loaded barrel backed by determined\n154\tyet silent enemy without undergoing more\n155\tor less strain.\n156\t\n157\t\n158\tTwas even wishing that the heavens would\n159\tlet loose bolt and finish the matter, or that\n160\tthe man opposite would open his lips and\n161\tspeak, despite the roar, when felt the floor\n162\tbeneath me tremble, as though the house\n163\twas shaken by mighty power. It passed\n164\tin the instant, and as it did 80 the thunder\n165\tfaded away for the moment, and Scam\n166\tnell, lowering his hand, let it rest on the\n167\tedge of the table, whence the pistol held me\n168\t| full in the chest. Then he spoke and per-\n169\thaps because the excitement indoors and out\n170\thad partly sobered him, his words came\n171\tclearly:\n172\t\n173\t\n174\tII have here your ID message in my\n175\thand, but, as you may well guess, it is fore\n176\t/ bCohC0s 1AClG Will DC ukuL UeGWeeQ un\n177\ttomorrow morning\"\n178\t\n179\t\n180\tBecause you deem it safer to murder me\n181\tnow?\" l returned calmly. \"Give me but\"\n182\t\n183\t\n184\tShut your MOUTH he interrupted, let\n185\tting go his temper. do you think am here\n186\tto handy words with rebel? Capt. Donald\n187\t| Thorndye, alias Lounsbury, you are close on\n188\tto the end of your rope, and you'll find that\n189\t| end has moose in it, you cursed spy! What\"\n190\the continued, rising in his increasing rage,\n191\tand Immediately reseating himself, while\n192\this voice grew louder, \"do you think am\n193\tfool, or SO blind that may not see through\n194\ta riddle? I have found you out, hide and\n195\thair-Thorndyke, of Martha's Vineyard\n196\tsmuggler- Had you escaped me\n197\tthis day, and been off on Clinton's business,\n198\twhich doubtless lies there\" (pointing to my\n199\tpapers, which lay on the table,, 1T would\n200\thave hunted you down had it cost me my\n201\tcommission. Escape me? No, by the gods!\n202\tFrom the beginning l fancied you the fraud\n203\tyou are, and at the Bulls Head, by your\n204\twords and fine airs, you made me sure Of it.\n205\tYou slipped your role. You'll find your hen.\n206\thawk has sharp eyes, and, giant though you\n207\tbe, you will see that seamew is nothing in\n208\this grip. Nay, I have not come to shoot you\n209\tdown as you deserve, but know your in\n210\tfernal tricks, and mean to hold you where\n211\tyou belong. take it twill afford me more\n212\tpleasure to see you dance at ropes end\n213\tthan dirty my hands with your blood, save\n214\tyou make it urgent. Let me clinch the mat\n215\tter now, and then make you ready to march.\n216\tBelow stairs at this moment is Lounsbury in\n217\tthe flesh, and with him file of men to take\n218\tyou off. This bit of play was for my own\n219\tpleasure, and, having had it, I'll be your\n220\tvalet and stay by your heels until you are\n221\tsafely delivered to Cunningham. Now God\n222\trest your miserable soul! Have you aught\n223\tto say before give the word to the rest?\"\n224\t\n225\t\n226\tTo state that was unaffected by these\n227\twords, which were poured forth in ton\n228\trent as though my tormentor feared inter\n229\truption, would be false. saw my plans for\n230\tthe future, my hope for life itself, swept\n231\taway together, and nothing but blank,\n232\tkfNksA Snlp ~ 4 S3ohna p ~ sna,nma\n233\t\n234\t=== 12_1890-10-23_pna_sn82016187_00211102949_1890102301_0596.json\n235\tThe National tribune. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1877-1917 · 1890-10-23\n236\tcrops: cached\n237\theadline: \n238\t\n239\tA Ceremony That is Only Proper for The Soldier.\n240\t\n241\t\n242\tlCiHciunnn Timer Sfar1\n243\t---\n244\tlCiHcilnah Tigaes-star.l\n245\t\n246\t\n247\tA reporter was talking 20 Gen. b. r. Cowen\n248\tabout what touching ceremony was the sound\n249\ting Of taps at the grave Of a soldier a. the last\n250\tfarewell. How sad. and tender, and beautiful\n251\til was And what a picture for memory it\n252\tmade-the grouping about the grave the list\n253\tening 10 the refrain that flows 50 piaintiyely\n254\tfrom the buglc, and steals echoing away, sad\n255\tplaintive, appealing wall, that has authority,\n256\tirreyocableness and tenderness In it. And one\n257\tcould well imagine, while sorrowfully listening\n258\t70 II, that the spirit Of the dead soldier must\n259\tbe a-listcuing 10 II, too, the martial spirit\n260\tia life had been lullcd by its 4\" GOOD. good\n261\tnight! Good goodnight! until his eyclids\n262\twould close ere the last sound stole eChoing\n263\tover the camp.\n264\t\n265\t\n266\tdo you know\" said he that the sounding\n267\tOf taps is only proper for the soldler-not for\n268\tthe civilianz IL is an Army regulation, pre-\n269\tscribed in the Army funeral service. OF course\n270\tIL would be proper for Gen Sherman He has\n271\tbeen retired from the Army. But. strictly\n272\tspeaking, 10 Is not proper 10 sound taps for a man\n273\twho has perhaps served four years in the Army.\n274\taud who has made his living and name and\n275\tfame ia civil life.\n276\t\n277\t\n278\t- No wonder Gen. Noyes insisted on quiet\n279\taud transformations funeral. Ii WAS his csue.\n280\tciaI wish that there be nothing of the pomp\n281\taud show Of a military burial. lie was a\n282\tcivilian, had earned his living and his hOnors\n283\tIa civilians life, although hc had served with\n284\tdistinction IN the years of the war. But he\n285\twas not a soldier. He was o civilian. OF\n286\tcourse, the tribute Of his comrades has 10 be\n287\texpected, and 1t was fitting. But Gen. Noyeg,\n288\tand have often heard him speak Of il, wanted\n289\t70 be put away quietly as a citizen aud as be-\n290\tcame such.\n291\t\n292\t\n293\t\" IT was proper and a regulation, Of course.\n294\tTO sound taps for Cen Sheridan But Gen.\n295\tLogan died Ia civilians life. He had been the\n296\tsoldier Of the emergency; that he was the\n297\tcivilian again, and so he died, full Of honor, il\n298\tIs true, aud beloved by all his comrades. Bnt\n299\t] think IL high time 10 cease that sort Of thing.\n300\tHere we arc % years after the war and fol\n301\tlowing the customs Of the military in burying\n302\ta civilian. Certainly il Is all very beautiful\n303\tbut the sooner these customs cease the better,\n304\tThey are useless. mean nothing. and are not be-\n305\tfitting tue civilian. The less pomp and osten-\n306\ttation about funerals the better for us all, any\n307\tway. ii would be good deal better il We were\n308\t70 drop the titles If General and Colonel and\n309\tMajor aud Captain aud 50 on for men who were\n310\tsoldiers four years aud have been civilians all\n311\tthe rest Of their lives. There should be no\n312\tmore out taps for civilians and less reminders\n313\tof war\"\n314\t\n315\t=== 1_1900-07-12_pna_sn92051501_00271763682_1900071201_0330.json\n316\tThe Press. (Stafford Springs, Conn.) 1883-1935 · 1900-07-12\n317\tcrops: cached\n318\theadline: dozen PAGES when my ciutcned mana\n319\t---\n320\tdozen PAGES when my ciutcned nand\n321\twas laid fatty upon q human face.\n322\tcould feel the nose and eye sockets\n323\tagainst my fingers, and a stubble Of\n324\tbeard pricked my palm. Now. gentle\n325\tmen, this may seem like nothing as !\n326\ttell it here. with the incandescents\n327\tablaze and people passing to and fro,\n328\tbut In that black, deserted rookery 1t\n329\twas the Incarnation Of horror. | real\n330\tlied then and there, for the first time\n331\tIn my life, the real meaning Of the\n332\tword fright. If 1 had actually seen q\n333\tghost, it would have shaken me less,\n334\tbut that face against my hand in the\n335\tdark-ah, will never forget it. never,\n336\tas long as live!\n337\t\n338\t\n339\t81 recoiled, and as ! did so my fin\n340\tgers encountered the matchbox. By\n341\tpure instinct l snatched out dozen\n342\tmatches and struck them on the table.\n343\tA gas jet was half a foot away, and in\n344\tIN instant had 1t lit. Then under\n345\tstood the situation, and you will smile,\n346\tno doubt, when l explain. A printer,\n347\twhom l knew very well and who was\n348\tthe sot OF the office. was standing be-\n349\tside one Of the cases In a drunken\n350\tdoze He had been on a spree, and\n351\twhen the Office was closed he was ~\n352\tIng asleep, unnoticed, behind a pile of\n353\tpaper. My steps on the stairs had\n354\taroused him. and he staggered to his\n355\tfeet and stood there, silent and stupid,\n356\tuntil blundered against him In the\n357\tdark. %}s's W.. fS satti& 4 sltW\n358\t\n359\t\n360\tTHAT'S the story, and, as said be-\n361\tfore, it seems like nothing to tell, yet\n362\tthe bare thought of it has brought the\n363\tsweat to my forehead\" Orleans\n364\tTimes-Democrat.\n365\t\n366\t=== 5_1842-08-06_p1_sn83016922_00200292108_1842080601_0155.json\n367\tNone · 1842-08-06\n368\tcrops: cached\n369\theadline: THY VOICE.\n370\t---\n371\tREMEMBER THY VOICE.\n372\tremember thy voice, when evening\n373\tIs shading the earth and sky;\n374\tWhen the light of the stars is falling\n375\tOn my sad and fearful eye.\n376\t! remember its tones in ~silance,\n377\t\n378\t\n379\tWhen the moonbesms float around,\n380\tAnd the wings of spirit are folded,\n381\t\n382\t\n383\tAnd hushed is every sound.\n384\tremember thy voice, when the heavens\n385\tAre stainless and pure as my love.\n386\tWhen 70 cloud casts its shadow around mt\n387\tAs 1 worship the blue above\n388\tAnd oh. when in stiffness I'm thinking\n389\tor the shadowy spirit land,\n390\tThen it comes like the harp-like voices\n391\tor a shining angel band.\n392\tremember thy voice, when gently\n393\tThe tones that / low TO hear,\n394\tBreathing thoughts of love and ofgladness\n395\tFall sweetly on my ear.\n396\tremember it then-but it never\n397\tWill mingle with other tone;\n398\tIn its own sweet, tender music\n399\t\n400\t\n401\tII falls on my heart alone.\n402\t\n403\t=== 15_1891-01-03_pna_sn85038614_00175031030_1891010301_0024.json\n404\tRichmond dispatch. [volume] (Richmond, Va.) 1884-1903 · 1891-01-03\n405\tcrops: cached\n406\theadline: THE SENATORIAL GHosT-pANcERs\n407\tThey Talk Closure and Propose to Caucus\n408\t\n409\t\n410\tFurther Thereon\n411\t---\n412\tWAsHrNorox. January 2.-Some quiet\n413\tconsultation has been in progress among\n414\tthe Republican senators now In the city\n415\twith reference TO next weeks proceed\n416\tings. The speeches made By Senators Tel\n417