t of the Indian meridian;\n709\tthat part of Swanson county north\n710\t\n711\t\n712\tOf the line between townships I and 3\n713\tnorth, that part Of Pottawatomie\n714\tcounty included in townships 10 and\n715\t/ II north. range ? east. This terri\n716\ttory had been cleaned previously and\n717\tplaced In the clean territory by pre-\n718\tyiou, ordinations.\n719\t\n720\t\n721\tThe state board Of agriculture viii\n722\t. Immediately issue a proclamation in\n723\taccordance with the one just issued\n724\t\n725\t\n726\t- 81\n727\t\n728\t=== 5_1912-09-05_pna_sn82014546_00393344623_1912090501_0219.json\n729\tThe Jeffersonian. [volume] (Atlanta, Ga.) 1907-1917 · 1912-09-05\n730\tcrops: cached\n731\theadline: THE SEANCE.\n732\t---\n733\tA night, still, warm, full of\n734\telectricity.\n735\t\n736\t\n737\tWe sat around the medium, quiet,\n738\texpectant, hand-in-hand, talking, if\n739\tat all, only murmurs.\n740\t\n741\t\n742\tA large, square room, hung on all\n743\tsides, even before the open windows.\n744\twith dark tapestry.\n745\t\n746\t\n747\tThe only light came from an an\n748\ttlque bronze lamp, depending from\n749\tthe ceiling, the fame covered with\n750\ta globe of dull greenish-yeIlow glass,\n751\tinscribed with mystical characters\n752\tresembling Sanscrit-a light, pale.\n753\tweird and unreal beyond descrip-\n754\tton.\n755\t\n756\t\n757\tMadame c. was a woman of\n758\tundoubted honesty and the deepest\n759\tenthusiasm in her faith. Dark. pale\n760\tand delicate, with high-bred, aristo-\n761\tcratic features and deep-set eyes.\n762\tshe was one, once seen, never to be\n763\tforgotten. Wonderful we're the\n764\trevelations that sometimes fell from\n765\ther lips\n766\t\n767\t\n768\tSeated in our midst, with bowed\n769\thead and folded hands, she seemed\n770\tto vibrate like a harp to the touch\n771\tOF invisible presences. And while\n772\tline current, like that from a Fara-\n773\tdie battery, ran through the linked\n774\thands about her, she gave a message\n775\tto each from loved ones on the\n776\tshadowy shore. Her voice, like her\n777\tface, was vibrant, wonderful. fitting\n778\tevery word with subtle intensity of\n779\tmeaning.\n780\t\n781\t\n782\tSuddenly she lifted her head, her\n783\tframe rigid, her eyes staring straight\n784\tbefore her.\n785\t\n786\t\n787\t81 see OH look at him!- his\n788\teyes are like balls of fire-his teeth\n789\tshine through drawn lips. Crouch\n790\ting, stealthy, he creeps upon her. 1\n791\tsee a long, curved dagger in mis-\n792\tsleeve. She sees, but seems para\n793\tlyzed. He is close to her-he raises\n794\tto strike O Goodwill\n795\t\n796\t\n797\tAt that instant rang through the\n798\troom a scream-long-d drawn, ago\n799\tnizing, awful. Never lad l heard a\n800\tcry so full of hopeless pleading, NOR\n801\ttai terror and inarticulate hate I\n802\tam no coward, but my every nerve\n803\tshook.\n804\t\n805\t\n806\tThe medium was silent, trance\n807\tlike, but all else was confusion.\n808\t\n809\t\n810\tA lady tainted my God! this is\n811\tawful said her escort-\"a air! air!'\n812\t\n813\t\n814\ttore open the window curtains\n815\tand there-were two big tailed\n816\tcats on the roof. VIM. Lloyd in\n817\tThe September International.\n818\t\n819\t\n820\tGet our prices on job work.\n821\tThey'll pleasantly surprise you. THE\n822\tTEEEFFNE< SONIA DTTTxEEGEsTNC 10\n823\t\n824\t=== 6_1928-10-20_p4_sn95063172_00414185733_1928102001_0209.json\n825\tThe Kusko times. · 1928-10-20\n826\tcrops: cached\n827\theadline:  The seance.\n828\t---\n829\tJudge Webster Thayer at dinner in Boston was talking about spiritual ism.\n830\t\n831\t \"sir Oliver Lodge, frst-rate sci tentist. is spiritualist,\" Ke said, \"but lhis spiritualistie writings-his spirit talks with.Jhis dead son, Raymond, :and so on--well, they all seem to me as ridiculous es the the anecdote.\n832\t\n833\t Its an aneedote about store swallker in 3 department store who was\n834\t\n835\t=== 3_1916-06-15_pna_sn84026824_00271767936_1916061501_0306.json\n836\tShepherdstown register. [volume] (Shepherdstown, Va. [W. Va.]) 1849-1955 · 1916-06-15\n837\tcrops: cached\n838\theadline: LIQUOR PLAYING\n839\t\n840\t\n841\tFAST AND LOOSE\n842\t\n843\tCLAIM SUFFRAGISTS ARE WET IN\n844\tDRY COUNTIES AND DRY IN\n845\t\n846\t\n847\tWET COUNTIES.\n848\t---\n849\t1t is singular fact that the only\n850\torganized opposition to the adoption\n851\tof equal suffrage by West Virginia\n852\tcomes from Columbus, Ohio, the head\n853\tquarters of the liquor organizations Of\n854\tthe United States, and from Philadel-\n855\tphia and New York City, where the\n856\tnational liquor interests maintain\n857\tbranch headquarters.\n858\t\n859\t\n860\tBut this is not the strangest part Of\n861\tthis outside interference with the in\n862\tternal affairs of this state. These same\n863\torganizations are sending tons Of \"lit-\n864\terature\" into dry counties of West\n865\tVirginia which declares that in those\n866\tstates where women now vote their\n867\tvotes have always been cast in favor\n868\tOf liquor Into the few counties OF\n869\tthis state where the sentiment is fav-\n870\torable to the cause Of the saloon these\n871\tsame organizations are sending also\n872\ttons of literature\" which quotes facts\n873\tand figures showing that the votes Of\n874\twomen in those same states have al\n875\tways been cast against whiskey and\n876\tthe saloon.\n877\t\n878\t\n879\tBoth of these statements are not\n880\ttrue According to the sophistry of\n881\tthese foreign interlopers the vote for\n882\tthe suffrage amendment in November\n883\tshould be unanimous in this state. If\n884\tall the wet people believed the dry lit\n885\terature and the dry people believed\n886\tthe wet literature, this would be the\n887\tonly logical conclusion. AS a matter\n888\tof fact, the \"antis,\" who are financed\n889\tlargely by the liquor interests, are\n890\twilling to say or do anything to win\n891\tvotes.\n892\t\n893\t\n894\tThe people of West Virginia are too\n895\tintelligent to be caught with any such\n896\tpolitical chaff as is being mailed to\n897\tthem from outside the state by organ\n898\tizations whose desire is to make void\n899\tand of no effect the prohibition amend\n900\tment to the constitution. The vote on\n901\tthe suffrage amendment is looked on\n902\tby the liquor men OF the nation as\n903\ttest of the present sentiment of the\n904\tpeople of this state. If equal suffrage\n905\tis lost in November millions will be\n906\tspent to render innocuous the prohibit\n907\tton laws at the next session of the\n908\tLegislature. II the amendment carries\n909\tthe liquor ghost will be laid in West\n910\tVirginia for all time to come.\n911\t\n912\t=== 16_1902-09-03_p1_sn84022374_00332895461_1902090301_0716.json\n913\tEllsworth American. [volume] (Ellsworth, Me.) 1855-current · 1902-09-03\n914\tcrops: cached\n915\theadline: A SPECTRAL\n916\t\n917\t\n918\tSEA FIGHT\n919\t---\n920\t\n921\t\n922\t\n923\tSs<e myself up for lost.\n924\t\n925\t\n926\twas lying on the deck only partly\n927\tconscious when heard, or thought !\n928\theard. ships bell. II struck six times,\n929\tand knew that the hour was Z o'clock.\n930\t! knew 1t was evening. for the fun\n931\tmoon mingled its rays with the long\n932\ttwilight of that high latitude. Rising.\n933\tcrawled to the gunwale and looked\n934\tover the starboard quarter.\n935\t\n936\t\n937\tA vessel flying the stars and stripes\n938\twas sailing past with all her canvas\n939\tset And what VESSEL She was\n940\tbuilt up very high in the poop and cut\n941\taway at the bow. a sort of PROV pro-\n942\tjecting with a figurehead. She was a\n943\tman-of-war, with ports open, showing\n944\tthe muzzies Of her guns. / counted\n945\tfourteen guns on her main deck and\n946\tfour astern on deck below. This was\n947\ther broadsIde The officers wore\n948\tuniform which I had seen In pictures\n949\tof hundred or more years ago. A\n950\tslender man stood on the quarter deck\n951\twith glass to his eye. Looking in the\n952\tdirection 1t pointed. saw another ship\n953\tsimilar In build. also a man-of-war.\n954\tThe British ensign was at her peak.\n955\tThere were other vessels scattered\n956\tabout. but / took no notice of them,\n957\tbeing profoundly interested in these\n958\ttwo. evidently about to meet In combat.\n959\t\n960\t\n961\tThe American wore ship and backed\n962\this topsaIl, bringing his broadside to\n963\tbear on the Englshman. I saw man\n964\ton the latter cry out, evidently to hail\n965\tthe other, but heard no sound. A sec\n966\tond time he hailed. / saw smoke\n967\tplumes leap from the Americans side,\n968\tand still | heard no sound.\n969\t\n970\t\n971\t/ was too weak to stand longer and\n972\tsank back on the deck. On the one\n973\thand was the moon silently mounting\n974\tthe sky, on the other the fierce though\n975\tsilent battle. Both passed from my\n976\tvision for time, for became uncon-\n977\tscious. but when l recovered and rose\n978\tto look over the gunwale there were\n979\tthe two men-of-war locked In an en-\n980\tbrace of war.\n981\t\n982\t\n983\t1t was now night, and / could see\n984\tonly by moonlight. The American was\n985\tdashed to the Englishman's forward\n986\tL\n987\tquarter deck was firing shots at his enc\n988\tmics, who seemed to be endeayoring to\n989\tcut the vessels loose. Marines were\n990\tloading the pieces for him. and he\n991\tpicked off every man who approached\n992\tthe lashings. Meanwhile the Britisher\n993\twas firing her guns right through the\n994\tAmericans main deck. could see that\n995\t40, vi httlty\n996\tIn the water. Then light burst forth\n997\tbetween decks. \"She will burn If she\n998\tdoes not sink\" l said. land she will\n999\tsink IL she does not burn\"\n1000\t\n1001\t\n1002\tAnd then came the strangest thing OF\n1003\tall. | could see by the freight men\n1004\ton the doomed vessel bailing water\n1005\tthat was sinking the ship to put out\n1006\tthe fire. Lashed to a stronger ship.\n1007\tburning, sinking, broadsides continual\n1008\tly pouring through her, she was still\n1009\tendeavoring to conquer. Was there\n1010\tever pluck like that on sea or land!\n1011\tSurely there must be some god of war\n1012\ton that wrecked vessel to breathe the\n1013\tspirit of invlncibility into its uncon-\n1014\tquerable crew.\n1015\t\n1016\t\n1017\tAnd there was. On the deck stood\n1018\tthe slender man, he who with his own\n1019\thands kept his enemy from the lash\n1020\tings, lighted by fire and moon, ges-\n1021\tticuiating, doubtless swearing, direct\n1022\ting every detail, every man to do the\n1023\tpart assigned him, and inspiring all\n1024\twith his own marvellous courage.\n1025\t\n1026\t\n1027\tThe American had ceased firing with\n1028\ther cannon. At any rate. she was SO\n1029\twrapped with the other In smoke that\n1030\tIf she fired | could not tell. Sailors\n1031\twith muskets were on the foretop, the\n1032\tmaintop the mizzentop; marines were\n1033\ton the quarter deck, the poop deck and\n1034\ttop of the roundhouse. They were fir\n1035\ting muskets and hand grenades, but as\n1036\ttheir enemies were all on the lower\n1037\tdeck they were protected Men crawl\n1038\ted out on 81 yardarm of the American\n1039\tand dropped hand grenades on the\n1040\tenemy, one of which fell in an open\n1041\thatchway and exploded below with\n1042\tterrific sound. Soon after this sailor\n1043\tleaped over the Britisher's rail, TO-\n1044\tlowed by boarders, and In few 10 II\n1045\tutes the British ensign was hauled\n1046\tdown.\n1047\t\n1048\t\n1049\tThis is the last l remember of tie\n1050\tnight. When | came to consciousness\n1051\tagain. the sun stood where the neon\n1052\thad been and was binding me witt\n1053\this intense light. Rising, | swept the\n1054\thorizon with my eye. Not a vessel was\n1055\tIn sight. The sea rolled on smoothly,\n1056\tsilently, as If 1t had never been dis\n1057\tturbed by the reyerberation OF guns.\n1058\t\n1059\t\n1060\tOne effect remained with me. If\n1061\tthose men ON that burning, sinking yes\n1062\tsci could conquer, why should l de-\n1063\tspair? Going below, found fishing\n1064\ttackle and an AK. With the ak l sacri-\n1065\tfeed my own finger and with this for\n1066\tbait caught fish. l had matches in\n1067\tmy pocket and made fire, heated sea\n1068\twater and, condensing the steam, had\n1069\twater again. this time that / could\n1070\tdrink. In this fashion l lived till was\n1071\tpicked up by an American cruiser.\n1072\t\n1073\t\n1074\tI told my story to the cruisers cap\n1075\t\n1076\t=== 1_1882-05-10_p1_sn83025121_00211108897_1882051001_0084.json\n1077\tThe Hawaiian gazette. · 1882-05-10\n1078\tcrops: cached\n1079\theadline: Hawaiian Gazette Supplement, May 10, 81.\n1080\t---\n1081\tannoyance of others. In matter like the liquor\n1082\t\n1083\t\n1084\tbusiness in which there is not merely question\n1085\t\n1086\t\n1087\tabout annoyance, but also about all the best in\n1088\t\n1089\t\n1090\tterests of society, the contrast is very striking\n1091\t\n1092\t\n1093\tbetween the meanness of the man who will\n1094\tsehshly cling to the enjoyment of his liquors in\n1095\t\n1096\t\n1097\tspite of the ruin of society,\" and the nobleness of\n1098\tthose who will deny themselves this qualification\n1099\t| for the good of the community and especially of\n1100\t! the weak and poorer classes. The ability to\n1101\tcontrol ones self in the use of liquors may be\n1102\t\n1103\t\n1104\tworth much. 1t would be unwise TO insist on\n1105\t! increasing the temptation to be incorporate in\n1106\t\n1107\t\n1108\torder to afford the opportunity of learning the\n1109\tpower of self restraint, when thereby the best\n1110\t/ interests of society must be ruined. But the\n1111\t\n1112\t\n1113\tcharacter to control ones self by ef-denials for\n1114\t\n1115\t\n1116\tthe benefit of others is higher attainment. 1t\n1117\t| is the highest manhood. 1t is illustrated in all\n1118\t\n1119\t\n1120\tthe heroism of history and in the sublimest ex-\n1121\t\n1122\t\n1123\tcollencies of Christianity. In this particular\n1124\tquestion about the proposed prohibitory law, 1t\n1125\t\n1126\t\n1127\tis opposed simply by spirit to persist in the low\n1128\t\n1129\t\n1130\tqualification of the appetites to the ruin of Others\n1131\t\n1132\t\n1133\twhich arises from the sel6shness that il the\n1134\t| of all human crime and misery.\n1135\t\n1136\t\n1137\t! For tine Government to persist IN maintaining\n1138\t\n1139\t\n1140\tthe liquor traffic in order to obtain revenue would\n1141\t\n1142\t\n1143\tbe for II TO go contrary to the principal mentioned\n1144\tlying at the basis Of civil society and contrary\n1145\tTo the object for which 1t established and to\n1146\tsacrifice the welfare of society for itself as we'll\n1147\tTO pursue the suicidal policy of pduperiZing\n1148\tand demoralizing and running the society on\n1149\twhich 1t depends for revenue. 1t has been well\n1150\tsaid, no questions of revenue should stand in the\n1151\tway of questions of ref rm.\n1152\t\n1153\t\n1154\tThe lowest form of this selfish opposition is\n1155\tthat Of those who simply seek money by the\n1156\t! hqCor traffic; and there can hardly be anything\n1157\tbaser than to use mercenAry influence with the\n1158\tlaw making powers in order to prevent the needed\n1159\treform. The public will understand any such\n1160\tinfluences, and will make known to whom to at\n1161\ttribute them.\n1162\t\n1163\t\n1164\tThe reason for the proposed measure is that\n1165\tthereby intemperance may be diminished in the\n1166\tislands. 1t is not anticipated that thereby the\n1167\tliquor business will be abolished by other laws !;\n1168\tbut only that thereby with the aid of earnest pub\n1169\tq lntemDerAuCg\n1170\tgreatly diminished. This is reason enough for\n1171\tthe law. A.\n1172\t\n1173\t=== 1_1892-10-10_pna_sn94052989_00175040195_1892101001_0367.json\n1174\tThe morning call. [volume] (San Francisco [Calif.]) 1878-1895 · 1892-10-10\n1175\tcrops: cached\n1176\theadline: LOOKING FOR A MESSIAH.\n1177\tThe Indians at Pine Ridge Getting\n1178\t\n1179\t\n1180\tRather SuIIen.\n1181\tTHERE is no GHOST DANCING YET.\n1182\tSitting Bulls Prophecy Vas That the Unknown\n1183\tWould Come in 1893, and There Is\n1184\t\n1185\t\n1186\tDecided Uneasiness.\n1187\t\n1188\tTHERE is no GHOST BANGING YET\n1189\t\n1190\tSpecial to THh MORNING CALI.\n1191\t---\n1192\tOMAHA. Neb.. Oct. 9.-Dr. V. T. MeGilli.\n1193\tcuddy of Rapid City, S. Dak., well known\n1194\tas one of the best-posted men in the coun-\n1195\ttry on Indian matters, is in Omaha, having\n1196\tjust come from the Pine Ridge Agency.\n1197\tTalking to-day to Bee reporter, he said\n1198\t''I don't wish to pose as an alarmist, but\n1199\tthe situation at Pine Ridge Is not at all\n1200\tsatisfactory just at present and there Is no\n1201\timmediate prospect Of an Improvement.\n1202\tThe Indians are sullen and In many ways\n1203\tshow resentful feeling. and unless some\n1204\tthing is done to counteract this there is\n1205\tdanger of trouble next spring.\n1206\t\n1207\t\n1208\t\"The Messiah spirit is not dead, and the\n1209\twhites must not depend on the statement\n1210\tthat the Indians are no longer considering\n1211\tthe promise made by Sitting Ball and the\n1212\tother medicine men who led the trouble Of\n1213\ttwo years ago. While there is no open\n1214\tdancing, there is quiet discussion on the\n1215\tsubject, and the fact that the promise was\n1216\tthat the Messiah would come after two\n1217\tsprings, which would bring the time in the\n1218\tspring of 1898. is being carefully kept in\n1219\tmind by the Indians.\n1220\t\n1221\t\n1222\t\"The Government recently paid the In.\n1223\tdians at Pine Ridge $85, 600. and this is used\n1224\tas All argument that the Great Father fears\n1225\this red sons, else why should he seek TO\n1226\tbuy them off. Then the return from Fort\n1227\tSheridan of Short Bull and Kicking Bear\n1228\tadded another disturbing element to the\n1229\talready deeply agitated state OF affairs at\n1230\tPine Ridge.\n1231\t\n1232\t\n1233\t'The most significant thing is the fact that\n1234\tthere has been communications between the\n1235\tvarious tribes of a secret and apparently\n1236\timportant nature during the summer. There\n1237\tis a line of communication we'll established\n1238\tand constantly used between the Indian\n1239\tterritory and British America, and the In\n1240\tdians all along the line understood each\n1241\tother perfectly\"\n1242\t\n1243\t=== 1_1920-01-29_p1_sn97071038_00295868442_1920012901_0494.json\n1244\tThe gazette-times. [volume] (Heppner, Or.) 1912-1925 · 1920-01-29\n1245\tcrops: cached\n1246\theadline: PHANTOM GAVE GOOD ADVICE\n1247\t\n1248\tApparition That Appeared to French\n1249\tShepherd Was Possibly Some Re-\n1250\t\n1251\t\n1252\tIation to the Leprechaun.\n1253\t---\n1254\tEvery IriShman can tell you about\n1255\tthe Lcprechaun, the little man who\n1256\tdresses in red with a peaked cap, and\n1257\tlives in the bitches. Only n person\n1258\tcan see him at a time, and If he does\n1259\tcatch sight of him, must never take\n1260\this eyes off for a single moment.\n1261\tWatching him carefully, he must run\n1262\tand catch him, and If he succeeds in\n1263\tdoing so the Leprechaun will ransom\n1264\thimself by leading his captor to a\n1265\tcreek of buried gold. At least, so runs\n1266\tthe legend.\n1267\t\n1268\t\n1269\tSome years ago a French shepherd\n1270\tof Ayeyron lost two sheep. The fol\n1271\tlowing evening a figure in a black robe\n1272\tand with tonsured head appeared to\n1273\thim, and told him to look for the lost\n1274\tmembers of his flock in a cave near\n1275\tAltes, adding that he would there find\n1276\tmore than sheep\"\n1277\t\n1278\t\n1279\tThe next day the shepherd found\n1280\tthe cave mouth exactly as described.\n1281\tInside were his two sheep. Beyond,\n1282\the came upon a chapel carved In the\n1283\trock and containing challces, censers,\n1284\tand candIe-sticks all made of solid\n1285\tsilver, and of very considerable value.\n1286\tA letter found In a mlssal showed that\n1287\tthe place had been used as a place Of\n1288\tworship during the Reign Of Terror in\n1289\t1893. The shepherd took the orn\n1290\tments to his parish priest and was\n1291\twell rewarded for his find.\n1292\t\n1293\t=== 10_1894-02-21_pna_sn87056600_00202191514_1894022101_0115.json\n1294\tThe Indiana State sentinel. [volume] (Indianapolis) 1868-1895 · 1894-02-21\n1295\tcrops: cached\n1296\theadline: PAINTERS UNCANNY GHOST\n1297\t\n1298\tReturns 90 Haunt His Fellow ConvIcts\n1299\t\n1300\t\n1301\tprisoners Scared.\n1302\t---\n1303\tCHICAGO. Feb. l6.-The prisoners in\n1304\tthe county jail are alarmed. or pretend\n1305\tTO be. over the stories of visitations by\n1306\tthe spirit Of George II. Painter. who\n1307\twas hanged there on Jan. &c The spirit\n1308\tOF the murderer is alleged TO make un-\n1309\tcanny calls al his old quarters. a number\n1310\tof the prisoners claiming 10 have seen\n1311\tthe ghost. Prendergast, the murderer\n1312\tof Mayor HARRISON is said 20 be especial\n1313\t\" alarmed and i'm great terror Of per-\n1314\tsonal visitation from his late neighbor\n1315\tin murderers' row.\n1316\t\n1317\t=== 34_1885-07-13_pna_sn90059522_00175035278_1885071301_0140.json\n1318\tSt. Paul daily globe. [volume] (Saint Paul, Minn.) 1884-1896 · 1885-07-13\n1319\tcrops: cached\n1320\theadline: New Trotters and Pncers.\n1321\t---\n1322\tWe publish In this department this morn\n1323\ting list of new trotters and PAGERS, and\n1324\talso those that have lowered their records.\n1325\tThis record was prepared by and published\n1326\tin the last number of the Spirit of the\n1327\tTimes, and includes all such records made\n1328\t\n1329\t\n1330\tup to July 4. There are fifty-nine new trot-\n1331\ttiers and thirteen new PAGERS, making total\n1332\tof seventy-two. The time is rapidly APP\n1333\tpreaching when $50 will be where 2:4O\n1334\twas. Almost two thousand trOtters have\n1335\talready beaten $3.50. and it is predicted that\n1336\tpersons are living who will see two minutes\n1337\testablished as the fastest mile at the trot-\n1338\tting gait in harness. The Spirit says: 1.-THE\n1339\tnew trotting sireS, as far as known 10 us,\n1340\tare sixteen in number, viz: Nil Desperan-\n1341\tdun. Auditor, HermeS, AntenOr, Leland,\n1342\t\n1343\t\n1344\tRustic, Daniel Boone, Victor Mohawk,\n1345\t\n1346\t\n1347\tHambletOnian Downing, Jenkins Almont,\n1348\t\n1349\t\n1350\tConfederate Chief. Ocean Chief, Atlantic,\n1351\t\n1352\t\n1353\tColonel Cross, VOsburg and Envoy. Ten\n1354\tare Hambletonlans, Confederate Chief. At\n1355\tlantic and VOsburg representing the Mann\n1356\t\n1357\t\n1358\tbrine Chief branch, Oceana Chief is claimed\n1359\tby the Vermont Black Hawk faction, and\n1360\tEnvoy is a member of the Clay tribe. The\n1361\tleading sires still add TO their quota, Blue\n1362\tBull, Happy Medium. George Wilkes, Al\n1363\tmont, Daniel Lambert and Messenger\n1364\tDuToc each having new representatives.\n1365\t\n1366\t=== 7_1884-09-26_pna_sn83016589_00393340678_1884092601_0270.json\n1367\tThe Mankato free press. [volume] (Mankato, Blue Earth County, Minn.) 1879-1924 · 1884-09-26\n1368\tcrops: cached\n1369\theadline: The Wonderful Planchette.\n1370\t---\n1371\tIn the year 1868 u gentleman named\n1372\tKirby, who is now living in Kansas\n1373\tCity, invented q contrivance which he\n1374\tcalled 'planchette. 1t was an ordin-\n1375\tairy board of walnut, shaped like an\n1376\teasel and supported by four small\n1377\trollers. 1t was in size about as large\n1378\tas q woman's Gainsborough hat.\n1379\t\n1380\t\n1381\tThe planchette would be placed on q\n1382\ttable over sheet of Virginia white\n1383\tfoolscap. A man would be told to\n1384\tshut both of his eyes and think of\n1385\tsomething he held dear. His fingers\n1386\twould rest on the easel and he was to\n1387\tforget all about them and about it. In\n1388\tIl few minutes he would he in q sort of\n1389\treverie. The thoughts in his mind\n1390\twould gradually become absorbed on\n1391\tone point. In another minute the\n1392\tplancnette would begin to move me-\n1393\tChemically on its rollers. And then,\n1394\twithout any apparent volition on his\n1395\tpart, the pencil which was held by the\n1396\teasel would slowly trace over the\n1397\tfoolscap the name of a sweetheart, or\n1398\tthe date OF some engagement or what\n1399\tever thought was uppcrmost in his\n1400\tmind.\n1401\t\n1402\t\n1403\tThis revelation of a latent power\n1404\twas thought at first to be the most\n1405\tsurprising. But after some 10000\n1406\tplanchettes had been sold for 81 3\n1407\tpiece it ceased to be so much of a won\n1408\tder, and Mr.. Kirby retired in 1870 to\n1409\tthe west. At the same time the dis\n1410\tcussion to which the planchette gave\n1411\trise-for craze it was-.established two\n1412\tor three things. The first was that\n1413\tsome temperamcnts possessed the\n1414\telectric force to much greater ex-\n1415\ttent than others! The second was\n1416\tthat, as u general rule. women were\n1417\tmore largely endowed with it than\n1418\tmen. The secret of the piancnette was\n1419\tthat the electricity which the mind\n1420\t:ouid generate could be exercised\n1421\tthrough the hands. The secret of\n1422\tMiss Hursv's \"inexplicable power\" is\n1423\tthat magnetism in Il strong, robust but\n1424\tnot intellectual person may adhere in\n1425\tThe hands themselves and thus be used\n1426\tmuscularly.\n1427\t\n1428\t=== 16_1890-09-04_pna_sn83040198_00212473479_1890090401_0062.json\n1429\tBarton County democrat. [volume] (Great Bend, Kan.) 1885-1915 · 1890-09-04\n1430\tcrops: cached\n1431\theadline: HRIEA,\n1432\tThe Half Breed.\n1433\t\n1434\t\"A Romance of Colonial Days.\n1435\t\n1436\t~ ROBERT A. CUNNING.\n1437\t---\n1438\tra;;e By ROBERT A. CUKMI9G.\n1439\t\n1440\t\n1441\tCHAPTER IL-CoNrINUED.\n1442\t.sAre the French stirring oagsin?\" he\n1443\tasked after a pause. iI7fa\n1