n1517\tprudent calculation. AS have ALREADY\n1518\ttold thee, my visit here tonight had the\n1519\tobject of arousing thee from these book\n1520\tish dreams; yet I counsel not rashness.\n1521\tWe will help zach other, Charles. Thy\n1522\tprompt action in raising troops will re-\n1523\tfound to the family credit whether I\n1524\tsucceed in Albany or not. But to march\n1525\tto- Oswego with o hundred men were\n1526\tsheer folly\"\n1527\t\n1528\t\n1529\t''Mars' Charles\" interrupted negro\n1530\tservant, putting his gray, woolly head\n1531\tinto the room, @dem ar dogs is mighty\n1532\tUneasy. Dey is rattlin' dar chains an\n1533\tbarlxin' as if dey smelt somefin 'spicious. y\n1534\t\n1535\t\n1536\t''They scent a wild animal larking\n1537\taround the barnyard, perhaps\" replied\n1538\t,Selwyn, taking rifle from the wall.\n1539\t\n1540\t\n1541\t''Injuns, mebbe,\" suggested Peter.\n1542\t. Selwyn shook his head. =\n1543\t\n1544\t\n1545\teThe Aohawl1s are our friends said\n1546\tne, \"and would come boldly if at all. >\n1547\tSO saying, he unbolted the hall door\n1548\tand was abou9 90 step firth upon the\n1549\tmoonlit lawn, but almost stumbled over\n1550\tan object Seated on the threshold, which\n1551\tarose and stood before him silent and\n1552\tmotionless. Selwyn AWas too we'll used\n1553\tto Indian habits so be surprised at this\n1554\tapparition, Che sex and features Of\n1555\twhich were concealed by a blanket\n1556\tthrown over the dead and almost touch;\n1557\ting the ground\n1558\t\n1559\t\n1560\t'sWhom do you seek?'' asked the\n1561\tyoung man, in the Mohawk dialect.\n1562\t\n1563\t\n1564\tSlowly. the eblApket dropped from\n1565\thead and shoulders, revealing hand\n1566\tsome boyish face8 whose features were\n1567\trather French than aboriginal, and q\n1568\tpair of large sparkling black eyes were\n1569\traised to HE face with an expression\n1570\tiidll oiLys hettG SOiiL0Chos ohA ohSA\n1571\tsmall brown hand drsw from the bosom\n1572\ta letter superscribed in bold Ehglisl\n1573\thand, Charles Selwyn.\"\n1574\t\n1575\t\n1576\tTaking the messenger by the arm\n1577\t:Selwin led him into the hell and, point\n1578\ting to chair, opened the letter. Tn\n1579\tlad, however, seated himself on bear\n1580\tskin mat, and, resting his head upol\n1581\this hand, remained mOtionless as before\n1582\t+Exhausted, y thought Selwyn, glanc\n1583\ting at him. Peter, give him some food.\n1584\tWhat, have you there?\" asked ST\n1585\tWilliam But his kinsman was to-\n1586\tmuch occupied to heed the question.\n1587\t\n1588\t\n1589\t''Old comgade and brother-in-arms,\n1590\tran the missive, ''if this letter eve\n1591\treaches you, 1 confide the bearer t\n1592\tyour protection and sympathy. He ha\n1593\tthe misfortune to fall into the hands O\n1594\ta band of Hurons. who, being in a play\n1595\tfut mood, cut out his tongue. and woul\n1596\tdoubtless have deprived him of his hal\n1597\talso if they had not been surprised by\n1598\tparty of, rangers, who quickly mad\n1599\tthem look on this practical joke in\n1600\tvery serious light. The boy is faithful\n1601\tand Intelligent, and will serve thee a\n1602\tAriel served his master Prospero--\n1603\tsaved him as the battle of Crown Poin,\n1604\t-. !. ,. j. Ill ElI !. i :.\n1605\t"}]},"uuid":"0dc9697c-88d1-4cc0-b4af-c85b1d56d94a","timestamp":"2026-10-31T08:02:18.103Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/c1/g13.txt","content":"=== 29_1909-12-27_pna_sn92053934_00202198764_1909122701_0152.json\nRock Island Argus. (Rock Island, Ill.) 1893-1920 · 1909-12-27\ncrops: cached\nheadline: The Society of Spiritual Truth.\n\n\nFREE! FREE!\n1O4H West Second street.\n---\nSpiritual meeting Sunday evening\nSpirit message demonstrating immortal\nity. Tests will be given. Doors open 7\np. m. Lecture and tests p. m. Ladies\nand gentlemen welcome. Your departed\nspirit friends viii talk to you through\nthe medium. Seats free. No collections.\nLeClaire Hall, Davenport, Iowa\n\n=== 23_1899-04-19_pna_sn84038306_00415668594_1899041901_0079.json\nManchester Democrat. [volume] (Manchester, Iowa) 1875-1930 · 1899-04-19\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nWulCu Wd6 UpcuSQ Gic L ulONcu W answer,\nand in the gathering gloom beheld Capt.\nportal behind him.\n\n\nCHAPTER VIII.\n\n\nTHE HINT OF THE EARTHQUAKE.\n\n\nThe advent of this man, whom all the laws\nof dueling prohibited seeking me at the pres\nent stage of affairs, oceasioned something of\nshock. The manner of his entry, the tragic\naspect of nature, and the sickly-green light\nenveloping everything within and without;\nthe clamorous advance of the storm, and per-\nhaps as much as anything, the sudden re-\nvulsion Of temper caused by seeing him,\nthrew me for the moment off my mental\nbalance, and stood without moving, gaz-\ning at him as might have gazed at an ap-\npartition.\n\n\nAs we thus faced each other, l noticed that\nhe was hatless, coatless, and without side\narms, the latter fact causing me further sur\nprise, as was aware of the extreme pride\nBritish officer has in his sword and his re-\nluctance to lay it beyond his reach.\n\n\nHard upon these details came three others\nof different nature. saw at once that the\nman was intoxicated, though not beyond his\nown control; that in his left hand he had an\nopen paper, while his right, which still hung\nby his side, held cooked pistol, though this\nI would not have seen but for the sudden\nCLEAN of flash of lightning. 1t gave me a\nquick realization of the fact that / was in\ngreat danger and without the means of COM\nbatting it, for my sword was in the closet,\nwhile both pistols and rife lay unloaded in\na corner.\n\n\nThe crash of thunder following the flash\nESQ H. ]\naction was equally out of the question, for\nwas casting searching glances about the room\nfor some means of defense, he raised the\nweapon and leveled it at me.\n\n\n/ could fathom no motive for his thus at\nlacking ME, save the heat in his rum-mad.\ndened brain, and, were he impelled by drink,\nmy position was doubly critical. Had there\nbeen ghost of chance to act in the ag-\ngressive, in this desperate moment / should\nhave availed myself of it; but not even that\nreally formidable weapon and guard, chair,\nwas nearer than the table, and to have made\nmove toward it felt meant certain de-\nBtruction. A sudden attack on him was out\nIF my power, for betwixt US interviewed the\nbroad table itself, which made it impossible\nTO avail myself of the tactics had used with\nLounsbury. There was naught for me to do\nbut stand as was and await the terrible\nissue.\n\n\nHowever, as he remained silent, man.\nnged to find my voice, and, as the thunder\n| rolled in the distance, ! shouted:\n\n\ncapt. Scanned, are you SO demented or\n% cowardly and 30 lost to honor that you\nwill murder the man you challenged to tight\nin the feld?\"\n\n\nWithout swervs of the arm, he answered\nstsrnly:\n\n\n\"Sit DOWN.\"\n\n\nEven thus beset, it irked me to obey him\nlike . schoolboy, but realized that, armed,\nunder the existing circumstances he was far\nstronger than with all my sinews. Wisely,\nthen, l swallowed my ire, and, approaching\nthe table, seated myself with the hope thai\nhe would come within reach.\n\n\nHe was not allow in following, for he\nstepped forward and carefully slid into the\nchair opposite, though not for second un-\ncovering me with the muzzle of his pistol, or,\nfor that matter, taking his eye from mine.\nAnd here we sat while flash and crash fol-\nlowed each other in quick succession. Ny\nbrain worked rapidly enough, but as the sec\nonds sped no way could 1 find to rid me of the\nshining ring of steel confronting me or turn\nfor moment the equally brilliant CLINT of\nhis eye, each of which acquired clearness\nwith the fitful lighting of the room.\n\n\nThe table was too broad for an onslaught,\nand now that we were closer, could see\nthe nerves of the man were at full tension,\nand thnt on the slightest hostile move on my\nFart he would pull the trigger.\n\n\nI hoped lightning would strike the house\nand put an end to the situation, which at\nlast began to have its effect on me; and I\ntake it that no man can long stand the scowl\nOf loaded barrel backed by determined\nyet silent enemy without undergoing more\nor less strain.\n\n\nTwas even wishing that the heavens would\nlet loose bolt and finish the matter, or that\nthe man opposite would open his lips and\nspeak, despite the roar, when felt the floor\nbeneath me tremble, as though the house\nwas shaken by mighty power. It passed\nin the instant, and as it did 80 the thunder\nfaded away for the moment, and Scam\nnell, lowering his hand, let it rest on the\nedge of the table, whence the pistol held me\n| full in the chest. Then he spoke and per-\nhaps because the excitement indoors and out\nhad partly sobered him, his words came\nclearly:\n\n\nII have here your ID message in my\nhand, but, as you may well guess, it is fore\n/ bCohC0s 1AClG Will DC ukuL UeGWeeQ un\ntomorrow morning\"\n\n\nBecause you deem it safer to murder me\nnow?\" l returned calmly. \"Give me but\"\n\n\nShut your MOUTH he interrupted, let\nting go his temper. do you think am here\nto handy words with rebel? Capt. Donald\n| Thorndye, alias Lounsbury, you are close on\nto the end of your rope, and you'll find that\n| end has moose in it, you cursed spy! What\"\nhe continued, rising in his increasing rage,\nand Immediately reseating himself, while\nhis voice grew louder, \"do you think am\nfool, or SO blind that may not see through\na riddle? I have found you out, hide and\nhair-Thorndyke, of Martha's Vineyard\nsmuggler- Had you escaped me\nthis day, and been off on Clinton's business,\nwhich doubtless lies there\" (pointing to my\npapers, which lay on the table,, 1T would\nhave hunted you down had it cost me my\ncommission. Escape me? No, by the gods!\nFrom the beginning l fancied you the fraud\nyou are, and at the Bulls Head, by your\nwords and fine airs, you made me sure Of it.\nYou slipped your role. You'll find your hen.\nhawk has sharp eyes, and, giant though you\nbe, you will see that seamew is nothing in\nhis grip. Nay, I have not come to shoot you\ndown as you deserve, but know your in\nfernal tricks, and mean to hold you where\nyou belong. take it twill afford me more\npleasure to see you dance at ropes end\nthan dirty my hands with your blood, save\nyou make it urgent. Let me clinch the mat\nter now, and then make you ready to march.\nBelow stairs at this moment is Lounsbury in\nthe flesh, and with him file of men to take\nyou off. This bit of play was for my own\npleasure, and, having had it, I'll be your\nvalet and stay by your heels until you are\nsafely delivered to Cunningham. Now God\nrest your miserable soul! Have you aught\nto say before give the word to the rest?\"\n\n\nTo state that was unaffected by these\nwords, which were poured forth in ton\nrent as though my tormentor feared inter\nruption, would be false. saw my plans for\nthe future, my hope for life itself, swept\naway together, and nothing but blank,\nkfNksA Snlp ~ 4 S3ohna p ~ sna,nma\n\n=== 12_1890-10-23_pna_sn82016187_00211102949_1890102301_0596.json\nThe National tribune. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1877-1917 · 1890-10-23\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n\nA Ceremony That is Only Proper for The Soldier.\n\n\nlCiHciunnn Timer Sfar1\n---\nlCiHcilnah Tigaes-star.l\n\n\nA reporter was talking 20 Gen. b. r. Cowen\nabout what touching ceremony was the sound\ning Of taps at the grave Of a soldier a. the last\nfarewell. How sad. and tender, and beautiful\nil was And what a picture for memory it\nmade-the grouping about the grave the list\nening 10 the refrain that flows 50 piaintiyely\nfrom the buglc, and steals echoing away, sad\nplaintive, appealing wall, that has authority,\nirreyocableness and tenderness In it. And one\ncould well imagine, while sorrowfully listening\n70 II, that the spirit Of the dead soldier must\nbe a-listcuing 10 II, too, the martial spirit\nia life had been lullcd by its 4\" GOOD. good\nnight! Good goodnight! until his eyclids\nwould close ere the last sound stole eChoing\nover the camp.\n\n\ndo you know\" said he that the sounding\nOf taps is only proper for the soldler-not for\nthe civilianz IL is an Army regulation, pre-\nscribed in the Army funeral service. OF course\nIL would be proper for Gen Sherman He has\nbeen retired from the Army. But. strictly\nspeaking, 10 Is not proper 10 sound taps for a man\nwho has perhaps served four years in the Army.\naud who has made his living and name and\nfame ia civil life.\n\n\n- No wonder Gen. Noyes insisted on quiet\naud transformations funeral. Ii WAS his csue.\nciaI wish that there be nothing of the pomp\naud show Of a military burial. lie was a\ncivilian, had earned his living and his hOnors\nIa civilians life, although hc had served with\ndistinction IN the years of the war. But he\nwas not a soldier. He was o civilian. OF\ncourse, the tribute Of his comrades has 10 be\nexpected, and 1t was fitting. But Gen. Noyeg,\nand have often heard him speak Of il, wanted\n70 be put away quietly as a citizen aud as be-\ncame such.\n\n\n\" IT was proper and a regulation, Of course.\nTO sound taps for Cen Sheridan But Gen.\nLogan died Ia civilians life. He had been the\nsoldier Of the emergency; that he was the\ncivilian again, and so he died, full Of honor, il\nIs true, aud beloved by all his comrades. Bnt\n] think IL high time 10 cease that sort Of thing.\nHere we arc % years after the war and fol\nlowing the customs Of the military in burying\na civilian. Certainly il Is all very beautiful\nbut the sooner these customs cease the better,\nThey are useless. mean nothing. and are not be-\nfitting tue civilian. The less pomp and osten-\ntation about funerals the better for us all, any\nway. ii would be good deal better il We were\n70 drop the titles If General and Colonel and\nMajor aud Captain aud 50 on for men who were\nsoldiers four years aud have been civilians all\nthe rest Of their lives. There should be no\nmore out taps for civilians and less reminders\nof war\"\n\n=== 1_1900-07-12_pna_sn92051501_00271763682_1900071201_0330.json\nThe Press. (Stafford Springs, Conn.) 1883-1935 · 1900-07-12\ncrops: cached\nheadline: dozen PAGES when my ciutcned mana\n---\ndozen PAGES when my ciutcned nand\nwas laid fatty upon q human face.\ncould feel the nose and eye sockets\nagainst my fingers, and a stubble Of\nbeard pricked my palm. Now. gentle\nmen, this may seem like nothing as !\ntell it here. with the incandescents\nablaze and people passing to and fro,\nbut In that black, deserted rookery 1t\nwas the Incarnation Of horror. | real\nlied then and there, for the first time\nIn my life, the real meaning Of the\nword fright. If 1 had actually seen q\nghost, it would have shaken me less,\nbut that face against my hand in the\ndark-ah, will never forget it. never,\nas long as live!\n\n\n81 recoiled, and as ! did so my fin\ngers encountered the matchbox. By\npure instinct l snatched out dozen\nmatches and struck them on the table.\nA gas jet was half a foot away, and in\nIN instant had 1t lit. Then under\nstood the situation, and you will smile,\nno doubt, when l explain. A printer,\nwhom l knew very well and who was\nthe sot OF the office. was standing be-\nside one Of the cases In a drunken\ndoze He had been on a spree, and\nwhen the Office was closed he was ~\nIng asleep, unnoticed, behind a pile of\npaper. My steps on the stairs had\naroused him. and he staggered to his\nfeet and stood there, silent and stupid,\nuntil blundered against him In the\ndark. %}s's W.. fS satti& 4 sltW\n\n\nTHAT'S the story, and, as said be-\nfore, it seems like nothing to tell, yet\nthe bare thought of it has brought the\nsweat to my forehead\" Orleans\nTimes-Democrat.\n\n=== 5_1842-08-06_p1_sn83016922_00200292108_1842080601_0155.json\nNone · 1842-08-06\ncrops: cached\nheadline: THY VOICE.\n---\nREMEMBER THY VOICE.\nremember thy voice, when evening\nIs shading the earth and sky;\nWhen the light of the stars is falling\nOn my sad and fearful eye.\n! remember its tones in ~silance,\n\n\nWhen the moonbesms float around,\nAnd the wings of spirit are folded,\n\n\nAnd hushed is every sound.\nremember thy voice, when the heavens\nAre stainless and pure as my love.\nWhen 70 cloud casts its shadow around mt\nAs 1 worship the blue above\nAnd oh. when in stiffness I'm thinking\nor the shadowy spirit land,\nThen it comes like the harp-like voices\nor a shining angel band.\nremember thy voice, when gently\nThe tones that / low TO hear,\nBreathing thoughts of love and ofgladness\nFall sweetly on my ear.\nremember it then-but it never\nWill mingle with other tone;\nIn its own sweet, tender music\n\n\nII falls on my heart alone.\n\n=== 15_1891-01-03_pna_sn85038614_00175031030_1891010301_0024.json\nRichmond dispatch. [volume] (Richmond, Va.) 1884-1903 · 1891-01-03\ncrops: cached\nheadline: THE SENATORIAL GHosT-pANcERs\nThey Talk Closure and Propose to Caucus\n\n\nFurther Thereon\n---\nWAsHrNorox. January 2.-Some quiet\nconsultation has been in progress among\nthe Republican senators now In the city\nwith reference TO next weeks proceed\nings. The speeches made By Senators Tel\nTer and Wolcott last Tuesday have made\nsome q impression, but = they we're expect;\nIs that resulting from the few re-\nmarks made BY Benator Hale. In bis\ncomplaint that trial had not been\nmade of old-fashioned nethods 10 secure\naetiop rpon the eleetions BIN he undoubt-\nodly Voiced The feelings of coma othet CON\nssgystiye &eRsblieen ssnstes who tel\n\n=== 2_1917-11-23_pna_sn89066617_00200292315_1917112301_0184.json\nThe weekly tribune and the Cape County herald. (Cape Girardeau, Mo.) 1914-1918 · 1917-11-23\ncrops: cached\nheadline: War Will End In\n\n\nJan. 1919, Says\nThe Ouija Board\n\nInstrument Which Transmits\nMessages From Spooks\nAlso Declares That Euro\npean Monarchs WillSkidoo\n---\nThe Ouija board, that wonderful\nlittle instrument which reads your flu,\nture without looking at the palm OF\nthe hand, sees the end of the war with\nin the next year. After peace is re-\nstored the world will be better than\never before and the peoples happier\naccording to Ouija.\n\n\nA. j. Schmittzehe Jr., clerk in the\nVorbeck-Dohogne store, has been en-\n\n\ntertaining his friends recently with\nlittle chats with the spirits. The Out\nia board is supposed to deliver mes\nsages from the dead. But whether\nthey come from the graves or not, it\nis a fact that it delivers messages\nand in manner that is reminiscent of\nghosts.\n\n\nMr.. Schmittzehe and home friends\nyesterday asked the Ouija board to\ndeliver 4 message about the war. 1t\nspelled these words: \"The war will end\nin year\" When asked to be more\nspecific, it spelled the month of JAN\nuary and then added 1919\" Accord\ning to Mr.. Schmittzehe, who plays\nwith the Ouija just as gracefully as a\ndebutante tickles the ivory on a pi-\nano, is positive that the war will end\nin January, 1919.\n\n\nThe Ouija informed its owner that\nthe world would be less sinful after\npeace had been declared and that the\npeople would live happily ever after.\n\n\nWhile it declined to say what dis\nposition would be made of the Euro\npean monarchs, it did say that the ski\ndoc sign would be hung on all of them\nand that Europe would not only be-\ncome safe for Democracy but every\nnation would be a republic. When\nasked. whether the report that Car\nNicholas has been made king of ST.\nIBERIA was true, the Ouija promptly re-\npied IN the negative. 1t said Nick\ncouldn't come back.\n\n\nThe war Will not be any worse than\n1t is today, according to Ouija, the\nspirits apparently believing that the\nlimit has been reached. When the\nOuiJa board told Mr.. Schmittzehe that\nCol. I. Roosevelt would not be Presi-\ndent again, Schmittzehe simply re-\nmarked: \"Ain't it the truth\" and\noulJa was pronounced a thoroughly\nboard and then placed back\nIN the showcase. \"Them spirits are\nnot as dead as you think\" added\nSchmittzehe.\n\n=== 2_1912-05-06_p1_sn93055779_00295872664_1912050601_0909.json\nThe Cairo bulletin. · 1912-05-06\ncrops: cached\nheadline: NEVER AGAIN FOR THIS MAN\n\nYouth Meant Well, but Recollection\nOf Episode Is Haunting\n\n\nMemory.\n---\nThe newly established practice In\nthe Equal Rights association Of APP\npointing attractive young women to\ndispose of suffrage literature, badges,\nsandwiches, etc, has been the cause of\nsome amusing mistakes. One Of these\nwas wholly lacking in humor, from the\npoint of view of the unfortunate per.\nson who lived through 1t.\n\n\nIt was this way: An amiable young\nman. walking down Broadway one\nmorning q short time ago, saw stand\ning on the corner q beautiful young\ngirl She held In her hand bunch Of\nviolets and camelias. At her feet lay\ntray Of these flowers, and she\nglanced shyly and appealingly at the\npassers-by. The young man had\nstarted across the street when he saw\nher. He hesitated, turned, looked\nback at her uncertainty, and then\nsquaring his Shoulders, strobe to her\nside. 3 ~\n\n\nMay be permitted to buy them an\nand help the cause along?\" he asked\nCALAMITY, standing before her, hat m\nhand, and smiling an irresistible\nsmilr. ..M...\n\n\nTie girl turned, looked at him for\nan instant In blank amazement, and\nthen flushed Indignantly, The wretch.\ned youth suddenly saw a boy dart\nfrom nearby store and rush up to\nthe young lady with a handful OF\nchange. She took the change, pinned\non her flowers and with whitening\nglance at the would-be supporter OF\nthe cause who remained rooted to\nthe spot, marched away.\n\n\nWhen ho was able to speak dispas.\nsionatcly Of the incident, the youth de-\nclared that to the best of his recollec-\nton the pavement opened and swal.\n\n=== 10_1911-11-10_pna_sn85042344_00237284501_1911111001_0683.json\nTulsa daily world. [volume] (Tulsa, Indian Territory [Okla.]) 1905-1919 · 1911-11-10\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Federal ACTION\n\n\nRAISES VALUES\n\nADDITIONAL TERRITORY PLACED\nABOVE UARANTlNE MEANS\n\n\n$500 \\ HEAD.\n\nTICK ABOUT ERAOICTED\n\nProclamation Affect, haOut 10000\nCattle Now Taucuing for Market.\n\n\nstate Board Also to Act\n---\nover two thousand heed ~ Catt'e\nare now being fsl at point just north\nOf the northern boundary line Of the\ncity OF Tulsa These and herds iu ,he\nUsage nation that are now being fed\nfor the market have been enhanced in\nvalue about $500 q head by the ace\nlion Of the federal government just\nrecently IN placing above the federal\nquarantine line additional territory .n\nOklahoma which has been worked by\nthe federal and state forces Ia fever\ntick eradication during 1911. The proc\nlamation affecting approximately 49,\n600 head Of cattle and increasing the\nvaluation Of this amount by about\n100000 was received this week by\nDr. L. J. Allen. in charge Of the quar-\nantine work for the federal govern\nment in Oklahoma and Texas.\n\n\nThe new territory placed above the\nfederal quarantine line this year em-\nbraces about 100000 acres in the\nbest Of the cattle country in the state.\nThis me. rs that cattle from this terri\ntory may 60 to the native markets as\nclean cattle and command the highest\nmarket price. Heretofore they could\nnot. and the difference in price aver\nages about SO head\n\n\nThe new territory in Oklahoma\nplaced above the quarantine line this\nyear according to the PROCLAMATION\njust out. includes all Of Kay county.\nnow under quarantine, the Kaw res\nervation. that part OF Usage county\nwest of line beginning at the Kan\nsas line between sections II and IS N\nrange east, thence south on section\nline to line between townships 2S and\n29 north: thence east on section line\nto line between section and 3.\ntownship 2S north, range east.\nthence south on section line to line\nbetween townships 25 and 20 north;\nthence east on section line TO its in\ntersection with line between sections\nand 6, township % north. range !\neast; thence south on section line TO\nits intersection with the Arkansas\nriver: all Of Noble and Payne counties\nnow under quarantine; that part of\nPawnee county west OF the NJ. X. &\nT.; all of Lincoln county except that\npart south OF line between townships\nIS and IA north, lying east of the A.,\nT. & 5. T.. and that part of Grady\ncounty west Of the c.. Il. l. & F.. ly\ning north Of the ST. /. q S F.\n\n\nThe past year has been one Of the\nmost successful in the history Of tick\neradication in Oklahoma From the\ntime farm to farm canvass. was be-\ngun by the territorial and federal\ngovernment about seven years ago the\nwork Of eradication has been carried\non increasingly. When the work was\nbegun there was hardly a spot in the\nwhole state but was infested and cat\n\n\ntie were commanding the minimum\nprice on the market as a consequence.\nAlthough il meant on an average\nOf 81 or IS a head for cattle cleaned\n\n\nof fever ticks as to market price.\nthere was no little opposition to the\neradication work. Farmers at first\ndid not believe it would do any good\nand many emphatically declared that\nthe ticks could not be eXterminated.\nPersistent effort showed they could.\nwhich was the best argument and\nwhen the results Of the work began\nto be shown many of those who SIN\ncerely hoped for results were con-\nWINGED and their cooperation began.\nIn addition TO the territory placed\nabove the quarantine line stated above,\nthe counties Of Cimarron, Texas, Bea-\nyer. Harper. Woods. Alfalfa, Grant,\n\n\nWoodward. Major. Ellis. Garfield,\nI Dewey. Blaine. Kingfisher. Logan.\nRoger Mills. Custer. Beckham. Wash\n/ ita. Greer Kiowa. Caddo. Jackson.\nOklahoma. Harmon. Canadian. that\n\n\npart Of Cleveland county north OF the\nlire between townships and north.\nand west of the Indian meridian;\nthat part of Swanson county north\n\n\nOf the line between townships I and 3\nnorth, that part Of Pottawatomie\ncounty included in townships 10 and\n/ II north. range ? east. This terri\ntory had been cleaned previously and\nplaced In the clean territory by pre-\nyiou, ordinations.\n\n\nThe state board Of agriculture viii\n. Immediately issue a proclamation in\naccordance with the one just issued\n\n\n- 81\n\n=== 5_1912-09-05_pna_sn82014546_00393344623_1912090501_0219.json\nThe Jeffersonian. [volume] (Atlanta, Ga.) 1907-1917 · 1912-09-05\ncrops: cached\nheadline: THE SEANCE.\n---\nA night, still, warm, full of\nelectricity.\n\n\nWe sat around the medium, quiet,\nexpectant, hand-in-hand, talking, if\nat all, only murmurs.\n\n\nA large, square room, hung on all\nsides, even before the open windows.\nwith dark tapestry.\n\n\nThe only light came from an an\ntlque bronze lamp, depending from\nthe ceiling, the fame covered with\na globe of dull greenish-yeIlow glass,\ninscribed with mystical characters\nresembling Sanscrit-a light, pale.\nweird and unreal beyond descrip-\nton.\n\n\nMadame c. was a woman of\nundoubted honesty and the deepest\nenthusiasm in her faith. Dark. pale\nand delicate, with high-bred, aristo-\ncratic features and deep-set eyes.\nshe was one, once seen, never to be\nforgotten. Wonderful we're the\nrevelations that sometimes fell from\nher lips\n\n\nSeated in our midst, with bowed\nhead and folded hands, she seemed\nto vibrate like a harp to the touch\nOF invisible presences. And while\nline current, like that from a Fara-\ndie battery, ran through the linked\nhands about her, she gave a message\nto each from loved ones on the\nshadowy shore. Her voice, like her\nface, was vibrant, wonderful. fitting\nevery word with subtle intensity of\nmeaning.\n\n\nSuddenly she lifted her head, her\nframe rigid, her eyes staring straight\nbefore her.\n\n\n81 see OH look at him!- his\neyes are like balls of fire-his teeth\nshine through drawn lips. Crouch\ning, stealthy, he creeps upon her. 1\nsee a long, curved dagger in mis-\nsleeve. She sees, but seems para\nlyzed. He is close to her-he raises\nto strike O Goodwill\n\n\nAt that instant rang through the\nroom a scream-long-d drawn, ago\nnizing, awful. Never lad l heard a\ncry so full of hopeless pleading, NOR\ntai terror and inarticulate hate I\nam no coward, but my every nerve\nshook.\n\n\nThe medium was silent, trance\nlike, but all else was confusion.\n\n\nA lady tainted my God! this is\nawful said her escort-\"a air! air!'\n\n\ntore open the window curtains\nand there-were two big tailed\ncats on the roof. VIM. Lloyd in\nThe September International.\n\n\nGet our prices on job work.\nThey'll pleasantly surprise you. THE\nTEEEFFNE< SONIA DTTTxEEGEsTNC 10\n\n=== 6_1928-10-20_p4_sn95063172_00414185733_1928102001_0209.json\nThe Kusko times. · 1928-10-20\ncrops: cached\nheadline:  The seance.\n---\nJudge Webster Thayer at dinner in Boston was talking about spiritual ism.\n\n \"sir Oliver Lodge, frst-rate sci tentist. is spiritualist,\" Ke said, \"but lhis spiritualistie writings-his spirit talks with.Jhis dead son, Raymond, :and so on--well, they all seem to me as ridiculous es the the anecdote.\n\n Its an aneedote about store swallker in 3 department store who was\n\n=== 3_1916-06-15_pna_sn84026824_00271767936_1916061501_0306.json\nShepherdstown register. [volume] (Shepherdstown, Va. [W. Va.]) 1849-1955 · 1916-06-15\ncrops: cached\nheadline: LIQUOR PLAYING\n\n\nFAST AND LOOSE\n\nCLAIM SUFFRAGISTS ARE WET IN\nDRY COUNTIES AND DRY IN\n\n\nWET COUNTIES.\n---\n1t is singular fact that the only\norganized opposition to the adoption\nof equal suffrage by West Virginia\ncomes from Columbus, Ohio, the head\nquarters of the liquor organizations Of\nthe United States, and from Philadel-\nphia and New York City, where the\nnational liquor interests maintain\nbranch headquarters.\n\n\nBut this is not the strangest part Of\nthis outside interference with the in\nternal affairs of this state. These same\norganizations are sending tons Of \"lit-\nerature\" into dry counties of West\nVirginia which declares that in those\nstates where women now vote their\nvotes have always been cast in favor\nOf liquor Into the few counties OF\nthis state where the sentiment is fav-\norable to the cause Of the saloon these\nsame organizations are sending also\ntons of literature\" which quotes facts\nand figures showing that the votes Of\nwomen in those same states have al\nways been cast against whiskey and\nthe saloon.\n\n\nBoth of these statements are not\ntrue According to the sophistry of\nthese foreign interlopers the vote for\nthe suffrage amendment in November\nshould be unanimous in this state. If\nall the wet people believed the dry lit\nerature and the dry people believed\nthe wet literature, this would be the\nonly logical conclusion. AS a matter\nof fact, the \"antis,\" who are financed\nlargely by the liquor interests, are\nwilling to say or do anything to win\nvotes.\n\n\nThe people of West Virginia are too\nintelligent to be caught with any such\npolitical chaff as is being mailed to\nthem from outside the state by organ\nizations whose desire is to make void\nand of no effect the prohibition amend\nment to the constitution. The vote on\nthe suffrage amendment is looked on\nby the liquor men OF the nation as\ntest of the present sentiment of the\npeople of this state. If equal suffrage\nis lost in November millions will be\nspent to render innocuous the prohibit\nton laws at the next session of the\nLegislature. II the amendment carries\nthe liquor ghost will be laid in West\nVirginia for all time to come.\n\n=== 16_1902-09-03_p1_sn84022374_00332895461_1902090301_0716.json\nEllsworth American. [volume] (Ellsworth, Me.) 1855-current · 1902-09-03\ncrops: cached\nheadline: A SPECTRAL\n\n\nSEA FIGHT\n---\n\n\n\nSs<e myself up for lost.\n\n\nwas lying on the deck only partly\nconscious when heard, or thought !\nheard. ships bell. II struck six times,\nand knew that the hour was Z o'clock.\n! knew 1t was evening. for the fun\nmoon mingled its rays with the long\ntwilight of that high latitude. Rising.\ncrawled to the gunwale and looked\nover the starboard quarter.\n\n\nA vessel flying the stars and stripes\nwas sailing past with all her canvas\nset And what VESSEL She was\nbuilt up very high in the poop and cut\naway at the bow. a sort of PROV pro-\njecting with a figurehead. She was a\nman-of-war, with ports open, showing\nthe muzzies Of her guns. / counted\nfourteen guns on her main deck and\nfour astern on deck below. This was\nher broadsIde The officers wore\nuniform which I had seen In pictures\nof hundred or more years ago. A\nslender man stood on the quarter deck\nwith glass to his eye. Looking in the\ndirection 1t pointed. saw another ship\nsimilar In build. also a man-of-war.\nThe British ensign was at her peak.\nThere were other vessels scattered\nabout. but / took no notice of them,\nbeing profoundly interested in these\ntwo. evidently about to meet In combat.\n\n\nThe American wore ship and backed\nhis topsaIl, bringing his broadside to\nbear on the Englshman. I saw man\non the latter cry out, evidently to hail\nthe other, but heard no sound. A sec\nond time he hailed. / saw smoke\nplumes leap from the Americans side,\nand still | heard no sound.\n\n\n/ was too weak to stand longer and\nsank back on the deck. On the one\nhand was the moon silently mounting\nthe sky, on the other the fierce though\nsilent battle. Both passed from my\nvision for time, for became uncon-\nscious. but when l recovered and rose\nto look over the gunwale there were\nthe two men-of-war locked In an en-\nbrace of war.\n\n\n1t was now night, and / could see\nonly by moonlight. The American was\ndashed to the Englishman's forward\nL\nquarter deck was firing shots at his enc\nmics, who seemed to be endeayoring to\ncut the vessels loose. Marines were\nloading the pieces for him. and he\npicked off every man who approached\nthe lashings. Meanwhile the Britisher\nwas firing her guns right through the\nAmericans main deck. could see that\n40, vi httlty\nIn the water. Then light burst forth\nbetween decks. \"She will burn If she\ndoes not sink\" l said. land she will\nsink IL she does not burn\"\n\n\nAnd then came the strangest thing OF\nall. | could see by the freight men\non the doomed vessel bailing water\nthat was sinking the ship to put out\nthe fire. Lashed to a stronger ship.\nburning, sinking, broadsides continual\nly pouring through her, she was still\nendeavoring to conquer. Was there\never pluck like that on sea or land!\nSurely there must be some god of war\non that wrecked vessel to breathe the\nspirit of invlncibility into its uncon-\nquerable crew.\n\n\nAnd there was. On the deck stood\nthe slender man, he who with his own\nhands kept his enemy from the lash\nings, lighted by fire and moon, ges-\nticuiating, doubtless swearing, direct\ning every detail, every man to do the\npart assigned him, and inspiring all\nwith his own marvellous courage.\n\n\nThe American had ceased firing with\nher cannon. At any rate. she was SO\nwrapped with the other In smoke that\nIf she fired | could not tell. Sailors\nwith muskets were on the foretop, the\nmaintop the mizzentop; marines were\non the quarter deck, the poop deck and\ntop of the roundhouse. They were fir\ning muskets and hand grenades, but as\ntheir enemies were all on the lower\ndeck they were protected Men crawl\ned out on 81 yardarm of the American\nand dropped hand grenades on the\nenemy, one of which fell in an open\nhatchway and exploded below with\nterrific sound. Soon after this sailor\nleaped over the Britisher's rail, TO-\nlowed by boarders, and In few 10 II\nutes the British ensign was hauled\ndown.\n\n\nThis is the last l remember of tie\nnight. When | came to consciousness\nagain. the sun stood where the neon\nhad been and was binding me witt\nhis intense light. Rising, | swept the\nhorizon with my eye. Not a vessel was\nIn sight. The sea rolled on smoothly,\nsilently, as If 1t had never been dis\nturbed by the reyerberation OF guns.\n\n\nOne effect remained with me. If\nthose men ON that burning, sinking yes\nsci could conquer, why should l de-\nspair? Going below, found fishing\ntackle and an AK. With the ak l sacri-\nfeed my own finger and with this for\nbait caught fish. l had matches in\nmy pocket and made fire, heated sea\nwater and, condensing the steam, had\nwater again. this time that / could\ndrink. In this fashion l lived till was\npicked up by an American cruiser.\n\n\nI told my story to the cruisers cap\n\n=== 1_1882-05-10_p1_sn83025121_00211108897_1882051001_0084.json\nThe Hawaiian gazette. · 1882-05-10\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Hawaiian Gazette Supplement, May 10, 81.\n---\nannoyance of others. In matter like the liquor\n\n\nbusiness in which there is not merely question\n\n\nabout annoyance, but also about all the best in\n\n\nterests of society, the contrast is very striking\n\n\nbetween the meanness of the man who will\nsehshly cling to the enjoyment of his liquors in\n\n\nspite of the ruin of society,\" and the nobleness of\nthose who will deny themselves this qualification\n| for the good of the community and especially of\n! the weak and poorer classes. The ability to\ncontrol ones self in the use of liquors may be\n\n\nworth much. 1t would be unwise TO insist on\n! increasing the temptation to be incorporate in\n\n\norder to afford the opportunity of learning the\npower of self restraint, when thereby the best\n/ interests of society must be ruined. But the\n\n\ncharacter to control ones self by ef-denials for\n\n\nthe benefit of others is higher attainment. 1t\n| is the highest manhood. 1t is illustrated in all\n\n\nthe heroism of history and in the sublimest ex-\n\n\ncollencies of Christianity. In this particular\nquestion about the proposed prohibitory law, 1t\n\n\nis opposed simply by spirit to persist in the low\n\n\nqualification of the appetites to the ruin of Others\n\n\nwhich arises from the sel6shness that il the\n| of all human crime and misery.\n\n\n! For tine Government to persist IN maintaining\n\n\nthe liquor traffic in order to obtain revenue would\n\n\nbe for II TO go contrary to the principal mentioned\nlying at the basis Of civil society and contrary\nTo the object for which 1t established and to\nsacrifice the welfare of society for itself as we'll\nTO pursue the suicidal policy of pduperiZing\nand demoralizing and running the society on\nwhich 1t depends for revenue. 1t has been well\nsaid, no questions of revenue should stand in the\nway of questions of ref rm.\n\n\nThe lowest form of this selfish opposition is\nthat Of those who simply seek money by the\n! hqCor traffic; and there can hardly be anything\nbaser than to use mercenAry influence with the\nlaw making powers in order to prevent the needed\nreform. The public will understand any such\ninfluences, and will make known to whom to at\ntribute them.\n\n\nThe reason for the proposed measure is that\nthereby intemperance may be diminished in the\nislands. 1t is not anticipated that thereby the\nliquor business will be abolished by other laws !;\nbut only that thereby with the aid of earnest pub\nq lntemDerAuCg\ngreatly diminished. This is reason enough for\nthe law. A.\n\n=== 1_1892-10-10_pna_sn94052989_00175040195_1892101001_0367.json\nThe morning call. [volume] (San Francisco [Calif.]) 1878-1895 · 1892-10-10\ncrops: cached\nheadline: LOOKING FOR A MESSIAH.\nThe Indians at Pine Ridge Getting\n\n\nRather SuIIen.\nTHERE is no GHOST DANCING YET.\nSitting Bulls Prophecy Vas That the Unknown\nWould Come in 1893, and There Is\n\n\nDecided Uneasiness.\n\nTHERE is no GHOST BANGING YET\n\nSpecial to THh MORNING CALI.\n---\nOMAHA. Neb.. Oct. 9.-Dr. V. T. MeGilli.\ncuddy of Rapid City, S. Dak., well known\nas one of the best-posted men in the coun-\ntry on Indian matters, is in Omaha, having\njust come from the Pine Ridge Agency.\nTalking to-day to Bee reporter, he said\n''I don't wish to pose as an alarmist, but\nthe situation at Pine Ridge Is not at all\nsatisfactory just at present and there Is no\nimmediate prospect Of an Improvement.\nThe Indians are sullen and In many ways\nshow resentful feeling. and unless some\nthing is done to counteract this there is\ndanger of trouble next spring.\n\n\n\"The Messiah spirit is not dead, and the\nwhites must not depend on the statement\nthat the Indians are no longer considering\nthe promise made by Sitting Ball and the\nother medicine men who led the trouble Of\ntwo years ago. While there is no open\ndancing, there is quiet discussion on the\nsubject, and the fact that the promise was\nthat the Messiah would come after two\nsprings, which would bring the time in the\nspring of 1898. is being carefully kept in\nmind by the Indians.\n\n\n\"The Government recently paid the In.\ndians at Pine Ridge $85, 600. and this is used\nas All argument that the Great Father fears\nhis red sons, else why should he seek TO\nbuy them off. Then the return from Fort\nSheridan of Short Bull and Kicking Bear\nadded another disturbing element to the\nalready deeply agitated state OF affairs at\nPine Ridge.\n\n\n'The most significant thing is the fact that\nthere has been communications between the\nvarious tribes of a secret and apparently\nimportant nature during the summer. There\nis a line of communication we'll established\nand constantly used between the Indian\nterritory and British America, and the In\ndians all along the line understood each\nother perfectly\"\n\n=== 1_1920-01-29_p1_sn97071038_00295868442_1920012901_0494.json\nThe gazette-times. [volume] (Heppner, Or.) 1912-1925 · 1920-01-29\ncrops: cached\nheadline: PHANTOM GAVE GOOD ADVICE\n\nApparition That Appeared to French\nShepherd Was Possibly Some Re-\n\n\nIation to the Leprechaun.\n---\nEvery IriShman can tell you about\nthe Lcprechaun, the little man who\ndresses in red with a peaked cap, and\nlives in the bitches. Only n person\ncan see him at a time, and If he does\ncatch sight of him, must never take\nhis eyes off for a single moment.\nWatching him carefully, he must run\nand catch him, and If he succeeds in\ndoing so the Leprechaun will ransom\nhimself by leading his captor to a\ncreek of buried gold. At least, so runs\nthe legend.\n\n\nSome years ago a French shepherd\nof Ayeyron lost two sheep. The fol\nlowing evening a figure in a black robe\nand with tonsured head appeared to\nhim, and told him to look for the lost\nmembers of his flock in a cave near\nAltes, adding that he would there find\nmore than sheep\"\n\n\nThe next day the shepherd found\nthe cave mouth exactly as described.\nInside were his two sheep. Beyond,\nhe came upon a chapel carved In the\nrock and containing challces, censers,\nand candIe-sticks all made of solid\nsilver, and of very considerable value.\nA letter found In a mlssal showed that\nthe place had been used as a place Of\nworship during the Reign Of Terror in\n1893. The shepherd took the orn\nments to his parish priest and was\nwell rewarded for his find.\n\n=== 10_1894-02-21_pna_sn87056600_00202191514_1894022101_0115.json\nThe Indiana State sentinel. [volume] (Indianapolis) 1868-1895 · 1894-02-21\ncrops: cached\nheadline: PAINTERS UNCANNY GHOST\n\nReturns 90 Haunt His Fellow ConvIcts\n\n\nprisoners Scared.\n---\nCHICAGO. Feb. l6.-The prisoners in\nthe county jail are alarmed. or pretend\nTO be. over the stories of visitations by\nthe spirit Of George II. Painter. who\nwas hanged there on Jan. &c The spirit\nOF the murderer is alleged TO make un-\ncanny calls al his old quarters. a number\nof the prisoners claiming 10 have seen\nthe ghost. Prendergast, the murderer\nof Mayor HARRISON is said 20 be especial\n\" alarmed and i'm great terror Of per-\nsonal visitation from his late neighbor\nin murderers' row.\n\n=== 34_1885-07-13_pna_sn90059522_00175035278_1885071301_0140.json\nSt. Paul daily globe. [volume] (Saint Paul, Minn.) 1884-1896 · 1885-07-13\ncrops: cached\nheadline: New Trotters and Pncers.\n---\nWe publish In this department this morn\ning list of new trotters and PAGERS, and\nalso those that have lowered their records.\nThis record was prepared by and published\nin the last number of the Spirit of the\nTimes, and includes all such records made\n\n\nup to July 4. There are fifty-nine new trot-\ntiers and thirteen new PAGERS, making total\nof seventy-two. The time is rapidly APP\npreaching when $50 will be where 2:4O\nwas. Almost two thousand trOtters have\nalready beaten $3.50. and it is predicted that\npersons are living who will see two minutes\nestablished as the fastest mile at the trot-\nting gait in harness. The Spirit says: 1.-THE\nnew trotting sireS, as far as known 10 us,\nare sixteen in number, viz: Nil Desperan-\ndun. Auditor, HermeS, AntenOr, Leland,\n\n\nRustic, Daniel Boone, Victor Mohawk,\n\n\nHambletOnian Downing, Jenkins Almont,\n\n\nConfederate Chief. Ocean Chief, Atlantic,\n\n\nColonel Cross, VOsburg and Envoy. Ten\nare Hambletonlans, Confederate Chief. At\nlantic and VOsburg representing the Mann\n\n\nbrine Chief branch, Oceana Chief is claimed\nby the Vermont Black Hawk faction, and\nEnvoy is a member of the Clay tribe. The\nleading sires still add TO their quota, Blue\nBull, Happy Medium. George Wilkes, Al\nmont, Daniel Lambert and Messenger\nDuToc each having new representatives.\n\n=== 7_1884-09-26_pna_sn83016589_00393340678_1884092601_0270.json\nThe Mankato free press. [volume] (Mankato, Blue Earth County, Minn.) 1879-1924 · 1884-09-26\ncrops: cached\nheadline: The Wonderful Planchette.\n---\nIn the year 1868 u gentleman named\nKirby, who is now living in Kansas\nCity, invented q contrivance which he\ncalled 'planchette. 1t was an ordin-\nairy board of walnut, shaped like an\neasel and supported by four small\nrollers. 1t was in size about as large\nas q woman's Gainsborough hat.\n\n\nThe planchette would be placed on q\ntable over sheet of Virginia white\nfoolscap. A man would be told to\nshut both of his eyes and think of\nsomething he held dear. His fingers\nwould rest on the easel and he was to\nforget all about them and about it. In\nIl few minutes he would he in q sort of\nreverie. The thoughts in his mind\nwould gradually become absorbed on\none point. In another minute the\nplancnette would begin to move me-\nChemically on its rollers. And then,\nwithout any apparent volition on his\npart, the pencil which was held by the\neasel would slowly trace over the\nfoolscap the name of a sweetheart, or\nthe date OF some engagement or what\never thought was uppcrmost in his\nmind.\n\n\nThis revelation of a latent power\nwas thought at first to be the most\nsurprising. But after some 10000\nplanchettes had been sold for 81 3\npiece it ceased to be so much of a won\nder, and Mr.. Kirby retired in 1870 to\nthe west. At the same time the dis\ncussion to which the planchette gave\nrise-for craze it was-.established two\nor three things. The first was that\nsome temperamcnts possessed the\nelectric force to much greater ex-\ntent than others! The second was\nthat, as u general rule. women were\nmore largely endowed with it than\nmen. The secret of the piancnette was\nthat the electricity which the mind\n:ouid generate could be exercised\nthrough the hands. The secret of\nMiss Hursv's \"inexplicable power\" is\nthat magnetism in Il strong, robust but\nnot intellectual person may adhere in\nThe hands themselves and thus be used\nmuscularly.\n\n=== 16_1890-09-04_pna_sn83040198_00212473479_1890090401_0062.json\nBarton County democrat. [volume] (Great Bend, Kan.) 1885-1915 · 1890-09-04\ncrops: cached\nheadline: HRIEA,\nThe Half Breed.\n\n\"A Romance of Colonial Days.\n\n~ ROBERT A. CUNNING.\n---\nra;;e By ROBERT A. CUKMI9G.\n\n\nCHAPTER IL-CoNrINUED.\n.sAre the French stirring oagsin?\" he\nasked after a pause. iI7fa\n1.-The news from the North rs impor-\ntant. The French are led by General\nMontca1m, man OF tireless energy.\nHe is preparing dotilla at Fort From\ntenac and the favoring breeze which\nwatts him across Lake Ontario, ~4H\nplace Oswego in great. peril\"\n\n\n.sMercer and his New Enslspders hold\nthat FORT\" said the youth ia . tone of\nconfidence. ..v..7-\n\n\n''And if they can not?'\n\n\nAS the full import of this suggestion\nflashed across his men vision. Selwyn\narose with the air of one who shakes off\na drowsy feeling of security.\n\n\ns'What then?'' he asked.\n\n\n-LsNhy, then in about a week your\npeaceful Arcadia will be full Of Cana\ndian savages and Frenchmen.\"\n\n\n'The British troops?\"r\n\n\n'.Are comfortably quartered in Bos-\nton, New York or Philadelphia. Z\n\n\nsReinforcementsf for Mercer?\"\n\n\ntI have no authority to raise troops\"\nreplied the knight. 49,\n\n\n--ssBut could get soluntsersEs hun\ndred rifemen-my friends end neigh\nbors, y said the youth, impetuously.\n\n\n\"How soon?s > t @G7aseh -.\n\n\nReady to march in tive weeks\"\n\n\nIA drop in the bucket, nephew; and\nyet as the nucleus OF a larger force, it\nmight be effecSlve. Try Whist thou\ncanst do, and in the meantime will\nto Albany with speed and try my infiu-\nrence with the yuthor4Hao - lend thee\naid, falling which tkou wilt OF course\nabandon the atsesnpx\" aft\n\n\n\"When my hand 10 on tbs elsw will\naot turn bae11, Sir william\"\n\n\ns.Boyl Boy!e, exclaimed the baronet,\nimpatiently, ambition finds it's way by\ndevious means, the etie< Of which is\nprudent calculation. AS have ALREADY\ntold thee, my visit here tonight had the\nobject of arousing thee from these book\nish dreams; yet I counsel not rashness.\nWe will help zach other, Charles. Thy\nprompt action in raising troops will re-\nfound to the family credit whether I\nsucceed in Albany or not. But to march\nto- Oswego with o hundred men were\nsheer folly\"\n\n\n''Mars' Charles\" interrupted negro\nservant, putting his gray, woolly head\ninto the room, @dem ar dogs is mighty\nUneasy. Dey is rattlin' dar chains an\nbarlxin' as if dey smelt somefin 'spicious. y\n\n\n''They scent a wild animal larking\naround the barnyard, perhaps\" replied\n,Selwyn, taking rifle from the wall.\n\n\n''Injuns, mebbe,\" suggested Peter.\n. Selwyn shook his head. =\n\n\neThe Aohawl1s are our friends said\nne, \"and would come boldly if at all. >\nSO saying, he unbolted the hall door\nand was abou9 90 step firth upon the\nmoonlit lawn, but almost stumbled over\nan object Seated on the threshold, which\narose and stood before him silent and\nmotionless. Selwyn AWas too we'll used\nto Indian habits so be surprised at this\napparition, Che sex and features Of\nwhich were concealed by a blanket\nthrown over the dead and almost touch;\ning the ground\n\n\n'sWhom do you seek?'' asked the\nyoung man, in the Mohawk dialect.\n\n\nSlowly. the eblApket dropped from\nhead and shoulders, revealing hand\nsome boyish face8 whose features were\nrather French than aboriginal, and q\npair of large sparkling black eyes were\nraised to HE face with an expression\niidll oiLys hettG SOiiL0Chos ohA ohSA\nsmall brown hand drsw from the bosom\na letter superscribed in bold Ehglisl\nhand, Charles Selwyn.\"\n\n\nTaking the messenger by the arm\n:Selwin led him into the hell and, point\ning to chair, opened the letter. Tn\nlad, however, seated himself on bear\nskin mat, and, resting his head upol\nhis hand, remained mOtionless as before\n+Exhausted, y thought Selwyn, glanc\ning at him. Peter, give him some food.\nWhat, have you there?\" asked ST\nWilliam But his kinsman was to-\nmuch occupied to heed the question.\n\n\n''Old comgade and brother-in-arms,\nran the missive, ''if this letter eve\nreaches you, 1 confide the bearer t\nyour protection and sympathy. He ha\nthe misfortune to fall into the hands O\na band of Hurons. who, being in a play\nfut mood, cut out his tongue. and woul\ndoubtless have deprived him of his hal\nalso if they had not been surprised by\nparty of, rangers, who quickly mad\nthem look on this practical joke in\nvery serious light. The boy is faithful\nand Intelligent, and will serve thee a\nAriel served his master Prospero--\nsaved him as the battle of Crown Poin,\n-. !. ,. j. Ill ElI !. i :.\n","numLines":1605,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1605}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"792e96a2-606b-4b84-b2f6-f0d5988d5163","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"other"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2c-reader","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
