{"parentUuid":"42a462c7-61cb-4a48-a648-a3d4a2585875","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"775e54ef-6487-4f79-8598-643421b87718","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HamrSRYj8mjewgxbZ7w9XW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t=== 11_1859-08-20_p4_sn83030179_00220720445_1859082001_0004.json\n2\tThe weekly Anglo-African. · 1859-08-20\n3\tcrops: cached\n4\theadline: \n5\t---\n6\t\n7\twas full of Austrian bank-notes.\n8\t\n9\t\n10\tHaving convinced himself THAT his money\n11\twas right, he placed both under his pillow.\n12\textinguished the light, and soon fell asleep\n13\tthanking God and all the saints for the sue\n14\tcess of his journey. He had slept but an\n15\thour or two when he was suddenly awak\n16\tened by the opening of the window, and\n17\tImmediately felt the night air blow upon\n18\thim.\n19\t\n20\t\n21\tStartled at this unforeseen circumstance\n22\tthe traveller raised himself up in bed, and\n23\tperceived the head and shoulders of man\n24\twho was struggling to get into the room:\n25\tat the same time he heard the voices of sew.\n26\teral persons who were standing under the\n27\twindow\n28\t\n29\t\n30\tA dreadful terror seized our traveller\n31\twho gave himself up for lost; and, scarcely\n32\tknowing what he did, crept under the bed\n33\tas quickly as possible. A moment after\n34\twards man sprung heavily into the room,\n35\tand staggered up to the bed, supporting\n36\thimself against the wall.\n37\t\n38\t\n39\tConfounded as the householder was, he\n40\tnevertheless perceived that the intruder\n41\twas inebriated; this circumstance, howev-\n42\ter, gave him little hope, for he had probably\n43\tgot intoxicated in order to summon up cour-\n44\tage for the contemplated crime; besides\n45\tthis the traveller had heard the voices of\n46\tpersons outside, so that the murderer, in\n47\tcase of resistance, could count upon the\n48\tassistance of his comrades.\n49\t\n50\t\n51\tBut how great was his astonishment when\n52\the saw the unknown person throw his coat\n53\tupon the floor, and stretch himself upon\n54\tthe bed which he had just quittedl A few\n55\tmoments afterwards he heard the intruder\n56\tsnore, and his terror began gradually to\n57\tgive way to reflection, although the whole\n58\taffair was quite incomprehensible to him.\n59\t\n60\t\n61\tHe was just preparing to quit his hiding\n62\tplace, in order to awake the inmates of the\n63\thouse, and ask another bed in place of that\n64\tfrom which he had been SO unceremoni-\n65\tously expelled, when a new incident oc-\n66\tCured\n67\t\n68\t\n69\tHe heard the outer door carefully open\n70\ted, and, on listening, the sound of cautious\n71\tfootsteps reached his ear. In few mo,\n72\tments, the door of his room opened, and\n73\ttwo figures, those of the landlord and bis\n74\tson, stood on the threshold.\n75\t\n76\t\n77\t\"Keep the lamp back\" muttered the\n78\tfather in a suppressed voice.\n79\t\n80\t\n81\t\"What have we to fear 7\" said the young\n82\tman: \"we are two against one; besides,\n83\the has only small knife with him, and is\n84\tsleeping soundly; hear how he snores.\"\n85\t\n86\t\n87\t\"Do what l tell you,\" said the father,\n88\tangrily; do you wish to awake him\n89\tWould you have his cries alarm the neigh\n90\tborhoodr'\n91\t\n92\t\n93\tThe householder was Mortified with the\n94\tspectacle. He remained motionless under\n95\tthe bed, scarcely daring to breathe. The\n96\tson shut the door after him, and the two\n97\twretches approached the bed on tiptoe.\n98\t\n99\t\n100\tAn instant afterwards the bed was shook\n101\tby convulsive motion, and stihed cry\n102\tOf pain confirmed the foreboding that the\n103\tunhappy man in the bed had had his throat\n104\tcut After short pause, of awful silence,\n105\tthe landlord said:\n106\t\n107\t\n108\tat is over now; look for the money\"\n109\t\n110\t\n111\t~ have found it under the pillow\" said\n112\tthe son; it is in leathern belt and a pock-\n113\tct book\"\n114\t\n115\t\n116\tThe murderers disappeared.\n117\t\n118\t\n119\tEverything being now quiet, the travel\n120\ter crept from under the bed, jumped out of\n121\tthe window, and fastened to the adjoining\n122\ttown to inform the authorities of what had\n123\thappened.\n124\t\n125\t\n126\tThe mayor Immediately assembled the\n127\tmilitary, and in less than three-quarters of\n128\tan hour the inn was surrounded by soldiers\n129\twho had been summoned to arrest the mur-\n130\tderers. The whole house seemed buried\n131\tin profound silence, but on approaching\n132\tthe stables they heard noise. The door\n133\twas Immediately broken in, and the land\n134\tlord and his son were seen busily digging\n135\tpit AS soon as the murderers saw the\n136\thouseholder, they uttered a cry of horror,\n137\tcovered their faces with their hands, and\n138\tfell to the ground.\n139\t\n140\t\n141\tThis was neither from repentance nor\n142\tthe fear of punishment, but they thought\n143\tthey saw before them the ghost of the\n144\tmurdered man, notwithstanding they heard\n145\thim speak There was some trouble in\n146\tconvincing them to the contrary. They\n147\twere then bound, and led to the clubhouse\n148\twhere the horrible deed had been commit.\n149\tted, anxious to see how the enigma would\n150\tbe solved.\n151\t\n152\t\n153\tThe prisoners appeared tolerably col\n154\tlected, at least calm and sullen; but when,\n155\tON entering the room, they perceived the\n156\tbody which lay on the bed, the son fell\n157\tsenseless to the earth, and the father threw\n158\thimself upon it, with loud lamentations,\n159\tclasped the bloody corpse, and exclaimed,\n160\t\n161\t\n162\t\"My son! oh my sonl |, thy father, am\n163\tthy murderer\n164\t\n165\t\n166\tThe murdered man was, in fact, the\n167\t1ro,1r,nrort co,, a. l.nk DIR, nrr\n168\t\n169\t=== 9_1919-10-24_p6_sn92050044_00393342912_1919102401_0476.json\n170\tThe Pulaskian. · 1919-10-24\n171\tcrops: cached\n172\theadline: \n173\t---\n174\tseem powerful 'tached to just plain goo