four champion yarn spinners there\n1420\twill be no dearth of material. In fact, there\n1421\tis enough potential talent at this moment\n1422\tclamoring for attention TO equip entire\n1423\tfleet. not forgetting subatern yarn spinners\n1424\tfor the lifeboats and incidental tenders.\n1425\t\n1426\t\n1427\t\"Do / know any sea yarns?\" commented\n1428\tone ardent seeker for the new job. Are\n1429\tthere any don't know? Forty eight years,\n1430\tservice. fore and aft seen on the high\n1431\tseas and what | don't know aint. Not\n1432\tmeanin' TO boast. you understand, hut just\n1433\tStain plain facts, | kin hold my head up in\n1434\tany old aggregation of marines you want\n1435\tto get together.\n1436\t\n1437\t\n1438\tII run away to sea when / just a\n1439\tkid. took my knocks till they made man OF\n1440\tme- and went up the ladder till I come TO\n1441\tport as first mate of the finest sain' vessel\n1442\tever shipped out OF san Francisco Bay.\n1443\tThat's my record. And perhaps / haven't\n1444\tpicked up yarn or two ia that time. DIE\n1445\tever hear the one about the girl and the\n1446\tequator? or the one about the iceberg and\n1447\tthe ghost ship Tyrant H.? Say, / know a\n1448\tlot more, too.\n1449\t\n1450\t\n1451\tPut me I'm the emokin' room and III\n1452\tguarantee TO break up all the poker and\n1453\tpinochle games In sight. Set me down I'm\n1454\tthe main saloon and there won't be no ladies\n1455\tknittln' or writin' homeslck letters back TO\n1456\tthe folks on shore. Give me the kids for an\n1457\t\n1458\t=== 4_1920-06-30_pna_sn88056159_00295868107_1920063001_0139.json\n1459\tThe Challis messenger. (Challis, Idaho) 1912-current · 1920-06-30\n1460\tcrops: cached\n1461\theadline: RIVAL MANAGERS\n1462\t\n1463\t\n1464\tHUSTLE FOR VOTES\n1465\t\n1466\tPRE-coNvENTloN SITUATION At\n1467\tSAN FRANCISCO ONE OF\n1468\t\n1469\t\n1470\tSKIES AND SEvENS.\n1471\t\n1472\tBoosters For Half z Dozen Asplrants\n1473\tFor Coveted Nomination Active\n1474\tAmong Uninstrueted 0eieoates-\n1475\t\n1476\t\n1477\tMeAdoo Boom viii Not Down.\n1478\t---\n1479\tSan FranciscO.-The pre-cOnventi0n\n1480\tsituation seems to be one Of skies and\n1481\tsevens. More and more elements Of\n1482\tuncertainty appear In the lineup Of the\n1483\tDemocratic national convention as the\n1484\tleaders of first magnitude arrive in\n1485\tincreasing numbers and put their\n1486\theads together over questions Of poll\n1487\tcies and men.\n1488\t\n1489\t\n1490\tFor time the platform held its\n1491\tplace as the big topic Of cOgitatiOn and\n1492\tnegotiation, with the problem Of bone\n1493\tdry prohibition the chief material for\n1494\tcontroversy.\n1495\t\n1496\t\n1497\tTalk Of candidates, in a mOribund\n1498\tstage for week, was revived appre-\n1499\tGladly as the convention crowds\n1500\tgathered. Boosters for Palmer, Cox\n1501\tand half dozen other aspirants be-\n1502\tcame increasingly active among I'M\n1503\tinstructed delegates, and a whole bevy\n1504\tOF favOrite sons and dark horses\n1505\tbl0ssomed out with headquarters of\n1506\ttheir own.\n1507\t\n1508\t\n1509\tMeantime the phantom McAdOo\n1510\tboom stalked serenely through hotel\n1511\tcorridors and conference rooms, leav-\n1512\ting behind trail of uncertainty and\n1513\tspeculation. Though publicly execut-\n1514\ted ut the hands of Mr.. McAdOO him\n1515\tself, the movement will not down.\n1516\tIndeed, its perambulations are sug-\n1517\tgestipe Of the pink of vigOr, and Ut\n1518\tturned up unlimited as the ghost at\n1519\tthe council table of more than one\n1520\tcandidates manager.\n1521\t\n1522\t\n1523\tOn several questions Of convention\n1524\torganization, too. the party chiefs\n1525\thave been in a quandary. There were\n1526\tsome rumblings of fight against\n1527\tSenator Glass, the administration\n1528\tchoice for chairman Of the platform\n1529\tcommittee. and the problem of choos-\n1530\ting a permanent convention chairman\n1531\tgot into mess which threatened to\n1532\tupset seriously the plans of some adj\n1533\tministration supporters.\n1534\t\n1535\t\n1536\tSenator Walsh of Montana, who\n1537\tvoted in the senate last March for\n1538\tratification Of the peace treaty with\n1539\tthe Republican reservations, promises\n1540\tto become the storm center of the or\n1541\tganization fight. In some quarters he\n1542\twas urged as a candidate against\n1543\tSenator Glass for head of the platform\n1544\tthough others thought he\n1545\tshould have the permanent chairman\n1546\tship instead. He had the backing of\n1547\tWilliam Jennings Bryan and was\n1548\tsaid to be favored also by other\n1549\tpowerful forces more or less out Of\n1550\taccord with some administration\n1551\tpolicies.\n1552\t\n1553\t\n1554\tSenator Glass, who arrived Thurs\n1555\tday from Washington as the COM\n1556\tmonty reputed spokesman of the\n1557\tWhite House, denied that the presi-\n1558\tdent would seek to control the de-\n1559\talterations here. The senator came to\n1560\tmake fight favorable to the admins\n1561\ttration, but If he brought any word of\n1562\tWhite House preference between can\n1563\tit did not show up on the sur\n1564\tface in the conferences.\n1565\t"}]},"uuid":"9321d2fc-fa22-4d0d-aacd-c50d234b4f09","timestamp":"2026-10-31T08:04:19.156Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/c1/g14.txt","content":"=== 11_1859-08-20_p4_sn83030179_00220720445_1859082001_0004.json\nThe weekly Anglo-African. · 1859-08-20\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\n\nwas full of Austrian bank-notes.\n\n\nHaving convinced himself THAT his money\nwas right, he placed both under his pillow.\nextinguished the light, and soon fell asleep\nthanking God and all the saints for the sue\ncess of his journey. He had slept but an\nhour or two when he was suddenly awak\nened by the opening of the window, and\nImmediately felt the night air blow upon\nhim.\n\n\nStartled at this unforeseen circumstance\nthe traveller raised himself up in bed, and\nperceived the head and shoulders of man\nwho was struggling to get into the room:\nat the same time he heard the voices of sew.\neral persons who were standing under the\nwindow\n\n\nA dreadful terror seized our traveller\nwho gave himself up for lost; and, scarcely\nknowing what he did, crept under the bed\nas quickly as possible. A moment after\nwards man sprung heavily into the room,\nand staggered up to the bed, supporting\nhimself against the wall.\n\n\nConfounded as the householder was, he\nnevertheless perceived that the intruder\nwas inebriated; this circumstance, howev-\ner, gave him little hope, for he had probably\ngot intoxicated in order to summon up cour-\nage for the contemplated crime; besides\nthis the traveller had heard the voices of\npersons outside, so that the murderer, in\ncase of resistance, could count upon the\nassistance of his comrades.\n\n\nBut how great was his astonishment when\nhe saw the unknown person throw his coat\nupon the floor, and stretch himself upon\nthe bed which he had just quittedl A few\nmoments afterwards he heard the intruder\nsnore, and his terror began gradually to\ngive way to reflection, although the whole\naffair was quite incomprehensible to him.\n\n\nHe was just preparing to quit his hiding\nplace, in order to awake the inmates of the\nhouse, and ask another bed in place of that\nfrom which he had been SO unceremoni-\nously expelled, when a new incident oc-\nCured\n\n\nHe heard the outer door carefully open\ned, and, on listening, the sound of cautious\nfootsteps reached his ear. In few mo,\nments, the door of his room opened, and\ntwo figures, those of the landlord and bis\nson, stood on the threshold.\n\n\n\"Keep the lamp back\" muttered the\nfather in a suppressed voice.\n\n\n\"What have we to fear 7\" said the young\nman: \"we are two against one; besides,\nhe has only small knife with him, and is\nsleeping soundly; hear how he snores.\"\n\n\n\"Do what l tell you,\" said the father,\nangrily; do you wish to awake him\nWould you have his cries alarm the neigh\nborhoodr'\n\n\nThe householder was Mortified with the\nspectacle. He remained motionless under\nthe bed, scarcely daring to breathe. The\nson shut the door after him, and the two\nwretches approached the bed on tiptoe.\n\n\nAn instant afterwards the bed was shook\nby convulsive motion, and stihed cry\nOf pain confirmed the foreboding that the\nunhappy man in the bed had had his throat\ncut After short pause, of awful silence,\nthe landlord said:\n\n\nat is over now; look for the money\"\n\n\n~ have found it under the pillow\" said\nthe son; it is in leathern belt and a pock-\nct book\"\n\n\nThe murderers disappeared.\n\n\nEverything being now quiet, the travel\ner crept from under the bed, jumped out of\nthe window, and fastened to the adjoining\ntown to inform the authorities of what had\nhappened.\n\n\nThe mayor Immediately assembled the\nmilitary, and in less than three-quarters of\nan hour the inn was surrounded by soldiers\nwho had been summoned to arrest the mur-\nderers. The whole house seemed buried\nin profound silence, but on approaching\nthe stables they heard noise. The door\nwas Immediately broken in, and the land\nlord and his son were seen busily digging\npit AS soon as the murderers saw the\nhouseholder, they uttered a cry of horror,\ncovered their faces with their hands, and\nfell to the ground.\n\n\nThis was neither from repentance nor\nthe fear of punishment, but they thought\nthey saw before them the ghost of the\nmurdered man, notwithstanding they heard\nhim speak There was some trouble in\nconvincing them to the contrary. They\nwere then bound, and led to the clubhouse\nwhere the horrible deed had been commit.\nted, anxious to see how the enigma would\nbe solved.\n\n\nThe prisoners appeared tolerably col\nlected, at least calm and sullen; but when,\nON entering the room, they perceived the\nbody which lay on the bed, the son fell\nsenseless to the earth, and the father threw\nhimself upon it, with loud lamentations,\nclasped the bloody corpse, and exclaimed,\n\n\n\"My son! oh my sonl |, thy father, am\nthy murderer\n\n\nThe murdered man was, in fact, the\n1ro,1r,nrort co,, a. l.nk DIR, nrr\n\n=== 9_1919-10-24_p6_sn92050044_00393342912_1919102401_0476.json\nThe Pulaskian. · 1919-10-24\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nseem powerful 'tached to just plain good old Jamaica rum\"\n\n \"They shall have it\" I said.\n\n I had noticed that there was sa- loon few yards away. so before many more minutes had passed had been there and come back again, and the decanter stood ruddily filled. ready for the resumption of our seance. But be- fore we began of course accepted the seer's invitation to join him and tue spirits in a friendly libation.\n\n Then-I having closed my eyes-we\n\n=== 14_1942-05-23_p3_sn83045462_00280603569_1942052301_0467.json\nEvening star. · 1942-05-23\ncrops: cached\nheadline: vichy Ghost Fleet\nExpected 10 Remain\nIn Egyptian Harbor\n\nCommander Says Simply\nHe Cove British His\nWord Not to Leuve\n---\nAssociated Press FOREIGN Correspondent.\n\n\nALEXANDRIA. Egypt. May 23.-\nThe Vichy French ghost fleet\"\nlying in Alexandria harbor prob\nably will remain just that regard\nless of whether Pierre Laval eventu-\nally decides to turn French warships\nover to the Axis.\n\n\nOne of the loneliest men of the\nworlds navies is Vice Admiral Rene\nEmile Godfrey, commander of the\nnine French men:of-war immobil-\nlied since July. 1910, in faithful\ncompliance with the agreement not\nto attempt to leave port.\n\n\nGodfrey keeps close watch on de-\nvelopments in Vichy and any Ger\nman pressure to obtain use OF the\nFrench war fleet. But he just\nshrugs his shoulders and says:\n\n\nThere is nothing / could do. I\ngave my word to Admiral Cunning\nham\"\n\n\nAdmiral Sir Andrew Browne\nCunningham formerly was COM\nmander in chief of the British\nMediterranean feet.\n\n\nAfter the battle at Oran when\nBritish warships fired on French\nships Godfrey agreed with Admiral\nCunningham to demobilize the\nFrench warships at Alexandria and\npromised never to try to move the\nfeet out of the harbor. The Brit\nish promised not to take over the\nmen-of-war forcibly.\n\n\nSince July, 1910. one French bat\ntleship. four cruisers, three destroy\ners and one submarine have been\nlying in the harbor Thcir crews\ntotal 1900 men. Nearly 1000 OFF,\nGERS and sailors were repatriated to\nFrance.\n\n=== 15_1911-05-15_pna_sn91066782_00415629072_1911051501_0300.json\nThe Barre daily times. (Barre, Vt.) 1897-1959 · 1911-05-15\ncrops: cached\nheadline: A Secret Service\n\n\nSpecter\n\n\nBY WILLIS BEACH\nCopyright by American Press Asso-\n\n\ncitation. 1911.\n---\nAt the time the foreign legations\nwere shut up In Picking and the united\nSrmies of the powers went there to\nfree them, with the American army\nTwas soldier by the name of Higgins.\nBE had become stagestruck when quite\nyoung and had Joined the supers in a\ntheater. He had never risen as an ac-\ntor but had been put in charge of the\nproperties. From the theater he had\ngone forth as an assistant to presu-\ndigitator and become sufficiently ex-\npert to take his principals place in\ncase of necessity The prestidigitator\ndied. and Higgins was stranded Then\nhe enlisted In the army.\n\n\nWhen the allied forces were working\ntheir way up to the walls of Picking an\nAmerican general wished for secret\nservice man to go forward and recon-\nhotter. 1t was desirable TO know what\ndistractions were in the way, where\nwere the entrances, etc. No one could\nbe found to undertake the work till 1t\nwas mentioned to Higgins, who said\nthat II he could get certain articles\nfrom the ordnance department and GER\ntain materials from other sources he\nwould undertake to bring the informa-\nton. He succeeded In gaining what\nhe wanted and. dressing himself In a\nfantastic costume, started forth on foot\nfor the city.\n\n\nwalking along road with no other\nweapon than light cane, he met a\nparty of Chinese Boxers Since Hig-\ngins was coming from the direction OF\ntheir enemies the Cinnamon were dis\nposed to chop him up at once. but\nthey couldn't understand his queer\ncostume There is not more super\nHtitious race in the world than the\nChinese unless 1t Is certain low grade\nMohammedans. As the Boxers APP\nproached Higgins, seeing him come\nright along without fear, their courage\nbegan to ooze out at their finger end\nThey stopped and waited for him to\ncome up. Higgins tossed a little\nsphere as big as a marble among them.\nwhich exploded. and. though 1t did no\nharm, it scattered them. They turn\ned and saw the stranger marching on\nsilently like a specter. He raised his\nhat, and every hair on his head stood\nstraight up. That took what courage\nthere was in them, and he saw them\nno more.\n\n\nThe next lot Of Boxers he met were\nholder. They blocked the road and\ncried out something in Chinese which\nHiggins didn't understand. Higgins\nstopped One Of the Cinnamon advanc-\ned, while the others skunk back. When\nhe got within a few yards of the\nstrange apparition 1t drew an imagi-\nnary circle of fire about it with the\nend OF its cane.\n\n\nThe advancing man retreated on his\nsupports.\n\n\nOne OF the Boxers now brought gun\nto his shoulder and fired at Higgins.\n1t was a close call for the soldier. for\nthe bullet whistled within an inch Of\nhis ear. Had not the Boxer who fred\nbeen affected by superstition he would\nprobably have killed his man Higgins\ndidn't lose his presence OF mind. but\nturned the incident to his advantage.\nHe stood with his lips parted and\ndrawn so that bis enemies could see |\nthe ball they had fred held between |\nhis teeth. Then. taking 1t in his hand. /\nhe threw II among them. Had be |\nthrown q hand grenade he couldn't !\nhave scattered them more efrectualiy. !\n\n\nHiggins was now getting near the\nwalls Of the city and began TO use his |\neyes. He also made notes and draw\nings. He had a pocket glass with him /\nand found IL valuable. While he was\ngazing a party Of Chinese horsemen\nswent down on him. Though puzzled\n\n=== 1_1920-05-13_pna_sn78000873_00279525036_1920051301_0165.json\nThe Republican journal. [volume] (Belfast, Me.) 1829-current · 1920-05-13\ncrops: cached\nheadline: CASTORIA\n\n\nFor Infants and Children\n\n\nIn Use For Over 30YearS\nz,rs III s. Of 1sf4zzSzzs<\n\nConvention of Maine Spiritualists\n---\nThe Gath annual convention of the Maine\nState Spiritualist Association will be held\nin Guilford, Maine, on May 18th and 10th.\n\n\nThe night before will be devoted to\nreception and general conference. All day\nSaturday will be taken up with The bust\nness sessions. On Saturday evening, Sun\nday afternoon and Sunday evening there\nwill be public lectures and seances con-\nQuoted by Maud Litch Torrey of Lynn,\nMass, one of New Eng lands most dis\ntinguished lecturers and psychics.\n\n\nSunday morning will be given up to\ngeneral conference in which prominent\nSpiritualists from various sections of the\nState will take part, discussing those\nthemes and problems connected with the\nadvancement of the science, philosophy\nand religion of Spiritualism in Maine\nThis convention bids fair to be one of\nthe most important in recent years as\nthe activities of the State Association\nduring the coming year will necessarily\nincrease in order to keep pace with the\nrapidly growing interest in psychic mat\ntiers on the part of the general public.\nThe State Association has received sever\nal requests of substantial amounts during\nthe past year, and the Endowment Fund\nis rapidly assuming proportions where it\nwill be possible to carry out some of the\nlong cherished plans which have been\ncrippled for many years by the lack of\nfunds.\n\n=== 29_1904-02-29_p4_sn84020358_00271744432_1904022901_0448.json\nThe daily morning journal and courier. · 1904-02-29\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nDr. Marcus E. Peterson of Brooklyn had a hurry call the other night, and took short cut across an old cemetery to save time. He fell head foremost into an empty grave, and might have died from exposure but for his dog, which jumped into the grave and licked his masters face and shook him till he revived him. The people of the neigh borhood think the cemetery is haunted, and number of people returning from a social affair and who, hearing the dog howl, looked over the fence, thought they had certainly surprised a ghost when they saw a form rise out of the grave and walk across the lot followed by the dog.\n\n=== 38_1954-10-02_p1_sn83045462_00280607319_1954100201_0078.json\nEvening star. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1854-1972 · 1954-10-02\ncrops: cached\nheadline: London Arms Accord Reported;\nGruenther Sees Reds Defeat\nEventually if War Should Come\n\nControl Formula\nFound in TuIks,\nSpook Says\n\n\nBULLETIN\n\nBULLETIN\n---\nLONDON (R.-French Pre-\nmier Pierre Mendes-France\nwalked out Of the nine-power\nconference on rearming Ger\nmany today saying he was i'll\nand wanted to go to bed, a\nGerman spokesman said.\n\n=== 6_1919-03-21_p1_sn00065154_0027952326A_1919032101_1288.json\nThe herald-advance. (Milbank, S.D.) 1890-1922 · 1919-03-21\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Franklin Gro6nd Squirrel.\n---\nsolution is spread over l. quarts OF\noats and mixed thoroughly in order to\ncoat each kernel. One quart of this\npoison is sufficient for 40 to 60 bats.\nThe material-scattered one teaspoon\nfull to Il place-shoulds be distributed\nalong the clean, hard surfaces rear the\nsquirrel holes where it will not EN-\ndanger live stock and where there is\nno chance for the rod ants 70 waste\nthe material by tramping over 1t or\nby covering it with refuse from their\nholes, as would occur We're the bait\nto be placed directly in their burrows.\n\n\nControl Columbian Squirrels.\n\n\nColumbian ground squirrels are not\ncontrolled by this method of eradica-\nton, as they hull oats very carefully\nbefore eating them, and hence avoid\nu e poison. Therefore Il special method\nOf preparing bait for these squirrels is\nused SO that al they hull the oats the\npoison flakes off in the rodents mouths\nand kills them.\n\n\nThe Columbian squirrel bait is pre-\npared by combining in dry mixture\n\n\n\n\n\nn ounce of powdered s%trychnine\n~(alkaloid) and n ounce OF baking\nsoda. one t~easpoonful of saccharin and\nthree tablespoonfuls of -four, adding a\nlittle cold water and stirring thorough\n\" to a smooth, creamy paste. This\nmixture is distributed uniformly over\nII quarts Of oats as in the former\ncase.sand the poison bait scattered as\npreviously described. This poison\nshould be used within 10 to IA days\nafter preparation, as otherwise the ma\nterial will dust off the. grain.\n\n\nPoison ground squirrels as early In\nthe spring us possible. as in this way\nthe natural increase Of young sqnir-\nrels IS eliminated. The poisoning catn-\npaign should be continued throughout\nthe year until the section is free Of\nthese pests. The rodents will eat the\npoison baitA at any time. Attention\nshould be given to destroying. the\nsquirrels In all their haunts In pas\nlures, uncultivated fields, fence rows,\nuhd roads. as well as from the culti-\nwaited fields where complete extermius-\nton Of the pests IS sought\n\n=== 4_1889-03-28_pna_sn86086770_00271768849_1889032801_1119.json\nMineral Point tribune. (Mineral Point, Wis.) 1869-1938 · 1889-03-28\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nThe domestic service problem Is one of\nthe most irritating IN the whole system\nOf social economy, In its ramifcatioHs\nIL extende TO every part of the household.\nIts respOnsible for much OF the friction\nIn family Affairs; like Ba.qnO's ghost, II\nwill not down. but comes an unbidden\nguest at every meal il goes with the\nmattress of the household TO market, and\neven to church; it forms theme OF oon-\nversatiou on shopping tours, in the street\noar and in the parlor; it follows men TO\ntheir places of business; IL ia responsible\n\n\nfor indigestion, and for the peevishness\nthat causes persons OF both sexes TO be\nguilty Of acts of impatience-of down\n\n\nright wrong doing.\n\n\nThe most successfully managed Ameri\n\n\ncan house hold is not entirely free from\n\n\nthe dinicultiea attendant upon domestic\n\n\nservice, while thousands OF homes are\n! constantly under it's malevolert influence,\n\n\nand the common complaint is that 1t is\n\n\ndaily becoming more puz2linR and an\n\n\nnoying. Many explanations have been\n\n\noffered az TO the underlying cause of tho\n\n\ntrouble, chief among which which ia the\n\n\nalmost universal difike OF the young of\nboth sexes for mammal labor or for any\noccupation which inferentially carries\n\n\nwith 81 a badge of seryitude. The good\nold daysn Of which we read, when the\nmistress of the house sat in her room\nsurrounded by her maid servants and\n| initiated them into the myBteries OF\npicking and preserving or weaving, OF\nknitting and of dyeinR, have passed\naway, never to return. In the place OF\nthat ideal state we have n system under\nwhich servants are selected without any\n\n\nregard 20 their fitness tor membership\n\n\nin the family.\n\n\nIL Is au open question whether the\nquality, or rather the lack OF quality, in\n| the available material be not largely re-\n\n\nsponsibe for many Of the perplexing diff\n! houlties 80 much complained of. The\n\n\nmuch Wanted independence of the Amer\nlean character is, in one sense, weakness\nwhen 1t leads 20 the rejection of an en-\n! ployment homo, able in itself because of\n| feeling that II implies dependency, and\nTO the selection of some other field of la\nDor In which the fact OF soryitude, II IL be\n\n\nnot so plainly apparent, nevertheless sx-\n| lists in an equal degree.\n\n\nBti /, We can't blame the girls\" Their\naspirations are natural. Put yourselves\n! in their placer, and you will understand\nthe feeling. May it not be said that bet\nter mattresses would make better \"giflsW\nA good housewife is apt 20 make good\nmistress. 10 Ia a lamentable fact that too\n\n\nmany young women who expect to have\n! households TO manage are ignorant OF\n| the art OF household management. Such\n! young women have false idea of \"edu-\ncation For an \"educatiou' they saeri-\n\n\nhoe everything. The mothers bake and\n\n\nwash and scrub and sew, while the\n\n\ndaughters study the rules Of grammar,\n\n\nthe mysteries Of algebra and the princi-\n| plies Of constitutional history, knowing\nnothing OF thoss duties that belong to\n\n\nwoman, nor Of those arts which make\n| home bright and cheerful and cause the\nfires OF affection TO burn steadily and\n| clearly.\n\n\nThe true school for the teaching Of\n| applied household science is in the bone.\n/ There the domestic virtues may be- Guilt\nwaited; there the dignity Of labor may be\n/ rightly taught,and the full significance be\n\n\nrealized Of those lines Of good old George\n/ Herberts, that\n\n\nwho sweeps q room az by Thy Law\nMakes that and the action fine.\n\n=== 74_1933-03-31_p1_sn83016244_00271760796_1933033101_0297.json\nThe Key West citizen. [volume] (Key West, Fla.) 1879-current · 1933-03-31\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\n\n\n\nINSTRUMENTATION of modern\nAmerican Spirituahsn the Fox\nsisters alleged to have first COM\nmunicatioR with the spirit world,\nat Hydesviile, N. Y.\n1854 - Historic Commodore\nPerry open Door treaty with\njapan.\n\n\nv-~-----s--s---p--r--~\nSubscribe for The Citizen.\n\n=== 27_1917-11-01_p1_sn85038709_00415669379_1917110101_0606.json\nAlma record. (Alma, Mich.) 1878-1928 · 1917-11-01\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \nThe Owners\nUnexpected\nReturn\nDid He See a Ghost\n---\n\n\n\ntime will come when IL will show a\nscientific relation between mortality\naud immortality\"\n\n\nIl do not believe in ghosts said\nJohn Sladc emphatically.\n\n\nDaniel Gilbert drew his chair nearer\nTo the cracking blaze.\n\n\nIL never believed in ghoSts-until last\nyear\" he said quietly.\n\n\nWhat happened last year,\" SHADES\ntone was skeptical.\n\n\nII saw a ghost\"\n\n\n\"Where had you been dining laugh\ned Slade.\n\n\n~ hadn't dined at all. ] came down\n10 the Elms quite Unexpectedly.\nwanted 10 look up some papers that |\nknew 10 be in the library. The house\nhad been closed an summer, and at\nthis time even the caretakcr was away\nfor few days.\n\n\nMY plan was to dine at the village\ninn. 20 up 10 the Elms. spend the even\ning searching for the papers, sleep IN\nmy old room and take the first train\n10 town after breakfast. Ny train was\nseveral hours late. and when we ar\nrived in Elmville the inn was shut up\nfor the night. If you are acquainted\nwith our village boniface you viii I'M\nderstand that for neither love nor mon\ney nor 'auld lang sync' would he open\nhis doors 10 a guest after midnight.\nSo It meant dinnerlcss tramp up to\nthe house.\n\n\n1T was nearly O'Clock when | en-\ntered the avenue, and | was surprised\nTO find the iron gates of the drive wide\nopen That was against my orders.\n\n\n1T was a dark night. The stars\nshone bright, and young moon threw\na thin silver veil of light III the open\nspaces As | came in sight of the\nhouse | stopped still in my tracks.\n\n\nyou may not believe me, John, but\nmy house was a blaze of light from\nbasement to cupola. and from behind\nthe lighted windows came the strains\nOf an orchestra. while forms floated 10\nand fro IN swaying rhythm 10 the\nwaltz melody\n\n\nLAS ! stood rooted to the spot the\nchurch clock in the village struck once.\nand as if 10y magic the music ceased\nand all motion stopped behind the thin\ncurtains. An instant later every light\nin the house died out as if extinguish-\ned by some mighty breath. l ran up\nthe steps ana fitted my key IN the\ngreat lock. II yielded, and the heavy\ndoor swung outward to my touch\n\n\n81 threw ray Of light about the\ngreat entrance hail ana reached for the\nbutton TO switch on the electric lights.\nThe button clicked without response.\nAgain aud again | tried. but In vain.\n\n\n\"a search 81 the drawing room and\nlibrary revealed the same condition\nThe current was slut op ana probably\nhad been since the departure of the\ncaretakcr\n\n\nII lighted the large oil lamp on the\nlibrary table II was an easy matter\n! TO dlg out the documents | wanted\nand store them into my leather bag\n] As l set the bag on the floor | saw\ne lying on the Turkish rug n white rose\n\n\nfresh ana fragrant, as If just plucked.\n& This was no earthly blossom ! dc\n{ cided as | laid 1t before me on the \"a\no bic. And then | had another shock\np for almost under my hand and ia thu\nq very spot on which ! had placed m.\np bag when entering the room was\nq small lace handkerchief Across GA\n\n\ncorner was embroidered in tiny letter\n\n\ngirls name\n\n\nGilbert blew cloud Of smoke tina\n\n\nobscurcd his face. John Slade leanc\n\\ forward In his chair.\n\n\nand the name-the girls Nance h\nii asked quickly.\n\n\n.. \"yioia-just Viola\" said Gilber\n!. lreamly.\n\n\n\"T'hat is not all of the story\" sal\n'r Slade imntiontly. Go on, old man.\n'c NO. that - not all. i turned\ne. tho mautcIpiccc ana irdned my hea\n\n\nagainst the high shelf, trying TO solN\n= the mystery Of my housc. for to m\n\n\nknowledao no woman save the car\n\n\ntakcr. an old black nanny, had darl\n70 cned-or shall ! say brighteuedf-m\nu doors for several years.\nvc when | turned back to the tab\n\n\nthe handkerchief was gone!\nr with hardly thought as to wheth'\n\"A / was seeking shadow OF substance\n\n\nthrust the rose in my pocket. an\n\n\nswapping on my pocket electric\n10 FASTENED into the hall. There was\nely CLEAN of white In the distance, and\nvn pursued It. 1t flicked around a corn\n\n\nand disappeared up small spiral stat\nr. case that red to the second floor.\nnj when reached the top of Il\n=, stairs the bit Of white became a W\n\n\nmans graceful form. with some eth\nw. real garment Of filmy stuff floating i\nIn hind her. And as followed | seem,\nMI 10 be walking among violets\ny Down the grand staircase she va\n\n\nished, this time with little clickir\non heels on the polished floor. held h\n\n\nsteadily in my ray OF light and W\n\n\nafter her like flash. We crossed {\n\n\ndrawing room. and then she disa\n\n\npearcd through the paneled door In\n\n\nleads into the west galery-you kno\n\n\nthat long. glass inclosed gallery III\n\n\nnv father used as a winter parlo\n> She flew down the length. and ] hop\n\n\n10 catch her at the end. but &C {\n80. door leading into the gardens s\nye turned her face toward me for one\nmt slant, and. by Jove. John. she was I\nry, lovelest girl / ever laid eyes upor\nnd and you know / don't care for WON,\nlisa \"Just \"a glimpse OF her beauty, a\ntn the door opened and closed sharp\n,rd leaving me alone i'm the west gallo\nfor / was tired out by the exciting adv,\n\n=== 3_1883-03-11_pna_sn83045160_00280779647_1883031101_0237.json\nMemphis daily appeal. [volume] (Memphis, Tenn.) 1847-1886 · 1883-03-11\ncrops: cached\nheadline: A Haunted Desk.\n---\nThe San Luis Obispo Tribune says tina.\nvery mysterious desk has for some lime been\nan object of great solicltude in the Office Of\nthe Pacific Coast railway. Several months\nsgO Freight Agent HasSiDs observed q sin\ngular rappiDg and rafting noise 10 the desk\naz which he was writing, and endssvored to\nascertain the cause. After a careful exam\nalton no cause could be found, and work\nwas resumed. At very inopportune times\nthis noise would be repeated, and a belief\nwas aroused that spirits had taken possession\nOF the desk and it WIS placed in another\nroom. But even from there it continued It's\nannoyarc ana II was sent down to the depot\nwithout any intimation Of lis tricks. But al\nthe depot It continued lis rsppings, and the\nclerk 10 whom it was assigned chose boxes OF\ntables or other decks TO write upon rather thar\ncoufess his fright at the haunted object which\n\n\nwas his companion. Luckily a desk was\n\n\nwanted il the depot at Los Alamos. The clerk\n\n\nrecomme nded that the one which had beer\n| assigned to him be sent, as he thought ht\n| could get along without it, and the officers o\n| the company relieved him of the object o\n\n\nterror. The haunted dess WAS sent 10 Lo,\nAlamos, bearing good character and frest\n| coat OF varnish. 1t was thought so great\n\n\nremoval would dislodge the troubled anC\n\n\ntroublesome spirit and give the clerks rest\n| But now comes the complaint from Los Ala\n| mos OF mysterious noises from that sam\n| desk. WHAT can be done with il? All ar,\n| getting frightened. Cannot some medium\n| investigate II and unravel The mystery, o\nsend the desk on the Lompoc, where nj\nspirits are allowed?\n\n=== 5_1898-02-07_p1_sn83016025_00414210697_1898020701_0232.json\nThe Portland daily press. · 1898-02-07\ncrops: cached\nheadline: AT THE sEANcE.\n\n| Rastus johnson Describes His Confah\n| With the Spirit of Bud Moses.\n---\nRastus JOHNSON Describes His Confat\n\n\nWith the Spirit of Bud Moses.\n\n\nRastus johnson attended a seance hello\nby the Roottown Colored Epiritualistit\nsociety one evening not long since. ane\nthe next morning expressed himself al\nfollows: \"Br'er Jones dun tole 1ue tel\ncome an hole confab wifi de spirits. Well\nwe dun go inter a dahk room, brack as d,\ndebbil, an he say ter sit down. Lords.\nhow dem shibbers chase cross ma spare\nribs, an ma very blood TUX chill Didn't\nknow what was cummin. Suffin was\nmoyin roun, but Br'er Jones seq twas\nsome his libbin frens.\n\n\n\"'What see dem spooks?' seq I.\n\n\nYou cain't see 'em; 20 jes heah 'em,\nseq he.\n\n\n\"Bimeby suffin hollers ma name\n'Rastus Johnson seq it.\n\n\n\"'Who is yo's' 'jaculated 1.\n\n\nm de spirit OBJ Bud Moses\n\n\n\"'Yo's liah, whoebber yo' is, kase Bud\ndidn't go ter hebben. Toll me suffin, So;\nknow fer suah.\n\n\nChickens at Mars Butler's,' seq do\nspeaka.\n\n\nDon't brefulate, yo' is him. / sez\nSo po' Bud was mascercated at dat coop.\n\n\n\"Now, how'n Jasp'd he get ter hebbeni\nSuah he didn't bride ST. Petah wifi nc\nchickens, TO- dey was lef benin.\n\n\nWhat yo' all doin up tina, Bud an\nnow yo' git up?' I axed.\n\n\nOh, I'm de do keepah fer Niger heb\nben, SEE he. I am ST. Petah's potah.\nYo' doan git up heah, yo' rascal, Ions TIE\nde main guy.\n\n\n\"'Yo amt SO pow full, I seq, IAN, mo\n'ober. yo' amt in hebben, chen eff yo\nvoice do Giro late from de ceilin.'\n\n\nII was list'nin wild raptuah, an nuthin\nmissed ma obsavashun. Somebody was in\nde room ovahead. jes pull ma gun az\nshoot quick. Somethin boiler an SEE, I'm\npunctuated, an when do light was fetched\nwhat a predicameht! Some fool nigga\nwas hanging half way down trapdo' in\ndo collin, an dem coon was Lbbid. Den\njes pull ma razza an seq, Make way ef\nyo' Tub yo' existence, an made ma 'scape.\n\n\nTHAT po' coon was mos dead, but serve\nhim right, he was SO foolish Dey's lots\nOBJ Guild folks what b'lieyes in spirits, but\n20 CANT fool dis blackjack. columbus\nDispatch.\n\n=== 2_1849-09-08_p1_sn82014434_00415661368_1849090801_0320.json\nThe republic. [volume] (Washington [D.C.]) 1849-1853 · 1849-09-08\ncrops: cached\nheadline: HARPER 4 BROTHERS,\n---\nuVuD pFTn,S History of the United States, from\nIL the first settlement of the country 70 the or\nganization of government under the + eoeral Con\nsntution sc 3Vols. Svo. Price per VOL, mushn, S2\nsheep extra, So 25; haltcalt ~ d.\nSontbey's Common. Place Book, beng choice selec-\nt;ons from celebrated authors. Edited by his\nlaw j Wood Warter, Is. d., syo Publish\ninc in narts so cents each.\n\n\nLyeil,s Second Visit 20 the United States, vols\nC;eseler,s Compendium of Ecclesiastical History\nFrom the fourth edition, revised and amended, by\nsamuel Davidson, LII. D. vols. Svo So\nMaybew's Magic oi Kindness; or the Wondrous\nStory of the Good Huan. With numerous illus\ntratons lSmo.\n\n\nDante's Divine Comedy: The Inferno. literal\nprose translation with the text OF the original\ncolated from the best edition