{"parentUuid":"e3603922-ea4c-4349-b97c-04787bf989f0","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"775e54ef-6487-4f79-8598-643421b87718","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FtbzuW9EaVXnGp8bMFXrPo","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t=== 27_1881-05-28_pna_sn84022553_00202197255_1881052801_0048.json\n2\tBradford opinion. [volume] (Bradford, Vt.) 1874-1881 · 1881-05-28\n3\tcrops: cached\n4\theadline: \n5\t---\n6\tRev. Waite did not give his ex-\n7\tpose of spiritualism as announced\n8\tlast Friday evening. He conclud-\n9\ted that he could not Wait for an\n10\taudience, and SO he started for\n11\tEly.\n12\t\n13\t=== 19_1906-06-15_pna_sn88076515_00295863936_1906061501_0777.json\n14\tDevils Lake inter-ocean and Devils Lake free press. (Devils Lake, Ramsey County, N.D.) 1905-1909 · 1906-06-15\n15\tcrops: cached\n16\theadline: SUFFER J /. |: FOR OPIUM.\n17\t\n18\tSAN FRANCISCO FIRE CON\n19\t\n20\t\n21\tsUMED ALL SUPPLIES.\n22\t\n23\tUsers Of the Drug to the Number Of\n24\t10000 Deprived Of It and\n25\tMany Rave for Their\n26\t\n27\t\n28\tI'D n stDope.'\n29\t---\n30\tSan Francisco. of the abundance\n31\tOF supplies which have been sent here\n32\tsince the calamity OF few weeks ago\n33\tnothing has been more heartily wei\n34\tcoined than the quantity, small though\n35\tit be, Of that brown, gummy substance,\n36\tthe dope fiend's', key to paradise, the\n37\tabsence Of which during the period Of\n38\tdevastation and turmoil is responsible\n39\tfor many cases OF insanity, temporary\n40\tand permanent. 3 &\n41\t\n42\t\n43\tThe opium fiend needed but little the\n44\tlack of food, water and shelter, but\n45\tthe want of his dope\" actually drove\n46\thim crazy. None but those who have\n47\tbecome a prey to this drug can realize\n48\twhat it means to go without it for\n49\t\"a hours, two days, a week, and in\n50\tsome cases even longer. A living hell\n51\twas theirs while they hunted from\n52\tplace to place besieging the few re-\n53\tnaming drug stores Of San Francisco.\n54\tscampering 10 Oakland, where the ar\n55\tticle was equally scarce and eventually\n56\tbecoming insane when their craving\n57\tcould not be satisfied.\n58\t\n59\t\n60\tAnd such prices! Here, where more\n61\tof the drug is consumed than in any\n62\tother four large cities Of the country\n63\tcombined and where it was almost as\n64\tcommon and marketable as the ordi-\n65\tnary articles Of diet, the diminished\n66\tsupply pushed the price up to $40 a\n67\tshell. and almost impossible to get at\n68\tthat. Those searching for relief from\n69\tthe pangs Of the fiendish habit gave\n70\tthe authorities nearly as much trouble\n71\tas the starving men, women and chill\n72\t\n73\t\n74\tdren who were piteously crying for\n75\tbread\n76\t\n77\t\n78\tSpokane and Seattle soon came to\n79\tthe relief of the pillmakers and rushed\n80\tin the hop by. the hogshead. but even\n81\tthen the supply was far below normal.\n82\t\n83\t\n84\tOnly those who know San Francisco\n85\tcan imagine the situation. Nearly all\n86\tthe opium-smoking ..joints\" were in\n87\tChinatown, yet the real swell pipe.\n88\thitters\" had their dreams in other sec\n89\tlions of the city. Every house in\n90\tChinatown could muster full quota\n91\tof t.dope\" fends, to say nothing OF the\n92\tvisitors who came along regularly for\n93\ttheir little seance. These conditions\n94\tnaturally would make Chinatown the\n95\tcenter of the industry, and it was here\n96\tthat most Of the stuff was stored in\n97\tadequate quantities. .$ I'D X; Il\n98\t\n99\t\n100\tThe fire soon annihilated Chinatown\n101\tThe WoeSsn shacks went down before\n102\t\"a Ao mo {, g VIII oat boPoras +bo ,ox,tpt,\n103\tand when the fire had spent its fury\n104\tthe smold ering embers and filthy de-\n105\tbris choked up the streets, the tunnels,\n106\tsubways, dugouts and underground\n107\tpassages for which this section was\n108\tnoted. These burrows contained most\n109\tOf the city's supply Of the opium, and\n110\twhile there may be some Of the drug\n111\tthat survived the flames, it has not yet\n112\tbeen brought to the surface.\n113\t\n114\t\n115\tThe HOP sharks were noticeable\n116\tall over San Francisco before the first\n117\tday of the conHagration had passed.\n118\tThey could be singled out running\n119\tabout in wild frenzy, begging at the\n120\tfew remaining drug stores for relief.\n121\tThey numbered fully $1,000, white and\n122\t4 r g t.\n123\tyellow. h. N\n124\t\n125\t\n126\tThe day after the fire started these\n127\tsufferers added thousandfold to the\n128\tdifficulties Of the authorities and Oak\n129\tland soon became the center of their\n130\tsiege. There was no relief in that\n131\tquarter and by the morning Of the sec\n132\tond day the fiends literally stormed\n133\tthe drug stores. with little result.\n134\t\n135\t\n136\tThe soldiers, vigiiant at all times,\n137\twould corral these morbid creatures,\n138\tand when they became temporarily in\n139\tsane would turn them loose. The sto-\n140\tries which travelled east that many\n141\tpeople were crazed by the excitement\n142\tand horror were in some cases nothing\n143\tmore than instances Of the \"pipe hit\n144\tters' rawlings. t 5s58\n145\t\n146\t=== 2_1930-03-08_p1_sn83045499_00514159567_1930030801_0461.json\n147\tThe Daily Alaska empire. · 1930-03-08\n148\tcrops: cached\n149\theadline: OUIJA BOARD\n150\t\n151\t SPELLED OUT\n152\t\n153\t WORD 'AvENCE'\n154\t\n155\tTribal Superstition Is Also Given as Cause for\n156\t\n157\t Buffalo Murder\n158\t---\n159\tBUFFALO, New York, March 8. Two Indian women are in jail charged with the murder of Mrs. Clothilde Marchand, wife of the famous artist Henry Marchand, Curator of the Buffalo Museum of Science. after one of the women, Lillian Jimer5on confessed she used an ouija board and also tribal su- perstitions which melted Mrs. Man. cy Bowen, aged 6 years, to kill the artists wife.\n160\t\n161\t Unrequited love for the artist, is blamed for the killing, Miss Jimer- son said. She is 35 years of age, whom Marchand used as model. She became infatuated with him.\n162\t\n163\t Miss Jimerson said she told the older Indian woman that messages from her husband, in the spirit land, cried out to avenge. Then the cuija board did the same thing as well as. spelling out instructions to go to the Marchand home and kill the woman. The woman did go. She struck Mrs. Marchand with a hammer over the head, then thrust a wad of paper and chloro- form down her throat.\n164\t\n165\t=== 13_1942-02-14_p1_sn93062828_00332899478_1942021401_0066.json\n166\tThe Omaha guide. · 1942-02-14\n167\tcrops: cached\n168\theadline: AT THE DANCE FEB. 9th\n169\t---\n170\tAT THE DANCE FEB. 9th\n171\t\n172\t\n173\tEdgar Alord pulling a snake\n174\twith Cert Yancey.\"\n175\t\n176\t\n177\tEdith Cottrell Seen having a gay\n178\ttime with Cowboy. Say, Cottrell\n179\tis he still your ideal?\n180\t\n181\t\n182\tElwood left Joyce at home.\n183\t\n184\t\n185\tWe saw Juanita Wynn and\n186\tPreacher. (They were pretty well\n187\twrapped up there.) Congratulate\n188\tions to the newly weds, Vineta\n189\tSwancey and Pauncho.\n190\t\n191\t\n192\tEloise seemed to be killing her\n193\tself with Herman Franklin. Does\n194\tnot she realize the little Jasper\n195\tBaby's feeling will be deeply hurt\n196\t-. and we mean deeply.\n197\tMack Franklin with this noJ-so\n198\tbad-too-look at Chick from Texas,\n199\tCrying to knock your broth.r\n200\tout p\n201\t\n202\t\n203\tPrice Terrell Jr., was seen with\n204\tMiss Frampton after the dance al\n205\tso Billy Richardson with Edith.\n206\tGoing Back Home.)\n207\t\n208\t=== 14_1913-02-20_p1_sn83045652_0028077845A_1913022001_0658.json\n209\tTaÌˆgliche Omaha tribuÌˆne. [volume] (Omaha, Nebr.) 1912-1926 · 1913-02-20\n210\tcrops: faro_1913/1913-02-20_p1_sn83045652_0028077845A_1913022001_0658.json: the article's words aren't in its boxes\n211\theadline: \n212\t---\n213\tHaunt trdgsn6 duf Gilas.\n214\t\n215\t\n216\tOr III es!\" oadts bieIsr. .Ntnl\n217\taud Der Shnurrbart iebl