moment at the sight of him. He had heavy, anxious look. All the old boy ish beauty of his face, which lasted long after he became man, was gone: the smooth plump cheeks seemed 10 be fall ing with their own weight the corners of the mouth went down. His talk was worse than his look he was absence of mind personhed. He could hardly an- swer you he was too much absorbed Tn his own grief. There was no human be- i ing who had less concern in the world than this man who lived by illustrating everything in II. He was the most pro- digious worker of this or any other time. His original drawings were to be counted by the thousand. They were ali wrong in costume or local color, but in the feel ing of the situation they were unsurpassed. He stood m abou; the same relationship To accuracy ct historical observations as Victor Hugo. and indeed he was a TO- mantle of the pencil, and was altogether out of his element if this our poor day of\n398\t\n399\t \n400\t\n401\t the medium of his wonderful masination. and this. which Was his success. has also ln measure been ms ruin. for he saw his own achievements and his own fame III the same way; and the sight was a l misery that weighed him down. IL be- came towards the last something of an insult to remind him that he had done good illustrations he would look at you ! <.ft th ~f see If you were simply stupid or if you were laughing in your sleeves, and would then lead you up to one of his paintings and wait for the verdict like a shy boy. If you knew anything at all of painting you could give but one answer. His pictures went into the Salon because the Salon could not refuse them-he was h0ys eon6oHrs. but no one looked at them. They found market, however, as things to exhibit in London, where all i the country parsons took them in very good faith indeed and led their children by the hand to see the judgments of scrip ture illustrated to the life by this gifted Frenchman. There was plenty of red in the = Dream of Pilates Wife\" if there was nothing else. and the Destruction of the Heather Gods by Triumphant Christian ity' was wonderful show of fantastic invention. though the eo.oring and the whole treatment put it clean into the cat egoy Of merely decorative art-it was scene painting, nothing more. Tenieui- Der Dore when he was young and joyous. and think once described his life al that time in ,ie HorM He lived with his mother until she died and after that he still occupied the quaint old family house in the Rue S,. Dominique in which he had passed 50 many happy years. He never married: he once had consuming tenderness for Patti and in being disap- pointed of her never cared about another woman. but his affection for his mother must have had much to do with his life of cellbacy. She always treated him as I great boy. The relations between them were something beautiful to see; he was not only gay but larkish\" when she was by. and she used to check him with d Hush. Gustaye. Be quiet, Gustase,\" as though he were still on her knee. He was seen at his best on Sunday evening, the day of his informal reception. First there used to be dinner in the extraor- dlnary dining-room that you reached from the door of the apartment without the ghost Of an ante-chamter then after din- her fiends dropped m and everybody went into the large studio beyond Mime Dores drawlng-room. Here it was the lrishman : Liberty Hall everybody did as he liked and those who would not do II were made. Dore hddled. sang, stood or chairs and played practical jokes with his friends. fasano, the opaniard, would sing too then one of the Dore brothers played. Sometimes Munkaczy would drop in. al that time very secondary light in art compared with Dore. can distinctly remember thinking it was kind of Dore to receive him. But now, of course, Munkaczy is the acknowledged master of all contemporary art. His as- founding success must have had some thing te do with Dore's disappointment What he Is Dore had intended to be, and the sight of him was a continual memo rial of frustrated hopes This, think, \n402\t\n403\t=== 11_1894-12-10_p1_sn83030214_00175038759_1894121001_0608.json\n404\tNew-York tribune. [volume] (New York [N.Y.]) 1866-1924 · 1894-12-10\n405\tcrops: faro_1894/1894-12-10_p10_sn83030214_00175038759_1894121001_0608.json: the article's words aren't in its boxes\n406\theadline: EUROPEAN PRODUCE MARKETS\n407\t---\n408\tLiverpool. Dec. $. 1880 p. m.-Bccf-Fxra India mesa\n409\ta.n at Ills 81; prime mess firm at SIS ID Pork-Prms\n410\tmess Western fine steady COS do MEDIUM steady a,\n411\tl5s ID Hams-Short cut. about II n 6 -. dull \"a\n412\tBacon-Cumberlani cut about IS q 30 10. steady at as\n413\tshort RIB - H. steady 2is; long clear midtss\n414\tabot q, r. steady AT 35s long and short clear nidss\n415\tabout - H. firm .537 81. shoulders. about 2 10 IA\n416\tn steady .ss Cheese American finest white colored\n417\tsteady az 5l. Tallow-i'rime city. nominal Cottonseed\n418\to-Iiverpo0 relied lss 81. Spirits Of lrnen,lne\n419\tsteady 2Ds Cd Rosil-COmmOn. firm az IS lmsi. Lard\n420\t\n421\t\n422\tPrime Western spot. steady az 3Gs 81 Wheat-No red\n423\twinter dull - ed: No red spring. stock Flour\n424\t\n425\t\n426\t81 Louis fancy winter steady at IS corn-Mxed West\n427\t\n428\t\n429\tspot fra az IS I'D December firm az - I'D January\n430\tqsy Is Gi1 February easy As Gi,d. Hops Lon.\n431\tlou Pacific Coast steady AT g 5s9f2 lbs\n432\t\n433\t=== 6_1921-12-31_pna_sn82014519_00414218945_1921123101_0676.json\n434\tNew Britain herald. [microfilm reel] (New Britain, Conn.) 1890-1976 · 1921-12-31\n435\tcrops: cached\n436\theadline: WEIRD FIGURE\n437\t\n438\t\n439\tCHASES WOMEN\n440\t\n441\tQuartette Of Females Flee LeapinS\n442\t\"GhOSt\" ON Cheshire Road\n443\t---\n444\t\n445\t\n446\t\n447\tCheshire, Dec, 3l,-Four women\n448\twhose homes are in southineton, were\n449\tchased by what was either a ghost\n450\tor possibly member Of the i'll,\n451\tfamed Ku KluX Khan, but probably a\n452\tpractical Joker in Cheshire Thursday\n453\tnight, as they were returning towards\n454\tn trolley station after spending the\n455\tevening at the home OF a friend near\n456\tthe Cheshire Reformatory.\n457\t\n458\t\n459\tThe affair has caused much eX-\n460\telement among the women OF the\n461\tnelghborhood and has caused the hus-\n462\tbands and male members of the WON\n463\tems families many moments of anger\n464\tMt the boldness of the person who\n465\tcommitted the act.\n466\t\n467\t\n468\tGhost Appears\n469\t\n470\t\n471\tThe incident happened on a lonely\n472\troad just off the turnpike where the\n473\ttrail Is bordered on one side by a\n474\tlong and dark stretch of woods and\n475\ton the other by an old barn where, 1t\n476\tis said, few months ago, a man was\n477\tkilled by an explosion. The women\n478\tconcerned claim that on the way\n479\tdown, they noticed a man lettering\n480\tabout the barn, but paid no attention\n481\tto him. On the return trip, however,\n482\tthe same person. as they suppose,\n483\twith a white sheet covering him from\n484\thead to foot came out and with long\n485\tgrotesque leaps and not a sound fol-\n486\tbowed them up the road.\n487\t\n488\t\n489\tWeird Appearance\n490\t\n491\t\n492\tFrightened, tn women fastened\n493\tIheir steps, but as fast as they would\n494\tgo, the faster the figure would come.\n495\tIn fact he approached to within four\n496\tyards of the quartet and one of them\n497\tholder perhaps than the others,\n498\tflashed on him a searchlight which she\n499\twas carrying in an attempt to scare\n500\thim away. It was noticed then that\n501\tthe mysterious thing had a mask\n502\twhich was painted in the most weird\n503\tand fantastic manner, and this Of\n504\tcourse, only served to increase the\n505\tfright of the women.\n506\t\n507\t\n508\tWomen Get Help\n509\t\n510\t\n511\tFinally they began to run, and the\n512\tghost was right after them, waving\n513\tIt's arms and leaping more wildly then\n514\tever. The race continued until finally,\n515\tthe panic stricken women sighted a\n516\tmouse ny the roadside all IN dark\n517\tness. Stumbling and screaming they\n518\tbegan to pound upon the door cry\n519\ting for help. A man came to the\n520\tdoor in his night shirt, carrying a\n521\tdouble barrelled shot gun in the crook\n522\tof his arm. 1t took some time for\n523\tthe women to recover enough to tell\n524\tthe story, but when the man found out\n525\twhat the trouble was, he hastily\n526\tdressed and with mutterings of the\n527\tKu Klux Klan, left the house.\n528\t\n529\t\n530\tGhost Disappears\n531\t\n532\t\n533\tHe made thorough search of the\n534\tneighborhood, but the ghost\" or\n535\twhatever it was, had disappeared and\n536\tno trace was found of him. After q\n537\ttime, the man of the hour escorted\n538\tthe women down the road to the troll\n539\tley car and saw them safely on their\n540\tway. s\n541\t\n542\t=== 9_1919-07-09_p1_sn89081128_0028076677A_1919070901_0223.json\n543\tNew Ulm review. (New Ulm, Brown County, Minn.) 1892-1961 · 1919-07-09\n544\tcrops: cached\n545\theadline: Sir Redvers Buffers Ghost Story.\n546\t---\n547\tSome time after the Fra German\n548\twar of 1870, writes Lady Butler in an\n549\tEnglish paper, Sir Redyers (then Cap\n550\ttain) Butler left England to visit the\n551\tbattlefelds, and on arriving at the\n552\ttown Of gave directions that his\n553\tletters should not be forwarded, and\n554\tstarted on his tour Of inspection.\n555\t\n556\t\n557\tAfter he had been away some days, he\n558\t.aWoke zsudd enly one night, thinking\n559\the saw Lord Wolseley (then Sir Gar\n560\tmetz, and that he heard him say ~\n561\twonder where that fellow Butler is.\n562\tcan't think why he has not answered\n563\t\n564\t\n565\tmy letter This so impressed him.\n566\tthat he returned at once to the town\n567\tof 49,, where he found a letter await:\n568\ting him from Lord Wolseley, saying\n569\tthat he must return to London imme-,\n570\tdiately as an expedition against the\n571\t\n572\t\n573\tAshantees was imminent\n574\t\n575\t=== 13_1920-06-11_pna_sn88084272_00294553484_1920061101_0531.json\n576\tEl Paso herald. (El Paso, Tex.) 1901-1931 · 1920-06-11\n577\tcrops: cached\n578\theadline: \n579\t---\n580\tone hose. and the knees Of hubby's\n581\ttrousers.\n582\t\n583\t\n584\t\n585\tAfter a few years OF married life\n586\tno woman needs ouija board to tell\n587\ther exactly what a man is going 10\n588\tdo OF say next.\n589\t\n590\t\n591\t\n592\tWhy do some husbands regard the\n593\twedding bell as the signal 10 stop\n594\tOffering woman sweets, compli-\n595\tments and kisses. and 10 begin offer\n596\ting her roast beef. advice and criti-\n597\tcism?\n598\t\n599\t\n600\t\n601\tWhen two artistic souls marry. the\n602\tdevil usually takes a malicious de-\n603\tlight in adding a touch OF birthstone\n604\tto the match\"\n605\t\n606\t\n607\tr\n608\t\n609\t\n610\tNOnsensei A man doesn't lose\n611\tall his sentiment after marriage.\n612\tII merely gets Bmothered under a\n613\t\n614\t=== 18_1921-09-06_p7_sn82014519_0041421891A_1921090601_0060.json\n615\tNew Britain herald. · 1921-09-06\n616\tcrops: cached\n617\theadline: TWO PERSONS HURT, IN MORNING CRASH\n618\t\n619\tMeriden Folks Come to Grief on\n620\t\n621\t WorthingtOn Ridge,\n622\t\n623\tDRIVERS JAW FRACTURED\n624\t\n625\tState Cops Patrol for Hours-Repub- \n626\t\n627\t Iicnn Caucus Call Issucd-Town Schools Reopen Tomorrow Morning g -other Items of Interest in Berlin.\n628\t---\n629\tMrs Emma Lamont and Ralph Turner Of Pratt street. Meriden. are in th. Britain hospital suffering from I...uries received when a Buick touring car in which they were driv- ing. crashed into a tree on worthing- ton Ridge at Q o'clock this morning. Mrs. Lamont is suffering from a frac- ture of the jaw and Turner is more seriously injured. suffering from a fracture of the skull The two were attended by Dr. T. c. Hodsson who ordered their immediate removal to the New Britain institution.\n630\t\n631\t A broken steering wheel was the cause Of 