tgeneral rule. Our people. only ask\n759\tscrupulous conformity to the law.\n760\tThey wish the business to be well and\n761\tdone, SO that when it is done,\n762\tthere will be no ground for cavil as to\n763\ttheir right to representation and\n764\tequality of privilege with the most\n765\tfavored States.\n766\t\n767\t=== 13_1921-10-07_pna_sn89058253_00415621991_1921100701_1148.json\n768\tThe Fayette falcon. (Somerville, Tenn.) 190?-current · 1921-10-07\n769\tcrops: cached\n770\theadline: \n771\t---\n772\t\n773\t\n774\t\n775\tpearls. Wrap them up inside the sill\n776\tyer and gold\" Aloofly he watched\n777\tthe process. Nov advance alone, and\n778\topen the box. Place the pearls in\n779\tside. Now shut and lock It.\" 0bedi-\n780\tentity the duchess did as she was bid !:\n781\tthen she stood waiting for further In\n782\tstructions.\n783\t\n784\t\n785\tBut apparently by this time the\n786\tGreat Brooding Spirit was beginning\n787\tto take effect. Singing a monotonous.\n788\tharsh chant. the Indian knelt on the\n789\tfloor, and poured some powder into\n790\ta little brazler. He was still close\n791\tto the open window, and finally he sat\n792\tdown with his elbows ON his knees,\n793\tand his head rocking to and fro in\n794\this hands.\n795\t\n796\t\n797\tLESS light-less lightr The words\n798\tseemed to come from a great distance\n799\t-ventriloqulsm in a mild way was\n800\tone of Lakington's accomplishments I;\n801\tand as the lights went out greenish.\n802\tspluttering flame rose from the brazicr\n803\tA heavy, odorous smoke filed the\n804\troom, but framed and motionless In\n805\tthe eerie light sat the Indian. staring\n806\tfxedly in front of him. After a time\n807\tthe chant began again; It grew and\n808\tswelled in volume till the singer grew\n809\tfrenzied and beat his head with his\n810\thands. Then abruptly II stopped.\n811\t\n812\t\n813\t\"Place the box upon the floor\" he\n814\tordered, ''in the light of the Sacred\n815\tFire.\" Hugh saw the duchess kneel\n816\tdown on the opposite side Of the\n817\tbrazier. and place the box on the floor,\n818\twhile the faces Of the guests-strange\n819\tand ghostly in the great llght-peered\n820\tlike specters out OF the heavy smoke.\n821\tThis was undoubtedly a show worth\n822\twatching.\n823\t\n824\t\n825\t\"Open the box\" Harshly the words\n826\trang through the silent room, and with\n827\tfingers that trembled a little the duch-\n828\tess turned the key and threw back\n829\tthe lid.\n830\t\n831\t\n832\t\"Why, it's empty!\" she cried in\n833\tamazement, and the guests craned for\n834\tward to look.\n835\t\n836\t\n837\t\"Put not your hand inside\" cried\n838\tthe Indian in sudden warning, \"or per-\n839\tchance 1t will remain empty\n840\t\n841\t\n842\tThe duchess rapidly withdrew her\n843\thand, and stared incredulously through\n844\tthe smoke at his impressive face\n845\t\n846\t\n847\t'Did not say that there was power\n848\tIn the box?\" he said dreamily. \"The\n849\tpower TO render invisible--the power\n850\tto render visible. Thus came pro-\n851\tlection to the jewels of the Chow\n852\tqueens\"\n853\t\n854\t\n855\tThat's all right, Mr.. Ram OAR\"\n856\tsaid the duchess little apprehens-\n857\tlively. \"There may be power in the\n858\tbox, but my pearls don't seem to be-\"\n859\t\n860\t\n861\tThe Indian laughed.\n862\t\n863\t\n864\t\"None but you has touched the cab\n865\tmet, your Grace; none but you must\n866\ttouch it till the pearls return. They\n867\tare there now; but not for mortal\n868\teyes to see.\"\n869\t\n870\t\n871\tWhich, Incidentally, was no more\n872\tthan the truth.\n873\t\n874\t\n875\t\"Look, oh sahibs, look; but do not\n876\ttouch. See that to your vision the box\n877\tis empty. :\" He waited notion\n878\tless, while the guests thronged round,\n879\twith expressions of Amusement; and\n880\tHugh. safe from view In the thick,\n881\tunderstanding smoke, came even near\n882\ter In bis excitement.\n883\t\n884\t\n885\tat is enough\" cried the Indian\n886\tsuddenly. \"Slnit the box. your grace.\n887\tand lock II as before. Now place II\n888\ton the table whence 1t came. Is 1t\n889\tthere?\"\n890\t\n891\t\n892\t\"Yes.\" The duchess voice came out\n893\tof the green fog.\n894\t\n895\t\n896\tgo mot too near he continued\n897\twarniEely. \"The gods must have\n898\tspace-the gods must have space\"\n899\t\n900\t\n901\tAgain the harsh chant began. at\n902\ttimes swelling to a shout. at times\n903\tdying away TO a whisper. And II was\n904\tduring one of these latter periods that\n905\tlow laugh. Instantly checked, dis\n906\tturbed the room. II was plainly audi\n907\tBILE, and someone irritably said. \"Be\n908\tquiet\" II was not repeated. which\n909\tafforded Hugh, at any rate. no sur\n910\tprise. For 1t had been Trina Peterson\n911\twho had laughed. ard 1t might have\n912\tbeen hilarity, or Il might have been \\\n913\ta signal.\n914\t\n915\t\n916\t\"Bring the box, your grace\" he cried\n917\tharshly. and once more the duchess\n918\tknelt In the circle of light. with a\n919\trow Of dimly seen faces above her.\n920\t\n921\t\n922\t''0pen; but as you value your pearls\n923\ttouch them not\" Excitedly she\n924\tthrew back the lid. and chorus of\n925\tcries greeted the appearance OF the\n926\tgold and silver tissue at the bottom of\n927\tthe box.\n928\t\n929\t\n930\tThey're here. Mr.. Ram Oar\"\n931\t\n932\t\n933\tIn the green light the Indians SON.\n934\tDer eyes stared round the group Of\n935\tdim faces.\n936\t\n937\t\n938\tlTo BE coNTINUEDJ\n939\t\n940\t\n941\t\n942\t\n943\t=== 26_1885-12-18_pna_sn84023416_00202197504_1885121801_1036.json\n944\tEssex County herald. [volume] (Guildhall, Vt.) 1873-1964 · 1885-12-18\n945\tcrops: cached\n946\theadline: \n947\t---\n948\tthankfulness towards the bounteous\n949\tGlver of all perfect gifts. Well, the\n950\tday had come and many were merry\n951\tand doubtless some were sad. 1t\n952\thad come, l say, and was swiftly\n953\tdrawing to its close.\n954\t\n955\t\n956\tIn a city near the Kaatskills ev-\n957\terything was hustle and confusion.\n958\tFamilies were gathered around their\n959\tfiresides. Busy throngs of people |\n960\thurried hither and thither and un-\n961\tnoticed wanderers loitered on the\n962\tstreet corners. In a room lighted\n963\tonly by the glow of the fire, right\n964\tin the heart of the city, figure sat\n965\tdreamily looking into the fire. The\n966\tloud click of the horses shoes on\n967\tthe finty pavement grated harshly\n968\tupon his ear and the clang of horse\n969\tcar bells sounded strangely unnat-\n970\tural.\n971\t\n972\t\n973\tLet us glance inside the room a\n974\tmoment. On one side the fire is a\n975\tcase of books; on the other stands a\n976\ttable with writing materials upon it.\n977\tStuffed birds, autumn leaves and\n978\t! bits of rocks give evidence that the\n979\troom is occupied by one who goes\n980\tunwillingly from the haunts of ma\n981\tture. Above the mantle the picture\n982\tof an intelligent bird dog hangs\n983\trevealed in the dim light; on the\n984\tmat before the fire the form of the\n985\tsame dog is worked in brown and\n986\twhite, and how | wish l could tell\n987\tyou the dog himself sat there by\n988\this master, but no, his resting place\n989\tupon the shore of q distant noun\n990\ttain lake is marked by tree famil-\n991\tlar only to him who placed it there.\n992\tBut l forget myself. must return\n993\tto the form before the fire.\n994\t\n995\t\n996\tFor a moment an expression of\n997\tmerriment crept over his face as he\n998\tsat looking at an ungainly bird that\n999\tseemed peering comically down at\n1000\thim from out the darkness, but as\n1001\this eyes rested on the picture of the\n1002\tdog the'smile left his lips. For a time\n1003\t= he gazed intently at the picture anc\n1004\t\n1005\t\n1006\tthen at the mat and finally his eyes\n1007\tclosed and an expression of deer\n1008\tthoughtfulness came over his feat\n1009\tures. Now can you guess who thi:\n1010\thuman being is that sits thus OF\n1011\t! Christmas night in the noisy city\n1012\tIs it Karl Henry? Why no, Thu\n1013\t\n1014\t\n1015\tcannot be he, for Karl Henry il\n1016\tl among the Green Mountains, fa\n1017\t\n1018\t\n1019\taway from the busy city. He il\n1020\t\n1021\t\n1022\twith the friends of his youth in thu\n1023\tlodge among the blrches at his ma\n1024\t\n1025\t\n1026\ttive town.\n1027\t\n1028\t\n1029\t! And the old lodge fairly resound\n1030\t! with merriment, for \"the gang\" il\n1031\tunbroken numbers has met there OF\n1032\tthis Christmas night. They have\n1033\tJust arrived, fire is started, tn,\n1034\t\n1035\t\n1036\tgenerous repast is spread upon thu\n1037\t\n1038\t\n1039\ttable and they have sat down, be\n1040\t:. fore commencing the feast, to Sing\n1041\tthe old familiar song, Gathering\n1042\tUp the Shells by the Seashore,\n1043\t\n1044\t\n1045\tStanley accompanying the piece\n1046\twith the banjo. Phllis there carry\n1047\t\n1048\t\n1049\ting his part in his usual thorough\n1050\tmanner but to the apparent peril Of\n1051\this constitution. Then there i,\n1052\t\n1053\t\n1054\tJohn who sings with an air Of\n1055\tsmoothness that plainly tells you he\n1056\t\n1057\t\n1058\tcan keep right along where he il\n1059\tuntil daybrcak if necessary. And\n1060\t\n1061\t\n1062\tthere, too, is Karl Henry carrying\n1063\tthe bass part, and base part it is\n1064\t\n1065\t\n1066\tindeed; he has learned the conclud\n1067\t\n1068\t\n1069\ting note of each verse and he comes\n1070\tin on that with such power that you\n1071\tat once feel convinced that he is in\n1072\tevery sense of the word base\n1073\tsinger. As the song is finished and\n1074\tStanley \"hangs up de ole banjo\n1075\t\n1076\t\n1077\tall attention is directed toward Phil\n1078\t\n1079\t\n1080\tthe punster, who is about to \"shlo[\n1081\tagain\" over Johns call for bouquets\n1082\t\n1083\t\n1084\tRising deliberately, he thus responds\n1085\t\n1086\t\n1087\tto the call: eyes, here is Stanley's\n1088\t\n1089\t\n1090\tmaple sugar, from Clarke's, which\n1091\t\n1092\t\n1093\twill go splendidly on fapjacks, and\n1094\ttherefore | feel highly honored III\n1095\t\n1096\t\n1097\tbeing the representative chosen tc\n1098\tpresent you with this flower flour\n1099\tdish\" OF course John does not\n1100\tgive way, and the hot griddle is\n1101\tsoon busy, but is not soon to rest\n1102\t\n1103\t\n1104\tfor Stanley and Karl Henry are\n1105\t\n1106\t\n1107\thungry, and even Phil condescends\n1108\tto take a bite tonight. But al\n1109\t\n1110\t\n1111\tlength Phil cries, ''for mercy's sake\n1112\t\n1113\t\n1114\tJack, don't flap another cake on tc\n1115\t\n1116\t=== 16_1876-11-16_p1_sn86053968_00383343781_1876111601_0076.json\n1117\tThe Brookhaven ledger. (Brookhaven, Miss.) 1875-1883 · 1876-11-16\n1118\tcrops: cached\n1119\theadline: For the Brookhaven Ledger.l\n1120\t\n1121\t\n1122\tTHE GHOSTS OF THE YEAR.\n1123\t\n1124\t\n1125\t---\n1126\tBIZ L. H. N.\n1127\t\n1128\t\n1129\tHow did get there? How should 1\n1130\tknow? How does anybody get into\n1131\tfairyland, or any other such place?\n1132\tSuffice it to say that at almost midnight\n1133\tupon the last night of the old year l\n1134\tstood alone in the weirdest place that\n1135\tever was haunted by ghost or goblin.\n1136\t1t WAS a great echoing cavern, down in\n1137\tthe heart of the earth. The sides re-\n1138\tceded into the gloom on every hand\n1139\tand the lofty roof bent in starry dome\n1140\tabove. The wallis were covered with\n1141\tquaint records of the struggles and tri-\n1142\tumphs of almost six thousand years,\n1143\tand on every hand were the relics of\n1144\tmany bygone age. A rich, mellow\n1145\tlight streamed from great golden\n1146\tchandelier that might have been\n1147\twrought under the eye of some beauty\n1148\tloving Egyptian monarch. While I\n1149\tstood wrapped in it's radiance, faint\n1150\ttinkling as of elfin bells, sounded above\n1151\tmy head. I looked up and saw fa-\n1152\tmiliar figure descending flom the dome.\n1153\t\n1154\t\n1155\tHe whistied shrill tune as he rode\n1156\tdown on fierce wind. Such on un-\n1157\tcanny, wrinkled little old man, with\n1158\ticicles clinging to his beard, his cap\n1159\tand his huge fur coat! He roughly\n1160\ttweaked his knobby nose, as he whis>\n1161\ttied and stamped his foot. At the sig\n1162\tnal thousand sprite-like forms few\n1163\tfrom thousand hiding places and as\n1164\tsembled around the cross little old\n1165\tman.\n1166\t\n1167\t\n1168\tWhen be had scanned his vassals\n1169\tclosely and begun to talk in thin, icy\n1170\tvoice, who would not have recognized\n1171\tthe ghost of Winter?\n1172\t\n1173\t\n1174\t\"You are oil here\" he snarled. \"Of\n1175\tcourse you are whenever nobody wants\n1176\tyou. What do you come to me for?\n1177\tYou know already what to do. Jack\n1178\tFrost, is that you? Well, you have a\n1179\tgreat deal to do. Creep into every place\n1180\tthat will hold yon. Nip all the flowers\n1181\tthat you can find. won't have flowers\n1182\tin winter. Consumption. 60 on as\n1183\tusual. Keep your victims in long\n1184\tline marching to the graveyard. Ha,\n1185\tha! I can hear their lean voices chatter\n1186\ting together ~ they go. Now isn't it\n1187\tmusic?\" and he gave q little fiendish\n1188\tlaugh. North Wind. West Wind.\n1189\tblow your hardest, and sing hoarse,\n1190\twild song around The houses in yonr\n1191\tway. And you, Clouds, keep off the\n1192\tsunshine. If people will have some fun\n1193\tout of us with their skating and sleigh\n1194\tbells, pinch their poses and toes for it. !\"\n1195\t\n1196\t\n1197\t=== 42_1923-08-13_p1_sn84026749_00222254024_1923081301_0041.json\n1198\tThe Washington times. · 1923-08-13\n1199\tcrops: faro_1923/1923-08-13_p19_sn84026749_00222254024_1923081301_0041.json: too few words to check\n1200\theadline: RAPPINGS.\n1201\t---\n1202\tNip-Heard two chaps arguing\n1203\tyesterday about departed spirits.\n1204\tzip-Bet they mentioned cid\n1205\tJohn Barleycorn.\n1206\t\n1207\t=== 7_1832-06-12_pna_sn83025561_00514