Der\nDor elmer bibiden tleinen\n9ila bieIt.\n\n\nBORT ftieg oas paar aus lino Der\n)err besleitete bic Dame binsir. IisB\nher ben Magen iparten.\n\n\n9empel beniibte Die 3eit uno fraete\nin aus Der benadbartsn 8iua toms\nDienftmdoden, Oh fe Die\nuno Den Serrn pieleidt ienne2\nsic bsiabte. Die Dame mar cine\n)pernIdnserin, Der 9err. tuelder et< I\n,as beim @enntlub iei, Gin Nerebrsr\noil ibr. Gr taue taft tdglid Der.\nSds Werhdltnis bauere nun fhon |\n\n=== 12_1883-02-24_p2_sn90050009_00393342924_1883022401_0311.json\nThe Arkansaw traveler. · 1883-02-24\ncrops: cached\nheadline: ous.ave Dore Dies Broken Hear,ed.\n---\n4 almost be said tha, in spite of his great success he died of broken heart at his failures. He was perhaps, take him for all in all, the greatest illustrator the world has ever known, but he wanted to be the greatest painier-and he could not paint al all. His coloring was something un- namable the faults of his drawing were too glaring when he worked, as he loved 10 work, on large scale His painting, in fact, was his general incompetence in technique seen through a power ul tele- scope. Yet he would be a painter or nothing. It was useless to remind him that he had illustrated Rabeiais as no man had ever illustrated him before that his .. Contes DrolAtiques\" was mas terpiece, and that in ' Don Quixoter and Tennyson he was inferior oniy to him. self. He would shake his head sadly that was not 1t. He worked to the last on his illustrations, but only as pot boilers\" All day long he WAS climbing about over the surface sf some huge can vas in his large studio on the Rue Bay ard, and at night he came home to earn his bread by the work he despised, though the public considered that his sole tue to glory. He worked half the night, and he was up early the next morning to paint once more. His want of rest, hls prodig- ious energy. must have shortened his life, | but the Chief thing that shortened IL w.s sorrow. No one could doubt that for a moment at the sight of him. He had heavy, anxious look. All the old boy ish beauty of his face, which lasted long after he became man, was gone: the smooth plump cheeks seemed 10 be fall ing with their own weight the corners of the mouth went down. His talk was worse than his look he was absence of mind personhed. He could hardly an- swer you he was too much absorbed Tn his own grief. There was no human be- i ing who had less concern in the world than this man who lived by illustrating everything in II. He was the most pro- digious worker of this or any other time. His original drawings were to be counted by the thousand. They were ali wrong in costume or local color, but in the feel ing of the situation they were unsurpassed. He stood m abou; the same relationship To accuracy ct historical observations as Victor Hugo. and indeed he was a TO- mantle of the pencil, and was altogether out of his element if this our poor day of\n\n \n\n the medium of his wonderful masination. and this. which Was his success. has also ln measure been ms ruin. for he saw his own achievements and his own fame III the same way; and the sight was a l misery that weighed him down. IL be- came towards the last something of an insult to remind him that he had done good illustrations he would look at you ! <.ft th ~f see If you were simply stupid or if you were laughing in your sleeves, and would then lead you up to one of his paintings and wait for the verdict like a shy boy. If you knew anything at all of painting you could give but one answer. His pictures went into the Salon because the Salon could not refuse them-he was h0ys eon6oHrs. but no one looked at them. They found market, however, as things to exhibit in London, where all i the country parsons took them in very good faith indeed and led their children by the hand to see the judgments of scrip ture illustrated to the life by this gifted Frenchman. There was plenty of red in the = Dream of Pilates Wife\" if there was nothing else. and the Destruction of the Heather Gods by Triumphant Christian ity' was wonderful show of fantastic invention. though the eo.oring and the whole treatment put it clean into the cat egoy Of merely decorative art-it was scene painting, nothing more. Tenieui- Der Dore when he was young and joyous. and think once described his life al that time in ,ie HorM He lived with his mother until she died and after that he still occupied the quaint old family house in the Rue S,. Dominique in which he had passed 50 many happy years. He never married: he once had consuming tenderness for Patti and in being disap- pointed of her never cared about another woman. but his affection for his mother must have had much to do with his life of cellbacy. She always treated him as I great boy. The relations between them were something beautiful to see; he was not only gay but larkish\" when she was by. and she used to check him with d Hush. Gustaye. Be quiet, Gustase,\" as though he were still on her knee. He was seen at his best on Sunday evening, the day of his informal reception. First there used to be dinner in the extraor- dlnary dining-room that you reached from the door of the apartment without the ghost Of an ante-chamter then after din- her fiends dropped m and everybody went into the large studio beyond Mime Dores drawlng-room. Here it was the lrishman : Liberty Hall everybody did as he liked and those who would not do II were made. Dore hddled. sang, stood or chairs and played practical jokes with his friends. fasano, the opaniard, would sing too then one of the Dore brothers played. Sometimes Munkaczy would drop in. al that time very secondary light in art compared with Dore. can distinctly remember thinking it was kind of Dore to receive him. But now, of course, Munkaczy is the acknowledged master of all contemporary art. His as- founding success must have had some thing te do with Dore's disappointment What he Is Dore had intended to be, and the sight of him was a continual memo rial of frustrated hopes This, think, \n\n=== 11_1894-12-10_p1_sn83030214_00175038759_1894121001_0608.json\nNew-York tribune. [volume] (New York [N.Y.]) 1866-1924 · 1894-12-10\ncrops: faro_1894/1894-12-10_p10_sn83030214_00175038759_1894121001_0608.json: the article's words aren't in its boxes\nheadline: EUROPEAN PRODUCE MARKETS\n---\nLiverpool. Dec. $. 1880 p. m.-Bccf-Fxra India mesa\na.n at Ills 81; prime mess firm at SIS ID Pork-Prms\nmess Western fine steady COS do MEDIUM steady a,\nl5s ID Hams-Short cut. about II n 6 -. dull \"a\nBacon-Cumberlani cut about IS q 30 10. steady at as\nshort RIB - H. steady 2is; long clear midtss\nabot q, r. steady AT 35s long and short clear nidss\nabout - H. firm .537 81. shoulders. about 2 10 IA\nn steady .ss Cheese American finest white colored\nsteady az 5l. Tallow-i'rime city. nominal Cottonseed\no-Iiverpo0 relied lss 81. Spirits Of lrnen,lne\nsteady 2Ds Cd Rosil-COmmOn. firm az IS lmsi. Lard\n\n\nPrime Western spot. steady az 3Gs 81 Wheat-No red\nwinter dull - ed: No red spring. stock Flour\n\n\n81 Louis fancy winter steady at IS corn-Mxed West\n\n\nspot fra az IS I'D December firm az - I'D January\nqsy Is Gi1 February easy As Gi,d. Hops Lon.\nlou Pacific Coast steady AT g 5s9f2 lbs\n\n=== 6_1921-12-31_pna_sn82014519_00414218945_1921123101_0676.json\nNew Britain herald. [microfilm reel] (New Britain, Conn.) 1890-1976 · 1921-12-31\ncrops: cached\nheadline: WEIRD FIGURE\n\n\nCHASES WOMEN\n\nQuartette Of Females Flee LeapinS\n\"GhOSt\" ON Cheshire Road\n---\n\n\n\nCheshire, Dec, 3l,-Four women\nwhose homes are in southineton, were\nchased by what was either a ghost\nor possibly member Of the i'll,\nfamed Ku KluX Khan, but probably a\npractical Joker in Cheshire Thursday\nnight, as they were returning towards\nn trolley station after spending the\nevening at the home OF a friend near\nthe Cheshire Reformatory.\n\n\nThe affair has caused much eX-\nelement among the women OF the\nnelghborhood and has caused the hus-\nbands and male members of the WON\nems families many moments of anger\nMt the boldness of the person who\ncommitted the act.\n\n\nGhost Appears\n\n\nThe incident happened on a lonely\nroad just off the turnpike where the\ntrail Is bordered on one side by a\nlong and dark stretch of woods and\non the other by an old barn where, 1t\nis said, few months ago, a man was\nkilled by an explosion. The women\nconcerned claim that on the way\ndown, they noticed a man lettering\nabout the barn, but paid no attention\nto him. On the return trip, however,\nthe same person. as they suppose,\nwith a white sheet covering him from\nhead to foot came out and with long\ngrotesque leaps and not a sound fol-\nbowed them up the road.\n\n\nWeird Appearance\n\n\nFrightened, tn women fastened\nIheir steps, but as fast as they would\ngo, the faster the figure would come.\nIn fact he approached to within four\nyards of the quartet and one of them\nholder perhaps than the others,\nflashed on him a searchlight which she\nwas carrying in an attempt to scare\nhim away. It was noticed then that\nthe mysterious thing had a mask\nwhich was painted in the most weird\nand fantastic manner, and this Of\ncourse, only served to increase the\nfright of the women.\n\n\nWomen Get Help\n\n\nFinally they began to run, and the\nghost was right after them, waving\nIt's arms and leaping more wildly then\never. The race continued until finally,\nthe panic stricken women sighted a\nmouse ny the roadside all IN dark\nness. Stumbling and screaming they\nbegan to pound upon the door cry\ning for help. A man came to the\ndoor in his night shirt, carrying a\ndouble barrelled shot gun in the crook\nof his arm. 1t took some time for\nthe women to recover enough to tell\nthe story, but when the man found out\nwhat the trouble was, he hastily\ndressed and with mutterings of the\nKu Klux Klan, left the house.\n\n\nGhost Disappears\n\n\nHe made thorough search of the\nneighborhood, but the ghost\" or\nwhatever it was, had disappeared and\nno trace was found of him. After q\ntime, the man of the hour escorted\nthe women down the road to the troll\nley car and saw them safely on their\nway. s\n\n=== 9_1919-07-09_p1_sn89081128_0028076677A_1919070901_0223.json\nNew Ulm review. (New Ulm, Brown County, Minn.) 1892-1961 · 1919-07-09\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Sir Redvers Buffers Ghost Story.\n---\nSome time after the Fra German\nwar of 1870, writes Lady Butler in an\nEnglish paper, Sir Redyers (then Cap\ntain) Butler left England to visit the\nbattlefelds, and on arriving at the\ntown Of gave directions that his\nletters should not be forwarded, and\nstarted on his tour Of inspection.\n\n\nAfter he had been away some days, he\n.aWoke zsudd enly one night, thinking\nhe saw Lord Wolseley (then Sir Gar\nmetz, and that he heard him say ~\nwonder where that fellow Butler is.\ncan't think why he has not answered\n\n\nmy letter This so impressed him.\nthat he returned at once to the town\nof 49,, where he found a letter await:\ning him from Lord Wolseley, saying\nthat he must return to London imme-,\ndiately as an expedition against the\n\n\nAshantees was imminent\n\n=== 13_1920-06-11_pna_sn88084272_00294553484_1920061101_0531.json\nEl Paso herald. (El Paso, Tex.) 1901-1931 · 1920-06-11\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\none hose. and the knees Of hubby's\ntrousers.\n\n\n\nAfter a few years OF married life\nno woman needs ouija board to tell\nher exactly what a man is going 10\ndo OF say next.\n\n\n\nWhy do some husbands regard the\nwedding bell as the signal 10 stop\nOffering woman sweets, compli-\nments and kisses. and 10 begin offer\ning her roast beef. advice and criti-\ncism?\n\n\n\nWhen two artistic souls marry. the\ndevil usually takes a malicious de-\nlight in adding a touch OF birthstone\nto the match\"\n\n\nr\n\n\nNOnsensei A man doesn't lose\nall his sentiment after marriage.\nII merely gets Bmothered under a\n\n=== 18_1921-09-06_p7_sn82014519_0041421891A_1921090601_0060.json\nNew Britain herald. · 1921-09-06\ncrops: cached\nheadline: TWO PERSONS HURT, IN MORNING CRASH\n\nMeriden Folks Come to Grief on\n\n WorthingtOn Ridge,\n\nDRIVERS JAW FRACTURED\n\nState Cops Patrol for Hours-Repub- \n\n Iicnn Caucus Call Issucd-Town Schools Reopen Tomorrow Morning g -other Items of Interest in Berlin.\n---\nMrs Emma Lamont and Ralph Turner Of Pratt street. Meriden. are in th. Britain hospital suffering from I...uries received when a Buick touring car in which they were driv- ing. crashed into a tree on worthing- ton Ridge at Q o'clock this morning. Mrs. Lamont is suffering from a frac- ture of the jaw and Turner is more seriously injured. suffering from a fracture of the skull The two were attended by Dr. T. c. Hodsson who ordered their immediate removal to the New Britain institution.\n\n A broken steering wheel was the cause Of the accident which happened an eighth of \\ mile south of the Berlin Town Hall. The story told by witnesses is to the effect that Tur- ner was driving at A slow rate of speed when tho accident happened, otherwise deaths would have resulted in the crash. Two cars passed the Turner car and a truck also passed, all the drivers turning out to pass the Meriden car because of its slow rate of speed.\n\n Without warning tho car sudden iy swerved from the roadway and Jumping over the roadbed at the side of the highway crashed head-on into tree. The car was wrecked. The engine was pushed back under the front seat, the rear seat was buckied up as SO much tin. the front mud guards were bent and torn, the head lights smashed. and the fan and radiator reduced to junk. Mir. Tur- ner was thrown over the broken steering wheel and suffered n slight ..oncussion of the brain as well as Inc fracture of the skull. Mrs. La nlont suffered her injuries in an neroic effort to save little Margaret 1rner from serious injury. Mrs. Ls- m'Int was sitting In the back seat VI th a Mrs. Grove also of Meriden. she jumped to save the girl and in 10 doing was thrown over the front sest and her face forcefully pushed against the dashboard. Her jaw was frastured, her face cut. and several teeth were knocked out. Mrs. Grove faini ed and took some time for re- vivai The little girl suffered several minor cuts and n general shock.\n\n At the hospital IL was reported that nofn would recover. The car was towed to n garage.\n\n State Cops Busy.\n\n surday and Monday were busy days Tor Ire state cops who kept n vigtl- ant watch over the newly laid ce- ment highway which runs through this Own on the pike between Hart ford ,nd New Haven. The state men were on the Job until II o'clock last night and in that time were kept busy ,yt,h vtoIatora\n\n One i. rr..t was reported this morn Ing in x,hIrb Inc driver wa. charged wnn fsckless driving and excessive speed. The mans name was not known by Judge Griswoid but he VIII apiear in court tonight to am swer to ,hA rb,rwea\n\n larIa runher or autotata were stopped \" the state men for having headlight an, of order ar in viola ton or ho state laws in regard to NKnkNz wx.nmf rumam hM penula \\\n\n=== 10_1878-03-07_p4_sn84027691_0023728334A_1878030701_0641.json\nThe Leavenworth weekly times. · 1878-03-07\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\na SsaFsling ltssle Episode which n\n\n\nclaAes . Real Ghess-Fact, and n0s\n\n\nFicsioa.\n\n\nThis is a real ghost story which is about\nto be related, an actual occurrence, founded\non stern facts, the work of neither disor-\ndered fancy, wild imagination nor overbur-\ndened stomach, but something which act\nually transp;red, and, while the reporter\ndid not hear it from the actual participator\nin the ghos.ly seance, he has it from au-\nthority which is unimpeachable. Some\ntune ago, and not 90 long = time either, q\nlady from the East was visiting at the resi-\ndsnce of q well-known family here. The\nAforesaid residence is not haunted, at least\nnever was before, Its visitants having always\nbeen of the real flesh and blood order, and\nNO spiritual manifestations having ever\ntaken place there before. 1t was in\n\n\nTHE DEAD OF NIGHT,\n\n\nthat time VIXEN ghosts and spirits most like\nto provl, when this beautiful vision appear\ned. 1t vas none Of your skull and crosl\nbone kind, but lovely lady dressed in the\nheight Of fashion (ghoss fashion oi\noourse) as though just having\ncone from . ball a party givsa,\n\n=== 29_1903-12-26_p12_sn85066387_00100480475_1903122601_0520.json\nThe San Francisco call. · 1903-12-26\ncrops: cached\nheadline: SPIRITUALISM.\n---\nPROF. Fred P Evans, noted psychic for slate\nwriting and clairvoyance; stamp for circular.\nI112 Eddy St nr. Octavia: phone Scott SS7-\nEDWARD EARLE 1852 Ellis readings daily,\n30 to 4. seances Sunday and Thursday.\n\n=== 54_1867-05-10_p1_sn83045232_00295878794_1867051001_0839.json\nThe Daily clarion. [volume] (Jackson, Miss.) 1866-1888 · 1867-05-10\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nRegistration of voters will very shortly\n| commence in the county of Adams The\n\n\nfollowing gentlemen have, we learn, beer\nappointed Registrars; Dr. II. Sellers, and\n'Messrs, I. p. Sears, and Jas; Dixon. From\n\n\nwhat we know and learn of these gentlemen\n\n\nwe believe their appointments will give\ngeneral satisfaction under all the circnm\n\n\nstances. We question not that they will\n\n\nendeavor to discharge their difficult duty, in\n\n\nfair and just manner. Dr. Sellers has been\n\n\nwell and favorably known In the communi-\n\n\n\" for long time; and the other officers\n\n\nnamed are spoken of in high terms\n.Natchez Courier.\n\n\nWe are gratified to receive similar\naccounts to the foregoing from other\nquarters. Gen. Ord's appointments\nindicate his intention to have the law\nexecuted fairly according to its letter\nand spirit, and not in the interest of\nany particular party or faction He\nnot permitted to appoint Registrars\nwho cannot take the. prescribed oatl !,\nbat among the class who can, will be\nfound persons in every county who\nwill decide justly upon the qualifca-\ntons of voters under the law. Com\nPEELED to rely upon representations of\nothers in many instances, he may\nsometimes be deceived into the ap-\npointment ofmere partizans but these\nwe are sure will be exceptions to the\ngeneral rule. Our people. only ask\nscrupulous conformity to the law.\nThey wish the business to be well and\ndone, SO that when it is done,\nthere will be no ground for cavil as to\ntheir right to representation and\nequality of privilege with the most\nfavored States.\n\n=== 13_1921-10-07_pna_sn89058253_00415621991_1921100701_1148.json\nThe Fayette falcon. (Somerville, Tenn.) 190?-current · 1921-10-07\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\n\n\n\npearls. Wrap them up inside the sill\nyer and gold\" Aloofly he watched\nthe process. Nov advance alone, and\nopen the box. Place the pearls in\nside. Now shut and lock It.\" 0bedi-\nentity the duchess did as she was bid !:\nthen she stood waiting for further In\nstructions.\n\n\nBut apparently by this time the\nGreat Brooding Spirit was beginning\nto take effect. Singing a monotonous.\nharsh chant. the Indian knelt on the\nfloor, and poured some powder into\na little brazler. He was still close\nto the open window, and finally he sat\ndown with his elbows ON his knees,\nand his head rocking to and fro in\nhis hands.\n\n\nLESS light-less lightr The words\nseemed to come from a great distance\n-ventriloqulsm in a mild way was\none of Lakington's accomplishments I;\nand as the lights went out greenish.\nspluttering flame rose from the brazicr\nA heavy, odorous smoke filed the\nroom, but framed and motionless In\nthe eerie light sat the Indian. staring\nfxedly in front of him. After a time\nthe chant began again; It grew and\nswelled in volume till the singer grew\nfrenzied and beat his head with his\nhands. Then abruptly II stopped.\n\n\n\"Place the box upon the floor\" he\nordered, ''in the light of the Sacred\nFire.\" Hugh saw the duchess kneel\ndown on the opposite side Of the\nbrazier. and place the box on the floor,\nwhile the faces Of the guests-strange\nand ghostly in the great llght-peered\nlike specters out OF the heavy smoke.\nThis was undoubtedly a show worth\nwatching.\n\n\n\"Open the box\" Harshly the words\nrang through the silent room, and with\nfingers that trembled a little the duch-\ness turned the key and threw back\nthe lid.\n\n\n\"Why, it's empty!\" she cried in\namazement, and the guests craned for\nward to look.\n\n\n\"Put not your hand inside\" cried\nthe Indian in sudden warning, \"or per-\nchance 1t will remain empty\n\n\nThe duchess rapidly withdrew her\nhand, and stared incredulously through\nthe smoke at his impressive face\n\n\n'Did not say that there was power\nIn the box?\" he said dreamily. \"The\npower TO render invisible--the power\nto render visible. Thus came pro-\nlection to the jewels of the Chow\nqueens\"\n\n\nThat's all right, Mr.. Ram OAR\"\nsaid the duchess little apprehens-\nlively. \"There may be power in the\nbox, but my pearls don't seem to be-\"\n\n\nThe Indian laughed.\n\n\n\"None but you has touched the cab\nmet, your Grace; none but you must\ntouch it till the pearls return. They\nare there now; but not for mortal\neyes to see.\"\n\n\nWhich, Incidentally, was no more\nthan the truth.\n\n\n\"Look, oh sahibs, look; but do not\ntouch. See that to your vision the box\nis empty. :\" He waited notion\nless, while the guests thronged round,\nwith expressions of Amusement; and\nHugh. safe from view In the thick,\nunderstanding smoke, came even near\ner In bis excitement.\n\n\nat is enough\" cried the Indian\nsuddenly. \"Slnit the box. your grace.\nand lock II as before. Now place II\non the table whence 1t came. Is 1t\nthere?\"\n\n\n\"Yes.\" The duchess voice came out\nof the green fog.\n\n\ngo mot too near he continued\nwarniEely. \"The gods must have\nspace-the gods must have space\"\n\n\nAgain the harsh chant began. at\ntimes swelling to a shout. at times\ndying away TO a whisper. And II was\nduring one of these latter periods that\nlow laugh. Instantly checked, dis\nturbed the room. II was plainly audi\nBILE, and someone irritably said. \"Be\nquiet\" II was not repeated. which\nafforded Hugh, at any rate. no sur\nprise. For 1t had been Trina Peterson\nwho had laughed. ard 1t might have\nbeen hilarity, or Il might have been \\\na signal.\n\n\n\"Bring the box, your grace\" he cried\nharshly. and once more the duchess\nknelt In the circle of light. with a\nrow Of dimly seen faces above her.\n\n\n''0pen; but as you value your pearls\ntouch them not\" Excitedly she\nthrew back the lid. and chorus of\ncries greeted the appearance OF the\ngold and silver tissue at the bottom of\nthe box.\n\n\nThey're here. Mr.. Ram Oar\"\n\n\nIn the green light the Indians SON.\nDer eyes stared round the group Of\ndim faces.\n\n\nlTo BE coNTINUEDJ\n\n\n\n\n=== 26_1885-12-18_pna_sn84023416_00202197504_1885121801_1036.json\nEssex County herald. [volume] (Guildhall, Vt.) 1873-1964 · 1885-12-18\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nthankfulness towards the bounteous\nGlver of all perfect gifts. Well, the\nday had come and many were merry\nand doubtless some were sad. 1t\nhad come, l say, and was swiftly\ndrawing to its close.\n\n\nIn a city near the Kaatskills ev-\nerything was hustle and confusion.\nFamilies were gathered around their\nfiresides. Busy throngs of people |\nhurried hither and thither and un-\nnoticed wanderers loitered on the\nstreet corners. In a room lighted\nonly by the glow of the fire, right\nin the heart of the city, figure sat\ndreamily looking into the fire. The\nloud click of the horses shoes on\nthe finty pavement grated harshly\nupon his ear and the clang of horse\ncar bells sounded strangely unnat-\nural.\n\n\nLet us glance inside the room a\nmoment. On one side the fire is a\ncase of books; on the other stands a\ntable with writing materials upon it.\nStuffed birds, autumn leaves and\n! bits of rocks give evidence that the\nroom is occupied by one who goes\nunwillingly from the haunts of ma\nture. Above the mantle the picture\nof an intelligent bird dog hangs\nrevealed in the dim light; on the\nmat before the fire the form of the\nsame dog is worked in brown and\nwhite, and how | wish l could tell\nyou the dog himself sat there by\nhis master, but no, his resting place\nupon the shore of q distant noun\ntain lake is marked by tree famil-\nlar only to him who placed it there.\nBut l forget myself. must return\nto the form before the fire.\n\n\nFor a moment an expression of\nmerriment crept over his face as he\nsat looking at an ungainly bird that\nseemed peering comically down at\nhim from out the darkness, but as\nhis eyes rested on the picture of the\ndog the'smile left his lips. For a time\n= he gazed intently at the picture anc\n\n\nthen at the mat and finally his eyes\nclosed and an expression of deer\nthoughtfulness came over his feat\nures. Now can you guess who thi:\nhuman being is that sits thus OF\n! Christmas night in the noisy city\nIs it Karl Henry? Why no, Thu\n\n\ncannot be he, for Karl Henry il\nl among the Green Mountains, fa\n\n\naway from the busy city. He il\n\n\nwith the friends of his youth in thu\nlodge among the blrches at his ma\n\n\ntive town.\n\n\n! And the old lodge fairly resound\n! with merriment, for \"the gang\" il\nunbroken numbers has met there OF\nthis Christmas night. They have\nJust arrived, fire is started, tn,\n\n\ngenerous repast is spread upon thu\n\n\ntable and they have sat down, be\n:. fore commencing the feast, to Sing\nthe old familiar song, Gathering\nUp the Shells by the Seashore,\n\n\nStanley accompanying the piece\nwith the banjo. Phllis there carry\n\n\ning his part in his usual thorough\nmanner but to the apparent peril Of\nhis constitution. Then there i,\n\n\nJohn who sings with an air Of\nsmoothness that plainly tells you he\n\n\ncan keep right along where he il\nuntil daybrcak if necessary. And\n\n\nthere, too, is Karl Henry carrying\nthe bass part, and base part it is\n\n\nindeed; he has learned the conclud\n\n\ning note of each verse and he comes\nin on that with such power that you\nat once feel convinced that he is in\nevery sense of the word base\nsinger. As the song is finished and\nStanley \"hangs up de ole banjo\n\n\nall attention is directed toward Phil\n\n\nthe punster, who is about to \"shlo[\nagain\" over Johns call for bouquets\n\n\nRising deliberately, he thus responds\n\n\nto the call: eyes, here is Stanley's\n\n\nmaple sugar, from Clarke's, which\n\n\nwill go splendidly on fapjacks, and\ntherefore | feel highly honored III\n\n\nbeing the representative chosen tc\npresent you with this flower flour\ndish\" OF course John does not\ngive way, and the hot griddle is\nsoon busy, but is not soon to rest\n\n\nfor Stanley and Karl Henry are\n\n\nhungry, and even Phil condescends\nto take a bite tonight. But al\n\n\nlength Phil cries, ''for mercy's sake\n\n\nJack, don't flap another cake on tc\n\n=== 16_1876-11-16_p1_sn86053968_00383343781_1876111601_0076.json\nThe Brookhaven ledger. (Brookhaven, Miss.) 1875-1883 · 1876-11-16\ncrops: cached\nheadline: For the Brookhaven Ledger.l\n\n\nTHE GHOSTS OF THE YEAR.\n\n\n---\nBIZ L. H. N.\n\n\nHow did get there? How should 1\nknow? How does anybody get into\nfairyland, or any other such place?\nSuffice it to say that at almost midnight\nupon the last night of the old year l\nstood alone in the weirdest place that\never was haunted by ghost or goblin.\n1t WAS a great echoing cavern, down in\nthe heart of the earth. The sides re-\nceded into the gloom on every hand\nand the lofty roof bent in starry dome\nabove. The wallis were covered with\nquaint records of the struggles and tri-\numphs of almost six thousand years,\nand on every hand were the relics of\nmany bygone age. A rich, mellow\nlight streamed from great golden\nchandelier that might have been\nwrought under the eye of some beauty\nloving Egyptian monarch. While I\nstood wrapped in it's radiance, faint\ntinkling as of elfin bells, sounded above\nmy head. I looked up and saw fa-\nmiliar figure descending flom the dome.\n\n\nHe whistied shrill tune as he rode\ndown on fierce wind. Such on un-\ncanny, wrinkled little old man, with\nicicles clinging to his beard, his cap\nand his huge fur coat! He roughly\ntweaked his knobby nose, as he whis>\ntied and stamped his foot. At the sig\nnal thousand sprite-like forms few\nfrom thousand hiding places and as\nsembled around the cross little old\nman.\n\n\nWhen be had scanned his vassals\nclosely and begun to talk in thin, icy\nvoice, who would not have recognized\nthe ghost of Winter?\n\n\n\"You are oil here\" he snarled. \"Of\ncourse you are whenever nobody wants\nyou. What do you come to me for?\nYou know already what to do. Jack\nFrost, is that you? Well, you have a\ngreat deal to do. Creep into every place\nthat will hold yon. Nip all the flowers\nthat you can find. won't have flowers\nin winter. Consumption. 60 on as\nusual. Keep your victims in long\nline marching to the graveyard. Ha,\nha! I can hear their lean voices chatter\ning together ~ they go. Now isn't it\nmusic?\" and he gave q little fiendish\nlaugh. North Wind. West Wind.\nblow your hardest, and sing hoarse,\nwild song around The houses in yonr\nway. And you, Clouds, keep off the\nsunshine. If people will have some fun\nout of us with their skating and sleigh\nbells, pinch their poses and toes for it. !\"\n\n\n=== 42_1923-08-13_p1_sn84026749_00222254024_1923081301_0041.json\nThe Washington times. · 1923-08-13\ncrops: faro_1923/1923-08-13_p19_sn84026749_00222254024_1923081301_0041.json: too few words to check\nheadline: RAPPINGS.\n---\nNip-Heard two chaps arguing\nyesterday about departed spirits.\nzip-Bet they mentioned cid\nJohn Barleycorn.\n\n=== 7_1832-06-12_pna_sn83025561_00514153139_1832061201_0294.json\nThe Rhode-Island Republican. [volume] (Newport, R.I.) 1809-1841 · 1832-06-12\ncrops: cached\nheadline: MAD TOMS SONG.\n---\n\nThe groat round moon !--Tu whit tn whoo\nrido on its rim when I've nothing to do\nride on its rim and sail away.\nAnd dash off the stairs from its sides like\n\n\nspray!\n\n\nhigh, 80\nAnd the ships ola nation went tumbling by\nDid you hear the cries Of tho soamen bold\nDid you hear the squsaking Of rats IN the hold\n| But what is voyage along tho sea,\n| To titing thro all the sky with me,\nOver the clouds an the rainbow's rim,\nOver the tops Of the seraphim?\n| The great round moon tu-whit tu.whoo\n| When there's frost in the air, her nose looks\nblue,\n| Her nose looks blue aud her cheeks looks red,\nAnd her eyes come starting half out OF her head\nYet better che loves the frosty night.\nWhen icicles round her are clanking bright,\nAnd jinging like bells az she journeys oil,\nThan sky made warm by the summer sun.\nBetter loves she the snow and tho hail,\nYelling tho earth with their gossamer veil,\nThan the Haunting flowers Of the rosy spring,\nThat lift up their hoads TO the sua--thoir\n\n\nking\n\n\nA way away before the wind\nTho long tailed comet far behind;\nAnd the tract that is left by our silver car\nIs bright as the trail Of q shooting star\nThe great round moon ,tu.whit tn. whoo\nl ride on her rim whom I've nothing to do;\nride on hor rim, and laugh as I 60,\nAt oil that is puszling the earth below.\nMen fattor lordling who comes into place,\nJust 90 soc planet extingushed in space ;\nMen weep DER score that have porished in\nfight,\n\n\nJust a. soc world emerging to light.\nII they rode on the moon thro' the boundless\nblue,\n\n\nThey would Join my chorus-tu-whit tu.whoo\nThey would alter their notions Of virtue and\nsin,\n\n\nAnd weigh against their world the head Of q\n| nm!\n\n=== 9_1938-09-22_pna_sn87075008_00340580047_1938092201_0879.json\nMorgan County Democrat. (McConnelsville, Ohio) 1886-1943 · 1938-09-22\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Worshipping The One God\n---\nCoadaism is a combination relig\non, including in it's worship not only\nBuddah, CONTIGUOUS, Laotse, but al\nSO Christ. Its leaders have taken\nquotations from many religious\nbooks, including the Bible, to make\nup their liturgy, and there is a spir-\nitistic element in their cult They\nuse of ouija board. Pictures of the\nEye, the symbol of the Great Intel\nligence, are sold to thousands for\nthirty-five cents, and are worshipped\nin the homes. 1t is religion of an\ncestor worship, of ritual and robes,\na formidable cult of 100000 people\nin Cochin, China, and an enemy and\ncompetitor Of Christian teaching.\n\n\nIn Turkey one of the higher Gov\nernment Schools has requested from\nthe Bible Society a small grant Of\nBibles for it's English Department\nTogether with these the Society in\ncluded copy of the Revised Turkish\nNew Testament. Later it was dis\ncovered that since it was put on the\nlibrary shelves, this New Testament\nhas been in almost constant circula-\nton among the students, and that\nthere was small waiting list OF\nthose who had signed for the Book\non its return.\n","numLines":1313,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1313}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e3603922-ea4c-4349-b97c-04787bf989f0","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"other"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2c-reader","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
