{"parentUuid":"efd096e4-cdc0-4bba-b868-18c4ba794cc8","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"775e54ef-6487-4f79-8598-643421b87718","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XHM2mmHnDpgevxVtXvBovF","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t=== 4_1918-10-01_pna_sn89055128_0041421146A_1918100101_1006.json\n2\tThe daily star-mirror. (Moscow, Idaho) 1911-1939 · 1918-10-01\n3\tcrops: cached\n4\theadline: PHRENOLOGIST\n5\tSPIRIT MEDIUM\n6\t---\n7\tThe great World Knowing\n8\tAstrologist Spirit Medium, anc\n9\tExpert Phrenologist. Gifted from\n10\tchildhood, and with a lifetime\n11\tpractice, now is ready to serve all\n12\twho will call upon him, at his pri-\n13\tvate room.\n14\t\n15\t\n16\tWithout knowing who you are,\n17\tor whence you came, this Hindoo\n18\tfrom Calcutta, closes his. eyes and\n19\ttells you everything. No matter\n20\twhat your trouble, or what you\n21\twant to know, he will give you\n22\tdates, facts, names, and advice\n23\tupon all undertakings.\n24\t\n25\t\n26\tCall and be convinced, twill be\n27\ta dollar we'll spent. Whether old\n28\tor young, have your head read and\n29\tknowing myself\" and for what\n30\tyou are adapted. Appointments\n31\tmade by telephone. No. TOJ.\n32\tRates S1.0O and up. Office hours,\n33\tweek days 1900 a. m. to 500 p. M;\n34\tSundays, 1000 a. m. to 400 p. m.\n35\tWill be here until the 9Th of Oc-\n36\ttober, Moscow, Idaho.\n37\t\n38\t\n39\tOne and one-half blocks outside\n40\tcity limits, south of Main St.,\n41\thouse on right hand side, 2 STORY\n42\tpainted white, away from road,\n43\tsign on door, across railroad track\n44\tand bridge. 303314\n45\t\n46\t=== 12_1868-09-12_p1_sn83016025_00279525267_1868091201_0167.json\n47\tThe Portland daily press. · 1868-09-12\n48\tcrops: cached\n49\theadline: VarietieS.\n50\t---\n51\twearing curious and Characteristic dresses\n52\tswan from the Oberbaum down to General\n53\tWon Ftuels river baths, a distance of about five\n54\thundred yards. When they had arrived Il\n55\tthe latter place they performed various leats of\n56\t\n57\t\n58\tstrength and agility in the water, to the de-\n59\t\n60\t\n61\tlight of vast crowd of spectators. A great\n62\t\n63\t\n64\tpart of the effect was produced by the quaint\n65\t\n66\t\n67\tness of the costumes. The first company rep-\n68\t\n69\t\n70\tresented \"the ladies\" and wore chignons and\n71\t\n72\t\n73\tlittle Paris hats, and carried parasols. There\n74\t\n75\t\n76\twere two companies of negroes, and a very\n77\tnumerous one of red Indians, with their bod\n78\tles painted and their faces tattooed, and wear\n79\ting head-dresses and feathers. Besides these\n80\tthere were swans, river gods, Chinese, dandies\n81\tand various other .characters. One of the\n82\tswimmers at the head of the procession per-\n83\tformed very difficult feat of playing the ma\n84\ttiOnal air, \"Ich bin Elm Prusse,\" Q am a Prus-\n85\tsian) on the horn. The yf5te lasted an hour,\n86\tand passed off without accident.\n87\t\n88\t\n89\tLookout Mountain is crumbling, and the\n90\tneighbors fear that some internal forces are at\n91\twork. One tive-hundred ton fragment tumb-\n92\tled from near the top, the other day.\n93\t\n94\t\n95\tr. w. Emerson is soon to commence in\n96\tBoston course OF six lectures the subjects to\n97\tbe as follows: Art\" t;\"\"Poetry and Criticism\n98\tDoctrine of Leasts and Mosts;\" Historical\n99\tNotes of American Life and Letters\" \"Hos-\n100\tpitahty, and How to Make Homes Attrac-\n101\ttive\" Greatness\"\n102\t\n103\t\n104\tThe real founder of the present Swedish\n105\tdynasty has just died at Stockholm. Count\n106\tOtto Won M0rner was born in 1881, aud during\n107\this long life of eighty-seven years took very\n108\tactive part in the politics of his country. In\n109\t1910, on the death of Charles Augustus, Count\n110\tWon M0rDer went to Paris and offered the Swe-\n111\tdish crown to Marshal Bernadotte. The Mar\n112\tshal then presented himself as a candidate for\n113\telection as Crown Prince, and Count Won\n114\tMorner labored so zealously in his behalf that\n115\tthe election resulted in his favor, notwith-\n116\tstanding the ill-concealed opposition of the\n117\tKing and his Court. Ultimately BernadOtte\n118\twas crowned king as Charles John of Sweden,\n119\tand Count Won M6rner was appointed his\n120\t\n121\t\n122\t\n123\tThe death of Christian Fredrick Schon-\n124\tbein, OF Basle, on Sunday last, removes one OF\n125\tthe most eminent chemists in the world from\n126\tthe useful labors Of his most practical science.\n127\tHe was the discoyerer of ozone, and the in\n128\tventor of gun-cotton, which, though useless\n129\tfor purposes of war, yet dissolved in ether be-\n130\tcame, under the name of collodion, valuable\n131\taid in photography and in medicine. He was\n132\tthe author of several chemical treatises, was\n133\tthe friend of Faraday, and at the time of his\n134\tdeath had almost completed his sixty-ninth\n135\tyear.\n136\t\n137\t\n138\tRev. DIR. Stebbins says it is an insult to\n139\tthe memory of Washington, and shame to\n140\tthe nation, that Mt. Vernon is so mismanaged,\n141\tand by rebel spirits, too. Everything is in the\n142\tmost slovenly, tumble-down condition imagin-\n143\table. 1t is owned by an association of ladies',\n144\tof which Miss Pamela Cunningham is Presi-\n145\tdent. She lives in the mansion, and shows\n146\tneither the ability nor disposition to take care\n147\tOF it.\n148\t\n149\t\n150\tThe Journal of Chemistry has long arti.\n151\tGle on Planchette, in which the editor express\n152\tes the belief shat there is one power, impulse,\n153\tor force in nature, regarding the character of\n154\twhich mankind are totally in the dark\"\n155\t\n156\t\n157\tJohn Aliens pious influence is being felt\n158\tIn his Immediate neighborhood. The Tribune\n159\tsays that Tommy Haddan, who is claimed by\n160\tthe sailors to be a wickeder man than John\n161\tAllen ever was; and Sojer (soldier) Brown,\n162\twho is wickeder than Tommy Haddan; and\n163\tKit Burns, who is wickeder than Sojer Brown,\n164\thave all signified their willingness to have\n165\tprayer-meetings in their respective dens al\n166\tsoon as they can get them in order for that\n167\tpurpose. The Tribune does not say that any\n168\tone of these gentry has offered to go to work\n169\tand earn an honest livelihood.\n170\t\n171\t\n172\tAT an intended wedding, just as the cere-\n173\tmonies were about to begin, the bride sudden\n174\tly called upon the bridegroom to abjure tobae-\n175\tCO, on pain of forfeiture of welded bliss, &C.,\n176\twhich he refused to do-then and there tax\n177\ting fresh quid from his tobacco box, to show\n178\this independence. WhereupOn the young la\n179\tdy took out dainty bex, took a chew\" /\" amid\n180\tthe applause of the guests, and ordered the\n181\tclergyman to go ahead. This was too much\n182\tfor the bridegroom, who fled the scene, leaving\n183\tthe girl triumphant with piece of liquorice\n184\tin her mouth.\n185\t\n186\t\n187\tLiverpool has a haunted house, the win\n188\tdows of which are broken by continuous\n189\tshowers of stones. The police can't discover\n190\tthe ghostly assailants.\n191\t\n192\t\n193\t-Ristori writes that she was surprised to\n194\tfind Americans so rich, and that this would\n195\tbe the finest country on earth but for its eli\n196\tmate.\n197\t\n198\t\n199\t\"A young New Orleans quadroon, Miss De-\n200\tlanney, is the most promising pupil in the Par\n201\tis Conseryatoire. Her voice is alto.\n202\t\n203\t\n204\tAWFUL death of twenty persons and =\n205\tpeer\" is the way London paper announced\n206\tthe Abergele accident.\n207\t\n208\t\n209\tMiss Edmonia Lewis, the colored sculptor,\n210\tnow in Rome, has had an order for a statue of\n211\tClio from Prince George of Prussia.\n212\t\n213\t\n214\t\"A recent Toronto paper contains the nGiue\n215\tanpouncement that \"the Rev. w. Morley\n216\tPunshon, having returned from his honey\n217\tmoon, will preach here next Sunday\"\n218\t\n219\t\n220\tThe influence of nineteenth century ideas\n221\thas reached even the Hapsburgs. The Arch\n222\tduke Albert was lately asked by priest to\n223\tgive money for masses for the Iepose OF Maxi\n224\tmilan's soul, when he replied-\"I wiz gladly\n225\tsubscribe to a collection in mo~.0ry OF my un~\n226\tfortunate brother, but shall insist on the\n227\tmoney being applied to pay his creditors,\n228\twhich appears to me the first thing to be\n229\tdoue.\"\n230\t\n231\t\n232\tA boy about five years old died in convul-\n233\tsions on Sunday, in New York, from drinking\n234\tBourbon whiskey. He had seen his father\n235\tdrink from the bottle during the day, and was\n236\ttrying to initiate him.\n237\t\n238\t\n239\tThe condition of the people on the Red\n240\tRiver of the North seems to be even worse\n241\tthan was at first reported. The st. Paul Press\n242\tsaws the case is Just this: \"Ten or twelve\n243\tthousand people, immured in distant region,\n244\tfour or five hundred miles beyond our western\n245\tfrontier, are the eve of starvation. The\n246\tgrasshoppers have utterly destroyed the crops\n247\tof the farmers, while, by an unprecedented\n248\tcombination Of misfortunes, the hunters, who\n249\tconstitute large part of the population, have\n250\treturned from the plains without their usual\n251\tsunplies of buffalo meat. While their wants\n252\tare tins pressing, the time within which they\n253\tcan be reached with relief from this quarter is\n254\tvery short. In few weeks, when the frost\n255\tshall have killed the grass on the praries, it\n256\twill be extremely difficult for the trains to\n257\treach there with supplies, for want of sub\n258\tsistence to feed the animals. Before Novem-\n259\tDer it is not improbable that these starving\n260\tpeople may be walled in by five hundred miles\n261\tof snow from any possible aid except what\n262\tmay dribble through on dog trains.\n263\t\n264\t\n265\tMics Blapche De Bat, a member of the\n266\t\n267\t\n268\tpo.t, family. granddaughter of j. b. Booth,\n269\t\n270\t\n271\t= onpounced to appear with Edwin Booth at\n272\t\n273\t\n274\t.ho National Theatre, Cincinnati, next week.\n275\t\n276\t\n277\tThose very accurate historians who are al\n278\tways hunting up facts, and who make such\n279\thavoc with old traditions, had no merits in tn o\n280\teyes OF Charles Lamb. II do not like\" said\n281\the, \"to have my old articles of faith destroyed ].\n282\tHere's some COMPOUNDED fellow published\n283\tnew Life of Shakespeare, in which he main\n284\ttlius that Cervantes and glorious Will dld not\n285\t\n286\t=== 4_1857-02-27_pna_sn84023127_00280776981_1857022701_0675.json\n287\tBurlington free press. [volume] (Burlington, Vt.) 1827-1865 · 1857-02-27\n288\tcrops: cached\n289\theadline: sDI ED.\n290\t---\n291\tAt his residence in Essex, Feb. 18th, Amos\n292\tBliss, aged 81 years.\n293\t\n294\t\n295\tIn Richmond, Feb. 9th, ls57,M Luther Prouty\n296\tOf Jer cio, aged 60 years lie was on visit to his\n297\tdaughter. lie left homo on Tuesday in his usual\n298\thealth. Thursdiy evening he was attacked with n\n299\tain in his side, which lasted short time, but\n300\ttired to rest free from pain. Upon entering his\n301\troom in the morning it was found his spirit had\n302\tde ptrted and he had entered into the Joy of his\n303\tLtrl, lie was kind husband, an indulgont paren\n304\tII loss will be deeply felt in his family. For the\n305\t,st six years he had suffered much with disease\n306\tof the eyes which affection he bore with Christian\n307\tpatience. He has resided in the town Of Jericho\n308\tAs years, and has been for the last Q years mem\n309\tDer Of the Baptist Church and a devoted Christian\n310\t\n311\t\n312\tMassachusetts, and Ohio papers please copy.\n313\t\n314\t\n315\tIn Randolph, Feb. :, Arlstus Clark, aged 70.\n316\t\n317\t\n318\tIn Barnard, Feb II, Mrs. DENIS Eaton, aged ca.\n319\t\n320\t\n321\tIn Mortified, Feb. 10, Hiram Kimbill, aged 40.\n322\t\n323\t\n324\tIn Rutland, Feb. 10, Minerva Bishop, wife of\n325\tAmos Weeks, aged 3j\n326\t\n327\t\n328\tIn Clar n on, Feb. JIG, Mrs A .Loantha Kinsman,\n329\taged - years.\n330\t\n331\t\n332\tIn East Franklin, Feb. 10, Capt. g. W. Kendall,\n333\taged 80.\n334\t\n335\t\n336\tIn Bridgcwatcr, John II. Thompson, aged II.\n337\t\n338\t=== 99_1898-01-07_p5_sn82015104_00280768790_1898010701_0061.json\n339\tThe record-union. · 1898-01-07\n340\tcrops: cached\n341\theadline: SPIRITUALISM.\n342\t---\n343\tMarE. JOHNSON. TEST AND BUSHES\n344\t\n345\t\n346\tmedium. 1410 Fifth street.\n347\t\n348\t=== 10_1848-08-15_p1_sn83030313_00271742988_1848081501_0201.json\n349\tThe New York herald. [volume] (New York [N.Y.]) 1840-1920 · 1848-08-15\n350\tcrops: cached\n351\theadline: THE VERY LATEST NEWS\n352\t\n353\t\n354\tFROM\n355\tALL PARTS OF EUROPE,\n356\t\n357\t\n358\tRECEIVED BY\n359\tELECTRIC TELEGRAPH AND SPECIAL EXPRESS,\n360\t\n361\tTELEGRAPHlC FROM BOSTON,\n362\t---\n363\t\n364\t\n365\t\n366\t~oO~~~~~>-~~>o~~~J>>0\"~\n367\t\n368\t\n369\tL,vrKpooI, July 90 Saturday Ev'g, O'Clock.\n370\t\n371\t\n372\tFrance.\n373\t\n374\t\n375\tWe are in possession of our advices from Paris OF\n376\tyesterday evening from which CLEAN the following\n377\titems Of intelligence -\n378\t\n379\t\n380\tThere are present confined in the prisons of Paris\n381\tthe instruction of June last 900 hu.\n382\tman beings, of whom ISIS are men, and l75 women\n383\tor these, $50 men, and Is women, ill., and laid up\n384\thospitals\n385\t\n386\t\n387\tNJ Maguin had given notice Of a motion on the\n388\tsubject of the foreign policy of the republic the de-\n389\tbate thereon was fixed for Monday next, July 3lst\n390\tThe Committee Foreign Affairs appointed, on\n391\tThursday last the following representatives to pre-\n392\tpare reports the principal questions which at this\n393\tmoment occupy public opinion in Europe -\n394\t\n395\t\n396\tDrcssn de Luhys is to treat the Spanish question;\n397\tNJ xavisT Durren will undertake those of Russia;\n398\t\n399\t\n400\tn Edmond lafayette, Moidava and waachiaj\n401\t\n402\t\n403\tA. Passer the German Confederation\n404\t\n405\t\n406\tNJ nceksIen, Prussia and Pruisian Poland; and\n407\t\n408\t\n409\tNJ Pusagur Egypt.\n410\t\n411\t\n412\tThe bill for regulating the clubs of Paris SC.\n413\tcognixed four descriptions Of associations each to be\n414\tdifferently dealt with. Clubs, properly called,\n415\tTO be public, and to be placed under the\n416\tinspection of magistrate. Secret associations\n417\tsimply forbidden. Associations not public,\n418\tand not having any political object, merely\n419\trequired to make declaration of their existence to\n420\tthe municipal authorities; and finally, associations\n421\tnot public but in which political subjects dis\n422\tcussed will in future be obliged to obtain permission\n423\tTO meet, from the authorities.\n424\t\n425\t\n426\tThe papers favorable to republican principles,\n427\tpress dissatisfaction at the measure because Of its\n428\tconstituting, they ceve, permanent check\n429\tVpon liberty. In this opinion they seem justified\n430\tfrom the fact that there can be society without\n431\texpress declaration Of its objects to the magistracy\n432\tand magisterial sanction.\n433\t\n434\t\n435\tThe committee of legislation considered, on Thurs\n436\tday, the important question of arrest for debt The\n437\tcommittee decided, that the question of press\n438\ting nature, they would confne themselves to recon-\n439\tmending simply that the decree of the Provisional\n440\tGovernment, abolshng arrest for debt, should be\n441\tvoked.\n442\t\n443\t\n444\tWe have letters Madrid to the 23d inst, incu-\n445\tsve They state that the Queen has far overed\n446\tfrom the incommodie attendant her disease as to\n447\tbe able to grant audience to the Pope's nuncio,\n448\tMonseigneur BraSnell for the purpose of presenting\n449\this crede initials.\n450\t\n451\t\n452\tWe have late accounts from Italy which state that\n453\tthe King of Naples has published formal protest\n454\tagainst the Act of Parliament which calls the Duke of\n455\tGenoa to the throne of Sicily 10000 said to\n456\tbe in process of equipment at Turin and to be in\n457\tstate Of forwardness to commence the campaign. Sup\n458\tplies Of muskets had been received from abroad and\n459\tthe foundries were busy casting pieces of cannon.\n460\t\n461\t\n462\tRussia.\n463\t\n464\t\n465\tst. Petersburg advices inform us that the Cholera\n466\twas beginning to diminish that city. On the 14th,\n467\tthere remained 37e2 patients under care the same\n468\tday there 525 cases. 2ls recoveries and 3l2\n469\tdeaths.\n470\t\n471\t\n472\tAustria.\n473\t\n474\t\n475\tThe last accounts from Innspruck state that the\n476\temperor and the royal family to leave that place\n477\to he nj nd to\n478\ttrun on the 2Sth.\n479\t\n480\t\n481\tGermany.\n482\t\n483\t\n484\tAdvices from Frankfort Of the 23d, state that Gen\n485\tCavagnac had in amicabTe letter appealed to the\n486\tgovernments Of Germany NO longer to give passage\n487\tthrough their territories to the Poles who wished go\n488\tFrance in order not to augment the elements of an\n489\tarchy at Paris.\n490\t\n491\t\n492\tEngland.\n493\t\n494\t\n495\tLONDON, Saturday Noon.\n496\tThe Conan der in Chiefs very busily engaged\n497\ting orders for the dispatch every available soldier\n498\twhich be spared from all the depots, to Ireland\n499\tlarge number sent of from London last night and\n500\tmere leaving by several trains to-day From the\n501\tWeldon Barracks several companies leaving\n502\tsame destination. Before the close of the week. it is\n503\tsaid that 900 soldiers viii be duty in that\n504\ttry More ships, with ordnance stores, ordered\n505\timmed iately to sail for Cork.\n506\tJust before the departure of the steamer a gentle\n507\tman Of the European Times office arrived from jour-\n508\tney through the principal agricultural counties of\n509\tEngland who found from personal inquiry and obser\n510\tvstiop that the failure Of the potato crop exists to\n511\tconsiderable extent, and in many districts, the\n512\ters gathering them up for immediate sale, to pre-\n513\tvent the entire loss which is anticipated from their\n514\tappearance.\n515\t\n516\t\n517\tA Cabinet council held at the Foreign office\n518\tthis morning All the Ministers present. The\n519\tevening papers say warrants have been issued this\n520\tmorning to arrest several Of the London Chartist\n521\tleaders\n522\t\n523\t\n524\tIreland.\n525\t\n526\t\n527\tThe Royal mail steamer Banshee, which sailed from\n528\tKingstown this morning states that Dublin continues\n529\tquiet. Mr.. J. F. Lalor has been arrested at BallyRs-\n530\thone, and was his way to Nsnagh Jail when the\n531\tmail left.\n532\t\n533\t\n534\tThe Latest Market Reports.\n535\tLONDON MONEY MARKET Saturday July 2.-The money\n536\t.e. opened s6 and closed at sss to\n537\tSS S vas q Sc Sss sna\n538\tstock is stX 20 s71 e1chequer bills premium The\n539\tSATURDAY az goods and yarn\n540\tto-day s better and there 10 eviden feeling in the trade\n541\tCorpt in better request and have upward tendency\n542\t\n543\t\n544\tNrar Law Intelligence\n545\tGErERAL SESSIONS, Aug. l-Before Judge DAILY and |\n546\tAldermen Crolius and stevens. John NcKeon Esq |\n547\tDistrict Attorney.\n548\tCatherine anderson and Catherine Je6ries we're |\n549\tboth pxt forward trial charged with having robbed\n550\tOf s35 The former fou nd guilty Of the theft and\n551\tthe latter the charge Of having received stolen\n552\tgoods. Both appeared. lived in house Of flame\n553\tin Water street They were remanded for sentence.\n554\tCharles Ferguson was put upon trial. on charge |\n555\tOf burelaiously attempting break open the desk In\n556\texchange once No sc West Broadwa belonging to\n557\t\n558\t=== 17_1896-07-24_pna_sn82014248_00514155963_1896072401_0167.json\n559\tDaily Kennebec journal. [microfilm reel] (Augusta, Me.) 1870-1975 · 1896-07-24\n560\tcrops: cached\n561\theadline: kennebec.\n562\t\n563\tWINTHROP\n564\t---\n565\tDavid Lewis. the Quaker evangelist who\n566\thas been holding meetings in Hallowell.\n567\tis in town. 1t is said he will not hold\n568\tmeetings here as he is resting from his\n569\tlabors now preparatory to beginning\n570\teVangeistic work in Sidney.-Miss At\n571\tkinson spoke at the Friends prayer meet\n572\ting held at their rooms in Packard's block\n573\tTuesday evening. Her theme was the\n574\tpossession of souls by the Holy Ghost.\n575\tHer preaching was very effective.\n576\t\n577\t=== 2_1916-12-18_p5_sn82014086_00295866081_1916121801_0501.json\n578\tNorwich bulletin. · 1916-12-18\n579\tcrops: cached\n580\theadline: FAMOUS NEW ENGLAND MEDIUM\n581\t\n582\t AT SPIRITUAL ACADEMY\n583\t\n584\tWelIman C. Whitney of Springfield\n585\t\n586\t Spoke on Haunted Houses.\n587\t---\n588\tMany attended the services at the First Spiritual Academy Sunday night and heard Wellman c. Whitney of Springfield, one of New Englands best known mediums. speak on things spir- itual. MT. Whitneys topic of the ev- ening was Haunted Houses, and he prefaced his talk with Longfellows 8 poem of the same name. MT. Whit ney said that we are continually hearing of some houses being haunt ed, but in the majority of cases there was always some trivial cause such as rats running in the walls which lead to the belief that the house was haunted. That there are haunted houses there is no doubt. Occasion ing and curious things happen which are unexplained. It is impossible to explain these happenings other than by the theory that those who once lived in 1t are attracted to the house by some dreadful experience which oc- cured during their lives there. As a rule these are earth bound spirits and these occasions such as rappihg, stone throwing and other things were never indulged in by progressive or enlight- ened individuals who passed through the change of death.\n589\t\n590\t Following. the lecture MT. Whitney gave psychic messages accompanied by names and the relationship that the spirit bore to the people in the audience. In each case the messages were readily recognized.\n591\t\n592\t=== 5_1871-03-10_pna_sn83025925_00280776312_1871031001_0476.json\n593\tThe evening telegraph. [volume] (Philadelphia [Pa.]) 1864-1918 · 1871-03-10\n594\tcrops: cached\n595\theadline: A GHOST AT COLLEGE.\n596\t---\n597\tUproar In n Tennessee Female RducRtIoBn nj In\n598\tsIitute-A Speculative Appar'tIon-WhO Owns\n599\tFrom the Memphis AUoldnche, March ..\n600\t\n601\t\n602\tSouth Memphis is in furore of excitement\n603\tover occurrences of q supernatural nature re-\n604\tGently made public, end which concern the\n605\tBrinkley Female College and it's inmates. The\n606\tcollege is spacious frame structure, with rather\n607\tq sombre and dreary aspect, situated at or near\n608\tthe intersection of Georgia and De Soto streets.\n609\tInc Inc sch school at present numbers between ,sn forty\n610\tRobinson, daughter of Mr.. Robinson, an attor\n611\tney, residing on De Soto street. Her age is\n612\tabout thirteen years, and her temperament is of\n613\tthe nervous kind, while her health is rather what\n614\tmight be called delicate.\n615\t\n616\t\n617\tTHE MYSTERY.\n618\t\n619\t\n620\tOne week ago last Tuesday Miss Clara was\n621\talone in one of the upper rooms of the institute\n622\tpractising her music lesson; an apparition sud\n623\tdenly appeared before her in the shape of girl\n624\tof about eight years of age, with sunken, lustre-\n625\tless eyes, and strikingly emaciated form and\n626\tfeatures. The object was virtually skeleton\n627\tin appearance, clad in dingy and tattered dress\n628\t0reenish and Shiny fold. 1t seemed also to be\n629\ttransparent. A sad expression rested upon the\n630\tfeatures of the strange visitor. Naturally the\n631\tvision spoke quickly, in mild, pleasant tone,\n632\tand said, Don't be alarmed, Clara,\n633\t\n634\t\n635\tMY NAME is LIZZIE.\n636\t\n637\t\n638\tl will not hurt you Clara stood trans6xed\n639\twith terror. The vision spoke again, and in\n640\tdistinct tone related that the Brinkley College\n641\tproperty was hers by right, title, and Decd\n642\tthat its present pretended owners held 1t ie-\n643\tgally, having no shadow of claim to 1t what\n644\tever; that there was no one else to claim it, as\n645\ther people we're all dead, and she herself was\n646\tthe last ono that had died. She desired Clara\n647\tto obtain the papers which she had previously\n648\tmentioned to her, and with them in her posses\n649\tsion claim and retain the property in her own\n650\tname and right. Unless she did so she never\n651\twould do good to or for any one. Of course all\n652\tthis soon spread among the usually quiet people\n653\tof South Memphis. Several young ladies went\n654\thome frightened out of their wits, and several\n655\tOthers were affected with illness, some it was\n656\tfeared seriously.\n657\t\n658\t\n659\trhe Alleged secret Treasure Discovered\n660\tDocuments and Parcels Found as Indicated\n661\tby the spectre.\n662\t\n663\t\n664\tThe Avalanche of the 7th inst. continues the\n665\tstrange story as follows\n666\t\n667\t\n668\tANOTHER APPARITIoN.\n669\t\n670\t\n671\tDuring Sunday Clara remain undsturbed\n672\tby ghostly visitants, and, although extensively\n673\tinterviewed, managed to attend Sunday school\n674\tin the morning and church in the evening, at\n675\ttended to the latter place by Mr.. Franklin, q\n676\tneighbor. She passed quiet night and was\n677\tquite cheerful yesterday morning, though think\n678\ting frequently of the occurrences of the past\n679\tfew days. While in the yard back of her resi-\n680\tdence, No. 261 De Soto street, however, in COM\n681\tpany with young friend, about o'clock, she\n682\twas startled by the sudden appearance of\n683\t\n684\t\n685\tTHE SPECTRE OF BRINKLEY COLLEGE,\n686\tand screaming directed to it the attention of her\n687\tcompanion who ran away In alright, though\n688\tSne sew nOlnlDS. Inc spectre, ike the ghOSt\n689\tof Hamlet, chided Clara for her tardiness in un-\n690\tearthing the secret, and was answered that it\n691\tcould not be found, when it rejoined that Clara\n692\tmust go and seek it herself, or that Others\n693\twould get it. The spectre then disappeared,\n694\tand Clara ran in trepidation into the house,\n695\twhere she narrated her adventure. II was re-\n696\tsolved by the inmates to p:oceed at once to the\n697\texcavation, which they did, Clara going with\n698\tMiss Franklin, q neighbor. Mr.. Robertson ac-\n699\tcompanied by the medium and two diggers,\n700\tand following the direction of the spirit to\n701\tmake long story short, after digging an hour\n702\tor more into the brick work,\n703\t\n704\t\n705\tFOUND A GLASS JAR,\n706\t\n707\t\n708\twhich he passed up TO the medium, whereupon\n709\tthey proceeded to Mr.. Robertson's residence\n710\tfollowed by an excited crowd. The jar was at\n711\tonce delivered to Clara, who regarding its pos\n712\tsession as an end to her unearthly visitations\n713\treceived it with transports of delight, and up to\n714\tlate hour was in the happiest moed imagin-\n715\table. The jar bore evidence of long conceal\n716\tment, being covered with mould. True to the\n717\tinstructions from the spirit, it was not opened,\n718\tbut through its sides could be seen several bags\n719\tand packages, together with what appeared to\n720\tbe large yellow envelope.\n721\t\n722\t\n723\tMr.. Robertson states that in accordance with\n724\tthe request of the spirit, the jar will not be\n725\topened for 60 days, at the end of which period\n726\tevery fact that may be elected by an investiga-\n727\ttion of the recovered treasure will be given to\n728\tthe public.\n729\t\n730\t=== 79_1897-04-18_p2_sn85042461_00280769721_1897041801_0554.json\n731\tThe herald. · 1897-04-18\n732\tcrops: cached\n733\theadline: \n734\t---\n735\tCHURCH OF THE UNITY. COR. THIRD\n736\tand Hill sts., Rev. J. S. Thoms on, pastor;\n737\tEaster services Sunday at IL a. m. ana\n738\t7:30 d. M; Sabbath school at $3.50 a. m.\n739\tsubject Sunday morning, The Bodles OF\n740\tthe Spirits of the Departed\" subject sun\n741\tday evening, \"Christs Doctrine ABOUT\n742\tNatural consequences ~\n743\t\n744\t=== 3_1903-10-14_pna_sn90059523_00206539367_1903101401_0176.json\n745\tThe Saint Paul globe. (St. Paul, Minn.) 1896-1905 · 1903-10-14\n746\tcrops: cached\n747\theadline: Here's the HxpnotIc Elourish.\n748\t---\n749\tTo the Editor of the Globe.\n750\t\n751\t\n752\tUnder date of Oct. Is you published\n753\tan article by D. s. B. Johnston, Of\n754\tst. Paul, relating to early history\n755\tof Minnesota newspapers, in which\n756\tthe Glencoe Register is given due\n757\tmention as having been established\n758\tin August, 1857, which is correct.\n759\tIn addition, however, Mr. Johnston\n760\tstates that of nineteen papers establish\n761\ted between Jan. 1 and Aug. \"a. 1857, only\n762\tone now survives, namely, the Mantor-\n763\tvile Express. This statement is wrong.\n764\tThe Glencoe Register is still very much\n765\talive and has been published regularly\n766\tevery week since it was started, fires\n767\tfloods, bad Indians and even the I'M\n768\tequivocal statement of Mr. Johnston to\n769\tthe contrary notwithstanding. No, the\n770\tRegister does not want to feel that it is\n771\tthe ghost of paper\" floating around\n772\tin the neither atmosphere like some dis\n773\tembodied wrath. 1t is really too sub\n774\tstantial a reality, especially when salary\n775\tday comes around and bills fall due to\n776\tbe classed in the list of dead\" ones. and\n777\tWe ask you to kindly give us the inky,\n778\thypnotic flourish that will bring us back\n779\tto life again and set us right with our\n780\tadvertisers who are already waiting on\n781\tus with dark hints about getting money\n782\tunder false pretenSe, etc. Just tell them\n783\tthat you saw US and that we are still in\n784\tthe ring as hale and hearty as we have\n785\tbeen at any time since we sprang into\n786\texistence as the fifty-ninth newspaper to\n787\tbe established in the great state of Min\n788\tneSota.\n789\t\n790\t\n791\tw. L. Williams, Editor\n792\t\n793\t\n794\tGlencoe, Mimi, Oct. 13.\n795\t\n796\t=== 25_1890-04-23_p1_sn82014381_00175036623_1890042301_0183.json\n797\tSacramento daily record-union. [volume] (Sacramento [Calif.]) 1875-1891 · 1890-04-23\n798\tcrops: cached\n799\theadline: A MAD MEDIUM.\n800\t\n801\tSlater Raps the Press Over the Knuckles,\n802\tand Gives Some Pulling Tests.\n803\t---\n804\tSlater Raps the Press over the hnuckies,\n805\tand Gives Some Puzziing Tests.\n806\tSlater. the mind reader and as he insists,\n807\tthe spiritually influenced had good\n808\t\n809\t\n810\thouse last night. He exbioited good deal\n811\tof feeling over the criticisms of the press,\n812\t| forgetting that when person poses as a\n813\t\n814\t\n815\tSnowman or fortune-teller exhibiting for\n816\t\n817\t\n818\tcoin, he is legitimate subject for criticism\n819\t\n820\t\n821\tand description, and that the press is justi-\n822\t| fed in telling its readers how he appears\n823\tand what impression his performance\n824\t\n825\t\n826\tmakes. He renewed his challenge for any,\n827\t! one to clean up some thousands of dollars\n828\tby exposing him.\n829\t\n830\t\n831\tThe performer gave better entertain\n832\t! ment last night. He made many mistakes.\n833\t| and was cornered by several witnesses, but\n834\t| insisted in each case that he was right that\n835\t| he was influenced by magnetism\" to re-\n836\t\n837\t\n838\tveal, and could not be in error. and that he\n839\t\n840\t\n841\tcould deal only in generalities, for reasons\n842\tpersonal to his subjects. 1t is common\n843\tjustice to say, without modifying the opin-\n844\t\n845\t\n846\tion already expressed, that he gave some\n847\ttests altogether remarkable, and leaving\n848\t\n849\t\n850\tscarcely any room for suspicion Of collision\n851\t\n852\t\n853\tor indirect previous ascertainment of facts.\n854\t\n855\t\n856\tYet it Is true that the 'crysta\" fortune\n857\ttellers sometimes make as close predic-\n858\t\n859\t\n860\ttons and reyealments.\n861\t\n862\t\n863\t| These tests were, in brief, as follows: A\n864\t\n865\t\n866\tyoung woman, very innocent, retiring and\n867\t\n868\t\n869\tmodest in appearance. was told that she\n870\t\n871\t\n872\twas alone in this country; that she was in\n873\t\n874\t\n875\tgreat doubt about taking an important\n876\t\n877\t\n878\tstep-engaging herself to her escort; that\n879\t\n880\t\n881\tshe was anxious about not hearing from\n882\t\n883\t\n884\thome, fearing death or illness in her fam.\n885\tlily; that certain of her relatives were dead,\n886\tLate sc ,rh, nh {1o LODI\n887\t| firmed. Finally he told her that she\n888\t\n889\t\n890\twould receive letter from home on Thurs\n891\tday morning; that it would come from\n892\t\n893\t\n894\tover the sea, and bring satisfactory news\n895\t\n896\t\n897\tabout a sister, concerning whom she\n898\t| worried\" that the letter would be in\n899\t| strange tongue. The witness confirmed\n900\this statements and the prophecy as to her\n901\texpectation of news. She gave RECORD\n902\tUNION reporter her name and address, and\n903\t| stated that she is Norwegian and is alone,\n904\t| as far as relatives are concerned, in this\n905\tState. The test\" was recognized by the\n906\taudience as something beyond shrewd\n907\tguessing.\n908\t\n909\t\n910\tMrs. w. G. Farnsworth was reminded\n911\tthat she knew some one named Martin in\n912\ther early life. She denied it. Slater in\n913\tsisted, declared the man was dead, but that\n914\t| long before she became Mrs. Farnsworth\n915\tMartin had paid her warm attention and\n916\tdesired to link his life with hers, but she\n917\trefused. Mrs. F., with a start, exclaimed\n918\t| that it was true, but she had forgotten it.\n919\tSeveral other revelations\" he made to her\n920\t\n921\t\n922\tas to her wishes, aims and experiences the\n923\t| lady emphatically denied.\n924\t\n925\t\n926\tGeorge w. Stafford, by nod, admitted\n927\t\n928\t\n929\tthat Slater had sized\" up certain perplexi-\n930\t| ties of his mind and traits of his character\n931\tAn Englishman was told that he had\n932\t| friend, the initials of whose name were II.\n933\t\n934\t\n935\tP. The Englishman denied it stoutly.\n936\tSlater insisted, said the man lived at the\n937\t| time in Melbourne, and his name was\n938\t| Hervey Phipps. \"Oh, yes\" exclaimed the\n939\t| Englishman, that is SO he passed out of\n940\t| my life years ago, and is in England now\"\n941\tSlater then made him confess that he had\n942\tcome distance to attend the show\" and\n943\t\n944\t\n945\thad hurried to the train \"like thunder\"\n946\t\n947\t\n948\tSlater added that he had come from the\n949\t\n950\t\n951\tnorth, from Red Bluff or Redding He\n952\t\n953\t\n954\twas told he was in error as to the direc-\n955\t\n956\t\n957\tton. Subsequently the Englishman, who\n958\t| declined to permit his name to be pub\n959\t| lished, said that he had come from Stock\n960\t\n961\t\n962\tton, had hurried to catch train to reach\n963\there, and was going to Redding. 1t de-\n964\t\n965\t\n966\tveloped that he is well-known San Fran\n967\tcisco insurance man. that he had attended\n968\tseveral of Skaters seances in San Francisco\n969\t\n970\t\n971\tand had received tests, but he insisted that\n972\tSlater does not know him, and declares\n973\t\n974\t\n975\tthat the Phipps referred to is Lord Hervey\n976\t| Phipps, an old chum of his youth, and\n977\t\n978\t\n979\tthat Slater nor no one in California could\n980\thave known of him.\n981\t\n982\t\n983\t| The operator pointed out lady and\n984\t\n985\t\n986\tasked her if she had not in the afternoon\n987\tbeen talking of marriage. She denied it.\n988\tSlater insisted. She reiterated her denial\n989\t\n990\t\n991\tso far as she could remember. But you\n992\t\n993\t\n994\tdid, retorted Slater, land you talked with\n995\t| the lady two seats from you on the right\n996\ta