 near\n1390\tPaines Hollow say has been haunt\n1391\ting them for seven years, is 'at\n1392\trest today. 1t disappeared several\n1393\tdays ago with the burial of the\n1394\tbody OF Henry Lipenstick, which\n1395\twas found In an abandoned well on\n1396\ta farm.\n1397\t\n1398\t\n1399\tLipenstick, farmland. disap-\n1400\tpeared seven years ago. Since then\n1401\tthe farm IS said to have had poor\n1402\tcrops A farmhouse burned down\n1403\tand the ghost\" was blamed for\n1404\tthat. owner after owner has\n1405\tcome and gone.\n1406\t\n1407\t\n1408\tCarl Logies purchased the farm\n1409\tabout n year ago. Whenever he\n1410\twent 10 his barn at night, Logies\n1411\tsaid today, he would see \"a white\n1412\twralth-like figure\" walking about.\n1413\tAt times he declared, he followed\n1414\tIl with his gun in hand and II al\n1415\tways disappeared in the direction\n1416\tOf the we'll.\n1417\t\n1418\t\n1419\tDetermined to find out about the\n1420\t} apparition, Logies cleaned out the\n1421\told we'll which had been filled with\n1422\tstones. He came upon the body of\n1423\tLipenstick. Sheriff Pink was\n1424\tcalled and he and his deputies sus-\n1425\tpected murder. The next day the\n1426\tsheriff ordered the body buried and\n1427\tnow the \"ehost\" is gone. Frank\n1428\tLerman, who owned the farm at\n1429\tthe time Lipenstick disappeared, IS\n1430\tIN Lake county jail. lie was bound\n1431\tover TO the grandjury on charges\n1432\tOf murder.\n1433\t\n1434\t=== 35_1910-10-01_p1_sn84029690_00212473182_1910100101_0629.json\n1435\tThe Hays free press. [volume] (Hays, Kan.) 1908-1924 · 1910-10-01\n1436\tcrops: cached\n1437\theadline: OOOOOOOOOQ OOOl0OOOO\n1438\t\n1439\t\n1440\tAngles Ambition\n1441\t\n1442\tBy GERALD PRIME\n1443\t\n1444\t\n1445\t\n1446\tcopyright 1910 by Associated Literary Press\n1447\t---\n1448\tOON|--~--' -\n1449\t\n1450\t\n1451\tThey were talking it over-Angie\n1452\tand her pretty mother.\n1453\t\n1454\t\n1455\t\"It certainly must have been graft\n1456\tfytng-in a way\" said Mrs. Calhoun,\n1457\tin her leisurely southern monotone.\n1458\t\n1459\t\n1460\tHer daughter was buttering piece\n1461\tOf toast, an occupation which seemed\n1462\tto demand her utmost concentration.\n1463\tMrs. Calhoun slipped her tea in silence.\n1464\tand with look of polite expectancy.\n1465\tShe always drank tea at breakfast,\n1466\thabit which gave her an added dis\n1467\ttinction in Alaabma, where such a\n1468\tpreference is unusual.\n1469\t\n1470\t\n1471\tThey were not in Alabama now.\n1472\tMrs. Calhoun regretted the fact, and\n1473\tthat they were perched-most tnse-\n1474\tcurely it seemed to heron the elev-\n1475\thotel She had been trying for at\n1476\tleast ten minutes to find out whether\n1477\tor not her daughter shared her regret,\n1478\ta bit of information which the young\n1479\ter woman seemed inclined to withhold\n1480\tfor the present.\n1481\t\n1482\t\n1483\tWe'll, do you really feel-pleased\n1484\tover your-I mean our venture?\n1485\tcontinued Mrs. Calhoun, with gentle\n1486\tinsistence, putting down her cup and\n1487\tsaucer and letting her hands fall list\n1488\tlessly into her lap.\n1489\t\n1490\t\n1491\tThis was a direct question, and as\n1492\tsuch demanded an answer-even\n1493\tthough it came from her mother, who.\n1494\tAngie knew perfectly well, would\n1495\thave been neither hurt nor resentful\n1496\thad her daughter elected to remain\n1497\tsilent.\n1498\t\n1499\t\n1500\tNO.\" she said, with a little laugh\n1501\twhich did not ring right-a fact which\n1502\tdid not escape the attention of her\n1503\tmother and added greatly to her dis\n1504\tquietude-\"it wasn't at all gratifying,\n1505\tand I'm not bit pleased with it. 1\n1506\tjust hate 1.-THE whole business. from\n1507\tbeginning to end\"\n1508\t\n1509\t\n1510\t\"Oh, Angie\n1511\t\n1512\t\n1513\t81 knew it,\" the girl interrupted\n1514\trecklessly. Il was determined to do\n1515\tit and nothing could have prevented\n1516\tme when the chance came. 1 wouldn't\n1517\thave accepted a kingdom in exchange\n1518\tfor my opportunity to come to New\n1519\tYork and be an actress. An actress,\n1520\tindeed\"\n1521\t\n1522\t\n1523\tYou certainly have talent, Angie, a\n1524\theap of it,\" her mother affirmed 10y\n1525\tally\n1526\t\n1527\t\n1528\tNot a little Bit\" declared her\n1529\tdaughter positively. I'm Angela Cal\n1530\thoun first, last and all the time, and 1\n1531\tnever lose my hateful identity for a\n1532\tsingle minute. 1t makes no difference\n1533\twhether the part is Queen Elizabeth\n1534\tor Little Eva-Fm Angela Calhoun all\n1535\tthe time\"\n1536\t\n1537\t\n1538\tI'm mighty glad you are\" said her\n1539\tmother, with a sudden assumption of\n1540\tspirit that almost transformed her.\n1541\tIl hope nothing will ever happen to\n1542\tchange you into anybody else. That\n1543\tis-unless-\n1544\t\n1545\t\n1546\tJust so-unless,\" Angie came to\n1547\tthe rescue.\n1548\t\n1549\t\n1550\tHow they did applaud you last\n1551\tnight\" said Mrs. Calhoun, returning\n1552\ttactfully to the matter in hand. \"All\n1553\tthose people must have enjoyed 1t, if\n1554\tyou didn't. though they never would\n1555\tget enough of that last song. 1 don't\n1556\tsee how you can feel that it wasn't q\n1557\tsuccess. Everybody in the house was\n1558\tdelighted. Mr. Goldsmith was charm\n1559\ted. He came into the box to congratu-\n1560\tlate me, you. know\"\n1561\t\n1562\t\n1563\tw LAT ulu ne say!\n1564\t\n1565\t\n1566\tHe was too excited to saw much of\n1567\tanything. Our little girls a big win\n1568\tner' was one thing. Really. Angie\n1569\twhen l saw how those people liked It\n1570\tand were carrying on, 1 couldn't help\n1571\tfeeling-well. proud of you\"\n1572\t\n1573\t\n1574\tYou dear! l wish for your sake\n1575\tthat l might do something really\n1576\tworth WHILE\" said Angie, in a voice\n1577\tthat was passing beyond her control.\n1578\t\n1579\t\n1580\tNever mind, DEAR\" becoming at\n1581\tonce the tender maternal CONSIDER:\n1582\tyou have but to say the word and\n1583\twe will go south at once. We can go\n1584\tback to Talladega in a blaze of glory\n1585\tYour success last night has made that\n1586\tpossible. Shall l begin to pack I'D.\n1587\tAngler\"\n1588\t\n1589\t\n1590\tThat was all that was REQUIRED to\n1591\tbring about instant reaction. The girl\n1592\tabandoned her sensational lapse with\n1593\ta dexterity that would have served her\n1594\tadmirably on the stage.\n1595\t\n1596\t\n1597\tDon't let us be sentimental. NOM\n1598\tsey,\" she said. I'VE really can't af-\n1599\tford it. No, we won't go back to Tai\n1600\tladega. There isn't soul in the town\n1601\tthat 1 ever want to set eyes on\n1602\tagain.\n1603\t\n1604\t\n1605\tMrs. Calhoun sighed family. .T\n1606\tused to think that you and Geoff Mar\n1607\ttin\" she began. but Angie would not\n1608\tpermit her.\n1609\t\n1610\t\n1611\t~~~~@~~~~~~~~~\"~o-~-~~~-~\n1612\t\n1613\t=== 27_1912-10-26_p1_sn86061215_00295875392_1912102601_0205.json\n1614\tOttumwa tri-weekly courier. [volume] (Ottumwa, Iowa) 1903-1916 · 1912-10-26\n1615\tcrops: cached\n1616\theadline: REV. FATHER FELIX BYRNE DIS\n1617\tcoURSED ON ORDINANCE or\n1618\t\n1619\t\n1620\tCATHOLIC CHURCH..\n1621\t\n1622\tQUOTED SCRIPTURE\n1623\t\n1624\tAuthority For Forelvins OF Sins Point\n1625\ted Out BY Speaker From,\n1626\tthe Non.CathoIic\n1627\t\n1628\t\n1629\tBible.\n1630\t\n1631\tOrder Of Services.\n1632\t---\n1633\t, Order Of Services.\n1634\t; 1880 and s a. m. - Low\n1635\t. mass. Five minute instruction\n1636\t\n1637\t\n1638\t. for children at the O'Clock\n1639\tservice.\n1640\t. 1880 p. m.--Answers to ques-\n1641\t. tions, lecture and benediction.\n1642\t\n1643\t\n1644\t, Lecture Subjects.\n1645\t. Thursday night-\"The God\n1646\t. on the Catholic Altar\"\n1647\t\n1648\t\n1649\t, Friday night THE Faith\n1650\t\n1651\t\n1652\t. Divine\"\n1653\t\n1654\t\n1655\t. Saturday night-\"The Wor-\n1656\t. ship of Mary\"\n1657\t. Sunday night-\"Ghosts.'\n1658\t\n1659\t=== 91_1894-03-09_p1_sn84022835_00237285050_1894030901_0739.json\n1660\tThe Red Cloud chief. · 1894-03-09\n1661\tcrops: None\n1662\theadline: \n1663\t---\n1664\tus the mistress OF the house in?\" in.\n1665\tCURED the peddler. 'No,\" replied the\n1666\ttired looking, timid woman who had\n1667\tgone to the kitchen door TO answer the\n1668\tknock. Its her afternoon out\"\n1669\t"}]},"uuid":"7f0fc180-e1c3-4b99-bbd4-a085be7304b6","timestamp":"2026-10-31T08:08:46.502Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/c1/d01.txt","content":"=== 4_1918-10-01_pna_sn89055128_0041421146A_1918100101_1006.json\nThe daily star-mirror. (Moscow, Idaho) 1911-1939 · 1918-10-01\ncrops: cached\nheadline: PHRENOLOGIST\nSPIRIT MEDIUM\n---\nThe great World Knowing\nAstrologist Spirit Medium, anc\nExpert Phrenologist. Gifted from\nchildhood, and with a lifetime\npractice, now is ready to serve all\nwho will call upon him, at his pri-\nvate room.\n\n\nWithout knowing who you are,\nor whence you came, this Hindoo\nfrom Calcutta, closes his. eyes and\ntells you everything. No matter\nwhat your trouble, or what you\nwant to know, he will give you\ndates, facts, names, and advice\nupon all undertakings.\n\n\nCall and be convinced, twill be\na dollar we'll spent. Whether old\nor young, have your head read and\nknowing myself\" and for what\nyou are adapted. Appointments\nmade by telephone. No. TOJ.\nRates S1.0O and up. Office hours,\nweek days 1900 a. m. to 500 p. M;\nSundays, 1000 a. m. to 400 p. m.\nWill be here until the 9Th of Oc-\ntober, Moscow, Idaho.\n\n\nOne and one-half blocks outside\ncity limits, south of Main St.,\nhouse on right hand side, 2 STORY\npainted white, away from road,\nsign on door, across railroad track\nand bridge. 303314\n\n=== 12_1868-09-12_p1_sn83016025_00279525267_1868091201_0167.json\nThe Portland daily press. · 1868-09-12\ncrops: cached\nheadline: VarietieS.\n---\nwearing curious and Characteristic dresses\nswan from the Oberbaum down to General\nWon Ftuels river baths, a distance of about five\nhundred yards. When they had arrived Il\nthe latter place they performed various leats of\n\n\nstrength and agility in the water, to the de-\n\n\nlight of vast crowd of spectators. A great\n\n\npart of the effect was produced by the quaint\n\n\nness of the costumes. The first company rep-\n\n\nresented \"the ladies\" and wore chignons and\n\n\nlittle Paris hats, and carried parasols. There\n\n\nwere two companies of negroes, and a very\nnumerous one of red Indians, with their bod\nles painted and their faces tattooed, and wear\ning head-dresses and feathers. Besides these\nthere were swans, river gods, Chinese, dandies\nand various other .characters. One of the\nswimmers at the head of the procession per-\nformed very difficult feat of playing the ma\ntiOnal air, \"Ich bin Elm Prusse,\" Q am a Prus-\nsian) on the horn. The yf5te lasted an hour,\nand passed off without accident.\n\n\nLookout Mountain is crumbling, and the\nneighbors fear that some internal forces are at\nwork. One tive-hundred ton fragment tumb-\nled from near the top, the other day.\n\n\nr. w. Emerson is soon to commence in\nBoston course OF six lectures the subjects to\nbe as follows: Art\" t;\"\"Poetry and Criticism\nDoctrine of Leasts and Mosts;\" Historical\nNotes of American Life and Letters\" \"Hos-\npitahty, and How to Make Homes Attrac-\ntive\" Greatness\"\n\n\nThe real founder of the present Swedish\ndynasty has just died at Stockholm. Count\nOtto Won M0rner was born in 1881, aud during\nhis long life of eighty-seven years took very\nactive part in the politics of his country. In\n1910, on the death of Charles Augustus, Count\nWon M0rDer went to Paris and offered the Swe-\ndish crown to Marshal Bernadotte. The Mar\nshal then presented himself as a candidate for\nelection as Crown Prince, and Count Won\nMorner labored so zealously in his behalf that\nthe election resulted in his favor, notwith-\nstanding the ill-concealed opposition of the\nKing and his Court. Ultimately BernadOtte\nwas crowned king as Charles John of Sweden,\nand Count Won M6rner was appointed his\n\n\n\nThe death of Christian Fredrick Schon-\nbein, OF Basle, on Sunday last, removes one OF\nthe most eminent chemists in the world from\nthe useful labors Of his most practical science.\nHe was the discoyerer of ozone, and the in\nventor of gun-cotton, which, though useless\nfor purposes of war, yet dissolved in ether be-\ncame, under the name of collodion, valuable\naid in photography and in medicine. He was\nthe author of several chemical treatises, was\nthe friend of Faraday, and at the time of his\ndeath had almost completed his sixty-ninth\nyear.\n\n\nRev. DIR. Stebbins says it is an insult to\nthe memory of Washington, and shame to\nthe nation, that Mt. Vernon is so mismanaged,\nand by rebel spirits, too. Everything is in the\nmost slovenly, tumble-down condition imagin-\nable. 1t is owned by an association of ladies',\nof which Miss Pamela Cunningham is Presi-\ndent. She lives in the mansion, and shows\nneither the ability nor disposition to take care\nOF it.\n\n\nThe Journal of Chemistry has long arti.\nGle on Planchette, in which the editor express\nes the belief shat there is one power, impulse,\nor force in nature, regarding the character of\nwhich mankind are totally in the dark\"\n\n\nJohn Aliens pious influence is being felt\nIn his Immediate neighborhood. The Tribune\nsays that Tommy Haddan, who is claimed by\nthe sailors to be a wickeder man than John\nAllen ever was; and Sojer (soldier) Brown,\nwho is wickeder than Tommy Haddan; and\nKit Burns, who is wickeder than Sojer Brown,\nhave all signified their willingness to have\nprayer-meetings in their respective dens al\nsoon as they can get them in order for that\npurpose. The Tribune does not say that any\none of these gentry has offered to go to work\nand earn an honest livelihood.\n\n\nAT an intended wedding, just as the cere-\nmonies were about to begin, the bride sudden\nly called upon the bridegroom to abjure tobae-\nCO, on pain of forfeiture of welded bliss, &C.,\nwhich he refused to do-then and there tax\ning fresh quid from his tobacco box, to show\nhis independence. WhereupOn the young la\ndy took out dainty bex, took a chew\" /\" amid\nthe applause of the guests, and ordered the\nclergyman to go ahead. This was too much\nfor the bridegroom, who fled the scene, leaving\nthe girl triumphant with piece of liquorice\nin her mouth.\n\n\nLiverpool has a haunted house, the win\ndows of which are broken by continuous\nshowers of stones. The police can't discover\nthe ghostly assailants.\n\n\n-Ristori writes that she was surprised to\nfind Americans so rich, and that this would\nbe the finest country on earth but for its eli\nmate.\n\n\n\"A young New Orleans quadroon, Miss De-\nlanney, is the most promising pupil in the Par\nis Conseryatoire. Her voice is alto.\n\n\nAWFUL death of twenty persons and =\npeer\" is the way London paper announced\nthe Abergele accident.\n\n\nMiss Edmonia Lewis, the colored sculptor,\nnow in Rome, has had an order for a statue of\nClio from Prince George of Prussia.\n\n\n\"A recent Toronto paper contains the nGiue\nanpouncement that \"the Rev. w. Morley\nPunshon, having returned from his honey\nmoon, will preach here next Sunday\"\n\n\nThe influence of nineteenth century ideas\nhas reached even the Hapsburgs. The Arch\nduke Albert was lately asked by priest to\ngive money for masses for the Iepose OF Maxi\nmilan's soul, when he replied-\"I wiz gladly\nsubscribe to a collection in mo~.0ry OF my un~\nfortunate brother, but shall insist on the\nmoney being applied to pay his creditors,\nwhich appears to me the first thing to be\ndoue.\"\n\n\nA boy about five years old died in convul-\nsions on Sunday, in New York, from drinking\nBourbon whiskey. He had seen his father\ndrink from the bottle during the day, and was\ntrying to initiate him.\n\n\nThe condition of the people on the Red\nRiver of the North seems to be even worse\nthan was at first reported. The st. Paul Press\nsaws the case is Just this: \"Ten or twelve\nthousand people, immured in distant region,\nfour or five hundred miles beyond our western\nfrontier, are the eve of starvation. The\ngrasshoppers have utterly destroyed the crops\nof the farmers, while, by an unprecedented\ncombination Of misfortunes, the hunters, who\nconstitute large part of the population, have\nreturned from the plains without their usual\nsunplies of buffalo meat. While their wants\nare tins pressing, the time within which they\ncan be reached with relief from this quarter is\nvery short. In few weeks, when the frost\nshall have killed the grass on the praries, it\nwill be extremely difficult for the trains to\nreach there with supplies, for want of sub\nsistence to feed the animals. Before Novem-\nDer it is not improbable that these starving\npeople may be walled in by five hundred miles\nof snow from any possible aid except what\nmay dribble through on dog trains.\n\n\nMics Blapche De Bat, a member of the\n\n\npo.t, family. granddaughter of j. b. Booth,\n\n\n= onpounced to appear with Edwin Booth at\n\n\n.ho National Theatre, Cincinnati, next week.\n\n\nThose very accurate historians who are al\nways hunting up facts, and who make such\nhavoc with old traditions, had no merits in tn o\neyes OF Charles Lamb. II do not like\" said\nhe, \"to have my old articles of faith destroyed ].\nHere's some COMPOUNDED fellow published\nnew Life of Shakespeare, in which he main\ntlius that Cervantes and glorious Will dld not\n\n=== 4_1857-02-27_pna_sn84023127_00280776981_1857022701_0675.json\nBurlington free press. [volume] (Burlington, Vt.) 1827-1865 · 1857-02-27\ncrops: cached\nheadline: sDI ED.\n---\nAt his residence in Essex, Feb. 18th, Amos\nBliss, aged 81 years.\n\n\nIn Richmond, Feb. 9th, ls57,M Luther Prouty\nOf Jer cio, aged 60 years lie was on visit to his\ndaughter. lie left homo on Tuesday in his usual\nhealth. Thursdiy evening he was attacked with n\nain in his side, which lasted short time, but\ntired to rest free from pain. Upon entering his\nroom in the morning it was found his spirit had\nde ptrted and he had entered into the Joy of his\nLtrl, lie was kind husband, an indulgont paren\nII loss will be deeply felt in his family. For the\n,st six years he had suffered much with disease\nof the eyes which affection he bore with Christian\npatience. He has resided in the town Of Jericho\nAs years, and has been for the last Q years mem\nDer Of the Baptist Church and a devoted Christian\n\n\nMassachusetts, and Ohio papers please copy.\n\n\nIn Randolph, Feb. :, Arlstus Clark, aged 70.\n\n\nIn Barnard, Feb II, Mrs. DENIS Eaton, aged ca.\n\n\nIn Mortified, Feb. 10, Hiram Kimbill, aged 40.\n\n\nIn Rutland, Feb. 10, Minerva Bishop, wife of\nAmos Weeks, aged 3j\n\n\nIn Clar n on, Feb. JIG, Mrs A .Loantha Kinsman,\naged - years.\n\n\nIn East Franklin, Feb. 10, Capt. g. W. Kendall,\naged 80.\n\n\nIn Bridgcwatcr, John II. Thompson, aged II.\n\n=== 99_1898-01-07_p5_sn82015104_00280768790_1898010701_0061.json\nThe record-union. · 1898-01-07\ncrops: cached\nheadline: SPIRITUALISM.\n---\nMarE. JOHNSON. TEST AND BUSHES\n\n\nmedium. 1410 Fifth street.\n\n=== 10_1848-08-15_p1_sn83030313_00271742988_1848081501_0201.json\nThe New York herald. [volume] (New York [N.Y.]) 1840-1920 · 1848-08-15\ncrops: cached\nheadline: THE VERY LATEST NEWS\n\n\nFROM\nALL PARTS OF EUROPE,\n\n\nRECEIVED BY\nELECTRIC TELEGRAPH AND SPECIAL EXPRESS,\n\nTELEGRAPHlC FROM BOSTON,\n---\n\n\n\n~oO~~~~~>-~~>o~~~J>>0\"~\n\n\nL,vrKpooI, July 90 Saturday Ev'g, O'Clock.\n\n\nFrance.\n\n\nWe are in possession of our advices from Paris OF\nyesterday evening from which CLEAN the following\nitems Of intelligence -\n\n\nThere are present confined in the prisons of Paris\nthe instruction of June last 900 hu.\nman beings, of whom ISIS are men, and l75 women\nor these, $50 men, and Is women, ill., and laid up\nhospitals\n\n\nNJ Maguin had given notice Of a motion on the\nsubject of the foreign policy of the republic the de-\nbate thereon was fixed for Monday next, July 3lst\nThe Committee Foreign Affairs appointed, on\nThursday last the following representatives to pre-\npare reports the principal questions which at this\nmoment occupy public opinion in Europe -\n\n\nDrcssn de Luhys is to treat the Spanish question;\nNJ xavisT Durren will undertake those of Russia;\n\n\nn Edmond lafayette, Moidava and waachiaj\n\n\nA. Passer the German Confederation\n\n\nNJ nceksIen, Prussia and Pruisian Poland; and\n\n\nNJ Pusagur Egypt.\n\n\nThe bill for regulating the clubs of Paris SC.\ncognixed four descriptions Of associations each to be\ndifferently dealt with. Clubs, properly called,\nTO be public, and to be placed under the\ninspection of magistrate. Secret associations\nsimply forbidden. Associations not public,\nand not having any political object, merely\nrequired to make declaration of their existence to\nthe municipal authorities; and finally, associations\nnot public but in which political subjects dis\ncussed will in future be obliged to obtain permission\nTO meet, from the authorities.\n\n\nThe papers favorable to republican principles,\npress dissatisfaction at the measure because Of its\nconstituting, they ceve, permanent check\nVpon liberty. In this opinion they seem justified\nfrom the fact that there can be society without\nexpress declaration Of its objects to the magistracy\nand magisterial sanction.\n\n\nThe committee of legislation considered, on Thurs\nday, the important question of arrest for debt The\ncommittee decided, that the question of press\ning nature, they would confne themselves to recon-\nmending simply that the decree of the Provisional\nGovernment, abolshng arrest for debt, should be\nvoked.\n\n\nWe have letters Madrid to the 23d inst, incu-\nsve They state that the Queen has far overed\nfrom the incommodie attendant her disease as to\nbe able to grant audience to the Pope's nuncio,\nMonseigneur BraSnell for the purpose of presenting\nhis crede initials.\n\n\nWe have late accounts from Italy which state that\nthe King of Naples has published formal protest\nagainst the Act of Parliament which calls the Duke of\nGenoa to the throne of Sicily 10000 said to\nbe in process of equipment at Turin and to be in\nstate Of forwardness to commence the campaign. Sup\nplies Of muskets had been received from abroad and\nthe foundries were busy casting pieces of cannon.\n\n\nRussia.\n\n\nst. Petersburg advices inform us that the Cholera\nwas beginning to diminish that city. On the 14th,\nthere remained 37e2 patients under care the same\nday there 525 cases. 2ls recoveries and 3l2\ndeaths.\n\n\nAustria.\n\n\nThe last accounts from Innspruck state that the\nemperor and the royal family to leave that place\no he nj nd to\ntrun on the 2Sth.\n\n\nGermany.\n\n\nAdvices from Frankfort Of the 23d, state that Gen\nCavagnac had in amicabTe letter appealed to the\ngovernments Of Germany NO longer to give passage\nthrough their territories to the Poles who wished go\nFrance in order not to augment the elements of an\narchy at Paris.\n\n\nEngland.\n\n\nLONDON, Saturday Noon.\nThe Conan der in Chiefs very busily engaged\ning orders for the dispatch every available soldier\nwhich be spared from all the depots, to Ireland\nlarge number sent of from London last night and\nmere leaving by several trains to-day From the\nWeldon Barracks several companies leaving\nsame destination. Before the close of the week. it is\nsaid that 900 soldiers viii be duty in that\ntry More ships, with ordnance stores, ordered\nimmed iately to sail for Cork.\nJust before the departure of the steamer a gentle\nman Of the European Times office arrived from jour-\nney through the principal agricultural counties of\nEngland who found from personal inquiry and obser\nvstiop that the failure Of the potato crop exists to\nconsiderable extent, and in many districts, the\ners gathering them up for immediate sale, to pre-\nvent the entire loss which is anticipated from their\nappearance.\n\n\nA Cabinet council held at the Foreign office\nthis morning All the Ministers present. The\nevening papers say warrants have been issued this\nmorning to arrest several Of the London Chartist\nleaders\n\n\nIreland.\n\n\nThe Royal mail steamer Banshee, which sailed from\nKingstown this morning states that Dublin continues\nquiet. Mr.. J. F. Lalor has been arrested at BallyRs-\nhone, and was his way to Nsnagh Jail when the\nmail left.\n\n\nThe Latest Market Reports.\nLONDON MONEY MARKET Saturday July 2.-The money\n.e. opened s6 and closed at sss to\nSS S vas q Sc Sss sna\nstock is stX 20 s71 e1chequer bills premium The\nSATURDAY az goods and yarn\nto-day s better and there 10 eviden feeling in the trade\nCorpt in better request and have upward tendency\n\n\nNrar Law Intelligence\nGErERAL SESSIONS, Aug. l-Before Judge DAILY and |\nAldermen Crolius and stevens. John NcKeon Esq |\nDistrict Attorney.\nCatherine anderson and Catherine Je6ries we're |\nboth pxt forward trial charged with having robbed\nOf s35 The former fou nd guilty Of the theft and\nthe latter the charge Of having received stolen\ngoods. Both appeared. lived in house Of flame\nin Water street They were remanded for sentence.\nCharles Ferguson was put upon trial. on charge |\nOf burelaiously attempting break open the desk In\nexchange once No sc West Broadwa belonging to\n\n=== 17_1896-07-24_pna_sn82014248_00514155963_1896072401_0167.json\nDaily Kennebec journal. [microfilm reel] (Augusta, Me.) 1870-1975 · 1896-07-24\ncrops: cached\nheadline: kennebec.\n\nWINTHROP\n---\nDavid Lewis. the Quaker evangelist who\nhas been holding meetings in Hallowell.\nis in town. 1t is said he will not hold\nmeetings here as he is resting from his\nlabors now preparatory to beginning\neVangeistic work in Sidney.-Miss At\nkinson spoke at the Friends prayer meet\ning held at their rooms in Packard's block\nTuesday evening. Her theme was the\npossession of souls by the Holy Ghost.\nHer preaching was very effective.\n\n=== 2_1916-12-18_p5_sn82014086_00295866081_1916121801_0501.json\nNorwich bulletin. · 1916-12-18\ncrops: cached\nheadline: FAMOUS NEW ENGLAND MEDIUM\n\n AT SPIRITUAL ACADEMY\n\nWelIman C. Whitney of Springfield\n\n Spoke on Haunted Houses.\n---\nMany attended the services at the First Spiritual Academy Sunday night and heard Wellman c. Whitney of Springfield, one of New Englands best known mediums. speak on things spir- itual. MT. Whitneys topic of the ev- ening was Haunted Houses, and he prefaced his talk with Longfellows 8 poem of the same name. MT. Whit ney said that we are continually hearing of some houses being haunt ed, but in the majority of cases there was always some trivial cause such as rats running in the walls which lead to the belief that the house was haunted. That there are haunted houses there is no doubt. Occasion ing and curious things happen which are unexplained. It is impossible to explain these happenings other than by the theory that those who once lived in 1t are attracted to the house by some dreadful experience which oc- cured during their lives there. As a rule these are earth bound spirits and these occasions such as rappihg, stone throwing and other things were never indulged in by progressive or enlight- ened individuals who passed through the change of death.\n\n Following. the lecture MT. Whitney gave psychic messages accompanied by names and the relationship that the spirit bore to the people in the audience. In each case the messages were readily recognized.\n\n=== 5_1871-03-10_pna_sn83025925_00280776312_1871031001_0476.json\nThe evening telegraph. [volume] (Philadelphia [Pa.]) 1864-1918 · 1871-03-10\ncrops: cached\nheadline: A GHOST AT COLLEGE.\n---\nUproar In n Tennessee Female RducRtIoBn nj In\nsIitute-A Speculative Appar'tIon-WhO Owns\nFrom the Memphis AUoldnche, March ..\n\n\nSouth Memphis is in furore of excitement\nover occurrences of q supernatural nature re-\nGently made public, end which concern the\nBrinkley Female College and it's inmates. The\ncollege is spacious frame structure, with rather\nq sombre and dreary aspect, situated at or near\nthe intersection of Georgia and De Soto streets.\nInc Inc sch school at present numbers between ,sn forty\nRobinson, daughter of Mr.. Robinson, an attor\nney, residing on De Soto street. Her age is\nabout thirteen years, and her temperament is of\nthe nervous kind, while her health is rather what\nmight be called delicate.\n\n\nTHE MYSTERY.\n\n\nOne week ago last Tuesday Miss Clara was\nalone in one of the upper rooms of the institute\npractising her music lesson; an apparition sud\ndenly appeared before her in the shape of girl\nof about eight years of age, with sunken, lustre-\nless eyes, and strikingly emaciated form and\nfeatures. The object was virtually skeleton\nin appearance, clad in dingy and tattered dress\n0reenish and Shiny fold. 1t seemed also to be\ntransparent. A sad expression rested upon the\nfeatures of the strange visitor. Naturally the\nvision spoke quickly, in mild, pleasant tone,\nand said, Don't be alarmed, Clara,\n\n\nMY NAME is LIZZIE.\n\n\nl will not hurt you Clara stood trans6xed\nwith terror. The vision spoke again, and in\ndistinct tone related that the Brinkley College\nproperty was hers by right, title, and Decd\nthat its present pretended owners held 1t ie-\ngally, having no shadow of claim to 1t what\never; that there was no one else to claim it, as\nher people we're all dead, and she herself was\nthe last ono that had died. She desired Clara\nto obtain the papers which she had previously\nmentioned to her, and with them in her posses\nsion claim and retain the property in her own\nname and right. Unless she did so she never\nwould do good to or for any one. Of course all\nthis soon spread among the usually quiet people\nof South Memphis. Several young ladies went\nhome frightened out of their wits, and several\nOthers were affected with illness, some it was\nfeared seriously.\n\n\nrhe Alleged secret Treasure Discovered\nDocuments and Parcels Found as Indicated\nby the spectre.\n\n\nThe Avalanche of the 7th inst. continues the\nstrange story as follows\n\n\nANOTHER APPARITIoN.\n\n\nDuring Sunday Clara remain undsturbed\nby ghostly visitants, and, although extensively\ninterviewed, managed to attend Sunday school\nin the morning and church in the evening, at\ntended to the latter place by Mr.. Franklin, q\nneighbor. She passed quiet night and was\nquite cheerful yesterday morning, though think\ning frequently of the occurrences of the past\nfew days. While in the yard back of her resi-\ndence, No. 261 De Soto street, however, in COM\npany with young friend, about o'clock, she\nwas startled by the sudden appearance of\n\n\nTHE SPECTRE OF BRINKLEY COLLEGE,\nand screaming directed to it the attention of her\ncompanion who ran away In alright, though\nSne sew nOlnlDS. Inc spectre, ike the ghOSt\nof Hamlet, chided Clara for her tardiness in un-\nearthing the secret, and was answered that it\ncould not be found, when it rejoined that Clara\nmust go and seek it herself, or that Others\nwould get it. The spectre then disappeared,\nand Clara ran in trepidation into the house,\nwhere she narrated her adventure. II was re-\nsolved by the inmates to p:oceed at once to the\nexcavation, which they did, Clara going with\nMiss Franklin, q neighbor. Mr.. Robertson ac-\ncompanied by the medium and two diggers,\nand following the direction of the spirit to\nmake long story short, after digging an hour\nor more into the brick work,\n\n\nFOUND A GLASS JAR,\n\n\nwhich he passed up TO the medium, whereupon\nthey proceeded to Mr.. Robertson's residence\nfollowed by an excited crowd. The jar was at\nonce delivered to Clara, who regarding its pos\nsession as an end to her unearthly visitations\nreceived it with transports of delight, and up to\nlate hour was in the happiest moed imagin-\nable. The jar bore evidence of long conceal\nment, being covered with mould. True to the\ninstructions from the spirit, it was not opened,\nbut through its sides could be seen several bags\nand packages, together with what appeared to\nbe large yellow envelope.\n\n\nMr.. Robertson states that in accordance with\nthe request of the spirit, the jar will not be\nopened for 60 days, at the end of which period\nevery fact that may be elected by an investiga-\ntion of the recovered treasure will be given to\nthe public.\n\n=== 79_1897-04-18_p2_sn85042461_00280769721_1897041801_0554.json\nThe herald. · 1897-04-18\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nCHURCH OF THE UNITY. COR. THIRD\nand Hill sts., Rev. J. S. Thoms on, pastor;\nEaster services Sunday at IL a. m. ana\n7:30 d. M; Sabbath school at $3.50 a. m.\nsubject Sunday morning, The Bodles OF\nthe Spirits of the Departed\" subject sun\nday evening, \"Christs Doctrine ABOUT\nNatural consequences ~\n\n=== 3_1903-10-14_pna_sn90059523_00206539367_1903101401_0176.json\nThe Saint Paul globe. (St. Paul, Minn.) 1896-1905 · 1903-10-14\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Here's the HxpnotIc Elourish.\n---\nTo the Editor of the Globe.\n\n\nUnder date of Oct. Is you published\nan article by D. s. B. Johnston, Of\nst. Paul, relating to early history\nof Minnesota newspapers, in which\nthe Glencoe Register is given due\nmention as having been established\nin August, 1857, which is correct.\nIn addition, however, Mr. Johnston\nstates that of nineteen papers establish\ned between Jan. 1 and Aug. \"a. 1857, only\none now survives, namely, the Mantor-\nvile Express. This statement is wrong.\nThe Glencoe Register is still very much\nalive and has been published regularly\nevery week since it was started, fires\nfloods, bad Indians and even the I'M\nequivocal statement of Mr. Johnston to\nthe contrary notwithstanding. No, the\nRegister does not want to feel that it is\nthe ghost of paper\" floating around\nin the neither atmosphere like some dis\nembodied wrath. 1t is really too sub\nstantial a reality, especially when salary\nday comes around and bills fall due to\nbe classed in the list of dead\" ones. and\nWe ask you to kindly give us the inky,\nhypnotic flourish that will bring us back\nto life again and set us right with our\nadvertisers who are already waiting on\nus with dark hints about getting money\nunder false pretenSe, etc. Just tell them\nthat you saw US and that we are still in\nthe ring as hale and hearty as we have\nbeen at any time since we sprang into\nexistence as the fifty-ninth newspaper to\nbe established in the great state of Min\nneSota.\n\n\nw. L. Williams, Editor\n\n\nGlencoe, Mimi, Oct. 13.\n\n=== 25_1890-04-23_p1_sn82014381_00175036623_1890042301_0183.json\nSacramento daily record-union. [volume] (Sacramento [Calif.]) 1875-1891 · 1890-04-23\ncrops: cached\nheadline: A MAD MEDIUM.\n\nSlater Raps the Press Over the Knuckles,\nand Gives Some Pulling Tests.\n---\nSlater Raps the Press over the hnuckies,\nand Gives Some Puzziing Tests.\nSlater. the mind reader and as he insists,\nthe spiritually influenced had good\n\n\nhouse last night. He exbioited good deal\nof feeling over the criticisms of the press,\n| forgetting that when person poses as a\n\n\nSnowman or fortune-teller exhibiting for\n\n\ncoin, he is legitimate subject for criticism\n\n\nand description, and that the press is justi-\n| fed in telling its readers how he appears\nand what impression his performance\n\n\nmakes. He renewed his challenge for any,\n! one to clean up some thousands of dollars\nby exposing him.\n\n\nThe performer gave better entertain\n! ment last night. He made many mistakes.\n| and was cornered by several witnesses, but\n| insisted in each case that he was right that\n| he was influenced by magnetism\" to re-\n\n\nveal, and could not be in error. and that he\n\n\ncould deal only in generalities, for reasons\npersonal to his subjects. 1t is common\njustice to say, without modifying the opin-\n\n\nion already expressed, that he gave some\ntests altogether remarkable, and leaving\n\n\nscarcely any room for suspicion Of collision\n\n\nor indirect previous ascertainment of facts.\n\n\nYet it Is true that the 'crysta\" fortune\ntellers sometimes make as close predic-\n\n\ntons and reyealments.\n\n\n| These tests were, in brief, as follows: A\n\n\nyoung woman, very innocent, retiring and\n\n\nmodest in appearance. was told that she\n\n\nwas alone in this country; that she was in\n\n\ngreat doubt about taking an important\n\n\nstep-engaging herself to her escort; that\n\n\nshe was anxious about not hearing from\n\n\nhome, fearing death or illness in her fam.\nlily; that certain of her relatives were dead,\nLate sc ,rh, nh {1o LODI\n| firmed. Finally he told her that she\n\n\nwould receive letter from home on Thurs\nday morning; that it would come from\n\n\nover the sea, and bring satisfactory news\n\n\nabout a sister, concerning whom she\n| worried\" that the letter would be in\n| strange tongue. The witness confirmed\nhis statements and the prophecy as to her\nexpectation of news. She gave RECORD\nUNION reporter her name and address, and\n| stated that she is Norwegian and is alone,\n| as far as relatives are concerned, in this\nState. The test\" was recognized by the\naudience as something beyond shrewd\nguessing.\n\n\nMrs. w. G. Farnsworth was reminded\nthat she knew some one named Martin in\nher early life. She denied it. Slater in\nsisted, declared the man was dead, but that\n| long before she became Mrs. Farnsworth\nMartin had paid her warm attention and\ndesired to link his life with hers, but she\nrefused. Mrs. F., with a start, exclaimed\n| that it was true, but she had forgotten it.\nSeveral other revelations\" he made to her\n\n\nas to her wishes, aims and experiences the\n| lady emphatically denied.\n\n\nGeorge w. Stafford, by nod, admitted\n\n\nthat Slater had sized\" up certain perplexi-\n| ties of his mind and traits of his character\nAn Englishman was told that he had\n| friend, the initials of whose name were II.\n\n\nP. The Englishman denied it stoutly.\nSlater insisted, said the man lived at the\n| time in Melbourne, and his name was\n| Hervey Phipps. \"Oh, yes\" exclaimed the\n| Englishman, that is SO he passed out of\n| my life years ago, and is in England now\"\nSlater then made him confess that he had\ncome distance to attend the show\" and\n\n\nhad hurried to the train \"like thunder\"\n\n\nSlater added that he had come from the\n\n\nnorth, from Red Bluff or Redding He\n\n\nwas told he was in error as to the direc-\n\n\nton. Subsequently the Englishman, who\n| declined to permit his name to be pub\n| lished, said that he had come from Stock\n\n\nton, had hurried to catch train to reach\nhere, and was going to Redding. 1t de-\n\n\nveloped that he is well-known San Fran\ncisco insurance man. that he had attended\nseveral of Skaters seances in San Francisco\n\n\nand had received tests, but he insisted that\nSlater does not know him, and declares\n\n\nthat the Phipps referred to is Lord Hervey\n| Phipps, an old chum of his youth, and\n\n\nthat Slater nor no one in California could\nhave known of him.\n\n\n| The operator pointed out lady and\n\n\nasked her if she had not in the afternoon\nbeen talking of marriage. She denied it.\nSlater insisted. She reiterated her denial\n\n\nso far as she could remember. But you\n\n\ndid, retorted Slater, land you talked with\n| the lady two seats from you on the right\nabout marriage, this afternoon at home\"\n| The second lady thus appealed to stated\n| that Slater was correct.\n\n\n| Another lady admitted that Slater's de-\nscription of her fathers family as to num-\n\n\nDer and sexes was accurate. Mrs. Wallace\n\n\nconfessed that his revelation of the subject\n\n\nof her thoughts at 3 in the afternoon was\n| right A railroad man was told his occu-\n\n\npatton, and warned to beware of Thursday\n\n\nweek. as on that day a railroad accident\n| would occur and he would be related to it.\n\n\nAn elderly woman adpitted that the\noperator correctly described a scene of\nhurry and some words at home\n\n\nabout coming to the SHOW\" at\n\n\na given hour. Proposals of marriage\nwere forecast. some ludlcrous advice given,\n\n\nsome mimicry INDUCED in, and as said\n\n\nnumerous guesses made wide of the mark.\n| but tne tests reclted were apparently sat\n| isfactory and suggest that some better ex-\nPLANTATION than guessing be given of them.\nThe mind-reader expressed the belief that\n\n\nhe would be able to still more puzzle his\n\n\nauditors tonight, and emphatically an\n\n\nnounced that he would make ii his last\n\n\nnight in Sacramento-this evidently in re-.\n\n\nSpouse to what he deems harsh treatment\n\n\nby the press. Slater reiterated last night\n\n\nthat he had foretold by some days the loss\n\n\nof the steamer Queen City and the explo-\n\n\nsion or the steamer Julia, and San Fran\n\n\ncisco newspaper men say that his state\n| ment in that respect is true. He would be\n\n\nin better favor with his audience if he\n\n\nwould not point out ladies and attempt to\n\n\ntell what physical ills sfflict them. The\n| Spirits\" should be more considerable.\n\n\n| SERIOUS ALLEGATION.\n\n=== 3_1825-01-20_pna_sn88053080_00271740244_1825012001_0301.json\nThe Wilmingtonian, and Delaware register. (Wilmington, Del.) 1824-1825 · 1825-01-20\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nsoon able to converse with HIM III MS own\nstyle and in short time he believed every\nthing told him as firmly as if it had been\nholy writ.-One morning, about two months\nfrom this time the Chevalicr entered my\nchamber with deep grief and told me ne\nmust depart, he could stay no longer, he nau\ncombatted with his passions like man, but\ncould do so no more. told him there was\nnothing to oppose the accomplishment Of ms\nwishes as his father and friends were all in\nits favour. He answered, all this was no.\nthing as long as there remained single ray\nof hope in Antoniette's bosom, although he\nhad long since given up all hopes of his\nbrothers. existance, yet his fate would re-\nmain wrctched until eternity would break its\nsilence and the grave testify for him. Is it\ncertainty that can make you happy?' Hap\npy. l doubt whether can ever be so. Oh\nif my brother saw my agonies perhaps he\nwould himself lead her to my arms\" de\nclared that | could fulfil his wishes, and re\nmove all his scruples as to the means OF\naccomplishing it. 1t was decided that l\nshould coniure the ghost Of the deceased, for\nwhich demanded IA days to prepare myself\nsuitably. Mistrust in the thing itself and\ndoubt Of my skill were the least obstacles\nhad to snr,nount AS soon rs the consent of\nthe family and Of Antoniette (Which found\nthe most difficulty had been obtained, the\nthird day was appointed for the deed. The\nintermediate time was spent BY them III\nprayer, fasting, vigis, solitude and mystic\nal instruction. Finally the expected hoar\narriyed-but | will not trouble you with\ndescription of all the jugglery | practlscd--\nsuffice it to say all my expectations were\nfulfilled The Marchcsc, tie Young Count\ness and her mother, with the Chevalier and\nIa, 70\nappeared in the dress of Barbarian slave,\nwith deep wound in the neck. lmalc this\nvariation from the general belief, that it\nmight not agree too much Herewith leafs It\nmight create suspicious. | asked the spec\ntire whether there was any thing on earth\nthat it called His, and whether he had LETT\nany thing behind that was dear to him! II\nshook its head three time and extended its\nhands towards heaven. Before it vanished\nit stript ring from its finger, which was\nafterwards found on the 1oor near the place\nit stood, and which the countess found to be\nher wedding ring. The family were now\nconvinced Of the death of lcronymo; they\npublished it abroad, aud from that time put\non mourning The circumstance Of the\nring likewise removed every doubt from\nAntoniette, but the impression which the\nappearance of the spectre had made on her\nwas SO great that it caused violent tcvei,\nand had nearly terminated her earthly ca\nrecr and with it all hopes OF her lover At\nter she had recovered she insisted on taking\nthe veil and 1t required the most torcible re-\nmonstranccs IT her father conlcssor to pre-\nvent her. With much trouble they forced\nher consent from her and the last day of her\nmourning was appointed for the happy period.\n1t arrived; and Lorenzo received his tremb-\nINriJf ot ti,a IL \"a MI\nlingly feast awaited the many guests whom\nthe happy old Marchesc wished to be Wit\nnesses OF his joy. Amongst this crowd some\nOf those around me pointed out a Franciscan\nmonk, who with long thin figure and ash\n\n\ncoloured countenanCe stood immoycablc IS q\npillar, regarding the welded pair with sad\nand serious look; he alone of all the joyous\nmultitude seemed mournful. This strange\nconduct was observed by the whole compA-\nny and caused sudden silence amongst them\nwithout however in the least disturbing the\nmonk, who continued in the same posture,\nregarding the pair in the same sad and serious\nlook. All were alarmed butthe young coant\n\n\ness, who, in the countenance of the only one\nthat seemed to sympathize with her, found\nmatter to noulish her own grief. The crowd\ngradually decreased, and midnight was pass\ned; the music grew fainter and the candles\nburnt QullGf, Dut the monk occupied tnc\nsame station. The tables were removed and\nthe family formed a narrower circle, but the\nmonk remained unlimited iii the midst OF\nthem and no one seemed willing to address\nhim. Already the time for retiring APP\n\n\npreached, the trademarks were busyng\nthemselves with the bride, WHO gave a sup\nplicating look to the venerable stranger, but\nhe returned it not. The groomsmen were\nsurrounding ;the groom when the Marchcsc\nwho alone Of all the others did not appear to\nsee the stranger, or at least was not suiprs-\ned at seeing him, thus began, \" Alas that\nwe are all 50 happy now, and my son Jero-\nnymo should be wanting - Have YOU in\n\n\nvited him and did he absent himself\" these\nwere the first words the stranger uttered.\nWe viewed him with terror \"Ah he is\ngone whence no traveller returns, replied\nthe Marchese, venerable Sir, you misunder-\nstand me, my son Jeronymo is dead\" 'Pcr-\nhaps he is only afraid to appear in such\ncompany, continued the monk, who knows\nhow your son Jeronymo may look Let him\nhear the last voice he ever heard Request\nyour son Lorenzo to call him What mean,\nthis, was whispered by all; Lorenzo chan\n\n\ned colour, and confess my hair began tc\n\n\nrise. The monk in the meantime had gone\n\n\nTO a side-board and filled glass and drank il\n\n\nsaving, The memory of our dear jcro-\n\n\nnymo, whosoever loved him Go as did ;\n\n\nWhosoever you may be, venerable Sir cx-\n\n\nclaimed the Marchese, you have named\n\n=== 1_1890-05-14_pna_sn84026409_00280776518_1890051401_0423.json\nThe Somerset herald. [volume] (Somerset, Pa.) 1870-1936 · 1890-05-14\ncrops: cached\nheadline: -'-l'''------7'-'7----\n\n\nA Dead Mans Ghost.\n---\nCUMBERLAxD, Nd, May Am interest\ning ghost story comes from the town of\nSharpsburg, Md. About ten days ago Sam\nuel Swain, ali aged citizen of the place, died\n\n\nat his residence and was buried. Last Sat\nurday evening about dusk Mr.. and Mrs.\nSamuel Cramer and Mr.. and Mrs. Eugene\nBowers were sitting in room on the ground\nfloor when all four were startled at seeing\nthe form and face of the deceased hr. Swain\nstanding at the window looking in. One OF\nthe ladies fell in a dead faint, while the 9TH\ners in the apartment ran from the room into\nthe open air. The attention of several mtn\nin the street was attracted to the house by\nthe commotion, and they, too. saw the fig\nure of Mr. Swain standing at the window\n\n\nAs they looked the figure vanished away in\nto air. The matter created a good deal o\n\n\nexcitement, and Sunday the grave was ex.\namined to see if il was intact, but no eyi.\ndence of disturbance was visible. The pco\n\n\npie residing in the house moved out Monday\nmorning, and cannot be induced to return\n\n=== 5_1916-04-15_p3_sn82014189_00279526028_1916041501_0057.json\nThe daily Alaskan. · 1916-04-15\ncrops: cached\nheadline: APRIL wRAITHs\n\nBy Perrin Holmes LOwley\n---\nAnd now another lyric April blows Her dim tunes from the hills. Spring roses twine. Clear floods of sunlight, coll with\n\n mountain snOws, Brim all the world with sparkling\n\n golden wine, Old joys are boIn again. oid\n\n dreams arise. And call my sport. like far, gleam-\n\n ing seas. Old sweethearts kiss my brow and\n\n mouth and eyes, With :!ps grown sweet as April rev\n\n cries. Forgotten Aprils, clad in cherry\n\n blooms, Come drifting back along the or\n\n chard streams: They fill my heart with olden, faint\n\n perfumes. And haunt me with the lethargy Of\n\n dreams!\n\n=== 29_1858-04-13_pna_sn84027851_00295862294_1858041301_0055.json\nThe Camden weekly journal. [volume] (Camden, South-Carolina) 1853-1861 · 1858-04-13\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Negro Haunting.\n---\n# Our British anti. slavery friends love to hunt\n.negros as well as they do Asiatiie BENG\n\n\nalleged that the Sqosoos, on the e5asf 5iflics,\nhad killeda Biti sabiees 81 twS f5fi Sons\ncause not stated, bat pEobafls sd}hcisa FOR 4\nverdict ,uf 5ustfEable 9holnicide, an expedi ton\n\n\nOf boats hota tie English ssquadfoh. iihla1ned,\nby $50 sailors and marines, fprocee edeed up the\nriver about the first Of February aud ii, seyea\ndays ut is boasteIi Fin letter from Sierra Ee6neY\nthey burned all she negee towns, aaf kiftsf be-\ntween three aud four hopdredayf ths, 65soos.\nwhile the Eagishhad not ,aSsi.gts a,4ir3 killed.,\n\n\nand only ten wounded q: As tile sostensibIe\n\n\nbusiness Of the British squadron tie coast\n\n\nof Africa isyto stop the slave trade; il. Cannot\n\n\nbe denied that they haYs done iit as ffaetualy\nin regardto the four hundred sSsoos5os as they\ndid, fsw years ago. forfi filftsea nssr,,E\nresidents Of Lagos, whom they ghtefsd fo,rr ;\nthe purpose of stopping the sinfernaI traffic .\nBut thst bloody Intervention ofs stheS SphiIanthros\npists praduced no great permanent fffset upos\nThe afficaas, beyond the 1900 aheec bones\n\n\nwere dedicated to freedom; and it prebabIg\n\n\nwillnot' be long befbre the soosoos, ifthere are\nany OF them ieft, win lifequfs to Be sfnittenagain\nblythe gentlehand Of Christian civilization,\n~ AZ ss7.t,s, X :, Bostoa Posi- -5-s,\n\n=== 45_1921-04-09_p11_sn83045462_00280656926_1921040901_0017.json\nEvening star. · 1921-04-09\ncrops: cached\nheadline: sPIRITUALISM.\n---\nMRS. HENKLE HOLDS MEETINGS SUNDAY.\nwednesday and Friday evenings At 3 O'Clock.\nIL 4th st. sc. Everybody gets a reading.\nPhone Line 1O49.\n\n=== 1_1922-08-14_pna_sn85042345_00237281445_1922081401_0238.json\nThe Morning Tulsa daily world. [volume] (Tulsa, Okla.) 1919-1927 · 1922-08-14\ncrops: cached\nheadline: FARMERS GHOST\n\n\nHAUNTED A FARM\n\nNew Owner Found Body\nof Man Missing Years\n\n\nin Old Well\n---\nGAINESVILLE, Aug. ia\nghost which farmers living near\nPaines Hollow say has been haunt\ning them for seven years, is 'at\nrest today. 1t disappeared several\ndays ago with the burial of the\nbody OF Henry Lipenstick, which\nwas found In an abandoned well on\na farm.\n\n\nLipenstick, farmland. disap-\npeared seven years ago. Since then\nthe farm IS said to have had poor\ncrops A farmhouse burned down\nand the ghost\" was blamed for\nthat. owner after owner has\ncome and gone.\n\n\nCarl Logies purchased the farm\nabout n year ago. Whenever he\nwent 10 his barn at night, Logies\nsaid today, he would see \"a white\nwralth-like figure\" walking about.\nAt times he declared, he followed\nIl with his gun in hand and II al\nways disappeared in the direction\nOf the we'll.\n\n\nDetermined to find out about the\n} apparition, Logies cleaned out the\nold we'll which had been filled with\nstones. He came upon the body of\nLipenstick. Sheriff Pink was\ncalled and he and his deputies sus-\npected murder. The next day the\nsheriff ordered the body buried and\nnow the \"ehost\" is gone. Frank\nLerman, who owned the farm at\nthe time Lipenstick disappeared, IS\nIN Lake county jail. lie was bound\nover TO the grandjury on charges\nOf murder.\n\n=== 35_1910-10-01_p1_sn84029690_00212473182_1910100101_0629.json\nThe Hays free press. [volume] (Hays, Kan.) 1908-1924 · 1910-10-01\ncrops: cached\nheadline: OOOOOOOOOQ OOOl0OOOO\n\n\nAngles Ambition\n\nBy GERALD PRIME\n\n\n\ncopyright 1910 by Associated Literary Press\n---\nOON|--~--' -\n\n\nThey were talking it over-Angie\nand her pretty mother.\n\n\n\"It certainly must have been graft\nfytng-in a way\" said Mrs. Calhoun,\nin her leisurely southern monotone.\n\n\nHer daughter was buttering piece\nOf toast, an occupation which seemed\nto demand her utmost concentration.\nMrs. Calhoun slipped her tea in silence.\nand with look of polite expectancy.\nShe always drank tea at breakfast,\nhabit which gave her an added dis\ntinction in Alaabma, where such a\npreference is unusual.\n\n\nThey were not in Alabama now.\nMrs. Calhoun regretted the fact, and\nthat they were perched-most tnse-\ncurely it seemed to heron the elev-\nhotel She had been trying for at\nleast ten minutes to find out whether\nor not her daughter shared her regret,\na bit of information which the young\ner woman seemed inclined to withhold\nfor the present.\n\n\nWe'll, do you really feel-pleased\nover your-I mean our venture?\ncontinued Mrs. Calhoun, with gentle\ninsistence, putting down her cup and\nsaucer and letting her hands fall list\nlessly into her lap.\n\n\nThis was a direct question, and as\nsuch demanded an answer-even\nthough it came from her mother, who.\nAngie knew perfectly well, would\nhave been neither hurt nor resentful\nhad her daughter elected to remain\nsilent.\n\n\nNO.\" she said, with a little laugh\nwhich did not ring right-a fact which\ndid not escape the attention of her\nmother and added greatly to her dis\nquietude-\"it wasn't at all gratifying,\nand I'm not bit pleased with it. 1\njust hate 1.-THE whole business. from\nbeginning to end\"\n\n\n\"Oh, Angie\n\n\n81 knew it,\" the girl interrupted\nrecklessly. Il was determined to do\nit and nothing could have prevented\nme when the chance came. 1 wouldn't\nhave accepted a kingdom in exchange\nfor my opportunity to come to New\nYork and be an actress. An actress,\nindeed\"\n\n\nYou certainly have talent, Angie, a\nheap of it,\" her mother affirmed 10y\nally\n\n\nNot a little Bit\" declared her\ndaughter positively. I'm Angela Cal\nhoun first, last and all the time, and 1\nnever lose my hateful identity for a\nsingle minute. 1t makes no difference\nwhether the part is Queen Elizabeth\nor Little Eva-Fm Angela Calhoun all\nthe time\"\n\n\nI'm mighty glad you are\" said her\nmother, with a sudden assumption of\nspirit that almost transformed her.\nIl hope nothing will ever happen to\nchange you into anybody else. That\nis-unless-\n\n\nJust so-unless,\" Angie came to\nthe rescue.\n\n\nHow they did applaud you last\nnight\" said Mrs. Calhoun, returning\ntactfully to the matter in hand. \"All\nthose people must have enjoyed 1t, if\nyou didn't. though they never would\nget enough of that last song. 1 don't\nsee how you can feel that it wasn't q\nsuccess. Everybody in the house was\ndelighted. Mr. Goldsmith was charm\ned. He came into the box to congratu-\nlate me, you. know\"\n\n\nw LAT ulu ne say!\n\n\nHe was too excited to saw much of\nanything. Our little girls a big win\nner' was one thing. Really. Angie\nwhen l saw how those people liked It\nand were carrying on, 1 couldn't help\nfeeling-well. proud of you\"\n\n\nYou dear! l wish for your sake\nthat l might do something really\nworth WHILE\" said Angie, in a voice\nthat was passing beyond her control.\n\n\nNever mind, DEAR\" becoming at\nonce the tender maternal CONSIDER:\nyou have but to say the word and\nwe will go south at once. We can go\nback to Talladega in a blaze of glory\nYour success last night has made that\npossible. Shall l begin to pack I'D.\nAngler\"\n\n\nThat was all that was REQUIRED to\nbring about instant reaction. The girl\nabandoned her sensational lapse with\na dexterity that would have served her\nadmirably on the stage.\n\n\nDon't let us be sentimental. NOM\nsey,\" she said. I'VE really can't af-\nford it. No, we won't go back to Tai\nladega. There isn't soul in the town\nthat 1 ever want to set eyes on\nagain.\n\n\nMrs. Calhoun sighed family. .T\nused to think that you and Geoff Mar\ntin\" she began. but Angie would not\npermit her.\n\n\n~~~~@~~~~~~~~~\"~o-~-~~~-~\n\n=== 27_1912-10-26_p1_sn86061215_00295875392_1912102601_0205.json\nOttumwa tri-weekly courier. [volume] (Ottumwa, Iowa) 1903-1916 · 1912-10-26\ncrops: cached\nheadline: REV. FATHER FELIX BYRNE DIS\ncoURSED ON ORDINANCE or\n\n\nCATHOLIC CHURCH..\n\nQUOTED SCRIPTURE\n\nAuthority For Forelvins OF Sins Point\ned Out BY Speaker From,\nthe Non.CathoIic\n\n\nBible.\n\nOrder Of Services.\n---\n, Order Of Services.\n; 1880 and s a. m. - Low\n. mass. Five minute instruction\n\n\n. for children at the O'Clock\nservice.\n. 1880 p. m.--Answers to ques-\n. tions, lecture and benediction.\n\n\n, Lecture Subjects.\n. Thursday night-\"The God\n. on the Catholic Altar\"\n\n\n, Friday night THE Faith\n\n\n. Divine\"\n\n\n. Saturday night-\"The Wor-\n. ship of Mary\"\n. Sunday night-\"Ghosts.'\n\n=== 91_1894-03-09_p1_sn84022835_00237285050_1894030901_0739.json\nThe Red Cloud chief. · 1894-03-09\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nus the mistress OF the house in?\" in.\nCURED the peddler. 'No,\" replied the\ntired looking, timid woman who had\ngone to the kitchen door TO answer the\nknock. Its her afternoon out\"\n","numLines":1669,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1669}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"efd096e4-cdc0-4bba-b868-18c4ba794cc8","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"co