l, cast of\n311\tplayers. Harry Green, the XII\n312\tbitzer Zone who knows all there\n313\tis to know about everything\n314\tand who is willing and ready\n315\tto tell it at little or no provo\n316\tcation), Is marvellous. The\n317\tplot, Of minor Importance, deals\n318\twith one business man, a cigar\n319\tstore owner, to be exact, who\n320\tdoes cash trade with his pa.\n321\tirons and credit with the other\n322\tfellow, until the time of reck-\n323\toning is forced. The way in\n324\twhich he finally puts it over\n325\tprovides the thread of the\n326\tstory. Mary Brian is the\n327\tcharming daughter, and Neil\n328\tHamilton is the boy friend\"\n329\tTenen Holtz, Guy Oliver and\n330\tAlbert Gran contribute striking\n331\tperformances in lesser charac-\n332\tter bits.\n333\t\n334\t\n335\tHarry Green, however, holds\n336\tthe center of the stage. capaDIy\n337\tfulfilling the demands of the\n338\tplay. The Kibitzer\" (ParG-\n339\tmount, which does indeed\n340\tabound in humor, logic and\n341\tt h rough entertainment, is\n342\theartily recommended to those\n343\twho find pleasure in a finished\n344\tand perfectly enacted perform.\n345\tance.\n346\t\n347\t\n348\tThe stage show, Haunting a\n349\tbizarre array of brilliant stage\n350\tand color effects, is entitled\n351\t\"Toytown.\" Mile. Pavla.\n352\tdancing doll, is charming:\n353\tAileen Clark. possessor of a\n354\tvery sweet voice, sings delight\n355\tfully; Fortunello and Cirillino,\n356\tEuropean acrobats, are seen\n357\tin some difficult tumbling\n358\tfeatS: Gus Alexander and\n359\tEnoch Light contribute to the\n360\texcellent bill, and Ivan Tries\n361\tault and Carlton Emmy, with\n362\this mad wagS, gather in a\n363\tgoodly share of applause.\n364\t\n365\t\n366\tSilly Symphony\" a sound\n367\tcomic, is about the best illus\n368\ttration of Synchronized screen\n369\tmovement conceivable, a gem\n370\tin its class. Hearst Movietone\n371\tis replete with news events.\n372\tHarry Borjes and his Synco-\n373\tpators and Charles Gaige at\n374\tthe organ provide their Gus\n375\ttomary melodies.\n376\t\n377\t=== 4_1904-06-16_pna_sn86082061_00415629710_1904061601_0913.json\n378\tHerald and news. [volume] (West Randolph, Vt.) 1878-1943 · 1904-06-16\n379\tcrops: cached\n380\theadline: DRAWING BY REFLECTION.\n381\t\n382\tSomething That May Seem Easy, But\n383\tIs in Fact Very Diff\n384\t\n385\t\n386\tfcult.\n387\t---\n388\t''Now here\" said the lively boarder,\n389\trs something that you may all have\n390\tseen before, but that was new TO me, and\n391\tl am sure it will interest you II you\n392\thaven't seen it.\n393\t\n394\t\n395\t'You take a small. square mirror.\n396\tlike this one that | find standing here\n397\ton the mantel, and rest II on the table, or\n398\tget somebody TO hold il there for you.\n399\tstanding upright and with face toward\n400\tyou.\n401\t\n402\t\n403\tThen you put down on the table, in\n404\tfront Of the mirror, a piece Of white pa\n405\tper, and you provide yourself with lead\n406\tpencil with which to draw on the paper.\n407\tThen you cover over that hand that\n408\tyou are to draw with, or have some\n409\tbody cover 1t over, with paper that will\n410\tconceal the hand and the pencil from d.\n411\trect view, though you can see the rehec-\n412\tton Of the pencil In the mirror before\n413\twhich you are holding it.\n414\t\n415\t\n416\tThen, by the aid Of the reflection in\n417\tthe mirror Of the lines you draw, you\n418\ttry TO draw on the paper a square with\n419\tdiagonal intersecting lines across It.\n420\t\n421\t\n422\t\"You will find that with the hand\n423\tholding the pencil covered over, and only\n424\tthe reflection Of the pencil IN the mirror\n425\tto guide you, you can draw the square\n426\tpretty accurately without any great diff\n427\tdeulty. You are likely to get II all right\n428\tat the first or at least at the second trial,\n429\tbut the drawing of the diagonal lines\n430\tyou will find to be very different prop\n431\tosition.\n432\t\n433\t\n434\tYou can see the pencil In the mirror,\n435\tand see just what you are doing with It,\n436\tbut even with that help It is by no means\n437\tas easy 20 draw those two diagonal lines\n438\tas you might imagine Oh, some people\n439\tcan do 1t pretty well, after one or two\n440\ttrials, but more cannot; and some peo-\n441\tpie that can draw the square all right\n442\tmake. when they come TO try to put in\n443\tthe diagonals, lines going every which\n444\tway, as zigzag and crooked as the err\n445\tratio markings Of a planchette pencil\n446\twhen you press your hand upon the\n447\tboard\n448\t\n449\t\n450\t1T seems as though II must be very\n451\teasy, but It is really very hard Now,\n452\tyou folks try IT,\"\n453\t\n454\t\n455\tAnd they did try It, and they found\n456\tmore fun in it, they said, than IN any\n457\tthing they had tried in a long time\n458\t\n459\t=== 1_1842-01-01_p1_sn86058217_00271744742_1842010101_0405.json\n460\tRichmond palladium. [volume] (Richmond, IA [i.e. Ind.]) 1839-1854 · 1842-01-01\n461\tcrops: cached\n462\theadline: WHOLE NUMBER 80.\n463\t---\n464\tclass 1t this time; and thinks i, preferable to i'm\n465\titaie the example of England By giving aid to\n466\tthe private enterprise Of our citizens in\n467\tconstructing packet ships, TO ply between this\n468\tcountry and foreign ports. He recommends\n469\tthat n steam ship IT medium size be5uili, TO\n470\ttest the utility of vessels built Of that matters\n471\tnj. lie discourigss an increase in the num-\n472\tDer of or lIne-o<batIle ships. and prefers\n473\tan addition 10 our irtgatcs Of The largest class\n474\tHe urges the propiIety Of establishing higher\n475\tgrades IN the navy, ms by former\n476\tSecretaries. and assigns his reasons for desiring\n477\tthis change.\n478\t\n479\t\n480\tA large increase in The Marine Corps is re-\n481\tcommended, as also an amendment Of ire laws\n482\tand regulations for the government Of that\n483\tcorps; for which purpose n new code has already\n484\tbeen prep