ffice\n1127\tseeking. 'The post of honor is private\n1128\tstation\"\n1129\t\n1130\t\n1131\tYour modest bid for Office reminds us\n1132\tof the answer of the old topper, who\n1133\twhen asked 10 take drink always said\n1134\t\"w-a-ll, l hardly ever take anything, but\n1135\twhen I do it's about this time.\"\n1136\tLook the ground over carefully, Kim\n1137\tball, then \"go for\" your drink.\n1138\t| -.-ss-\n1139\t\n1140\t\n1141\td. A. Baldwin, Esq, President of the\n1142\tWest Wisconsin r. r. in speech at th'\n1143\tcelebration in Eau Claire last week speak\n1144\ting of the railroad said:\n1145\t\n1146\t\n1147\t\"For the success of the enterprise they\n1148\tshould thank Congress, the Legislature. thu\n1149\t| Governor, Messrs. Thorpe, Marston, Capt\n1150\t| Wilson, Senator Price and especially jacob\n1151\tHumbird, who had opened his long pursC\n1152\tand enabled them to do the work thus far\"\n1153\t\n1154\t\n1155\t1t has been asserted by some of those\n1156\twho oppose Judge Price in the congress\n1157\tsional contest, that he is 10 no way res\n1158\tponsible for the success of the above\n1159\troad, and by others that he actually\n1160\tused his influence in opposition TO III\n1161\t1 construction.\n1162\t\n1163\t\n1164\tHow IA this gentlemen? \"a it posi-\n1165\tBE President Baldwin Is misiakeng\n1166\t\n1167\t=== 9_1900-11-30_p1_sn98060035_00202199203_1900113001_0222.json\n1168\tBarre evening telegram. (Barre, Vt.) 1898-19?? · 1900-11-30\n1169\tcrops: None\n1170\theadline: \n1171\t\n1172\tSpirit, Medium and Occult\n1173\t\n1174\t\n1175\tScientist.\n1176\t\n1177\tExpert PaImist'y and\n1178\tIPsychometric. Cneir-\n1179\t\n1180\t\n1181\t0macy Healer.\n1182\t\n1183\tHOW TO OVERCOME ALL\n1184\t\n1185\t\n1186\tTECUELEs.\n1187\t---\n1188\tYour past, your present life and your\n1189\tentire future and every thing can be told\n1190\tby consulting Professor De Matthew, the\n1191\tWorld Eminent Cl1iroyant and Scientific\n1192\tPalmist, who is the greatest master of\n1193\toccult science and psychic forces the world\n1194\thas ever produced.\n1195\t\n1196\t\n1197\tHis predictions are always correct, and\n1198\tnever fail to come true. as many people\n1199\tin this citv and elsewhere viii testify.\n1200\t\n1201\t\n1202\tAll the events OF life are written in your\n1203\thand. Changes in business, sickness,\n1204\tfamily troubles and personal affairs OF\n1205\twhatever kind are absolutely recorded.\n1206\tMany owe their entire success to wise\n1207\tcounsel given through PALMiSTRY, the\n1208\tonly scientific method Of reading the\n1209\tfail, and you are in doubt, it will point the\n1210\tway. Mistakes can be avoided, enemies\n1211\toutwitted, and success attained. There\n1212\tare always two paths in life: a little more\n1213\tknowledge and victory will be yours. 1t\n1214\tis worth trying, as thousands are con-\n1215\tstantly doing. Prof. Matthew, through\n1216\tyears of study and travel, combined with\n1217\tpsychical gifts that have won the admira-\n1218\tton of the world, can guide and direct\n1219\tyou. He gives you the exact facts and\n1220\tadvises you as only a thorough student\n1221\tcan. Thousands in this and other lands\n1222\tindorse his wonderful powers as a bust\n1223\tness and social adviser.\n1224\t\n1225\t\n1226\tIf you are contemplating a change, are\n1227\tunhappy or seemingly unfortunate; il\n1228\tthings go wrong in your affairs, an inter-\n1229\tview with him will set you right.\n1230\tFLORENCE MARRYAT says\" Professor\n1231\t| Matthew saved me serious mistakes. His\n1232\tpredictions have been fulfilled. was\n1233\tamazed at his accuracy\" Thousands OF\n1234\tothers say the same thing. There is help\n1235\tfor you.\n1236\t\n1237\t\n1238\tProf. Matthew has been consulted by the\n1239\tGear of Russia, Queen Of England, Emper-\n1240\tors of Germany. China and Japan; also by\n1241\tdistinguished men and women in every\n1242\t| walk of life. He has in his possession a\n1243\tlarge collection Of photographs of palms\n1244\tand autograph letters from the crowned\n1245\theads of Europe, He is acknowledged\n1246\tthe greatest living authority on palmistry.\n1247\t\n1248\t=== 21_1839-10-03_pna_sn84026897_00237288336_1839100301_0136.json\n1249\tEdgefield advertiser. [volume] (Edgefield, S.C.) 1836-current · 1839-10-03\n1250\tcrops: None\n1251\theadline: \n1252\t\n1253\t@@ostie &&eeess.\n1254\t\n1255\tFor the .idcertiscr.\n1256\tSIR HUMPHREY DAVY.\n1257\t\n1258\tMr.. Editor /:\n1259\t---\n1260\tSIR HUMPHREY DAVY.\n1261\tMr.. Editor:\n1262\t\n1263\t\n1264\tDid YOU ever hear Of Sir Hunphry\n1265\tDavy as poet Asa rhymsr. his fame\n1266\thas spread over the civilized world hui\n1267\tit is no, generally known that he had a\n1268\tpoetic genius of the highest order.\n1269\t\n1270\t\n1271\tso\",hey, Coleri'lge. and others. said Of\n1272\thim, that had he turned his attention to\n1273\tpne,ry, he would have attained the first\n1274\trank.\n1275\t\n1276\t\n1277\t1t is said. by his hiographer. that, like\n1278\tP\"pe, \" he lisped in numbers\" is\n1279\tperhaps, not To he regretted that he cult\n1280\twaited his talent for philosophical pursuits\n1281\tas The world has heen so largely henefiIrd\n1282\thy his investigations His safety lamp is\n1283\ta richer offering 10 the world, than all the\n1284\tpoetry of Hnmer, Milton. and Byron.\n1285\t\n1286\t\n1287\tHis poem. called \"The Sons of\n1288\tGenius\" contains many passages, A.\n1289\tbeautiful ] think. as any in the EnglI>i'\n1290\tlanguage. Wllyou publish the following\n1291\textract\n1292\t\n1293\t\n1294\tBright bursting through thr awful veil Of night\n1295\tThu lunar beams upoI the ocean play;\n1296\tThe watery iows shine with trembling light,\n1297\tv here the swift breezes skim alon. the sea.\n1298\tTho glirnmering stars in yon ethereal plain\n1299\tGrow rale, and fade before the lunar-heams;\n1300\tSave where fair Veus. shining Ger the main\n1301\tConspicuous still, with fainter radiance CLEANS\n1302\tClear as the azure firmlmen above,\n1303\tSave where the white cloud luats upon the\n1304\thrrcz.;\n1305\t\n1306\t\n1307\tAl tranquil is tie bosom of the grove.\n1308\tSave where the zephyr warbles through the\n1309\ttrees.\n1310\t\n1311\t\n1312\tNow The poor shepherd wandcrm, 10 his home.\n1313\tSurveys the darkrning s,'ene with fearful eye\n1314\tOn every green sees little elfins roam\n1315\tAnd haggard sprifes along the monnbcams fy.\n1316\tWhile Superstition rules tho vulGars soul,\n1317\tForbds the energies of man TO rise.\n1318\tRaised far above her low. her mean control,\n1319\tAspiring Genils seeks her native skies.\n1320\tShe loves The silent, solitary hours;\n1321\tShe loves the stiffness Of the starry night,\n1322\tWhen Ger the bright'ning view SeIenc pours\n1323\tThe soft ellulgence other pcusve light.\n1324\t\n1325\t\n1326\tTHE SWAY OF REASON.\n1327\tBefore her lucid, al-enli ghtening ray,\n1328\tThe paIlid spcctres oftlle night retire;\n1329\tShe drive. tho gloumv terrors far away,\n1330\t| And fills the bosom with celestial fire.\n1331\tInspired by her, the sons Of genius rise\n1332\tAbove all earthly thoughts, all vulgar care\n1333\tWealth, power, and grandeur, they alike\n1334\t\n1335\t\n1336\tdesrse,\n1337\t\n1338\t\n1339\tEnraptur'd by the good, the great, the fair.\n1340\ta thousand varying Joys to them belong,\n1341\tThe charms of nature and her changefn\n1342\t\n1343\t\n1344\tscenes:\n1345\t\n1346\t\n1347\tTheirs is the music of the vcrnal song;\n1348\tAnd theirs the colcrs of the vernal plains.\n1349\tTheirs is the purple-,inged evening ray,\n1350\tWith all the radiance ofthe evening sky;\n1351\tThere's is the splendor of thc'rise day,\n1352\tEnshrined in glory by the suns bright eye.\n1353\tFor them, the zephyr fans The odorous dale;\n1354\tFor them the warbling streamlet softly flows;\n1355\tFor them the Dryads shade the verdant vale\n1356\tFor them sweet Phiome attunes her woes.\n1357\tTo them no wakefnl moonbeam shines in vair\n1358\tOn the dark bosom of the trackess wood;\n1359\tSheds its mild radiance DER the desert plain;\n1360\tOr softly glides along the chrystal floods\n1361\tYet not alone delight the soft and fair,\n1362\tAlike the grander scenes ofnaturc move;\n1363\tYe, not alone her beauties claim their care,\n1364\tThe great, sublime, aud terrible thcy love.\n1365\t\n1366\t\n1367\t\n1368\tWhen the red lishnings though the ether fly,\n1369\tAnd the white flaming pillows ash:he shores\n1370\tWhen to the rafting tIrundcrs of the sky,\n1371\tThe angry demon Of the waters roars;\n1372\tAnd when, untouched BY Natures living fires\n1373\tNo native rapture fills ttedrowsy soul,\n1374\tThen former ages. with theirnnefu Tyres,\n1375\tCan bid the fury of the passions fall.\n1376\tBy the blue taper's melancholy light,\n1377\tWhilst all around The midnight torrents pour,\n1378\tAnd awful glooms beset the face of night\n1379\tThey near the silent, solitary hour.\n1380\tAh then, how sweet to pass the nil ght away\n1381\tIn silent converse with the Grecian page;\n1382\tWhilst Homer tunes his everything lay,\n1383\t\n1384\t\n1385\t~\n1386\t\n1387\t=== 13_1905-08-27_pna_sn85042104_00332892393_1905082701_0522.json\n1388\tThe news &amp; observer. [volume] (Raleigh, N.C.) 1894-current · 1905-08-27\n1389\tcrops: None\n1390\theadline: In Reply to RooseveIt's\n1391\t\n1392\t\n1393\tLatest Appeal.\n1394\t\n1395\tNO MORE is KNOWN\n1396\t\n1397\tThe President Had an Interview With\n1398\tBaron Kaneko Yesterday, and Was\n1399\tin Almost Constant communi-\n1400\taation With Toxic and st.\n1401\t\n1402\t\n1403\tPetersburu.\n1404\t\n1405\tPetersburg.\n1406\t---\n1407\t\n1408\t\n1409\t\n1410\tPortsmouth, N. H., Aug. 25.-The\n1411\tAssociated Press is permitted to an\n1412\tmounce that Emperor Nicholas, an\n1413\tsver to President Rooseveits latest\n1414\tappeal was partially responsive.\n1415\t\n1416\t\n1417\tPortsmouth, n. H., Aug. 26-At 1880\n1418\ta. M. a long cipher message arrived\n1419\tfrom Oyster Bay for Assistant Secre-\n1420\ttary PIERCE.\n1421\t\n1422\t\n1423\tPortsmouth, N. H., Aug. 25.-The\n1424\tpeace conference seems headed\n1425\tstraight for the rocks. Despite the\n1426\tfact that every question OF principle\n1427\tinvolved in the quarrel between the\n1428\ttwo countries has been settled in IA\n1429\tvor of the victor, that in reality only\n1430\twords and money\" still separate\n1431\tthem, the negotiations seem on the\n1432\tverge iof a final rupture. The air\n1433\twas filled tonight with gloomy fore\n1434\tbodings as specifically everything in\n1435\tDictated that tomorrow'S session of the\n1436\tconference will prove the \"Seance\n1437\td'adieu.\" In the most positive fash\n1438\tion it is declared that no hew instruc-\n1439\ttions had reached Mr.. White up to\n1440\tthis evening, which would permit\n1441\thim to entertain the consideration of\n1442\tthe compromise proposition submitted\n1443\tby the Baron Komura on Wednesday,\n1444\tand therefore unless Japan has a new\n1445\tproposal to make tomorrow all that\n1446\tseems to remain is for the plehlpo-\n1447\ttentiaries to meet, sign declaration\n1448\tthat the conference has completed its\n1449\tlabors shake hands and part as enc\n1450\tmics, not as friends.\n1451\t\n1452\t\n1453\tFactors Still Unknown.\n1454\t\n1455\t\n1456\tBut there are still unknown fac\n1457\ttors in the situation, which in twink\n1458\tling might change the situation. The\n1459\tresult of President RooSeveltS sec\n1460\tond appeal direct to Emperor\n1461\tNicholas has not transpired and the\n1462\tresult of the pressure on Japan to al\n1463\tter the form of her proposal by the\n1464\telimination of purchase price of the\n1465\tnorthern half of Sakhalin is not\n1466\tknown. But the indications on both\n1467\tscores are not reassuring. The press\n1468\tdispatches from Toxic and st. Peters\n1469\tburg. read with the most intense in\n1470\ttent here indicate an intransigent at\n1471\ttitude on the part of both govern\n1472\tments. Yet both. are anxious for\n1473\tpeace and only words and money\"\n1474\tbar the path. Probably it is not\n1475\tmatter of more than $200,000,00\n1476\tYet the Japanese cling doggedly to\n1477\ttheir demands. Their people at home\n1478\tinsist upon it. The most competent\n1479\tJapanese authority who did not con-\n1480\tceal his -pessimism when asked to-\n1481\t\n1482\t\n1483\t\n1484\t\n1485\t\n1486\tJapan would yield further replied:\n1487\t\n1488\t\n1489\tRead the dispatches from Toxic\n1490\tand draw your own conclusions\n1491\t\n1492\t\n1493\tNevertheless there is warrant for\n1494\tthe statement that they are not as inn\n1495\tplacable as they appear and from a\n1496\tconversation had by the Associated\n1497\tPress tonight the distinct impression\n1498\twas gathered that they would scale\n1499\ttheir money demand and use any\n1500\t'fOrOntIIa'' which would no arpnnta HE\n1501\tto Russia. 1t is not believed they\n1502\tpropose to let the conference end to-\n1503\tmorrow.\n1504\t\n1505\t\n1506\tOn the Russian side all C.\"etenSe of\n1507\tclaiming that Mr.. White is any longer\n1508\tnegotiating has been abandoned. He\n1509\thas gone to the furthermost limit Of\n1510\this imperial masters orders. More,\n1511\the cannot do, sincerely as he person\n1512\tally may desire to bring the negOtia-\n1513\ttons to a successful conclusion.\n1514\t\n1515\t\n1516\tWitte Expects no Change.\n1517\t\n1518\t\n1519\tShortly before midnight Mr. White\n1520\tgave absolutely no encouragement to\n1521\tthe idea that there would be a change\n1522\tof heart at ST. Petersburg. Unless\n1523\tToxic was ready to recede further he\n1524\texpressed the opinion that all was\n1525\tover. He used the following words to\n1526\tthe Associated Press:\n1527\t\n1528\t\n1529\tHave received no telegram from\n1530\tst. Petersburg which changes the sit\n1531\tuation. Indeed | do not expect any.\n1532\t/ have not asked instructions, so that\n1533\tl cannot receive any. If tomorrow\n1534\tthere is to be a change it must come\n1535\tfrom the Japan