suddenly, the same horse and carriage\n1595\tpassed them as before. Nothing was\n1596\tdiscernible of the driver except his\n1597\tfeet, the carriage cushion hiding his\n1598\tbody. There was no cross-road by\n1599\twhich a vehicle in front could possibly\n1600\thave got behind without making\n1601\tcircuit of many miles and consuming\n1602\tseveral hours.\n1603\t\n1604\t\n1605\tYet there was not the shadow of q\n1606\tdoubt IS to the identity of the vehicle,\n1607\tand the two gentlemen gazed at each\n1608\tother in blank amazement, and with a\n1609\tcertain defined sense of awe which\n1610\tprecluded any discussion of the matter,\n1611\tparticularly as the horse was to all\n1612\tappearances the well-known white\n1613\thabitually driven by the deceased\n1614\tJudge. A half-mile brought them in\n1615\tsight of Judge SIS gate, when, for the\n1616\tthird time. the ghostly team dashed\n1617\tby in the same dreadful, mysterious\n1618\tsilence. This time it turned in full\n1619\tview into the gate. Without word\n1620\tof comment the doctor quickened his\n1621\thorses speed, and reached the gate\n1622\tonly a few yards behind the silent\n1623\tdriver.\n1624\t\n1625\t\n1626\tBoth gentlemen peered eagerly up,\n1627\tthe long, open lane leading to the\n1628\thouse; but neither carriage nor wheel.\n1629\ttrack was visible, though it was still\n1630\tclear daylight, and there was no outlet\n1631\tfrom the lane, nor could any vehicle\n1632\tin the time occupied accomplish half\n1633\tthe distance. The peculiar features of\n1634\tthis strange incident are that it was\n1635\tequally and simultaneously evident to\n1636\ttwo witnesses, both entirely unpre-\n1637\tpared for any such manifestation, and\n1638\tdiffering widely in temperament. habits\n1639\tof life, mental capacity, and educa-\n1640\ttional attachments. and by mere &C.\n1641\tcident making this journey together,\n1642\tand that to this day, both of. them\n1643\twitnesses, be it noted, of unimpeach-\n1644\table credibility-attest it, and fully\n1645\tcorroborate. each other, but without\n1646\tbeing able to suggest the slightest ex'\n1647\t| n1anati\n1648\t\n1649\t=== 21_1847-05-29_p2_sn82016488_00513688040_1847052901_0791.json\n1650\tArkansas intelligencer. · 1847-05-29\n1651\tcrops: None\n1652\theadline: OUR NEXT REPRESENTATIVES,\n1653\t---\n1654\tLsq', nave oeen pfematurely, we think, presented\n1655\tTO the public as suitable persons to be selected as\n1656\tcandidatesin lsis. These gentlemen are as well\n1657\tknown 10 our readers the limited circulation\n1658\tOF our paper can make them. From wha! cause,\n1659\tsuch early nominations have been made, We can\n1660\tnot conjecture, unless like TOO ..raccrs,\" the\n1661\tfriends of each, are determined 10 get the inside\n1662\ttract, and thus have, Unexpectedly, thrown us in\n1663\tview Of 4 race. Other gentlemen have been\n1664\tsuggested 10 US, and their friends will no doubt.\n1665\tenter them in TIM.\n1666\t\n1667\t\n1668\tBelieving as VIC do, that i, is an unwise poll,\n1669\tcy, 10 aglIntc this question, SO early, ye. as we\n1670\tarc the peoples organ, and those in whose service\n1671\tVIC are engaged, havo though, proper 10 move\n1672\tin the matter, We are willing to givo space in\n1673\tour columns 10 the rrcnds of cither party, and we\n1674\tcan assuro readers that we will not cspouse\n1675\ttho cause Of either of the ca nddu!cs until after\n1676\tthe meeting of the Conventions. But at the same\n1677\ttime we shall deem It our d\"ty, at the proper\n1678\ttime, TO advocate tho paramount Importance Of\n1679\tselecting Western man. We have rights and\n1680\tinterests in the vest, which cannot be represent\n1681\ted or protected, except by man whose resi-\n1682\tdencc among us, can give him a knowledge of\n1683\tour wants. One whose worldly gear is si.ua-\n1684\tted in our midst, for \"whercvcr is his treas.\n1685\ture, there is his heart one whose IncaI braches\n1686\tare locked and cntwined in the community, which\n168