understood, had made an\nagreement to buy the present\nplant from Mr.. Foshay and his\nassociates.\n\n\nHowever, the deal between\nFoshay and Stevens did not go\nthrough. Mr.. Foshay, it now ap-\npears, has either sold or trans\nferred his interests to 4 Mr.\nBigle, which gentlemen now\ncomes forward with an appllca-\ntion for a franchise. He seeks\nto have the council grant him\nthe same franchise that it granted\nMessrs. Havens and Stevens,\nwith his name substituted. That\nis the proposition that the council\nwill consider tonight.\n\n\nThe News has a suspicion that\nthe council, which all along has\nacted in good faith, is beginning\nto scent an African in the wood\npile. 1t begins to feel that 1t Is\nbeing put through a course OF\niockeying and the city's much\nneeded Improvements retarded\nfor the qualification OF selfish\nself-interests.\n\n\nThe city council will get its\nback up pretty soon and cut its\nfetters, one Of them intimated.\n1t isn't going to be a one.man\nadministration, and he OF the\nthird house\" variety, much\nlonger.\n\n\nThe council is getting wise, as\nthe public has been for some\ntime.\n\n=== 46_1830-08-04_pna_sn83021167_00514152743_1830080401_0084.json\nHerald of the times. [volume] (Newport, R.I.) 1830-1846 · 1830-08-04\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\ned, the summon issued, and the point\nOF reunion settled by a Providence ev-\nor indulgent to mankind--ever studi-\nGus to treat us with dainties, and load us\nwith benefits Not only do the Herrings,\nthe poor mans feast, visit our shores at\nstated periods and solicit us by their num-\nbers to partake of the bounties of Provi-\ndence, but the Pilchard, the Mackerel,\nLampdries, the TUNES and the Salmon\nare regular in their migrations\n\n\nThe N. York Commercial Advertiser\nstates, upon the authority OF commune\ncation from ga most honorable source\"\nthat Mr.. Randolph took airs with the com\nmandcr of the ship of war in which he\nhas embarked, as soon as he got on board.\nHe directed Capt. Perry to sail immedi-\natcly, though the surgeon and the purscr\nlofthc ship were still at Norfolk. She did\nnot sail however, until the next mo rning.\nThis story may not be quite exact, but we\nshall not be surprised to hear of some por-\ntentous freak or fracas; perhaps, of the\narrival of a Minister Plcnipotentiary at\nCronstadt in durance vile. We know\ncaptains of the navy as well as comman-\ners of packets, who would rather take\nthe sca-scrpent, or the ghost of DIR. Faus-\ntus, acrost the Atlantic, than the orator of\nRoanoke .-.Nat. Gazette.\n\n\n\n\n\nSale ETA chmrh-.Thc elegant Meet\ning III ouse lately belonging to the West\nminister Congregational Society N Prov\nidcnce, was sold at auction on Tuesday\nby the Assignces. 1t cost originally S32,-\n900, and was bought by &. +. Tllinghast,\nEsq. for S 200 subject to the claims of\n32 pew owners, amounting to about Sl2,-\n600. The Providence Daily Advertiser\nprosumes it was bought for the AJsignces\nby their Attorney\n\n\nAsa Wheeler, a private soldier, in the\nu. 5. service, was executed in Virginia\non the 10th inst. for the murder ola Ser\ngeant. 1t is said he confessed having\nbeen guilty Of thirteen murders.\n\n\nMcssrs. G.. s. C. & III. Carvill of New\nYork, are preparing for public alton a\nstereotype edition, revised and improved\nby an American Editor, of that important\nwork for classical scholars, Donnegan's.\nGreek and English Lericon.\n\n\nA great Improvement in the manufac-\nture Ofpapcr, is said to have been intro\nduccd at New Haven, Conn. whereby\nlaid as well as wove paper may be made\nby the dipping process, and Il saving of\n$1,000 per year be secured. The ma\nchnery is patented.\n\n\nThe Z. S. Gazette contradicts the re-\ncent statement in the Newspaper, re-.\nspccting the purloining of the secret of\nmaking lever watches, by Roskell of.\nEng gland, from Mr.. Spencer an American.\n\n\nLand has recently been sold at Chilli\ncothe at thirty, forty, fifty, and even six\nty dollars foot. The Scioto Gazette\nstates that market house has been erect\ncd this year, go feet long, and equal to\nany on the Atlantic. This prosperity is,\nowing to the vicinity of a canal.\n\n=== 36_1920-09-01_pna_sn88065726_00279521201_1920090101_0664.json\nEvening capital and Maryland gazette. (Annapolis, Md.) 1910-1922 · 1920-09-01\ncrops: cached\nheadline: ssial b. MRS. EMMA ABBOTT GAGE\n\nr.. phantom Caravan\n---\no. /. me a.termost paies of the\n\n\ne.srk of their lives was III\n\"a. me horns and their courses\nnldel and cast\n,.ral-ade they ride them iorth'\n\" line from A5 TO you;\n\n\nt-4g1 theirs and your brain is\n,s,.rr. you DO as they bid you do.\n\n\noe az The million sires and\n.mae IN the blood OF your pulse\n\n\n- oan urge will sometimes\n,g. m The sons Of your chill\nd-.gs SONS ......\n\n\n~sr .ges are spun as the threads Of\n,r cloak. through the warp and\nrae Wo9f of your Whole;\nyar hands are theirs and your eyes\n,rs theirs. and your mold and\nymr Self and your Soul.\n-.. dreams they dreamed and the\nlights they fought and the prayers\ntbs, their lips have prayed\n...u ba your dreams and shall be\nr1nr prayers: your fights are the\nfghfs they made.\n\n\nrte lives they lived and the depths\nthew died you shall live and die\nagain,\n\n\nd, Won Is The seed of million hopes of\nmillion maids and men.\neol grant. my son, that YOU fight the\nfight and hold 10 the faith. Amen!\n\n\nKENDALL BANNING.\n\n=== 42_1891-07-08_pna_sn83016025_00414210946_1891070801_0622.json\nThe Portland daily press. [volume] (Portland, Me.) 1862-1921 · 1891-07-08\ncrops: cached\nheadline: new EXCURSION ROUTE /!\n\nNv Steamer Phantom.\n---\nviii leave Portland Pier for Mere Point and all\nlandings, week days, at 0.55 a. m. Returning,\nleave Mere Point for all landings at 20 p. m.\n\n\nMondays, Wednesdays and Fridays will leave\nMere Point for Portland and all landings az a.\nm. Returning, leave Portland Tuesdays, Thurs\ndays and Saturdays at 45O p. IN. for Mere Point.\n\n\nTIME TABLE FOX 1891.\n\n\nCommencing July LIST, steamer PHANTOM will\nleave FreepOrt GAO a. m, 200 p M, VOLS Point\n650 a. M, 205 p. M, Great Chebeague 7.l5 a. m,\n$50 D. m, LittlejOhns' Island 7.25 a. m. 2.35 D.\nm, Cousins Island q 80 am, 2.40 pm, Falmouth\nForesde 7.56 a. M, 800 p. M, arrive at Portland\n$40 a. m, $50 p. m.\n\n\nReturning, leave Portland Pier 9.55 a. m, 4 90\np. m, Falmouth Foreside 1925 a. m, $40 p m,\nCousins Island 1880 a. M, so p. M, LitteiOhns\nIsland HOO a. m, 5.55 p. M, Great Chebeague\nlilo a. m, Cos y. m, Wolls Point IL 80 a. m,\n635 PM, arrive at Freep0rt II 40 m, $40 p. m.\nFare to South FreepOrt, including drive Of I'VE\nmiles, making an excursion Of 38 miles for S1.OO\nthe round trip.\n\n\nPassengers taking the $50 a. m boat for Free\nport Will arrive in time for trains going East.\n\n\nPassengers coming West by all q. m. trains can\nhave delightful ride and san by taking our car\nrages at Freeport village for 156 pm. boat arriv.\ning In Portland at $50 p. m.\n\n\nivIdtf H. IL SOUIE Manager\n\n=== 9_1930-01-27_p10_sn84026749_0022225467A_1930012701_0563.json\nThe Washington times. · 1930-01-27\ncrops: cached\nheadline: PILED\nUP FOR \"THE\n\n\nKIBITZER\"\n---\nTHE Kibitzer,\" current at\nLoew's Palace, is an excellent\nproduction. Diverting, out of\nthe ordinary, this play is Ger.\ntain to diSpel that ennui which\nmay result from a constant\nfare Of musical comedy offer\nings.\n\n\nThe Kibitzer\" features a\nsuperb, though small, cast of\nplayers. Harry Green, the XII\nbitzer Zone who knows all there\nis to know about everything\nand who is willing and ready\nto tell it at little or no provo\ncation), Is marvellous. The\nplot, Of minor Importance, deals\nwith one business man, a cigar\nstore owner, to be exact, who\ndoes cash trade with his pa.\nirons and credit with the other\nfellow, until the time of reck-\noning is forced. The way in\nwhich he finally puts it over\nprovides the thread of the\nstory. Mary Brian is the\ncharming daughter, and Neil\nHamilton is the boy friend\"\nTenen Holtz, Guy Oliver and\nAlbert Gran contribute striking\nperformances in lesser charac-\nter bits.\n\n\nHarry Green, however, holds\nthe center of the stage. capaDIy\nfulfilling the demands of the\nplay. The Kibitzer\" (ParG-\nmount, which does indeed\nabound in humor, logic and\nt h rough entertainment, is\nheartily recommended to those\nwho find pleasure in a finished\nand perfectly enacted perform.\nance.\n\n\nThe stage show, Haunting a\nbizarre array of brilliant stage\nand color effects, is entitled\n\"Toytown.\" Mile. Pavla.\ndancing doll, is charming:\nAileen Clark. possessor of a\nvery sweet voice, sings delight\nfully; Fortunello and Cirillino,\nEuropean acrobats, are seen\nin some difficult tumbling\nfeatS: Gus Alexander and\nEnoch Light contribute to the\nexcellent bill, and Ivan Tries\nault and Carlton Emmy, with\nhis mad wagS, gather in a\ngoodly share of applause.\n\n\nSilly Symphony\" a sound\ncomic, is about the best illus\ntration of Synchronized screen\nmovement conceivable, a gem\nin its class. Hearst Movietone\nis replete with news events.\nHarry Borjes and his Synco-\npators and Charles Gaige at\nthe organ provide their Gus\ntomary melodies.\n\n=== 4_1904-06-16_pna_sn86082061_00415629710_1904061601_0913.json\nHerald and news. [volume] (West Randolph, Vt.) 1878-1943 · 1904-06-16\ncrops: cached\nheadline: DRAWING BY REFLECTION.\n\nSomething That May Seem Easy, But\nIs in Fact Very Diff\n\n\nfcult.\n---\n''Now here\" said the lively boarder,\nrs something that you may all have\nseen before, but that was new TO me, and\nl am sure it will interest you II you\nhaven't seen it.\n\n\n'You take a small. square mirror.\nlike this one that | find standing here\non the mantel, and rest II on the table, or\nget somebody TO hold il there for you.\nstanding upright and with face toward\nyou.\n\n\nThen you put down on the table, in\nfront Of the mirror, a piece Of white pa\nper, and you provide yourself with lead\npencil with which to draw on the paper.\nThen you cover over that hand that\nyou are to draw with, or have some\nbody cover 1t over, with paper that will\nconceal the hand and the pencil from d.\nrect view, though you can see the rehec-\nton Of the pencil In the mirror before\nwhich you are holding it.\n\n\nThen, by the aid Of the reflection in\nthe mirror Of the lines you draw, you\ntry TO draw on the paper a square with\ndiagonal intersecting lines across It.\n\n\n\"You will find that with the hand\nholding the pencil covered over, and only\nthe reflection Of the pencil IN the mirror\nto guide you, you can draw the square\npretty accurately without any great diff\ndeulty. You are likely to get II all right\nat the first or at least at the second trial,\nbut the drawing of the diagonal lines\nyou will find to be very different prop\nosition.\n\n\nYou can see the pencil In the mirror,\nand see just what you are doing with It,\nbut even with that help It is by no means\nas easy 20 draw those two diagonal lines\nas you might imagine Oh, some people\ncan do 1t pretty well, after one or two\ntrials, but more cannot; and some peo-\npie that can draw the square all right\nmake. when they come TO try to put in\nthe diagonals, lines going every which\nway, as zigzag and crooked as the err\nratio markings Of a planchette pencil\nwhen you press your hand upon the\nboard\n\n\n1T seems as though II must be very\neasy, but It is really very hard Now,\nyou folks try IT,\"\n\n\nAnd they did try It, and they found\nmore fun in it, they said, than IN any\nthing they had tried in a long time\n\n=== 1_1842-01-01_p1_sn86058217_00271744742_1842010101_0405.json\nRichmond palladium. [volume] (Richmond, IA [i.e. Ind.]) 1839-1854 · 1842-01-01\ncrops: cached\nheadline: WHOLE NUMBER 80.\n---\nclass 1t this time; and thinks i, preferable to i'm\nitaie the example of England By giving aid to\nthe private enterprise Of our citizens in\nconstructing packet ships, TO ply between this\ncountry and foreign ports. He recommends\nthat n steam ship IT medium size be5uili, TO\ntest the utility of vessels built Of that matters\nnj. lie discourigss an increase in the num-\nDer of or lIne-o<batIle ships. and prefers\nan addition 10 our irtgatcs Of The largest class\nHe urges the propiIety Of establishing higher\ngrades IN the navy, ms by former\nSecretaries. and assigns his reasons for desiring\nthis change.\n\n\nA large increase in The Marine Corps is re-\ncommended, as also an amendment Of ire laws\nand regulations for the government Of that\ncorps; for which purpose n new code has already\nbeen prepared by n board of officers.\n\n\nThe Secretary recommends the establishing Of\nNavy Schools. The use of steam vessels rendering\nn ecessary a dItfercnt order Of scientific knowledge\nfrom that which has heretofore been required\nlie advises, in connexion with this subject, that\na certain rank or position be assigned 10 profess\nsors Of mathematics, and that permanent, provi-\nsion be made for them.\n\n\nThe estimates for the ensuing year are much\nla rger than they have heretofore been, % a con\nsiderable increase of the navy is proposed, and il\nis contemplated to keep the officers in active en-\nplovmcnt.\n\n\nThe Secretary calls attention TO the Navy Yard\nal Brooklyn, and suggests various improvements\n10 promote convenience O nd security. A small\nappropriation is asked TO enclose the grounds at\n,ached 10 the navy hospital at Norfolk-a and also\nto provide a suitable depot for the charts and nau-\nteal instruments belonging TO the navy. Il 10\nlikewise stated that additional marine barracks\nare required\n\n\nThe Secretary closes his elaborate and able To-\nport, by expressing the determination 10 PRACTICE\na pruden, economy in all things--but he condemns\nthe saving which would expose the country, in a\n3eTpnralpsG cnnIIfInn IN hOetIla attacks SsIhn\nspirit which pauses,\" says he, \"to calculate tho\ncost Of measures Necessary for the support Of the\nhonor and glory Of our country will never, it 10\nhoped, display itself in this department\" We\nhope that such a spiri. will never be exhibited by\nany department Of our government.\n\n=== 12_1942-11-26_p40_sn83045462_00280603296_1942112601_0163.json\nEvening star. · 1942-11-26\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nPerceptions drive opponents frantic.\nand against Navy he took one s5\nyards to score.\n\n\nGreat forte of the Cannonball Is\npunt returns. He Zoomed back five\ntimes against Duke for a total Of\napproximately 200 yards. He helped\nbeat Auburn, Notre Dame. Navy.\nKentucky and Alabama with his\nWraith-like gallops after kicks. 1t\nwas, in fact. 27-yard return in the\nopening period against Alabama\nwhich set off the scoring drive which\nwon the game, TO.\n\n\nBoth Shine on Defense.\n\n\nSinkwich kicks averaging 361\nyards, and Castleberry doesn't\nalthough he could if necessary and\nwas considered a good punter in\nhigh school.\n\n\nBoth are great defensive players,\nhard hitting in backing up the line,\nalert in breaking up opponents\npasses.\n\n\nHow will Castleberry do Saturday.\nwhen he goes against an ALL\nAmerica?\n\n\nHis coach, Bobby Dodd, thinks\nhe'll do all right.\n\n\nClint goes best when the game IS\ntoughest\" he says.\n\n=== 3_1922-04-23_p27_sn83045774_00271744262_1922042301_0817.json\nThe New York herald. · 1922-04-23\ncrops: cached\nheadline: 'AWAKING MAGYARS'\n\n\nACCUSED BY SPIRITS\n\nMediums Revelations Lead\nPolice to Arrests in Buda-\n\n\npest Bomb Plot.\n---\nSpecial Gable 10 TIE NEW YoRK HERALD.\nCopyYi0ht, 1922, by THE NEW YORK HERALD.\nVIENNA, April 22.-The mystery which\nsurrounded the recent bombing of the\nLiberal Club at Budapest and puzzled\nthe authorities is now nearing solu-\ntion. The police are certain that the\ncrime can be laid at the door of pow\nerful reactionary and Chauvinistic or\ngarization known as the \"Awaking\nMagyars,, who are known to have\nformed a center Of violence against the\nJewish population. Several arrests of\nmembers Of this organization already\nhave been made, including Editor CAL\nlay two lawyers ar-l non-commis-\nstoned officer OF the imperial navy\nknown as Kovacz. alias karacsony\n\n\nThe latter already has admitted that\nhe received hand grenades from Gallay,\nbut asserts that he had nothing to do\nwith the Liberal Club outrage and re-\nfuses steadfastly TO betray who did the\nbombing.\n\n\nStrange rumors are afloat that the\npolice first received intimation Of the\ncompletely OF the \"Awaking Magyars\nthrough a spiritualistic seance. in which\nthe medium described the men who laid\nthe bombs and gave the details OF their\nmovements following the attempt which\nwere then easily traced by the police.\n\n\nSo far ten members OF the \"Awakin2\nMagyars\" have been confiped in cells,\nbut II Is doubted whether the Govern\nment will be able successfully to COM\nbat this new commorra. which already\nis supported by a strong section OF the\nBudapest public.\n\n=== 31_1921-10-31_p1_sn86072160_00514150382_1921103101_0538.json\nNone · 1921-10-31\ncrops: faro_1921/1921-10-31_p1_sn86072160_00514150382_1921103101_0538.json: too few words to check\nheadline: sPoOK PARTY.\n---\nSP0OK PARTY.\nWhere? Methodist church.\nWhen7 October 81.\nBy whom7 Epworth league.\nComet You are welcome. 1o.3I.lt.\n\n=== 8_1900-08-10_pna_sn90059182_00393341087_1900081001_0258.json\nThe Ely miner. [volume] (Ely, Minn.) 1895-1986 · 1900-08-10\ncrops: cached\nheadline: GHOSTS PLAINLY VISIBLE.\n\nSome People Said to Be Consti.\ntuted That They See Super\n\n\nnatural Beings,\n---\nThere is no doubt that person may\napparently see objects and hear words\nwhich another person close by cannot\nsee or hear. Such impressions are to\nbe referred not to actually existing OBJ.\njects, but to the action of the subjects\nmind, says the Westminster Review.\nDR.. Abercromby tells of one patient\nwho could, by directing his attention\nto an idea, call up to sight the appro.\npirate image or scene, though the thing\ncalled up were an object he had never\nseen. but had merely imagined. When\nmeeting friend in the street he could\nnot be sure whether the appearance\nwas his friend or a specter illusion till\nhe had tried to touch it and had heard\nthe voice. Goethe saw an exact coun.\nterpart of himself advancing toward\nhim, an experience repeated by Wilkie\nCollins. Sir Walter Scott relates that\nsoon after the death of Lord Byron he\nread an account of the deceased poet.\nOn stepping into the hall immedia tely\nafter he saw right before him il\nstanding posture the exact representa-.\nton of his departed friend, whose rec.\nobjection had been so strongly brought\nto his Imagination. After stopping\nmoment to note the extraordinary re-\nsemblance he advanced toward it and\nthe figure gradually disappeared.\n\n\nSome of the cases narrated by Sir\nDavid Brewster are particularly in.\nstructive. The subject was lady (Mrs.\nAl and her hallucina tons were care.\nfully studied by her husband and Sir\nDavid. On one occasion she saw her\nhusband, as she thought, who had\ngone out half an hour before, standing\nwithin two feet of her in the drawing.\nroom. She was astonished to receive\nno response when she spoke to him.\nShe remembered that Sir David had told\nher to press one eyeball with the finger.\nwhen the impression of any real objeo,\nwould be doubled. She tried to appl .\nthe test, but the figure walked awa5\nand disappeared. The simple scientific,\nexperiment diverted her attention frorl\nthe creation of her mind, and this, NJ\nlonger being in sole possession, could\nnot maintain Itself and was dissolved.\nAnother hallucination took the form\nof her dead sister-in-law. The figure\nappeared in a dress which Mrs. A. had\nnever seen, but which had been de.\nscribed to her by common friend.\n\n=== 12_1900-11-08_pna_sn78000873_00279524834_1900110801_0363.json\nThe Republican journal. [volume] (Belfast, Me.) 1829-current · 1900-11-08\ncrops: None\nheadline: THE NEWS OF BELFAST.\n---\n.b