nd his family when\nhad given his daughter her marriage\nportion. or buried his dead. or done\nhim any, other great service. he re-\neeiy Gs, this sertiflcate: in the name\nGhost. Amen. /. Kaspar Kiauben\ntailor. of Bremen, stand indebted to\nMax Schmidt. merchant, Of the same\ntown, In the sum of 100 tha1ers, given\nand unable to work. for my own COM\nfort and the support of my wife and\nseven children; which sum promise\nshall be paid him in Heaven, by God.\nfollowed and the town seal was ar\nfixed, and no one but the impious ever\ndoubted that it would be paid, either.\nThese documents We're carefully kept\nby men among their most valuable\ncollins when they We're buried. 1t\nsometimes happened that coffin was\nstuffed with them, which, considering\nthat we brought nothing. into this\nthat shrouds have no pockets and\nmighty narrow sleeves, was certainly a\nhappy and beautiful thing for them\nand those who loved them,\n\n\nTolerance.\n\n\nWhat we have lost In this simple,\nlovely faith we are said to have gained\nIN tolerance. But is that quite true?\nYes, and no. Yes. because we no\nlonger burn people at the stake be-\ncause they differ from US in non\ncause the world is turning out more\nand more Christians Of type who\nare not indeed bigots. defining bigot as\nlike the pupil Of the eye. contracting.\npoured upon IT,\" or murderers, gloomy\nand cruel and bloodthirsty, but Of the\ntype Of the man who said she had no\nidea. Of having religion interfere with\nis painted in Mccracken, who gave\nas little as he could while the meenes-\nter was looking the other way\" and\nthe preacher who made the best prayer\never addressed to o Boston audience,\nand the man who said, Plague take\ngot so much agin each other. and so\nmany good reasons for not believing\nthis doctrine and that doctrine. that\nbeing a plain man myself. ! read over\nChristmas, and bless my mothers Lord\nknowing Him to be good, for He made\nher every night OF my life. and 60\nbim eYsry sso oetas regplar\ncountry walk. for I amt no sot and\n/ amt no sport. nuthar. Sometimes\nAT Is hunting OF fishing Sometimes\nit's baseball and beer. Sometimes II\ntimes it is a Joy ride In Blll's auto\nThough 1t is the smellingest snorting.\nest old can of nitro-glycerine tina,\never was built to kill Sometimes 1,\nunder my big tree in the back yard\n/ with my baby on my knee and my Ole\n! woman cleaning up like mad inside.\n\"But Sunday BIN I will have, My\nwife staggers me, though, sometimes\n| She says, Amt you got no duty ia\nthe Lord that give you all you've go,\nand no pleasure in Him, my dear hus-\nband?\" in that mild, quiet way OF herr\n| II never see her hollerin' and fussin\nand rip-snortin' since we been mar\nyled), and 7 declare can't sense my\n| lSever ger to go toscnurch. Til go With\nIner,. and between her and my mother\nIII reckon I'll get to Heaven THEY'D\nbe mis'able anywhere without me-,\n\n\nThe AnafnGinists.\n\n\nA friend OF mine hos been heard \"a\n\n=== 31_1870-08-24_pna_sn85033221_00514152378_1870082401_0504.json\nPrescott journal. [volume] (Prescott, Wis.) 1861-1871 · 1870-08-24\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nThe Hudson Star and Times fears\nthat an effort will be made to transfer the\nSt. Croix and Superior Land Giant TO\nthe Chippewa Falls g. Bayfield, road;\nand she also fears that this diversion Of\nthe Chippewa Valley interests, with the\nstrong prejudices in older states against\nlaud grants will prove the death ofaour\nland grant bill. And The fact that the\nRepublican State Convention of Ohio\npronounced against land grants, compel\nling Bingham, Schenck aud Garfield to\nvote against the bill, makes the old lady\nstill more afraid. 1t is about time she\nsaw ghosts and hob-goblins her mind\ngels so sgitated about caucusses, conven\nTONS dc. We expect she will bresk in\nto the \"unningcst kind of skeer' next\nweek. But the people need not he\nalarmed. The country is safe. The\nLand Grant is all right. In her calmer\nmoments, few weeks ago, the old lady\nsaid the bill would pass early next ses\nsion, and her head was probably \"level, !\"\nthen.\n\n\nNow, we expect Gen. Wsshburn viii at\ntend to the interests of the st. Croix\nValley and do all lie can to secure the\npassage of the land grant bill, and we\nhave no fears but that he will succeed -\nBut il lie should fail we are confident\nthat his successor-if Mr.. Price be the\nman-will carry forward the good work\nto completion, and the ST. Croix Valley\nwill lose nothing by the change. We\nare not afraid to trust our railroad in\nterests in the hands Of Mr.. Price.\n\n\nWe wonder what next will scare our\npoor, old cotemporary out of her wits.\nWe do not expect any government\nstpap\" by way of an assessors Office or\nrelationship through Mr.. Washburn's\nsuccessor IN Congress and as conse-\nquence we can look the ground over at\nleisure and then ''go for\" the man whom\nwe think viii best subserve the interests\nof the District. all things considered\nELLSWORTH Herald\n\n\nWe are glad that Kimball is such a\nmodest creature, He hasn't very \"oreat\nexpectations\" in the \"pap\" line, at least\nin government pap. Our word for it\nKimball, government Office will be TO\nyou but s'yanity and vexstion Of spirit,\nFollow our example and renounce Office\nseeking. 'The post of honor is private\nstation\"\n\n\nYour modest bid for Office reminds us\nof the answer of the old topper, who\nwhen asked 10 take drink always said\n\"w-a-ll, l hardly ever take anything, but\nwhen I do it's about this time.\"\nLook the ground over carefully, Kim\nball, then \"go for\" your drink.\n| -.-ss-\n\n\nd. A. Baldwin, Esq, President of the\nWest Wisconsin r. r. in speech at th'\ncelebration in Eau Claire last week speak\ning of the railroad said:\n\n\n\"For the success of the enterprise they\nshould thank Congress, the Legislature. thu\n| Governor, Messrs. Thorpe, Marston, Capt\n| Wilson, Senator Price and especially jacob\nHumbird, who had opened his long pursC\nand enabled them to do the work thus far\"\n\n\n1t has been asserted by some of those\nwho oppose Judge Price in the congress\nsional contest, that he is 10 no way res\nponsible for the success of the above\nroad, and by others that he actually\nused his influence in opposition TO III\n1 construction.\n\n\nHow IA this gentlemen? \"a it posi-\nBE President Baldwin Is misiakeng\n\n=== 9_1900-11-30_p1_sn98060035_00202199203_1900113001_0222.json\nBarre evening telegram. (Barre, Vt.) 1898-19?? · 1900-11-30\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n\nSpirit, Medium and Occult\n\n\nScientist.\n\nExpert PaImist'y and\nIPsychometric. Cneir-\n\n\n0macy Healer.\n\nHOW TO OVERCOME ALL\n\n\nTECUELEs.\n---\nYour past, your present life and your\nentire future and every thing can be told\nby consulting Professor De Matthew, the\nWorld Eminent Cl1iroyant and Scientific\nPalmist, who is the greatest master of\noccult science and psychic forces the world\nhas ever produced.\n\n\nHis predictions are always correct, and\nnever fail to come true. as many people\nin this citv and elsewhere viii testify.\n\n\nAll the events OF life are written in your\nhand. Changes in business, sickness,\nfamily troubles and personal affairs OF\nwhatever kind are absolutely recorded.\nMany owe their entire success to wise\ncounsel given through PALMiSTRY, the\nonly scientific method Of reading the\nfail, and you are in doubt, it will point the\nway. Mistakes can be avoided, enemies\noutwitted, and success attained. There\nare always two paths in life: a little more\nknowledge and victory will be yours. 1t\nis worth trying, as thousands are con-\nstantly doing. Prof. Matthew, through\nyears of study and travel, combined with\npsychical gifts that have won the admira-\nton of the world, can guide and direct\nyou. He gives you the exact facts and\nadvises you as only a thorough student\ncan. Thousands in this and other lands\nindorse his wonderful powers as a bust\nness and social adviser.\n\n\nIf you are contemplating a change, are\nunhappy or seemingly unfortunate; il\nthings go wrong in your affairs, an inter-\nview with him will set you right.\nFLORENCE MARRYAT says\" Professor\n| Matthew saved me serious mistakes. His\npredictions have been fulfilled. was\namazed at his accuracy\" Thousands OF\nothers say the same thing. There is help\nfor you.\n\n\nProf. Matthew has been consulted by the\nGear of Russia, Queen Of England, Emper-\nors of Germany. China and Japan; also by\ndistinguished men and women in every\n| walk of life. He has in his possession a\nlarge collection Of photographs of palms\nand autograph letters from the crowned\nheads of Europe, He is acknowledged\nthe greatest living authority on palmistry.\n\n=== 21_1839-10-03_pna_sn84026897_00237288336_1839100301_0136.json\nEdgefield advertiser. [volume] (Edgefield, S.C.) 1836-current · 1839-10-03\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n\n@@ostie &&eeess.\n\nFor the .idcertiscr.\nSIR HUMPHREY DAVY.\n\nMr.. Editor /:\n---\nSIR HUMPHREY DAVY.\nMr.. Editor:\n\n\nDid YOU ever hear Of Sir Hunphry\nDavy as poet Asa rhymsr. his fame\nhas spread over the civilized world hui\nit is no, generally known that he had a\npoetic genius of the highest order.\n\n\nso\",hey, Coleri'lge. and others. said Of\nhim, that had he turned his attention to\npne,ry, he would have attained the first\nrank.\n\n\n1t is said. by his hiographer. that, like\nP\"pe, \" he lisped in numbers\" is\nperhaps, not To he regretted that he cult\nwaited his talent for philosophical pursuits\nas The world has heen so largely henefiIrd\nhy his investigations His safety lamp is\na richer offering 10 the world, than all the\npoetry of Hnmer, Milton. and Byron.\n\n\nHis poem. called \"The Sons of\nGenius\" contains many passages, A.\nbeautiful ] think. as any in the EnglI>i'\nlanguage. Wllyou publish the following\nextract\n\n\nBright bursting through thr awful veil Of night\nThu lunar beams upoI the ocean play;\nThe watery iows shine with trembling light,\nv here the swift breezes skim alon. the sea.\nTho glirnmering stars in yon ethereal plain\nGrow rale, and fade before the lunar-heams;\nSave where fair Veus. shining Ger the main\nConspicuous still, with fainter radiance CLEANS\nClear as the azure firmlmen above,\nSave where the white cloud luats upon the\nhrrcz.;\n\n\nAl tranquil is tie bosom of the grove.\nSave where the zephyr warbles through the\ntrees.\n\n\nNow The poor shepherd wandcrm, 10 his home.\nSurveys the darkrning s,'ene with fearful eye\nOn every green sees little elfins roam\nAnd haggard sprifes along the monnbcams fy.\nWhile Superstition rules tho vulGars soul,\nForbds the energies of man TO rise.\nRaised far above her low. her mean control,\nAspiring Genils seeks her native skies.\nShe loves The silent, solitary hours;\nShe loves the stiffness Of the starry night,\nWhen Ger the bright'ning view SeIenc pours\nThe soft ellulgence other pcusve light.\n\n\nTHE SWAY OF REASON.\nBefore her lucid, al-enli ghtening ray,\nThe paIlid spcctres oftlle night retire;\nShe drive. tho gloumv terrors far away,\n| And fills the bosom with celestial fire.\nInspired by her, the sons Of genius rise\nAbove all earthly thoughts, all vulgar care\nWealth, power, and grandeur, they alike\n\n\ndesrse,\n\n\nEnraptur'd by the good, the great, the fair.\na thousand varying Joys to them belong,\nThe charms of nature and her changefn\n\n\nscenes:\n\n\nTheirs is the music of the vcrnal song;\nAnd theirs the colcrs of the vernal plains.\nTheirs is the purple-,inged evening ray,\nWith all the radiance ofthe evening sky;\nThere's is the splendor of thc'rise day,\nEnshrined in glory by the suns bright eye.\nFor them, the zephyr fans The odorous dale;\nFor them the warbling streamlet softly flows;\nFor them the Dryads shade the verdant vale\nFor them sweet Phiome attunes her woes.\nTo them no wakefnl moonbeam shines in vair\nOn the dark bosom of the trackess wood;\nSheds its mild radiance DER the desert plain;\nOr softly glides along the chrystal floods\nYet not alone delight the soft and fair,\nAlike the grander scenes ofnaturc move;\nYe, not alone her beauties claim their care,\nThe great, sublime, aud terrible thcy love.\n\n\n\nWhen the red lishnings though the ether fly,\nAnd the white flaming pillows ash:he shores\nWhen to the rafting tIrundcrs of the sky,\nThe angry demon Of the waters roars;\nAnd when, untouched BY Natures living fires\nNo native rapture fills ttedrowsy soul,\nThen former ages. with theirnnefu Tyres,\nCan bid the fury of the passions fall.\nBy the blue taper's melancholy light,\nWhilst all around The midnight torrents pour,\nAnd awful glooms beset the face of night\nThey near the silent, solitary hour.\nAh then, how sweet to pass the nil ght away\nIn silent converse with the Grecian page;\nWhilst Homer tunes his everything lay,\n\n\n~\n\n=== 13_1905-08-27_pna_sn85042104_00332892393_1905082701_0522.json\nThe news &amp; observer. [volume] (Raleigh, N.C.) 1894-current · 1905-08-27\ncrops: None\nheadline: In Reply to RooseveIt's\n\n\nLatest Appeal.\n\nNO MORE is KNOWN\n\nThe President Had an Interview With\nBaron Kaneko Yesterday, and Was\nin Almost Constant communi-\naation With Toxic and st.\n\n\nPetersburu.\n\nPetersburg.\n---\n\n\n\nPortsmouth, N. H., Aug. 25.-The\nAssociated Press is permitted to an\nmounce that Emperor Nicholas, an\nsver to President Rooseveits latest\nappeal was partially responsive.\n\n\nPortsmouth, n. H., Aug. 26-At 1880\na. M. a long cipher message arrived\nfrom Oyster Bay for Assistant Secre-\ntary PIERCE.\n\n\nPortsmouth, N. H., Aug. 25.-The\npeace conference seems headed\nstraight for the rocks. Despite the\nfact that every question OF principle\ninvolved in the quarrel between the\ntwo countries has been settled in IA\nvor of the victor, that in reality only\nwords and money\" still separate\nthem, the negotiations seem on the\nverge iof a final rupture. The air\nwas filled tonight with gloomy fore\nbodings as specifically everything in\nDictated that tomorrow'S session of the\nconference will prove the \"Seance\nd'adieu.\" In the most positive fash\nion it is declared that no hew instruc-\ntions had reached Mr.. White up to\nthis evening, which would permit\nhim to entertain the consideration of\nthe compromise proposition submitted\nby the Baron Komura on Wednesday,\nand therefore unless Japan has a new\nproposal to make tomorrow all that\nseems to remain is for the plehlpo-\ntentiaries to meet, sign declaration\nthat the conference has completed its\nlabors shake hands and part as enc\nmics, not as friends.\n\n\nFactors Still Unknown.\n\n\nBut there are still unknown fac\ntors in the situation, which in twink\nling might change the situation. The\nresult of President RooSeveltS sec\nond appeal direct to Emperor\nNicholas has not transpired and the\nresult of the pressure on Japan to al\nter the form of her proposal by the\nelimination of purchase price of the\nnorthern half of Sakhalin is not\nknown. But the indications on both\nscores are not reassuring. The press\ndispatches from Toxic and st. Peters\nburg. read with the most intense in\ntent here indicate an intransigent at\ntitude on the part of both govern\nments. Yet both. are anxious for\npeace and only words and money\"\nbar the path. Probably it is not\nmatter of more than $200,000,00\nYet the Japanese cling doggedly to\ntheir demands. Their people at home\ninsist upon it. The most competent\nJapanese authority who did not con-\nceal his -pessimism when asked to-\n\n\n\n\n\nJapan would yield further replied:\n\n\nRead the dispatches from Toxic\nand draw your own conclusions\n\n\nNevertheless there is warrant for\nthe statement that they are not as inn\nplacable as they appear and from a\nconversation had by the Associated\nPress tonight the distinct impression\nwas gathered that they would scale\ntheir money demand and use any\n'fOrOntIIa'' which would no arpnnta HE\nto Russia. 1t is not believed they\npropose to let the conference end to-\nmorrow.\n\n\nOn the Russian side all C.\"etenSe of\nclaiming that Mr.. White is any longer\nnegotiating has been abandoned. He\nhas gone to the furthermost limit Of\nhis imperial masters orders. More,\nhe cannot do, sincerely as he person\nally may desire to bring the negOtia-\ntons to a successful conclusion.\n\n\nWitte Expects no Change.\n\n\nShortly before midnight Mr. White\ngave absolutely no encouragement to\nthe idea that there would be a change\nof heart at ST. Petersburg. Unless\nToxic was ready to recede further he\nexpressed the opinion that all was\nover. He used the following words to\nthe Associated Press:\n\n\nHave received no telegram from\nst. Petersburg which changes the sit\nuation. Indeed | do not expect any.\n/ have not asked instructions, so that\nl cannot receive any. If tomorrow\nthere is to be a change it must come\nfrom the Japanese side\"\n\n\nThis would seem to indicate that\nthe Presidents second effort had fail\ned at st. Petersburg. What, if an\nanything he has been able to do at\n\n\n=== 10_1886-11-10_pna_sn85025431_00212479792_1886111001_0411.json\nMower County transcript. [volume] (Lansing, Minn.) 1868-1915 · 1886-11-10\ncrops: None\nheadline: spectre CaEriaEs.\n---\n\n\n\nBaltimore American: For forty\nyears the Rev. DR.. B. has been the REQ.\n.torof . prominent parish on the East\nern Shore. He had, when the scene.\nrecorded below happened twenty-five.\nyears ago. mission charge sixteen\nmiles distant from the town IN which\nhe resided, and he was therefore con-\n.stantly travelling between these two\nplaces. About six miles distant was\nthe country residence Of Judge s., \"a.\nwell-known and prominent parishioner\nOf the worthy doctor. The sod had\nbeen turned above this gentleman's\ngrave only about six weeks, when DR..\nB. Glanced to be returning from his\nmission charge, in company with q\nfriend. It was broad daylight, just\nabout sunset. and not far from Judge\nSIS gate, when a carriage, drawn by\n. white horse, passed them rapidly\nfrom behind, and was soon out Of\nsight. ~ !:\n\n\ns'That fellow must be in hurry to\nreach c.\" remarked the doctor.\n\n\ndid you notice anything peculiar\nabout that vehicle?'' inquired his COM\npanion. -\n\n\nOnly that it moves very quickly.\nl heard no sound as it went by\nT. '.Nor did l.\" said his friend. ''Nei.\nTheir rafting of wheels nor noise OF\nhoofs. It is certainly strange\"\n& The matter. however was soon for\ngotten in other conversation, and they\nhad travelled perhaps mile. when,\nsuddenly, the same horse and carriage\npassed them as before. Nothing was\ndiscernible of the driver except his\nfeet, the carriage cushion hiding his\nbody. There was no cross-road by\nwhich a vehicle in front could possibly\nhave got behind without making\ncircuit of many miles and consuming\nseveral hours.\n\n\nYet there was not the shadow of q\ndoubt IS to the identity of the vehicle,\nand the two gentlemen gazed at each\nother in blank amazement, and with a\ncertain defined sense of awe which\nprecluded any discussion of the matter,\nparticularly as the horse was to all\nappearances the well-known white\nhabitually driven by the deceased\nJudge. A half-mile brought them in\nsight of Judge SIS gate, when, for the\nthird time. the ghostly team dashed\nby in the same dreadful, mysterious\nsilence. This time it turned in full\nview into the gate. Without word\nof comment the doctor quickened his\nhorses speed, and reached the gate\nonly a few yards behind the silent\ndriver.\n\n\nBoth gentlemen peered eagerly up,\nthe long, open lane leading to the\nhouse; but neither carriage nor wheel.\ntrack was visible, though it was still\nclear daylight, and there was no outlet\nfrom the lane, nor could any vehicle\nin the time occupied accomplish half\nthe distance. The peculiar features of\nthis strange incident are that it was\nequally and simultaneously evident to\ntwo witnesses, both entirely unpre-\npared for any such manifestation, and\ndiffering widely in temperament. habits\nof life, mental capacity, and educa-\ntional attachments. and by mere &C.\ncident making this journey together,\nand that to this day, both of. them\nwitnesses, be it noted, of unimpeach-\nable credibility-attest it, and fully\ncorroborate. each other, but without\nbeing able to suggest the slightest ex'\n| n1anati\n\n=== 21_1847-05-29_p2_sn82016488_00513688040_1847052901_0791.json\nArkansas intelligencer. · 1847-05-29\ncrops: None\nheadline: OUR NEXT REPRESENTATIVES,\n---\nLsq', nave oeen pfematurely, we think, presented\nTO the public as suitable persons to be selected as\ncandidatesin lsis. These gentlemen are as well\nknown 10 our readers the limited circulation\nOF our paper can make them. From wha! cause,\nsuch early nominations have been made, We can\nnot conjecture, unless like TOO ..raccrs,\" the\nfriends of each, are determined 10 get the inside\ntract, and thus have, Unexpectedly, thrown us in\nview Of 4 race. Other gentlemen have been\nsuggested 10 US, and their friends will no doubt.\nenter them in TIM.\n\n\nBelieving as VIC do, that i, is an unwise poll,\ncy, 10 aglIntc this question, SO early, ye. as we\narc the peoples organ, and those in whose service\nVIC are engaged, havo though, proper 10 move\nin the matter, We are willing to givo space in\nour columns 10 the rrcnds of cither party, and we\ncan assuro readers that we will not cspouse\ntho cause Of either of the ca nddu!cs until after\nthe meeting of the Conventions. But at the same\ntime we shall deem It our d\"ty, at the proper\ntime, TO advocate tho paramount Importance Of\nselecting Western man. We have rights and\ninterests in the vest, which cannot be represent\ned or protected, except by man whose resi-\ndencc among us, can give him a knowledge of\nour wants. One whose worldly gear is si.ua-\nted in our midst, for \"whercvcr is his treas.\nure, there is his heart one whose IncaI braches\nare locked and cntwined in the community, which\n\n\nhe Will represent. These requisites will ensure\na thorough knowledge Of our interests, a dcvoi-\ned adherence to them, and faithful discharge Of\nduty, Of the Representative.\n\n\nAZ. I,r,\nlin, Scott, Yell, Polk, Washington, Benton,\nMadison, and Carroll couutio>, that determine\nalton exists among their citizens, TO select Wcs-\ntern nan TO go 10 Congress in $40, auL from\nverbal sources we are assured tina, such is the\nlctcnton in other counties of the West, and\nfrom this section of The State wi,l satisfy the\npeople.\n\n\nCommunications have been addycsscl to US,\nrecommending other ndiviluuIS and it would\nhave given US pleasure to have published them,\nbut We were deferred from doing so. because\nthey embodied uncharitnbie illusions to other\naspirouts. We lave admitted communications.\nto individuals in such manner. as\nnow, \"fcr ref elton, VIC udn..: should not up-\npear in our columns.\n\n\nTile Ii1!ison Boys.-On Thurslny last, Capt.\nALLEN WOOD, L. S, A., with T. recruits. passed\nthrough our town, to rendezvous, at Font Smith,\nwhere ihey will be joined by Lieut. FElcu with\n~ others, raised al that place. for the same com\npany. II is now complete and ready to march\nto Mexico, whenever funds arc sent to fit them\nout.\n\n\nThese recruits wcro all raised in the prtrio!ic\ncounty Of Madison, who's\" citizens are emitted to\ngreat praise for tAo spirit in which they got up\nhis cornpany, in SO short time, and for furnish\ning such good materials. They are fine athletic,\nspirited young farmers, WHO hqyo abandoned\nhe furrows Of the plough, for the sqnguinary\npath Of glory, and are warranted to make the\n1cst kind of troops.\n\n\nAlthough tho government has neglected to\nOnward funds for recruiting purposes. tho 900\nwork never sacked, Col. II. S. WlL~ov, a lo,ri.\n'tic citizen Of Huntsville, stepped forth. and qd-\n'anced the funds required to keep up the Recruit\nng Rendezvous. and for their subsistence, on\nheir march to Fort Smith. We hope 1t will\n''' ~ 'N'S DC'OIG iutHS \"lC sun on, 10 pace\nhis fac compnny en- route for the wars; and