{"parentUuid":"00deb47b-1cad-4423-8284-f01226cf8f2c","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"775e54ef-6487-4f79-8598-643421b87718","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K7UYG5YfZ3q5yZU8LwRLdw","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t=== 36_1935-08-08_pna_sn87093377_00200291414_1935080801_0449.json\n2\tThe Northwest enterprise. [volume] (Seattle, Wash.) 1931-1952 · 1935-08-08\n3\tcrops: None\n4\theadline: PHANTOM ARMY\n5\t\n6\tlc.ontInlled from paee Onel\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t\n10\t\n11\tIN the scorehing plains Of Eritrea\n12\tand italian somaliland.\n13\t\n14\t\n15\tBattle at Wal Wal\n16\t\n17\t\n18\tIl imagine that If war is deelar-\n19\ted the first clash win come at Wal\n20\tWal, where there is q string Of\n21\tlife-giying wells, some Of which\n22\tare held by italy and some by Eth\n23\tiopia.\n24\t\n25\t\n26\t\"The Italians should very quick\n27\t\" gain the lines Of wells.\n28\t\n29\t\n30\tEthiopian troops viii put up a\n31\tcertain amount of resistance here.\n32\tThey wiz also resist to some ex-\n33\ttent In Eritrea.\n34\t\n35\t\n36\teThey ezill then disappear into\n37\tthe mountains. leaving the Italians\n38\tto march across the burning desert\n39\tland facing all the difficulties Of\n40\tdisease ard supplies with an ever\n41\tincreasing line of communications\n42\topen to sniping and direct attack.\n43\t\n44\t\n45\tmo one 1ut a fool would fight\n46\tin the plains, the Emperor told me\n47\tand his policy is sound.\n48\t\n49\t\n50\tMany Difficulties\n51\t\n52\t\n53\t1.-THE road to Adds Ababa. the\n54\tcapital, and ultimate conquest is\n55\tstrewn with difficulties for q Euro\n56\tpean army.\n57\t\n58\t\n59\t1T is true that water is to be\n60\thad In the hills. It is true that\n61\tyou leave behind the heat and the\n62\tdisease of the desert land.\n63\tbut it takes time to get accli-\n64\tmatized to the heights in which\n65\tthe Philippians are at home.\n66\t\n67\t\n68\tSome Europeans take month\n69\tto get used to the rarefied air at\n70\theights of 1000 and 5000 feet.\n71\tSome take a year. A mile climb is\n72\tagony without equipment. 1t will\n73\tbe a painting army that tries to\n74\tforce its way through the boulder\n75\tstrewn mountains.\n76\t\n77\t\n78\tInvisible Army\n79\t\n80\t\n81\tIAN the time 1t win be fighting\n82\tan almost invisible army that can\n83\tmove fom point to point with in\n84\tcredible swlftness.\n85\t\n86\t\n87\tut viii Ne fighting men whose\n88\tone ambition is to die in battle.\n89\tAlthough Christians, the fighting\n90\tmen of Ethiopia are fatalists. All\n91\ttheir sins are forgiven by the\n92\tpriests before they go into battle.\n93\tDeath in war is the direct road to\n94\theaven.\n95\t\n96\t\n97\t''They have their priests in the\n98\trearguard to comfort them. They\n99\thave their women to tend to their\n100\tneeds. They have no problem of\n101\tsupply. Each soldier carries a\n102\tsmall bag of dried beans that will\n103\tfeed him for at least month.\n104\t\n105\t\n106\tHE carries a knife which is used\n107\tfor picking thorns from his bare\n108\tfeet, or for cuttng up animals he\n109\tcatches and eats raw. The whole\n110\tarmy, wives. and priests included,\n111\tcan run for miles on end even in\n112\tthis mountainous county.\n113\t\n114\t\n115\tHidden among the mountains\n116\tthey can vanish from one point\n117\tand appear at another to renew the\n118\tattack with incredible speed\n119\t\n120\t\n121\tFine Shots\n122\t\n123\t\n124\t\"They are excellent shots, and\n125\texcel at sniping. Those who have\n126\tno trifles win fight with spears.\n127\tHeavy casualties will not deter\n128\tthem. OF what use are airplanes\n129\tagainst this phantom army? OF\n130\twhat use tanks in this rocky land :?\n131\t\n132\t\n133\tAThese are some of the problems\n134\tthat the italian generals win have\n135\tto face.\n136\t\n137\t\n138\t'oThe modern mechanical army\n139\tNAV win through. But it must be\n140\ta slow business.\n141\t\n142\t\n143\tEvery march forward will have\n144\tto be made with large vanguards,\n145\trearguards and flank guards to\n146\tprevent surprise. Heavy guards\n147\twin have to be kept to protect the\n148\tlines Of communications\n149\t\n150\t\n151\teAnd If airplanes, the eyes Of the\n152\tarmy, fail in the craggy heights,\n153\thundreds Of men will have to be\n154\tkept alert night and day in the\n155\tdeadly game of hide and seek at\n156\twhich fine .F,thionian soldier is past\n157\t\n158\t=== 9_1921-02-08_p6_sn85038485_00340583012_1921020801_0751.json\n159\tThe Birmingham age-herald. · 1921-02-08\n160\tcrops: None\n161\theadline: \n162\t---\n163\tThe practice of letting Sing Sing convicts go to the bedside of dying relatives has been temporarily aban- doned. A sorrowfufl convict who went away for that purpose with x guard hasn't returned yet\n164\t\n165\t # # #\n166\t\n167\t After listening to few heated re- marks from General Dawes. that con gressional committee had chastened feeling. Let US hope 1t did them lot of good.\n168\t\n169\t r Charles of Austria still retains his high-sounding titles. They mean about as little as ''coloner' when applied to an ld-fashioned southern gentleman who never saw military service.\n170\t\n171\t # # <\n172\t\n173\t The Nevada assembly has passed a law making it harder for outsiders to get divorce at Reno. A longer pe- riod of residence will be great help to the hotel proprietors.\n174\t\n175\t Before starting to dismember Ger many, France should consider the size of the job. and the probability that she might need help. that would not be forthcoming.\n176\t\n177\t < F a\n178\t\n179\t MT. Gompers' admiration for the new government of Mexico is not shared evidently by our state depart men..\n180\t\n181\t The New Jersey man who would rather spend a year in jail than return to his wife knows, where to find peace and quiet.\n182\t\n183\t America is making most of the worlds motion pictures. This explains the international reputation enjoyed by MT. eChaplin and MT. Arbuckle.\n184\t\n185\t is * x The Detroit spiritualist who killed himself to prove that the dead can talk to the living will be terribly dis appointed if he finds the line busy.\n186\t\n187\t # y *\n188\t\n189\t Now that we know practically ev- erything Mrs Harding bought in New York we can resume our study of the Harding cabinet.\n190\t\n191\t The French journalist who tricked Wilhelm into an interview with the aid of female relative will have to pin his medal on himself.\n192\t\n193\t * * z\n194\t\n195\t Raleigh, N. C.. has a boy, twelve years old, who can read dozen books day. We would recommend in his case plenty of exercise in the open air.\n196\t\n197\t * * #\n198\t\n199\t The housing shortage doesn't ac- count for the fact that so many peo- pie are practically living in their cars\n200\t\n201\t as x * War with Japan is declared \"un- thinkable.' The World war was un- thinkable until after it started.\n202\t\n203\t x # *\n204\t\n205\t careless compiler includes state ment from Crown Prince Willie in his list of remarkable remarks\"\n206\t\n207\t * * # It doesn't take much of an optimist to be glad he isn't living in Betrograd or Moscow.\n208\t\n209\t=== 34_1911-10-26_p2_sn85042354_00383343161_1911102601_1077.json\n210\tEvening journal. · 1911-10-26\n211\tcrops: None\n212\theadline: sPOOK SOCIAL IN CHuRcH,.\n213\t---\n214\tGrace Church Epworth League will\n215\tgive a Spook Social in the Social Hall\n216\tof the church this evening. Witches\n217\tand ghosts will receive the guests.\n218\t\n219\t=== 2_1920-04-27_pna_sn87055779_00296021477_1920042701_0690.json\n220\tSouth Bend news-times. (South Bend, Ind.) 1913-1938 · 1920-04-27\n221\tcrops: None\n222\theadline: OUIJA BOARD BENEFITS.\n223\t---\n224\tWe rapidly becoming a nation of ouja board\n225\tfans No home. in localities is considered\n226\tcomplete without its ouja board which is con-\n227\tsuited by every member OF the family either in fun\n228\tin foolish earnest The popular idea seems to be\n229\tthat ouija decide question with litt