s remain upon the\n412\ttables, mourning women are gathered\n413\taround. who cause the air to resOund with\n414\ttheir wailings. The wife, concubines, anG\n415\tdaughters-in-law, or any friend, may join\n416\tin these wailings; but often there are only\n417\thired mourners.\n418\t\n419\t\n420\tThese lamentations are exceedingly lu\n421\tgubrious, and are mixture of sobbing, oil\n422\teulogies of the dead, and of regrets for the\n423\tbereavement, and deprecating the sad lol\n424\tof those who have been robbed of a friend\n425\tor ofa support and provider. The speeches\n426\tare generally improvised; but sometimes\n427\tare according to formulas which have long\n428\tbeen used, and which have been walled\n429\tover myriads of corpses.\n430\t\n431\t\n432\tAny relation or friend who is so dis\n433\tposed may contribute his quota to these\n434\taudible demonstrations of grief; and one\n435\twill say: 40 thou departed one, / am\n436\tthy relative; this day hast thou suddenly\n437\tdeceased. Never can our affection perish\n438\tit is impossible to restrain weeping; from\n439\tthis time never more may we behold thee.\n440\tin the parting our heart is torn; but we\n441\thope that after death thy soul has Joy and\n442\tpeace, having ascended to the heavenly\n443\tpalace, there continually to confer pros\n444\tperlty on thy children and grand children.\n445\tWhile in life, all thy dealings with men\n446\twere benevolent and righteous; with an\n447\tupright heart dwelling amongst men, per\n448\tforming thy business with wisdom By\n449\tright, heaven ought to have prolonge\n450\tthy age to hundred years. Wherefore,\n451\tthen, by this one sickness art thou already\n452\tdead? We are thy relatives, we are thy\n453\tfriends; and how shall not our bowels be\n454\tsundered by the force of our distress and\n455\tlamentations Alas, alas\"\n456\t\n457\t\n458\tWhen one mourner ceases another COM\n459\tmences and chants his or her dirge, and\n460\tsays \"Alas, alasi Why was it not that\n461\tin the land of the living, an inheritor of\n462\tThou, so talented and wise; thou Oughtest\n463\tto have been spared to become an officer\n464\tof the empire, even as pillar of the royal\n465\tpalace\" And perhaps another adds: 90\n466\tthou Oughtest to have been spared to thy\n467\tnative town, the hope of the inhabitants,\n468\tand to whom they look. Thou vast one\n469\twho vert able to teach thy sons all\n470\trighteousness, end all upright measures.\n471\tBut now thou art gone. Alas, alasp\n472\t\n473\t\n474\tWe have been told that it is not an un-\n475\tcommon occurrence for old family troubles\n476\tto be referred to in some indirect way, as\n477\twhen a secondary wife with no occasion\n478\tfor the use of counterfeit sOrrow) will\n479\twall out: \"Ah me! Who now will take\n480\tmy part when oppressed by the mistresss\"\n481\tand as when the daughter-in-law sobs out\n482\ther apprehensions of increasing tyranny\n483\tfrom the mother-in-law, by saying\n484\tALAS! what will become of me since my\n485\tonly friend is departed?\"\n486\t\n487\t\n488\tAt Chinese funerals in San Francisco,\n489\tthese hired mourning women are some\n490\ttimes put into carriages to follow the body\n491\tto the grave. They may be known by\n492\tthe white garments and white hoods\n493\twhich they wear-white being the funeral\n494\tcolor.\n495\t\n496\t\n497\tIn many cases band of Chinese musi-\n498\tclans is employed to join in the procession,\n499\tand escort the deceased to his last resting\n500\tplace. Whatever may be the design in\n501\tfurnishing this music, we outside barba-\n502\trians are apt to regard it as better adapted\n503\tto frighten away evil spirits than to fur\n504\tnish agreeable entertainment to disem-\n505\tbodied soul.\n506\t\n507\t\n508\tFunerals of aged men or dignitaries,\n509\twhich are designed to be very impressive,\n510\toften have one or more young men follow\n511\ting the hearse on foot. These represent\n512\tthe sons of the deceased, and are dressed\n513\tscantily in some coarse fabric of dirty\n514\twhite; they are bare-footed, leaning upon\n515\ta cane, and go bowling down with their\n516\tface toward the earth, being supported by\n517\tfriend on either side. All this is en-\n518\tblematical not only of their crushing sor-\n519\trow, but also of the irreparable loss sus-\n520\ttanned by the family; intimating that now\n521\tas the head and support of the house is\n522\tremoved, the survivors will be left without\n523\tq provider, and must therefore pursue the\n524\tremainder of life's journey in poverty and\n525\tsorrow.\n526\t\n527\t\n528\tThose strips of brown paper, pierced\n529\twith holes, to represent strings of copper\n530\tcoin, and which are scattered in such pro-\n531\tfusion as Chinese funeral c0rtege proceeds\n532\tto the place of interment, are denOminated\n533\tmoney for buying the road\"\n534\t\n535\t\n536\tThe theory is, that everywhere there\n537\tmay be hungry or ill-disposed spirits who\n538\thave it in their power to stop on the way\n539\tthe spirit of the deceased, or by other\n540\tmeans to interfere and prevent his peace\n541\tfull settlement at the tomb provided for\n542\thim; therefore this paper, representing\n543\tmoney, is scattered everywhere along the\n544\troad to buy from the vagrant spirits the\n545\tright of way.\n546\t\n547\t\n548\tAt the place of cepulture those pro-\n549\tvisions previously mentioned are again ar\n550\tranged before the grave; and lbations of\n551\twine and tea are poured out; and large\n552\tsupplies OF money, clothing, and other\n553\tthings, supposed to be f1eeded by the de-\n554\tceased in the world to which he has gone,\n555\tare sent on after him. The money is pa\n556\tper cd: and folded so as to represent gold\n557\tand silver bars, or copper cash; and this\n558\tis burned in large amounts. Paper is\n559\tmade into boxes to represent chests of\n560\tclothing. There may be paper servants,\n561\talso a sedan chair with its bearers; and\n562\tall these are burned, and thus sent over\n563\tinto the world of spirits. While the\n564\tcorpse remained in the house, before the\n565\tfuneral, these images and paper represent\n566\tations of furniture were arranged around\n567\tthe body; the servants being represented\n568\tas in the act of waiting upon their master.\n569\t-Ooertand Monthly.\n570\t\n571\t\n572\tManufacturing Popularity.\n573\t\n574\t\n575\tA letter from Paris in the New York\n576\t\n577\t=== 19_1895-01-26_p1_sn85025350_00415627671_1895012601_0459.json\n578\tFrostburg mining journal. [volume] (Frostburg, Md.) 1871-1913 · 1895-01-26\n579\tcrops: None\n580\theadline: GHOSTS OF THE SEA.\n581\t\n582\tSAILORS DREAD TO SEE THE SHIPS\n583\t\n584\t\n585\tTHAT NEVER SAILED.\n586\t---\n587\tTHAT NEVER SAILED.\n588\t\n589\t\n590\tAn Old Salt Tells of His FIRST Experience\n591\t\n592\t\n593\tWith Phantom Ship-XhiIe a Terri\n594\t\n595\t\n596\tBILE Hurricane HowIs 1t Rides Easily\n597\t\n598\t\n599\twith All Sails Set.\n600\t\n601\t\n602\t\"These tales of the ships that never\n603\tcame back are sad enough, but it's the\n604\tship that never went out, the ghosts Of\n605\tcreepy feeling when he meets them out\n606\twhere the waves are rolling high and\n607\tthe winds are singing funeral songs\n608\t\n609\t\n610\tThe speaker was Mate Bob Alling,\n611\twho has followed the sea as boy and\n612\tman for nearly so years: Surrounded by\n613\ta group of interested listeners in the\n614\tpublic room of the Sailors Happy home\n615\tin South street, Mate Bob, as all the\n616\tsailors call him. was telling stories of\n617\tstrange sights that he has witnessed at\n618\tsea- Alling is now mate of a coastwise\n619\tfishing schooner, but in the old days he\n620\tsailed in some of the largest trading ships\n621\tand the strongest whalers that ever\n622\tsailed out of an American port flying\n623\tthe stars and stripes.\n624\t\n625\t\n626\tGas said, boys\" the old man went\n627\ton, its tho ships that never went out\n628\tfrom any port that sailor never forgets\n629\twhen he sees one Of them. We may re-\n630\tmember the ships that went out and\n631\tnever came back for time if we had\n632\tshipmate aboard. but we can forget.\n633\tBut there's no forgetting ghost of the\n634\tsea.\n635\t\n636\t\n637\tut was back in the early fifties that\n638\tl saw my first sea ghost, and today\n639\tcan shut my eyes and see it just as plain\n640\tas l could see it then. / was sailor ON\n641\ta fishing schooner, and we were catch\n642\ting cod off the coast of Newfoundland.\n643\tWe had been out ten days and were al\n644\tmost ready to start for home with full\n645\tcargo when nasty off shore breeze\n646\tcame on late one evening. We stood out\n647\tto sea, for there was heavy fog along\n648\twith the wind. 1t was stiff and steady\n649\tblow, so we rode the waves under bare\n650\tpoles during the night, waiting for day\n651\tlight and the fog to lift before shaping\n652\tour course for home.\n653\t\n654\t\n655\t''All through the night we sent up\n656\trockets at intervals and kept the ships\n657\tbell going, because we could not see\n658\tten feet ahead at times on account of\n659\tthe fog. But it was nearly morning be-\n660\tfore we heard or saw an answering sig\n661\tnal to warn US that another vessel was\n662\tnear.\n663\t\n664\t\n665\tIl was on the carly morning watch,\n666\tand about half an hour before daylight\n667\t| made out a signal light short dis\n668\tlance ahead on our port bow. 1t was a\n669\tstrange light, a pale blue in color, and\n670\tit flashed up and down at irregular in\n671\ttervals. Tho fog was still thick, and it\n672\twas impossible to tell how near we were\n673\tto the vessel\n674\t\n675\t\n676\t81 called the mate OH deck and point\n677\ted to the strange signal light He look\n678\ted at it long time, and, with a shake\n679\tof his head, said he could not make it\n680\tout at all. The lights showed that the\n681\tvessel could not be far away, so we\n682\tchanged our course little, and then\n683\tfired gun. There was no answer, and\n684\twe fired again and again with the same\n685\tresult.\n686\t\n687\t\n688\t\"By and by 1 saw that the mate was\n689\tvery grave and queer looking. He was\n690\tpacing back and forth on the deck, not\n691\tminding the cold misty rain that was\n692\tfalling, and all the time he kept his\n693\teyes fixed on that queer looking blush\n694\tlight that flashed up out of the fog and\n695\tdarkness ahead. At last | made so bold\n696\tas to- ask the mate what he thought of\n697\tit.\n698\t\n699\t\n700\tIt's ghostn he said, looking at\n701\tme with plying look.\n702\t\n703\t\n704\t\"'A ghost?\" says /.\n705\t\n706\t\n707\tYes, a ghost Of the sea- Wait till\n708\tthe fog lifts. You may see it then\n709\t\n710\t\n711\t''The fog lifted bit when daylight\n712\tcame, and then saw the ghost, as the\n713\tmate said would.\n714\t\n715\t\n716\t''Rising grim and white out of the\n717\tfog and waves that were rolling high, l\n718\tsaw full rugged ship of queer design.\n719\tThe wind was blowing half gale, but\n720\tnot a mast Or pole OF the ghost ship\n721\tbent an inch, and not one of the broad\n722\twhite sails seemed to strain at the ropes.\n723\t\n724\t\n725\t1.-THE phantom was close ON our port\n726\tbow, and as the fog cleared away we\n727\thad a splendid view from her water line\n728\tto the top of her masts. With the waves\n729\tpitching and rolling mountain high, and\n730\tthe wind bowling around our poles, the\n731\tghost ship was riding out the storm as\n732\tsteady as painted ship on painted\n733\tcanvas ocean.\n734\t\n735\t\n736\tEVERY man OH board crowded ON\n737\tdeck, and while they had to hold ON to\n738\tthe railing to keep from being washed\n739\toverheard they stood and gazed at the\n740\tphantom ship as long as it was in sight.\n741\tSome Of them that We're bit religious\n742\tlike made tho sign of the cross, and 9TH\n743\ters tried to say bit of prayer The\n744\tfact is that every man on board thought\n745\tthe phantom ship q warming Of DEATH\n746\t\n747\t\n748\t\"The captain went below and drank\n749\thot grog till he was that reckless no\n750\tghost had any terrors for him. Then, as\n751\tthe wind went down a bit, he ordered\n752\tus to make sail and bear down on the\n753\tphantom ship.\n754\t\n755\t\n756\t\"Up went our sail in jitfy, and we\n757\tflew along before the wind, but we\n758\tcould get no nearer the phantom ship.\n759\tSuddenly we saw the white ghost ship\n760\tlurch forward, her sails trembled for an\n761\tinstant, and then she seemed to sink\n762\tstraight down into the sea- Ten seconds\n763\tfrom the time we saw the first culver of\n764\ther sails the shin had vanished, and we\n765\t\n766\t=== 2_1896-09-16_pna_sn85047084_00202195933_1896091601_0535.json\n767\tThe Pacific commercial advertiser. [volume] (Honolulu, Hawaiian Islands) 1885-1921 · 1896-09-16\n768\tcrops: None\n769\theadline: Saw Ghosts.\n770\t---\n771\tcorrespondent writing from Hilo\n772\ttells of mysterious happening on the\n773\tthe following language:\n774\t\n775\t\n776\tImmediately after the earthquake a\n777\twoman was seen to run across the\n778\tcourthouse yard and disappear while\n779\trunning, Whether she was a ghost\n780\twitch or some other kind of mystery,\n781\tno one seems to know. Just as she got\n782\tabout the middle of the yard three more\n783\tdressed in the same manner were seen\n784\trunning in the opposite direction along\n785\tFront street. This remarkable occur\n786\trenee was witnessed by many people.\n787\t1t may be that these apparitions were\n788\tsome of Peles delegates.\n789\t\n790\t=== 15_1908-06-12_pna_sn84020731_00416157133_1908061201_0962.json\n791\tThe Durham recorder. [volume] (Durham, N.C.) 1879-19?? · 1908-06-12\n792\tcrops: None\n793\theadline: \n794\t---\n795\t:himed II Randolph reused himself\n796\twith slight start from the half doze\n797\tin which he had INDUCED.\n798\t\n799\t\n800\tlnvoluntarily he gazed at the por-\n801\ttrait. and great wonder came into his\n802\teyes. He wheeled his chair away from\n803\tthe fireplace and stared. He rubbed\n804\this eyes. looked about the softly light\n805\ted room and stared again. @\n806\t\n807\t\n808\tThere was the same youthful figure,\n809\twith its gray plumed hat. gray gown\n810\tmd rose pink cloak caught together by\n811\tone slender white hand. 1t was all as\n812\tIt had been before save that now\n813\tthere was life in it--life In the soft\n814\tbrown eyes ard In certain tremulous\n815\tmovement of the pink bowed lips.\n816\t\n817\t\n818\tWith white face and unbelieving\n819\teyes he watched the figure step slowly\n820\tdown from the frame, and he saw the\n821\tmovement of the purple velvet dra.\n822\tperies that formed the background.\n823\t\n824\t\n825\tSac stepped upon the wide mantel.\n826\tpiece, which was devoid Of ornament,\n827\tand made an imperious gesture with\n828\ther hand. and as she did so he was\n829\tconscious of the heavy scent Of white\n830\tlilac from the branch she carried.\n831\t\n832\t\n833\tMY good sir. pray assist me to the\n834\tfloor\" she said In low. vibrant voice.\n835\t\n836\t\n837\tAS in dream. Randolph hastily\n838\tfetched chair and supplemented Its\n839\tIa fyht Enr\n840\tcourtesy he extended a hand TO the\n841\tlady on the mantel. and as she gave\n842\thim the tips of her fingers he felt the\n843\twarmth Of flesh and blood.\n844\t\n845\t\n846\tLightly she stepped down. gathering\n847\ther skirts gracefully about her dainty\n848\tgray shod feet. and presently she stood\n849\tbeside him. looking timidly at him\n850\tfrom beneath the shadow Of her long\n851\tlashes.\n852\t\n853\t\n854\tMy GOdw he muttered. staring from\n855\tthe vision before him to the empty\n856\tframe above the chimney piece.\n857\t\n858\t\n859\tWith \"A quick movement the girl\n860\tslipped the pink cloak from her shout.\n861\tders and removed the gray plumed\n862\that from her crown OF dusky hair. \"It\n863\tIi only l, Mark\" she said wistfully.\n864\t\n865\t\n866\t\"l-don't understand-yet\" He pass.\n867\tI'd hand over his dazed eyes.\n868\t\n869\t\n870\tShe stepped forward and pressed\n871\tupon carved portion Of the mantel.\n872\tsimultaneously there was movement,\n873\tand the painted canvas swung into\n874\tplace with a little click. There was\n875\tMistress Mowbray In her accustomed\n876\tplace above the shelf. and beside him\n877\twas-Beth.\n878\t\n879\t\n880\tut Is one Of Harry's jokes\" she ex'\n881\tplanned In low. breathless tone. \"He\n882\twrote me a month ago and asked me\n883\tTO come here and enact the apparition\n884\tOF Mistress Mowbray. II was favor\n885\tlite pastime when we were children.\n886\tfor there Is a secret stair behind the\n887\tchimney. and the portrait is really\n888\tdoor which opens upon it. Harry said\n889\tthat 'skeptical friend would spend\n890\ttle night Of the 18th here and asked\n891\tme 10 play the part. was reluctant to\n892\tdo such a thing. but Harry has been\n893\tvery kind to me-and consented.\n894\tThey did not tell me who the 'skep-\n895\tTeal friend would be. and II was-a\n896\tsurprise 10 me-when I looked down |\n897\tupon your upturned face\"\n898\t\n899\t\n900\tShe leaned against the tall back of a |\n901\tchair with down bent head and CULVER\n902\tIng lips.\n903\t\n904\t\n905\tAnd you could have gone back-and |\n906\taway from me again-and never |\n907\twould have known\" he said, n vague\n908\twonder dawning In his eyes.\n909\t\n910\t\n911\tShe did not reply\n912\t\n913\t\n914\tBut you Stayed-you made yourself\n915\tknown TO me-you came down TO me!\n916\tWhat do you mean. BETH\"\n917\t\n918\t\n919\tShe lifted her head slowly and look\n920\tRI at him through misty. Joyous eyes,\n921\taud he understood.\n922\t\n923\t\n924\tHannah's voice broke crispIy upon\n925\tthe blissful silence a cablegram. Mr..\n926\t\n927\t\n928\t\n929\tMark tore open the yellow envelope.\n930\t\n931\t\n932\t1T is from Harry\" he said. gather\n933\tIng his love into his arms. and he\n934\tsays. Bless you. my children\"\n935\t\n936\t=== 22_1889-11-23_p1_sn86063034_0041418201A_1889112301_0193.json\n937\tThe morning news. [volume] (Savannah, Ga.) 1887-1900 · 1889-11-23\n938\tcrops: None\n939\theadline: WEEKLY REPORT.\n940\tOFFICE MORNING NEWS,\n941\t\n942\t\n943\tSAVANNAH, Nov. y, 1889.\n944\t---\n945\tWEEKLY REPORT.\n946\t\n947\t\n948\tOFFICE MORNING NEWS,\n949\t\n950\t\n951\tSAVANNAH. Nov p. 1889.\n952\t\n953\t\n954\tGENERAL RExARKs-A fair amount Of bust\n955\tness was accomplished in the general market\n956\tlin, the mast week II was. however, quieter\n957\t:, anpearance, there being an absence Of the\n958\t.,-,al hustle and activity so prevalent at this\n959\ttime of the year. Among the wholesale job\n960\thrs there was considerable Stacking up\n961\t:, orders. The fact of the matter is\n962\t,bat the trade in the interior chooses to work\n963\tfl their present stocks and purchases rather\n964\than to have any accumulations, especially as\n965\tif is on the verge of the holiday season, as also\n966\ttafore the usual stock-taking occurs. In other\n967\tlines there was good business going on and\n968\touite heavy shipping movement was had. The\n969\tweather, for the most part, remains\n970\t-a+ber mild. and does not encourage\n971\tseasonable trade. There were few important\n972\tfeatures in commercial circles worth noting\n973\texcept that values pretty much all around have\n974\texhibited much easier tendency. Collections\n975\twe're good The money market continues re-\n976\tmarkably easier. with considerable amounts\n977\tsaeking investment but the latter is limited\n978\tonly by the scarcity of all desirable securities\n979\tIn the grocery trade there was a steady de-\n980\tmand. but orders were not as full as usual, and\n981\tthe trading was mostly confined TO the heavy\n982\tand staple goods, in which there is always a\n983\tmore or less regular movement. In srovsions\n984\tthere was a reasonably fair demand for this\n985\ttime of the year. but there is a slight scarcity\n986\tnoticeable in some cuts of meats. In dry goods\n987\tthe trade was somewhat slow, buyers having\n988\tpreviously been pretty well stocked up. In\n989\thardware there was good steady movement\n990\tin building materials, the demand was heavy as\n991\twe'll as in lumber, but the trade is very much\n992\thammered for lack of railroad facilities to move\n993\tthe stuff from the mills. In all other depart\n994\tments business was very fair to good. The fol-\n995\tlowing resume of the weeks business will show\n996\tthe tone and the latest closing quotations of\n997\tthe different markets to-day:\n998\t\n999\t\n1000\tNAVAL SToREs.-The market for spirits tur-\n1001\tpentine was quiet during last week, while the\n1002\tcontinuance of heavy receipts has weakened\n1003\tnrces. and regulars sold down to 42c, a clear\n1004\tdecline of 116c. There was only moderate\n1005\tinquiry, but on the slumps buyers AFFORDED a\n1006\tfair amount of stock. Any firmness, however,\n1007\ton the part of holders caused buyers to be-\n1008\tcome quite Indifferent. The total sales\n1009\tduring the week were fully $500 casks.\n1010\tRosin-T market was quiet during the week.\n1011\tand prices were somewhat easier and fell off\n1012\t5n1Oc all around There was only a fair in\n1013\tauiry, with pretty free offerings The total\n1014\tsales were about 5000 barrels. In another\n1015\tcolumn will be found weekly comparative\n1016\tstatement of the receipts and exports from\n1017\tthe beginning of the season to date, and for\n1018\tthe same time last year, showing the stocks on\n1019\thand and shipboard not cleared, together with\n1020\tthe official closing quotations.\n1021\t\n1022\t\n1023\tCoTToN-The market was somewhat dull dur\n1024\ting the past week, while stocks in factors hands\n1025\thave shown some accommodations. Prices have\n1026\tdeclined 1s0136c for all grades. There seems to\n1027\tbe some dearth of orders among exporters,\n1028\twhich, of course, was expected little later in\n1029\tthe season; the lull in the demand, however\n1030\tsI,,,ta Il\n1031\tOf stock atloat. The local receipts\n1032\tcontinue in light volume The total sales for\n1033\tthe week were S025 bales. The market closed\n1034\ttoday quiet at the following spot quotations\n1035\tbased on the standard types, and which were\n1036\trevised on last Monday:\n1037\t\n1038\t\n1039\tMiddling fair Observation-Disclosures.\n1040\t\n1041\t\n1042\t1000 nhiudhhg -.....'..............'.. 1910\n1043\tMldullnS Observation-Disclosures. J2S\n1044\t\n1045\t\n1046\tLow middling ................. -. 936\n1047\t\n1048\t\n1049\tGood ordinary Observation-Disclosures. S6\n1050\t\n1051\t\n1052\tSea IsLGpds.-The total receipts for the week\n1053\tup to p. m were 2,137 bags, which include\n1054\t1911 bags to factors and 400 bags Of through\n1055\tcotton. The sales were 1868 bags. The total\n1056\texports were 1855 bags, distributed as fol-\n1057\tlows: 300 bags to Liverpool, 1852 bags to\n1058\tHavre and $50 bags to northern mills. The\n1059\tmarket closed very firm, with the total sales\n1060\tlarger than factors receipts for the week.\n1061\tSellers were asking an advance of Ac on fine\n1062\tForidas over quotations The following prices\n1063\tare based on the above sales:\n1064\t\n1065\t\n1066\tNCCdJ CObbOA VI SOOu ottb .HS-4 W0S%12\n1067\tGo9d nedlum .....-..............2 20\n1068\tMedium fine. ........................2 316 1884\n1069\trlhe :......; -. '.''..'''.' \"a\n1070\t\n1071\t\n1072\tExtra fine and Choice. .2415\n1073\t\n1074\t\n1075\tThe receipts of cotton at this port from all\n1076\tsources the past week were 4 ,49f bales of up\n1077\tland and 2,137 bales sea island, against 81, 767\n1078\tbales of upland and 1852 bales sea island last\n1079\tyear.\n1080\t\n1081\t\n1082\tThe particulars of the receipts have been as\n1083\tfollows: Per Central railroad, 81, GAG bales up\n1084\tland; per Savannah, Florida and Western\n1085\trailway, 9.223 bales upland and 2,137\n1086\tbales sea i