38\tCopy't. 1915. by Newspaper Feature Service. J\n1139\t- n the Andaman Islands. best\n1140\t\n1141\t\n1142\tknown to the world as the\n1143\t: Indian penal settlement main\n1144\ttanned by Great Britain, dis\n1145\t. covered a strange custom in the\n1146\tnortherly island of the Nicobar\n1147\tgroup, that may best be termed the\n1148\trrite of the dying.\n1149\t\n1150\t\n1151\t- Walking through the cultivated\n1152\tlands that have been wrestcd from\n1153\tthe jungle, our party came upon\n1154\tgraveyard. Strangely carved head\n1155\tstones of wood rose above the\n1156\tground ell over the tiny clearing,\n1157\tand on the edge nearest the jungle\n1158\tstood thatched hut. My first\n1159\tthought was that the hut was\n1160\t= mortuary chapel, such as we have at\n1161\tthe gates of our cemeteries. Inquiry\n1162\trevealed that its purpose was quite\n1163\tdifferent.\n1164\t\n1165\t\n1166\tThe Nicobar natives believe dy-\n1167\ting person Qcfiles the hut in which he\n1168\tdies. The spirit of the dead man Is\n1169\tsupposed to linGer around the spot.\n1170\tAnd rs the dead are feared equally\n1171\twith evil spirits, in whom all the\n1172\t\n1173\t\n1174\tAndaman Islanders implicitly be-\n1175\t\n1176\t\n1177\tlicVe, even tho wife of the dead man\n1178\twill rush wildly out of the hut where\n1179\t\n1180\t\n1181\the dies and never enter it again.\n1182\t- To overcome this fearful imaginary\n1183\t\n1184\t\n1185\tdanger to the living the Nicobar\n1186\t\n1187\t\n1188\tnatives erect huts of death beside\n1189\ttheir GraVeyards. When a person\n1190\t\n1191\t\n1192\tII about to die he is taken from\n1193\t\n1194\t\n1195\this hut and carried to the hut be-\n1196\tside the graveyard.\n1197\t\n1198\t\n1199\tThe journey is precisely in the\n1200\t\n1201\t\n1202\tnature of 4 funeral procession. Con-\n1203\tGsequcntIy, when the man does not die,\n1204\t\n1205\t\n1206\tbut recovrs, as sometimes happens.\n1207\t\n1208\t\n1209\the Is as one who has returned from\n1210\t\n1211\t\n1212\tthe dead. He must undergo many\n1213\tceremonies before his defilement is\n1214\tspurged and he Is permitted TO\n1215\t:.minglc once more with sthe living.\n1216\tBut, as the Nicobar islander nights\n1217\t\n1218\t\n1219\ttransportation TO the hut of death\n1220\t\n1221\t\n1222\tWith all his power. the contretemps\n1223\t\n1224\t\n1225\tseldom occurs When n dying man\n1226\t. once finds himself in the hut of\n1227\tdeath no gives up the fight 10 1--we.\n1228\t, Dying he Gazes upon the open grave\n1229\t} that Is TO be mis- last resting place.\n1230\t\n1231\t=== 16_1870-12-04_pna_sn85033699_00200293423_1870120401_0539.json\n1232\tNashville union and American. [volume] (Nashville, Tenn.) 1868-1875 · 1870-12-04\n1233\tcrops: None\n1234\theadline: CAPT. KIDD's TREASURE.\n1235\t\n1236\tThe Story of the Discovery of Capt.\n1237\tkidd's Hidden Treasure \"a Hoax\n1238\tand a SXyindIe.\n1239\t---\n1240\tThe story of the Discovery of Capt.\n1241\t\n1242\t\n1243\tKidd's Hidden Treasure a Hoax\n1244\t\n1245\t\n1246\tand a SVyindIe.\n1247\t\n1248\t\n1249\tCorrespondence of the Cincinnati Commercial\n1250\t\n1251\t\n1252\tNEW YORK, Nov. 30.-The story of the\n1253\tfinding seven thousand dollars of Capt.\n1254\tKidd's treasure in the Hudson, turns out\n1255\tto have been not only hoax, but a\n1256\tswindle, by which three gentlemen were\n1257\tdiddled out of two thousand and four hun\n1258\tbred dollars. The affidavit made by\n1259\tJohn Mearns, and signed by his two fell\n1260\tlows, discloses the following facts.\n1261\tBriggs is silversmith, and lived on\n1262\tEighth avenue, near Sixteth street. He\n1263\tsaid he worked in Maiden Lane, but l\n1264\tcan't find any one that knows him by\n1265\tthat name. l believe he lived by hum\n1266\t5ugging people in this or some other way.\n1267\tl met him at Spiri'ualist seance in Grand\n1268\tstreet, Williamsburg, last winter. We\n1269\tare all Spiritualists. He came to me last\n1270\tspring, ard SAID he had dream three\n1271\ttimes over the same way, and had seen\n1272\tand talked with spirits, and they showed\n1273\thim the place where ships wreck was\n1274\tlying at the bottom of the river, ntar the\n1275\tshore, just below an island on\n1276\tthe Hudson River, not quite up to\n1277\tWest Point. ThSy said rusty iron chest,\n1278\tfull Of gold and silver money and jewelry,\n1279\twas i'll that wreck. All one needed to do\n1280\twas to Try and get it. Then there was\n1281\tanother place. Up among the rocks on\n1282\tthe mountain side, in cave, were pots\n1283\tand boxes of diamonds, jewelry, spoons,\n1284\tbullion, and lots Of precious things. He\n1285\twent to an old wench who lives in an al\n1286\tley near the Brooklyn Navy Yard, near\n1287\tthe Military Park, and we all went there\n1288\t\n1289\t\n1290\t1t was a nasty place. She had dark\n1291\tloom, and on a table in it were human\n1292\tskull and bones. She burned some drugs\n1293\tand had a mumbling TO herself, and then\n1294\ttold us where to go and dig. Briggs then\n1295\tproposed that we should al put in and\n1296\tmake up five hundred dollars, and go 10\n1297\twork, living on that as a general fund\n1298\t\n1299\t\n1300\tWe chartered boat and went and dug.\n1301\tTwice the police came upon us, thinking\n1302\twe were river thieves. We worked a good\n1303\tpart of the summer. Briggs would 90\n1304\tdown and see the wench now and then,\n1305\tas he said, to get directions, and he would\n1306\task for money to pay for the drugs that\n1307\tshe burned in the lamp to make the\n1308\tcharm. We kept on giving him money\n1309\t\n1310\t\n1311\tl gave 5000, Vredenburg gave him\n1312\tsome 5000, and futile gave him\n1313\tnearly 5000. After a while we gave up\n1314\tthe other places, and tried to find the\n1315\twreck that was full If treasure. We\n1316\tworked about three weeks dragging the\n1317\triver. We dare not do anything by day,\n1318\tfor the charm would not work in the\n1319\tdaylight, and besides, if it did, other fel-\n1320\tlows would find out our secret and get the\n1321\tprize perhaps. Briggs was in New York\n1322\tmuch # tn time. We had a hut and\n1323\tboarded ourselves, hiding our boat by Gay,\n1324\tonly going over to Garrison, or Peekskill,\n1325\tfor grOc ries. Once we went to Newburg\n1326\tand read in the paper about the Hudson\n1327\triver pirates, and knew it was all on our\n1328\taccount, so we kept more careful. FL\n1329\tnally Briggs said that great piece of\n1330\tledge of rock that had fallen from the\n1331\tabove concealed the wreck, so we drilled\n1332\tinto it in four places. First we tried a\n1333\tsmall blast, and finally we put in great\n1334\tbig one in the center, with twelve pounds\n1335\tof coarse powder. Sunday night week\n1336\tago, we fired it, at twelve o'clock. 1t was\n1337\tall split apart Briggs rushed down in\n1338\tthe water to his waist and hollered, 'ENCw\n1339\twe have got it.\" and we all took hold and\n1340\teulled out the wreck, and it was the iron\n1341\tchest We broke it open and found\n1342\tit full of coin. Briggs said it was all\n1343\tgold dOubloons, and give US some bright\n1344\tones, the rest were all black and covered\n1345\twith mud, as he said, from age. We heard\n1346\tsome one coming, and hurried and put\n1347\tup all the coin in bags, then Briggs took\n1348\tthe iron chest and sunk it in the river.\n1349\t7 hat is the last we have seen of him.\n1350\tWhen we got to New York we had the\n1351\tcoins examined and found that, except\n1352\tthe few pieces he had put in cur hands,\n1353\tthe whole collection was a lot of copper\n1354\tAmerican and English pennies mixed with\n1355\tmud. We believe he has swindled us out\n1356\tof twenty-fOur hundred dollars, our hard\n1357\tearned savings. The chest fished up\n1358\tproves to be condemned iron box, such\n1359\tas is used by Express Companies. Even\n1360\tthe rust on it seems to be artificial and\n1361\twas probably made by acids. Undoubted-\n1362\tly Briggs planned and executed a most\n1363\tcomplete swindle. Some of the drugs\n1364\tused IN the charm, were found to be cas-.\n1365\tserbia bark and alcohol, with ohlorate of\n1366\tpotash and nitrate acid. He must have\n1367\tlowed the rotten old Prov of the wreck\n1368\tfrom its muddy bed just below New.\n1369\tburg, and then sunk the chest and mock\n1370\ttreasure On it just beside the ledge that\n1371\twas blasted. Previous to bis last appear\n1372\tance on the spot he asked for more money,\n1373\twhich was refused, as The diggers were\n1374\tdiscourag 4. They were all mechanics,\n1375\tand declare that if they catch Briggs they\n1376\twill send him to the work of spirits in 8\n1377\tjiffy. The probability is that he has\n1378\tsailed for England, which is his native\n1379\tplace Very few traces of the explosion\n1380\tare visible where the chest was found.\n1381\t\n1382\t=== 17_1883-11-17_p1_sn88064005_00200299048_1883111702_0299.json\n1383\tThe Meridional. (Abbeville, La.) 1856-1906 · 1883-11-17\n1384\tcrops: None\n1385\theadline: \n1386\t---\n1387\tII parait que Pesplit de Ouiteau\n1388\tparcourt, presque chaque mutt, les\n1389\tcorridors ct les celluIcs de la pri-\n1390\tson de Washington. Tellc est, do\n1391\tmoins, l'hailucination dune dou-\n1392\taaine de prisonniers, la plupart\n1393\tdes femmes de couleur. Un hom'\n1394\tme a failli devenir fou par suite de\n1395\tces prftendues apparitions. Quand\n1396\tlesprit apparait, les femmes pous\n1397\tsent de grands eris /; celia a, sem\n1398\tble.til, Ia vertu de les faire dis\n1399\tparaitre. Les croyances sont fort\n1400\tenracinees dams l'esprit des gems\n1401\tde conleur, impossible de les er\n1402\ttirer.\n1403\t\n1404\t=== 4_1880-11-05_p3_sn82015483_00271764169_1880110501_0441.json\n1405\tMorning journal and courier. · 1880-11-05\n1406\tcrops: None\n1407\theadline: LOCAL NEWS.\n1408\t\n1409\tGhostly GrabberS.\n1410\t---\n1411\tExtraordinary Case of Burglary a nd R es- titution i'm Connecticut.\n1412\t\n1413\t strange story of robbery and restitution comes from the town O. Ledyard, on the Thames river, just below Norwich. About the middle of last month burglars entered the country seat of Captain H. M. Randall, 8 prosperous resident of that town, and stole $15. 1000 of United States government bonds and other securities. Captain Randalrs resi- dence is wide, breezy, old fashioned farm house. It is on the Norwich road, and is 8 mile north of the village of Gales Ferry the nearest dwelling is that of John Watson, twenty rods away. Just before the robbery Captain Randall and his wife sailed for New York in the captains large three-masted schooner, the Hettie V. Kelsey. Mrs Ran dall returned to her home a week later, leav- ing her husband in New York. During the absence of the family the house was left in charge of Mrs. Randall's father, an elderly gentleman, and his sister. At no time in the absence of Mrs Randall were the suspicious of the occupants of the house aroused. One day only did they leave the dwelling unoccupied. AS soon as Mrs. Randall had returned she discovered that the large private desk of the captain had been opened and it's contents disturbed She found that several savings bank books, gov ernment bonds, Atlantic Insurance compa- nys scrip, registered in Hartford, and other papers, among which were notes of large amounts. had been taken. Mrs. Randall tel egraphed that payment be stopped on the bonds. Captain Randall was appraised of the robbery by telegraph, and he placed the case in the hands of detective, who set to work with official zeal and sagacity. In other words, he made no progress and no discovery.\n1414\t\n1415\t A few days ago, on bright autumnal morning, Mrs. Randall was chatting about the robbery with lady friend on the lawn in front of her house. The doors and many of the windows were open. When Mrs. Randall came out of the house no one was left within. Everything inside was in its usual position. A quarter of an hour later she returned to the dwelling. An errand led her to the room in which was the desk of the captain Her astonishment may be imagined at discovering her box containing the missing securities on stand near the desk. She eagerly opened it. and her surprise was re- doubled at finding the securities within and undisturbed. By whom the property was stolen and returned, and in what manner, are still puzzles for the captain and his wife. Superstitious neighbors hint at ghosts. But the ghosts certainly live around there some- where-Hartt0rd Times.\n1416\t\n1417\t=== 5_1907-02-22_p1_sn83045081_00296025823_1907022201_0469.json\n1418\tDer Deutsche correspondent. [volume] (Baltimore, Md.) 1841-1918 · 1907-02-22\n1419\tcrops: None\n1420\theadline: Snititia ipridt.\n1421\t---\n1422\ts.. @e1r &. 50. ,acpinnen eillclt llod)\n1423\t\n1424\t\n1425\tbonn groBeu Seltcr 6errii6renScn\n1426\t\n1427\t\n1428\t8rojeb. - Gic Baltimore bios\n1429\t\n1430\t\n1431\t@a6n\" Mill feline Steiicrn be5ob-\n1432\t\n1433\t\n1434\tlen.\n1435\t\n1436\t\n1437\t&idter S~todbrioge crfonntc ge-\n1438\titern Der 6irmo $. @ebr & so. in ci'\n1439\tmem Yro5eiie gegen Die vltniteo Goil-\n1440\tman & electric 60\" $3S0.91 81. Ger\n1441\t6aQ fono ieinen llripriing bei'm\n1442\tgroBen Selier, ais Die Sirrno @ebr\n1443\t& So. BON Der Gompagnie alles olte\n1444\tGlen Der groBen Grottitroben-araft-\n1445\titotion fir ben Yreis BON So Die Son-\n1446\tne fauite, SpGter perfaufte ieood\n1447\tDie nSereinigte\" fit $1,000 oltes Mc.\n1448\ttai nod Ebilooelpbio. IliS oieiem\n1449\tangebliden Go%ntraft6rude reinltirte\n1450\tDer NniprlId.\n1451\t\n1452\t\n1453\tHifffabrt 6ebinbcrt.\n1454\t\n1455\t\n1456\tSic y9oltinorc.S bio g.. Giienba6ll-\n1457\t80\" muroe I'M Common EleoSs@c-\n1458\trid)t bon 2eniu @. Goaiion oui $4,000.\n1459\tSfooencriob perflo9t, neil iie, ipie\n1460\tbcboitptct miro, ourd) ibrc goilten\n1461\tbic S6ifffobrt ollf benz meItli6en\n1462\tGbeile bes TatopSco bcbinOert. Ser\n1463\talGgcr 9ing I'M Sllli 1906 einen Con-\n1464\ttraft Elm, lponod] CT nlcbrcrc Sdiffs.\n