eice of Carolinian\n1678\tmarble he could find, and chiscled it into\n1679\tshape amid the gibes and sneers of the\n1680\tpeople. What folly they said, .for this\n1681\tplasterer to presume to sculpture marble\n1682\tlike the great artists of Italy!' He shut\n1683\thimself up from their gaze-he hid himself\n1684\tfrom their sneers in his little shop until he\n1685\thad finished his work. He had never\n1686\tseen works of art, the manner in which\n1687\tthe sculptor does his work, nor the instru-\n1688\t\n1689\t\n1690\t\n1691\t\n1692\t\n1693\the produced one of the finest pieces of\n1694\tsculpture in the world in the bust of Mr..\n1695\tCalhoun. 1t was the admiration and as\n1696\ttonishment of all. The city of Charleston, ),\n1697\tas an appreciation of the work, presented\n1698\thim splendid gold medal, and placed the\n1699\tbust in the City Hall. Some friends, ad\n1700\tmiring his genius, offered the means to send\n1701\thim to Italy; and he was, as we have said,\n1702\ton his way there. passing through Wash\n1703\tington, at the fortunate moment when the\n1704\tJackson-statue committee was in search of\n1705\tan artist. He had never before been in\n1706\t| Washington. But spending a few days in\n1707\tthis city, to look at its public buildings and\n1708\tits works of art, he became acquainted with\n1709\tsome of the committee and with their OBJ\n1710\tject. They invited him to give design.\n1711\tAt first he declined, never having seen an\n1712\tequestrian statue. He was modest-that\n1713\tis his character. But the genius of his\n1714\tfame disturbed his rest, and woke up the\n1715\tstrong native powers of his mind. 1t\n1716\thaunted his Imagination when all the\n1717\tworld was asleep, a thousand beautiful\n1718\tforms tloated before his waking vision-\n1719\tThere was the great jackson who has i'm\n1720\tpressed the grandeur of his soul and the\n1721\trepublican simplicity of his character on\n1722\tthe institutions and mind of his country,\n1723\tand on the future of the world. That\n1724\t. form passed before the imagination of the\n1725\tartist in the character of poor peasant\n1726\tboy, of hardy pioneer of the wilderness,\n1727\tof IL Judge, of Il senator, of general, and\n1728\tas President of the United States; the ar\n1729\ttst saw him in all the remarkable scenes of\n1730\this life. He imbodied them in marble and\n1731\t| in bronze. One form he seized-one vi\n1732\tsion he arrested and fixed in his mind\n1733\t1t was the hero of New Orleans, in all his\n1734\tcharacteristic majesty ,intrepidity and firm\n1735\tness, mounted on ma jestic war-steed,\n1736\tpaving the air with ferceness and restless\n1737\timpatience, as if conscious of the coming\n1738\tbattle, yet yielding to the iron nerve Of\n1739\this rider. 1t was the battle of New Or\n1740\tleans, when the General was hurriedly\n1741\triding in review of his forces, few mo-\n1742\tments before the engagement, which was\n1743\tthe form and scene that fastened itself up\n1744\ton the artists mind. In AN instant he\n1745\tworked it into bronze, and it stood before\n1746\thim living thing. His mind was agi-\n1747\t| tated he was \"haunted with an idea. -\n1748\tBut doubts came. He looked back to his\n1749\tpast history, and the difficulties before\n1750\thim-a fatal stumbling block to modest\n1751\tgenius. The world of art, with its schools,\n1752\tits authorities and its rules, stood up to\n1753\trebuke the presumptuous plasterer. 1t\n1754\tdid not recognize him; he was out of the\n1755\tpale of its society; he had never gradua-\n1756\tted For moment he felt discouraged.\n1757\tBut then he heard a whisper in his ear\n1758\tthe Genius of his fame said ''Follow Ma\n1759\tture-Genius is superior to Art-Art\n1760\tmakes its rules from Nature-such is the\n1761\tevidence of history-Nature is as grand,\n1762\tas beautiful, as various in America as in\n1763\t| Italy or EurOpe-study Nature-folIow\n1764\t\n1765\t=== 9_1914-12-23_pna_sn84020558_0020219610A_1914122301_0573.json\n1766\tArizona republican. [volume] (Phoenix, Ariz.) 1890-1930 · 1914-12-23\n1767\tcrops: None\n1768\theadline: DEPosED RECTOR WILL FIGHT\n1769\t---\n1770\t\n1771\t\n1772\t\n1773\tPHILADELPHIA, Pa, Dec. 22.-\n1774\tThat Rev. DIR. George Chalmers Rich\n1775\tmOnd, the deposed rector of ST Johns\n1776\tchurch, will put up a vigorous fight\n1777\twhen the ecclesiastical court tries him\n1778\ton January 18th, is predicted by his\n1779\tstatements that the Protestant Episco-\n1780\tpal church takes its cue from Wall\n1781\tstreet. The vestry of st. Johns is\n1782\tstanding by the deposed rector and\n1783\tthere are threats that the church\n1784\twould withdraw from communion with\n1785\tthe Protestant Episcopal chuch and\n1786\tunite with the reformed Episcopal\n1787\tchurch. DR. Richmond is accused by\n1788\tBishop Rhinelander of violation of\n1789\this ordination vows and disregard of\n1790\tthe bishops authority. DR. Richmond\n1791\tasserts he is being assailed for his\n1792\tfreedom of speech in preaching the\n1793\ttruth frankly and fearlessly. DR..\n1794\tRichmond charges that the bishop\n1795\twas elected by convention controll-\n1796\ted by capitalists. and that the Holy\n1797\tGhost was not present at that meet\n1798\ting\n1799\t\n1800\t=== 14_1920-05-25_p1_sn86058226_00271745321_1920052501_0051.json\n1801\tThe Richmond palladium and sun-telegram. [volume] (Richmond, Ind.) 1907-1939 · 1920-05-25\n1802\tcrops: None\n1803\theadline: \n1804\t---\n1805\tfor the amount due on my accident\n1806\tpolicy\" he said. Il fell down a long\n1807\tflight of stairs the other evening and\n1808\tsustained damages that will disable\n1809\tme for some time to come\"\n1810\t\n1811\t\n1812\tThe manager gave him a firm look.\n1813\t\n1814\t\n1815\tYoung man.\" he replied, Il have\n1816\tinvestigated your case, and find that\n1817\tyou are not entitled to anything. 1t\n1818\tcould not be called an accident, for\n1819\tyou certainly knew that the young\n1820\tlady's father was at home\"\n1821\t\n1822\t\n1823\tThey were rafting along the coun-\n1824\ttry road in an old-fashioned horse\n1825\tdrawn vehicle. Suddenly large\n1826\twhite house doomed up on the right\n1827\tof the road.\n1828\t\n1829\t\n1830\tThat house.\" drawled the farmer,\n1831\tnodding his head in its direction, lis\n1832\trun by couple of these here spirit\n1833\tualists.'\n1834\t\n1835\t\n1836\tDO they take summer boarders?\"\n1837\tasked one of the city girls.\n1838\t\n1839\t\n1840\tNope\" said the farmer, only\n1841\tOuija boarders. Judge.\n1842\t"}]},"uuid":"6de56052-c8d1-4019-b278-2cbce42ce82d","timestamp":"2026-10-31T08:18:23.345Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/c1/d03.txt","content":"=== 36_1935-08-08_pna_sn87093377_00200291414_1935080801_0449.json\nThe Northwest enterprise. [volume] (Seattle, Wash.) 1931-1952 · 1935-08-08\ncrops: None\nheadline: PHANTOM ARMY\n\nlc.ontInlled from paee Onel\n---\n\n\n\nIN the scorehing plains Of Eritrea\nand italian somaliland.\n\n\nBattle at Wal Wal\n\n\nIl imagine that If war is deelar-\ned the first clash win come at Wal\nWal, where there is q string Of\nlife-giying wells, some Of which\nare held by italy and some by Eth\niopia.\n\n\n\"The Italians should very quick\n\" gain the lines Of wells.\n\n\nEthiopian troops viii put up a\ncertain amount of resistance here.\nThey wiz also resist to some ex-\ntent In Eritrea.\n\n\neThey ezill then disappear into\nthe mountains. leaving the Italians\nto march across the burning desert\nland facing all the difficulties Of\ndisease ard supplies with an ever\nincreasing line of communications\nopen to sniping and direct attack.\n\n\nmo one 1ut a fool would fight\nin the plains, the Emperor told me\nand his policy is sound.\n\n\nMany Difficulties\n\n\n1.-THE road to Adds Ababa. the\ncapital, and ultimate conquest is\nstrewn with difficulties for q Euro\npean army.\n\n\n1T is true that water is to be\nhad In the hills. It is true that\nyou leave behind the heat and the\ndisease of the desert land.\nbut it takes time to get accli-\nmatized to the heights in which\nthe Philippians are at home.\n\n\nSome Europeans take month\nto get used to the rarefied air at\nheights of 1000 and 5000 feet.\nSome take a year. A mile climb is\nagony without equipment. 1t will\nbe a painting army that tries to\nforce its way through the boulder\nstrewn mountains.\n\n\nInvisible Army\n\n\nIAN the time 1t win be fighting\nan almost invisible army that can\nmove fom point to point with in\ncredible swlftness.\n\n\nut viii Ne fighting men whose\none ambition is to die in battle.\nAlthough Christians, the fighting\nmen of Ethiopia are fatalists. All\ntheir sins are forgiven by the\npriests before they go into battle.\nDeath in war is the direct road to\nheaven.\n\n\n''They have their priests in the\nrearguard to comfort them. They\nhave their women to tend to their\nneeds. They have no problem of\nsupply. Each soldier carries a\nsmall bag of dried beans that will\nfeed him for at least month.\n\n\nHE carries a knife which is used\nfor picking thorns from his bare\nfeet, or for cuttng up animals he\ncatches and eats raw. The whole\narmy, wives. and priests included,\ncan run for miles on end even in\nthis mountainous county.\n\n\nHidden among the mountains\nthey can vanish from one point\nand appear at another to renew the\nattack with incredible speed\n\n\nFine Shots\n\n\n\"They are excellent shots, and\nexcel at sniping. Those who have\nno trifles win fight with spears.\nHeavy casualties will not deter\nthem. OF what use are airplanes\nagainst this phantom army? OF\nwhat use tanks in this rocky land :?\n\n\nAThese are some of the problems\nthat the italian generals win have\nto face.\n\n\n'oThe modern mechanical army\nNAV win through. But it must be\na slow business.\n\n\nEvery march forward will have\nto be made with large vanguards,\nrearguards and flank guards to\nprevent surprise. Heavy guards\nwin have to be kept to protect the\nlines Of communications\n\n\neAnd If airplanes, the eyes Of the\narmy, fail in the craggy heights,\nhundreds Of men will have to be\nkept alert night and day in the\ndeadly game of hide and seek at\nwhich fine .F,thionian soldier is past\n\n=== 9_1921-02-08_p6_sn85038485_00340583012_1921020801_0751.json\nThe Birmingham age-herald. · 1921-02-08\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nThe practice of letting Sing Sing convicts go to the bedside of dying relatives has been temporarily aban- doned. A sorrowfufl convict who went away for that purpose with x guard hasn't returned yet\n\n # # #\n\n After listening to few heated re- marks from General Dawes. that con gressional committee had chastened feeling. Let US hope 1t did them lot of good.\n\n r Charles of Austria still retains his high-sounding titles. They mean about as little as ''coloner' when applied to an ld-fashioned southern gentleman who never saw military service.\n\n # # <\n\n The Nevada assembly has passed a law making it harder for outsiders to get divorce at Reno. A longer pe- riod of residence will be great help to the hotel proprietors.\n\n Before starting to dismember Ger many, France should consider the size of the job. and the probability that she might need help. that would not be forthcoming.\n\n < F a\n\n MT. Gompers' admiration for the new government of Mexico is not shared evidently by our state depart men..\n\n The New Jersey man who would rather spend a year in jail than return to his wife knows, where to find peace and quiet.\n\n America is making most of the worlds motion pictures. This explains the international reputation enjoyed by MT. eChaplin and MT. Arbuckle.\n\n is * x The Detroit spiritualist who killed himself to prove that the dead can talk to the living will be terribly dis appointed if he finds the line busy.\n\n # y *\n\n Now that we know practically ev- erything Mrs Harding bought in New York we can resume our study of the Harding cabinet.\n\n The French journalist who tricked Wilhelm into an interview with the aid of female relative will have to pin his medal on himself.\n\n * * z\n\n Raleigh, N. C.. has a boy, twelve years old, who can read dozen books day. We would recommend in his case plenty of exercise in the open air.\n\n * * #\n\n The housing shortage doesn't ac- count for the fact that so many peo- pie are practically living in their cars\n\n as x * War with Japan is declared \"un- thinkable.' The World war was un- thinkable until after it started.\n\n x # *\n\n careless compiler includes state ment from Crown Prince Willie in his list of remarkable remarks\"\n\n * * # It doesn't take much of an optimist to be glad he isn't living in Betrograd or Moscow.\n\n=== 34_1911-10-26_p2_sn85042354_00383343161_1911102601_1077.json\nEvening journal. · 1911-10-26\ncrops: None\nheadline: sPOOK SOCIAL IN CHuRcH,.\n---\nGrace Church Epworth League will\ngive a Spook Social in the Social Hall\nof the church this evening. Witches\nand ghosts will receive the guests.\n\n=== 2_1920-04-27_pna_sn87055779_00296021477_1920042701_0690.json\nSouth Bend news-times. (South Bend, Ind.) 1913-1938 · 1920-04-27\ncrops: None\nheadline: OUIJA BOARD BENEFITS.\n---\nWe rapidly becoming a nation of ouja board\nfans No home. in localities is considered\ncomplete without its ouja board which is con-\nsuited by every member OF the family either in fun\nin foolish earnest The popular idea seems to be\nthat ouija decide question with little strain\n10 the tired human brain of the questioner\n\n\nWhether not the consulters have gained\nfort and sage advice leading to health wealth and\nhappiness that little board has brought\nto group of citizens. Manufacturers OF\nouija boards have prospered greatly in recent\nmonths.\n\n\nOne eastern factory found It necessary to enlarge\nplant The addition S125,OOO. I contains\n$4,000 square Of floor space devoted entirely\nand exclusively 6uija boards. Not spirits. but\nfinancial returns led the concern undertake this\nstep of expansion.\n\n\none might expect this factory to be haunted by\ntakative spOoks But it is reported that nothing has\nmaterialized date EXCEPT increased business and\ngood profits No spirit writing has appeared walls\nfloors tables and the newly manufactured\noUija boards present wooden faces to the workers\nthey tend to their respective jobs the\nfactory.\n\n=== 6_1869-03-24_p1_sn84028490_00280775009_1869032401_0175.json\nThe Stark County Democrat. [volume] (Canton, Ohio) 1833-1912 · 1869-03-24\ncrops: None\nheadline: SPECIAL NOTICES.\n\nNJ\n---\nEEMALES, OWING TO THE\n\n\n1 peculiar and important relations which\nthey sustain, their peculiar organization. and the\nohces they preforA, are subject 10 many suherings\nFreedom from these contribute in NO small degree\nto their happiness and welfare, for none can bG\nhappy who are I'll. Not only so, but no one OF\neaed to run on without Involving the general\nhealth OF the individual. and ere long producing\nis It pleasant to consult a phyeician for the relief OF\nthese Aarious delicate affection, and only upon the\nmost urgeLt necessity viii a true womao so far\nsacrifice her greatest charm as 10 do this. The\nsex will then thank US for placing in their hands\nsimp, = specifies which will be found efacaeious in\nrelieving aud curing almost every one OF those\ntroublesome complaints peculiar to the sex\n\n\nHELNBoLD's EXTRACT OF BUoHU Hundreds\nsuffer on IN silence, and hundreds OF others apply\nYainly TO druggists'an and DOCTORS. who merely { ant\nI aiZe them with the hope ola cure or apply rem\nedies which make them Worse / would not wish\nTO assert anything that would do Injustice to the\nafflicted. but I am obliged to say that although 1t\npowers OF life. oy laborious emplo yment, unwhole-\nsome air and food, prctusE menstruation, the use\nOF tea and eohee, and frequent childbirth, It is far\nmucous membrane OF the vir