s,\nprovisions there must be five kinds o\nanimal food uncooked, and the five kind\nwhich are cooked; also, variety Of cakes\nand dishes of vegetables, with fruits, wine\nand tea. The spectator may notice Who'll.\nfowls and fish fantastically ornamented\nalso, pigs head, or an entire hog; will\npyramids of cakes and fruits, and vases o\nflowers All these are borne to the grav,\nat the time of the interment, where they\nare again arranged in order, and suffered\nto remain awhile as an offering to the\ndead, and are then brought home to fur\nnish a repast to the family and friends\n\n\nBefore the body is placed in the coffin\nand while the offerings remain upon the\ntables, mourning women are gathered\naround. who cause the air to resOund with\ntheir wailings. The wife, concubines, anG\ndaughters-in-law, or any friend, may join\nin these wailings; but often there are only\nhired mourners.\n\n\nThese lamentations are exceedingly lu\ngubrious, and are mixture of sobbing, oil\neulogies of the dead, and of regrets for the\nbereavement, and deprecating the sad lol\nof those who have been robbed of a friend\nor ofa support and provider. The speeches\nare generally improvised; but sometimes\nare according to formulas which have long\nbeen used, and which have been walled\nover myriads of corpses.\n\n\nAny relation or friend who is so dis\nposed may contribute his quota to these\naudible demonstrations of grief; and one\nwill say: 40 thou departed one, / am\nthy relative; this day hast thou suddenly\ndeceased. Never can our affection perish\nit is impossible to restrain weeping; from\nthis time never more may we behold thee.\nin the parting our heart is torn; but we\nhope that after death thy soul has Joy and\npeace, having ascended to the heavenly\npalace, there continually to confer pros\nperlty on thy children and grand children.\nWhile in life, all thy dealings with men\nwere benevolent and righteous; with an\nupright heart dwelling amongst men, per\nforming thy business with wisdom By\nright, heaven ought to have prolonge\nthy age to hundred years. Wherefore,\nthen, by this one sickness art thou already\ndead? We are thy relatives, we are thy\nfriends; and how shall not our bowels be\nsundered by the force of our distress and\nlamentations Alas, alas\"\n\n\nWhen one mourner ceases another COM\nmences and chants his or her dirge, and\nsays \"Alas, alasi Why was it not that\nin the land of the living, an inheritor of\nThou, so talented and wise; thou Oughtest\nto have been spared to become an officer\nof the empire, even as pillar of the royal\npalace\" And perhaps another adds: 90\nthou Oughtest to have been spared to thy\nnative town, the hope of the inhabitants,\nand to whom they look. Thou vast one\nwho vert able to teach thy sons all\nrighteousness, end all upright measures.\nBut now thou art gone. Alas, alasp\n\n\nWe have been told that it is not an un-\ncommon occurrence for old family troubles\nto be referred to in some indirect way, as\nwhen a secondary wife with no occasion\nfor the use of counterfeit sOrrow) will\nwall out: \"Ah me! Who now will take\nmy part when oppressed by the mistresss\"\nand as when the daughter-in-law sobs out\nher apprehensions of increasing tyranny\nfrom the mother-in-law, by saying\nALAS! what will become of me since my\nonly friend is departed?\"\n\n\nAt Chinese funerals in San Francisco,\nthese hired mourning women are some\ntimes put into carriages to follow the body\nto the grave. They may be known by\nthe white garments and white hoods\nwhich they wear-white being the funeral\ncolor.\n\n\nIn many cases band of Chinese musi-\nclans is employed to join in the procession,\nand escort the deceased to his last resting\nplace. Whatever may be the design in\nfurnishing this music, we outside barba-\nrians are apt to regard it as better adapted\nto frighten away evil spirits than to fur\nnish agreeable entertainment to disem-\nbodied soul.\n\n\nFunerals of aged men or dignitaries,\nwhich are designed to be very impressive,\noften have one or more young men follow\ning the hearse on foot. These represent\nthe sons of the deceased, and are dressed\nscantily in some coarse fabric of dirty\nwhite; they are bare-footed, leaning upon\na cane, and go bowling down with their\nface toward the earth, being supported by\nfriend on either side. All this is en-\nblematical not only of their crushing sor-\nrow, but also of the irreparable loss sus-\ntanned by the family; intimating that now\nas the head and support of the house is\nremoved, the survivors will be left without\nq provider, and must therefore pursue the\nremainder of life's journey in poverty and\nsorrow.\n\n\nThose strips of brown paper, pierced\nwith holes, to represent strings of copper\ncoin, and which are scattered in such pro-\nfusion as Chinese funeral c0rtege proceeds\nto the place of interment, are denOminated\nmoney for buying the road\"\n\n\nThe theory is, that everywhere there\nmay be hungry or ill-disposed spirits who\nhave it in their power to stop on the way\nthe spirit of the deceased, or by other\nmeans to interfere and prevent his peace\nfull settlement at the tomb provided for\nhim; therefore this paper, representing\nmoney, is scattered everywhere along the\nroad to buy from the vagrant spirits the\nright of way.\n\n\nAt the place of cepulture those pro-\nvisions previously mentioned are again ar\nranged before the grave; and lbations of\nwine and tea are poured out; and large\nsupplies OF money, clothing, and other\nthings, supposed to be f1eeded by the de-\nceased in the world to which he has gone,\nare sent on after him. The money is pa\nper cd: and folded so as to represent gold\nand silver bars, or copper cash; and this\nis burned in large amounts. Paper is\nmade into boxes to represent chests of\nclothing. There may be paper servants,\nalso a sedan chair with its bearers; and\nall these are burned, and thus sent over\ninto the world of spirits. While the\ncorpse remained in the house, before the\nfuneral, these images and paper represent\nations of furniture were arranged around\nthe body; the servants being represented\nas in the act of waiting upon their master.\n-Ooertand Monthly.\n\n\nManufacturing Popularity.\n\n\nA letter from Paris in the New York\n\n=== 19_1895-01-26_p1_sn85025350_00415627671_1895012601_0459.json\nFrostburg mining journal. [volume] (Frostburg, Md.) 1871-1913 · 1895-01-26\ncrops: None\nheadline: GHOSTS OF THE SEA.\n\nSAILORS DREAD TO SEE THE SHIPS\n\n\nTHAT NEVER SAILED.\n---\nTHAT NEVER SAILED.\n\n\nAn Old Salt Tells of His FIRST Experience\n\n\nWith Phantom Ship-XhiIe a Terri\n\n\nBILE Hurricane HowIs 1t Rides Easily\n\n\nwith All Sails Set.\n\n\n\"These tales of the ships that never\ncame back are sad enough, but it's the\nship that never went out, the ghosts Of\ncreepy feeling when he meets them out\nwhere the waves are rolling high and\nthe winds are singing funeral songs\n\n\nThe speaker was Mate Bob Alling,\nwho has followed the sea as boy and\nman for nearly so years: Surrounded by\na group of interested listeners in the\npublic room of the Sailors Happy home\nin South street, Mate Bob, as all the\nsailors call him. was telling stories of\nstrange sights that he has witnessed at\nsea- Alling is now mate of a coastwise\nfishing schooner, but in the old days he\nsailed in some of the largest trading ships\nand the strongest whalers that ever\nsailed out of an American port flying\nthe stars and stripes.\n\n\nGas said, boys\" the old man went\non, its tho ships that never went out\nfrom any port that sailor never forgets\nwhen he sees one Of them. We may re-\nmember the ships that went out and\nnever came back for time if we had\nshipmate aboard. but we can forget.\nBut there's no forgetting ghost of the\nsea.\n\n\nut was back in the early fifties that\nl saw my first sea ghost, and today\ncan shut my eyes and see it just as plain\nas l could see it then. / was sailor ON\na fishing schooner, and we were catch\ning cod off the coast of Newfoundland.\nWe had been out ten days and were al\nmost ready to start for home with full\ncargo when nasty off shore breeze\ncame on late one evening. We stood out\nto sea, for there was heavy fog along\nwith the wind. 1t was stiff and steady\nblow, so we rode the waves under bare\npoles during the night, waiting for day\nlight and the fog to lift before shaping\nour course for home.\n\n\n''All through the night we sent up\nrockets at intervals and kept the ships\nbell going, because we could not see\nten feet ahead at times on account of\nthe fog. But it was nearly morning be-\nfore we heard or saw an answering sig\nnal to warn US that another vessel was\nnear.\n\n\nIl was on the carly morning watch,\nand about half an hour before daylight\n| made out a signal light short dis\nlance ahead on our port bow. 1t was a\nstrange light, a pale blue in color, and\nit flashed up and down at irregular in\ntervals. Tho fog was still thick, and it\nwas impossible to tell how near we were\nto the vessel\n\n\n81 called the mate OH deck and point\ned to the strange signal light He look\ned at it long time, and, with a shake\nof his head, said he could not make it\nout at all. The lights showed that the\nvessel could not be far away, so we\nchanged our course little, and then\nfired gun. There was no answer, and\nwe fired again and again with the same\nresult.\n\n\n\"By and by 1 saw that the mate was\nvery grave and queer looking. He was\npacing back and forth on the deck, not\nminding the cold misty rain that was\nfalling, and all the time he kept his\neyes fixed on that queer looking blush\nlight that flashed up out of the fog and\ndarkness ahead. At last | made so bold\nas to- ask the mate what he thought of\nit.\n\n\nIt's ghostn he said, looking at\nme with plying look.\n\n\n\"'A ghost?\" says /.\n\n\nYes, a ghost Of the sea- Wait till\nthe fog lifts. You may see it then\n\n\n''The fog lifted bit when daylight\ncame, and then saw the ghost, as the\nmate said would.\n\n\n''Rising grim and white out of the\nfog and waves that were rolling high, l\nsaw full rugged ship of queer design.\nThe wind was blowing half gale, but\nnot a mast Or pole OF the ghost ship\nbent an inch, and not one of the broad\nwhite sails seemed to strain at the ropes.\n\n\n1.-THE phantom was close ON our port\nbow, and as the fog cleared away we\nhad a splendid view from her water line\nto the top of her masts. With the waves\npitching and rolling mountain high, and\nthe wind bowling around our poles, the\nghost ship was riding out the storm as\nsteady as painted ship on painted\ncanvas ocean.\n\n\nEVERY man OH board crowded ON\ndeck, and while they had to hold ON to\nthe railing to keep from being washed\noverheard they stood and gazed at the\nphantom ship as long as it was in sight.\nSome Of them that We're bit religious\nlike made tho sign of the cross, and 9TH\ners tried to say bit of prayer The\nfact is that every man on board thought\nthe phantom ship q warming Of DEATH\n\n\n\"The captain went below and drank\nhot grog till he was that reckless no\nghost had any terrors for him. Then, as\nthe wind went down a bit, he ordered\nus to make sail and bear down on the\nphantom ship.\n\n\n\"Up went our sail in jitfy, and we\nflew along before the wind, but we\ncould get no nearer the phantom ship.\nSuddenly we saw the white ghost ship\nlurch forward, her sails trembled for an\ninstant, and then she seemed to sink\nstraight down into the sea- Ten seconds\nfrom the time we saw the first culver of\nher sails the shin had vanished, and we\n\n=== 2_1896-09-16_pna_sn85047084_00202195933_1896091601_0535.json\nThe Pacific commercial advertiser. [volume] (Honolulu, Hawaiian Islands) 1885-1921 · 1896-09-16\ncrops: None\nheadline: Saw Ghosts.\n---\ncorrespondent writing from Hilo\ntells of mysterious happening on the\nthe following language:\n\n\nImmediately after the earthquake a\nwoman was seen to run across the\ncourthouse yard and disappear while\nrunning, Whether she was a ghost\nwitch or some other kind of mystery,\nno one seems to know. Just as she got\nabout the middle of the yard three more\ndressed in the same manner were seen\nrunning in the opposite direction along\nFront street. This remarkable occur\nrenee was witnessed by many people.\n1t may be that these apparitions were\nsome of Peles delegates.\n\n=== 15_1908-06-12_pna_sn84020731_00416157133_1908061201_0962.json\nThe Durham recorder. [volume] (Durham, N.C.) 1879-19?? · 1908-06-12\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\n:himed II Randolph reused himself\nwith slight start from the half doze\nin which he had INDUCED.\n\n\nlnvoluntarily he gazed at the por-\ntrait. and great wonder came into his\neyes. He wheeled his chair away from\nthe fireplace and stared. He rubbed\nhis eyes. looked about the softly light\ned room and stared again. @\n\n\nThere was the same youthful figure,\nwith its gray plumed hat. gray gown\nmd rose pink cloak caught together by\none slender white hand. 1t was all as\nIt had been before save that now\nthere was life in it--life In the soft\nbrown eyes ard In certain tremulous\nmovement of the pink bowed lips.\n\n\nWith white face and unbelieving\neyes he watched the figure step slowly\ndown from the frame, and he saw the\nmovement of the purple velvet dra.\nperies that formed the background.\n\n\nSac stepped upon the wide mantel.\npiece, which was devoid Of ornament,\nand made an imperious gesture with\nher hand. and as she did so he was\nconscious of the heavy scent Of white\nlilac from the branch she carried.\n\n\nMY good sir. pray assist me to the\nfloor\" she said In low. vibrant voice.\n\n\nAS in dream. Randolph hastily\nfetched chair and supplemented Its\nIa fyht Enr\ncourtesy he extended a hand TO the\nlady on the mantel. and as she gave\nhim the tips of her fingers he felt the\nwarmth Of flesh and blood.\n\n\nLightly she stepped down. gathering\nher skirts gracefully about her dainty\ngray shod feet. and presently she stood\nbeside him. looking timidly at him\nfrom beneath the shadow Of her long\nlashes.\n\n\nMy GOdw he muttered. staring from\nthe vision before him to the empty\nframe above the chimney piece.\n\n\nWith \"A quick movement the girl\nslipped the pink cloak from her shout.\nders and removed the gray plumed\nhat from her crown OF dusky hair. \"It\nIi only l, Mark\" she said wistfully.\n\n\n\"l-don't understand-yet\" He pass.\nI'd hand over his dazed eyes.\n\n\nShe stepped forward and pressed\nupon carved portion Of the mantel.\nsimultaneously there was movement,\nand the painted canvas swung into\nplace with a little click. There was\nMistress Mowbray In her accustomed\nplace above the shelf. and beside him\nwas-Beth.\n\n\nut Is one Of Harry's jokes\" she ex'\nplanned In low. breathless tone. \"He\nwrote me a month ago and asked me\nTO come here and enact the apparition\nOF Mistress Mowbray. II was favor\nlite pastime when we were children.\nfor there Is a secret stair behind the\nchimney. and the portrait is really\ndoor which opens upon it. Harry said\nthat 'skeptical friend would spend\ntle night Of the 18th here and asked\nme 10 play the part. was reluctant to\ndo such a thing. but Harry has been\nvery kind to me-and consented.\nThey did not tell me who the 'skep-\nTeal friend would be. and II was-a\nsurprise 10 me-when I looked down |\nupon your upturned face\"\n\n\nShe leaned against the tall back of a |\nchair with down bent head and CULVER\nIng lips.\n\n\nAnd you could have gone back-and |\naway from me again-and never |\nwould have known\" he said, n vague\nwonder dawning In his eyes.\n\n\nShe did not reply\n\n\nBut you Stayed-you made yourself\nknown TO me-you came down TO me!\nWhat do you mean. BETH\"\n\n\nShe lifted her head slowly and look\nRI at him through misty. Joyous eyes,\naud he understood.\n\n\nHannah's voice broke crispIy upon\nthe blissful silence a cablegram. Mr..\n\n\n\nMark tore open the yellow envelope.\n\n\n1T is from Harry\" he said. gather\nIng his love into his arms. and he\nsays. Bless you. my children\"\n\n=== 22_1889-11-23_p1_sn86063034_0041418201A_1889112301_0193.json\nThe morning news. [volume] (Savannah, Ga.) 1887-1900 · 1889-11-23\ncrops: None\nheadline: WEEKLY REPORT.\nOFFICE MORNING NEWS,\n\n\nSAVANNAH, Nov. y, 1889.\n---\nWEEKLY REPORT.\n\n\nOFFICE MORNING NEWS,\n\n\nSAVANNAH. Nov p. 1889.\n\n\nGENERAL RExARKs-A fair amount Of bust\nness was accomplished in the general market\nlin, the mast week II was. however, quieter\n:, anpearance, there being an absence Of the\n.,-,al hustle and activity so prevalent at this\ntime of the year. Among the wholesale job\nhrs there was considerable Stacking up\n:, orders. The fact of the matter is\n,bat the trade in the interior chooses to work\nfl their present stocks and purchases rather\nhan to have any accumulations, especially as\nif is on the verge of the holiday season, as also\ntafore the usual stock-taking occurs. In other\nlines there was good business going on and\nouite heavy shipping movement was had. The\nweather, for the most part, remains\n-a+ber mild. and does not encourage\nseasonable trade. There were few important\nfeatures in commercial circles worth noting\nexcept that values pretty much all around have\nexhibited much easier tendency. Collections\nwe're good The money market continues re-\nmarkably easier. with considerable amounts\nsaeking investment but the latter is limited\nonly by the scarcity of all desirable securities\nIn the grocery trade there was a steady de-\nmand. but orders were not as full as usual, and\nthe trading was mostly confined TO the heavy\nand staple goods, in which there is always a\nmore or less regular movement. In srovsions\nthere was a reasonably fair demand for this\ntime of the year. but there is a slight scarcity\nnoticeable in some cuts of meats. In dry goods\nthe trade was somewhat slow, buyers having\npreviously been pretty well stocked up. In\nhardware there was good steady movement\nin building materials, the demand was heavy as\nwe'll as in lumber, but the trade is very much\nhammered for lack of railroad facilities to move\nthe stuff from the mills. In all other depart\nments business was very fair to good. The fol-\nlowing resume of the weeks business will show\nthe tone and the latest closing quotations of\nthe different markets to-day:\n\n\nNAVAL SToREs.-The market for spirits tur-\npentine was quiet during last week, while the\ncontinuance of heavy receipts has weakened\nnrces. and regulars sold down to 42c, a clear\ndecline of 116c. There was only moderate\ninquiry, but on the slumps buyers AFFORDED a\nfair amount of stock. Any firmness, however,\non the part of holders caused buyers to be-\ncome quite Indifferent. The total sales\nduring the week were fully $500 casks.\nRosin-T market was quiet during the week.\nand prices were somewhat easier and fell off\n5n1Oc all around There was only a fair in\nauiry, with pretty free offerings The total\nsales were about 5000 barrels. In another\ncolumn will be found weekly comparative\nstatement of the receipts and exports from\nthe beginning of the season to date, and for\nthe same time last year, showing the stocks on\nhand and shipboard not cleared, together with\nthe official closing quotations.\n\n\nCoTToN-The market was somewhat dull dur\ning the past week, while stocks in factors hands\nhave shown some accommodations. Prices have\ndeclined 1s0136c for all grades. There seems to\nbe some dearth of orders among exporters,\nwhich, of course, was expected little later in\nthe season; the lull in the demand, however\nsI,,,ta Il\nOf stock atloat. The local receipts\ncontinue in light volume The total sales for\nthe week were S025 bales. The market closed\ntoday quiet at the following spot quotations\nbased on the standard types, and which were\nrevised on last Monday:\n\n\nMiddling fair Observation-Disclosures.\n\n\n1000 nhiudhhg -.....'..............'.. 1910\nMldullnS Observation-Disclosures. J2S\n\n\nLow middling ................. -. 936\n\n\nGood ordinary Observation-Disclosures. S6\n\n\nSea IsLGpds.-The total receipts for the week\nup to p. m were 2,137 bags, which include\n1911 bags to factors and 400 bags Of through\ncotton. The sales were 1868 bags. The total\nexports were 1855 bags, distributed as fol-\nlows: 300 bags to Liverpool, 1852 bags to\nHavre and $50 bags to northern mills. The\nmarket closed very firm, with the total sales\nlarger than factors receipts for the week.\nSellers were asking an advance of Ac on fine\nForidas over quotations The following prices\nare based on the above sales:\n\n\nNCCdJ CObbOA VI SOOu ottb .HS-4 W0S%12\nGo9d nedlum .....-..............2 20\nMedium fine. ........................2 316 1884\nrlhe :......; -. '.''..'''.' \"a\n\n\nExtra fine and Choice. .2415\n\n\nThe receipts of cotton at this port from all\nsources the past week were 4 ,49f bales of up\nland and 2,137 bales sea island, against 81, 767\nbales of upland and 1852 bales sea island last\nyear.\n\n\nThe particulars of the receipts have been as\nfollows: Per Central railroad, 81, GAG bales up\nland; per Savannah, Florida and Western\nrailway, 9.223 bales upland and 2,137\nbales sea island; per Charleston and Savannah\nrailway, SAG bales upland; per Savannah river\nsteamers, $40 bales upland.\n\n\nThe exports for the week were 60. 202 bales of\nupland and ,53S bales sea island moving as fol-\nlows: To New York, S4S4 bales upland and 1077\nbales sea island; to Baltimore, 1,431 bales up\nland; to Boston ,215 baleSsupland and 161 bales\nto Liverpool, $3.50 bales upland and 800\nbales sea island; to Bremen, 9,73S bales up\nand; to Reval, sol bales upland to Barcelona,\nIo767 bales upland; to Antwerp, $500 bales up\nland; to Hango $3.50 bales upland; to Genoa,\n42g0 bales upland; to Amsterdam, 1850 bales\nupland; to Philadelphia, 119 bales upland.\nInc stock on hand to-day was 90,540 bales\nupland and 1853 bales sea island, against ia 761\nOales upland and 5,644 bales sea island last year.\nRIcE.-The market throughout last week was\ncomparatively dull and prices easier and lower.\nThere was very slow and somewhat indiffer-\nent demand. The offerings were ample for the\nNDuAoud, DuC buyers anu SeleTS were again\napart in their views. and transactions were few\nand far between. The total sales were about\n80 barrels.\n\n\nThe following are the official quotations;\nsmall job lots are held at 1s014c. higher:\nFAR; 900. Observation-Disclosures. - $40\n1oou OBSERVATION-DISCLOSURES.\nL llme. .......... -............41614M\nuuanIy ''..'.-..-.-- .4S4 W5A\ni0tu' .''.-...-.............. .51s06\nRough-Nomlnal-\n\n\nCountry lots son 70\n4ide water. -. .............. GOAL 10\nd III55|.|||fiI|E'||!|I| / !\n\n=== 5_1915-09-01_p7_sn84026749_00294557945_1915090101_0613.json\nThe Washington times. · 1915-09-01\ncrops: None\nheadline: Three-Minute\n\n\nJourney\n---\nCopy't. 1915. by Newspaper Feature Service. J\n- n the Andaman Islands. best\n\n\nknown to the world as the\n: Indian penal settlement main\ntanned by Great Britain, dis\n. covered a strange custom in the\nnortherly island of the Nicobar\ngroup, that may best be termed the\nrrite of the dying.\n\n\n- Walking through the cultivated\nlands that have been wrestcd from\nthe jungle, our party came upon\ngraveyard. Strangely carved head\nstones of wood rose above the\nground ell over the tiny clearing,\nand on the edge nearest the jungle\nstood thatched hut. My first\nthought was that the hut was\n= mortuary chapel, such as we have at\nthe gates of our cemeteries. Inquiry\nrevealed that its purpose was quite\ndifferent.\n\n\nThe Nicobar natives believe dy-\ning person Qcfiles the hut in which he\ndies. The spirit of the dead man Is\nsupposed to linGer around the spot.\nAnd rs the dead are feared equally\nwith evil spirits, in whom all the\n\n\nAndaman Islanders implicitly be-\n\n\nlicVe, even tho wife of the dead man\nwill rush wildly out of the hut where\n\n\nhe dies and never enter it again.\n- To overcome this fearful imaginary\n\n\ndanger to the living the Nicobar\n\n\nnatives erect huts of death beside\ntheir GraVeyards. When a person\n\n\nII about to die he is taken from\n\n\nhis hut and carried to the hut be-\nside the graveyard.\n\n\nThe journey is precisely in the\n\n\nnature of 4 funeral procession. Con-\nGsequcntIy, when the man does not die,\n\n\nbut recovrs, as sometimes happens.\n\n\nhe Is as one who has returned from\n\n\nthe dead. He must undergo many\nceremonies before his defilement is\nspurged and he Is permitted TO\n:.minglc once more with sthe living.\nBut, as the Nicobar islander nights\n\n\ntransportation TO the hut of death\n\n\nWith all his power. the contretemps\n\n\nseldom occurs When n dying man\n. once finds himself in the hut of\ndeath no gives up the fight 10 1--we.\n, Dying he Gazes upon the open grave\n} that Is TO be mis- last resting place.\n\n=== 16_1870-12-04_pna_sn85033699_00200293423_1870120401_0539.json\nNashville union and American. [volume] (Nashville, Tenn.) 1868-1875 · 1870-12-04\ncrops: None\nheadline: CAPT. KIDD's TREASURE.\n\nThe Story of the Discovery of Capt.\nkidd's Hidden Treasure \"a Hoax\nand a SXyindIe.\n---\nThe story of the Discovery of Capt.\n\n\nKidd's Hidden Treasure a Hoax\n\n\nand a SVyindIe.\n\n\nCorrespondence of the Cincinnati Commercial\n\n\nNEW YORK, Nov. 30.-The story of the\nfinding seven thousand dollars of Capt.\nKidd's treasure in the Hudson, turns out\nto have been not only hoax, but a\nswindle, by which three gentlemen were\ndiddled out of two thousand and four hun\nbred dollars. The affidavit made by\nJohn Mearns, and signed by his two fell\nlows, discloses the following facts.\nBriggs is silversmith, and lived on\nEighth avenue, near Sixteth street. He\nsaid he worked in Maiden Lane, but l\ncan't find any one that knows him by\nthat name. l believe he lived by hum\n5ugging people in this or some other way.\nl met him at Spiri'ualist seance in Grand\nstreet, Williamsburg, last winter. We\nare all Spiritualists. He came to me last\nspring, ard SAID he had dream three\ntimes over the same way, and had seen\nand talked with spirits, and they showed\nhim the place where ships wreck was\nlying at the bottom of the river, ntar the\nshore, just below an island on\nthe Hudson River, not quite up to\nWest Point. ThSy said rusty iron chest,\nfull Of gold and silver money and jewelry,\nwas i'll that wreck. All one needed to do\nwas to Try and get it. Then there was\nanother place. Up among the rocks on\nthe mountain side, in cave, were pots\nand boxes of diamonds, jewelry, spoons,\nbullion, and lots Of precious things. He\nwent to an old wench who lives in an al\nley near the Brooklyn Navy Yard, near\nthe Military Park, and we all went there\n\n\n1t was a nasty place. She had dark\nloom, and on a table in it were human\nskull and bones. She burned some drugs\nand had a mumbling TO herself, and then\ntold us where to go and dig. Briggs then\nproposed that we should al put in and\nmake up five hundred dollars, and go 10\nwork, living on that as a general fund\n\n\nWe chartered boat and went and dug.\nTwice the police came upon us, thinking\nwe were river thieves. We worked a good\npart of the summer. Briggs would 90\ndown and see the wench now and then,\nas he said, to get directions, and he would\nask for money to pay for the drugs that\nshe burned in the lamp to make the\ncharm. We kept on giving him money\n\n\nl gave 5000, Vredenburg gave him\nsome 5000, and futile gave him\nnearly 5000. After a while we gave up\nthe other places, and tried to find the\nwreck that was full If treasure. We\nworked about three weeks dragging the\nriver. We dare not do anything by day,\nfor the charm would not work in the\ndaylight, and besides, if it did, other fel-\nlows would find out our secret and get the\nprize perhaps. Briggs was in New York\nmuch # tn time. We had a hut and\nboarded ourselves, hiding our boat by Gay,\nonly going over to Garrison, or Peekskill,\nfor grOc ries. Once we went to Newburg\nand read in the paper about the Hudson\nriver pirates, and knew it was all on our\naccount, so we kept more careful. FL\nnally Briggs said that great piece of\nledge of rock that had fallen from the\nabove concealed the wreck, so we drilled\ninto it in four places. First we tried a\nsmall blast, and finally we put in great\nbig one in the center, with twelve pounds\nof coarse powder. Sunday night week\nago, we fired it, at twelve o'clock. 1t was\nall split apart Briggs rushed down in\nthe water to his waist and hollered, 'ENCw\nwe have got it.\" and we all took hold and\neulled out the wreck, and it was the iron\nchest We broke it open and found\nit full of coin. Briggs said it was all\ngold dOubloons, and give US some bright\nones, the rest were all black and covered\nwith mud, as he said, from age. We heard\nsome one coming, and hurried and put\nup all the coin in bags, then Briggs took\nthe iron chest and sunk it in the river.\n7 hat is the last we have seen of him.\nWhen we got to New York we had the\ncoins examined and found that, except\nthe few pieces he had put in cur hands,\nthe whole collection was a lot of copper\nAmerican and English pennies mixed with\nmud. We believe he has swindled us out\nof twenty-fOur hundred dollars, our hard\nearned savings. The chest fished up\nproves to be condemned iron box, such\nas is used by Express Companies. Even\nthe rust on it seems to be artificial and\nwas probably made by acids. Undoubted-\nly Briggs planned and executed a most\ncomplete swindle. Some of the drugs\nused IN the charm, were found to be cas-.\nserbia bark and alcohol, with ohlorate of\npotash and nitrate acid. He must have\nlowed the rotten old Prov of the wreck\nfrom its muddy bed just below New.\nburg, and then sunk the chest and mock\ntreasure On it just beside the ledge that\nwas blasted. Previous to bis last appear\nance on the spot he asked for more money,\nwhich was refused, as The diggers were\ndiscourag 4. They were all mechanics,\nand declare that if they catch Briggs they\nwill send him to the work of spirits in 8\njiffy. The probability is that he has\nsailed for England, which is his native\nplace Very few traces of the explosion\nare visible where the chest was found.\n\n=== 17_1883-11-17_p1_sn88064005_00200299048_1883111702_0299.json\nThe Meridional. (Abbeville, La.) 1856-1906 · 1883-11-17\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nII parait que Pesplit de Ouiteau\nparcourt, presque chaque mutt, les\ncorridors ct les celluIcs de la pri-\nson de Washington. Tellc est, do\nmoins, l'hailucination dune dou-\naaine de prisonniers, la plupart\ndes femmes de couleur. Un hom'\nme a failli devenir fou par suite de\nces prftendues apparitions. Quand\nlesprit apparait, les femmes pous\nsent de grands eris /; celia a, sem\nble.til, Ia vertu de les faire dis\nparaitre. Les croyances sont fort\nenracinees dams l'esprit des gems\nde conleur, impossible de les er\ntirer.\n\n=== 4_1880-11-05_p3_sn82015483_00271764169_1880110501_0441.json\nMorning journal and courier. · 1880-11-05\ncrops: None\nheadline: LOCAL NEWS.\n\nGhostly GrabberS.\n---\nExtraordinary Case of Burglary a nd R es- titution i'm Connecticut.\n\n strange story of robbery and restitution comes from the town O. Ledyard, on the Thames river, just below Norwich. About the middle of last month burglars entered the country seat of Captain H. M. Randall, 8 prosperous resident of that town, and stole $15. 1000 of United States government bonds and other securities. Captain Randalrs resi- dence is wide, breezy, old fashioned farm house. It is on the Norwich road, and is 8 mile north of the village of Gales Ferry the nearest dwelling is that of John Watson, twenty rods away. Just before the robbery Captain Randall and his wife sailed for New York in the captains large three-masted schooner, the Hettie V. Kelsey. Mrs Ran dall returned to her home a week later, leav- ing her husband in New York. During the absence of the family the house was left in charge of Mrs. Randall's father, an elderly gentleman, and his sister. At no time in the absence of Mrs Randall were the suspicious of the occupants of the house aroused. One day only did they leave the dwelling unoccupied. AS soon as Mrs. Randall had returned she discovered that the large private desk of the captain had been opened and it's contents disturbed She found that several savings bank books, gov ernment bonds, Atlantic Insurance compa- nys scrip, registered in Hartford, and other papers, among which were notes of large amounts. had been taken. Mrs. Randall tel egraphed that payment be stopped on the bonds. Captain Randall was appraised of the robbery by telegraph, and he placed the case in the hands of detective, who set to work with official zeal and sagacity. In other words, he made no progress and no discovery.\n\n A few days ago, on bright autumnal morning, Mrs. Randall was chatting about the robbery with lady friend on the lawn in front of her house. The doors and many of the windows were open. When Mrs. Randall came out of the house no one was left within. Everything inside was in its usual position. A quarter of an hour later she returned to the dwelling. An errand led her to the room in which was the desk of the captain Her astonishment may be imagined at discovering her box containing the missing securities on stand near the desk. She eagerly opened it. and her surprise was re- doubled at finding the securities within and undisturbed. By whom the property was stolen and returned, and in what manner, are still puzzles for the captain and his wife. Superstitious neighbors hint at ghosts. But the ghosts certainly live around there some- where-Hartt0rd Times.\n\n=== 5_1907-02-22_p1_sn83045081_00296025823_1907022201_0469.json\nDer Deutsche correspondent. [volume] (Baltimore, Md.) 1841-1918 · 1907-02-22\ncrops: None\nheadline: Snititia ipridt.\n---\ns.. @e1r &. 50. ,acpinnen eillclt llod)\n\n\nbonn groBeu Seltcr 6errii6renScn\n\n\n8rojeb. - Gic Balti