 9citz0 nltimore-S traBe, mad.\nDie llriafe lit idledte 9eanblung,\ng~corobnng nut Dem GoOe uno bos-\nipiligcs 8erlaiien.\n\n\n\n\n=== 10_1872-11-05_pna_sn84026844_0020219087A_1872110501_1059.json\nThe Wheeling daily intelligencer. [volume] (Wheeling, W. Va.) 1865-1903 · 1872-11-05\ncrops: None\nheadline: A PHANTOM TRAIN.\n\nThe Dead LincOln's Yearly Trip Over\nthe New York Central Railroad.\n---\nA correspondent In the Albany IN. \"\nhreaing Times relates conversat lion with\na sapcmIitious night watchmau on tho\nNew York Central Railroad. Said the\nwa'chman: II believe in spirits and\nghosts. l known such exist. II YOU will\nGame up III April I will convince you\"\nde then told Of the phantorn train that\nevery year comes up the road with the\nbody ol Abraham Lincoln Regularly\n10 the month Of Apr,l. about midnight,\ntue aIr ON the tack becomes very keen\nand eating. On either side Il It iS warm\nand still, Every watchmau when he feels\nthis air steps of the track and sits down\nTO watch. Soon alter the pilot engine,\nwith long black streamers, and o band\nwith Instruments, playing drges, grip-\nning skeletons sitting allbout, will pass\nup nolSelessly, and the very air grows\nblack. lit Is moonlight clouds always\ncome over the moon, and the music seems\nTO liDger, as Il frozen with horror. A\nfew moments after and the phantom\ntrain glides by. Flags and streamerS\nhang about The track ahead seems GOV.\ncred with u black carpet, and the wheels\nare drsped with the same. The coflnS Of\nthe murdered Lincoln Is seen lying on\nthe centre ola car, and oil about It in the\nair and the train behind are vast numbers\nOf bluecoated men, some with coffin on\ntheir backs, others leaning on them. It\nseems then that I'LL the vast armies that\ndied during the war are escorting the\nphantom train ot the President. The wind\ni blowing, dies away at once, and over all\nthe earth solemn hush, almost sitting,\nprevails. ia train were passing, It's noise\nwould be drowned in the silence, and the\nphantom train would ride over ii. Clocks\nand watches always stop. and when look\ncd \" are found to be from Ave to elghts\nminutes behind. Everywhere on;the rad\nabout the 27th, Of April, the ttmeiof\nwatches and trains Is found suddenly bO.\nhind. This, said the leading watchmau,\nwas from the passage Of the phantom\ntrain.\n\n=== 40_1852-04-24_p1_sn82015753_0020219039A_1852042401_0404.json\nNew Orleans daily crescent. [volume] ([New Orleans, La.]) 1851-1866 · 1852-04-24\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nsection de lsacte de Consolidation, approuye lie\n70 Feyrer 1862, ce bureau nommera DIES COM\nmlssaires charges de faire lie recensement DIES\ndiferents Districts ct ACCOMPLISHMENTS de la vile\nde Ia Nile. Orleans.\n\n\n20. III y aura DIES commissaires elus pour cha\nque District\n\n\n30. Les dts commissaires entreront en- fonc-\n.tions dams les dix jours qui suivront leur cleo\nton, ct termincront lie recensement lie Is JUN\nproclaim, ct les retours en- seront fats ayant lie\nTer janvierprochain sur DIES tablcaux prepares\na Get effet.\n\n\n40. Lesdits commissaires -inscriront les mons\nct les demeures DIES votans de chaquc arrondis-\nscment dams leur District respectif\n\n\n90. Lie nombre total DIES residents blancs,\nhommes ct femmes respeetivement, entire | ct 5\nans, . ct l6 ans, II ct 80 aus 81 ct 45 ans 46 ct\n~ ans, 81 ct 20 ans, ainsi qn'unc enumeration\nde la population de couleur ibre de chaque sexc,\nde II ans, de y ans ct au-dessus, enfin de la\npopulation escaye de la meme periodic\n\n\n90. LIE contr6leur sera tenu de fournir les for\nmules en- blanc necessaires\n\n\nNJ Lugenbuhl fait la motion de remettre Ie-\nlection des commlssaires du recensement la\nseance suiyante. Cette motion est adoptee.\n\n\nM. Converse propose Performance suivante oui\nest adoptee apres dispense DIES regles.\n\n\nOrdonnance relative d f.fpocat de Ia PILE.\n\n\nlie Conseil de Ia vile de Ii Nouyelle- Orleans\nordonne ce qui suit\n\n\nConformement la trentc- euatrieme section\ndc l,acte ne consolidation approuve lie % fevrier\nloo2. LIE Consell de VILE elira un ayocat pour la\nvile de la Nouvelle- Orleans, aui sera connu sous\nle nom d Ayocat de la Vile il sera lie conseiller\nlegal du Gonseil ct de tous les officers de ladte\nvile, quand son avis sera necessairc ct il re-\npresentera Ia willie dams Get Fiat, en- CO aui tou-\nche les procedures, les process les actions ct les\nCONVERSATIONS on ladite vile serat interessee. II\nsera nomme pour deux ans, ct receyra un salaire\nannuel de s. II ne recevra nj compensation nj\nqualification, ct dams aucun cas- lon ne nomme-\n&c ne soft par i'm vote des deux tiers DIES mem\nbres presents de chaque bureau du Conseil dc\nVile.\n\n=== 9_1853-02-09_pna_sn85059716_00202193018_1853020901_0270.json\nCooper&#39;s Clarksburg register. [volume] (Clarksburg, Va. [W. Va.]) 1851-1861 · 1853-02-09\ncrops: None\nheadline: HISTORY OF THE JACKSON STA.\n\n\n\n---\n\n\n\n1t was in the year 184G, while the Jack\nson statute committee were looking for an\nartist, that Mr.. Mills chanced to be pas\nsing through Washington City on his way\nto Italy. He had been house plasterer\nin Charleston, South Carolina. He was\ncitizen of that State, having left the State\nof New York, the State of his nativity,\nwhen boy, and settled in South Carolina\nHis first efforts in art were directed to\nmaking plaster busts. He succeeded in\ngiving such admirable likenesses that he\nsoon was much employed. He then re-\nsolved to make a bust of Mr. Calhoun in\nmarble.\n\n\nThe great South Carolinian saw there\nwas genius in the man, and he sat to him.\nMills chose the finest peice of Carolinian\nmarble he could find, and chiscled it into\nshape amid the gibes and sneers of the\npeople. What folly they said, .for this\nplasterer to presume to sculpture marble\nlike the great artists of Italy!' He shut\nhimself up from their gaze-he hid himself\nfrom their sneers in his little shop until he\nhad finished his work. He had never\nseen works of art, the manner in which\nthe sculptor does his work, nor the instru-\n\n\n\n\n\nhe produced one of the finest pieces of\nsculpture in the world in the bust of Mr..\nCalhoun. 1t was the admiration and as\ntonishment of all. The city of Charleston, ),\nas an appreciation of the work, presented\nhim splendid gold medal, and placed the\nbust in the City Hall. Some friends, ad\nmiring his genius, offered the means to send\nhim to Italy; and he was, as we have said,\non his way there. passing through Wash\nington, at the fortunate moment when the\nJackson-statue committee was in search of\nan artist. He had never before been in\n| Washington. But spending a few days in\nthis city, to look at its public buildings and\nits works of art, he became acquainted with\nsome of the committee and with their OBJ\nject. They invited him to give design.\nAt first he declined, never having seen an\nequestrian statue. He was modest-that\nis his character. But the genius of his\nfame disturbed his rest, and woke up the\nstrong native powers of his mind. 1t\nhaunted his Imagination when all the\nworld was asleep, a thousand beautiful\nforms tloated before his waking vision-\nThere was the great jackson who has i'm\npressed the grandeur of his soul and the\nrepublican simplicity of his character on\nthe institutions and mind of his country,\nand on the future of the world. That\n. form passed before the imagination of the\nartist in the character of poor peasant\nboy, of hardy pioneer of the wilderness,\nof IL Judge, of Il senator, of general, and\nas President of the United States; the ar\ntst saw him in all the remarkable scenes of\nhis life. He imbodied them in marble and\n| in bronze. One form he seized-one vi\nsion he arrested and fixed in his mind\n1t was the hero of New Orleans, in all his\ncharacteristic majesty ,intrepidity and firm\nness, mounted on ma jestic war-steed,\npaving the air with ferceness and restless\nimpatience, as if conscious of the coming\nbattle, yet yielding to the iron nerve Of\nhis rider. 1t was the battle of New Or\nleans, when the General was hurriedly\nriding in review of his forces, few mo-\nments before the engagement, which was\nthe form and scene that fastened itself up\non the artists mind. In AN instant he\nworked it into bronze, and it stood before\nhim living thing. His mind was agi-\n| tated he was \"haunted with an idea. -\nBut doubts came. He looked back to his\npast history, and the difficulties before\nhim-a fatal stumbling block to modest\ngenius. The world of art, with its schools,\nits authorities and its rules, stood up to\nrebuke the presumptuous plasterer. 1t\ndid not recognize him; he was out of the\npale of its society; he had never gradua-\nted For moment he felt discouraged.\nBut then he heard a whisper in his ear\nthe Genius of his fame said ''Follow Ma\nture-Genius is superior to Art-Art\nmakes its rules from Nature-such is the\nevidence of history-Nature is as grand,\nas beautiful, as various in America as in\n| Italy or EurOpe-study Nature-folIow\n\n=== 9_1914-12-23_pna_sn84020558_0020219610A_1914122301_0573.json\nArizona republican. [volume] (Phoenix, Ariz.) 1890-1930 · 1914-12-23\ncrops: None\nheadline: DEPosED RECTOR WILL FIGHT\n---\n\n\n\nPHILADELPHIA, Pa, Dec. 22.-\nThat Rev. DIR. George Chalmers Rich\nmOnd, the deposed rector of ST Johns\nchurch, will put up a vigorous fight\nwhen the ecclesiastical court tries him\non January 18th, is predicted by his\nstatements that the Protestant Episco-\npal church takes its cue from Wall\nstreet. The vestry of st. Johns is\nstanding by the deposed rector and\nthere are threats that the church\nwould withdraw from communion with\nthe Protestant Episcopal chuch and\nunite with the reformed Episcopal\nchurch. DR. Richmond is accused by\nBishop Rhinelander of violation of\nhis ordination vows and disregard of\nthe bishops authority. DR. Richmond\nasserts he is being assailed for his\nfreedom of speech in preaching the\ntruth frankly and fearlessly. DR..\nRichmond charges that the bishop\nwas elected by convention controll-\ned by capitalists. and that the Holy\nGhost was not present at that meet\ning\n\n=== 14_1920-05-25_p1_sn86058226_00271745321_1920052501_0051.json\nThe Richmond palladium and sun-telegram. [volume] (Richmond, Ind.) 1907-1939 · 1920-05-25\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nfor the amount due on my accident\npolicy\" he said. Il fell down a long\nflight of stairs the other evening and\nsustained damages that will disable\nme for some time to come\"\n\n\nThe manager gave him a firm look.\n\n\nYoung man.\" he replied, Il have\ninvestigated your case, and find that\nyou are not entitled to anything. 1t\ncould not be called an accident, for\nyou certainly knew that the young\nlady's father was at home\"\n\n\nThey were rafting along the coun-\ntry road in an old-fashioned horse\ndrawn vehicle. Suddenly large\nwhite house doomed up on the right\nof the road.\n\n\nThat house.\" drawled the farmer,\nnodding his head in its direction, lis\nrun by couple of these here spirit\nualists.'\n\n\nDO they take summer boarders?\"\nasked one of the city girls.\n\n\nNope\" said the farmer, only\nOuija boarders. Judge.\n","numLines":1842,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1842}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"00deb47b-1cad-4423-8284-f01226cf8f2c","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"other"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2c-reader","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
