{"parentUuid":"e3e81f65-3b15-4538-b528-3c5b5a5d7136","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"775e54ef-6487-4f79-8598-643421b87718","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GW7DXW71i58ZpboHtW6nzd","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t=== 10_1908-03-30_p14_sn85066387_00175047852_1908033001_0668.json\n2\tThe San Francisco call. · 1908-03-30\n3\tcrops: None\n4\theadline: PURITY OF nAuERs PLAYING\n5\t---\n6\tAbsolute purity of tone, fine dynamic\n7\tshading and q technic equal to any\n8\tthing from q simple GIuck air TO\n9\tBrahms variations on Pagininf theme\n10\twere qualities displayed yesterday\n11\trecital in this city since the days be-\n12\tfore the fire. 1t was not a large audi\n13\tence that greeted him-not nearly as\n14\tlarge as 1t should have been, but it\n15\twas attentive and enthusiastic Bauer's\n16\tgenius does not create a furore; n inn\n17\tpresses by virtue of it's extreme and\n18\tintrinsic worth. He is a sane and\n19\thealthy player, to whom the piano is\n20\ta medium Of Intellectual and poetic ex-\n21\tpreston.\n22\t\n23\t\n24\tHis choice of MacDowells Sonata\n25\tEroica.\" op. so, for his opening num-\n26\tDer. was wise. 1t is really program\n27\tmusic, illustrating broadly the life of\n28\tArthur. but 1t is knit together by skill\n29\tfull theme development and is In its\n30\tessentials sonata Bauer played It\n31\twith great effect. The first move\n32\tment illustrating the coming of\n33\tArthur. was done with nobility and\n34\tbreadth; the second movement a\n35\tscherzo, inspired, it is said, by Dore\n36\tpicture. showing the night in the\n37\twoods, surrounded by elves, was as\n38\tlight as possible. and a realization in\n39\ttone of the spirit OF the Dore picture.\n40\tGuinevere is suggested in the third\n41\tmovement, which Bauer interpreted\n42\twith almost languorous grace. There\n43\twas more evidence Of emotion in this\n44\t\n45\t=== 10_1910-11-30_p14_sn85042462_00175035904_1910113001_0661.json\n46\tLos Angeles herald. · 1910-11-30\n47\tcrops: None\n48\theadline: VISION OF PIC IN\n49\t\n50\t\n51\tDIVORCE ACTION\n52\tSpirituaIiSt Charges That Hubby\n53\tThreatened to Starve Self\n54\t\n55\t\n56\tand Haunt Her\n57\tCAST SPELL, TOO, SHE SAYS\n58\tHusband Admits He Saw Things,\n59\tbut Declares Wife Separat-\n60\t\n61\t\n62\ted Him from $4,000\n63\t\n64\tCAST SPELL, TOO, SHE SAYS\n65\t---\n66\tTwo versions of spiritualistic vision\n67\twere told yesterday in Judge Conrey's\n68\tdepartment of the superior court, where\n69\tMrs. Esther Dye Smith, a magnetic\n70\thealer and spiritualist, obtained a dl-\n71\tvorce from John H. Smith, formerly a\n72\tlecturer and still a member of the same\n73\tcult, whom she charged with cruelty.\n74\t\n75\t\n76\tMrs Smith told \"A long tale of her\n77\thusbands refusing to give her \"a cent\n78\tfor the table,\" though he had more\n79\tthan S10O; of his threatening to starVe\n80\thimself to death and haunt her as long\n81\tas she lived; of his asserting that he\n82\twould least a spell' over her and make\n83\ther uneasy during her life; of his\n84\tchoking her, threatening to break\n85\tthings, pounding the table with ham\n86\tmer and demanding breakfast after re-\n87\tfusing to eat for a week, and otherwise\n88\tfrightening her\n89\t\n90\t\n91\tThen she came to the vision\n92\t\n93\t\n94\tHE told me that he had vision out\n95\tin the backyard, under an apricot tree, .\n96\ttestified Mrs. Smith, an elderly woman\n97\tHe said that he saw big white hog\n98\tand that it was me-\"\n99\t\n100\t\n101\tHE TELLS ABOUT VISION\n102\t\n103\t\n104\tMr.. Smith. when placed on the stand,\n105\trecited another version of the vision.\n106\tHe is a war veteran, of advanced age.\n107\t\n108\t\n109\tMrs. Smith and her friends used to\n110\thave seances at the house.\" he said.\n111\tI'VE saw strange sights and thlngs.\n112\tOnce they asked me to join their circle.\n113\tI answered, Don't disturb me; l am\n114\tseeing things for myself / did not\n115\twant to be annoyed then, because 1 was\n116\tseeing vision. The dark room became\n117\tstill darker. and 1 was enveloped in a\n118\tdark cloud. The cloud dissolved itself\n119\tinto net, through the meshes of\n120\twhich l saw large white object which\n121\tdeveloped into a great hog. It was\n122\tstaring at me. I stared at it. 1t was\n123\tImmediately clean and beautiful to\n124\tlook upon, but it was only hog.\n125\tOnce when | had wrangle with Mrs.\n126\tSmith [ mentioned the vision, but 1 did\n127\tnot say she was the hog\"\n128\t\n129\t\n130\tMr.. Smith told a story of marrying\n131\tMrs. Smith last spring and of her ask\n132\ting him to deed property valued at\n133\t$4,000 to her so often that he did so four\n134\tdays later. After that, he asserted,\n135\tshe drove him from the house. He\n136\twants his realty back. He had pre-\n137\tpared a cross-complaint, alleging cru\n138\tcity, and in which he asked for $50 a\n139\tmonth alimony, but he did not pre-\n140\tsent it.\n141\t\n142\t=== 13_1910-09-26_pna_sn85042373_00212475865_1910092601_0726.json\n143\tThe evening times. [volume] (Grand Forks, N.D.) 1906-1914 · 1910-09-26\n144\tcrops: None\n145\theadline: BIRD IN HIS OLD HAUNTS\n146\t\n147\tSandhfll Crane Holding Forth Near\n148\tMichigan in Goodly Numbers\n149\t\n150\t\n151\tOn Their Southwark journey.\n152\t---\n153\tMichigan Arena: The sandhill\n154\tcrane is back to his old haunts north\n155\tOf Michigan, the prairie in that sec\n156\tton being favorite feeding and\n157\troosting country for the big birds,\n158\tgreat flocks Of which appear each\n159\tfall in migration and frequent that\n160\tsection until the first severe storm of\n161\twinter ushers them southwark to the\n162\tfens of Florida. Formerly they bred\n163\tIn these parts, but the increase of\n164\tsettlement has driven the wary bird\n165\t\n166\t=== 8_1881-01-27_pna_sn84022374_00332895369_1881012701_0018.json\n167\tEllsworth American. [volume] (Ellsworth, Me.) 1855-current · 1881-01-27\n168\tcrops: None\n169\t