his time the cells\n219\twere filled with smoke. and a. the prospect\n220\t% serious ,on1agratou va, not cheering\n221\t10 the prisoners. they gave the alarm and\n222\tSheriff pcv,rcuN. with hi, usual presence\n223\tOf mind. soon eA;Ingu.shcl the fame, With\n224\tou, calling oa, the engines Five minute.\n225\tdella, would have given the FIR, complete\n226\tcontrol Of the roof and made the saving q\n227\tthe binding well nigh impossible\n228\t\n229\t\n230\ton rhur.Ia, ia,, c Ch.,ro.t. E>q\n231\tD-ga,, Collector Of ,h.. port. received 4.\n232\t,,t..-,..,, r,,., \\.u ~. .-.,ry.x-r,.. -. g 4\n233\t\n234\t=== 2_1884-01-09_pna_sn84026089_00514155884_1884010901_0416.json\n235\tThe Lambertville record. [volume] (Lambertville, N.J.) 1872-1968 · 1884-01-09\n236\tcrops: None\n237\theadline: INVESTIGATING a sEANcE.\n238\t---\n239\tSouthern paper contains the following\n240\tIN its New York letter, describing one.\n241\tdollar admittance seance up town.\n242\t\n243\t\n244\t81 was on the committee against fraud,\n245\tand when the door was opened, revealing\n246\tthe medium tied fast in the chair, was\n247\tinvited to see that the knots were secure.\n248\tIn the performance of that duty took oc-\n249\tcasion to look for the concealed organ; the\n250\tchair was common, except that its uphol-\n251\tstored bottom seemed thicker than its gen.\n252\teral style warranted. Ifelt underneath, and\n253\tthere, covered by removable lower cloth,\n254\twas fastened the real medium of musical\n255\tcommunication between the spirits and the\n256\taudience. The alert girl was quick to play\n257\tpart in which, doubtless, she had been\n258\tcarefully instructed.\n259\t\n260\t\n261\t81 What are you doing, sir? she ex-\n262\tclaimed, rising in all the wrath of insulted\n263\tinnocence.\n264\t\n265\t\n266\tEvery spiritualist of that company Of\n267\tsome forty or so believed that l had taken\n268\tsome outrageous liberty with the tender\n269\tcreature. She completed her task expertly\n270\tby bursting into hysterical weeping. The\n271\tfond father came holly to the front. !\n272\tgrabbed up the chair. He slammed it\n273\tdown. Iprotested that wished to investi-\n274\tgate\n275\tYou have disturbed the harmony of\n276\tthe circle he said, depressing his resent\n277\tment with a carefully manifested effort,\n278\tand will cheerfully refund your dollar II\n279\tyou will kindly CO.\n280\t\n281\t\n282\t\"As it is wrong to ruin religious faith,\n283\tdeparted\"\n284\t\n285\t=== 14_1909-05-19_pna_sn95073194_00206537723_1909051901_0469.json\n286\tThe Columbus journal. (Columbus, Neb.) 1874-1911 · 1909-05-19\n287\tcrops: None\n288\theadline: CHAPTER XIX.\n289\tThe Eaole's FLIGHT.\n290\t---\n291\tThe Eagles Flight.\n292\t\n293\t\n294\tNo stranger spectacle was ever out.\n295\tlined against the sky than that Of the\n296\tfate-laden morning, when at height\n297\tof more than three miles above the\n298\tsea the emperor's ships were borne\n299\taway A child full of life. energy and\n300\tvivacity, clutched in the inflexible\n301\ttalons of merciless eagle. and hur-\n302\tried into the empyrean. would have\n303\tbeen no more helpless. The suns rays\n304\twere now painting the surface of a far\n305\treaching, untenanted ocean. on whose\n306\twaves no sail caught the breeze. and\n307\tacross whose depths sailed no squad\n308\trOns. The day of its abandonment\n309\twas at hand. and the time not distant\n310\twhen seafaring was to become merely\n311\tLegendary.\n312\t\n313\t\n314\tIn the profound solitude of the up\n315\tper air the radioplanes swung males\n316\ttically in a wide circle, and then like\n317\tflock of geese in homeward flight\n318\tformed a long line which in stately\n319\tprocession directed its course to the\n320\teast.\n321\t\n322\t\n323\tThe japanese knew by the faces\n324\tstaring at them from the bellies of the\n325\tmonsters which had gathered them in\n326\tthat they were in the grasp of the\n327\tenemy. and impotent, although\n328\tthroughout that trying day no word\n329\twas addressed to them. Once. from\n330\ttheir lofty planes of transit. they saw\n331\tthrough their glasses dim outline on\n332\tthe far southern horizon whose faint\n333\tblue haze held Honolulu, designated\n334\tas stopping place. but which they\n335\twere never to reach--destined to be a\n336\tport for naught but phantom ships-a\n337\tport OF dreams.\n338\t\n339\t\n340\tTo them their progress through the\n341\tair was at terrifying speed. and the\n342\twind OF fight sweeping in a gale\n343\tacross across their decks drove them\n344\tto shelter; but they did not know that\n345\tthe machines above them were work\n346\ting at slow strength, in order that\n347\ttheir coming to the shores of the\n348\tAmerican continent might be unwit-\n349\tmessed and unheralded. The hours\n350\tslipped away, until below them the\n351\tshadows heightened and deepened and\n352\tthe waves were no longer seen. Up\n353\tin their aerial path the dusk was fall\n354\ting. when simultaneously they came to\n355\ta halt and hung motionless in midair.\n356\tOfficers and men hurried to the decks\n357\tto learn what this change might pro-\n358\ttend, and As they did SO they discov-\n359\tcred that the silent monsters were\n360\tclustered in what seemed perilous\n361\tproximity to the craft holding their\n362\tredOubtable flagship, the Ito. On the\n363\tbridge of the latter appeared the ad-\n364\tmiral of the fleet. Kamigawa, his i'm\n365\tpassive face showing nothing to his\n366\tfollowers of the strain under which he\n367\thad labored during those long hours\n368\tof captivity. Like his fellow oflcers.\n369\the looked at the assemblage, anxious\n370\tto know the cause of the abrupt stop,\n371\tand then aloft to the engines of vic\n372\ttory. from each of which there\n373\twhipped and cracked in the evening\n374\tbreeze the stars and stripes.\n375\t\n376\t\n377\tThe grinding noise of metal sliding\n378\tover metal attracted his attention, and\n379\talmost before it had ceased a man\n380\tclad in the blue uniform of the\n381\tUnited States navy leaned out of q\n382\thuge port. holding himself by his hand.\n383\tand browning down at the men upon\n384\tthe ItO's bridge.\n385\t\n386\t\n387\tGood evening, Kamigawa:\" a\n388\tvoice hailed with gruff resonance. and\n389\the recognized Bevins, who had been\n390\tan Instructor in the naval academy of\n391\tthe nation which had presented him\n392\twith postgraduate instruction, and\n393\twhich he had come to assault.\n394\t\n395\t\n396\tGood evening. admiral\" he re-\n397\tsponded in excellent English, mentally\n398\tthankful that he had offered no great\n399\ter resistance to this famous bulldog\n400\tOf the sea; but with no faint of shame\n401\tclouding his mind in thus being re-\n402\tcalled to the fact that he had at\n403\ttempted war upon a country which\n404\thad helped to educate him. of which\n405\the had been a guest of honor, and to\n406\twhich he owed much in knowledge of\n407\tseamanship.\n408\t\n409\t\n410\t1T gives me much pleasure\" re.\n411\tsumed the voice above in a tone of\n412\tcold courtesy. to request that you at\n413\tonce 60 through the formality of sur\n414\trendering your entire feet, and sig\n415\tnal the other vessels from your flag\n416\tship that in behalf of yourself and\n417\tmen you accept parole under the usual\n418\tprovision that neither you nor any\n419\tof your men will bear arms against\n420\tthe United States during the re-\n421\tof this war\"\n422\t\n423\t\n424\tBut l can't do that\" Kamigawa |\n425\tprotested in tone of bitterness.\n426\t\n427\t\n428\tVery we'll\" came the curt answer.\n429\tIl shall at once drop you. and 1 can |\n430\tassure you that nothing will give me\n431\tgreater JOY\"\n432\t\n433\t\n434\tThe blue figure with its tapping |\n435\t\n436\t=== 18_1899-02-26_pna_sn86086481_00517171840_1899022601_0622.json\n437\tThe Worcester spy. [volume] (Worcester, Mass.) 1898-1904 · 1899-02-26\n438\tcrops: None\n439\theadline: \"HAIL COLUMBIA\n440\t\n441\tThe Song That Electrified\n442\t\n443\t\n444\tthe Country.\n445\t---\n446\tNew York Observer.)\n447\t\n448\t\n449\tAn American song that has been re-\n450\tmembered and sung for hundred years\n451\tis too good not to have its centennial\n452\trecognized and honored. Rumors of\n453\twar were rife when the song was writ\n454\tten. War was an actually between the\n455\tUnited States andT SPAN when the\n456\tspring and summer of its hundredth\n457\tyear were passing. A revival of the con-\n458\tditions that gave it birth is a coinci-\n459\tdence worthy of mention. The recent\n460\twar ended without giving birth to a\n461\tsorely needed creation-a new Ameri-\n462\tcan song, a song that will survive not\n463\tmerely campaign of war, but a gen\n464\tGRAFTON.\n465\t\n466\t\n467\tHail, Columbia\" was written in 1898\n468\tby Joseph Hopkinson Q770-1S42) vice\n469\tpresidentof the American Philosophical\n470\tsociety AID president of the Pennsyl-\n471\tNania Academy of Fine Arts, War be-\n472\ttween France and England was then\n473\tthough inevitable. Congress was in ses-\n474\tsion in Philadelphia and celebrating on\n475\tthat subject. The theatre in Philadel-\n476\tphia was open. Gilbert Fox profess\n477\tsional vocalist, was about to receive a\n478\t~ A-NAit\" IL sllli IlppAllsOI NEIG lOl\n479\tlner schoolmates. He called upon Mr..\n480\tHuplinson at No. 1872 Spruce street,\n481\t, Philadelphia, one Saturday afternoon in\n482\tApril, previous to the Monday when the\n483\tbenefit was to be received. He wanted\n484\ta patriotic song adapted to Gen. Wash\n485\t/ lngton's March or THE President S\n486\t! March that had been composed in 1889\n487\tI by German named Feyles in honor of\n488\t/ President Washington and that was\n489\tplayed in 1889, when Washington came\n490\t10 New York to be inaugurated.\n491\t\n492\t\n493\tHopkinson presented Washington,\n494\t\n495\t\n496\twhose praises were sounded in the\n497\t! fourth stanza, with copy of his song.\n498\t\n499\t\n500\tFox believed that the song desired\n501\twould gain for him what up to that date\n502\t\n503\t\n504\twas not assured-a fun house. Hopkin-\n505\tI son promised TO try his gift and art as\n506\t\n507\t\n508\trequested\n509\t\n510\t\n511\t/ He completed the song Sunday after\n512\tnoon and gave it to the singer when he\n513\tcalled. 1t was announced Monday morn\n514\t\n515\t\n516\ting and sung Monday evening, accom-\n517\tI panied by a full band and chorus. 1t\n518\twas encored eight times. the audience\n519\t/ finally Joining in the chorus. 1t found\n520\tfavor with both American parties that\n521\tI sided with England or with France. 1t\n522\ti was sung in Philadelphia by congress\n523\tmen at night on the streets and in all\n524\t\n525\t\n526\tparts Of the land by crowds. 1t cleo\n527\ttrlfied Washington, Hamilton, Jefferson\n528\t+ and McHenly.\n529\t\n530\t\n531\t/ The object Of Hopkinson was to get\n532\t/ up an American spirit that should re\n533\tgard American honor and rights, IL\n534\t( respective Ct the issue between France\n535\tand England. The object seems to have\n536\t\n537\t\n538\tbeen accomplished for biographer OF\n539\t\n540\t\n541\tHopkinson says that this song, melting\n542\t\n543\t\n544\tnational pride, probably helped to avert\n545\t/ enlargement in the European conflict\n546\ti Messrs. Reinagle and Wignall. of the\n547\t\n548\t\n549\tthen new theatre on Chestnut street\n550\t\n551\t\n552\tarranged the music for the song and for\n553\t/ The Presidents March. -\" Both were\n554\t/ produced by Willig, a music dealer on\n555\tSouth Fourth street, Philadelphia. 1t is\n556\t+ not customary for regimental bands to\n557\t/ play during battle but in the civil\n558\twar at Williamsburg, Shiloh and Get\n559\t/ tysburg, they played Hail Columbia\n560\tand Yankee Doodle\" During the CEN\n561\t. tennial exhibition in Philadelphia. 1876\n562\t\n563\t\n564\tan autograph copy was displayed in the\n565\tmuseum at Independence hall.\n566\t\n567\t\n568\tThe Rev r. p. Mason, a Congrega-\n569\ttional chaplain in the civil war, recall\n570\ting IN 1863 the power of vocal and in\n571\tstrumental music over the soldiers\n572\t\n573\t\n574\tsaid: L sometimes thought that the\n575\t\n576\t\n577\tgrand old patriotic peal of Hail Column\n578\t\n579\t\n580\tbia,' and the heart thrilling war SONG\n581\tOF The Star Spangled Banner exer-\n582\tcised a mightier sway than any other\n583\t\n584\t\n585\tsingle cause whatever. Jefferson\n586\t\n587\t\n588\tgave us the Declaration of Indepens\n589\t\n590\t\n591\tdencei Hamilton gave us the Constitu-\n592\tton; Washington gave us his Farewell\n593\t/ Address-was the benediction less when\n594\t/ Hopkinson gave US Hail Columbia? - {\"\n595\tHopkinson was a native of PhiladeI-\n596\t! phla, graduate of the University OF\n597\tPennsylvania, in the class Of 1856, and\n598\t\n599\t\n600\tsubsequently one of its trustees. He\n601\t\n602\t\n603\tbecame an attorney of the highest rank\n604\tthe leading counsel for Dr. BENJAMIN\n605\tRush in 1899, in his suit against VIII\n606\t/ liam Corbett and the defender of Sen\n607\tator Samuel Chase when he was i'm\n608\t\n609\t\n610\tpeached by the United States SEN\n611\t\n612\t\n613\tate. In 1914 he was elected to con\n614\t\n615\t\n616\tgress as a federalist and served one\n617\tterm. President John Quincy Adams\n618\tappointed him United States Judge for\n619\tthe eastern district of Pennsylvania,\n620\t\n621\t\n622\tan office which he held for ia years, or\n623\tuntil his death. Sos ~~t-\n624\t\n625\t\n626\t! The criticism of ''HaiI Columbia\" and\n627\tkindred songs is that they lack literary\n628\t\n629\t\n630\tmerit; that as poems they would not\n631\tT obtain currency and permanency fol-\n632\ttheir tunes. That they are not of high\n633\trank as literary productions is con\n634\tfessed That they owe much of their\n635\t\\ popularity to their tunes is acknowl-\n636\tfedged. But if they were more literary\n637\t\n638\t\n639\tand less emotional they would never\n640\t\n641\t\n642\t~ RCRAAGA\" IiiG very ldCl lIldk I0DN\n643\t\n644\t\n645\tstimulate national sentiment and pride\n646\t\n647\t\n648\ttouch the memory, imagination ano\n649\t\n650\t\n651\theart Of the people is the explanation OF\n652\t\n653\t\n654\ttheir naturalization and perpetuity.\n655\t/ II, however, the object of Hopkinsor\n656\t\n657\t\n658\twas to Great an American spirit, and\n659\this object was realized, there is q\n660\t\n661\t\n662\tgreater need and a more potential re-\n663\t\n664\t\n665\tsuit now. For the spirit needed is that\n666\t\n667\t\n668\tof universal brotherhood. Patriotism is\n669\t\n670\t\n671\tnot the ultimate, the superlative virtue\n672\t\n673\t\n674\t1t is the positive degree. Cosmopoli-\n675\t\n676\t\n677\ttanism is another name for Chris\n678\t\n679\t\n680\ttianity, and when its full fruition APP\n681\t\n682\t\n683\tpears and is disseminated, wars and\n684\t\n685\t\n686\trumors of wars will cease, and Column\n687\t\n688\t\n689\tbia will be happier land than in 1896\n690\tor 1898. The international song is a\n691\t\n692\t\n693\tpotentiality for the mute or the unborn\n694\t\n695\t\n696\tMillions.\n697\t\n698\t\n699\tWANTS OF CONSTITUENTS.\n700\t\n701\t=== 6_1910-07-02_pna_sn82016014_00295870722_1910070201_0026.json\n702\tThe Topeka state journal. [volume] (Topeka, Kansas) 1892-1980 · 1910-07-02\n703\tcrops: None\n704\theadline: \n705\t---\n706\tter a request to his wife to sit for\n707\ther portrait, and to place in the envel-\n708\tope four blank sheets of paper. The\n709\tmediums\" then placed this in a dou-\n710\tBILE slate, over which Mr.. Mattern\n711\tplaced two rubber bands crosswise and\n712\tlengthwise. A rapping noise followed\n713\t\n714\t\n715\tWhen the slate was opened, and Mr..\n716\tMattern cut the envelope he found on\n717\tthe paper a message written with ink\n718\tin what he says was his wife's hand\n719\twriting.\n720\t\n721\t\n722\tMr.. Mattern says he received another\n723\tspirit letter from his wife, except that\n724\tthis time he used his own private en-\n725\tvelopes and stationery. In the second\n726\tmessage, he declares his wife referred\n727\tto a remark made by a neighbor, Mrs.\n728\tWatson, prior to Mrs. Mattern's death.\n729\tOn his return, Mr. Mattern says he\n730\tlearned that the same remark was\n731\tmade by his neigbbor while he was away\n732\tfrom the house.\n733\t\n734\t\n735\tTHE handwriting was like that of\n736\tmy wife when writing fast\" said Mr..\n737\tMattern. And the trouble she refers\n738\tto has come.\n739\t\n740\t\n741\tIl was not a Spiritualist,\" he con-\n742\ttipued, only since getting a likeness\n743\tof my wife; also the letter; it is diff\n744\tcult not to believe in spirits. 1 don't\n745\tknow how the portrait was made, but\n746\tI am satisfied with it so far as the\n747\tlikeness is concerned.\n748\t\n749\t=== 14_1884-03-27_pna_sn94056414_00206537747_1884032701_0235.json\n750\tMcCook weekly tribune. (McCook, Neb.) 188?-1886 · 1884-03-27\n751\tcrops: None\n752\theadline: Guiteau'S Ghost.\n753\t---\n754\tWashington Republican.\n755\t\n756\t\n757\tA weird and remarkable scene &C.\n758\tCured in the rotunda of the jail yes\n759\t\n760\t=== 17_1913-11-05_p1_sn84022374_00332895424_1913110501_0783.json\n761\tEllsworth American. [volume] (Ellsworth, Me.) 1855-current · 1913-11-05\n762\tcrops: None\n763\theadline: The Look That\n764\tHaunts lin\n765\t---\n766\ti've been ubeeged. said sheriff\n767\t\\ HarSer of New Mexico. ITO XIII num-\n768\t4 Der Of men in my day. and they was\n769\tMI Of 'em the all firedest villains you\n770\tEVER seen. Consequently don't lay\n771\tawake nights seein' EN- lookin' at me\n772\tRepresentatives, There wasn't con-\n773\tsarned one of en- that wouldn't a\n774\tkilled me II he'd got the drop on he\n775\tfust. But there was one KILN that I\n776\twon't never git over\"\n777\t\n778\t\n779\t\"a manT'\n780\t\n781\t\n782\tsNc.\"\n783\t\n784\t\n785\tNot a woman or child2\"\n786\t\n787\t\n788\tmot any o that nuther.\"\n789\t\n790\t\n791\tWe'll, then. what was ity'\n792\t\n793\t\n794\t81 never was good hand to begin\n795\tat the wrong end OF yarn. II you're\n796\ta mind to listen I'll tell you about Il.\n797\tbut don't want no interruptions\"\n798\t\n799\t\n800\t\"Go ahead\n801\t\n802\t\n803\twhen | was sheriff OF county,\n804\tArizony. there was good deal goin'\n805\ton In the shape OF crime. with all sorts\n806\tof pussons a-doin' of II. Some OF 'em\n807\twould stay and fight 1t out after a\n808\tKILN or a robbery. and some Of EN-\n809\twould light out. Them as lit cut must\n810\tbe foilered, and. seein' they allus stole\n811\tthe best bosses there was to be had, It\n812\twasn't no easy job TO catch EN-.\n813\twas young Teller then and as fond of\n814\tn good boss as any one Besides. had\n815\tto have the best In ruunin' down\n816\tthieves and cntthroats. Another AMI.\n817\tmai needed was bioodhonnds. had\n818\tthree Of the likeliest dogs you ever\n819\tsaw. but only one favorite. called\n820\ther after Queen Victoria. II ever a\n821\tdog loved a man Vic loved me. onct\n822\twhen I got shot by road agent and\n823\twas laid mp for awhile she stayed\n824\taround the door Of my room lookin'\n825\twistful at every one that came out. as\n826\tmuch as to say. is he Eoin' TO hand In\n827\this chips or nots' And when she'd\n828\tcatch sight OF me lynn on the bed\n829\tshe'd moan dreadful.\n830\t\n831\t\n832\tzone mernin early was Weakened\n833\tand told that a desperate named Jim\n834\tRynders had broke Into the honse Of a\n835\tcitizen and carried away his daughter.\n836\tgal about eighteen years old No\n837\tbody could tell which way he had gone.\n838\tand the only way he could be tracked\n839\twas by hound mounted my horse\n840\tMaple was named for Napoleon\n841\tBonyparte-and. lettin' Vie loose. took\n842\ther TO the house where the outrage\n843\thad been committed She PICKED up\n844\tthe scent right away ard struck of\n845\twestward followed on Nap.\n846\t\n847\t\n848\t\"Vic ran along with her nose to the\n849\tground till she came to creek. where\n850\tshe lost the scent. hut picked IT up on\n851\tthe other side some distance below.\n852\tshown that the rascal bnd been tryiu'\n853\tto throw us Off by waikiki In water.\n854\tWe followed till night. when, comm tn\n855\ta wooded place saw Rynders ahead\n856\tHe had Just come 70 Il halt and was\n857\tpickin' up wood IN make a tire with.\n858\tThe gut was sittin' ON the ground.\n859\tleanin up agin the trunk Of tree\n860\tlookln' like corpse. Vic saw 'em Just\n861\tas soon as did and stopped stock still\n862\tlike a pointer on seem game. put\n863\tmy hand on her to quiet her. for\n864\tthought might get shot at the man\n865\tbefore he knowed ! was near Vie\n866\tdidn't give me away. but Nap did by\n867\tsplutterin'. Rynders turned IKE a\n868\tfash. bnt not before ia sent q bullet\n869\tat him. The gal jumped to her feet\n870\tand RynderS dArted behind her. IL\n871\tcowarily rascal thinkin' Ia prevent my\n872\tKILN him without klil.u her\n873\t\n874\t\n875\tThe way for me- ~ do was to send\n876\tVic at him. and that's rhaf I lid. She\n877\tneared him saw him draw revolver\n878\tto protect himself He fred NJ the dog.\n879\tand saw her 30 ahead on three legs.\n880\tShe got her teeth into his side and held\n881\thim so that the raj could get away\n882\tfrom him. He fired ag'in at Vic. and |\n883\tsaw that he had disabled her. but |\n884\tnow had free shot at him and\n885\tdropped him.\n886\t\n887\t\n888\tIl ran down and tried to quiet the\n889\tgal, who was hysterieai. and partially\n890\tsucceeded. Vie was iyin' still. but I\n891\tknowed she was alive. for she was\n892\tfoIiowin' me around with melancholy\n893\teyes.\n894\t\n895\t\n896\t1.-The dog saved you I said to the\n897\tgal. If II hadn't been for her I couldn't\n898\thave tracked you\n899\t\n900\t\n901\tThis set the young woman TO\n902\tthtnkin' about the dog instead Of her\n903\tself. and she went up to Vic and patted\n904\ther. Fright was turned into gratitude.\n905\t\n906\t\n907\t81 called 70 Vic. but she could not\n908\tcome to me. A shot had struck her\n909\tback and paralyxed her.\n910\t\n911\t\n912\tI'm sorry. miss | said, but the\n913\tdog must be killed\n914\t\n915\t\n916\t\"'Killed. Why sos\n917\t\n918\t\n919\tITO put her out Of misery. She'll\n920\tnever move again\n921\t\n922\t\n923\t''The gal bust ont. cryin'. waited\n924\tfor her to compose herself. then got\n925\ther to go behind n tree while ! did\n926\tthe business. Vic saw me aim at her\n927\tand knowed what | was goin' TO do\n928\tThe look she gave me stays by me 10\n929\t\n930\t=== 47_1901-12-25_pna_sn85040451_00414215142_1901122501_1193.json\n931\tVernon County censor. [volume] (Viroqua, Wis.) 1865-1955 · 1901-12-25\n932\tcrops: None\n933\theadline: TO CURE GOBLIN ONE DAY\n934\t---\n935\tGentility sent to'market will not buy\n936\tq peck of meal.\n937\t\n938\t=== 10_1854-04-22_pna_sn83030213_00206530431_1854042201_0767.json\n939\tNew-York daily tribune. [volume] (New-York [N.Y.]) 1842-1866 · 1854-04-22\n940\tcrops: None\n941\theadline: MaIhe... C.nErtILr RlroK1lp roa rHE I raIag,I\n942\t---\n943\t\n944\t\n945\t\n946\tMarkets. Carefully RfFORTED FOR THKS TRIBvsr\n947\tThe srtt srr bat qui.t TO- Pas IS mess 81\n948\tco1TOx H. so.s -,psr. h .rt. :. yer, fin With an n\n949\tvsres n ofs cent Nondsys quosSs' sales 7ii h.s-sr\n950\tFLOUR AND Oar nsrfs, Tor %s.srs .aa s..s Fsar\n951\t\n952\t\n953\tletter Ito tie oir and better sr.ess they being Ia\n954\tts b ia .hoqld b called Flor\n955\to. IS The ..s Of ss-.Ssa c.n. -. 90 Dbl :. ST 80 III\n956\tfo oT.n u 70 stse sad ~ 81 r. nr-t 70 god\n957\t81 Est7 -tt e.Il-d ,upsrdr. Fo,i 81 in., d.\n958\ts h cpd bsy' ,he s,nad kept wssrs last ,a'unaa\n959\teisIy Of 5000 brsoe. ,.i g %. ~ ct g 81 ss9 IS f,r\n960\tsf45a 81 ~ soI fs. an: q IS p.rsss css ~. ,frn\n961\tsno f.f sne es of 20 bos JsTseJ 80 s2i . IS;\n962\tGPAiNTts se,b buey.l Cor Wheat the milling demand\n963\thqgie i've 70 IT,, lbs 900 beshs.\n96