t be gradual and natural. not volcanic or\n1055\tconvulsive.\n1056\t\n1057\t\n1058\tThe Most wonderful Development Of tho\n1059\tNegro Race.\n1060\t\n1061\t\n1062\tBlind Tom, the negro pianist now perform\n1063\ting at Dodworth Hall, Is certainly one Of tho\n1064\tgreatest musical geniuses of this or any other\n1065\tage. With the utmost ease he plays the most\n1066\tdifficult compositions by ear alone, after hear\n1067\tIng them played but once by some other plan\n1068\tlist. His own compositions are far above the\n1069\taverage. Although his other mental faculties\n1070\tseem to be darkened, his musical sense is 80\n1071\tacute that no can Immediately distinguish and\n1072\tname every note in the most complicated dis\n1073\tcord. To this negro pianoforte prodigy we\n1074\tcall the attention of Henry Ward Beecher,\n1075\tHorace Greeley, Wendell Phillips and the rest\n1076\tOf the abolition leaders. He is very splendid\n1077\tillustration of their arguments in regard to\n1078\tthe intellectual development OF the African\n1079\trace. Born and reared upon a South\n1080\tern plantation, the war has emancipated\n1081\thim that he may ast0nish the world.\n1082\tHow many more such wonders may there\n1083\tbe among the freedmen of the South? We\n1084\thave discovered col0red Gottschalk,\n1085\tnegro List, an Ethiopian Thalberg, why\n1086\tshould we not find mulatto Milton, negro\n1087\tNewton, a slave Sbakspere, freedman Fulton,\n1088\tan emancipated Ericsson, contraband Column\n1089\tbus and darky Dcmosthencs? The aboli-\n1090\tlion editors and parsons ough. 10 be writing\n1091\tand preaching copiously about Blind Tom. he\n1092\tis one of the strongest arguments they could\n1093\tpresent in favor Of negro suffrage.\n1094\t\n1095\t\n1096\tBut there is another point Of view in which\n1097\tBlind Tom is exceedingly interesting. In spite\n1098\tof the verdict against Colchester, at Buffalo,\n1099\t| and the exposure of the Davenport Brothers at\n1100\t| Paris, spiritualism still lives, and our friend\n1101\tJudge Edmonds is its prophet. Let the\n1102\tspiritualists 60 and take look at the negro\n1103\tpianist. His ungainly body and his musical\n1104\tsoul consort together so oddly that il appears\n1105\talmost probable that he is inspired by the\n1106\tspirit of some defunct musician, who has come\n1107\tback from the seventh circle Oh Paradise or\n1108\telsewhere to show this ungodTy gentration the\n1109\tmusic Of the spiritual spheres. Why not claim\n1110\tBlind Tom, then, as the highest order OF\n1111\tmedium yet developed! HIS music is far\n1112\tin advance Of that of the Fox Sisters\n1113\tor the Davenport Brothers. They could\n1114\tonly rattle tambourincs, blow tin trumpe's,\n1115\tring dinner bells and twang phosphorescent\n1116\ti guitars, and they required a dark room and\n1117\tselect circle to accomplish even that. But\n1118\tBlind Tom comes out on an open platform, in\n1119\tthe full glare Of light, before large audience\n1120\tthe larger The better-and produces the finest\n1121\tmelodies upon the best Instrument that man.\n1122\tkind has been able to invent. Observe him\n1123\twhen he is not playing and no one would ever\n1124\tsuspect the tremendous musical genius be pos.\n1125\tsesscs but when he once touches The keys OF\n1126\tThe piano he is like one inspired. Is his in\n1127\tspiration spiritual, or Is it merely musical\n1128\tThat is a question which Judge Edmonds and\n1129\tthe spiritualists must answer. If Blind Tom be\n1130\tnot accepted by the abolltionists as the most\n1131\tmarvellous development Of the negro race, and\n1132\tas striking and convincing proof Of the extra.\n1133\t\n1134\t\n1135\tordinary capacity Of the emancipatcd slaves\n1136\t\n1137\t\n1138\tthen the spiritualists should take him up and\n1139\tclaim him as a first class musical medium. He\n1140\twill afford an excellent text for the abolition\n1141\tparsons, if they take advantage OF his appear\n1142\t\n1143\t\n1144\tance among us but if they miss the COLDER\n1145\t\n1146\t\n1147\topportunity the spiritual professors will not bt\n1148\t\n1149\t\n1150\tso foolish as 10 let this amazing phenomenon\n1151\t\n1152\t\n1153\tpass unnoticed.\n1154\t\n1155\t\n1156\tDodwortb Hall is loo small for thu\n1157\t\n1158\t\n1159\taudiences that crowd in to hear Blind\n1160\t\n1161\t\n1162\tTom. Poor Kingslapd, Of the Academy\n1163\t\n1164\t\n1165\tOf Music, is Boosting that the upper tic\n1166\t\n1167\t\n1168\tof his ediace--the low-priced portion of TIT\n1169\t\n1170\t\n1171\thousc-is now filed every opera evening\n1172\t\n1173\t\n1174\tbut If he would engage Blind Tom he wculc\n1175\t\n1176\t\n1177\tfind this American prodigy Of African dcsccn\n1178\t\n1179\t\n1180\tmore attractive than Italian singers, and th,\n1181\t\n1182\t\n1183\tparquet, the balcony and the boxes OF III,\n1184\t\n1185\t\n1186\tAcademy would then be throDged with fash\n1187\t\n1188\t\n1189\tionable people. Poor Stuart, OF The Winter\n1190\t\n1191\t\n1192\tGarden, might probably withdraw Our Amcri\n1193\t\n1194\t\n1195\tcan Cousin and bring ont our African cousin\n1196\t\n1197\t\n1198\tBlind Tom. The Joyce Heth in brooches o\n1199\t\n1200\t\n1201\tthe Muscum-whose fat woman is becoming a.\n1202\t\n1203\t\n1204\tthin as ths living skeleton q account Of thu\n1205\t\n1206\t\n1207\tbad busiDessa-m'gbt have The credit Of exhibit\n1208\t\n1209\t\n1210\ting one genuine curiosity If he could only\n1211\t\n1212\t\n1213\tsecure Blind Tom. Why should poor Wallacl\n1214\t\n1215\t\n1216\tcontinue TO persecute the public with stupid\n1217\t\n1218\t\n1219\tRussian Serf, when here Is an astonishing\n1220\t\n1221\t\n1222\tAmerican serf. just released from slavery and\n1223\t\n1224\t\n1225\tsure to draw thousands TO the theatre? Indeed\n1226\t\n1227\t\n1228\twe may with equal force recommend this\n1229\t\n1230\t\n1231\tmusical wonder TO all the theatrical mana\n1232\t\n1233\t\n1234\tgers. Except dandy \"sam,\" at the Broadway\n1235\t\n1236\t\n1237\tBlind Tom is the 