t was very seldom that we bungled a\n1420\tjob. We used to begin at the foot OF\n1421\tthe ladder, the rope ladder, II I may\n1422\tsay so, in my younger days, and then\n1423\twork our way up, step by step, so that\n1424\twe were what you might call good\n1425\tmen all through\"\n1426\t\n1427\t\n1428\tII see,\" said l.\n1429\t\n1430\t\n1431\tII was always reckoned hard-worl'\n1432\ting, conscientious man, and had talent,\n1433\ttoo-the very cleverest Of them allowed\n1434\tthat. l began as a blacksmith, and\n1435\tthen did little engineering and car\n1436\tpentering, and then / took to sleight.\n1437\tof-hand tricks, and then to picking\n1438\tpockets. | remember, when was home\n1439\ton visit, how my poor old father used\n1440\tto wonder why | was always lowering\n1441\taround him. lie little knew that\n1442\tused to clear everything out Of hi,\n1443\tpockets dozen times a day, and ther\n1444\treplace them, just to keep my hand in\n1445\tHe believes to this day that | am in ar\n1446\tOffice in the city. There are few o.\n1447\tthem could touch me in that particular\n1448\tline Of business, though\"\n1449\t\n1450\t\n1451\t81 suppose 1t is matter Of practice?'\n1452\tj remarked.\n1453\t\n1454\t\n1455\tITO great extent. Still, a mar\n1456\tnever quite loses it, if he has once beer\n1457\tan adept-excuse me; you have dropped\n1458\tsome cigar ash on your coat\" and hi\n1459\twaved his hand politely in front of my\n1460\tbreast, as if to brush 1t off. There,\n1461\the said, handing me my gold scarf pin\n1462\tyou see | have not forgot my old Gun\n1463\tming yet\"\n1464\t\n1465\t\n1466\tHe had done it SO quickly that\n1467\thardly saw the hand whisk over my\n1468\tbosom, nor did l feel his fingers touch\n1469\tme, and yet there was the pin glitter\n1470\ting in his hand. ut is wonderful\"\n1471\tsaid as l fixed it again in its place.\n1472\t\n1473\t\n1474\t\"0h, that's nothingl But l have\n1475\tbeen IN some really smart jobs. was\n1476\tIN the gang that picked the new patent\n1477\tsafe. You remember the case. 1t wa.\n1478\tguaranteed to resist anything; and we\n1479\tmanaged to open the first that wa\n1480\tever issued, within a week Of its ap\n1481\tpearance. twas done with graduated\n1482\twedges, sir, the first so small that you\n1483\tcould hardly see 1t against the light\n1484\tand the last strong enough to prize Il\n1485\topen. 1t was clever managed affair.\n1486\t\n1487\t\n1488\t81 remember it\" said L But surely\n1489\tsome one was convicted for that9\"\n1490\t\n1491\t\n1492\tEyes, one was nabbed. But he didn'\n1493\tsplit, nor even let on how it was done\n1494\tWe'd have cut his soul out If\" 81,\n1495\tsuddenly damped down the very ugll\n1496\tfires which were peeping from his eyes\n1497\tperhaps I am boring you, TALKING\n1498\tabout these old wicked days Of mine?\n1499\t\n1500\t\n1501\tION the contrary,\"I said, \"you in\n1502\tterest me extremely\" .I..\n1503\t\"I lika IN rot q lietanar ann t,-,,et\n1504\t\n1505\t=== 1_1903-02-28_pna_sn82015679_00296029750_1903022801_0331.json\n1506\tThe Indianapolis journal. [volume] (Indianapolis [Ind.]) 1867-1904 · 1903-02-28\n1507\tcrops: None\n1508\theadline: THEY CALL 1T spOoK NIGHT.\n1509\t\n1510\tManner fin Which the Capital Guards\n1511\t\n1512\t\n1513\tScare Each other.\n1514\t---\n1515\tTonight (Feb. 23) will be 'spook night \"\n1516\t\n1517\t\n1518\tremarked one of the Capitol guards to-day.\n1519\t\n1520\t\n1521\tand there's hardly one of the men on\n1522\t\n1523\t\n1524\tnight watch duty that doesn't hate TO see IL\n1525\t\n1526\t\n1527\tcome.\n1528\t\n1529\t\n1530\tEven the coolest of the watchmen breads\n1531\t\n1532\t\n1533\tTINS night, because every year at just mid\n1534\t\n1535\t\n1536\tnight of the I'D the ghost of John Quincy\n1537\t\n1538\t\n1539\tAdams has been seen in the corridors of the\n1540\t\n1541\t\n1542\tCapitol.\n1543\t\n1544\t\n1545\t1T was on this day in 1SiS that the great\n1546\tAdams died in the chamber OF the House or\n1547\t\n1548\t\n1549\tRepresentatives\" continued the guard. \"A\n1550\tbrass tablet marks the spot where he fell.\n1551\t\n1552\t\n1553\tOn the 23d of every February since a pale.\n1554\tphosphorescent glow is seen to hover over\n1555\t\n1556\t\n1557\tthe tablet. Those who have been brave\n1558\tenough to stay have related that this glow\n1559\tslowly merges into the majestic figure of\n1560\tthe statesman. The face has an expression\n1561\tof sorrow that seems to be increased as the\n1562\t\n1563\t\n1564\torator goes through the motion of making\n1565\t\n1566\t\n1567\tan address.\n1568\t\n1569\t\n1570\t'It is wordless speech. and the sight of\n1571\tthe noble form of the statesman in the pale\n1572\tglow, making gestures as If addressing his\n1573\tcolleagues Of a former day upon the floor\n1574\tof the Senate. has caused even the strong\n1575\test nerves to tremble.\n1576\tAfter a few moments the light fades\n1577\tand the sound of heavy footsteps is heard\n1578\tgoing slowly down the vacant corridors.\n1579\tIl tell you, even the bravest of us hates to\n1580\tsee the I'D come round again.\n1581\tYes, there are other ghostly visitants to\n1582\tthe echoing halls of the Capitol, but none\n1583\tso remarkable the shade who comes to-\n1584\tnight. The entire Congress of 1s4S appears\n1585\t| every once in while in the hall of statuary\n1586\tand carries on an energetic session in dumb\n1587\t| show.\n1588\t\n1589\t\n1590\tSometimes the hurried wrath of Vice\n1591\tPresident Wilson, who died in his room in\n1592\t\n1593\t\n1594\tthe Senate end of the Capitol, is seen to go\n1595\t! quickly through some empty hall. His face\n1596\t| is always troubled, and the spirit seems to\n1597\tbe hurrying upon some errand of impor-\n1598\t| lance. Whenever the body of dead legis-\n1599\t| lator is lying in state in the Capitol the Vice\n1600\t| Presidents shade enters and makes a visit\n1601\t| to the bier.\n1602\t\n1603\t\n1604\tThe ghost of General John A. Logan is\n1605\t| also often seen by Capitol guards lowering\n1606\t| about the room Of the committee on mill\n1607\t| tary affairs. IL is a large and commanding\n1608\t| presence, that stalks in dignity into the\n1609\t| waiting shadow.\n1610\t\n1611\t\n1612\t| But, as / said before, when the new night\n1613\t| guard has to be really initiated, we send\n1614\t| him alone, at midnight, Feb. g. past the\n1615\t| brass tablet that is sacred to the memory of\n1616\t| John Quincy Adams\n1617\t\n1618\t=== 7_1906-10-14_p46_sn84026749_100492647_1906101401_0653.json\n1619\tThe Washington times. · 1906-10-14\n1620\tcrops: None\n1621\theadline: Ghost Taxes Visible Form.\n1622\t---\n1623\tMiss Sadie glanced Up, and. 70 her\n1624\thorror. saw the ghost for the hrst tina\n1625\thead poked In at the door. and .\n1626\tform Ike that Of a man. but white.\n1627\tas II dressed fo- the tropics, stepped\n1628\tboldly into the room. Miss Sadie was\n1629\tstartled, but she had presence Of mind\n1630\tenough 10 rise 10 welcome the IN\n1631\tJruder. letting the darning drop on the\n1632\tnoor. But before she got halfway to\n1633\ther feet there was a disappearance that\n1634\tbetrayed the unearthIy character OF hsr\n1635\tvisitor. The man-head ard all-Eons\n1636\tThe form did not 90 back 20 the door\n1637\tand then drop out Of sight, but. rather,\n1638\twas instantly dissipated Just whess II\n1639\tstood.\n1640\t\n1641\t\n1642\tFor months the Misses Taft kept\n1643\ttheir secret. not mentioning. except 20\n1644\tthe priests at St. EIizabeth's, and 20\n1645\tsome dear and trusty friends. the\n1646\tmystic lappenirgs IN their apartment.\n1647\tBut anaIly the ghost became too\n1648\ttroublesome 10 be longer tolerated.\n1649\tTie story was told 20 doctor, who\n1650\tgot one OF the women from St Eliza\n1651\tbeth's and a boy TO remain IN the\n1652\trooms with him all night They heard\n1653\tthe creeks. the Kissing, the rumbles.\n1654\tbut saw no ghost later the other\n1655\tfriends Of the girls were Informed,\n1656\tand in turn braved the CHESTS. sems\n1657\tOf these we're more fortunate, and By\n1658\tthis time az least a dozen persons are\n1659\tready 10 testify 20 seeing tbs strange\n1660\tmoving and disappearing figures that\n1661\thave created such a ue.ghborhood *\n1662\telement.\n1663\t\n1664\t\n1665\tLately the TAFT sisters have been q\n1666\thit unnerved. Several times. when the\n1667\tthumpings and groaaings were mors\n1668\tthan ordinarily disturbing the twO\n1669\tyoung women rose together IN the\n1670\tnight. AMI at o'clock in The morn\n1671\ting ned the house. They went usual\n1672\t\" TO the home OF their washerwoman.\n1673\tin Juniper street, not far awav.\n1674\t\n1675\t\n1676\tSuch Is the story Of PhnadeIphia's\n1677\tlatest ghost. Il IS causing consider\n1678\table excitement IA the neighhorhood Of\n1679\t2025 South Broad street. and consider\n1680\tbIe indignation in the breasts OF Mr..\n1681\tand Mrs David Marsh. the Iesseez OF\n1682\tthe building. It is driving the Taft\n1683\tsisters 20 other quarters. and viii\n1684\tdoubtless make tenants a bit shy OF\n1685\tthe third floor rooms they now occupy.\n1686\tBut whether one believes IN ghosts or\n1687\tnot-and OF course one doesn't-if one\n1688\tvera going 10 meet one, one would\n1689\tprefer 70 have Il Al ''out-and-outer'\n1690\tIke him OF the hacked head\n1691\t\n1692\t=== 12_1912-10-25_pna_sn88085187_00211108344_1912102501_0180.json\n1693\tThe Tacoma times. [volume] (Tacoma, Wash.) 1903-1949 · 1912-10-25\n1694\tcrops: None\n1695\theadline: BEAUTIFUL DOE NvADES MANS HAUNTS DIES nMpALED\n1696\t\n1697\t\n1698\tON FENCE WITHIN MILES OF GREAT METROPOI.IS\n1699\t---\n1700\tA fleet doe deer. at home on\n1701\twooded hills, ventured into the\n1702\thaunts of men and died. impaled\n1703\tthrough the heart on an iron\n1704\tpicket fence.\n1705\t\n1706\t\n1707\tThis happened within 80 miles\n1708\tof New York city, the metropo-\n1709\tlis OF America, ON the outskirts\n1710\tOF Noroton, Conn.\n1711\t\n1712\t\n1713\tHere Ia her first and only pho\n1714\ttograph, taken at the end OF her\n1715\tbrief and fatal holiday In civili-\n1716\tzation.\n1717\t\n1718\t\n1719\tOnce the beauty Of the forest\n1720\tnow the carcass at barbecue.\n1721\t\n1722\t\n1723\tMany deer thrive in New Eng\n1724\tland, protected by the game laws.\n1725\tAnd this doe had lived and\n1726\tthrived in the shadows of kindly\n1727\tbushes and low-branched trees,\n1728\tsince Noroton hunters first sight\n1729\ted her, a young, graceful fawn.\n1730\t\n1731\t=== 6_1876-05-14_p1_sn88064616_00295878605_1876051401_0069.json\n1732\tThe New Orleans Democrat. (New Orleans, La.) 1875-1876 · 1876-05-14\n1733\tcrops: None\n1734\theadline: CHINESE GHOSTS.\n1735\t\n1736\t\n1737\t\n1738\t\n1739\t---\n1740\tMatcria1tzed Animals.\n1741\t\n1742\t\n1743\tFrom the Hong Kong tChina) Rcviewl\n1744\t\n1745\t\n1746\tOne popularity about Chinese ghosts\n1747\tIL that they do not always rnaterialize,\n1748\tbut are frequently seen in incomplete\n1749\tform, the herd first becoming visible,\n1750\tthen the feet, then the body. and SO on.\n1751\tAnother quaint belief is that ghosts\n1752\tmi mo ha-pa you've no chin is\n1753\tequivalent to saying you're u ghost. :\"\n1754\tLike English ghosts, they appear dur\n1755\ting the hours Of darkness, and are COM\n1756\tPEELED to vanish at cock-crow but the\n1757\tconventional white clothing Is absent\n1758\tfrom the Chinese idea. A ghost In China\n1759\tALWAYS appears In the dress he common\n1760\tIy wore during life, and his advent Is\n1761\tannounced by candles burning not blue\n1762\tbut green the Greeks call supernatural\n1763\tterror green fear.\n1764\t\n1765\t\n1766\tAnimals also have their ghosts. A\n1767\tresident at Canton, named Ling, had q\n1768\tvery intelligent monkey, which having\n1769\tbeen severely whipped for some mis-.\n1770\tchief. became sulky. refused food, and\n1771\tdied. Shortly after the monkeys ghost\n1772\tbegan to haunt the house. Food placed\n1773\ton the tables mysteriously Disappeared,\n1774\tand strange annoyances took pale. At\n1775\tlength fire broke out in the house I'M\n1776\taccountably, and Mr.. Ling shifted his\n1777\tresidence. But the monkeys ghost\n1778\tstill followed and prosecuted him, until,\n1779\tas a last resource, he took a room in the\n1780\tTemple OF the Five Hundred Worthless !,\n1781\tthe monkey ghost did not dare to face\n1782\tthe gods, and left him in peace. Mr..\n1783\tLing was still resi- ling In the Temple\n1784\tlast year.\n1785\t\n1786\t\n1787\tGhost stories OF the ordinary kind are\n1788\tnumerous. A mandarin met his death\n1789\tIn the late collision between the Chinese\n1790\tsteamer Fusing and the British steamer\n1791\tOcean. The Fusing was sunk and over\n1792\tsixty drowned. The mandarin's ghost\n1793\tappeared to his wife, who was living In\n1794\tSoochow, streaming with water from\n1795\thead to foot. He told her he had been\n1796\tdrowned, and that he had sent by q Ger\n1797\ttain friend some money for her use be.\n1798\tfore he took passage In the Fusing, and\n1799\tthat the friend would arrive shortly:\n1800\tTwo days after the friend named arrived\n1801\twith the money, and SHORTLY after came\n1802\tthe intelligence OF the Fusing's disaster.\n1803\tThe commander Of Chinese man OF.\n1804\twar belonging to the Foochow Arsenal !\n1805\tfleet, was suddenly taken i'll, and died\n1806\tut a trends house. After his death,\n1807\tthe friend frequently saw his ghost, and\n1808\tono night addressed il, saying: You\n1809\tshould not lament: 1t was your fate ;\n1810\tYOU should be satisfied with it There\n1811\tupon the ghost disappeared, and never\n1812\tshowed itself again to the same party,\n1813\t| but continued often to appear To the\n1814\tmen on board the ship he had been\n1815\tcommanding, being frequently seen to\n1816\tpace up and down the deck as he was\n1817\twent during his lifetime at night, and\n1818\t| would sometimes place himself in the\n1819\tattitude OF drilling the men. =,\n1820\t| Here is parallel to the celebrated\n1821\tBerkely Square story. Thrust be noted\n1822\t1 that the ghosts of suicides are distin-\n1823\tguished 6v wearing red silk handker-\n1824\tchiefs. ''Outside the north gate OF\n1825\tHang chow there was a house haunted\n1826\tnv evil ghosts, where no human being\n1827\tdared reside, OF which the doors were\n1828\tever barred and locked. A scholar\n1829\tnamed Ts'ai bought the house; people\n1830\tall told him he was doing dangerous\n1831\tthing, but he did not heed them. None\n1832\tOF his family would enter the house, so\n1833\tTs'ai went by himself. opened the\n1834\tdoors, lit candle and sat down. In the\n1835\tmiddle OF the night a woman slowly\n1836\tapproached, with d red silk handkerchief\n1837\thanging to her neck, and having salut.\n1838\ted him, fastened a rope to the beam OF\n1839\tthe ceiling, and put her neck In the\n1840\tmoose. Ts'ai did not In the least change\n1841\tcountenance. The woman again fast\n1842\tened u rope and called on Ts'ai to do rs\n1843\tshe had done, but he only lifted his leg\n1844\tand put his foot In the moose. The\n1845\twoman said, You're wrong. Ts'al\n1846\tlaughed and answered, On the contrary\n1847\t1t was YOU who were wrong u long time\n1848\tago, or else You would not have come &c\n1849\tthis pass. The ghost cried bitterly,\n1850\tand having bowed to Ts'ai, departed\n1851\taud from that time the house was nc\n1852\tlonger haunted. This Tsai was after\n1853\t\n1854\t\n1855\tward a very distinguished scholar\n1856\tknown as the Provincial Chancellor.\n1857\t\n1858\t\n1859\tThe writer remembers having read\n1860\tsome time similar curfons story OF 9\n1861\t\n1862\t\n1863\tpolice cell, in which woman had com\n1864\t\n1865\t\n1866\tmitted suicide by hanging herself, after\n1867\t\n1868\t\n1869\twhich inmate after inmate continued &c\n1870\tattempt to do the same, Till at last a CIR\n1871\t\n1872\t\n1873\tdeclared that fiendish little woman\n1874\tappeared in the cell at night and com\n1875\t\n1876\t\n1877\tmanded her to hang herself, which she\n1878\t\n1879\t\n1880\tfelt herself irresistibly impelled to at\n1881\t\n1882\t\n1883\ttempt doing. The cell, it is said, hac\n1884\t~ 4 Il h, II\n1885\t"}]},"uuid":"0689f739-0702-4974-a169-aa8ede1e1720","timestamp":"2026-10-31T08:21:01.063Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/c1/d04.txt","content":"=== 10_1908-03-30_p14_sn85066387_00175047852_1908033001_0668.json\nThe San Francisco call. · 1908-03-30\ncrops: None\nheadline: PURITY OF nAuERs PLAYING\n---\nAbsolute purity of tone, fine dynamic\nshading and q technic equal to any\nthing from q simple GIuck air TO\nBrahms variations on Pagininf theme\nwere qualities displayed yesterday\nrecital in this city since the days be-\nfore the fire. 1t was not a large audi\nence that greeted him-not nearly as\nlarge as 1t should have been, but it\nwas attentive and enthusiastic Bauer's\ngenius does not create a furore; n inn\npresses by virtue of it's extreme and\nintrinsic worth. He is a sane and\nhealthy player, to whom the piano is\na medium Of Intellectual and poetic ex-\npreston.\n\n\nHis choice of MacDowells Sonata\nEroica.\" op. so, for his opening num-\nDer. was wise. 1t is really program\nmusic, illustrating broadly the life of\nArthur. but 1t is knit together by skill\nfull theme development and is In its\nessentials sonata Bauer played It\nwith great effect. The first move\nment illustrating the coming of\nArthur. was done with nobility and\nbreadth; the second movement a\nscherzo, inspired, it is said, by Dore\npicture. showing the night in the\nwoods, surrounded by elves, was as\nlight as possible. and a realization in\ntone of the spirit OF the Dore picture.\nGuinevere is suggested in the third\nmovement, which Bauer interpreted\nwith almost languorous grace. There\nwas more evidence Of emotion in this\n\n=== 10_1910-11-30_p14_sn85042462_00175035904_1910113001_0661.json\nLos Angeles herald. · 1910-11-30\ncrops: None\nheadline: VISION OF PIC IN\n\n\nDIVORCE ACTION\nSpirituaIiSt Charges That Hubby\nThreatened to Starve Self\n\n\nand Haunt Her\nCAST SPELL, TOO, SHE SAYS\nHusband Admits He Saw Things,\nbut Declares Wife Separat-\n\n\ned Him from $4,000\n\nCAST SPELL, TOO, SHE SAYS\n---\nTwo versions of spiritualistic vision\nwere told yesterday in Judge Conrey's\ndepartment of the superior court, where\nMrs. Esther Dye Smith, a magnetic\nhealer and spiritualist, obtained a dl-\nvorce from John H. Smith, formerly a\nlecturer and still a member of the same\ncult, whom she charged with cruelty.\n\n\nMrs Smith told \"A long tale of her\nhusbands refusing to give her \"a cent\nfor the table,\" though he had more\nthan S10O; of his threatening to starVe\nhimself to death and haunt her as long\nas she lived; of his asserting that he\nwould least a spell' over her and make\nher uneasy during her life; of his\nchoking her, threatening to break\nthings, pounding the table with ham\nmer and demanding breakfast after re-\nfusing to eat for a week, and otherwise\nfrightening her\n\n\nThen she came to the vision\n\n\nHE told me that he had vision out\nin the backyard, under an apricot tree, .\ntestified Mrs. Smith, an elderly woman\nHe said that he saw big white hog\nand that it was me-\"\n\n\nHE TELLS ABOUT VISION\n\n\nMr.. Smith. when placed on the stand,\nrecited another version of the vision.\nHe is a war veteran, of advanced age.\n\n\nMrs. Smith and her friends used to\nhave seances at the house.\" he said.\nI'VE saw strange sights and thlngs.\nOnce they asked me to join their circle.\nI answered, Don't disturb me; l am\nseeing things for myself / did not\nwant to be annoyed then, because 1 was\nseeing vision. The dark room became\nstill darker. and 1 was enveloped in a\ndark cloud. The cloud dissolved itself\ninto net, through the meshes of\nwhich l saw large white object which\ndeveloped into a great hog. It was\nstaring at me. I stared at it. 1t was\nImmediately clean and beautiful to\nlook upon, but it was only hog.\nOnce when | had wrangle with Mrs.\nSmith [ mentioned the vision, but 1 did\nnot say she was the hog\"\n\n\nMr.. Smith told a story of marrying\nMrs. Smith last spring and of her ask\ning him to deed property valued at\n$4,000 to her so often that he did so four\ndays later. After that, he asserted,\nshe drove him from the house. He\nwants his realty back. He had pre-\npared a cross-complaint, alleging cru\ncity, and in which he asked for $50 a\nmonth alimony, but he did not pre-\nsent it.\n\n=== 13_1910-09-26_pna_sn85042373_00212475865_1910092601_0726.json\nThe evening times. [volume] (Grand Forks, N.D.) 1906-1914 · 1910-09-26\ncrops: None\nheadline: BIRD IN HIS OLD HAUNTS\n\nSandhfll Crane Holding Forth Near\nMichigan in Goodly Numbers\n\n\nOn Their Southwark journey.\n---\nMichigan Arena: The sandhill\ncrane is back to his old haunts north\nOf Michigan, the prairie in that sec\nton being favorite feeding and\nroosting country for the big birds,\ngreat flocks Of which appear each\nfall in migration and frequent that\nsection until the first severe storm of\nwinter ushers them southwark to the\nfens of Florida. Formerly they bred\nIn these parts, but the increase of\nsettlement has driven the wary bird\n\n=== 8_1881-01-27_pna_sn84022374_00332895369_1881012701_0018.json\nEllsworth American. [volume] (Ellsworth, Me.) 1855-current · 1881-01-27\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nThe Savage Bros. of Bangor, have\ntaken the Geo \" Black store. on State St.\nand are repairing it preparatory to putting\nin large stock Of groceries and provisions.\n\n\nThe Boston Comedy Company opened\nin Hancock Hall. last evening. to good\nhouse. The performance gave general salts\nfaction. the characters being well repre-\nscented, especially that ofthe Gypsy Queen, 50\nby Miss GRAY. The orchestra gave some\nfine selections between the acts. Tonight\nthe great drama of Not Guilty\" and the\nfarce Of The Ghost\" will be the attraction.\nand ought to draw a crowded house.\n\n\nThe Bangor Commercial reports that on\nFRIDAY last Mr. Dana Kent. Of Ellsworth\nwhile At work hauling logs on Township No.\n,. for Jerr, Giles y Co. Of Amherst, had\nhi. leg crushed in terrible manner at the\nknee Nj Kent was unloading logs from\nhis sled when his leg was caught between\ntwo logs lie remained in thi, condition an\nAodI AuA quarter Nfl'it H'4P o'tt''N Az\nrelieve him from his painful situation. Mr..\nKent va- taken to Oldtown. FRIDAY evening.\na distance Of 81 miles DIR. Bradbury was\ncalled 10 set the bones and found a com\npound fracture Of the tibia and fibula. The\nYoung man j. being cared for a, the Goldman\nHouse and ! a. comfortable a. could be e1\npectcd.\n\n\nA desperate attempt al escape was made\nby the prisoners in the the jail about 3\nO'Clock TIN. morning Three Of them built\nfire IN the window. or stone port hole, from\nfuel ,hcy had seorcted, hoping to break the\nstone by TIE heat. remove the iron bars and\nmake their escape Unfortunately for them.\na wooden spout, extending from the cave. to\npoint near the window took FIRE, aud IN\na frv minutes vas blazing 10 the caves and\nfiring the roof About this time the cells\nwere filled with smoke. and a. the prospect\n% serious ,on1agratou va, not cheering\n10 the prisoners. they gave the alarm and\nSheriff pcv,rcuN. with hi, usual presence\nOf mind. soon eA;Ingu.shcl the fame, With\nou, calling oa, the engines Five minute.\ndella, would have given the FIR, complete\ncontrol Of the roof and made the saving q\nthe binding well nigh impossible\n\n\non rhur.Ia, ia,, c Ch.,ro.t. E>q\nD-ga,, Collector Of ,h.. port. received 4.\n,,t..-,..,, r,,., \\.u ~. .-.,ry.x-r,.. -. g 4\n\n=== 2_1884-01-09_pna_sn84026089_00514155884_1884010901_0416.json\nThe Lambertville record. [volume] (Lambertville, N.J.) 1872-1968 · 1884-01-09\ncrops: None\nheadline: INVESTIGATING a sEANcE.\n---\nSouthern paper contains the following\nIN its New York letter, describing one.\ndollar admittance seance up town.\n\n\n81 was on the committee against fraud,\nand when the door was opened, revealing\nthe medium tied fast in the chair, was\ninvited to see that the knots were secure.\nIn the performance of that duty took oc-\ncasion to look for the concealed organ; the\nchair was common, except that its uphol-\nstored bottom seemed thicker than its gen.\neral style warranted. Ifelt underneath, and\nthere, covered by removable lower cloth,\nwas fastened the real medium of musical\ncommunication between the spirits and the\naudience. The alert girl was quick to play\npart in which, doubtless, she had been\ncarefully instructed.\n\n\n81 What are you doing, sir? she ex-\nclaimed, rising in all the wrath of insulted\ninnocence.\n\n\nEvery spiritualist of that company Of\nsome forty or so believed that l had taken\nsome outrageous liberty with the tender\ncreature. She completed her task expertly\nby bursting into hysterical weeping. The\nfond father came holly to the front. !\ngrabbed up the chair. He slammed it\ndown. Iprotested that wished to investi-\ngate\nYou have disturbed the harmony of\nthe circle he said, depressing his resent\nment with a carefully manifested effort,\nand will cheerfully refund your dollar II\nyou will kindly CO.\n\n\n\"As it is wrong to ruin religious faith,\ndeparted\"\n\n=== 14_1909-05-19_pna_sn95073194_00206537723_1909051901_0469.json\nThe Columbus journal. (Columbus, Neb.) 1874-1911 · 1909-05-19\ncrops: None\nheadline: CHAPTER XIX.\nThe Eaole's FLIGHT.\n---\nThe Eagles Flight.\n\n\nNo stranger spectacle was ever out.\nlined against the sky than that Of the\nfate-laden morning, when at height\nof more than three miles above the\nsea the emperor's ships were borne\naway A child full of life. energy and\nvivacity, clutched in the inflexible\ntalons of merciless eagle. and hur-\nried into the empyrean. would have\nbeen no more helpless. The suns rays\nwere now painting the surface of a far\nreaching, untenanted ocean. on whose\nwaves no sail caught the breeze. and\nacross whose depths sailed no squad\nrOns. The day of its abandonment\nwas at hand. and the time not distant\nwhen seafaring was to become merely\nLegendary.\n\n\nIn the profound solitude of the up\nper air the radioplanes swung males\ntically in a wide circle, and then like\nflock of geese in homeward flight\nformed a long line which in stately\nprocession directed its course to the\neast.\n\n\nThe japanese knew by the faces\nstaring at them from the bellies of the\nmonsters which had gathered them in\nthat they were in the grasp of the\nenemy. and impotent, although\nthroughout that trying day no word\nwas addressed to them. Once. from\ntheir lofty planes of transit. they saw\nthrough their glasses dim outline on\nthe far southern horizon whose faint\nblue haze held Honolulu, designated\nas stopping place. but which they\nwere never to reach--destined to be a\nport for naught but phantom ships-a\nport OF dreams.\n\n\nTo them their progress through the\nair was at terrifying speed. and the\nwind OF fight sweeping in a gale\nacross across their decks drove them\nto shelter; but they did not know that\nthe machines above them were work\ning at slow strength, in order that\ntheir coming to the shores of the\nAmerican continent might be unwit-\nmessed and unheralded. The hours\nslipped away, until below them the\nshadows heightened and deepened and\nthe waves were no longer seen. Up\nin their aerial path the dusk was fall\ning. when simultaneously they came to\na halt and hung motionless in midair.\nOfficers and men hurried to the decks\nto learn what this change might pro-\ntend, and As they did SO they discov-\ncred that the silent monsters were\nclustered in what seemed perilous\nproximity to the craft holding their\nredOubtable flagship, the Ito. On the\nbridge of the latter appeared the ad-\nmiral of the fleet. Kamigawa, his i'm\npassive face showing nothing to his\nfollowers of the strain under which he\nhad labored during those long hours\nof captivity. Like his fellow oflcers.\nhe looked at the assemblage, anxious\nto know the cause of the abrupt stop,\nand then aloft to the engines of vic\ntory. from each of which there\nwhipped and cracked in the evening\nbreeze the stars and stripes.\n\n\nThe grinding noise of metal sliding\nover metal attracted his attention, and\nalmost before it had ceased a man\nclad in the blue uniform of the\nUnited States navy leaned out of q\nhuge port. holding himself by his hand.\nand browning down at the men upon\nthe ItO's bridge.\n\n\nGood evening, Kamigawa:\" a\nvoice hailed with gruff resonance. and\nhe recognized Bevins, who had been\nan Instructor in the naval academy of\nthe nation which had presented him\nwith postgraduate instruction, and\nwhich he had come to assault.\n\n\nGood evening. admiral\" he re-\nsponded in excellent English, mentally\nthankful that he had offered no great\ner resistance to this famous bulldog\nOf the sea; but with no faint of shame\nclouding his mind in thus being re-\ncalled to the fact that he had at\ntempted war upon a country which\nhad helped to educate him. of which\nhe had been a guest of honor, and to\nwhich he owed much in knowledge of\nseamanship.\n\n\n1T gives me much pleasure\" re.\nsumed the voice above in a tone of\ncold courtesy. to request that you at\nonce 60 through the formality of sur\nrendering your entire feet, and sig\nnal the other vessels from your flag\nship that in behalf of yourself and\nmen you accept parole under the usual\nprovision that neither you nor any\nof your men will bear arms against\nthe United States during the re-\nof this war\"\n\n\nBut l can't do that\" Kamigawa |\nprotested in tone of bitterness.\n\n\nVery we'll\" came the curt answer.\nIl shall at once drop you. and 1 can |\nassure you that nothing will give me\ngreater JOY\"\n\n\nThe blue figure with its tapping |\n\n=== 18_1899-02-26_pna_sn86086481_00517171840_1899022601_0622.json\nThe Worcester spy. [volume] (Worcester, Mass.) 1898-1904 · 1899-02-26\ncrops: None\nheadline: \"HAIL COLUMBIA\n\nThe Song That Electrified\n\n\nthe Country.\n---\nNew York Observer.)\n\n\nAn American song that has been re-\nmembered and sung for hundred years\nis too good not to have its centennial\nrecognized and honored. Rumors of\nwar were rife when the song was writ\nten. War was an actually between the\nUnited States andT SPAN when the\nspring and summer of its hundredth\nyear were passing. A revival of the con-\nditions that gave it birth is a coinci-\ndence worthy of mention. The recent\nwar ended without giving birth to a\nsorely needed creation-a new Ameri-\ncan song, a song that will survive not\nmerely campaign of war, but a gen\nGRAFTON.\n\n\nHail, Columbia\" was written in 1898\nby Joseph Hopkinson Q770-1S42) vice\npresidentof the American Philosophical\nsociety AID president of the Pennsyl-\nNania Academy of Fine Arts, War be-\ntween France and England was then\nthough inevitable. Congress was in ses-\nsion in Philadelphia and celebrating on\nthat subject. The theatre in Philadel-\nphia was open. Gilbert Fox profess\nsional vocalist, was about to receive a\n~ A-NAit\" IL sllli IlppAllsOI NEIG lOl\nlner schoolmates. He called upon Mr..\nHuplinson at No. 1872 Spruce street,\n, Philadelphia, one Saturday afternoon in\nApril, previous to the Monday when the\nbenefit was to be received. He wanted\na patriotic song adapted to Gen. Wash\n/ lngton's March or THE President S\n! March that had been composed in 1889\nI by German named Feyles in honor of\n/ President Washington and that was\nplayed in 1889, when Washington came\n10 New York to be inaugurated.\n\n\nHopkinson presented Washington,\n\n\nwhose praises were sounded in the\n! fourth stanza, with copy of his song.\n\n\nFox believed that the song desired\nwould gain for him what up to that date\n\n\nwas not assured-a fun house. Hopkin-\nI son promised TO try his gift and art as\n\n\nrequested\n\n\n/ He completed the song Sunday after\nnoon and gave it to the singer when he\ncalled. 1t was announced Monday morn\n\n\ning and sung Monday evening, accom-\nI panied by a full band and chorus. 1t\nwas encored eight times. the audience\n/ finally Joining in the chorus. 1t found\nfavor with both American parties that\nI sided with England or with France. 1t\ni was sung in Philadelphia by congress\nmen at night on the streets and in all\n\n\nparts Of the land by crowds. 1t cleo\ntrlfied Washington, Hamilton, Jefferson\n+ and McHenly.\n\n\n/ The object Of Hopkinson was to get\n/ up an American spirit that should re\ngard American honor and rights, IL\n( respective Ct the issue between France\nand England. The object seems to have\n\n\nbeen accomplished for biographer OF\n\n\nHopkinson says that this song, melting\n\n\nnational pride, probably helped to avert\n/ enlargement in the European conflict\ni Messrs. Reinagle and Wignall. of the\n\n\nthen new theatre on Chestnut street\n\n\narranged the music for the song and for\n/ The Presidents March. -\" Both were\n/ produced by Willig, a music dealer on\nSouth Fourth street, Philadelphia. 1t is\n+ not customary for regimental bands to\n/ play during battle but in the civil\nwar at Williamsburg, Shiloh and Get\n/ tysburg, they played Hail Columbia\nand Yankee Doodle\" During the CEN\n. tennial exhibition in Philadelphia. 1876\n\n\nan autograph copy was displayed in the\nmuseum at Independence hall.\n\n\nThe Rev r. p. Mason, a Congrega-\ntional chaplain in the civil war, recall\ning IN 1863 the power of vocal and in\nstrumental music over the soldiers\n\n\nsaid: L sometimes thought that the\n\n\ngrand old patriotic peal of Hail Column\n\n\nbia,' and the heart thrilling war SONG\nOF The Star Spangled Banner exer-\ncised a mightier sway than any other\n\n\nsingle cause whatever. Jefferson\n\n\ngave us the Declaration of Indepens\n\n\ndencei Hamilton gave us the Constitu-\nton; Washington gave us his Farewell\n/ Address-was the benediction less when\n/ Hopkinson gave US Hail Columbia? - {\"\nHopkinson was a native of PhiladeI-\n! phla, graduate of the University OF\nPennsylvania, in the class Of 1856, and\n\n\nsubsequently one of its trustees. He\n\n\nbecame an attorney of the highest rank\nthe leading counsel for Dr. BENJAMIN\nRush in 1899, in his suit against VIII\n/ liam Corbett and the defender of Sen\nator Samuel Chase when he was i'm\n\n\npeached by the United States SEN\n\n\nate. In 1914 he was elected to con\n\n\ngress as a federalist and served one\nterm. President John Quincy Adams\nappointed him United States Judge for\nthe eastern district of Pennsylvania,\n\n\nan office which he held for ia years, or\nuntil his death. Sos ~~t-\n\n\n! The criticism of ''HaiI Columbia\" and\nkindred songs is that they lack literary\n\n\nmerit; that as poems they would not\nT obtain currency and permanency fol-\ntheir tunes. That they are not of high\nrank as literary productions is con\nfessed That they owe much of their\n\\ popularity to their tunes is acknowl-\nfedged. But if they were more literary\n\n\nand less emotional they would never\n\n\n~ RCRAAGA\" IiiG very ldCl lIldk I0DN\n\n\nstimulate national sentiment and pride\n\n\ntouch the memory, imagination ano\n\n\nheart Of the people is the explanation OF\n\n\ntheir naturalization and perpetuity.\n/ II, however, the object of Hopkinsor\n\n\nwas to Great an American spirit, and\nhis object was realized, there is q\n\n\ngreater need and a more potential re-\n\n\nsuit now. For the spirit needed is that\n\n\nof universal brotherhood. Patriotism is\n\n\nnot the ultimate, the superlative virtue\n\n\n1t is the positive degree. Cosmopoli-\n\n\ntanism is another name for Chris\n\n\ntianity, and when its full fruition APP\n\n\npears and is disseminated, wars and\n\n\nrumors of wars will cease, and Column\n\n\nbia will be happier land than in 1896\nor 1898. The international song is a\n\n\npotentiality for the mute or the unborn\n\n\nMillions.\n\n\nWANTS OF CONSTITUENTS.\n\n=== 6_1910-07-02_pna_sn82016014_00295870722_1910070201_0026.json\nThe Topeka state journal. [volume] (Topeka, Kansas) 1892-1980 · 1910-07-02\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nter a request to his wife to sit for\nher portrait, and to place in the envel-\nope four blank sheets of paper. The\nmediums\" then placed this in a dou-\nBILE slate, over which Mr.. Mattern\nplaced two rubber bands crosswise and\nlengthwise. A rapping noise followed\n\n\nWhen the slate was opened, and Mr..\nMattern cut the envelope he found on\nthe paper a message written with ink\nin what he says was his wife's hand\nwriting.\n\n\nMr.. Mattern says he received another\nspirit letter from his wife, except that\nthis time he used his own private en-\nvelopes and stationery. In the second\nmessage, he declares his wife referred\nto a remark made by a neighbor, Mrs.\nWatson, prior to Mrs. Mattern's death.\nOn his return, Mr. Mattern says he\nlearned that the same remark was\nmade by his neigbbor while he was away\nfrom the house.\n\n\nTHE handwriting was like that of\nmy wife when writing fast\" said Mr..\nMattern. And the trouble she refers\nto has come.\n\n\nIl was not a Spiritualist,\" he con-\ntipued, only since getting a likeness\nof my wife; also the letter; it is diff\ncult not to believe in spirits. 1 don't\nknow how the portrait was made, but\nI am satisfied with it so far as the\nlikeness is concerned.\n\n=== 14_1884-03-27_pna_sn94056414_00206537747_1884032701_0235.json\nMcCook weekly tribune. (McCook, Neb.) 188?-1886 · 1884-03-27\ncrops: None\nheadline: Guiteau'S Ghost.\n---\nWashington Republican.\n\n\nA weird and remarkable scene &C.\nCured in the rotunda of the jail yes\n\n=== 17_1913-11-05_p1_sn84022374_00332895424_1913110501_0783.json\nEllsworth American. [volume] (Ellsworth, Me.) 1855-current · 1913-11-05\ncrops: None\nheadline: The Look That\nHaunts lin\n---\ni've been ubeeged. said sheriff\n\\ HarSer of New Mexico. ITO XIII num-\n4 Der Of men in my day. and they was\nMI Of 'em the all firedest villains you\nEVER seen. Consequently don't lay\nawake nights seein' EN- lookin' at me\nRepresentatives, There wasn't con-\nsarned one of en- that wouldn't a\nkilled me II he'd got the drop on he\nfust. But there was one KILN that I\nwon't never git over\"\n\n\n\"a manT'\n\n\nsNc.\"\n\n\nNot a woman or child2\"\n\n\nmot any o that nuther.\"\n\n\nWe'll, then. what was ity'\n\n\n81 never was good hand to begin\nat the wrong end OF yarn. II you're\na mind to listen I'll tell you about Il.\nbut don't want no interruptions\"\n\n\n\"Go ahead\n\n\nwhen | was sheriff OF county,\nArizony. there was good deal goin'\non In the shape OF crime. with all sorts\nof pussons a-doin' of II. Some OF 'em\nwould stay and fight 1t out after a\nKILN or a robbery. and some Of EN-\nwould light out. Them as lit cut must\nbe foilered, and. seein' they allus stole\nthe best bosses there was to be had, It\nwasn't no easy job TO catch EN-.\nwas young Teller then and as fond of\nn good boss as any one Besides. had\nto have the best In ruunin' down\nthieves and cntthroats. Another AMI.\nmai needed was bioodhonnds. had\nthree Of the likeliest dogs you ever\nsaw. but only one favorite. called\nher after Queen Victoria. II ever a\ndog loved a man Vic loved me. onct\nwhen I got shot by road agent and\nwas laid mp for awhile she stayed\naround the door Of my room lookin'\nwistful at every one that came out. as\nmuch as to say. is he Eoin' TO hand In\nhis chips or nots' And when she'd\ncatch sight OF me lynn on the bed\nshe'd moan dreadful.\n\n\nzone mernin early was Weakened\nand told that a desperate named Jim\nRynders had broke Into the honse Of a\ncitizen and carried away his daughter.\ngal about eighteen years old No\nbody could tell which way he had gone.\nand the only way he could be tracked\nwas by hound mounted my horse\nMaple was named for Napoleon\nBonyparte-and. lettin' Vie loose. took\nher TO the house where the outrage\nhad been committed She PICKED up\nthe scent right away ard struck of\nwestward followed on Nap.\n\n\n\"Vic ran along with her nose to the\nground till she came to creek. where\nshe lost the scent. hut picked IT up on\nthe other side some distance below.\nshown that the rascal bnd been tryiu'\nto throw us Off by waikiki In water.\nWe followed till night. when, comm tn\na wooded place saw Rynders ahead\nHe had Just come 70 Il halt and was\npickin' up wood IN make a tire with.\nThe gut was sittin' ON the ground.\nleanin up agin the trunk Of tree\nlookln' like corpse. Vic saw 'em Just\nas soon as did and stopped stock still\nlike a pointer on seem game. put\nmy hand on her to quiet her. for\nthought might get shot at the man\nbefore he knowed ! was near Vie\ndidn't give me away. but Nap did by\nsplutterin'. Rynders turned IKE a\nfash. bnt not before ia sent q bullet\nat him. The gal jumped to her feet\nand RynderS dArted behind her. IL\ncowarily rascal thinkin' Ia prevent my\nKILN him without klil.u her\n\n\nThe way for me- ~ do was to send\nVic at him. and that's rhaf I lid. She\nneared him saw him draw revolver\nto protect himself He fred NJ the dog.\nand saw her 30 ahead on three legs.\nShe got her teeth into his side and held\nhim so that the raj could get away\nfrom him. He fired ag'in at Vic. and |\nsaw that he had disabled her. but |\nnow had free shot at him and\ndropped him.\n\n\nIl ran down and tried to quiet the\ngal, who was hysterieai. and partially\nsucceeded. Vie was iyin' still. but I\nknowed she was alive. for she was\nfoIiowin' me around with melancholy\neyes.\n\n\n1.-The dog saved you I said to the\ngal. If II hadn't been for her I couldn't\nhave tracked you\n\n\nThis set the young woman TO\nthtnkin' about the dog instead Of her\nself. and she went up to Vic and patted\nher. Fright was turned into gratitude.\n\n\n81 called 70 Vic. but she could not\ncome to me. A shot had struck her\nback and paralyxed her.\n\n\nI'm sorry. miss | said, but the\ndog must be killed\n\n\n\"'Killed. Why sos\n\n\nITO put her out Of misery. She'll\nnever move again\n\n\n''The gal bust ont. cryin'. waited\nfor her to compose herself. then got\nher to go behind n tree while ! did\nthe business. Vic saw me aim at her\nand knowed what | was goin' TO do\nThe look she gave me stays by me 10\n\n=== 47_1901-12-25_pna_sn85040451_00414215142_1901122501_1193.json\nVernon County censor. [volume] (Viroqua, Wis.) 1865-1955 · 1901-12-25\ncrops: None\nheadline: TO CURE GOBLIN ONE DAY\n---\nGentility sent to'market will not buy\nq peck of meal.\n\n=== 10_1854-04-22_pna_sn83030213_00206530431_1854042201_0767.json\nNew-York daily tribune. [volume] (New-York [N.Y.]) 1842-1866 · 1854-04-22\ncrops: None\nheadline: MaIhe... C.nErtILr RlroK1lp roa rHE I raIag,I\n---\n\n\n\nMarkets. Carefully RfFORTED FOR THKS TRIBvsr\nThe srtt srr bat qui.t TO- Pas IS mess 81\nco1TOx H. so.s -,psr. h .rt. :. yer, fin With an n\nvsres n ofs cent Nondsys quosSs' sales 7ii h.s-sr\nFLOUR AND Oar nsrfs, Tor %s.srs .aa s..s Fsar\n\n\nletter Ito tie oir and better sr.ess they being Ia\nts b ia .hoqld b called Flor\no. IS The ..s Of ss-.Ssa c.n. -. 90 Dbl :. ST 80 III\nfo oT.n u 70 stse sad ~ 81 r. nr-t 70 god\n81 Est7 -tt e.Il-d ,upsrdr. Fo,i 81 in., d.\ns h cpd bsy' ,he s,nad kept wssrs last ,a'unaa\neisIy Of 5000 brsoe. ,.i g %. ~ ct g 81 ss9 IS f,r\nsf45a 81 ~ soI fs. an: q IS p.rsss css ~. ,frn\nsno f.f sne es of 20 bos JsTseJ 80 s2i . IS;\nGPAiNTts se,b buey.l Cor Wheat the milling demand\nhqgie i've 70 IT,, lbs 900 beshs.\nY Censeisn 81 80 iSsoie o. bek-. prime -hs G-n..ee\nTroy 90 Is $50 babel. az s,ra %hs .nd\nsSH..G of .hipSiss ..s Of 50 bg.ss J.t q aud 1850\nn,.h.. ki', to ;i,s ,oon At ~ .S B;sI = care .sa\nstd w'.n ard a4,e for Jse5 Carr better AID ia Air\nOr ur.og.o 1893 for southrn mixed sJe for Jersey ye,r\nWHIsKY~Ife asrts !. rather lever with more oasrr. s.\nOf e. bh. Il \"a re for OHIO and 4 G27.e Tor rrs\n\n\nfuus The ss. ar. 900 s.. sls s or NJ.\n10 Ne. iOi Ma, juns 