 that he may ast0nish the world.\nHow many more such wonders may there\nbe among the freedmen of the South? We\nhave discovered col0red Gottschalk,\nnegro List, an Ethiopian Thalberg, why\nshould we not find mulatto Milton, negro\nNewton, a slave Sbakspere, freedman Fulton,\nan emancipated Ericsson, contraband Column\nbus and darky Dcmosthencs? The aboli-\nlion editors and parsons ough. 10 be writing\nand preaching copiously about Blind Tom. he\nis one of the strongest arguments they could\npresent in favor Of negro suffrage.\n\n\nBut there is another point Of view in which\nBlind Tom is exceedingly interesting. In spite\nof the verdict against Colchester, at Buffalo,\n| and the exposure of the Davenport Brothers at\n| Paris, spiritualism still lives, and our friend\nJudge Edmonds is its prophet. Let the\nspiritualists 60 and take look at the negro\npianist. His ungainly body and his musical\nsoul consort together so oddly that il appears\nalmost probable that he is inspired by the\nspirit of some defunct musician, who has come\nback from the seventh circle Oh Paradise or\nelsewhere to show this ungodTy gentration the\nmusic Of the spiritual spheres. Why not claim\nBlind Tom, then, as the highest order OF\nmedium yet developed! HIS music is far\nin advance Of that of the Fox Sisters\nor the Davenport Brothers. They could\nonly rattle tambourincs, blow tin trumpe's,\nring dinner bells and twang phosphorescent\ni guitars, and they required a dark room and\nselect circle to accomplish even that. But\nBlind Tom comes out on an open platform, in\nthe full glare Of light, before large audience\nthe larger The better-and produces the finest\nmelodies upon the best Instrument that man.\nkind has been able to invent. Observe him\nwhen he is not playing and no one would ever\nsuspect the tremendous musical genius be pos.\nsesscs but when he once touches The keys OF\nThe piano he is like one inspired. Is his in\nspiration spiritual, or Is it merely musical\nThat is a question which Judge Edmonds and\nthe spiritualists must answer. If Blind Tom be\nnot accepted by the abolltionists as the most\nmarvellous development Of the negro race, and\nas striking and convincing proof Of the extra.\n\n\nordinary capacity Of the emancipatcd slaves\n\n\nthen the spiritualists should take him up and\nclaim him as a first class musical medium. He\nwill afford an excellent text for the abolition\nparsons, if they take advantage OF his appear\n\n\nance among us but if they miss the COLDER\n\n\nopportunity the spiritual professors will not bt\n\n\nso foolish as 10 let this amazing phenomenon\n\n\npass unnoticed.\n\n\nDodwortb Hall is loo small for thu\n\n\naudiences that crowd in to hear Blind\n\n\nTom. Poor Kingslapd, Of the Academy\n\n\nOf Music, is Boosting that the upper tic\n\n\nof his ediace--the low-priced portion of TIT\n\n\nhousc-is now filed every opera evening\n\n\nbut If he would engage Blind Tom he wculc\n\n\nfind this American prodigy Of African dcsccn\n\n\nmore attractive than Italian singers, and th,\n\n\nparquet, the balcony and the boxes OF III,\n\n\nAcademy would then be throDged with fash\n\n\nionable people. Poor Stuart, OF The Winter\n\n\nGarden, might probably withdraw Our Amcri\n\n\ncan Cousin and bring ont our African cousin\n\n\nBlind Tom. The Joyce Heth in brooches o\n\n\nthe Muscum-whose fat woman is becoming a.\n\n\nthin as ths living skeleton q account Of thu\n\n\nbad busiDessa-m'gbt have The credit Of exhibit\n\n\ning one genuine curiosity If he could only\n\n\nsecure Blind Tom. Why should poor Wallacl\n\n\ncontinue TO persecute the public with stupid\n\n\nRussian Serf, when here Is an astonishing\n\n\nAmerican serf. just released from slavery and\n\n\nsure to draw thousands TO the theatre? Indeed\n\n\nwe may with equal force recommend this\n\n\nmusical wonder TO all the theatrical mana\n\n\ngers. Except dandy \"sam,\" at the Broadway\n\n\nBlind Tom is the only novelty among oul\n\n\namusemens tAs season: Everybody hes sssV\n\n\nthe Ravel family, who have 40 worn themselves\nout with their fip-aaps that there are only two\n\n\nOf them left TO look at but Blind Tom Is per-\n| factlv frocb and Ia az 0noA an q half do1ou mar\n\n=== 1_1941-11-20_p8_sn92070146_00414189015_1941112001_0444.json\nImperial Valley press. · 1941-11-20\ncrops: None\nheadline: Mexico. u. S. Lay\nAncient Ghosts\n---\nfair valuation independently and\nthen seek a compromise on their\nvaluations. II they cannot agree\nthey will report to their govern\nments. The agreement specifically\nprovides that if an agreement Is\nnot reached seven months hence,\nsettlement will be sought through\ndiplomatic channels.\n\n\nSecretary of State Cordell HUI\nmade it clear that the oil conf\npanies forfeit no rights under this\nagreement. Observers foreSaW the\nlikelihood that, if the companies\nrefuse to accept the findings, they\nwould be in a poor position to push\ntheir case. They would be unlikely\nto obtain any support from the\nstate department for another settle\nment.\n\n\nThe signing of the agreements\ntook place in a holiday atmoS-\nphere. 1t was done on the eve\nof the American Thanksgiving and\nthe eve of the Mexican independ-,\nence day celebrating the Dist an\nniverSary of the revolution which\noverthrew the dictatorship of Port\ntirio DiaZ.\n\n=== 10_1953-06-22_p11_sn83045462_00280606698_1953062201_0184.json\nEvening star. · 1953-06-22\ncrops: None\nheadline: The Ghost Of Major Andre\n\nSimilarity Seen in Gen. Woshngtons Attitude on Treason\nTo Eisenhowers Firmness on Fate Of the Rosenberes\n---\nPresident Eisenhower's tense\nmoments while waiting for\nthe RosenbergS to tell all they\nknew and demonstrate their\nallegiance to the United\nStates instead Of to Soviet\nRussia had in some respects\nq curious parallel to the incl\ndents just before Gen. Wash\ntngton in 1880 ordered the\nexecution Of Major Andre,\nBritish spy.\n\n\nLast week reference was\nmade in these columns to the\ntreason of Benedict Arnold\nand the dispatch should have\nread that it was his accom-\nplice, Major Andre, who paid\nthe death penalty. For the\nfact Is that Arnold himself\nescaped and had the effron-\nterry to demand that Gen.\nWashington grant clemency to\nAndre, failing which, Arnold\nthreatened reprisals against\nContinental prisoners in the\nhands Of the British.\n\n\nBut Gen. Washington was\nunmoved. He did grant a re-\nprieve at the last minute on\nthe day the execution was\nfirst ordered. Then he post\nponed it only until a hearing\ncould be granted to a British\nmajor general and two civilian\nlawyers who came by permiS-\nsion across from enemy lines\nto argue that Andre had\nentered the Continental lines\nunder a flag of truce and\nhence was not deserving of\nthe death penalty. But while\nGen. Washington agreed to\nhave one of his generals re-\nceive the delegation, he made\n1t clear he was not receiving\nthem as bargaining repre-\nsentatives of a hostile govern\nment that had nothing to do\nwith the enforcement Of\nAmerican military law\"\n\n\nGen. Washington read the\n\n=== 17_1896-02-27_p1_sn85032801_00212473662_1896022701_0406.json\nPeople&#39;s voice. [volume] (Wellington, Sumner County, Kansas) 1890-1917 · 1896-02-27\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nam a medical man, ana observation is\neverything in my profession. had no\nidea you were looking\"\n\n\n81 can see without looking\" he an\nBwered. 81 thought you were a de-\ntective, at first; but | couldn't recall\nyour face at the time / knew the force\"\n\n\n\"Were you detective, then2\" said |.\n\n\nmo\" he answered, with a laugh; II\nwas the other thing-the detected, you\nknow. Old scores are wiped out now,\nand the law cannot touch me. so\ndon't mind confessing to a gentleman,\nlike yourself, what scoundrel have\nbeen in my time\"\n\n\n1--WE are none Of US perfect\" said l.\n\n\nmo; but was real out-and-outer.\nA fake, you know, to start with, and\nafterwards 'cracksman.' It Is easy\nto talk Of these things now, for I've\nchanged my spirit. It's as II | was\ntalking of some other man, you see.\"\n\n\nExactly So\" said |. Being med\nzeal man | had none Of that shrinking\nfrom crime and criminals which many\nmen possess. could make all allow\nances for congenital influence and the\nforce Of circumstances. No company,\ntherefore, could have been more ac-\nceptable to me than that Of the old\nmalefector; and as | sat putting at my\ncigar, was delighted to observe that\nmy air ofinterest was gradually loosen\ning his tongue. ....\n\n\n\"Yes; I'm converted now\" he con-\ntinued, land Of course am happier\nman for that And yet\" he added\nwistfully, 'there are times when long\nfor the old trade again, and fancy my\nself strolling out on a cloudy night\nwith my jimmy in my pocket. left a\nname behind me in my profession, sir.\n] was one Of the old school, you know.\n1t was very seldom that we bungled a\njob. We used to begin at the foot OF\nthe ladder, the rope ladder, II I may\nsay so, in my younger days, and then\nwork our way up, step by step, so that\nwe were what you might call good\nmen all through\"\n\n\nII see,\" said l.\n\n\nII was always reckoned hard-worl'\ning, conscientious man, and had talent,\ntoo-the very cleverest Of them allowed\nthat. l began as a blacksmith, and\nthen did little engineering and car\npentering, and then / took to sleight.\nof-hand tricks, and then to picking\npockets. | remember, when was home\non visit, how my poor old father used\nto wonder why | was always lowering\naround him. lie little knew that\nused to clear everything out Of hi,\npockets dozen times a day, and ther\nreplace them, just to keep my hand in\nHe believes to this day that | am in ar\nOffice in the city. There are few o.\nthem could touch me in that particular\nline Of business, though\"\n\n\n81 suppose 1t is matter Of practice?'\nj remarked.\n\n\nITO great extent. Still, a mar\nnever quite loses it, if he has once beer\nan adept-excuse me; you have dropped\nsome cigar ash on your coat\" and hi\nwaved his hand politely in front of my\nbreast, as if to brush 1t off. There,\nhe said, handing me my gold scarf pin\nyou see | have not forgot my old Gun\nming yet\"\n\n\nHe had done it SO quickly that\nhardly saw the hand whisk over my\nbosom, nor did l feel his fingers touch\nme, and yet there was the pin glitter\ning in his hand. ut is wonderful\"\nsaid as l fixed it again in its place.\n\n\n\"0h, that's nothingl But l have\nbeen IN some really smart jobs. was\nIN the gang that picked the new patent\nsafe. You remember the case. 1t wa.\nguaranteed to resist anything; and we\nmanaged to open the first that wa\never issued, within a week Of its ap\npearance. twas done with graduated\nwedges, sir, the first so small that you\ncould hardly see 1t against the light\nand the last strong enough to prize Il\nopen. 1t was clever managed affair.\n\n\n81 remember it\" said L But surely\nsome one was convicted for that9\"\n\n\nEyes, one was nabbed. But he didn'\nsplit, nor even let on how it was done\nWe'd have cut his soul out If\" 81,\nsuddenly damped down the very ugll\nfires which were peeping from his eyes\nperhaps I am boring you, TALKING\nabout these old wicked days Of mine?\n\n\nION the contrary,\"I said, \"you in\nterest me extremely\" .I..\n\"I lika IN rot q lietanar ann t,-,,et\n\n=== 1_1903-02-28_pna_sn82015679_00296029750_1903022801_0331.json\nThe Indianapolis journal. [volume] (Indianapolis [Ind.]) 1867-1904 · 1903-02-28\ncrops: None\nheadline: THEY CALL 1T spOoK NIGHT.\n\nManner fin Which the Capital Guards\n\n\nScare Each other.\n---\nTonight (Feb. 23) will be 'spook night \"\n\n\nremarked one of the Capitol guards to-day.\n\n\nand there's hardly one of the men on\n\n\nnight watch duty that doesn't hate TO see IL\n\n\ncome.\n\n\nEven the coolest of the watchmen breads\n\n\nTINS night, because every year at just mid\n\n\nnight of the I'D the ghost of John Quincy\n\n\nAdams has been seen in the corridors of the\n\n\nCapitol.\n\n\n1T was on this day in 1SiS that the great\nAdams died in the chamber OF the House or\n\n\nRepresentatives\" continued the guard. \"A\nbrass tablet marks the spot where he fell.\n\n\nOn the 23d of every February since a pale.\nphosphorescent glow is seen to hover over\n\n\nthe tablet. Those who have been brave\nenough to stay have related that this glow\nslowly merges into the majestic figure of\nthe statesman. The face has an expression\nof sorrow that seems to be increased as the\n\n\norator goes through the motion of making\n\n\nan address.\n\n\n'It is wordless speech. and the sight of\nthe noble form of the statesman in the pale\nglow, making gestures as If addressing his\ncolleagues Of a former day upon the floor\nof the Senate. has caused even the strong\nest nerves to tremble.\nAfter a few moments the light fades\nand the sound of heavy footsteps is heard\ngoing slowly down the vacant corridors.\nIl tell you, even the bravest of us hates to\nsee the I'D come round again.\nYes, there are other ghostly visitants to\nthe echoing halls of the Capitol, but none\nso remarkable the shade who comes to-\nnight. The entire Congress of 1s4S appears\n| every once in while in the hall of statuary\nand carries on an energetic session in dumb\n| show.\n\n\nSometimes the hurried wrath of Vice\nPresident Wilson, who died in his room in\n\n\nthe Senate end of the Capitol, is seen to go\n! quickly through some empty hall. His face\n| is always troubled, and the spirit seems to\nbe hurrying upon some errand of impor-\n| lance. Whenever the body of dead legis-\n| lator is lying in state in the Capitol the Vice\n| Presidents shade enters and makes a visit\n| to the bier.\n\n\nThe ghost of General John A. Logan is\n| also often seen by Capitol guards lowering\n| about the room Of the committee on mill\n| tary affairs. IL is a large and commanding\n| presence, that stalks in dignity into the\n| waiting shadow.\n\n\n| But, as / said before, when the new night\n| guard has to be really initiated, we send\n| him alone, at midnight, Feb. g. past the\n| brass tablet that is sacred to the memory of\n| John Quincy Adams\n\n=== 7_1906-10-14_p46_sn84026749_100492647_1906101401_0653.json\nThe Washington times. · 1906-10-14\ncrops: None\nheadline: Ghost Taxes Visible Form.\n---\nMiss Sadie glanced Up, and. 70 her\nhorror. saw the ghost for the hrst tina\nhead poked In at the door. and .\nform Ike that Of a man. but white.\nas II dressed fo- the tropics, stepped\nboldly into the room. Miss Sadie was\nstartled, but she had presence Of mind\nenough 10 rise 10 welcome the IN\nJruder. letting the darning drop on the\nnoor. But before she got halfway to\nher feet there was a disappearance that\nbetrayed the unearthIy character OF hsr\nvisitor. The man-head ard all-Eons\nThe form did not 90 back 20 the door\nand then drop out Of sight, but. rather,\nwas instantly dissipated Just whess II\nstood.\n\n\nFor months the Misses Taft kept\ntheir secret. not mentioning. except 20\nthe priests at St. EIizabeth's, and 20\nsome dear and trusty friends. the\nmystic lappenirgs IN their apartment.\nBut anaIly the ghost became too\ntroublesome 10 be longer tolerated.\nTie story was told 20 doctor, who\ngot one OF the women from St Eliza\nbeth's and a boy TO remain IN the\nrooms with him all night They heard\nthe creeks. the Kissing, the rumbles.\nbut saw no ghost later the other\nfriends Of the girls were Informed,\nand in turn braved the CHESTS. sems\nOf these we're more fortunate, and By\nthis time az least a dozen persons are\nready 10 testify 20 seeing tbs strange\nmoving and disappearing figures that\nhave created such a ue.ghborhood *\nelement.\n\n\nLately the TAFT sisters have been q\nhit unnerved. Several times. when the\nthumpings and groaaings were mors\nthan ordinarily disturbing the twO\nyoung women rose together IN the\nnight. AMI at o'clock in The morn\ning ned the house. They went usual\n\" TO the home OF their washerwoman.\nin Juniper street, not far awav.\n\n\nSuch Is the story Of PhnadeIphia's\nlatest ghost. Il IS causing consider\nable excitement IA the neighhorhood Of\n2025 South Broad street. and consider\nbIe indignation in the breasts OF Mr..\nand Mrs David Marsh. the Iesseez OF\nthe building. It is driving the Taft\nsisters 20 other quarters. and viii\ndoubtless make tenants a bit shy OF\nthe third floor rooms they now occupy.\nBut whether one believes IN ghosts or\nnot-and OF course one doesn't-if one\nvera going 10 meet one, one would\nprefer 70 have Il Al ''out-and-outer'\nIke him OF the hacked head\n\n=== 12_1912-10-25_pna_sn88085187_00211108344_1912102501_0180.json\nThe Tacoma times. [volume] (Tacoma, Wash.) 1903-1949 · 1912-10-25\ncrops: None\nheadline: BEAUTIFUL DOE NvADES MANS HAUNTS DIES nMpALED\n\n\nON FENCE WITHIN MILES OF GREAT METROPOI.IS\n---\nA fleet doe deer. at home on\nwooded hills, ventured into the\nhaunts of men and died. impaled\nthrough the heart on an iron\npicket fence.\n\n\nThis happened within 80 miles\nof New York city, the metropo-\nlis OF America, ON the outskirts\nOF Noroton, Conn.\n\n\nHere Ia her first and only pho\ntograph, taken at the end OF her\nbrief and fatal holiday In civili-\nzation.\n\n\nOnce the beauty Of the forest\nnow the carcass at barbecue.\n\n\nMany deer thrive in New Eng\nland, protected by the game laws.\nAnd this doe had lived and\nthrived in the shadows of kindly\nbushes and low-branched trees,\nsince Noroton hunters first sight\ned her, a young, graceful fawn.\n\n=== 6_1876-05-14_p1_sn88064616_00295878605_1876051401_0069.json\nThe New Orleans Democrat. (New Orleans, La.) 1875-1876 · 1876-05-14\ncrops: None\nheadline: CHINESE GHOSTS.\n\n\n\n\n---\nMatcria1tzed Animals.\n\n\nFrom the Hong Kong tChina) Rcviewl\n\n\nOne popularity about Chinese ghosts\nIL that they do not always rnaterialize,\nbut are frequently seen in incomplete\nform, the herd first becoming visible,\nthen the feet, then the body. and SO on.\nAnother quaint belief is that ghosts\nmi mo ha-pa you've no chin is\nequivalent to saying you're u ghost. :\"\nLike English ghosts, they appear dur\ning the hours Of darkness, and are COM\nPEELED to vanish at cock-crow but the\nconventional white clothing Is absent\nfrom the Chinese idea. A ghost In China\nALWAYS appears In the dress he common\nIy wore during life, and his advent Is\nannounced by candles burning not blue\nbut green the Greeks call supernatural\nterror green fear.\n\n\nAnimals also have their ghosts. A\nresident at Canton, named Ling, had q\nvery intelligent monkey, which having\nbeen severely whipped for some mis-.\nchief. became sulky. refused food, and\ndied. Shortly after the monkeys ghost\nbegan to haunt the house. Food placed\non the tables mysteriously Disappeared,\nand strange annoyances took pale. At\nlength fire broke out in the house I'M\naccountably, and Mr.. Ling shifted his\nresidence. But the monkeys ghost\nstill followed and prosecuted him, until,\nas a last resource, he took a room in the\nTemple OF the Five Hundred Worthless !,\nthe monkey ghost did not dare to face\nthe gods, and left him in peace. Mr..\nLing was still resi- ling In the Temple\nlast year.\n\n\nGhost stories OF the ordinary kind are\nnumerous. A mandarin met his death\nIn the late collision between the Chinese\nsteamer Fusing and the British steamer\nOcean. The Fusing was sunk and over\nsixty drowned. The mandarin's ghost\nappeared to his wife, who was living In\nSoochow, streaming with water from\nhead to foot. He told her he had been\ndrowned, and that he had sent by q Ger\ntain friend some money for her use be.\nfore he took passage In the Fusing, and\nthat the friend would arrive shortly:\nTwo days after the friend named arrived\nwith the money, and SHORTLY after came\nthe intelligence OF the Fusing's disaster.\nThe commander Of Chinese man OF.\nwar belonging to the Foochow Arsenal !\nfleet, was suddenly taken i'll, and died\nut a trends house. After his death,\nthe friend frequently saw his ghost, and\nono night addressed il, saying: You\nshould not lament: 1t was your fate ;\nYOU should be satisfied with it There\nupon the ghost disappeared, and never\nshowed itself again to the same party,\n| but continued often to appear To the\nmen on board the ship he had been\ncommanding, being frequently seen to\npace up and down the deck as he was\nwent during his lifetime at night, and\n| would sometimes place himself in the\nattitude OF drilling the men. =,\n| Here is parallel to the celebrated\nBerkely Square story. Thrust be noted\n1 that the ghosts of suicides are distin-\nguished 6v wearing red silk handker-\nchiefs. ''Outside the north gate OF\nHang chow there was a house haunted\nnv evil ghosts, where no human being\ndared reside, OF which the doors were\never barred and locked. A scholar\nnamed Ts'ai bought the house; people\nall told him he was doing dangerous\nthing, but he did not heed them. None\nOF his family would enter the house, so\nTs'ai went by himself. opened the\ndoors, lit candle and sat down. In the\nmiddle OF the night a woman slowly\napproached, with d red silk handkerchief\nhanging to her neck, and having salut.\ned him, fastened a rope to the beam OF\nthe ceiling, and put her neck In the\nmoose. Ts'ai did not In the least change\ncountenance. The woman again fast\nened u rope and called on Ts'ai to do rs\nshe had done, but he only lifted his leg\nand put his foot In the moose. The\nwoman said, You're wrong. Ts'al\nlaughed and answered, On the contrary\n1t was YOU who were wrong u long time\nago, or else You would not have come &c\nthis pass. The ghost cried bitterly,\nand having bowed to Ts'ai, departed\naud from that time the house was nc\nlonger haunted. This Tsai was after\n\n\nward a very distinguished scholar\nknown as the Provincial Chancellor.\n\n\nThe writer remembers having read\nsome time similar curfons story OF 9\n\n\npolice cell, in which woman had com\n\n\nmitted suicide by hanging herself, after\n\n\nwhich inmate after inmate continued &c\nattempt to do the same, Till at last a CIR\n\n\ndeclared that fiendish little woman\nappeared in the cell at night and com\n\n\nmanded her to hang herself, which she\n\n\nfelt herself irresistibly impelled to at\n\n\ntempt doing. The cell, it is said, hac\n~ 4 Il h, II\n","numLines":1885,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1885}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e3e81f65-3b15-4538-b528-3c5b5a5d7136","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"other"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2c-reader","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
