s lips,\n61\tdrank deep. 1t was another mis-\n62\ttake. The next few moments\n63\twhirled by in haze and confusion.\n64\tAt the end Of them, without cere-\n65\tnaOny, without fanfare, Mr.. Slyn-\n66\tcniski passed out.\n67\t\n68\t\n69\tJonah\" said Hildy Charming.\n70\tyou're pet\"\n71\t\n72\t\n73\tMr.. Logan nodded in partial\n74\tagreement. In his opinion. Hildys\n75\tthinking was sound as far as 1t\n76\twent. But 1t didn't go far enough\n77\t\n78\t\n79\tII mean,\" Hildy said. amplify-\n80\ting It. that It's simply wonderful\n81\tOf you to spare Mahoney to throw\n82\tout those flares so that Chef can\n83\tmake q safe landing. And it's\n84\twonderful Of you TO have thought\n85\tup gag TO lure father to the Tai\n86\tMahal. You're so clever, Jonah. !\n87\tdon't know what ! would have\n88\tdone without you\"\n89\t\n90\t\n91\tsxeah?\" said Jonah dismalIy.\n92\tElm wonderful. 81 right\"\n93\t\n94\t\n95\tyou really are Q good guy,\n96\tJonah\" Hildy said. Ell could\n97\t\n98\t\n99\t.Weli, why don't your\" said\n100\tJonah. q man who liked 10 i'm.\n101\tprove Idle nomentG.\n102\t\n103\t\n104\tSomewhat 70 his surprise, she\n105\tdid Kiss him. Il was quite q kiss.\n106\tMo Logan had preview OF\n107\tsHWoman.\" he said. reeiing q iits\n108\t&c. docs that come natural o. did\n109\tpoli learn Il ~ e0liege7\"\n110\t\n111\t\n112\tDon't be coarse. jonah\" said\n113\tEildy. 4 Just wanted 10 ali0W you\n114\tthat I really like you. IN fact\n115\tslle iiealtaied and, IN the darkness,\n116\tAfF eyes were Ike tiny pools \"\n117\tHAi18rwHw0a, sometimes Wish\n118\tanal EIaet Wefen'f coming AZ aii.\"\n119\t\n120\t\n121\t~ keEan's heaft, normally q\n122\t\n123\t\n124\trun organ. fufnea . hand=\n125\t\n126\t\n127\t=== 1_1899-09-09_pna_sn85049554_00416159300_1899090901_0286.json\n128\tEvening times-Republican. [volume] (Marshalltown, Iowa) 1890-1923 · 1899-09-09\n129\tcrops: None\n130\theadline: CHALLENGE TO MINISTER'S.\n131\t\n132\tspiritualists Offer to Indulge In a\n133\t\n134\t\n135\tJoint Debate With the Preachers.\n136\t---\n137\tspiritualists Offer to Indulge in a\n138\t\n139\t\n140\tJoint Debate With the Preachers.\n141\t\n142\t\n143\tAt the Spiritualist camp Mrs. Carrie\n144\tFuller Weatherford will lecture tonight\n145\tupon the \"Hells and Heavens of Spirit\n146\tLife\" and also give a song improvisa\n147\tfrom subjects selected by skeptics\n148\tShe is to be followed by the renowned\n149\ttest medium. Mr.. Max Hoffman. of Chi\n150\tcago, who will deliver spirit messages\n151\tand descriptions.\n152\t\n153\t\n154\tThe camp management, in order to\n155\tenlighten the people upon the identity\n156\tof bible and modern spiritualism, here\n157\tby extend most earnest and cordial\n158\tinvitation to any orthodox or liberal\n159\tclergyman to meet Mrs. Weatherford,\n160\twho is a noted lecturer, upon the ros-\n161\ttrum of any church or Spiritualist asso-\n162\tcitation of this vicinity in debate, the\n163\ttopic to be discussed to be: Resolved\n164\tThat spiritual gifts as described by\n165\tPaul in the 18th chapter of First Cor\n166\tinthians, King James version. are iden-\n167\tteal in fact and origin with the spirit\n168\tual gifts among media today\" said dis\n169\tcussion to be held under the usual rules\n170\tof debate and on or before September\n171\t%. 1899.\n172\t\n173\t=== 36_1907-09-19_p1_sn84038335_00415622788_1907091901_0309.json\n174\tMissouri Valley times. [volume] (Missouri Valley, Iowa) 1874-1931 · 1907-09-19\n175\tcrops: None\n176\theadline: STRIKES AT GHOST\n177\tOF HIS VICTIM AND\n178\t\n179\t\n180\tINJURIES CELLMATE\n181\t---\n182\tAbba. la, Sept, Is. i:ailey Rlack,\n183\tnegro in Jail for the murder of John\n184\tcoleman in a fight following a poker\n185\tgame. sees the ghost of his victim each\n186\tnight. Black rises screaming from his\n187\tbunk and seeks protection Of the other\n188\tnris oners. Last night he attacked an\n189\tother prisoner with a guitar, supposing\n190\the was striking the ghost. severely In.\n191\tturing him.\n192\t\n193\t=== 17_1915-01-22_p1_sn83025182_00295875161_1915012201_0062.json\n194\tThe daily Gate City. [volume] (Keokuk, Iowa) 1855-1916 · 1915-01-22\n195\tcrops: None\n196\theadline: ST. Paul \"Phantom\" Now Has Best\n197\tClaim to Middle Weight Cham\n198\tpionship of the\n199\t\n200\t\n201\tWorld.\n202\t\n203\tIN A CLASS BY HIMSELF\n204\t\n205\tMike Took Seven of Ten Rounds by\n206\tBig Marein-other Three\n207\t\n208\t\n209\tWere About\n210\tEven.\n211\t\n212\tby James O'Dea, written for the\n213\t\n214\t\n215\tUnited Press.1\n216\t---\n217\tby james O,Dea, written for the\n218\t\n219\t\n220\tUnited Press.l\n221\t\n222\t\n223\tMILWAUKEE,, wis, Jan. 90\n224\tJimmy Clabby. claimant of the middle\n225\tweight title, knows more about box\n226\ting today than he did yesterday. That\n227\tis, il he profited any by the lesson\n228\tgiven him last night by Mike Gibbons.\n229\t\n230\t\n231\tThe st. Paul phantom\" certainly\n232\tmaterialized. He out-boxed, out-slug-\n233\tged and out-fought the Hammond boy.\n234\tHe demonstrated to the 5000 men and\n235\twomen who crowded the auditorium\n236\tthat he is in class by himself.\n237\tClabby never had chance and was\n238\tlucky to hola Gibbons even In three\n239\tof the ten rounds. The other seven\n240\twere Mikes by so big a margin that\n241\tmost of Clabby's partisans conceded\n242\tthem.\n243\t\n244\t\n245\tThe first two periods were produc-\n246\ttive of the prettiest boxing ever seen\n247\tin these parts In the third, Gibbons,\n248\twith a series of lefts and rights to\n249\tthe head. punched all the cleverness\n250\tout of his opponent. After that 1t\n251\twas merely a question OF how much\n252\tpunishment Clabby could stand up\n253\tunder.\n254\t\n255\t\n256\tMike repeatedly jabbed his light\n257\tming left into Clabby's face without a\n258\treturn. The fourth and fifth were all\n259\tMikes. In the sixth. Clabby changed\n260\this tactics by working into close quar-\n261\ttiers. covering up and whaling away\n262\twith both hands. This period was\n263\tfairly even\n264\t\n265\t\n266\tIn the seventh. eighth ano ninth\n267\tEFa55y took beautiful beating. The\n268\tonly eood bow he landed was right\n269\tswing that cut Gibbons eye. Clabby\n270\tIVES bleeding a. the mouth AID nose.\n271\tThe last round Clsbby fought desper-\n272\tateIE and they beth slugged all over\n273\tthe ring. Nike had the best of every\n274\texchange and It was his round and\n275\this fight\n276\t\n277\t\n278\tJoe yelling. the Chicago light\n279\tweight. dropped Battling Terry OF\n280\tNew Castle, Pa. three times in the\n281\tsemi final. Each time Terry took the\n282\tcount of nine.\n283\t\n284\t\n285\tEddie McGoorty, Billy Murray and\n286\tBob Moha were at the ring side and\n287\tchallenged the winner Of the main\n288\tevent.\n289\t\n290\t=== 9_1890-07-18_pna_sn85038614_00175031029_1890071801_0078.json\n291\tRichmond dispatch. [volume] (Richmond, Va.) 1884-1903 · 1890-07-18\n292\tcrops: None\n293\theadline: How Shall Our Delegates Be Elected\n294\t---\n295\tHow Shall Our Delegates Be Elected =?\n296\t\n297\t\n298\tAs between ward and precinct meetings\n299\tfor the election of delegates to the Con-\n300\tvressional Nominating Convention OF this\n301\tdistrict, we yesterday expressed prefer\n302\tence for the latter mode.\n303\t\n304\t\n305\tThere are twenty-four voting precincts\n306\tIN this city and there would be q meeting\n307\tfor each. No voter would have to go far\n308\tfrom home The people, divided into two\n309\tdozen instead of six sssemblages, would\n310\toyercrowd no hall, and with a correspond\n311\tincly reduced number of ballots TO count,\n312\tthe proceedings would be short. At each\n313\tOf these popular gatherings chairman,\n314\tsecretary, and other officers would have 70\n315\tbe elected. ard thus the honors OF the party\n316\tcould be more widely distributed than by\n317\tthe old way. What is still more important\n318\tin this arrangement, the sentiments OF the\n319\tDemocrats of Richmond could be voiced\n320\tbetter than by ward-meetings.\n321\t\n322\t\n323\tA primary election is also suggested and\n324\tably urged. 1t would be superior to the\n325\tptecinct-meeting plan, provided 1t could\n326\the adopted all over the district. If the\n327\tcounties approve it this city certainly will\n328\tnot object, but uniformity is desirable.\n329\t\n330\t\n331\tOn one occasion-possibly on more than\n332\tone-when nomination for Congress was\n333\tTO be made. we opened a primary poll at\n334\teach yoting-place in the district, and the\n335\tcandidates were voted for directly, and\n336\tupon aggregating the votes in each county\n337\tor city the number of delegates each can\n338\tdidate was entitled to appoint was ascer-\n339\ttanned and declared.\n340\t\n341\t\n342\tAt this critical point of our country's\n343\thistory. when reckless majority IN Con-\n344\tgress is seeking to bind us 80 that no future\n345\tefforts of ours will be availing TO liberate\n346\tus from Republican domination, we want\n347\tnot only the greatest unity. but the great\n348\test enthusiasm in our party. Pluck, spirit,\n349\tand organization can safely carry us\n350\tthrough the election force bill or no. We\n351\tmust aim and work in this direction all the\n352\ttime.\n353\t\n354\t\n355\tWe desire to see our folks preparing to\n356\twin over to their party all the white Re\n357\tpublicans in Virginia who revere the\n358\thonor, principles. and traditions of this old\n359\tState. and who detest such tyranny as has\n360\tbeen practiced by the majority in Congress\n361\tduring this session. The idea that An cleo\n362\tton bill can manacle the true sons Of the\n363\tOld Dominion will be dissipated the mo-\n364\tment it is found that such measure will\n365\tsoldify the white vote. We have faith in\n366\tour people. We know their love for Vir-\n367\tginia. We trust to this for escape from the\n368\tnet work Of rascality proposed IN the\n369\tRowEII-LoDGE bill. We must plead with\n370\tthe whites personally and politically. Ina\n371\tsomewhat similar emergency they rallied\n372\tand overthrow MALONE. and as HARRISON\n373\taud REED are but little better men they\n374\twill be doomed to the same fate.\n375\t\n376\t=== 25_1920-11-02_p1_sn89053713_00393343801_1920110201_0740.json\n377\tAtlanta tri-weekly journal. [volume] (Atlanta, Ga.) 1920-19?? · 1920-11-02\n378\tcrops: None\n379\theadline: \n380\t---\n381\tA mans idea of time is so fluctu-\n382\tating, that every wife needs a ouija\n383\tboard around the house to tell her\n384\texactly when her husband will come\n385\tin, evenings. and when to put the\n386\tsteak in the oven.\n387\t\n388\t=== 2_1919-11-23_p1_sn83030214_00206532452_1919112302_0761.json\n389\tNew-York tribune. [volume] (New York [N.Y.]) 1866-1924 · 1919-11-23\n390\tcrops: None\n391\theadline: nd'lrngg Mystery of the Ouija Board Reincarnation\n392\t---\n393\tabout the child and expresses de-\n394\tsires as to what should be done\n395\twith her.\n396\t\n397\t\n398\tThe ouija board, while the appar-\n399\tent instrument for conveying the\n400\tcommunications of Patience, has\n401\tnow become more a habit than a\n402\tnecessity with Mrs. Curran, she\n403\tdeclares. Patience talks to her\n404\twithout it, but she believes the\n405\tboard helps her to concentrate and\n406\tto keep her mind sufficiently blank.\n407\tAS result of the recent Patience\n408\tWorth meetings held here, there has\n409\tbeen rush to the stores for ouija\n410\tboards. Toy manufacturers are\n411\tselling them at the rate of twenty\n412\tday. The department stores are\n413\thaving an unusual demand for them.\n414\t\n415\t\n416\tThe ouija board has the letters Of\n417\tthe alphabet arranged on it in two\n418\tconcentric arcs, with the ten numer-\n419\tais below and Eyes\" and mo\" in\n420\tscribed on the upper corners. The\n421\tplanchette, or pointer, is a thin,\n422\theart-shaped piece of wood provided\n423\twith three legs upon which it moves\n424\tabout on the board, its point indicat-\n425\ting the letters of the words it is\n426\tspelling. Two persons are needed\n427\tto operate it. They hold the tips Of\n428\tthe fingers lightly on the pointer\n429\tand wait. Perhaps it moves; per-\n430\thaps it does not. Its powers have\n431\tbeen attributed by some to super\n432\tnatural influence; by others to SUBJ\n433\tconsciousness. Scientists, for the\n434\tmost part, treat it with disdain.\n435\t\n436\t=== 12_1868-01-11_pna_sn83030214_00206530881_1868011101_0073.json\n437\tNew-York tribune. [volume] (New York [N.Y.]) 1866-1924 · 1868-01-11\n438\tcrops: None\n439\theadline: .nrndant On oOfl at 5G7 pcRsOut. In\n440\t---\n441\t.OItt\n442\tanve is ouoted London, 90 days. Nv so\n443\t-, paris. long, 51llGA1os Short o,T.\n444\t.rn b1s3 a51ci; Swiss ole496lS'i\n445\taailsatl. Amsterdam, lli0shl; Frankfort\n446\tBremen, 70.S799 Berlin, 72is \"a; cotto, ~ s,el\n447\tobt. 70 Iiverpoa 800 bales cotton at io W.\n448\t\n449\t\n450\tooTl., ,r,,,,oco at ga; and ny Steamer l'VOO\n451\t- Cotton Mt l11id To Glasgow os s.o'''io'\n452\tInch Grain at o.. shippers opNo\" n,raJ.1aL;o\n453\t-, -, soo hills Petroleum nom rAu'uoipii'\n454\t- 81 10 Gibraltar, 800 cases looacoo y\" pf -\n455\t% % \"a. kortcr, are o Norwegian bark TO Cork\n456\t- market with $500 Gfs' Corn az W.. y. - cn\n457\taz\" - pet,oleum az - 20 the Continent or Us Od\n458\tbrig wiz 1\",to5 Kingdom; British bark TO London\n459\t- With 20 900 bush Corn at S. 3d.. and 800 Dds\n460\t&C., n r.. vessel to Bristol with Resin, Spirits\n461\t-,,,rncptine and Petroleum. on private terms; and\n462\t.,.to from Savannah TO Liverpool with 5000 bales\n463\tCotton on private terms.\n464\t\n465\t\n466\tGov Fairchilds annual message 70 the Legislature\n467\tosWisconsn shows that the finances OF the State are\n468\tIn excellent condition the present Indebtedness\n469\tkng So 279OOO The receipts exceed the expenses by\n470\tsome 5000 notwithstanding many permanent I'M\n471\tmovements have been made, such 