1469\t| The day passed away monotonously enough.\n1470\t| The crew slept. Tread and smoked: anti\n1471\t| II o'clock that night found Inc in my chair\n1472\t| at the head of the table in my cabin. Gas\n1473\tper- who had quite got over his fears, lay\n1474\t| ON the hatch at the head 0f the cabin stairs.\n1475\tIcoud hear the pat oi his tail, as u greeting\n1476\t| to the mate as he kept passing him in his\n1477\twalk up and down the deck. This evening\n1478\tmy thoughts were tranquil, Tor was read\n1479\ting volume of Longfelow's poems, and\n1480\twas in the midst of g Evangclinc,\" when l\n1481\theard a howl from Casper\" and the scurry\n1482\tof his feet as he scampercd forward. Look\n1483\ting up l saw my visitor Of the previous\n1484\tnight again descending the companion way.\n1485\tHe st cm,l mitch weaker, and came slowly\n1486\t| down, clutching the hand rail with his one\n1487\tsound arm and hand. lie glanccd al the\n1488\t| table with anxious looks. as il to sre\n1489\t| whether The Chart was still there. Ii was\n1490\tnot. He then gaZed at me with Il sorrowful\n1491\tand disappointed stare and disappeared. l\n1492\tremained quietly in my chair about ten\n1493\tminutes after the spectre had departed.\n1494\tWhen went on deck found the mate\n1495\tagain cndeavorina tn soothc the dog, who\n1496\tsnapped and sriaricl in his kennel, until it\n1497\twas unsafe 10 approach him. Leaving him\n1498\t| there, went below, and throwing myself,\n1499\tdressed MS was, on my cot, tried tn sleep.\n1500\tUnrefreshing and feyerish was the sleep\n1501\twhich visited me that night, and / rose\n1502\tin the morning totally unfit for work of any\n1503\tkind, and with my nerves unstrung. What\n1504\twas expected of me? For what purpose\n1505\twas wanted What must 1 do? Thus\n1506\tmused the entire day. Casper kept close\n1507\tto his kennel all the time and steadily re-\n1508\tfused to cat or drink. When any of us APP\n1509\tpreached his kennel he attempted to snap\n1510\tat us, and appeared as if entirely under the\n1511\t\n1512\t\n1513\tInfluence of some great fear. The calm\n1514\t\n1515\t\n1516\thad continued the entire day, and we had\n1517\t\n1518\t\n1519\tmade no progress whatever. Again evening\n1520\t\n1521\t\n1522\tapproached, and the same hour found nc,\n1523\t\n1524\t\n1525\tas on the two previous nights, sitting at my\n1526\t\n1527\t\n1528\ttable. This time eagerly awaited my Wis\n1529\t\n1530\t\n1531\titor. Would the figure appear? found\n1532\t\n1533\t\n1534\tmyself hoping that it would This time |\n1535\t\n1536\t\n1537\tdid not attempt to read, for my thoughts\n1538\t\n1539\t\n1540\twere too troubled to permit If my book.\n1541\t\n1542\t\n1543\tThe same chart, with the blood stain ON it,\n1544\t\n1545\t\n1546\tlay before me. Would my visitor. if he\n1547\t\n1548\t\n1549\tcame again touch that spot, ur would he\n1550\t\n1551\t\n1552\tgive me some other sign which might in.\n1553\t\n1554\t\n1555\tdicatc to Inc the course l ought To pur\n1556\t\n1557\t\n1558\tsue. This time there was no Caspcr\n1559\t\n1560\t\n1561\tto warn me. | felt the figure was\n1562\t\n1563\t\n1564\tcoming before it appeared Presently\n1565\t\n1566\t\n1567\tsaw it coming down the stairs. This time\n1568\t\n1569\t\n1570\tit was evidently far weaker than before, fol\n1571\t\n1572\t\n1573\twith difficulty could it stand, and it toiled\n1574\t\n1575\t\n1576\tdown the steps in manifest suffering and\n1577\t\n1578\t\n1579\tagony. At last it reached the cabin door\n1580\t\n1581\t\n1582\tand attempted TO advance to me; but ne\n1583\t\n1584\t\n1585\tsooner had it let 90 the hand rail than il\n1586\t| sank cyhaustcd / suranm LII and rilsLlG\n1587\t\n1588\t=== 82_1906-03-29_pna_sn88085947_00211108563_1906032901_0558.json\n1589\tThe Spokane press. [volume] (Spokane, Wash.) 1902-1939 · 1906-03-29\n1590\tcrops: None\n1591\theadline: \n1592\t---\n1593\thaunts after the wreck. The story\n1594\tgoes that he stayed on duty for the\n1595\tnight operator after working all\n1596\tday himself. The railroad company\n1597\tdeclares that the action was purely\n1598\tvoluntary on his part and that he\n1599\thad no business to work for the\n1600\tother man, nor the other man to\n1601\tallow him to take his place. a\"\n1602\t\n1603\t=== 5_1919-06-01_pna_sn83045389_00296029233_1919060101_0387.json\n1604\tRichmond times-dispatch. [volume] (Richmond, Va.) 1914-current · 1919-06-01\n1605\tcrops: None\n1606\theadline: CHICAGO PACKER DECLARES\n1607\t\n1608\t\n1609\tHUNGER HAUNTS EUROPE\n1610\t\n1611\tSays France. Germany and Italy In\n1612\tWorst Stnte-LfttIc Live\n1613\t\n1614\t\n1615\tstock Remains.\n1616\t---\n1617\tNEW YORK. MAY 3I--Europe is on\n1618\tThomas L. Wilson. tho Chicago packer\n1619\twho arrived here today after SIX\n1620\tweeks survey OF tho European food\n1621\tsituation.\n1622\t\n1623\t\n1624\tFrance, Germany and Italy are in\n1625\tthe worst state\" Mr. Wilson said\n1626\tThere is doubt that Germany is\n1627\t$500 square miles found only 200\n1628\thorses, 900 cattle and 1900 hogs or\n1629\tone-haf horse and one-hali head OF\n1630\tmile.\n1631\t\n1632\t\n1633\tThe markets OF the world are now\n1634\topen 10 the United States. But in\n1635\tGreat Britain we have rival IN a\n1636\tstrong position who will take advant-\n1637\tage OF every opening she gets We\n1638\tmust be prepared To meet her com\n1639\tpetition IN every foreign market\"\n1640\t"}]},"uuid":"09684b4b-f2f8-4b11-8098-d00ea54e50a7","timestamp":"2026-10-31T08:23:32.189Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/c1/d05.txt","content":"=== 4_1943-02-26_pna_sn82014085_00393347090_1943022601_0733.json\nThe Waterbury Democrat. [volume] (Waterbury, Conn.) 1917-1946 · 1943-02-26\ncrops: None\nheadline: BY EDWIN RUTT\n---\nEven II 1t had been carefully eX-\noiainCd to Mr.. Slyncniski that the\nWhite arrangement was merely Hill\nGly Charming, in frilly dress, fit\nhing about in search Of Jonah Lo-\ngan, his warped mind would have\nrefused to accept the fact. He was\nTar too stepped in Old Smoky and\nthe spirit world. To him that I'M\nEarthly pursuer represented only a\nlarge consignment Of ectoplasm,\nbent upon the destruction Of the\nmost important member OF the\nElyncniski family.\n\n\nMr.. Slyncniski's speed Of a mo-\nment before became but a circums\nstance. Now he moved as move the\ngazelle.\n\n\nHe reached the wall He found\nThe little door in 1t, for which\nMeath had given him key. The\nkey was unnecessary now, since\nhe had left the door open some\ntime earlier. With a gasp Of re-\nlief, MIR. Slyncniski quilted Wild\nover.\n\n\nAcross the road from the estate\nthere was a field. He gained 1t.\nBut halfway across the field, he\nslowed and glanced fearfully be-\nhind him.\n\n\nThere was no ectoplasm' on the\nhorizon. There was nothing any,\nwhere, except haystack. Pant\ning. and dripping perspiration\nafter the manner Of Douglas Cot\nterby, Mr.. Slyncniski sank into\nthe hay.\n\n\nAnd then, to his intense gratif1-\ncation, he became aware that he\nwas still holding the bottle of Old\nsmoky.\n\n\nLovingly, he raised it to his lips,\ndrank deep. 1t was another mis-\ntake. The next few moments\nwhirled by in haze and confusion.\nAt the end Of them, without cere-\nnaOny, without fanfare, Mr.. Slyn-\ncniski passed out.\n\n\nJonah\" said Hildy Charming.\nyou're pet\"\n\n\nMr.. Logan nodded in partial\nagreement. In his opinion. Hildys\nthinking was sound as far as 1t\nwent. But 1t didn't go far enough\n\n\nII mean,\" Hildy said. amplify-\ning It. that It's simply wonderful\nOf you to spare Mahoney to throw\nout those flares so that Chef can\nmake q safe landing. And it's\nwonderful Of you TO have thought\nup gag TO lure father to the Tai\nMahal. You're so clever, Jonah. !\ndon't know what ! would have\ndone without you\"\n\n\nsxeah?\" said Jonah dismalIy.\nElm wonderful. 81 right\"\n\n\nyou really are Q good guy,\nJonah\" Hildy said. Ell could\n\n\n.Weli, why don't your\" said\nJonah. q man who liked 10 i'm.\nprove Idle nomentG.\n\n\nSomewhat 70 his surprise, she\ndid Kiss him. Il was quite q kiss.\nMo Logan had preview OF\nsHWoman.\" he said. reeiing q iits\n&c. docs that come natural o. did\npoli learn Il ~ e0liege7\"\n\n\nDon't be coarse. jonah\" said\nEildy. 4 Just wanted 10 ali0W you\nthat I really like you. IN fact\nslle iiealtaied and, IN the darkness,\nAfF eyes were Ike tiny pools \"\nHAi18rwHw0a, sometimes Wish\nanal EIaet Wefen'f coming AZ aii.\"\n\n\n~ keEan's heaft, normally q\n\n\nrun organ. fufnea . hand=\n\n\n=== 1_1899-09-09_pna_sn85049554_00416159300_1899090901_0286.json\nEvening times-Republican. [volume] (Marshalltown, Iowa) 1890-1923 · 1899-09-09\ncrops: None\nheadline: CHALLENGE TO MINISTER'S.\n\nspiritualists Offer to Indulge In a\n\n\nJoint Debate With the Preachers.\n---\nspiritualists Offer to Indulge in a\n\n\nJoint Debate With the Preachers.\n\n\nAt the Spiritualist camp Mrs. Carrie\nFuller Weatherford will lecture tonight\nupon the \"Hells and Heavens of Spirit\nLife\" and also give a song improvisa\nfrom subjects selected by skeptics\nShe is to be followed by the renowned\ntest medium. Mr.. Max Hoffman. of Chi\ncago, who will deliver spirit messages\nand descriptions.\n\n\nThe camp management, in order to\nenlighten the people upon the identity\nof bible and modern spiritualism, here\nby extend most earnest and cordial\ninvitation to any orthodox or liberal\nclergyman to meet Mrs. Weatherford,\nwho is a noted lecturer, upon the ros-\ntrum of any church or Spiritualist asso-\ncitation of this vicinity in debate, the\ntopic to be discussed to be: Resolved\nThat spiritual gifts as described by\nPaul in the 18th chapter of First Cor\ninthians, King James version. are iden-\nteal in fact and origin with the spirit\nual gifts among media today\" said dis\ncussion to be held under the usual rules\nof debate and on or before September\n%. 1899.\n\n=== 36_1907-09-19_p1_sn84038335_00415622788_1907091901_0309.json\nMissouri Valley times. [volume] (Missouri Valley, Iowa) 1874-1931 · 1907-09-19\ncrops: None\nheadline: STRIKES AT GHOST\nOF HIS VICTIM AND\n\n\nINJURIES CELLMATE\n---\nAbba. la, Sept, Is. i:ailey Rlack,\nnegro in Jail for the murder of John\ncoleman in a fight following a poker\ngame. sees the ghost of his victim each\nnight. Black rises screaming from his\nbunk and seeks protection Of the other\nnris oners. Last night he attacked an\nother prisoner with a guitar, supposing\nhe was striking the ghost. severely In.\nturing him.\n\n=== 17_1915-01-22_p1_sn83025182_00295875161_1915012201_0062.json\nThe daily Gate City. [volume] (Keokuk, Iowa) 1855-1916 · 1915-01-22\ncrops: None\nheadline: ST. Paul \"Phantom\" Now Has Best\nClaim to Middle Weight Cham\npionship of the\n\n\nWorld.\n\nIN A CLASS BY HIMSELF\n\nMike Took Seven of Ten Rounds by\nBig Marein-other Three\n\n\nWere About\nEven.\n\nby James O'Dea, written for the\n\n\nUnited Press.1\n---\nby james O,Dea, written for the\n\n\nUnited Press.l\n\n\nMILWAUKEE,, wis, Jan. 90\nJimmy Clabby. claimant of the middle\nweight title, knows more about box\ning today than he did yesterday. That\nis, il he profited any by the lesson\ngiven him last night by Mike Gibbons.\n\n\nThe st. Paul phantom\" certainly\nmaterialized. He out-boxed, out-slug-\nged and out-fought the Hammond boy.\nHe demonstrated to the 5000 men and\nwomen who crowded the auditorium\nthat he is in class by himself.\nClabby never had chance and was\nlucky to hola Gibbons even In three\nof the ten rounds. The other seven\nwere Mikes by so big a margin that\nmost of Clabby's partisans conceded\nthem.\n\n\nThe first two periods were produc-\ntive of the prettiest boxing ever seen\nin these parts In the third, Gibbons,\nwith a series of lefts and rights to\nthe head. punched all the cleverness\nout of his opponent. After that 1t\nwas merely a question OF how much\npunishment Clabby could stand up\nunder.\n\n\nMike repeatedly jabbed his light\nming left into Clabby's face without a\nreturn. The fourth and fifth were all\nMikes. In the sixth. Clabby changed\nhis tactics by working into close quar-\ntiers. covering up and whaling away\nwith both hands. This period was\nfairly even\n\n\nIn the seventh. eighth ano ninth\nEFa55y took beautiful beating. The\nonly eood bow he landed was right\nswing that cut Gibbons eye. Clabby\nIVES bleeding a. the mouth AID nose.\nThe last round Clsbby fought desper-\nateIE and they beth slugged all over\nthe ring. Nike had the best of every\nexchange and It was his round and\nhis fight\n\n\nJoe yelling. the Chicago light\nweight. dropped Battling Terry OF\nNew Castle, Pa. three times in the\nsemi final. Each time Terry took the\ncount of nine.\n\n\nEddie McGoorty, Billy Murray and\nBob Moha were at the ring side and\nchallenged the winner Of the main\nevent.\n\n=== 9_1890-07-18_pna_sn85038614_00175031029_1890071801_0078.json\nRichmond dispatch. [volume] (Richmond, Va.) 1884-1903 · 1890-07-18\ncrops: None\nheadline: How Shall Our Delegates Be Elected\n---\nHow Shall Our Delegates Be Elected =?\n\n\nAs between ward and precinct meetings\nfor the election of delegates to the Con-\nvressional Nominating Convention OF this\ndistrict, we yesterday expressed prefer\nence for the latter mode.\n\n\nThere are twenty-four voting precincts\nIN this city and there would be q meeting\nfor each. No voter would have to go far\nfrom home The people, divided into two\ndozen instead of six sssemblages, would\noyercrowd no hall, and with a correspond\nincly reduced number of ballots TO count,\nthe proceedings would be short. At each\nOf these popular gatherings chairman,\nsecretary, and other officers would have 70\nbe elected. ard thus the honors OF the party\ncould be more widely distributed than by\nthe old way. What is still more important\nin this arrangement, the sentiments OF the\nDemocrats of Richmond could be voiced\nbetter than by ward-meetings.\n\n\nA primary election is also suggested and\nably urged. 1t would be superior to the\nptecinct-meeting plan, provided 1t could\nhe adopted all over the district. If the\ncounties approve it this city certainly will\nnot object, but uniformity is desirable.\n\n\nOn one occasion-possibly on more than\none-when nomination for Congress was\nTO be made. we opened a primary poll at\neach yoting-place in the district, and the\ncandidates were voted for directly, and\nupon aggregating the votes in each county\nor city the number of delegates each can\ndidate was entitled to appoint was ascer-\ntanned and declared.\n\n\nAt this critical point of our country's\nhistory. when reckless majority IN Con-\ngress is seeking to bind us 80 that no future\nefforts of ours will be availing TO liberate\nus from Republican domination, we want\nnot only the greatest unity. but the great\nest enthusiasm in our party. Pluck, spirit,\nand organization can safely carry us\nthrough the election force bill or no. We\nmust aim and work in this direction all the\ntime.\n\n\nWe desire to see our folks preparing to\nwin over to their party all the white Re\npublicans in Virginia who revere the\nhonor, principles. and traditions of this old\nState. and who detest such tyranny as has\nbeen practiced by the majority in Congress\nduring this session. The idea that An cleo\nton bill can manacle the true sons Of the\nOld Dominion will be dissipated the mo-\nment it is found that such measure will\nsoldify the white vote. We have faith in\nour people. We know their love for Vir-\nginia. We trust to this for escape from the\nnet work Of rascality proposed IN the\nRowEII-LoDGE bill. We must plead with\nthe whites personally and politically. Ina\nsomewhat similar emergency they rallied\nand overthrow MALONE. and as HARRISON\naud REED are but little better men they\nwill be doomed to the same fate.\n\n=== 25_1920-11-02_p1_sn89053713_00393343801_1920110201_0740.json\nAtlanta tri-weekly journal. [volume] (Atlanta, Ga.) 1920-19?? · 1920-11-02\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nA mans idea of time is so fluctu-\nating, that every wife needs a ouija\nboard around the house to tell her\nexactly when her husband will come\nin, evenings. and when to put the\nsteak in the oven.\n\n=== 2_1919-11-23_p1_sn83030214_00206532452_1919112302_0761.json\nNew-York tribune. [volume] (New York [N.Y.]) 1866-1924 · 1919-11-23\ncrops: None\nheadline: nd'lrngg Mystery of the Ouija Board Reincarnation\n---\nabout the child and expresses de-\nsires as to what should be done\nwith her.\n\n\nThe ouija board, while the appar-\nent instrument for conveying the\ncommunications of Patience, has\nnow become more a habit than a\nnecessity with Mrs. Curran, she\ndeclares. Patience talks to her\nwithout it, but she believes the\nboard helps her to concentrate and\nto keep her mind sufficiently blank.\nAS result of the recent Patience\nWorth meetings held here, there has\nbeen rush to the stores for ouija\nboards. Toy manufacturers are\nselling them at the rate of twenty\nday. The department stores are\nhaving an unusual demand for them.\n\n\nThe ouija board has the letters Of\nthe alphabet arranged on it in two\nconcentric arcs, with the ten numer-\nais below and Eyes\" and mo\" in\nscribed on the upper corners. The\nplanchette, or pointer, is a thin,\nheart-shaped piece of wood provided\nwith three legs upon which it moves\nabout on the board, its point indicat-\ning the letters of the words it is\nspelling. Two persons are needed\nto operate it. They hold the tips Of\nthe fingers lightly on the pointer\nand wait. Perhaps it moves; per-\nhaps it does not. Its powers have\nbeen attributed by some to super\nnatural influence; by others to SUBJ\nconsciousness. Scientists, for the\nmost part, treat it with disdain.\n\n=== 12_1868-01-11_pna_sn83030214_00206530881_1868011101_0073.json\nNew-York tribune. [volume] (New York [N.Y.]) 1866-1924 · 1868-01-11\ncrops: None\nheadline: .nrndant On oOfl 