to the spirits. Thereupon DR.. Ian.\nkester prosecuted Mr.. Slade for swindling,\nand in evidence produced Mr.. Maskelyne,\nan expert, who fully explained the \"slate\ntrick )\" and Mr.. 81 ade has been sentenced to\nthree months imprisonment.\n\n\nAt lecture delivered in Boston Music\nHall, on Saturday evening, Ash inst Mr..\nIrving Bishop, Of New York, went through\nthe whole range Of spifitualistic tricks,\nfully explaining that they were ali neither\nmore nor less than feats of jugglery. Some\nof the tests to WHICH be submitted, such as\nthe playing on instumenrs with his hands\nand feet tied in the cabinet, were very se-\nwe're, but we're all successfully performed.\nSince then Mr.. Bishop has made the round\nof the Boston mediums whom he exposes\nwith his usual ability. He is doing useful\nwork, for which he deserves the thanks of\nthe public.\n\n=== 2_1895-10-26_p1_sn99068076_00415623379_1895102601_0707.json\nThe Kimball graphic. (Kimball, Brule County, Dakota [S.D.]) 1883-1905 · 1895-10-26\ncrops: None\nheadline: Political\n---\nThe young enthusiast in politics went\nto offer his services for the campaign to\nan old wheel-horse who had seen serv-\nice aud done it, too, before the youth\nwas born.\n\n\n\"What can you do?\" asked the vet\neran.\n\n\nIl can raise my voice in defense Of\nprinciples AL over the state\" was\nthe proud reply.\n\n\n\"Um- UNI err yes err anything\nelse?\"\n\n\nIl can raise party clubs everywhere\"\nMISUNDERSTANDING elseP\"\n\n\nIl can raise the spirit of patriotism in\nevery village\"\n\n\n\"Um-yes-anvthing elseP\"\n\n\nu can raise the standard of revolt\nagainst corruption\"\n\n\n\"Ah! Um-ycs-anything elseP\"\n\n\nThe young mans enthusiasm was\nJared.\n\n\nGreat Caesar\" he exclaimed, ISN'T\nthat enough?\"\n\n\nHardly\"\n\n\n.What more could you want?-\n\n\nCan you raise $40. 00O?\" asked the\nveteran, in a profoundly yearning tone.\nand the enthusiast vanished with his\nenthusi y rot Free Press.\n\n=== 50_1914-03-02_pna_sn83045462_00280658881_1914030201_0282.json\nEvening star. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1854-1972 · 1914-03-02\ncrops: None\nheadline: SPIRITUALISM.\n---\nMEETINGS MONDAY, WEDNESDAY. FRIDAY\n\n\npm. sharp; q message each: daily read\niRgs. Mrs. J. T.. MAI.TBl. So1 Mi Vernon pi.\nNV. back Of Carnegie Library. Phone NJ sos.\n\n=== 7_1922-03-11_p1_sn85038022_00202195027_1922031101_0244.json\nThe public ledger. [volume] (Maysville, Ky.) 1913-1968 · 1922-03-11\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nOf forty miracle plays all celebrating\nin some way the interventi on of the\nBlessed Virgin, produced back in the\ndays when the theater had its begin\nnings in the church.\n\n\nNy\n\n\nHere are two disappointed immi-\ngrants to our shores who hold no\ngrievance against us because Of\nthreatened deportation. Mr.. and Mrs.\nFilippo Quattrocchi landed en- Ellis\nIsland from Italy recently. Because\nthe husbands health is not good, de-\nportattion was ordered. During an ap-\npeal from this edict, they have re-\nmanned at the immigration station\nthere on the Island and the other day\nwhen a daughter was born to them,\ntthey named her Ellis. That seems\nlike full-hearted appreciation for half\nhearted hospitality. rather hope the\ngovernment will decide to stretch a\npoint and let them stay here.\n\n\nNy\n\n\nPeriwinkle and mimosa are the new\nand popular colors for spring and\nsummer. That was made plain at the\nannual meeting here of the Textile\nColor Card Association Of the United\nStates. Periwinkle is that new Law\nendar-y blue and mimosa is an ex-\nceedingly dainty yellow.\n\n\nNy\n\n\nThere are more ways than one OF\nshifting responsibility. Fo rinstance,\nthe ouija board. Down in Greenwich\n\n=== 5_1837-03-25_p4_sn83030311_00271743117_1837032501_0297.json\nThe herald. · 1837-03-25\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n\n\n---\nLene'y and glsony was the path\nWhich led him through he wilderness\nNo human sound 10 him W, heard\nNought 10 cheer the d.smalshadc\nSave the rumbing winds oil heaven\nThrough the forest swiftly drives-\nNo fre-d was near him 10 rcga'e\nExhausted na'mes sasmy tae.\nNow dreaming on d spell was broken,\nAad a new scene before him cpen'd-\ngarden ofreses 10 him appears,\nTO suit all fashions, taste nd love\nNow his fastdioas one\nEach was fragrant and thorny TOO\nSO passing on, a change of srene\nDisclosed the phantom was dream.\nAnn., you alone to my iastisious taste impSu\nThs excelencies OF noble heaIt-\nThough shwars and Disappointed IN my cxpecta'ioh'\nThe hope OF return is my consolation\n\n\nWILLIAM\n\n=== 39_1891-06-25_p1_sn94053256_00271763098_1891062501_0619.json\nWaterbury evening Democrat. · 1891-06-25\ncrops: None\nheadline: THE RUSSIAN DOCTOR.\n\nIL tsrtShc; ent T?Rornniitic Story\n\n\nfrom Real Lifo.\n---\ntApAPrED PROM TUE GERNAN OF MIME\nELrsE poETE.1\n\n\n\"Uncle told you that our guest had\nled the usual frivolous life Of rich\nyoung men in st. Petersburg\" said\nDesirec one day to Marianne, as they\nsat together in the garden, and the\nyoung girls deft fingers fashioned q\nbreakfast cap of true Partisan elegance.\n\"What did he mean by thatP\" she\nadded, bending low over her work and\nblushlng deeply.\n\n\n\"He must have meant that the\nyoung baron had done little else than\nfit from one ball and assembly to an\nothor-that he squandered much money\nand INDUCED in countless flirtations.\nOne must not look deeply into the\nlives of men. There are few model\nmon like my cousin\"\n\n\nbut Ussikow looks melancholy and\ni'll. can not understand why he has\nfound no woman to love him and be\nloved by him in return\"\n\n\n\"Hc has found plenty, dear child,\nbut such love lasts on neither side\"\n\n\n\"A love that can not remain true is\nno love at all. This poor man has not\nfound the love he sought\"\n\"Hilmar is not in the least to be\npiticd, you foolish child. He is happy\nand content. He will some day marry\nrich wife. l do not understand why\nwomen think him handsome. He can\nin no way compare with my cousin,\nwho has the only beauty ncedful in a\nman-that of perfect health.\n\n\n\"But uncle has gray hair and his\nwhiskcrs begin\"--\n\n\nTHAT is because he has undergone\nso many fntigues,\" interrupted Mari\nanne, \" because he still studies so\nhard He is in all ways the superior\nof Hilmar Won Ussikow. Now let me\ntry on the cap before the glass. 1t is\ncharming. But that ribbon over the\ntop must come off. 1t looks too coquet-\nfish for me-\"\n\n\nThe same hour found Hilmar and\nArnim roaming through the forest, and\ntalking over Hilmar's choice of his fut-\nure home. 1.-The physicians recommend\nthe environs of Lake Geneva\" said the\ninvalid.\n\n\nTHAT is the best air for your nerves.\nreplied Arnim. I'm winter you can &C\nover the Alps to Lake Como. With\nproper care and the right atmosphere\nyou may soon be restored to perfect\nhealth. Most of all, you need rest. \"\n\n\nwherever have sought rest it has\nseemed that of the church-yard. How\nhappy you are. What q housekeepcl\nyou have in Marianne\"\n\"Docs she please youP\"\n\n\n~ envy the man to whose comfort\nshe ministers\"\n\n\n\"Thcn WOO a'f win her. She may\nbe the wife you tccd.'\n\n\nPOSSIBLY. But here we are in my\nfavorite haunt, the forest-meadow\njust look ut that enchanting group o\n} old berchcs! Trees grow more bcauti\nfull and interesting with age. Pity it i\nnot so with men! l would like t.\nsketch little here\"\n\n\nThen 1 will go back to the house\nDo not stay ont late, Hilmar.\"\n\n\nArnim Elbthal walked slowly home\nmoderate us was his step, his hear\nbeat violently. He half regretted hav\ning so spoken to his friend, yet tn\ny thought that Dcsirce might one da\ntake Marianne's place in his house fill\nell him with quiet happiness. Wher\n= could she be more safely guarded\nmore lovingly chershed-this preciou\njewel of his lost llortense-than in tn\n' vine-wrcathcd house where her mothe\nhad passed such halcyOn hoursP\n\n\n\"Hcaven has arranged ALL\" cried\nvoina in biS hOaTt have hnf\ncalmly wait\"\n\n\nFrom this day the doctor SCENE\nmore absorbed in his work. Him:\nwas given over mostly to the societ\nof Marianne and Desiree.\n= 1t gave him pleasure to know tn;\nhis guest took daily walks with I'll\ntwo women, that he sat with them @\nthe terrace, reading aloud by the hou\nthat his conversation was mostly wit\nw. Marianne. What would Papillon sa\n1t when called upon to greet Marianne\n\n\nHilmar's betrOthed?\n\n\nThe morning shadows fell oblique\non the strawberry bed, a light viii\nwas stirring. Papillon went with\n\n\nlittle basket on her arm to gather Il\n\n\nwonted store. She had thrown bat\n\n\nher straw hat. Her brow was though\n? full. her head bent forward. Steps Il\n\n\npreached.\n\n\ntn \"May help, DesireeP\" asked H\n'st mars voice.\n\n\n1ts She turned toward him face 81\n,e. fused with bushes, and placed h\nst. forefinger on her lip.\n\n\n\"Hush!' she whispered. 81 W\n\n\nhow you a happy family\"\n\n=== 2_1907-10-04_pna_sn82014248_00513682426_1907100401_0051.json\nDaily Kennebec journal. [microfilm reel] (Augusta, Me.) 1870-1975 · 1907-10-04\ncrops: None\nheadline: STATE sPiRITUALIsT Asso.\n\nWill Hold Annual Meeting in City To\n\n\nday, Saturday and Sunday.\n---\nThe annual session of the Maine\n\n\nstate Spiritualist Association will open\n\n\nin this city to-day and continue through\n\n\nsaturday and sunday. The Saturday\n\n\nand sunday sessions will be held in 6.\n\n\ny. Il. hall \\ practical method Of or\n\n\nganiZnS the several thousand Spirit\n\n\nualists Of tins State into a religious\n\n\nbody which shall be elastic, and at the\n\n\nsane time efficient for advancing the\n\n\ncause of >pirtualism, is the largest\n\n\nproblem with which the convention\n\n\nwill have to Srapple.\n\n\nMrs. Nettie Holt Harding of Somer-\n\n\nVile. Mass, formerly Il Maine girl. one\n\n\nof the best psychics in New England,\n\n\nwill hr present and her lectures and\n\n\nmessages will add greatly to the inter-\n\n\nest in the convention. Mr. Arthur\n\n\nSmith. formerly with the banner Of\n\n\nLight\" and Mrs. Minerva A. Barwise\n\n\nof Bangor. will each deliver Il lecture.\nBoth are speakers of Well known abil-\nity. Mrs. Maud Could of Bangor. for\nyears recognized as a fine psychic.\nwill follow some of the lectures with\n\n\ntest work. The convention from the\n\n\nstandpoint of talent as we'll as from\nits business side. bids fair to be Il no.\ntable one.\n\n\nAll day Saturday will be devoted to\nthe business sessions. In the forenoon\nwill occur the reports Of the state Of\nficerS and the appointment of commit\ntees. 1t is said that the presidents adj\ndre.S will advocate the establishment\n\n\nof permanent fund something after\n| the manner Of the fund established by\n| the UnivcrsaliSts 40 or 80 years ago.\n| Then. also. it is thought in view of\nthe increased powers Of the state as\ns\"ciatian as to charterine local socic-\n| ties. that 2 definite and energetic cam\n| paign will be urged toward the organ\n| iza;ion of local societies in all the large\n| towns, as Augusta Belfast. Portland\nana Madison have already done.\n\n\nThen there is the question of Il def\n\n\nmite and final agreement between the\nMaine State Spiritualist Association\nand the Nationalist Spiritualist Assoc\ncitation, which is one of the most inn\n\n\npcrative questions Of the convention,\n| will come up for a thorough discussion.\nThe presence Of every SpiritualiSt in\n| Maine whether or not a member of the\nSTATE association is earnestly desired at\n| all the sessions. as there is real work\nto be done and Il large field to cover.\nSunday forenoon, afternoon and\n\n\nevening. Will be devoted to lectures\nand something Of the phenomena. All\n| liberals and whoever may be interest\ned in a rational religion based upon ma\ntural phenomena will be made well\ncome ut All the lectures.\n\n=== 10_1937-10-29_p3_sn92070146_00414188837_1937102901_1025.json\nImperial Valley press. · 1937-10-29\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\n1ueeIS, not lar from the scene OF\nthe \"red circle\" murders.\n\n\nMrs. Guggenheim and John Fry,\njr., % year old employe on Guggen-\nhelms Firenze farm at Babylon,\nLong Island, walked into diner\nat Mineola and reported they were\nattacked and robbed of $461 by two\nheavy set men as they left Felecci's\nCommercial club on Springfield\nboulevard.\n\n\nTheir clothes were torn and both\nwere bleeding from wounds. They\nsaid they had started to enter their\nautomobile, parked two blocks from\nthe restaurant, when the men ap-\npeared\n\n\nThe men dragged Fry away from\nthe car, he said, hit him on the\nhead, kicked him and took his wal\nlet containing $751; The bandits\nthen struck Mrs. Guggenheim on\nthe chin, knocking her down, and\n\n=== 10_1901-05-18_pna_sn86086499_00414214502_1901051801_0268.json\nWauwatosa news. [volume] (Wauwatosa, Wis.) 1900-1948 · 1901-05-18\ncrops: None\nheadline: A Swap Party.\n---\nAll those women who are in the habit\nOf haunting bargain tables in the vain\nhope of getting something for nothing,\nwill be delighted TO hear of this new\nscheme in the way of entertainment\n\n\n1t is called a Swap Party\n\n\nNot a very elegant title, it is true, but\nyour friends will probably forgive that\nafter the party is over Gf not beforel\nand declare that they never had SO much\nfun in their lives.\n\n\nThe invitations should read something\nlike this: Mr. and Mrs. John Street re-\nquest the pleasure of your company at\nSwap Party on Friday, May the third\nat half-paSt o'clock. The swapping will\nbegin promptly at and YOU are request\ned to bring Il package containing some\nthing which you are willing TO part\nwith\"\n\n\n1t is highly probable that your friends\nwill take advantage of this kind permits\nsion to get rid Of the most undesirable\nthings in their possession.\n\n\nBut no matter; the more outlandish the\narticle the more fun its owner will have\nin exchanging it for something else.\n\n\nThe packages should be securely\nwrapped and at a given signal the swap\nping\" begins.\n\n\nThe person who succeeds in obtaining\nsome othes persons donation in eX-\n| change for her own is privileged tU15ben\n] it and if not satisfied may rewrap and\n| swap it off for another thing. This\n| process continues until the person is SAT-\n| sfied with what he has or until the bell\nannounces that the contest is closed\n| Nc prizes are necessary. Each person\n! keeps his latest swap.\n\n=== 20_1870-06-08_pna_sn87070095_00296022688_1870060801_0260.json\nThe weekly standard. [volume] (Raleigh, N.C.) 1869-18?? · 1870-06-08\ncrops: None\nheadline: A Spectre at Sea.\n---\nIn the year 1865 | was in command OF\n\n\nthe Dolphin. fine barque of 600 tons. We\n\n\nhad been on whaling voyage, and had Obj\n\n\ntlincd an unusual good cargo of n,l, both\n\n\nin quality and quantity. With our course\n\n\nlaid for home, the crew in good health, and\n\n\nfair prospect of per centa9e money on the\ncargn, what more could We want t\" keep\n\n\nthe song and The Jest on the lips ot The\n\n\ncrew2 Time pissed quickly alnn@. \" HON.\n\n\nward bound\" has an intensified meaning to\n\n\nthe whaler's ciew, and US We Lowlcd along\n\n\nwill, Il good breeze, each day getting near\n\n\nCT to our homes and firesidcs. there were few\n\n\non the vessel who lid not feel their spirits\n\n\nrising with each days progress.\n\n\nOne night about eleven O'Clock was sit\n\n\nting at the upper end of my cabin table,\n\n\nconsulting chart on which the vessels\ncourse was laid down. had been reading\nDante's \"Inferno,\" and the horrible nature\n\n\nof the work aroused my sensitive feelings\nto such an extent that had determined to\ntry how far cigar and walk on deck\n\n\nwould steady my nerves. Just as | was\nabout rising from my chair to go on deck\nnoticed figure descending the companion\n\n\nway before me- A single glance told me\n\n\nthat it was not one of my crew. No one\nof my well-fed, sleek men could possibly\nlook as miserable as this figure looked,\n| even at the distance at which | saw him.\n| Slowly he descended the steps, grasping the\nhand-rail to support himself, as ii he were\ntoo feeble to descend without supports\nHis back was bent, and his head lay forward\non his brast, as il he was carefully watch\n| ing his footsteps; his feet were bare, while\nhis head was envclopcl in Il piece of old\nsail-cl\"tli instead of cap. His left arm\n| hung down by his side carefully rolled up\nin his coat, which had evidently been taken\n| off Tor that purpose, and the arm itself ap-\n| peared to he broken. Quietly, and without\n! uttering word he approached Inc, and at\nlast sat down at the opposite end of the ia\n\n\nble which was my accustomed place He\n| then slowly raised his head, and sight was\npresented to my astonishcd gaze the like of\nwhich hope may never sec again. A fine,\n| noble face it was, but attcnuated by disease\nand suffering, or perhaps both. The features\nhad been good and regular, but now the\n| checks wcrc sunkcn and hollow; the teeth,\nwhite and even, were firmly set together,\nwhile the tbln, parched lips were drawn\nback from them. The eyes were as black rs\ncoal, but sunk far back into the head, and l\nsaw that they were at once fixed on me,\nwith dull, unmeaning stare. The figure\nnow stOod up just Opposite me, and felt\nmyself bound to my seat, without the power\nto address it. Having looked at me tixedly\nfor some time, the figure walked up to my\nside till it touched my shoulder.\n\n\nReaching out lis arm, he laid IL bony fin\nger on the chart which was on the table, and\nbefore could muster courage to articulate,\n10! he hi d disappeared.\n\n\nRecovering my Gomposure somewhat, l\n| sprang with hound up the companion-Hay,\nand shouted for the mate. He speedily re\npiled to my call, and came aft, drag\nging something after him which kept\nup continual howling. As he came nearer\nto me ] saw that it was my Labrador dog\nCasper, and inquired the meaning of what\nhe was doing. why, SIR\" lle replied, \"thc\ndog lay on the companion-way hatch, until\nu few minutes ago, app pIrently sound asleep,\nwhen all of II sudden he sprang up with\nhorrible howl, and ran to his kennel forward\nwith his tail between his legs, shaking with\nfright. | went forward after him to his ken\nmel, an when put my hand in 10 pat him\nhe attempted to bite me. Hearing you call,\n| dragged him ait with ME 10y his collar, to\nsee if nc had been hurt in some manner. for\n/ meyer saw him act SO before\"\n\n\ndid not examine the terrified dog felt\nthat he had seen my visitor rs well as my\nself With some difficulty sootllcd him,\nand then went below. Walking up to ny\ntable cast look down At the chart At\nthe exact spot where my visitor had placed\nhis atienua;c,l finger saw dull inark,\nand, on closer examination, | perceived\na.. \"a \"A\nindicatcd u postum un the chart which MAS\nabout day S saiI frsinI, and right an:l,s 10\nour course. For two hours | paced thu.\n| deck, wondering what had happened. The\n| visitor was surely no creation of my own\n| Imagination. for the dog had seen the fig\nure well myself, and the spot un the\n| chart was plain evidence What could it\nmean half determined 10 ask the mates\n| advice. lie was firm believer in spiritual\nmanifestations, and would cndr:lvor To solve\n| the enigma i'll some way or other. But\n| then had always ridiculed his notions, anil\n| it was too Calling to my pride tn ask his adj\n| vice now on that of which ! had always dc\n| nicd the existence, save in II Icatcd imagi-\nnation; sD | determined to keep my own\n| counsel. Turning in sonn after, slept well\n| and awoke refreshed determined in my\nown mind to say nothing and think no\nmore about it.\n\n\nWhen got tn deck found that We were\nhccalmed. Not ripple was ON the water.\n| The day passed away monotonously enough.\n| The crew slept. Tread and smoked: anti\n| II o'clock that night found Inc in my chair\n| at the head of the table in my cabin. Gas\nper- who had quite got over his fears, lay\n| ON the hatch at the head 0f the cabin stairs.\nIcoud hear the pat oi his tail, as u greeting\n| to the mate as he kept passing him in his\nwalk up and down the deck. This evening\nmy thoughts were tranquil, Tor was read\ning volume of Longfelow's poems, and\nwas in the midst of g Evangclinc,\" when l\nheard a howl from Casper\" and the scurry\nof his feet as he scampercd forward. Look\ning up l saw my visitor Of the previous\nnight again descending the companion way.\nHe st cm,l mitch weaker, and came slowly\n| down, clutching the hand rail with his one\nsound arm and hand. lie glanccd al the\n| table with anxious looks. as il to sre\n| whether The Chart was still there. Ii was\nnot. He then gaZed at me with Il sorrowful\nand disappointed stare and disappeared. l\nremained quietly in my chair about ten\nminutes after the spectre had departed.\nWhen went on deck found the mate\nagain cndeavorina tn soothc the dog, who\nsnapped and sriaricl in his kennel, until it\nwas unsafe 10 approach him. Leaving him\n| there, went below, and throwing myself,\ndressed MS was, on my cot, tried tn sleep.\nUnrefreshing and feyerish was the sleep\nwhich visited me that night, and / rose\nin the morning totally unfit for work of any\nkind, and with my nerves unstrung. What\nwas expected of me? For what purpose\nwas wanted What must 1 do? Thus\nmused the entire day. Casper kept close\nto his kennel all the time and steadily re-\nfused to cat or drink. When any of us APP\npreached his kennel he attempted to snap\nat us, and appeared as if entirely under the\n\n\nInfluence of some great fear. The calm\n\n\nhad continued the entire day, and we had\n\n\nmade no progress whatever. Again evening\n\n\napproached, and the same hour found nc,\n\n\nas on the two previous nights, sitting at my\n\n\ntable. This time eagerly awaited my Wis\n\n\nitor. Would the figure appear? found\n\n\nmyself hoping that it would This time |\n\n\ndid not attempt to read, for my thoughts\n\n\nwere too troubled to permit If my book.\n\n\nThe same chart, with the blood stain ON it,\n\n\nlay before me. Would my visitor. if he\n\n\ncame again touch that spot, ur would he\n\n\ngive me some other sign which might in.\n\n\ndicatc to Inc the course l ought To pur\n\n\nsue. This time there was no Caspcr\n\n\nto warn me. | felt the figure was\n\n\ncoming before it appeared Presently\n\n\nsaw it coming down the stairs. This time\n\n\nit was evidently far weaker than before, fol\n\n\nwith difficulty could it stand, and it toiled\n\n\ndown the steps in manifest suffering and\n\n\nagony. At last it reached the cabin door\n\n\nand attempted TO advance to me; but ne\n\n\nsooner had it let 90 the hand rail than il\n| sank cyhaustcd / suranm LII and rilsLlG\n\n=== 82_1906-03-29_pna_sn88085947_00211108563_1906032901_0558.json\nThe Spokane press. [volume] (Spokane, Wash.) 1902-1939 · 1906-03-29\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nhaunts after the wreck. The story\ngoes that he stayed on duty for the\nnight operator after working all\nday himself. The railroad company\ndeclares that the action was purely\nvoluntary on his part and that he\nhad no business to work for the\nother man, nor the other man to\nallow him to take his place. a\"\n\n=== 5_1919-06-01_pna_sn83045389_00296029233_1919060101_0387.json\nRichmond times-dispatch. [volume] (Richmond, Va.) 1914-current · 1919-06-01\ncrops: None\nheadline: CHICAGO PACKER DECLARES\n\n\nHUNGER HAUNTS EUROPE\n\nSays France. Germany and Italy In\nWorst Stnte-LfttIc Live\n\n\nstock Remains.\n---\nNEW YORK. MAY 3I--Europe is on\nThomas L. Wilson. tho Chicago packer\nwho arrived here today after SIX\nweeks survey OF tho European food\nsituation.\n\n\nFrance, Germany and Italy are in\nthe worst state\" Mr. Wilson said\nThere is doubt that Germany is\n$500 square miles found only 200\nhorses, 900 cattle and 1900 hogs or\none-haf horse and one-hali head OF\nmile.\n\n\nThe markets OF the world are now\nopen 10 the United States. But in\nGreat Britain we have rival IN a\nstrong position who 