{"parentUuid":"68ebcef0-e905-4a26-a9b8-5b7b26135d91","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"775e54ef-6487-4f79-8598-643421b87718","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WURrFiBEgz8YJi3rGgXHYu","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t=== 22_1920-01-04_p49_sn83030313_00271744080_1920010401_0099.json\n2\tThe New York herald. · 1920-01-04\n3\tcrops: None\n4\theadline: 'N|CKNAMES' OF\n5\tBRITISH M.P.'S\n6\t\n7\t\n8\tBADGES OF HONOR\n9\t\n10\tGladstone's Son Known as\n11\t\"Tuppence\" and Lloyd\n12\t\n13\t\n14\tGeorge as 81. G.\"\n15\t\n16\tBy +. p. O'cONNOR.\n17\t---\n18\t! By T. p. O'CoNNOR.\n19\t\n20\t\n21\t[Special Despatch to the Hcrold. Copy\n22\t\n23\t\n24\tright, 1920, by the Philadelphia Public\n25\t\n26\t\n27\tLedger Co. Az Rights Rescroed.]\n28\t\n29\t\n30\tLONDON. Dec. II.\n31\t\n32\t\n33\tIt is rather a distinction in the House OF\n34\tCommons to have more nicknames than\n35\tany other member. for the House has an\n36\textraordinary love for clubbing manor\n37\t\"A party-with a nickname. Nobody knows\n38\thow these nicknames arise, and they pass\n39\tINTO the vernacular of the smoke rooms\n40\twith extraordinary rapidity. In my young\n41\tdays Sir Stafford Northcote. the Tory\n42\tleader. who had long beard, was always\n43\tknown as 1.-The Goat\" and Sir William\n44\tHarcourt. who was man Of huge bulk\n45\tand great height, was spoken OF as\n46\tJumbo\n47\t\n48\t\n49\tThe favorite conundrums today In the\n50\tsmoke room are First, Why is the gov\n51\ternment like an immoral goose? Because\n52\t1t is untrue to .ts proper gander tpropa-\n53\tganda>. The second and better conundrum\n54\tis, WHAT IL t0e plutdl w wv\n55\tGeddes, a play upon the fact that the\n56\tGeddes brothers are chosen by Lloyd\n57\tGeorge apparently to be the superman OF\n58\tevery hard Job that he takes In hand. one\n59\tOf the nicknames for one Of the brothers-\n60\tq play upon his Christian name OF AUCKLAND\n61\tard on his somewhat portentous appear\n62\tance, with his dark complexion, his rather\n63\ttulging eyes and his prominent nose-ls\n64\t.the Great AUX\" Herbert. now Loro\n65\t\n66\t\n67\t~\n68\tGladstone, was known at Eton school al\n69\tways as \"Tuppence.\" He happened to oe\n70\tat the school at the moment when his IL\n71\tlustrious father had just added two-pence\n72\tto the income tax, and fy that name ne\n73\twas known to his old school fellows when\n74\tthey and he were members OF the House OF\n75\tCommons.\n76\t\n77\t\n78\tAnother habit Of the House is to call men\n79\twith longish names by the initials OF tneir\n80\tChristian names, or by the 1irst letter Va\n81\ttheir surname. Gladstone was always\n82\tspoken of as Mr.. c.; Sir Henry Camp\n83\tbell Bannermann as &C. b.; Thomas wal\n84\tlace Russell, once prominent politician\n85\tnow in retirement as a hotel proprietor IN\n86\tDublin, was always called IT. W.. neeu\n87\tnot repeat the initials by which am al\n88\tways addressed by political friends ana\n89\tfoes in the House. The Prime Minister IS\n90\tusually spoken Of as Il. g.\n91\t\n92\t\n93\tBenn Has Most Nicknames.\n94\t\n95\t\n96\tBut the member who has the most nick\n97\tnames is Captain Wedgwood Benn. He IS\n98\tnot very much liked by his Tory oppon-\n99\tents. so their title for him is the somewhat\n100\topprobrious one Of Buttons\" A more\n101\tpopular and respectful name is that OF\n102\tLittle Ben\" The great clock In the tower\n103\tof the House OF Commons, as everybody\n104\tknows. is known as big Ben,\" and this\n105\tsuggests often the application Of Its name\n106\tto members Of the House. There were two\n107\tBentincks once in the House OF commons\n108\tone big and the other small; they we're\n109\tknown accordingly as Big Zen and\n110\tLittle Ben\" -\n111\t\n112\t\n113\twhen you see wedgwood Ben you are\n114\tnot surprised that he should suggest the\n115\tnickname of Buttons\" Though about\n116\tforty years OF age, he looks just the\n117\theight, size and age Of the boy Buttons or\n118\tthe English household. He Is scarcely five\n119\tfeet high. His body is as slender as\n120\tboys. His cheek Is peach blossom and the\n121\texpression Of the face. with rather conf\n122\teyes, is exactly the look OF the typicai\n123\tstreet gamin of London, with its shrewd-\n124\tness. its imperturbable good humor and its\n125\treadiness to take on any little job. Wedg-\n126\twood Benn might act the part OF Peter\n127\tPan on the stage. or perhaps the apter\n128\tanalogy is to Charlie Chaplin OF the films.\n129\tAnd here comes the curious part OF the\n130\tstory. Little Benn has proved himself one\n131\tOF the heroes of the war. He Is passion\n132\tately devoted to politics. and up to the\n133\twar never thought OF anything else. The\n134\tHouse Of Commons is as much his home as\n135\tIL he had been born in II. To men OF that\n136\tthere Is one fore-ordained\n137\tjob--a job which consists of vigilance to\n138\tthe least detail of House Of Commons life.\n139\twith an interest In gossip, a knowledge OF\n140\tthe haunts and the weaknesses OF every\n141\tmember. of the telephone number either\n142\tin his own home or somebody else's home\n143\tduring his absence from the House; :\n144\tpower to tabulate strictly. &c Those arc\n145\tthe functions Of the whips. So when his\n146\tparty came into power Wedgwood Benn\n147\twas appointed one OF the whips.\n148\t\n149\t\n150\t~Fiery Young Particle\"\n151\t\n152\t\n153\tYou could see him every night, while the\n154\tHouse of Commons was sitting, at the door\n155\tOf the inner lobby OF the House by which\n156\tmembers enter or leave, with a pencil\n157\tbehind his ear, a notebook in his hand, a\n158\tlook OF Interrogation In his eye and with\n159\this slight dapper boyish figure and his\n160\tspruce manner. looking just like the fiery\n161\tyoung particle\" as Gilbert and Sullivan\n162\tcall Il In the play.\n163\t\n164\t\n165\tThen came the war, and TO the surprise\n166\tOf everybody the eternal boy left the\n167\twhips room and the House OF Commons\n168\tand his country, and the next hearo OF\n169\thim was that he was at Gallipoli, and had\n170\tentered on all the perils and destinations\n171\tOf the airman's career. Stories occasional\n172\tly trickled home of feats OF his which\n173\tshowed quite reckless daring. Then he\n174\tcame home and stood again for Pariia-\n175\tment, and was again returned, and now\n176\tIs in the House of Commons. He has just\n177\tbrought out volume full OF wit and OF\n178\tstories of breakneck adventures told very\n179\tand almost casually.\n180\t\n181\t\n182\tA change has come in his fortunes with\n183\tthe upset In all the old lines Of political life\n184\twhich came with Lloyd Georges seizure\n185\tOf the dictatorship OF the House OF COM\n186\tmons, by the defeat Of all the really pow\n187\terful men who once confronted him. The\n188\tvacant places left in the ranks Of the lead\n189\ters Of the opposition gave the opportunity\n190\tto all the young fellows TO come along.\n191\tAnd thus II comes to pass that you can see\n192\tthe extraordinary and almost incredible\n193\tspectacle OF this Peter Pan getting UP from\n194\tthe most sacred OF all spots In the House\n195\tOF Commons. except the bench where the\n196\tmembers of the government cit. namely\n197\ttho front opposition bench. and leading.\n198\tfor the timo being. on some measure all\n199\tthe elderly middle aged men that form the\n200\tpresent Liberal party.\n201\t\n202\t\n203\tTho best Of 1t is. the speeches are quite\n204\t\n205\t=== 31_1894-01-12_p1_sn99062034_00279523313_1894011201_1045.json\n206\tThe Madison daily leader. [volume] (Madison, S.D.) 1890-current · 1894-01-12\n207\tcrops: None\n208\theadline: Underaakers Don't believe In spooks.\n209\t---\n210\tI An aged undertaken of this city was\n211\tasked il he had aver seen any spooks.\n212\t\n213\t\n214\tHe laughed derisiyely and said: mo,\n215\tand ! doubt il you will find any M in\n216\t\n217\t\n218\tmy line Of business who believes in the\n219\texistence of such things. We find there\n220\t10 nothing more harmless and pitiful\n221\tthan dead people. Philadelphia Rec\n222\tord.\n223\t\n224\t=== 6_1903-04-07_p2_sn84024827_0021110661A_1903040701_0047.json\n225\tBisbee daily review. · 1903-04-07\n226\tcrops: None\n227\theadline: A NIGHT WITH\n228\t\n229\t A MYSTERY\n230\t---\n231\tlCopyrlght, lSuS. Dy .. U. Warnet.l\n232\t\n233\t Thirty years ago a widow named\n234\t\n235\t Haynes, living in village about forty\n236\t\n237\t miles out of London, disappeared one\n238\t\n239\t afternoon and could not be traced. She.\n240\t\n241\t owned and was living in large stone\n242\t\n243\t house built hundred years before she\n244\t\n245\t bought it, and only portion of it\n246\t\n247\t was habitable.\n248\t\n249\t Here was a woman known by sight\n250\t\n251\t at least to four-fifths of the townspeo-\n252\t\n253\t pie. She lived on the best street. It\n254\t\n255\t did not seem that she could have walk\n256\t\n257\t ed block without meeting friend.\n258\t\n259\t She was in her sitting room at 2\n260\t\n261\t o'clock in the afternoon. An hour later\n262\t\n263\t she was missing. She had not dressed\n264\t\n265\t for the street-had not left the room. so,\n266\t\n267\t far as the maid could say. Scotland\n268\t\n269\t Yard men worked at the mystery for\n270\t\n271\t six months before they gave up in\n272\t\n273\t despair.\n274\t\n275\t Six months after the widows disap-\n276\t\n277\t pearance the house was for rent, al\n278\t\n279\t though stories were already current\n280\t\n281\t that it was haunted. The place was,\n282\t\n283\t first taken by grocer who was.\n284\t\n285\t known to everybody as a hard headed\n286\t\n287\t man with plenty of nerve, and yet aft\n288\t\n289\t CT six weeks residence in the house he\n290\t\n291\t moved out. He had leased 1t for a year. do not know what he may have told others. but he informed me. under promise of secrecy, that he be- ueved the house to be haunted. It was no use to ridicule his state,\n292\t\n293\t ments. for he was serious and earnest, The fact of his moving out proved that\n294\t\n295\t It was a year before any one else | took the house, and 1t was then yacat- | ed for good. Everybody now firmly, | believed in the stories of ghost, and. | though it was offered rent free to poor ! people, no one would move into it.\n296\t\n297\t When it was put up at auction sale. l there was only one bid, and 80 no sale, was effected.\n298\t\n299\t While ready to admit that half be- lieved in ''the curse\" I can truthfuIly declare that the stories reaching me from time to time regarding the house excited my contempt. For several months before my opportunity came was anxious to pass night there ands see what would occur. House and grounds had long been abandoned to tramps. but yet on certain occasions these tranps had made queer state-\n300\t\n301\t ments about what had happened there. The windows had been broken, the doors torn away. and the once fine old\n302\t\n303\t mansion was bad wreck when 1 in spected it one afternoon.\n304\t\n305\t The only two doors left intact were the sliding doors separating the par lors. The others had been used for fuel In the fireplaces. l told no one of my intentions, but at 10 o'clock that night, armed with a revolver. candle and matches. I returned to the house and sat down on the floor in corner of the back parlor. There was a doorway between this room and the dining room and one between that and the kitchen. It was warm night in summer, with never breath of wind. It was as quiet as a graveyard in the house and about the grounds. You must not think me boastful when 1 tell you that\n306\t\n307\t I was not even shaky. was some-\n308\t\n309\t what excited, but not at aii nervous In the first place, I believed that all the, Edu bttu and. in the ne:t. I was cf the opinioI. that some practical joker had hand\n310\t\n311\t in the business if there was anything l in it.\n312\t\n313\t From 10 to 1 o'clock nothing hap pered = nothing except my going to sleep. heard the town clock strike | F, and heard the first quarter chime\n314\t\n315\t I didn't feel sleepy. and yet dropped of, and about minute after awoke the clock struck 1. The sounds of fooi I steps awoke me. Some one came ~ the graveled walk from the gate, pass- | ed around to the kitchen door and enI tered. It was a shuffiing. dragging\n316\t\n317\t step. It passed across the kitchen. | passed across the dining room. and l firmly expected to see person fin the doorway. It was not so dark but that | could have made out even a rat. No one appeared. l crept on hands and knees to the doorway. No one could | eseen-\n318\t\n319\t=== 16_1921-04-16_pna_sn88064057_00280761771_1921041601_0501.json\n320\tAbbeville progress. (Abbeville, Vermilion Parish, La.) 1913-1944 · 1921-04-16\n321\tcrops: None\n322\theadline: \n323\t---\n324\t\n325\t= .,,..o,sr his disappointment and wourc\n326\t\n327\t\n328\tenrfors. | thought the physico,\n329\t,,..,-cr ro Jed was greater than eve\n330\tand found some pleasure in teiin-\n331\tuu $50.\n332\t\n333\t\n334\ty. WAS convinced of thai himself\n335\tand WAS not happy.\n336\t\n337\t\n338\t-Eaa don't You end your ra~cau,y:.\n339\t,r,..l him. why don't you give m.\n340\tr..py~rrpr ro Mrs. Sidney aud ano.\n341\tt.. ro ulaSc whatever disposition >t.\n342\t,,.,rt. nf il: Then your con-rf.r...\n343\twill me- ra-y-your position IN This\n344\tInns. will for the first time he toIrr;,.\n345\tbI. IA decent man, and your physi\n346\t,aI security will be promoted\"\n347\tHe would not. He seemed 10 hest.\n348\tfate for moment, but his purpose\n349\twas TOO long fixed and too much q\n350\tpart of his life.\n351\t\n352\t\n353\tHe no longer was .surly with me\n354\tand seemed to have lost my ability\n355\tI, eprare him. We parted with nv\n356\ttelling him that there would be no\n357\tnossihlr truce or peace between u\n358\tu,Iess he respected the women of the\n359\thousehold. He bowed.\n360\t\n361\t\n362\t\"Anything else, sir?\" he asked; and\n363\tthen ne departed as the servant.\n364\t\n365\t\n366\tMrs. Sidney's relief to find that\n367\tthe robbery had proved only hoax on\n368\tthe robbers was such as would come\n369\t| from escape from tangible horrors.\n370\t| The lady had been keeping control of\n371\therself. as was necessary to protect\n372\t/ her husband and daughter from hcf\n373\town agony of mind and to keen tn,\n374\thousehold from finding significance il\n375\twhat could be- passed over as a triy\n376\t| ial piece of robbery.\n377\t\n378\t\n379\twhen she learned that the alarm\n380\tWAS over. She relaxed Imply in he.\n381\t/ chair. and | feared that She Nigh\n382\t! collapse: but in moment she har\n383\t| STRUGGLED back to command of herself\n384\t/ Then She excused herself and wen\n385\t/ into her hcdroom-fOr prayer, | knew\n386\t\n387\t\n388\tMr.. Siducy'S JOY at the return\n389\tJed was robust, and Jed went to hcG\n390\tvery tip~y with two bottles of win,\n391\ti'm him | found him in the hall as F!\n392\twent my rounds Of the house. H.\n393\twas singing.\n394\t\n395\t\n396\t| knew we'd hear again and soon\n397\tfrom Dravada, but not in what man\n398\tncr. Naturally l was apprchenSive.\n399\tand no doubt Jed was more 50, al\n400\tthough to save himself from my con\n401\ttempt he tried to conceal his fears.\n402\t\n403\t\n404\tThe Spaniard could not be expected\n405\tto accept his failure as final. He\n406\twould try again. That expectation\n407\twas fulfilled in a disconcerting fash\n408\tion.\n409\t\n410\t\n411\tThus far we had been dealing witt,\n412\tchance, with apparitions and threats\n413\tWe now came to deal with inevitable\n414\tity. Our experiences had been disa\n415\tgreeabIe. bur they had not presented\n416\tunacceptable consequence. We had n\n417\tchoice of ways. Now we entered q\n418\tway from which there was no es\n419\tcape.\n420\t\n421\t\n422\tFour days after Jed's return maj\n423\tcame TO Hartley house and inquired\n424\tfor me. He was detective. Hi,\n425\tname was Morgan; he was the head\n426\tof the Morgan Metropolitan Detectiv,\n427\tagency. | thought on first seeing hin\n428\twhile yet wOndering'w what his bust\n429\tness with us could be- and yet know\n430\ting instinctively that itbRd ~ do witt\n431\tDra-rtr----As-e--thSs aenulsr, re-\n432\tmore than a suggestion Of shrewt\n433\tmalevolence in his fice.\n434\t\n435\t\n436\tBefore he was through his inter\n437\tview with me. or rather his inquire\n438\tof me. | knew that inevitability ha,\n439\tentered our case. We were no lOnge\n440\tprogressing at the mercy of Opportuni\n441\t~ or chance. Morgan was fate. Tn\n442\twhole aspect had been altered. Nor\n443\tgan. a muscular, black.haired. Sordid\n444\tINSCRIPTIONS man of action ana oh\n445\tviouSly of queer action. was pur\n446\t! Greek tragedy to us\n447\t\n448\t\n449\t| CTO BE CONTINUEDD'\n450\t\n451\t=== 21_1888-04-30_pna_sn84024448_00296020758_1888043001_1024.json\n452\tThe Memphis appeal. [volume] (Memphis, Tenn.) 1886-1890 · 1888-04-30\n453\tcrops: None\n454\theadline: SPIRITUALISM.\n455\t\n456\tContinued From Ninth Page.\n457\t---\n458\t\n459\t\n460\t\n461\tpcrdtion, where she holds her everlasting\n462\tseancc. Many years ago the steamer At\n463\tlantlc started from Europe for the United\n464\tStates. Getting mid-ocean the machinery\n465\tbroke and she floundered around day\n466\tafter day, and week after week, and for a\n467\twhole month after she was due people\n468\twondered, and finally gave her up. There\n469\twas great anguish in the cities, for there\n470\tWe're many who had friends aboard that\n471\tvessel. Some OF the women, in their dis\n472\ttress, went TO the spiritual mediums, and\n473\tinquired as to the fate of that vessel. The\n474\tmediums called up the spirits, and the\n475\trappmgs on the table indicated the steam\n476\tsiip lost, with all on board. Women\n477\twent raving mad, and were carried to the\n478\tlunatic asylum. After awhile one day\n479\tgun was heard off quarantine. The flags\n480\twent up on the shipping, and the bells OF\n481\tthe churches We're rung. The boys ran\n482\tthrough the streets, crying: \"Extrai The\n483\tAtlantic is sale\" There was the embrac-\n484\ting as from the dead, when friends came\n485\tagain TO friends but some OF those passcn-\n486\tgers went up to find their wives in\n487\tthe lunatic asylum, where this cheat of in\n488\tformal spiritualsm had put them. A man\n489\tin Bellevue hospital, dying from wounds\n490\tmade by his own hands, was asked why\n491\tbe tried to commit suicide, and he said.\n492\t'The spirits told me to-\" Parents have\n493\tstrangled their children, and when asked\n494\twhy they did it, replied \"Spiritualism\n495\tdemanded it.\" 1t is the patronizer and\n496\tforager for the mad-house. Judge Ed\n497\tmunds, in Broadway Tabernacle, New\n498\tYork, delivering a lecture in behalf of\n499\tspiritualism, admitted, in so many words:\n500\t\"Therc is a fascination about consultation\n501\twith the spirits OF the dead that has ten\n502\tdc ncy TO lead people off from their right\n503\tjudgment, and to instil into them a fanati-\n504\tcisn that is revolving to the natural\n505\tmini\"\n506\t\n507\t\n508\tIL not only ruins its disciples, but it\n509\truins the mediums also, only give IL time.\n510\tThe Gadarean swine, on the banks of the\n511\tlake OF Galiee, no sooner became spiritual\n512\tmediums than down they went, in an\n513\tavalanche of pork, 10 the CONSERVATION Of\n514\tall the herdsmen. The office OF medium\n515\tIs bad for a man, bad for a woman, bad\n516\tfor beast\n517\t\n518\t\n519\t] bring against this delusion more\n520\tfearful Indictment; il ruins the soul inn\n521\tmortal. First, it makes II man a quarter\n522\tOF an iniidel then it makes him half an\n523\tin!idci; then il madcs him whole inGdel\n524\tthe whole system, as ! conceive it, is\n525\tfounded on the insutliciency of the word\n526\tOF God rs a revelation. God says the\n527\tbib'e is enough for you TO know about the\n52