the House of Commons yesterday after.\n961\tnoon, Mr. Gladstone said that the negota.\n962\ttions with Russia concerning the Afghan\n963\tquestion were still unfinished, and on that\n964\taccount the government was unable TO\n965\tpresent any more papers on the subject TO\n966\tParliament now, as it was difficult to sepa.\n967\trate those relating to AkTepe from those\n968\trelating to other affairs belonging to the\n969\tsame question.\n970\t\n971\t\n972\tIn the House of Lords yesterday Baron\n973\tWemyss denounced the government for\n974\ttheir policy in the Soudan. He said that\n975\tevery man killed in the Soudan was as\n976\tmuch slain by the government, through\n977\ttheir vacillation, Inconsistency and uncer-\n978\ttainty, as if done to death by their own\n979\thands. The only memorial that England\n980\t\n981\t\n982\twould leave in the Soudan would be an\n983\tabandoned railway and bleaching skeletons.\n984\tThe news of the Gear having presented\n985\tswords of honor to General KomaroT and\n986\t| Gen. Zakjewski has created an uneasy feel\n987\t| ing in Europe and it is thought the present\n988\tpeace is only an armistice, and that the ar\n989\tbitration proceedings are the merest mock\n990\tcry. The Daily News, in commenting on\n991\t| the Afghan papers presented in Parliament,\n992\tsays They show that the seizure of Pend\n993\tjeh had long been a foregone conclusion\n994\t| 1t is difficult to believe that the Russians\n995\t\n996\t\n997\twere ever in earnest about joint commits\n998\tsion.\"\n999\t\n1000\t\n1001\tIn the House of Commons yesterday Lord\n1002\t\n1003\t\n1004\tRandolph, referring to the Penjdeb Incl\n1005\t\n1006\t\n1007\tdent, said that the indignation aroused by\n1008\t| perusal of the dispatches was not confined\n1009\t\n1010\t\n1011\tTO the tory party but was shared By the\n1012\t\n1013\t\n1014\tcountry at large and even by the liberal\n1015\t\n1016\t\n1017\tnewspapers. Lord Randolph maintained\n1018\t\n1019\t\n1020\tnat NJ. de Giers had