was handsome stranger who stand\n1076\ting on her superfuous silk. The frown changed to\n1077\tq smile, the adjectves retired to the inner depths\n1078\tand in the most fascinating voice she said. Pardon\n1079\t\n1080\t\n1081\t\n1082\t\n1083\t\n1084\tthought it was my husband\"\n1085\t\n1086\t\n1087\tThere is q certain logic in business which is ex-\n1088\tceedingly fascinating. and great many persons do\n1089\tbusiness in the logical way to which refer. In\n1090\tstance:-A gentleman bought horse Of . dealer\n1091\twho was never before known to get the wrong END\n1092\tOF bargain for his portion The agreement-and\n1093\tq very simple and fair one It appeared to be-was\n1094\tthat one-half of the price named should be paid\n1095\tdown and that the buyer should owe for the other\n1096\thalo - I~-I~~\n1097\thimself and demanded his pay our logician replied\n1098\tthat the horse had been paid for. How can that\n1099\tbe\" cried the unhappy jockey. ''Its clear the\n1100\tlight\" replied the man OF superior intelligence.\n1101\t.'Our agreement was that should pay you one-half\n1102\t| your price. which did, and that should owe you\n1103\tfor the other half\" Exactly\" said the jockey;\n1104\t\"those were the precise words and now I've come\n1105\tfor the other half; don't you see?' II prefer to\n1106\tstand by my agreement. for q Of my\n1107\tword\" the philosopher responded. \"and how in\n1108\tthe world can you for the second half OF your\n1109\tprice. IS promised would il L pay it ? No. my\n1110\tfriend. I'll stick to my bargain and owe you just\n1111\tas said would\"\n1112\t\n1113\t\n1114\tDR.. Fulton is very remarkable man. that in\n1115\tteresting conversation which he had with the Lord\n1116\tabout taking the Rink amply proves. We have long\n1117\tknown that he was not appreciated by the people\n1118\tand that he had certain eccentricities Of temper\n1119\twhich made long continued fellowship impossible.\n1120\tBut that this want of appreciation the part\n1121\tpublic the result of its ignorance of his superb\n1122\tand wonderful privileges and gifts were not\n1123\taware. The greatest among us are always misun-\n1124\tderstood. The people refused to call him to the\n1125\tRink. and the Lord did it in dream. He is q\n1126\ttemperance man. and therefore the dream could not\n1127\thave been induced by the application Of any alco-\n1128\tholic stimulus to the BRAIN OF course 1t could not\n1129\thave been Satan who appeared to him and told him\n1130\t| to 60 to the Rink. though that would be one way,\n1131\t| and some people foolish enough to say q very\n1132\treasonable way. to account for the vision. There\n1133\tonly one thing In the story which rather confuses\n1134\t| us. and that is that Dr. Fulton wanted to go\n1135\t| to the Rink than tongue can tell,\n1136\tand that Immediately after he had con-\n1137\tcluded to do so the Lord appeared to\n1138\thim and told him to 60. We may remark just here\n1139\tthat is very curious fact that certain people al\n1140\tways have visions in which they are positively\n1141\tmanded to do the very thing which they have been\n1142\twanting do for months. 1t is so easy to hear the\n1143\tvoice of the Lord when he calls you from small\n1144\tfield Of activity to larger ono; but as the old\n1145\tfarmer said. If the Lord should tell you to go from\n1146\tbig placeto little he would have call three\n1147\tfour times before you would hear him. and then\n1148\tyou would certainly declare that 1t Satan\n1149\t\n1150\t\n1151\tIn some parts Of Europe very curious customs\n1152\tprevail. the relics Of other days. In Heidelberg for\n1153\tinstance. the practice of duelling is popular\n1154\tThe law is against 1t, to be but for that\n1155\tvery 1t is considered absolute necessity by\n1156\tthe students of the university AS you pass along\n1157\tthe street your attention is attracted to the faces\n1158\tcertain young men which are. according to the popu-\n1159\tlar estimation. beauthed and glorihed by the scars\n1160\tOF ugly sword cuts They the symbols Of initis-\n1161\tton into some one of the college secret societies\n1162\tEvery in q while you meet grave Herr profess\n1163\twith long his forehead or cheek. the\n1164\tproof OF his vaor in early life 1t is mark Of hon.\n1165\torable distinction which money cannot buy; it can\n1166\tbe possessed only by the man who has felt the\n1167\tkeen edge of the sword. Two students meet in the\n1168\tstreet. for example, and the gentlest Of all quarrels\n1169\tensues. Then follows the duel In little\n1170\tbuilding on the Necksr the chosen and favored\n1171\tcompany meets. The bodies of the contest\n1172\tants are protected, also the neck,\n1173\twhere the jugular vein offers tempting\n1174\tmark and the eyes. The duellsts kiss each other\n1175\ton both cheeks. and then swords& When the\n1176\tlittle affair Is the doctor up the cash, tho\n1177\tcontestants kiss again. and the whole company re-\n1178\ttire to favorite beer saloon. where the festv\n1179\ttics continue indefinitely. On the whole this GUS\n1180\t| tom Is not 80 barbarous the \"hazing\" In\n1181\tAmerican colleges. for both parties have an equal\n1182\tchance. We q freshman up in sack and sus-\n1183\tpend him from third story window. or make q\n1184\tbaseball OF him and hustle him from one end of the\n1185\tcollege grounds to another. and leave him with a\n1186\tbroken and entire loss of selfrespect; while\n1187\tthe German simply puts n sword in his hand. and\n1188\teither gives or receives q wound. However. boys\n1189\twill be boys, and IL is absolutely necessary to have\n1190\tsome vent for their tremendous and sometimes\n1191\tawful exuberance of spirits.\n1192\t\n1193\t\n1194\tThe Spiritualists ought really to be little more\n1195\tcareful. cautious. or prudent. or something. We\n1196\thardly know what and II may be that the word ret\n1197\tcent is what after II the rule holds good that\n1198\tno man has right to speak unless he has something\n1199\tpleasan