pitiate Heaven in favor of the first\n1314\tsettlers of the great western wilderness:\n1315\tand hen;e the first chop was for the\n1316\tFather, the second for the Son, and the\n1317\tthird for the Holy Ghost; and the blaze\n1318\twas merely designated to attract the eve\n1319\tof the observer.\n1320\t\n1321\t\n1322\tWhen Lambert struck this old road.\n1323\the discovered on the north side of it di.\n1324\tlapidated log-cabin. Approaching the\n1325\tcabin, he noticed a light within, and,\n1326\tfeeling satisfied that the murderers were\n1327\tclose at hand, he gave loud cough.\n1328\tThis brought to the rear door of the\n1329\thouse the old woman who occupied it,\n1330\tand who obtained living by selling\n1331\tcakes and beer to passing wayfarers.\n1332\tLambert, in a feigned voice, asked to\n1333\tspend the night within, and was at once\n1334\tadmitted. He had but crossed the\n1335\tthreshold when the report of muskct\n1336\twas heard in the next room, and the ball\n1337\tsplintered the woodwork above his\n1338\thead.\n1339\t\n1340\t\n1341\tWithout moments hesitation, Lam\n1342\tbert, with nothing but his tomahawk in\n1343\this hand, sprang into the next room and\n1344\tstruck down the man in whose hand was\n1345\tthe still smoking musket. The williams\n1346\tcompanion, the smaller man of tn, two,\n1347\tat once fed the house. but before he had\n1348\tpassed beyond the light Of the fire with\n1349\tin. the avenger with the wretch's own\n1350\trifle, brought him to the earth in strug.\n1351\tgle with death that lasted but second. i.\n1352\tAS soon as he had fired the shot, Lam\n1353\tbert turned upon the PROSTATE murderer,\n1354\twho was about to arise, and struck him a\n1355\tsecond time, telling him to the floor again.\n1356\tHe then, with rope furnished him by\n1357\tthe old woman, bound the williams hands\n1358\tand legs securely. In the morning the\n1359\tavenger borrowed horse from the wo.\n1360\tman. strapped upon its back the living and\n1361\tdead murderers, and before nightfal! ar.\n1362\trived atStottler' inn. On the following\n1363\tday the living man was publicly lynched\n1364\tin front Of the inn, and the bodies were\n1365\tthen consigned to an unhcnored grave.\n1366\tLambert's companions, craveu-hearted\n1367\tas they had proven themselves, we're\n1368\tloud in praise of their daring leader, who\n1369\tuntil the day Of his death, was hero\n1370\tin the humble circle of life in which he\n1371\twas placed.\n1372\t\n1373\t=== 16_1919-08-08_p1_sn86058226_00271745278_1919080801_0505.json\n1374\tThe Richmond palladium and sun-telegram. [volume] (Richmond, Ind.) 1907-1939 · 1919-08-08\n1375\tcrops: None\n1376\theadline: \n1377\t---\n1378\tVenice. He had learned how to sing\n1379\tin Professor Porpora's school. He was\n1380\thandsome. He had imagination which\n1381\tcolored his fine voice when he sang.\n1382\tConsuelo, scrawny, dark-skinned\n1383\tSpanish peasant girl, was another Por-\n1384\tpora's pupils. She was plain, but had\n1385\tbeautiful voice and spirit.\n1386\tShe and Anzoleto were good friends,\n1387\tnothing more, but he-an errant phil\n1388\tanderer-was always falling in love\n1389\tand coming to Consuelo for hympathy\n1390\tand advice. He was a rascal and an\n1391\tmigrate.\n1392\t\n1393\t\n1394\tConsuelo made her first public ap-\n1395\tpearance in the simple dress OF peas\n1396\tant girl, and her voice-filled with the\n1397\tfire of her soul-took the city. Every\n1398\tJ one was at her feet. Young suitors\n1399\tsought her hand and among them was\n1400\tAnzoleto. 1t was the romantic period\n1401\tin Venetian history. about 1850. Con-\n1402\tsuelo engaged herself to her fellow\n1403\tpupil, but repulsed the others. Her\n1404\tgenius had won admiration; her dig\n1405\tnifty and modesty had won respect\n1406\tThe Count Justinjan not only en-\n1407\tgagged her for his theatre, but fell in\n1408\tlove with her and tried to win her\n1409\tfrom Anzoleto. She would not be led\n1410\taway oiAu 1lduC ~ part VI utT COLI\n1411\ttract that her betrothed should also be\n1412\tengaged to sing. Her sweetness and\n1413\tfidelity the more deeply enslaved the\n1414\tcount\n1415\t\n1416\t\n1417\tConsuelo's rival, singer named\n1418\t| Gorilla was bitterly incensed by the\n1419\taction of the count. Anzoleto played\n1420\ton her jealousy and pretended to be\n1421\ther lover. OF this perfidy Consuelo\n1422\tknew nothing.\n1423\t\n1424\t\n1425\tAt the first public performance Con-\n1426\tsuelo achieved renewed success. but\n1427\tAnzoleto was almost overlooked.\n1428\tHer teacher warned her not to\n1429\tmarry Anzoleto and to prove his case\n1430\tforced her to visit the home Of Gorilla.\n1431\tThey found AnZoleto there and Con-\n1432\tsuelo was convinced of his perfidy\n1433\tShe repulsed Anzoleto, refused the\n1434\tlove of the count and fled to Vienna\n1435\ton the advice of Porpora.\n1436\t\n1437\t\n1438\tA little later, Porpora sent her to\n1439\this friend, Count Christian Of b.\n1440\thernia, to serve as companion to his\n1441\tniece, the Baroness Amelia, just come\n1442\tfrom convent school at Prague Con-\n1443\tsuelo, who arrived at the castle on a\n1444\tstormy night. was in awe of the count\n1445\tand his surroundings, but Amelia, a\n1446\tlovely girl, treasured her and won her\n1447\tinstant affection. That night, as Con-\n1448\tsuelo came, an ancient tree on the es-\n1449\ttate, known as the treee of misfortune\n1450\tfell in the storm.\n1451\t\n1452\t\n1453\t| News of its fall threw the counts\n1454\thousehold into confusion and terror\n1455\tSome evil is abroad\" the countess\n1456\tsaid, and soon after Count Albert, the\n1457\tson, entered, handsome. pale and\n1458\tsad young man, who announced that\n1459\tq 10 i,\n1460\tover the house. He smiled at Con.\n1461\tsuelo, touched her hand and withdrew,\n1462\tleaving her deeply mystified.\n1463\t\n1464\t\n1465\tAlbert, she soon learned, was a gen.\n1466\ttie and admirable soul, but man Of\n1467\tmorbid moods which in effect 