oasses shocks\n2028\t32nch with heads 81 Us 81 9D shocks with heads\n2029\tSsncn \" 81 90; do, 35neh 81 50 81 box\n2030\tSOC 6oc. rum do, 81. pipe do s6 10 5o empty hogs.\n2031\theads 81 50; hoops IA teet ordinary prime $50 635\n2032\tdo. II ieet. do S2S $50 per NJ\n2033\t\n2034\t\n2035\tt,opPEn We note sales OF about 10000 bs lake at\n2036\ti13fc. l71sc. The market closed firm at the above\n2037\tH. ures.\n2038\t\n2039\t\n2040\tCoRDAGE dull We quote :-Maniia. large and all\n2041\tsize per h. Ike l71ac: do coriagc. bolt yarns\n2042\t,6c l7c.: tarred Manila lac; sisa rope. 9c. Otc.\n2043\tNew Zealand. IL he. a IKE; Russia l7c.\n2044\t\n2045\t\n2046\tCorroS the spot was dull, but unchanged. Futures\n2047\topsned about points lower The quiet and\n2048\tquiet steady the decline tor the day being\n2049\tpoints fhe ohiclA closing prices to-day\n2050\twith MONDAYS. were as follows:-\n2051\t"}]},"uuid":"faf3dcad-03c7-4f8f-a31d-5fdc781e5262","timestamp":"2026-10-31T08:25:40.109Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/c1/d06.txt","content":"=== 22_1920-01-04_p49_sn83030313_00271744080_1920010401_0099.json\nThe New York herald. · 1920-01-04\ncrops: None\nheadline: 'N|CKNAMES' OF\nBRITISH M.P.'S\n\n\nBADGES OF HONOR\n\nGladstone's Son Known as\n\"Tuppence\" and Lloyd\n\n\nGeorge as 81. G.\"\n\nBy +. p. O'cONNOR.\n---\n! By T. p. O'CoNNOR.\n\n\n[Special Despatch to the Hcrold. Copy\n\n\nright, 1920, by the Philadelphia Public\n\n\nLedger Co. Az Rights Rescroed.]\n\n\nLONDON. Dec. II.\n\n\nIt is rather a distinction in the House OF\nCommons to have more nicknames than\nany other member. for the House has an\nextraordinary love for clubbing manor\n\"A party-with a nickname. Nobody knows\nhow these nicknames arise, and they pass\nINTO the vernacular of the smoke rooms\nwith extraordinary rapidity. In my young\ndays Sir Stafford Northcote. the Tory\nleader. who had long beard, was always\nknown as 1.-The Goat\" and Sir William\nHarcourt. who was man Of huge bulk\nand great height, was spoken OF as\nJumbo\n\n\nThe favorite conundrums today In the\nsmoke room are First, Why is the gov\nernment like an immoral goose? Because\n1t is untrue to .ts proper gander tpropa-\nganda>. The second and better conundrum\nis, WHAT IL t0e plutdl w wv\nGeddes, a play upon the fact that the\nGeddes brothers are chosen by Lloyd\nGeorge apparently to be the superman OF\nevery hard Job that he takes In hand. one\nOf the nicknames for one Of the brothers-\nq play upon his Christian name OF AUCKLAND\nard on his somewhat portentous appear\nance, with his dark complexion, his rather\ntulging eyes and his prominent nose-ls\n.the Great AUX\" Herbert. now Loro\n\n\n~\nGladstone, was known at Eton school al\nways as \"Tuppence.\" He happened to oe\nat the school at the moment when his IL\nlustrious father had just added two-pence\nto the income tax, and fy that name ne\nwas known to his old school fellows when\nthey and he were members OF the House OF\nCommons.\n\n\nAnother habit Of the House is to call men\nwith longish names by the initials OF tneir\nChristian names, or by the 1irst letter Va\ntheir surname. Gladstone was always\nspoken of as Mr.. c.; Sir Henry Camp\nbell Bannermann as &C. b.; Thomas wal\nlace Russell, once prominent politician\nnow in retirement as a hotel proprietor IN\nDublin, was always called IT. W.. neeu\nnot repeat the initials by which am al\nways addressed by political friends ana\nfoes in the House. The Prime Minister IS\nusually spoken Of as Il. g.\n\n\nBenn Has Most Nicknames.\n\n\nBut the member who has the most nick\nnames is Captain Wedgwood Benn. He IS\nnot very much liked by his Tory oppon-\nents. so their title for him is the somewhat\nopprobrious one Of Buttons\" A more\npopular and respectful name is that OF\nLittle Ben\" The great clock In the tower\nof the House OF Commons, as everybody\nknows. is known as big Ben,\" and this\nsuggests often the application Of Its name\nto members Of the House. There were two\nBentincks once in the House OF commons\none big and the other small; they we're\nknown accordingly as Big Zen and\nLittle Ben\" -\n\n\nwhen you see wedgwood Ben you are\nnot surprised that he should suggest the\nnickname of Buttons\" Though about\nforty years OF age, he looks just the\nheight, size and age Of the boy Buttons or\nthe English household. He Is scarcely five\nfeet high. His body is as slender as\nboys. His cheek Is peach blossom and the\nexpression Of the face. with rather conf\neyes, is exactly the look OF the typicai\nstreet gamin of London, with its shrewd-\nness. its imperturbable good humor and its\nreadiness to take on any little job. Wedg-\nwood Benn might act the part OF Peter\nPan on the stage. or perhaps the apter\nanalogy is to Charlie Chaplin OF the films.\nAnd here comes the curious part OF the\nstory. Little Benn has proved himself one\nOF the heroes of the war. He Is passion\nately devoted to politics. and up to the\nwar never thought OF anything else. The\nHouse Of Commons is as much his home as\nIL he had been born in II. To men OF that\nthere Is one fore-ordained\njob--a job which consists of vigilance to\nthe least detail of House Of Commons life.\nwith an interest In gossip, a knowledge OF\nthe haunts and the weaknesses OF every\nmember. of the telephone number either\nin his own home or somebody else's home\nduring his absence from the House; :\npower to tabulate strictly. &c Those arc\nthe functions Of the whips. So when his\nparty came into power Wedgwood Benn\nwas appointed one OF the whips.\n\n\n~Fiery Young Particle\"\n\n\nYou could see him every night, while the\nHouse of Commons was sitting, at the door\nOf the inner lobby OF the House by which\nmembers enter or leave, with a pencil\nbehind his ear, a notebook in his hand, a\nlook OF Interrogation In his eye and with\nhis slight dapper boyish figure and his\nspruce manner. looking just like the fiery\nyoung particle\" as Gilbert and Sullivan\ncall Il In the play.\n\n\nThen came the war, and TO the surprise\nOf everybody the eternal boy left the\nwhips room and the House OF Commons\nand his country, and the next hearo OF\nhim was that he was at Gallipoli, and had\nentered on all the perils and destinations\nOf the airman's career. Stories occasional\nly trickled home of feats OF his which\nshowed quite reckless daring. Then he\ncame home and stood again for Pariia-\nment, and was again returned, and now\nIs in the House of Commons. He has just\nbrought out volume full OF wit and OF\nstories of breakneck adventures told very\nand almost casually.\n\n\nA change has come in his fortunes with\nthe upset In all the old lines Of political life\nwhich came with Lloyd Georges seizure\nOf the dictatorship OF the House OF COM\nmons, by the defeat Of all the really pow\nerful men who once confronted him. The\nvacant places left in the ranks Of the lead\ners Of the opposition gave the opportunity\nto all the young fellows TO come along.\nAnd thus II comes to pass that you can see\nthe extraordinary and almost incredible\nspectacle OF this Peter Pan getting UP from\nthe most sacred OF all spots In the House\nOF Commons. except the bench where the\nmembers of the government cit. namely\ntho front opposition bench. and leading.\nfor the timo being. on some measure all\nthe elderly middle aged men that form the\npresent Liberal party.\n\n\nTho best Of 1t is. the speeches are quite\n\n=== 31_1894-01-12_p1_sn99062034_00279523313_1894011201_1045.json\nThe Madison daily leader. [volume] (Madison, S.D.) 1890-current · 1894-01-12\ncrops: None\nheadline: Underaakers Don't believe In spooks.\n---\nI An aged undertaken of this city was\nasked il he had aver seen any spooks.\n\n\nHe laughed derisiyely and said: mo,\nand ! doubt il you will find any M in\n\n\nmy line Of business who believes in the\nexistence of such things. We find there\n10 nothing more harmless and pitiful\nthan dead people. Philadelphia Rec\nord.\n\n=== 6_1903-04-07_p2_sn84024827_0021110661A_1903040701_0047.json\nBisbee daily review. · 1903-04-07\ncrops: None\nheadline: A NIGHT WITH\n\n A MYSTERY\n---\nlCopyrlght, lSuS. Dy .. U. Warnet.l\n\n Thirty years ago a widow named\n\n Haynes, living in village about forty\n\n miles out of London, disappeared one\n\n afternoon and could not be traced. She.\n\n owned and was living in large stone\n\n house built hundred years before she\n\n bought it, and only portion of it\n\n was habitable.\n\n Here was a woman known by sight\n\n at least to four-fifths of the townspeo-\n\n pie. She lived on the best street. It\n\n did not seem that she could have walk\n\n ed block without meeting friend.\n\n She was in her sitting room at 2\n\n o'clock in the afternoon. An hour later\n\n she was missing. She had not dressed\n\n for the street-had not left the room. so,\n\n far as the maid could say. Scotland\n\n Yard men worked at the mystery for\n\n six months before they gave up in\n\n despair.\n\n Six months after the widows disap-\n\n pearance the house was for rent, al\n\n though stories were already current\n\n that it was haunted. The place was,\n\n first taken by grocer who was.\n\n known to everybody as a hard headed\n\n man with plenty of nerve, and yet aft\n\n CT six weeks residence in the house he\n\n moved out. He had leased 1t for a year. do not know what he may have told others. but he informed me. under promise of secrecy, that he be- ueved the house to be haunted. It was no use to ridicule his state,\n\n ments. for he was serious and earnest, The fact of his moving out proved that\n\n It was a year before any one else | took the house, and 1t was then yacat- | ed for good. Everybody now firmly, | believed in the stories of ghost, and. | though it was offered rent free to poor ! people, no one would move into it.\n\n When it was put up at auction sale. l there was only one bid, and 80 no sale, was effected.\n\n While ready to admit that half be- lieved in ''the curse\" I can truthfuIly declare that the stories reaching me from time to time regarding the house excited my contempt. For several months before my opportunity came was anxious to pass night there ands see what would occur. House and grounds had long been abandoned to tramps. but yet on certain occasions these tranps had made queer state-\n\n ments about what had happened there. The windows had been broken, the doors torn away. and the once fine old\n\n mansion was bad wreck when 1 in spected it one afternoon.\n\n The only two doors left intact were the sliding doors separating the par lors. The others had been used for fuel In the fireplaces. l told no one of my intentions, but at 10 o'clock that night, armed with a revolver. candle and matches. I returned to the house and sat down on the floor in corner of the back parlor. There was a doorway between this room and the dining room and one between that and the kitchen. It was warm night in summer, with never breath of wind. It was as quiet as a graveyard in the house and about the grounds. You must not think me boastful when 1 tell you that\n\n I was not even shaky. was some-\n\n what excited, but not at aii nervous In the first place, I believed that all the, Edu bttu and. in the ne:t. I was cf the opinioI. that some practical joker had hand\n\n in the business if there was anything l in it.\n\n From 10 to 1 o'clock nothing hap pered = nothing except my going to sleep. heard the town clock strike | F, and heard the first quarter chime\n\n I didn't feel sleepy. and yet dropped of, and about minute after awoke the clock struck 1. The sounds of fooi I steps awoke me. Some one came ~ the graveled walk from the gate, pass- | ed around to the kitchen door and enI tered. It was a shuffiing. dragging\n\n step. It passed across the kitchen. | passed across the dining room. and l firmly expected to see person fin the doorway. It was not so dark but that | could have made out even a rat. No one appeared. l crept on hands and knees to the doorway. No one could | eseen-\n\n=== 16_1921-04-16_pna_sn88064057_00280761771_1921041601_0501.json\nAbbeville progress. (Abbeville, Vermilion Parish, La.) 1913-1944 · 1921-04-16\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\n\n= .,,..o,sr his disappointment and wourc\n\n\nenrfors. | thought the physico,\n,,..,-cr ro Jed was greater than eve\nand found some pleasure in teiin-\nuu $50.\n\n\ny. WAS convinced of thai himself\nand WAS not happy.\n\n\n-Eaa don't You end your ra~cau,y:.\n,r,..l him. why don't you give m.\nr..py~rrpr ro Mrs. Sidney aud ano.\nt.. ro ulaSc whatever disposition >t.\n,,.,rt. nf il: Then your con-rf.r...\nwill me- ra-y-your position IN This\nInns. will for the first time he toIrr;,.\nbI. IA decent man, and your physi\n,aI security will be promoted\"\nHe would not. He seemed 10 hest.\nfate for moment, but his purpose\nwas TOO long fixed and too much q\npart of his life.\n\n\nHe no longer was .surly with me\nand seemed to have lost my ability\nI, eprare him. We parted with nv\ntelling him that there would be no\nnossihlr truce or peace between u\nu,Iess he respected the women of the\nhousehold. He bowed.\n\n\n\"Anything else, sir?\" he asked; and\nthen ne departed as the servant.\n\n\nMrs. Sidney's relief to find that\nthe robbery had proved only hoax on\nthe robbers was such as would come\n| from escape from tangible horrors.\n| The lady had been keeping control of\nherself. as was necessary to protect\n/ her husband and daughter from hcf\nown agony of mind and to keen tn,\nhousehold from finding significance il\nwhat could be- passed over as a triy\n| ial piece of robbery.\n\n\nwhen she learned that the alarm\nWAS over. She relaxed Imply in he.\n/ chair. and | feared that She Nigh\n! collapse: but in moment she har\n| STRUGGLED back to command of herself\n/ Then She excused herself and wen\n/ into her hcdroom-fOr prayer, | knew\n\n\nMr.. Siducy'S JOY at the return\nJed was robust, and Jed went to hcG\nvery tip~y with two bottles of win,\ni'm him | found him in the hall as F!\nwent my rounds Of the house. H.\nwas singing.\n\n\n| knew we'd hear again and soon\nfrom Dravada, but not in what man\nncr. Naturally l was apprchenSive.\nand no doubt Jed was more 50, al\nthough to save himself from my con\ntempt he tried to conceal his fears.\n\n\nThe Spaniard could not be expected\nto accept his failure as final. He\nwould try again. That expectation\nwas fulfilled in a disconcerting fash\nion.\n\n\nThus far we had been dealing witt,\nchance, with apparitions and threats\nWe now came to deal with inevitable\nity. Our experiences had been disa\ngreeabIe. bur they had not presented\nunacceptable consequence. We had n\nchoice of ways. Now we entered q\nway from which there was no es\ncape.\n\n\nFour days after Jed's return maj\ncame TO Hartley house and inquired\nfor me. He was detective. Hi,\nname was Morgan; he was the head\nof the Morgan Metropolitan Detectiv,\nagency. | thought on first seeing hin\nwhile yet wOndering'w what his bust\nness with us could be- and yet know\ning instinctively that itbRd ~ do witt\nDra-rtr----As-e--thSs aenulsr, re-\nmore than a suggestion Of shrewt\nmalevolence in his fice.\n\n\nBefore he was through his inter\nview with me. or rather his inquire\nof me. | knew that inevitability ha,\nentered our case. We were no lOnge\nprogressing at the mercy of Opportuni\n~ or chance. Morgan was fate. Tn\nwhole aspect had been altered. Nor\ngan. a muscular, black.haired. Sordid\nINSCRIPTIONS man of action ana oh\nviouSly of queer action. was pur\n! Greek tragedy to us\n\n\n| CTO BE CONTINUEDD'\n\n=== 21_1888-04-30_pna_sn84024448_00296020758_1888043001_1024.json\nThe Memphis appeal. [volume] (Memphis, Tenn.) 1886-1890 · 1888-04-30\ncrops: None\nheadline: SPIRITUALISM.\n\nContinued From Ninth Page.\n---\n\n\n\npcrdtion, where she holds her everlasting\nseancc. Many years ago the steamer At\nlantlc started from Europe for the United\nStates. Getting mid-ocean the machinery\nbroke and she floundered around day\nafter day, and week after week, and for a\nwhole month after she was due people\nwondered, and finally gave her up. There\nwas great anguish in the cities, for there\nWe're many who had friends aboard that\nvessel. Some OF the women, in their dis\ntress, went TO the spiritual mediums, and\ninquired as to the fate of that vessel. The\nmediums called up the spirits, and the\nrappmgs on the table indicated the steam\nsiip lost, with all on board. Women\nwent raving mad, and were carried to the\nlunatic asylum. After awhile one day\ngun was heard off quarantine. The flags\nwent up on the shipping, and the bells OF\nthe churches We're rung. The boys ran\nthrough the streets, crying: \"Extrai The\nAtlantic is sale\" There was the embrac-\ning as from the dead, when friends came\nagain TO friends but some OF those passcn-\ngers went up to find their wives in\nthe lunatic asylum, where this cheat of in\nformal spiritualsm had put them. A man\nin Bellevue hospital, dying from wounds\nmade by his own hands, was asked why\nbe tried to commit suicide, and he said.\n'The spirits told me to-\" Parents have\nstrangled their children, and when asked\nwhy they did it, replied \"Spiritualism\ndemanded it.\" 1t is the patronizer and\nforager for the mad-house. Judge Ed\nmunds, in Broadway Tabernacle, New\nYork, delivering a lecture in behalf of\nspiritualism, admitted, in so many words:\n\"Therc is a fascination about consultation\nwith the spirits OF the dead that has ten\ndc ncy TO lead people off from their right\njudgment, and to instil into them a fanati-\ncisn that is revolving to the natural\nmini\"\n\n\nIL not only ruins its disciples, but it\nruins the mediums also, only give IL time.\nThe Gadarean swine, on the banks of the\nlake OF Galiee, no sooner became spiritual\nmediums than down they went, in an\navalanche of pork, 10 the CONSERVATION Of\nall the herdsmen. The office OF medium\nIs bad for a man, bad for a woman, bad\nfor beast\n\n\n] bring against this delusion more\nfearful Indictment; il ruins the soul inn\nmortal. First, it makes II man a quarter\nOF an iniidel then it makes him half an\nin!idci; then il madcs him whole inGdel\nthe whole system, as ! conceive it, is\nfounded on the insutliciency of the word\nOF God rs a revelation. God says the\nbib'e is enough for you TO know about the\nfuture world. You say it is not enough\naud there is where you and the Lord dii-\nTer. You clear the table, you shove aside\n-thr Bible, YOU put your hand on the\ntable, and say: \"Now let spirits of the\nfuture world come and tell me something\nthe Bible has not told me-\" And al\nthough the scriptures say: \"Add\nthou not unto his words, lest\nhe reprove thee, and thou be\nfound 4 liar\" you risk IL, and say: \"Come\nbuck, spirit Of my departed father, come\nback, spirit Of my departed mother, OF my\ncompanions, OF my little child, aud tell me\nsore things / don't know about you and\nnlOnt the unseen world\" II God is ever\nslammed square in the face, Il is when n\nspiritual medium puts down her hand\non the table, invoking spirits departed 10\nmake revelation. God has told YOU all\nYou ought 10 know, and how dare you be-\nvrying into that which is none OF your\nbusiness? You cannot keep the Bible in\nono hand and spirtualism in the other.\nonc or the other will slip out Of your grasp,\ndepend npon II.\n\n\nSplritualism Is adverse 10 the Bible in\nthe lact that IL has in these last DAYS called\nfrom tho future world Christian men TO\nlastly against Christianity. Its mediums\nc1ll back Lorenzo Dow, the celebrated\nevangelist and Lorenzo Dow tcstiiies that\nChristians are idolators. Spiritualism\ncalls back Tom Payne, and he tcstiGcs\ntha' he is stopping in the same house in\nheaven with John Bunyan. They call\nback John Wesley, and he testiies against\nth\" Christian life which ho all his life\nglusiously preached. Andrew jackson\nDavis, the greatest OF all the spirtualisis,\ncomes 10 the front and declares that the\nNew Testament is but 'the dismal echo Of\nNarbaric age, and the Bible only zone\noi the pen and ink relics Of Christianity\"\nThey attempt 10 substitute the writings oI\nSWedcnborg, and Andrew Jackson Davis,\nmd other religious baldcrlash, in the place\noil this old Bible. have in my house q\nbook which was used in this very city III\nthe public service OF spiritnalisls. II is\nwe'll worn with much service. | open\nthat book aud il says \"What is oui\nbnntism2 Answer: Frequent abluiion,\nOF water. What is our inspiration? Plenty\noi fresh air and sunlight What Is oui\nprdyc2 Abundant physical exercise.\nWhat is our love fcast2 A clear conscience\nAMI sound sleep\" And | end from the\nfa'uc book that the chief item In thcii\npublic worship Is gymnastic exercise, and\nthat whenever they want to rouse up theil\nsouls to very high pitch Of devotion they\nsiug page slxty-hyc: \"The night Ia,\ngathered up her moonlit fringes;\" or pag,\nsixteen: \"Comc 10 the woods, hcigho!\nYou shy you arc not such fool MS that\nbut YOU will be II you keep on In III,\ntrack you have started.\n\n\nBut, says some one, Wouldn't IL bS\nOF advantage 10 hear from the futur,\nwurid7 Don't you think Il would strength\nen- Christians? There are great man,\nmaicralisis who do not believe there ar,\nsouls, but II spirits from the future worlt\nshould knock and talk over 10 us the\nwould be persuaded\" To that answer\nin the ringing words Of the Son Of God\n\"If they believe not Moses and th'\nprophets, neither will they be persuaded\nthough one rose from the dead\"\n\n\nNow, | believe, undor God, that thi\nsermon will save many from disease, in\nsanity and pcrdition. | believe these ar\nthe days OF which the apostle spake whel\nhe said. \"ln the latter times some shal\nDEPART from the faith giving heed TO scduc\nIn spirits\" think my audience, as wei\nNJ other audiences in this day, need &\nhave reiterated in their hearing the pas\nkigcs quoted some minutes ago 'Thcr\nshall not he among you q consulior Of lamil\nlar spirits, OF wizard, or necromancor; \"a\nthey that do these things arcan abomina\nttun 10 the lord - and \"Ihe soul tina\n\n=== 11_1905-09-15_pna_sn83030214_0017504131A_1905091501_0299.json\nNew-York tribune. [volume] (New York [N.Y.]) 1866-1924 · 1905-09-15\ncrops: None\nheadline: PHILADELPHIA'S GHOSTLY ELECTORATE.\n---\nOne beneficial result of the revolt against ring\nrule in Philadelphia has been the recuncauoh\nof the city's poll list and the suppression OF an\nenormous phantom or graveyard vote. 1t mad\nbeen known for years that the assessors hSts\nwhich serve a register Of voters, were PADDED\nwith the names Of dead men, minors, aliens and\nother bogus electors The machine paid the poll\ntaxes assessed against these mythical patriots.\nand in return that their votes were cast and\ncounted-once or oftener-for its own candi-\ndates. The existence Of this resurrected vote\ncould, indeed, have been established by SIM\npie process In arithmetic. Philadelphias popula-\nton In 1900 was .293 600 1t is now probably\n100000 Allowing one voter to every FIVE In\nhabtants-the normal ratio in American cities\nthe total vote In Philadelphia should be to-day\nbetween 100000 and 100000. But at the May\nassessment Of this year-completed just before\nthe popular uprising against the machine be-\ncause Of its attempted footing Of the city gas\nPLAINTIFF registration list was stretched so al\nto include 375,615 names Were this list honest\nit would show population Of 1875, 900, figure\nwhich Philadelphia's rate of growth In the last\nfour decades will not justify. Presumably the\nassessors had admitted to their registers some\n10000 or 10000 bogus voters.\n\n\nMayor Weaver and the forces now struggling\nto free the city from the grasp of what Secre-\ntary Root recently denounced as corrupt and\ncriminal combination\" wisely directed their en-\nergies toward exposing and undoing the frauds\nIn registration. 1t is needless to say that their\nefforts were hampered at every step by the dis\ncredited but still powerful Republican machine\nMost OF the assessors were put In office by that\nmachine and are still secretly bound to its SER\nvice; but so determined was the pressure Of the\ncity administration through the partly reorgan-\nlied police, and so effective the vigilance of vol\nunteer watchers, that at the September revision\nno less than 1888 electoral ghosts were ree-\ngated once more TO the city cemeteries.\n\n\n1t Is more than likely that many bogus names\nremain for under the lax election laws of Penn\nSylvania the mere payment OF q poll tax may\ncount FOR more In determining a claimants right\nTO vote than proof or lack OF proof Of bona fde\nresidence. 1t may take two or three years to\nweed out lists so easily falsified and so difficult\nOF correction; but the friends of honest govern\nment have accomplished a notable work In ex-\nposing the wholesale frauds perpetrated by the\nmachine and In stripping that machine of\nlarge fraction Of its most useful and loyaI fol-\nlowing. Philadelphia has begun wisely by at\nlacking the weak spot in the organizations\narmor; but that organization power for evil\nis only to be annulled by overwhelming IL this\nfall at the polls, and then securing personal\nregistration law, which will end all forms of\nvoting by proxy.\n\n=== 3_1929-08-19_pna_sn82015313_00383349199_1929081901_0244.json\nThe Indianapolis times. [volume] (Indianapolis [Ind.]) 1922-1965 · 1929-08-19\ncrops: None\nheadline: HOLLYWOOD HAS\n\n\nEXTRA SHARE OF\n\n\nTRAGEDY HOMES\n\nSiqhtSeers Directed to\nMansions Haunted by\n\n\nBroken Careers.\n---\nBJy Times Special\n\n\nHOLLYWOOD, Cal. Aug. 10. -\nEven as American cities go, Holly\nwood is still a boom town, grown\nUP in twenty years from a few scat\ntered farm houses to city known\nin the far corners of the world\nBut for all its youth, Hollywood\nalready has more than its share of\ntragedy houses, mansions haunted\nwith memories Of vivid careers shat\ntered by scandal and homes closed\nby violent death.\n\n\nEvery town has at least one such\nhouse around which dramatic tales\nare woven, but the Hollywood sight\nseer nowadays is directed to fully\na score of them, and every one has\nits story eerie or sad, notes a cor\nrespondent for Photoplay magazine.\n\n\nRudolph Valentines palatial\nhome, Falcons Lair, perched high\non the ledge of a mountain, is still\nthe chief point of interest for the\nmorbidly curious, for it has never\nbeen occupied since the untimely\ndeath of its master. Stripped Of its\nfurnishings and isolated from town,\nit mas taken on a bleak look that\ngives substance to the irresponsible\nlegends of weird noises and panic\nstricken caretakers which have\ngrown up around it.\n\n\nBY contrast, there is the little\n\n=== 10_1895-04-28_pna_sn90059522_00206536822_1895042801_0618.json\nSt. Paul daily globe. [volume] (Saint Paul, Minn.) 1884-1896 · 1895-04-28\ncrops: None\nheadline: OVER ONE HUNDRED DEATHS\n\nSaid to Have Occurred Within\nthe Walls Of the Old\n\n\nPlace.\n\nIS THE DWELLING HAUNTED?\n\nThe Good People Of the An\ncient Quaker Settlement\n\n\nThink 1t Is.\n---\nCNew London Cor. New York Sum-\n\n\nThe signing of some papers here\nthis week relating to the estate of\nthe late John Kenyon, of the neigh\nboring town of Ledyard, brought up\nthe strange story of the house in\nwhich Kenyon died. The house, one\nOf the oldest in Connecticut, was the\nfirst built by that sect of QualSers\nknown as Rogeneens, who colonized\nthe sou thwe5tern part of Ledyard in\n1880. Their settlement is now known\nas Quakertown. Among their de-\nscendants today the building is\nknown as the 0evil House. They\nbelieve that the, su-se of death has\nbeen upon it for many years, and in\nproof of this belief they point out\nthat over 100 persons have died in\nct during the present century. Near\n\n=== 14_1894-01-02_p1_sn83009653_00279524123_1894010201_0220.json\nOxford Democrat. [volume] (Paris, Me.) 1833-1933 · 1894-01-02\ncrops: None\nheadline: A Transferred Identity.\n\nBy EDITH SESSIONS ruPPEl\n\nlcopyrisLt. is<l b, American Prss Asssis'\n\n\ntion.1\n\nCHAPTER TX.\nrtsr prrtfuN\n---\n4\n\n\nIiIE RETURN.\n\n\nCrouchei by the gate. I waited. Wait.\ncd for wha1\n\n\nTwas at q loss 20 know what courss 10\ntake. Ought 10 return TO the house.\nrouse Colonel Marchmont and tell him\nof this strange midnight visit of his wife\nTo that terrible and uncanny place,\nWould IL not increase his evident antip-\nathy for heri feared -. and as was\nsincerely anxious 90 bring the two to-\ngether instead of seeing them more wide\nly separated I decided against that sug.\ngcstiOn.\n\n\nNo. I would wait Portals return, Join\nher. be- her 90 con!ide all 10 me-to ex.\nplain what possible connection there\ncould H. between the wife ~ q proud\nplanter AMI southern gentleman and a\nset ~ the lowest ami most debAse,i Of\nhuman beings\n\n\nFor little by little had the appalling\nth-ory cept In my mind that Portia\nMarchmont, bcautiful. refined anil elu.\nca,el woman. was victim ~ some un.\nholy spell; that she was in SYMPATHY\nwith those mysterious and inexplicable\nrites Of Dcad Mans swamp. Her fury\nwhenever the subject OF vooslooi-m was\nmentioned: her anxiety lest her husband\nshould visit the haunt % these night\nbirds: her evident anger when Colonel\nMarchmon, ihra,euci 10 punish the old\nncgr,ss, Iast mistress 20 thcrs awful\nocrymonies. all strengthened my opin.\nIon. Yes, I firmly believed my old friend\na. L. u disciple of thi. ghastly school.\n\n\nThis was what hal nearly wrecked\nher reason. aliena,ei her husbands AZ.\nfiction ani caused her to dislike her\nchild.\n\n\nWas sheto l.. blameil \"No.\" my heart\ncricl ont. No more than n she had by\nsome chance become victim ~ NOR.\nphine or some other insilious drug. In\nsome fashion old Jezclcl hal obtained\ncontrol of AMI enslaycl her mistress ]\nwas determined 10 break the spell and\nsave my friends.\n\n\nhut the kuifc--the knifei What was\nshe duing with that gli,trring WEAPON\nwith the kecn. wicket bla,l,i Oh. NY\npoor Portal Hal she sought that grew.\nsome spot 90 kill hsrsslf' rrinenh-r,l\nher threat. \"J.rmyn Marvhuiont s.lall\nremember one day what hc has acc Im\nplish.d tonight\" Ola. I-rhaI~ thsI\nthen. while was standing idly anl help\nlcssly bs.id, tha, carefully locked : ate.\nsomewhere away in the dim anl awful\nrcccsses \"a Dead Mans swamp ther, was\nfearful work going on. must sav. her.\n| would\n\n\nIn desperation I shock the gate anf\nl.ai uIpn a. callcl-l moan,,i aloud\nD. fear ani agony and then sank dowL\nexhausted Ia the thick ianglcs Of cr-ep\nIng vines.\n\n\nsuddenly heard voices anil sound\nq hurried footsteps on the other side q.\nthe wall. Who was coming\n\n\nstarted a. my fc.t and crowded hack\nout II sight among the vines. The key\nclicked in the lock. ,h. gate swung open\naud POrtia s,epI-,l hurriedly through\nand stopped. looking about. Behind her\nIA the moonlight | saw the awful face\nwhich had loolssi In the arhor q night\naz two before. som. instinct sounded\ndanger signal. ani r-pr-s-~i my incli-\nnation to -pring forward and speak to\nPnrtia.\n\n\nIl was snre I heard something\" she\nmurmured.\n\n\nThe old hag listened with bent head.\n\n\n1t was lie wind. honey. an yet 10y dc\n\n=== 23_1846-12-03_pna_sn84024656_00414184637_1846120301_1106.json\nRichmond daily Whig. [volume] (Richmond, Va.) 1842-1861 · 1846-12-03\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nErfItrs o. RtgpRsE.-d WDTdcfsd WDs.-M1sef,\nwho was convicted last Thursday Philadelphia Of\nderng his wife, hung himself his cell Monday\ning with pieces Of his d.shcois Tho nail out md\n\n\ntho a u he body bfoughta watchman into bic coll whon\nmeasures taken prevent any further attempt de-\nstrYy himself This wfcchcd ~ in most frighuul\nstate Of mind tie continually haunted b. the appartor\nol hs murdered WIFE, who is before hmber cash\ncd ihfoa with the blood streaming from H. staring bin ~\nthe face and be boys Tor his immediate execution.\n\n=== 14_1885-05-19_pna_sn85025007_00415663912_1885051901_0461.json\nAlexandria gazette. [volume] (Alexandria, D.C.) 1834-1974 · 1885-05-19\ncrops: None\nheadline: FOREIGN NEWS.\n---\nIn the House of Commons yesterday after.\nnoon, Mr. Gladstone said that the negota.\ntions with Russia concerning the Afghan\nquestion were still unfinished, and on that\naccount the government was unable TO\npresent any more papers on the subject TO\nParliament now, as it was difficult to sepa.\nrate those relating to AkTepe from those\nrelating to other affairs belonging to the\nsame question.\n\n\nIn the House of Lords yesterday Baron\nWemyss denounced the government for\ntheir policy in the Soudan. He said that\nevery man killed in the Soudan was as\nmuch slain by the government, through\ntheir vacillation, Inconsistency and uncer-\ntainty, as if done to death by their own\nhands. The only memorial that England\n\n\nwould leave in the Soudan would be an\nabandoned railway and bleaching skeletons.\nThe news of the Gear having presented\nswords of honor to General KomaroT and\n| Gen. Zakjewski has created an uneasy feel\n| ing in Europe and it is thought the present\npeace is only an armistice, and that the ar\nbitration proceedings are the merest mock\ncry. The Daily News, in commenting on\n| the Afghan papers presented in Parliament,\nsays They show that the seizure of Pend\njeh had long been a foregone conclusion\n| 1t is difficult to believe that the Russians\n\n\nwere ever in earnest about joint commits\nsion.\"\n\n\nIn the House of Commons yesterday Lord\n\n\nRandolph, referring to the Penjdeb Incl\n\n\ndent, said that the indignation aroused by\n| perusal of the dispatches was not confined\n\n\nTO the tory party but was shared By the\n\n\ncountry at large and even by the liberal\n\n\nnewspapers. Lord Randolph maintained\n\n\nnat NJ. de Giers had said nothing to justify\n| Mr.. Gladstone's statement in Parliament\n| that it had been agreed that no further al.\n| vance should be made on either side. He\n| declared that Mr.. Gladstone's statement OF\n| March Is was u fiction and phantom with\n\n\nout The smallest justification.\n\n=== 5_1879-09-21_p6_sn83030313_00271744067_1879092101_1021.json\nThe New York herald. · 1879-09-21\ncrops: None\nheadline: CHAT BY THE WAY.\n---\nCdAI By IdE WAY.\n\n\nThe man who is blind to the faults of his friends\nwill And that he has unusually good eyesight when\nhe looks for their good qualities.\n\n\nlittle girl said to her sister, who wanted to take\nsome cakes which her mother had left on the table,\nmo, Mary. don't take any. for though mother didn't\ncount them. perhaps God did, and then there would\nbe trouble. you know\"\n\n\nWe are apt to take the motto Of our lives from\ncas- You Like it,\" AID it is always perfectly\ncusable for us to do as please; but when\njudge the lives Of our neighbors we read another\nplay and give Measure for Measure\"\n\n\nWhen q gentleman at banquet speaking of\nfriend OF his who had had the smallpox twice. and\nhad died of it. Irishman who inquired\nwhether the man died Of the first or of the second\nattack.\n\n\n1t is all very we'll to send q stupid young man\nabroad that he may the world, but it is great\npity that the world should see him in turn.\n\n\nFrench paper gives new illustration of the\ngeneral subjugaton to which every husband must\nin turn submit Some one stepped lady's\nlong and magnificent train. and she turned\nbrusquely with a few choice epithets just\nready for leap from the end of her tongue.\n1t was handsome stranger who stand\ning on her superfuous silk. The frown changed to\nq smile, the adjectves retired to the inner depths\nand in the most fascinating voice she said. Pardon\n\n\n\n\n\nthought it was my husband\"\n\n\nThere is q certain logic in business which is ex-\nceedingly fascinating. and great many persons do\nbusiness in the logical way to which refer. In\nstance:-A gentleman bought horse Of . dealer\nwho was never before known to get the wrong END\nOF bargain for his portion The agreement-and\nq very simple and fair one It appeared to be-was\nthat one-half of the price named should be paid\ndown and that the buyer should owe for the other\nhalo - I~-I~~\nhimself and demanded his pay our logician replied\nthat the horse had been paid for. How can that\nbe\" cried the unhappy jockey. ''Its clear the\nlight\" replied the man OF superior intelligence.\n.'Our agreement was that should pay you one-half\n| your price. which did, and that should owe you\nfor the other half\" Exactly\" said the jockey;\n\"those were the precise words and now I've come\nfor the other half; don't you see?' II prefer to\nstand by my agreement. for q Of my\nword\" the philosopher responded. \"and how in\nthe world can you for the second half OF your\nprice. IS promised would il L pay it ? No. my\nfriend. I'll stick to my bargain and owe you just\nas said would\"\n\n\nDR.. Fulton is very remarkable man. that in\nteresting conversation which he had with the Lord\nabout taking the Rink amply proves. We have long\nknown that he was not appreciated by the people\nand that he had certain eccentricities Of temper\nwhich made long continued fellowship impossible.\nBut that this want of appreciation the part\npublic the result of its ignorance of his superb\nand wonderful privileges and gifts were not\naware. The greatest among us are always misun-\nderstood. The people refused to call him to the\nRink. and the Lord did it in dream. He is q\ntemperance man. and therefore the dream could not\nhave been induced by the application Of any alco-\nholic stimulus to the BRAIN OF course 1t could not\nhave been Satan who appeared to him and told him\n| to 60 to the Rink. though that would be one way,\n| and some people foolish enough to say q very\nreasonable way. to account for the vision. There\nonly one thing In the story which rather confuses\n| us. and that is that Dr. Fulton wanted to go\n| to the Rink than tongue can tell,\nand that Immediately after he had con-\ncluded to do so the Lord appeared to\nhim and told him to 60. We may remark just here\nthat is very curious fact that certain people al\nways have visions in which they are positively\nmanded to do the very thing which they have been\nwanting do for months. 1t is so easy to hear the\nvoice of the Lord when he calls you from small\nfield Of activity to larger ono; but as the old\nfarmer said. If the Lord should tell you to go from\nbig placeto little he would have call three\nfour times before you would hear him. and then\nyou would certainly declare that 1t Satan\n\n\nIn some parts Of Europe very curious customs\nprevail. the relics Of other days. In Heidelberg for\ninstance. the practice of duelling is popular\nThe law is against 1t, to be but for that\nvery 1t is considered absolute necessity by\nthe students of the university AS you pass along\nthe street your attention is attracted to the faces\ncertain young men which are. according to the popu-\nlar estimation. beauthed and glorihed by the scars\nOF ugly sword cuts They the symbols Of initis-\nton into some one of the college secret societies\nEvery in q while you meet grave Herr profess\nwith long his forehead or cheek. the\nproof OF his vaor in early life 1t is mark Of hon.\norable distinction which money cannot buy; it can\nbe possessed only by the man who has felt the\nkeen edge of the sword. Two students meet in the\nstreet. for example, and the gentlest Of all quarrels\nensues. Then follows the duel In little\nbuilding on the Necksr the chosen and favored\ncompany meets. The bodies of the contest\nants are protected, also the neck,\nwhere the jugular vein offers tempting\nmark and the eyes. The duellsts kiss each other\non both cheeks. and then swords& When the\nlittle affair Is the doctor up the cash, tho\ncontestants kiss again. and the whole company re-\ntire to favorite beer saloon. where the festv\ntics continue indefinitely. On the whole this GUS\n| tom Is not 80 barbarous the \"hazing\" In\nAmerican colleges. for both parties have an equal\nchance. We q freshman up in sack and sus-\npend him from third story window. or make q\nbaseball OF him and hustle him from one end of the\ncollege grounds to another. and leave him with a\nbroken and entire loss of selfrespect; while\nthe German simply puts n sword in his hand. and\neither gives or receives q wound. However. boys\nwill be boys, and IL is absolutely necessary to have\nsome vent for their tremendous and sometimes\nawful exuberance of spirits.\n\n\nThe Spiritualists ought really to be little more\ncareful. cautious. or prudent. or something. We\nhardly know what and II may be that the word ret\ncent is what after II the rule holds good that\nno man has right to speak unless he has something\npleasant to say then these curious folk who play ut\ntahl to nj hpir lnat\nrevelations concerning the future life AXE 1t very\nundesirable thing to die. in old age. II the\nother world is what they describe it to be 1t is q\nplace to live In than this world is. We have\nunhappy marriages here, and when wo choose an\nelective affinity and after while find that the affinity\n\n=== 6_1921-01-17_p8_sn85042354_00383342739_1921011701_0336.json\nEvening journal. · 1921-01-17\ncrops: None\nheadline: DECLARES OUIJA\n\n\nMAKES EN- CRAZY\n\n10000 Lose Minds Delving\nInto Unknown World,\n\n\nAdventist Says\n\nCONFIDENT DEAD\n\n\nWILL RISE AGAIN\n---\n'Nfore than 10000 people went\ncrazy last year through studying the\nmysteries Of the oulia board\" de-\nclared A. G. Hazlett, OF Pittsburgh\nyesterday afternoon, in the course Of\nan address before a meeting at the\nJr. q. u. A. NJ. Hall, held under The\nauspices Of the international Bible\nStudents Association. Mr.. Hazleft\nTOOK as his subject, Resurrection of\nthe Dead Soon\"\n\n\nThe apostles OF Christ tell us\nABOUT the evil spirits Astounding i'm\nlhe earths atmosphere, where thev\nare sent after DEATH\" said Mr.. Haz-\nlett. The actions Of these spirits are\nentirely responsible for the sO-called\nappanii0ns seen by spiritualists and\ncause the actions OF the ouiia board.\n\n\nphysicians Of Great Britain de-\nclare that over 10000 people went\ncrazy last year studying the Ouiia\nboard and attempting TO pierce the\ngreat unknown with their own minds\nwhich they say can conquer mat\nters,\" continued Mr.. Hazlett.\n\n\nThe Bible teaches us that the dead\nWill rise again.\" said Mr.. Hazlett\n\"and when Ve resurrection comes\nwhat will take place has given rise\n! To a great variety Of opinions. 10 Is\nnot for us to Interpret the Bible \"a\nsuit ourselves, we must simply be-\nlieve what Is recorded there. There\nwould be no resurrection IL the dead\nwere not dead, and the Book OF Life\nplainly says that he returns TO the\nearth the very day his thoughts per\nish.\n\n=== 7_1876-02-09_pNone_sn85025567_00212472232_1876020901_0049.json\nThe Grange advance. · 1876-02-09\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nby horseback. Lambert and his com\n\n\npanions knowing this to be the case, de-\n\n\nterminded to follow their prey on foot\nalso. Accordingly the major part of the\nparty at once continued on the trail.\nwhile three or four of the men removed\nthe body and the horses to Stottler's\ninn. Shortly after night had fallen,\nLambert's associates became dissatisfied\nwith their errand. and in body returned\nto the inn.\n\n\nThe intrepid blacksmith noiwithstand.\ning this demoralizing detection, contin.\nued the pursuit alone. After travelling\nin northwestern direction for twelve or\nfourteen miles, he came to an old and\ndisused road, running nearly parallel\nwith the one he had left. This was called\nthe \"Old Pennsylvania Trace\" between\nPhiladelphia and Red Stone Old Fort,\nsituated on the waters of the Mononga-\nhela river. 1t was the first road ever\nopened between Eastern Pennsylvania\nand the head-waters of the Ohio river:\nand the ancient landmarks, ''threc chops\nand blaze were still visible by dav.\nlight on the old trees. This ancient road\nand landmarks were adopted by the sur\nveyors and openers of roads in the West\nto propitiate Heaven in favor of the first\nsettlers of the great western wilderness:\nand hen;e the first chop was for the\nFather, the second for the Son, and the\nthird for the Holy Ghost; and the blaze\nwas merely designated to attract the eve\nof the observer.\n\n\nWhen Lambert struck this old road.\nhe discovered on the north side of it di.\nlapidated log-cabin. Approaching the\ncabin, he noticed a light within, and,\nfeeling satisfied that the murderers were\nclose at hand, he gave loud cough.\nThis brought to the rear door of the\nhouse the old woman who occupied it,\nand who obtained living by selling\ncakes and beer to passing wayfarers.\nLambert, in a feigned voice, asked to\nspend the night within, and was at once\nadmitted. He had but crossed the\nthreshold when the report of muskct\nwas heard in the next room, and the ball\nsplintered the woodwork above his\nhead.\n\n\nWithout moments hesitation, Lam\nbert, with nothing but his tomahawk in\nhis hand, sprang into the next room and\nstruck down the man in whose hand was\nthe still smoking musket. The williams\ncompanion, the smaller man of tn, two,\nat once fed the house. but before he had\npassed beyond the light Of the fire with\nin. the avenger with the wretch's own\nrifle, brought him to the earth in strug.\ngle with death that lasted but second. i.\nAS soon as he had fired the shot, Lam\nbert turned upon the PROSTATE murderer,\nwho was about to arise, and struck him a\nsecond time, telling him to the floor again.\nHe then, with rope furnished him by\nthe old woman, bound the williams hands\nand legs securely. In the morning the\navenger borrowed horse from the wo.\nman. strapped upon its back the living and\ndead murderers, and before nightfal! ar.\nrived atStottler' inn. On the following\nday the living man was publicly lynched\nin front Of the inn, and the bodies were\nthen consigned to an unhcnored grave.\nLambert's companions, craveu-hearted\nas they had proven themselves, we're\nloud in praise of their daring leader, who\nuntil the day Of his death, was hero\nin the humble circle of life in which he\nwas placed.\n\n=== 16_1919-08-08_p1_sn86058226_00271745278_1919080801_0505.json\nThe Richmond palladium and sun-telegram. [volume] (Richmond, Ind.) 1907-1939 · 1919-08-08\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nVenice. He had learned how to sing\nin Professor Porpora's school. He was\nhandsome. He had imagination which\ncolored his fine voice when he sang.\nConsuelo, scrawny, dark-skinned\nSpanish peasant girl, was another Por-\npora's pupils. She was plain, but had\nbeautiful voice and spirit.\nShe and Anzoleto were good friends,\nnothing more, but he-an errant phil\nanderer-was always falling in love\nand coming to Consuelo for hympathy\nand advice. He was a rascal and an\nmigrate.\n\n\nConsuelo made her first public ap-\npearance in the simple dress OF peas\nant girl, and her voice-filled with the\nfire of her soul-took the city. Every\nJ one was at her feet. Young suitors\nsought her hand and among them was\nAnzoleto. 1t was the romantic period\nin Venetian history. about 1850. Con-\nsuelo engaged herself to her fellow\npupil, but repulsed the others. Her\ngenius had won admiration; her dig\nnifty and modesty had won respect\nThe Count Justinjan not only en-\ngagged her for his theatre, but fell in\nlove with her and tried to win her\nfrom Anzoleto. She would not be led\naway oiAu 1lduC ~ part VI utT COLI\ntract that her betrothed should also be\nengaged to sing. Her sweetness and\nfidelity the more deeply enslaved the\ncount\n\n\nConsuelo's rival, singer named\n| Gorilla was bitterly incensed by the\naction of the count. Anzoleto played\non her jealousy and pretended to be\nher lover. OF this perfidy Consuelo\nknew nothing.\n\n\nAt the first public performance Con-\nsuelo achieved renewed success. but\nAnzoleto was almost overlooked.\nHer teacher warned her not to\nmarry Anzoleto and to prove his case\nforced her to visit the home Of Gorilla.\nThey found AnZoleto there and Con-\nsuelo was convinced of his perfidy\nShe repulsed Anzoleto, refused the\nlove of the count and fled to Vienna\non the advice of Porpora.\n\n\nA little later, Porpora sent her to\nhis friend, Count Christian Of b.\nhernia, to serve as companion to his\nniece, the Baroness Amelia, just come\nfrom convent school at Prague Con-\nsuelo, who arrived at the castle on a\nstormy night. was in awe of the count\nand his surroundings, but Amelia, a\nlovely girl, treasured her and won her\ninstant affection. That night, as Con-\nsuelo came, an ancient tree on the es-\ntate, known as the treee of misfortune\nfell in the storm.\n\n\n| News of its fall threw the counts\nhousehold into confusion and terror\nSome evil is abroad\" the countess\nsaid, and soon after Count Albert, the\nson, entered, handsome. pale and\nsad young man, who announced that\nq 10 i,\nover the house. He smiled at Con.\nsuelo, touched her hand and withdrew,\nleaving her deeply mystified.\n\n\nAlbert, she soon learned, was a gen.\ntie and admirable soul, but man Of\nmorbid moods which in effect frances\nand was only mildly interested in\nAmelia who was intended to be his\nwife.\n\n\nAmelia told Consuelo that Albert\nwas not only medium but seer as\nwe'll as scholar and that she found\nhim unattractive.\n\n\nConsuelo, on the contrary, was\ncharmed with Albert, who seemed\nmuch benefited by her presence He\naccepted her as the consolation prom\nisd by an inner voice. Her singing\ncalled him from his frances, trans.\nported and strengthened him. She be.\ncame his physician as we'll as his con.\nlessor\n\n\nShe found herself surrounded by\nmysteries. Secret doors, inexplicable\nflames, gliding phantoms, stirred her\ncuriosity and allured her to explora\nton. Once when Albert had been\nmissing for time they led her to a\nwell. She descended it and found a\nsecret passage. This she traversed\nand hpvnnH If\n\n\ned and i'll, in the care Of an imbecfle\nservant. She nursed him back to\nhealth.\n\n\nConsuelo's exposure and excitemen,\nin this midnight expedition produced q\n\n\nviolent fever and Albert's love and\ncare deepened. For time it seemed\n\n\nas though he might win her hand, but\n\n\nbetween these devoted young people\n\n\nthe dissolute Anzoleto thrust himself\n\n\nAlthough at first she gave way be\nfore him, Consuelo regained her sell\ncontrol and put him aside. The gooc\n\n\nCount Christian, finding her nobly\nfrank about her early life, not only\nexpressed his admiration, but request\n\n\ned her to marry his son, whose reason\n\n\nshe had restored.\n\n\nTo this she answered, The honor is\nvery great, but l am singer, l mus,\n\n\nreturn to my art\"\n\n\nTo avoid Anzoleto she fled by nigh\n\n\ntoward Vienna in order to rejoin Por\npora, her teacher. Consuelo reached\nthere only to find old Porpora {\n\n\nmaster without school or pupil\n\n\nIn her attempts to secure a position\nat the Court Theatre she failed BY\n: reason Of the opposition of the EN-\npress Maria and the enmity Of Gorilla\n\n\nher bitter rival. The corruption, thu\nsavage hatreds which marked the at\nmosphere Of the court and the theatre\n, led her to dream Of Albert and the\n\n\nmarriage he had offered.\nAt last she wrote to Albert, express\n| ing her love for him.\n\n=== 10_1922-06-30_pna_sn85058259_0023728561A_1922063001_1007.json\nIron County record. [volume] (Cedar City, Utah) 1893-1982 · 1922-06-30\ncrops: None\nheadline: NNIIElNINMTNNlNNlIL\n---\nlllh lllIllIlllltlllfa\noverhead the truck separated them\nIL bore down upon Alan, holding him\nmotionless and, on the other side, II\ncrushed upon Corvets legs.\n\n\nHe turned over as far as he could,\nand spoke TO Alan. \"You have beea\nsaving me, SO now l tried to save YOU\nhe said simply. what reason did yos\nhave for doing that7 Why have yoi\nbeen Keeping by met\"\n\n\n\"Im Alan Conrad Of Blue Rapids,\nKansas\" Alan cried TO him. And\nyou're BENJAMIN Corvetl You know\nme; you sent for mel Why did you\ndo thnt2\"\n\n\nCoryet made no reply to this. Alan.\npeering at him underneath the truck,\ncould see that his hands were pressed\nagainst his face and that his body\nshook, Whether this was from some\nnew physical pain from the movement\nof the wreckage, Alan did not know\nTIN he lowered his hands after mo-\nment; and now he did not heed Alan\nor seem even to be aware of him.\n\n\nDear little Connlel\" he said aloud\nDear little Connie She mustn't\nmarry hlm-not him That must be\nseen to. What shall do. what shall\nI do?\"\n\n\nAlan worked nearer him. why\nmustn't she marry him?' he cried to\ncorvet \"why1 Ben Corvet, tell me |!\nTell me. Why\"\n\n\n\"Who are your\" Corvet seemed only\nwith an effort to become conscious OF\nAlan's presence.\n\n\nI'm Alan Conrad, whom you used\nTO take care of. I'm from Blue Rap\nIds You know about me; are you my\nfather, Ben Corvet2 Are you my ia\nTheir or what--what are you to mel\"\n\n\n\"YOur fatherT Corvet repeated.\n\"Did ne tell you tbat2 He killed your\nfather\"\n\n\nKILLED him? Killed him, howl\"\n\n\n\"Of course. lie killed them all-all,\nBut your father--he shot him; he shot\nhim through the head!\"\n\n\nAlan twinged. Sight of spearman\ncame before him as he had first seen\nSpearman, covering In Corvets H.\nbrary in terror at an apparition. \"And\nthe bullet hole above the eyel\" SO\nthat was the hole made by the shot\nSpearman fired which had killed\nAans father-which shot him through\nthe head! Alan peered at COrvet and\ncalled TO him.\n\n\nFather Benitotl\" Corvet called In\nresponse, not directly In reply TO\nAlan's question. rather in response to\nwhat those questions stirred. \"Father\nBenitot %\n\n\nSome one. drawn by the cry, was\nmoving wreckage near them. A hand\nand arm with torn sleeve showed\nAlan could not see the rest of the fig.\nIRE, but by the sleeve he recognized\nthat II vas the mate.\n\n\n\"Who's caught here2\" he called\ndown.\n\n\n\"Benjamin Corvet of Corvet, Sher\nrill and Spearman. ship owners Of Chi\ncago.\" Corvet's voice replied deeply\nfully; there was authority In II and\nwonder too-the wonder OF n man find\ning himself In situation which his\nrecollection cannot explain.\n\n\n\"Ben Corvetl\" the mate shouted In\nsurprise; he cried II 10 the others\nthose who had followed Corvet and\n| obeyed him during the hour before ant\nhad not known why. The mate tried tC\npull the wreckage aside and make his\nway TO COrvet but the old man stopped\nhim. 1.-THE priest. Father Benitot\n| 90 4 oh %\nhere; send Father Benitot -\n\n\nThe word was passed without the\nmate moving away. The mate. after {\nminute, made no further attempt Il\nfree Corvet; that indeed was useless\nand Corvet demanded his right Of sac\nrament from the priest who came ant\ncrouched under the wreckage beside\nhim.\n\n\n\"Father Benitotl\"\n\n\nIL am not Father Benltot. am ia\nTheir Perron of LAnse.\"\n\n\nat was 10 Father Benitot OF ST, Is\nmace l should have gone, Father .\nThe priest got Il little closer as Cor\nvet spoke, and Alan heard only voice\nnow aud then through the sounds o\nchanging metal ana the drum of ic\nagainst the hull. The mate and hi\nhelpers were working to get him free\nThey had abandoned all effort to saw\nthe ship; Il was settling. And Wit\nthe settling. the movement Of tn\nwreckage lmprisoning Alan was In\ncreaslng. This movement made useles\nthe efforts of the mate; II would FIRE\nAlan Of Itself In moment, II 1t dl\nnot kin him; It would free or finig\nCoryet too. But he, as Alan saw bin\nwas wholly obllvious OF that now. Ill\nlips moy'i Quietly, firmly; and his eye\nwere fixed steadily on the eyes ~ IN\npriest\n\n\nITO BE CONTINUED\n\n=== 4_1917-04-02_pna_sn95066012_00202197115_1917040201_0003.json\nThe Bennington evening banner. (Bennington, Vt.) 19??-1961 · 1917-04-02\ncrops: None\nheadline: ---- ------------=-----=\nBrevity OF a Dream.\n---\nOne evening Victor Hugo was dictat-\nIng letters to his secretary. Overcome\nby fatigue, the great man dropped into\nd slurnber. A few moments afterward he\nawoke. haunted by dream which. as\nbe thought, had extended over several\nhours, and he blamed his secretary for\nsitting there waiting for him instead\nOf Weakening him or else going away.\nWhat was his surprise when the be.\nwildered secretary told. him that he\nhad only just finished writing the last\nsentence dictated to him and that Hugo\ncould have been doing only for few\nseconds.\n\n=== 20_1931-04-11_pna_sn84020097_00332897676_1931041101_0400.json\nThe daily worker. [volume] (Chicago, Ill.) 1924-1958 · 1931-04-11\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nEOREcAsTLE, a low. narrow and\n4 q dark hole. An evil-smelhng hur-\nricane lamp is suspended from the\nceiling. The Pickering flame is sick,\nvery sick, and yet 1t is vaiantly\nstruggling to keep alive; suddenly 1t\nflares up angrily, almost spitetully,\nand then Just as suddenly, the flame\nshrinks as if taken by internal\ncramps and draws contours Of the\nmen before the mast, contours that\nmay look grotesque, but, nevertheless,\nare real, very real indeed.\n\n\nStuffy. heat-charged, oppressive\natmosphere. Half a dozen men, tired,\ndead, beat after the sweltering day,\nare already in their bunks-bunks\nfrom which comes smell Of perspira-\nton, coal dust and tar, all mixed\nin one. Several men sitting around\nthe table are reading, or trying to\nread watching their chance when the\nlight flares.\n\n\nSewing burlap patches over the\nshoulder and back - two men are\nmending their shirts. their only\nshirts, and, consequently, they have\nstripped themselves to the waist.\nThey are sewing, their broad should\ners and backs are bent, the red\nstreaks Of sun-buun. like lsshes Of\nwhip, run over the ripping muscles\nthat stand out so prominently and\nsignify-POWER.\n\n\nAn oppressive atmosphere is stuf-\nfed in the forecastle, and al is so\nfrightfully quiet, Yet, one can feel\nthat there Is something in the air.\nAS If somebody here in agony, and\nwanted to scream aloud, protest\nbut IS suppressing his pain, which\nleaves him feverish, trembling with\ninternal heat. burning with unrest\nand then being unable to hold any\nlonger. he would get up, grit his teeth\nand grapple with his mortal enemy\nwhom he would smash, tear to pie\nces thus it feels.\n\n\nOn deck all is quiet. The tropical\nnight is calmly looking down, and\nadnliring with a sarcastic grin the\nold ship Margaret, her lofty spars,\nTiggings that are painted white and\nin the moonl1t night shine and gilt\nter like silver, giving her the look\nof q phantom ship that on leaving\nport and sailing forth - is never\nheard of again\n\n\nA rowboat coming from shore\nhangs against the side Of the ship.\nThe ladder clatters as somebody is\ncoming up, and jumps on deck.\nPowerful steps are heard going for\nward. they stop. then turn and enter\nthe forecastle The hurricane light\nIn shame and anguish. it shines\nbrighter, gives welcome flare and\nreveals deep-chested sailor man\nwho has just entered and stands in\nthe middle Of the floor with his\nbrawny arms folded across his chest.\nHe is about to speak, He looks\naround Again the light contracts\nand then suddenly flushing red as if\nin shame and anguish, it hines\nupon the late corners weather beaten\nand rather handsome face that is\nnow bruised. battered, swollen and\none eye almost closed.\n\n\nwell, Olaf, what did the consul\ntell you?\" Anders confronted him\nAnders big, raw-boned Finn in\nquiringly looking through his deep\nset eyes that made a strange con-\ntrast with his weather. beaten, battle\nSCARED face by virtue of being so\nblue, so clear and of such strength\nand determination rightly to be adj\nnlired.\n\n\nooh, the b\" exclaimed Olaf\nHe looked around, his swollen face\ntook a sad expression. HE threw\nme out\" he began, and after a pause,\ncontinued: what could 7 do? I\ntold the consul how the captain had\nframed an innocent man, thrown him\nIn prison in order to clear himself Of\nhaving deliberately may as well say:\nthrown the soldier overheard! I also\nshowed the consul our statement\nsigned by all Of US, how the captain\nis responsible for the death of three\nOf our shiprlates, who we're washed\noverheard when he preached the ship\ninto the gale.\n\n\nBut the consul simply laughed\nHe tore up our statement He did\n1t SO coolly, deliberately tearing it into\nsmall bits, over and over again, and\nthen he let the small bits fall into\nthe waste basket, like he was play\ning. Again he laughed. stood and\nlooked, just looked. The consul saw\nme and frowned. Get out Of herer\nhe cried\"\n\n\n7 would not go. 7 still persisted,\nwanted to tell, explain how the\ncaptain had so unjustly thrown jim\nmy in prison. I wanted him to be\n\n=== 3_1843-11-17_pna_sn84020074_00415661320_1843111701_0481.json\nThe daily Madisonian. [volume] (Washington City [i.e. Washington, D.C.]) 1841-1845 · 1843-11-17\ncrops: None\nheadline: NEW MUSIC.\n---\nlust RECEIVED, the following pieces Of New\nd Music, at the old established store, two doors\neast of 18th street, Pennsylvania Avenue.\n\n\nw. FISCHER.\nI'll neer forget that sunny glen-by Miss c. r., of\n\n\nBaltimore.\nIn the valley by the mildly John J. Davis.\nEve wandered afar from the land of my brth-by X.\nG. Shrval.\nI'll lo'e thee, Mary-by J. Williams.\nMy Cousin Mary Bell.\n| never have been false to thoe-by +. Romani.\nWe met strangers, Lady-by ii. Rohbock.\nseek thy bower-by \" Peter Spyke\"\nForget thee\nOh forget not the time when delighted met thee\n\n\nby r. c. Shrival.\nThy image haunts me yet.\nThe surprise waltz-by J. kuiner.\nIyanhoe waltz.\nPolish march-by J. NJ. Deems.\nMonumental quick step.\nFrankfort Gallop-by F. Burgmuller.\nWhite Sulphur gallop.\nMagnolia gaop-by H. Rohbock.\nTwo rondos from the opera Of Car and thinner\n\n\nmann.\nGems from the opera of Normally J. +. Petri.\n\n\noct 10\n\n=== 6_1878-01-30