e needed bro\n49\tlection the heritage affords TO the\n50\troots is wanting, and the rop cannst\n51\twithstand the severe frosts. from\n52\twhich il is no longer sheltered. The\n53\tresult Is a short crop, for which the\n54\tspring rains are blamed, or the heavy\n55\tshowers in May or June, which knock\n56\tof the blossom, or the hot sun which\n57\tblasts it. or the nudge which sucks\n58\tits juices, and many OTHER things ; all\n59\tOf w%hich,arc probably innocent, and\n60\tthe real blame attaches TO the farmer\n61\twho allowed his cows To ent Off the\n62\tfall growth. 1t is very rarely indeed\n63\tthat grain needs to be eaten off: and\n64\tIf such should be the case, calves.\n65\tsheep or pigs, well ringed, are better\n66\tthnn cows or heavy stock.-[Hearfh\n67\tand Home.\n68\t\n69\t\n70\tEREAKFAsT ROLLS, Pour sufa.\n71\tGlen. quantity boiling water on one\n72\tpint cornmeai to make a thick cruel\n73\tthen lei it stand until nearly cold\n74\tthen add salt aud one large spoonful\n75\tof yeas. mix II wheat -four until it il\n76\tIt'll dough ; kncad II well, aud then\n77\tlet il stand in q warm place for seven\n78\tor eight hours 10 rise: when hight\n79\tbake into rolls, aud bake one hour.\n80\t\n81\t=== 5_1885-02-02_pna_sn84022871_00202194412_1885020201_0226.json\n82\tOrleans County monitor. [volume] (Barton, Vt.) 1872-1953 · 1885-02-02\n83\tcrops: None\n84\theadline: \n85\t\n86\tHr All notices ofdeaths inserted free. Obituary\n87\tnotices and poetry Jfveeemtsperline.\n88\t---\n89\tIn Craftsbury, Jan. 25, Candace Mason, aged 81\n90\tyears.\n91\t\n92\t\n93\tIn Morgan, Jan. 20, of congestion of the lungs,\n94\tAbbie Lord, wife of Arthur Daggett, aged IS yrs.\n95\tIn Holland, Jan. %, L. C. Heath, aged az years.\n96\tIn Sutton, Jan. 2Sd, of cancer, Jane, wife of Luz\n97\tTheir Gray, aged So years, months, 81 days.\n98\tShe was the aAly daughter of the late Luther\n99\tRice. Was horn in Sutton, and respected in\n100\tchildhood by all who knew her. In early life she\n101\tl