 Hawaiian gazette. [volume] (Honolulu [Oahu, Hawaii]) 1865-1918 · 1888-09-25\n165\tcrops: None\n166\theadline: \n167\t---\n168\tTHE \"Aki Claim mot to use the\n169\t\n170\t\n171\tharsher term OF pre-revolution times\n172\t\n173\t\n174\thas a second time gone indelibly upon\n175\tthe page of Hawaiian history Every\n176\t\n177\t\n178\tpatriotic heart must fain have wished\n179\t\n180\t\n181\tthat there was an end to that disagreea-\n182\t\n183\t\n184\tBILE matter being Haunted before the\n185\t\n186\t\n187\teyes OF the world, when His Majesty\n188\t\n189\t\n190\tmade solemn engagement with dele\n191\t\n192\t\n193\tCATION OF the people to see that justice\n194\t\n195\t\n196\twas done without stint in the matter.\n197\t\n198\t\n199\t1t must therefore have caused q\n200\t\n201\t\n202\tgeneral sensation OF pain through\n203\tout the community when, few\n204\t\n205\t\n206\tmonths ago, it was whispered\n207\t\n208\t\n209\tabout that the King through his\n210\tagents-appointed primarily so that the\n211\tstipulated justice should be executed\n212\thad taken the responsibility OF repudi-\n213\tsting the obligation OF kingly honor.\n214\tThis position adopted by His Majesty\n215\tcompelled the devisee in trust OF the\n216\ttestator-claimant to seek redress for the\n217\theirs OF the partly self-wronged man in\n218\tthe Courts of the Kingdom. Thus has\n219\tcome the second PRONUNCIATION of the\n220\tnations shame through the public hear\n221\ting and records of the cause The re-\n222\tsuit OF the suit is to be found in Judge\n223\tPreston's lucid and closely-reasoned de-\n224\tcision published in full in this issue. 1t\n225\tis that the claim having been publicly\n226\tacknowledged and arrangements to\n227\twhich the King was freely party hav-\n228\ting been made for its liquidation,\n229\tit has passed out of the province\n230\twhere it might be outlawed\n231\tas part OF an illegal transaction.\n232\tMoreover and here it will be OBJ\n233\tserved that the royal privilege of i'm\n234\tmunity from all imputation of wrong\n235\tdoing proves two-edged sword-it is\n236\timpossible that any matter or thing per-\n237\ttanning to the Kings person, or estate,\n238\tor dominion, can be considered on any\n239\tbasis OF recognizing that anything illegal\n240\tor wrong can be done by the Sovereign\n241\tunder our Constitution. The decision\n242\tjust rendered is not necessarily final,\n243\tyet it is surely improbable that an\n244\tappeal will be taken to the full bench.\n245\tJustice Preston has woven a seemingly\n246\tweb OF fact, law and equity\n247\tin front OF his conclusions, and, if pos\n248\tsibly, there are loopholes through which\n249\tto escape the essential points in His\n250\tHonor's reasoning, it would be subject\n251\tOF universal regret if the unsavory sub\n252\trelating to the pecuniary\n253\tissue should once more have to be par\n254\tAIDED before the nation and the world.\n255\t\n256\t=== 39_1909-03-27_p10_sn83045433_100493299_1909032701_0329.json\n257\tThe Washington herald. · 1909-03-27\n258\tcrops: None\n259\theadline: SERVICES TO-MORROw\n260\t\n261\tSPIRITUALISM.\n262\t---\n263\tMESSAGES FROM THE SPIRIT WORLD. Al\n264\tfred II. Terry VIII hold a meeting SUNDAY even\n265\ting n O'Clock. Lecture followed hy Spirit mes\n266\tsageS and psschcaetric readings 81 Schmidts\n267\tHan SIC sth st. NV\n268\t\n269\t\n270\tDIR. J. NJ. TEMPLE. BUSINESS AND TEST\n271\tmedium viii hold grand test meeting for spiritual\n272\trnessar-s Sunday. Tuesday. ard Friday az s D. m.\n273\tReadings dsiy. so 9Th 81. NV\n274\t\n275\t\n276\tMRS. NOBLE HOLDS SPIRITUAL MEETINGS\n277\tevery sunday Tuesday. and Friday nights az\n278\tO'Clock. Private readings daiiy. l12s llth S. nE\n279\t\n280\t=== 3_1898-03-05_p12_sn85066387_00175037676_1898030501_0072.json\n281\tThe San Francisco call. · 1898-03-05\n282\tcrops: None\n283\theadline: Refugee Took a Purse Supported by a Strong\n284\t\n285\t Company.\n286\t\n287\tCharley Thorpe and Tod Sloan Divided the Riding\n288\t\n289\t HOnOPS.\n290\t\n291\tGold Scratch Won the Two Year Old Event at Long Odds-Ostler Joe\n292\t\n293\t First in the Handlcap.\n294\t---\n295\tFog was again strong factor at h. eleside yesterday. One event only was decided with the spectators enjoying an unobstructed view of the horses. The favorites met with three reverses, and it is more than likely that in one race at least there were some big do- ings\" Tod Sloan piloted three and Charley Thorpe two winners.\n296\t\n297\t The opening mile run for maidens was only a gallop for Twinkle Twink, which ran in Don Cameron's colors and won eased up to a walk, after going to the post a 4 to choice in the betting In a drive Durward took the place from Whirlwind.\n298\t\n299\t Johnny Campbell finally started the speedy Punter in the six fur- long selling dash that followed, and the wise ones grabbed the even money that was offered about the lilly. It took all of Tod Sloan skill in the saddle to land her winner, a head before the second choice, Prompto Moringa, as usual, finished in the show\n300\t\n301\t The stable, it was said, did not play Gold Scratch to win the four furlong scramble for year-olds At any rate, the gray youngster got the best of a struggling start, and at odds of F to 1 finished over lenStn III front of Primavera, the second choice. Ach, the Corrigan entry, an II to 10 favorite, was beaten for the show by Mossbrae.\n302\t\n303\t Fog undoubtedly proved a strong factor in the victory of Refugee. In the betting on the mile selling event Willie Sinks cripple opened at odds of to l. His price heightened until 20 was reached, when he was played down to and TO- lie won by less than a length from the favorite. Key del was evidently a \"shoo in,\" as two or three of the horses which finished be- hind him are capable of running a much faster mile Of the other start CTS Fashion Plate was probably the only one that was not a lobster and she was dead last time out It was passed over like many of the other dis graceful seances of the present racing season.\n304\t\n305\t A big consignment of coin forced the odds of Caliente down from to to to In the betting on the six furlong selling affair following, and be became lost in the fog. Hurly Burly, the Cor rigan entry, a to 1 chance in the ring, won handily from Bonita, a 12 to 1 shot Chihuahua, another rank out sider was third.\n306\t\n307\t The black horse, Ostler Joe, was fa- vorite for the mile and a sixteenth handicap. which, like most of the others at this track. was a pronounced frost, and he scored without difficulty from Glorian, at one time as good as 20 to in the betting. The second choice, Greyhurst, quit like dog, ending up in the far rear.\n308\t\n309\t=== 21_1897-12-09_p1_sn84026355_00211100242_1897120901_0567.json\n310\tThe Scranton tribune. [volume] (Scranton, Pa.) 1891-1910 · 1897-12-09\n311\tcrops: None\n312\theadline: Gen. Goblin Calls n Mee,i,,\n313\t---\n314\tHarrisburg, Dec. S.-General Goblin.\n315\tcommander-in-Ghief of the Grand Ar\n316\tmy of the Republic, has called a\n317\tmeeting Of the executive committee of\n318\tthe national council at Cincinnati, De-\n319\tcember 10. The date for the next ma\n320\t\n321\t=== 21_1922-04-27_p1_sn86063952_00295864850_1922042701_0828.json\n322\tThe Guthrie daily leader. [volume] (Guthrie, Okla.) 1893-1996 · 1922-04-27\n323\tcrops: None\n324\theadline: Bold\n325\t\n326\t\n327\tBandits\n328\t\n329\t\n330\tat Tulsa\n331\t---\n332\tTulsa, Apfn \"''nih ~\n333\tthan = hieek o. ihe station VII\"\n334\t. night ~s'shmsn sleeping IN Inc\n335\trear 81 the building an unhno\"n\n336\t\n337\t\n338\tburglar 81 s.\" o'clock 'h's\n339\tmorning limm'ed OH the swinging\n340\tdoors IN the icyer OF = do\"h'\n341\ttown fheatre' rr'e1 open me uoer\n342\t10 the Office OF \"i''.Hm Sn''n'\n343\tproprietor; knocked ihr e\"muih,\n344\ta''.h OH 'he o'k >a'' s\"a esson ,oa\n345\t\n346\t=== 8_1905-06-22_p6_sn84020358_00271744456_1905062201_1353.json\n347\tThe daily morning journal and courier. · 1905-06-22\n348\tcrops: None\n349\theadline: COMPOUNCE sPIRITUALIsTs.\n350\t---\n351\tPast Year Prosperous One for the\n352\t\n353\t Society-Election of Officers and a\n354\t\n355\t Seance.\n356\t\n357\t Bristol, June 21-At the annual meet ing of the Compounce Spiritualists' association, which was held at the lake to-day, the. following officers were elected:\n358\t\n359\t President-T. B. Kenyon, of New Ha- ven.\n360\t\n361\t Vice-president-Mrs. J. S. Storrs, of Hartforr.\n362\t\n363\t Secretary-Mrs. J. E. Dillon, of Hart ford.\n364\t\n365\t Delegate to the national convention Mrs J. s. storrs;s alternate, Mrs. J. E. Dillon. h. There were about 300 in attendanle.\n366\t\n367\t=== 30_1906-11-13_p8_sn85042462_00175047414_1906111301_0233.json\n368\tLos Angeles herald. · 1906-11-13\n369\tcrops: None\n370\theadline: \n371\t---\n372\tish girl. the sorrowing widow and the\n373\tcallow youth, answer this question,\n374\tfor l cannot.\n375\t\n376\t\n377\tWorse than the old. old query, why\n378\tis a cow\" it has proven.\n379\t\n380\t\n381\tSunday night, in company with Mr..\n382\tHazel, l went to a meeting of the\n383\tTruth Seekers society. / sat there and\n384\thad the pleasure of listening TO re-\n385\tmarks the reverse of complimentary\n386\tabout The Herald. myself and Mr.. Ha-\n387\teel for being so unkind as to abuse\n388\tthe poor fake mediums who are trying\n389\tso hard to make a living by fooling\n390\tpeople who can i'll afford to be fooled.\n391\t\n392\t\n393\tUndoubtedly the speakers in that\n394\tmeeting had read The Herald expose\n395\tof the takers and they had even taken\n396\tthe trouble to visit the exhibit of spir-\n397\titualistic paraphernalia in The Herald\n398\twindow, for they commented upon 1t\n399\tfreely. Unfortunately, however, the\n400\tguide, he who is supposed to see that\n401\tno Objectionable\" characters enter the\n402\tsacred precincts of the circle. had not\n403\ttaken the precaution to look upon Mr..\n404\tHazeIs picture. for we passed the door\n405\twithout a challenge.\n406\t\n407\t\n408\tAgain, Why is a guide?\n409\t\n410\t\n411\tIconocIasts Not Recognized\n412\t\n413\t\n414\tThe spirits that pervade ''the third\n415\tdimension of space\" according to the\n416\tmediums. are supposed to be cognizant\n417\tof everything going on in the room\n418\tThese spirits should have warned the\n419\tmedium that there were aliens\" in\n420\tthe room, but alas, they were remiss in\n421\ttheir duties, and we were permitted to\n422\tsit unnoticed and listen to our arraign-\n423\tment To sit there with our ears burn\n424\ting to a scarlet while women told us\n425\twhat deep dyed villains we are for tell\n426\ting the uninitiated how the faker fools\n427\tthe unwary, the charlatan swindles the\n428\tunfortunate.\n429\t\n430\t\n431\tPart of all they said about us we\n432\tmissed We entered Mammoth hall,\n433\twhere the meeting was held, after\n434\tmost of the neophytes, believers and\n435\tfrauds had taken their seats and set\n436\ttied themselves for q night of marvel\n437\tGus relevations.\n438\t\n439\t\n440\tA man with a Vandyke beard and\n441\tmelancholy expression was talking.\n442\t\n443\t\n444\tIn high pitched, strident voice, rem\n445\tiniscent of Little Effie\" of Elsie\n446\tReynolds creation, he was telling his\n447\tfriends that The Herald should be con-\n448\tdemned for roasting spiritualism be-\n449\tcause spiritualism is in the minority.\n450\t\n451\t\n452\tWe suppressed our desire to make\n453\tspeech to the effect that The Herald\n454\tdoes not condemn spiritpalism, but\n455\tonly those imposterS who pull the heart\n456\t\n457\tOffering a vulgar substitute for that\n458\twhich is most sacred to- man.\n459\t\n460\t\n461\tWhen the speaker had finished his\n462\tcondemnation of The Herald, the as\n463\tsembIy joined In a song, and then\n464\tmedium sang and played under in\n465\tspiration.\n466\t\n467\t\n468\tMusic Fright\n469\t\n470\t\n471\toh, that song! If there were any\n472\tspirits floating around in the atmos-\n473\txr,ha troll\n474\tbegan to sing in one key and play in\n475\tanother it would be safe to wager\n476\tthey all departed at the second bar.\n477\t\n478\t\n479\tIf such is inspiration, may heaven\n480\thereafter deliver us from it.\n481\t\n482\t\n483\tIf such is harmony, we might pray\n484\tthat we will always sound the discord.\n485\t\n486\t\n487\tThe inspired musician\" was not the\n488\tgreatest attraction.\n489\t\n490\t\n491\tShe was only prologue to the main\n492\tshow of the evening.\n493\t\n494\t\n495\tThe medium requested that the lights\n496\tover her head be turned out, and then\n497\tshe closed her eyes. Her two diamond\n498\tearrings glistened in the half light and\n499\ther voice, pleasant voice, was stilled\n500\tfor a moment while she allowed her\n501\tspirit control to take possession of her.\n502\t\n503\t\n504\tThis occupied but a moment as she\n505\tannounced that the conditions were\n506\tvery harmonious. You see, the guides\n507\thad not told her that there were any\n508\tObjectionable persons in the room.\n509\t\n510\t\n511\tEverything is very pleasant and ev-\n512\terything is in harmony, so 1 think we\n513\twill be able to give some very good\n514\tmessages this evening\"\" said the me-\n515\tdunn.\n516\t\n517\t\n518\tters of the audience, old truth seek\n519\ters'' who have been there year in and\n520\tyear out and whose lives have long\n521\tbeen ancient history to the mediums.\n522\t\n523\t\n524\tWe hoped for a message but we were\n525\tdoomed to disappointment, for the me-\n526\tdunn had nothing for US.\n527\t\n528\t\n529\tMessages\" came freely to others,\n530\tbut they were the usual commonplace\n531\tpromises and commands.\n532\t\n533\t\n534\tGo Away Disappointed\n535\t\n536\t\n537\tThe woman asked if there were any\n538\twealthy gentleman in the audience,\n539\tbut there was no one who evidently\n540\tfelt himself so qualified, or if so he\n541\twas too baShful to answer. A women\n542\tarose. Perhaps you mean me-\" she\n543\tsaid.\n544\t\n545\t\n546\tThat's right; do mean you, Sister\n547\t-\" calling her by name.\n548\t\n549\t\n550\tI'm about five months two men will\n551\tcome to you to get you to sign a con-\n552\ttract. One of them will be an old\n553\tman and another a young man. Be\n554\tcareful of the young man and do not\n555\ttrust him too much. Do not sign the\n556\tcontract for two months, then every\n557\tthing will come out all right\"\n558\t\n559\t\n560\tTo one woman the medium brought\n561\tson who told her that by the first\n562\tof May everything would be lovely.\n563\tThe flowers would be blooming, the\n564\tbirds singing and she would have\n565\tpeace of mind. The spirit is evidently\n566\tfamiliar with California in spring. or a\n567\tmessage so definite could never have\n568\tbeen given.\n569\t\n570\t\n571\tTo mother she brought the spirit\n572\tOF little girl and the child came with\n573\tbouquet of flowers in her arms and a\n574\tmessage of hope and love. Told her\n575\tthat she was doing everything she\n576\tcould to help her and asked the mother\n577\tTO send out her best thoughts to the\n578\tspirits tht-t the spirits might be helped\n579\t1t is good rule, for 1t works both\n580\tways. The spirits help the mortals and\n581\tthe mortals help the spirits.\n582\t\n583\t\n584\tFinally, disappointed that we had\n585\tpaid 10 cents each and had received nc\n586\tmessages, we left.\n587\t\n588\t\n589\tThe Herald spook editor was show\n590\tcred with requests for knowledge on\n591\tvarious points yesterday and some OF\n592\tthem appear I'm this issue.\n593\t\n594\t\n595\tMANY LETTERS COME\n596\t\n597\t\n598\ttn Tue cuo0H rnIrnu\n599\t\n600\t=== 3_1909-09-07_p1_sn89053204_00393344283_1909090701_0143.json\n601\tAmericus times-recorder. [volume] (Americus, Ga.) 1891-current · 1909-09-07\n602\tcrops: None\n603\theadline: GHOST OF BOB FULTON\n604\t\n605\t\n606\tSEEN INsNEW YORK\n607\t\n608\tCIermOnt Has Trial Trip\n609\t\n610\t\n611\tOn Hudson.\n612\t---\n613\tNew York, Sept Cone hundred\n614\tyears was bridged when the very\n615\tmuch materialized ghost of Robert\n616\tFulton's steamboat, Clermont, whirl-\n617\ted her wheels in the waters of New\n618\tYork bay. AS great modern craft\n619\tpassed her she seemed a pigmy of the\n620\t| river, but they footed their whistles in\n621\t| respectful salutation, as a small boy\n622\ttakes off his cap to his granddad, even\n623\tthough the old gentleman is creaky\n624\tin the joints and sadly behind the\n625\ttimes.\n626\t\n627\t\n628\tThe new Clermont is nearly ready\n629\tto take her place in the Hudson-Ful-\n630\tton celebration, and her builders, the\n631\tStaten Island Shipbuilding Company\n632\tgave her a trial spin yesterday\n633\tThrough the Kill van Kull as far\n634\tup as New Brighton she ran against\n635\tthe tide at a speed of almost six\n636\tmiles an hour. That she exceeded\n637\tslightly the rate of her old self was\n638\tdue to the better condition of her en-\n639\tgines She returned with equal ease,\n640\tand when she was again at her pier\n641\tw. J. Davidson, president of the com\n642\tpany expressed thorough satisfaction\n643\twith the result of the test\n644\t\n645\t\n646\tAs the Clermont moved along UNI\n647\tder her own steam she attracted much\n648\tattention along the Staten Island and\n649\tJersey shores. Low lying, and only\n650\tlao feet Ions, she. contrdsted oddly\n651\twith the larger vessels along the\n652\tshores.\n653\t\n654\t\n655\tThe first things that struck the eye\n656\twere the unprotected paddle wheels,\n657\tthrowing the foam in great masses\n658\tbehind. The stern overhung on each\n659\tside and the bow was sharp In the\n660\t\n661\t\n662\tgntre the big boiler, with it's diminu-\n663\t\n664\t=== 6_1925-08-07_p2_sn96060765_00383341929_1925080701_0576.json\n665\tThe Winslow mail. · 1925-08-07\n666\tcrops: None\n667\theadline: VAST GHOSTS OR\n668\tPLAIN K. K. ks\n669\t\n670\t\n671\tASK THE OLD MAN\n672\t---\n673\tJack O'Brien tells this one about\n674\tone of the hot nights at Needles not\n675\tlong ago.\n676\t\n677\t\n678\t1t was about midnight, and sud\n679\tdenly from one of the rooms of the\n680\thotel at which he was stopping,\n681\tthere came terrifying scream,\n682\twhich was followed by another\n683\tmore terrifying, and still another.\n684\tThen the screaming ceased, but\n685\twas succeeded by a series of\n686\tweird means and low sobs\n687\t\n688\t\n689\tNow, Mr.. O'Brien says, in Needles\n690\tthat night the inmates of the hotel\n691\twere sleeping just about as nature\n692\tmade them, and even then it was\n693\tplenty warm enough.\n694\t\n695\t\n696\tNevertheless in moments sine\n697\tafter the screaming started the\n698\tdoor of every room opened and\n699\tas many as twenty men ran out\n700\tinto the hall wrapping sheets\n701\taround them as they went, with\n702\tMr.. O'Brien and his sheet included\n703\tin the procession.\n704\t\n705\t\n706\t''All we lacked were the pillow\n707\tcases.\" Mr.. O'Brien said, \"and the\n708\tKu Kluxers wouldn't have been in\n709\tit,\"\n710\t\n711\t\n712\tOne after another they tried to\n713\topen the door whence came the\n714\tlow means and walls. The door\n715\twas locked and their manipulations\n716\twere in vain. Then one Of the\n717\tsheet-clad members was detailed\n718\tto go outside and break in through\n719\tthe window He did so and then\n720\topened the door for all to enter\n721\t\n722\t\n723\tOne by one they filed into the\n724\troom and surrounded the bed,\n725\twith the sheets wrapped closely\n726\taround them There before them\n727\tlay an old man who was saying:\n728\t\"Oh, my daughter! oh, my daugh\n729\tteri\" while he writhed and moaned\n730\tas if in great pain.\n731\t\n732\t\n733\twhy, the poor old fellow has a\n734\tnightmare\" one fellow said from\n735\tbeneath his sheet to all the other\n736\tsheets around him. Another re-\n737\tcruit stepped forward and tapped\n738\tthe old gentleman on the forehead.\n739\t\n740\t\n741\tThe old man slowly opened his\n742\teyes and reached out his arms:\n743\tooh, my dau-!\"\n744\t\n745\t\n746\tThen he suddenly blinked. And\n747\tthe whole congregation of sheets\n748\thad to jump on him at once to\n749\tkeep from landing outside the win\n750\tdow in one leap.\n751\t\n752\t\n753\tAS soon as explanations could be\n754\tmade the old man was left alone.\n755\tAS might be expected. no more\n756\twailing was heard from the room\n757\tthat night The poor old fellow\n758\tprobably didn't get far enough\n759\tasleep again to have even a symp-\n760\ttom of a nightmare\n761\t\n762\t\n763\tBut take Mr.. O'Brien's word for\n764\tit, it was a fine parade.\n765\t\n766\t=== 13_1897-07-17_pna_sn83045462_00280655144_1897071701_0585.json\n767\tEvening star. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1854-1972 · 1897-07-17\n768\tcrops: None\n769\theadline: CORRECT SUMNER ATTIRE\n770\t\n771\tProper Articles of Dress for Men During the\n772\t\n773\t\n774\tHeated Term.\n775\t\n776\tSuits VIII De Blue-Shirts and Neck\n777\twear Will Not BE sLoud--Russct\n778\t\n779\t\n780\tShoes Continue I'm Favor.\n781\t\n782\tFro m Harper's Bazaar.\n783\t---\n784\trftu bahar\n785\t\n786\t\n787\tSummer was so long in coming that 1t\n788\tseemed as IL tweeds and overcoats would\n789\tbe the fashion we'll into July. Tailors and\n790\thaberdashers and matters were in despair.\n791\tBolts Of cloths were uncut, and the show\n792\twindows were filled with shirt materials,\n793\tsummer ties and straw hats, but orders\n794\twere few. Now that summer has set in\n795\tand the watering places are in the glory OF\n796\ttheir season. one can begin to judge what\n797\tmen are wearing-quite different thing\n798\t! from that which the shopkeepers would\n799\tlike them to wear\n800\t\n801\t\n802\tBlue Suits Arc in Favor\n803\tThere has been a reaction in favor of the\n804\tblue serge suit. This year they are most\n805\tpopular. In cut there is much variation\n806\tThe sack coat is either four button or three\n807\tbutton. with a roll collar made very much\n808\tlike the Tuxedo or dinner jacket. Again\n809\tthe double-breasted REFER of either rough\n810\tcheviot or smooth blue cloth is very much\n811\tworn. With white duck trousers this coat\n812\tgives man nautical air, which Is quite\n813\t! Fetching at the seaside. Rough cheviots\n814\tmake excellent and very smart sack SUITS\n815\tand some men having their coats in\n816\tthis material-the favorite colors being blue\n817\tand black-made into short three-buttoned\n818\tgetaways. The blues are worn as business\n819\tor morning suits, and the black coats seem\n820\t~ the question afternoon dress IN\n821\tspring and summer. and to prove formida-\n822\t!, bic rivals to the frock, which is not an\n823\tagreeable garment in the warm season.\n824\tA material for sack or business suits\n825\tThlch mas come into great favor IS a thin\n826\trough cheviot of very dark blue or even\n827\tlack, with stripes of lighter blue These\n828\tsuits resemble somewhat the older-fash-\n829\tToned ones, still seen in cheap clothiers'\n830\twindows. of black with thin white stripes\n831\tThe new material, however, is heavier and\n832\tfor stout and short men the pattern is use\n833\tful In making them at least look taller and\n834\tthinner.\n835\t\n836\t\n837\tShirts Are Not \"BriIIfant.\"\n838\t\n839\t\n840\tIn shirts the very brilliant hues have\n841\tbeen put aside, and the blues, the lavenders\n842\tand not too vivid pinks are the most worn\n843\tSolid colors have the preference over pat\n844\tterms, although white stripes are still ia\n845\tvorites Even for the warmest weather linen\n846\tis more in form than either Madras or pom\n847\tgee SILK. One sees fewer brown shirts\n848\tworn, and the semi-neglige, with great\n849\tmother-of-pearl buttons, has been reiegated\n850\t10 the bargain counters. So active has\n851\tbeen the competition among the manufac-\n852\tturerS that today very excellent ready\n853\tmade shirts are being turned out and Of\n854\tTered for sale at very low prices. These\n855\tare well made, with narrow cuffs and two\n856\tTO three button holes-either number now\n857\tbeing fashionable. Colored shirts with de-\n858\ttached cuffs will never be in the wardrobe\n859\tOF any well-dreSsei man. Detached collars\n860\tare permitted, but false\" cuffs never. 1t\n861\tIs needless to state that all .colored shirts,\n862\texcept iannelS, have cuffs of the same\n863\tmaterial, and white collars. The collar in\n864\tvogue this summer is the ali-around turned\n865\tdown. There are numerous variations of\n866\tthese. many of which are excellent In\n867\tbuying or having collars made, be careful\n868\tas to the quality of the linen, and see that\n869\t1t Is of medium thickness. Do not have\n870\tthe wings SLOPE but that they are\n871\talmost rectangular. The straight standing\n872\tcollar Is worn with evening and semi-even-\n873\ting dress, and its height is undiminished.\n874\tCollars with turned down wings at the col\n875\tlar button, otherwise standing, are not as\n876\thigh, and for men with short necks are\n877\tcomfortable and seemly.\n878\t\n879\t\n880\tThe hannel shirts especially those Of tar\n881\ttan and plaid patterns, has come into\n882\tfashion Tor travelling and midsummer ex-\n883\tIRENE neglige. One must have with these\n884\twhite linen collars and cuffs. The collars\n885\tare usually detached and in this one in\n886\tstance, where there is some uncertainty as\n887\tTO good laundry work, the detached cuff\n888\tmay be permitted. There is a raiscn detre\n889\tfor it.\n890\t\n891\t\n892\tNo Change in Neckwear.\n893\t\n894\t\n895\tIn ties there Is absolutely little change in\n896\tfashion, shape, or in effects. One may\n897\twear those OF last summer, If yet fresh,\n898\tard be quite up to date. Shopkeapers are\n899\tpushing the hunting Ascot or stock-the\n900\tlatter a misnomer-and have christened it\n901\tthe golf and bicycle tie. The Madras\n902\tand liEen Ascots attached to a white Mad\n903\tras band, to button around the neck, like\n904\ta Roman collar, are certainly neat, and\n905\twith neglige shirt have good effect. but\n906\tthey lack GOODNESS. The club tie, square,\n907\tan inch in width, to be adjusted by ones\n908\tself in a plain neat bow, will remain the\n909\tfavorite neckwear of the season. Foulards\n910\tin the Old inevitable dark blues, reds,\n911\tgreens and black, with the familiar polka\n912\tcot or diamond, crescent, crescent and\n913\tstar, leaf, or flower patterns univer-\n914\tsally worn. The designs numerous, and\n915\tthe combinations all artistic and in excel\n916\tlent taste. Cotton linen and Ma iras lies\n917\tVIII also be worn, but the foulards arc al\n918\tways i'll good form, and it is difficult TO find\n919\ta substitute for them. There are a few\n920\tnovelties in ties. One is in pattern only-a\n921\tdark blue, green, or biaci: ground. with\n922\tlarge poika-dots of Two contrasting sha les.\n923\tThus white and light blue on a darker\n924\tground Again the London ha5er,lashars\n925\thave sent to this country large consign-\n926\tnients of yiolet-purple and brick-lust red,\n927\tbut there are not many bold spirits who\n928\thave ventured to adopt them.\n929\t\n930\t\n931\tWith the Tuxedo. which will be worn n\n932\tgreat deal this summer, especially at hotel\n933\tana watering-place hops, the black silk or\n934\tsatin ties are smaller, and quite square in\n935\tshape. Although supposed authority has\n936\tbeen quoted to support the theory. it will\n937\tnever be good form TO wear a white waist\n938\tcoat and white tie with Tuxedo. Some\n939\tfearful sins against the canons of good\n940\ttaste and form have been committed re-\n941\t\n942\t=== 3_1853-12-06_pna_sn84024735_00415664345_1853120601_0050.json\n943\tRichmond enquirer. [volume] (Richmond, Va.) 1815-1867 · 1853-12-06\n944\tcrops: None\n945\theadline: POLITICAL.\n946\t\n947\tPHANTOMS.\n948\t---\n949\t:, h,usas wherein men have lived and died,\n950\t\n951\t\n952\tA. hnunt.J houses Through the npen tu\"rs\n953\tThe harmless phan,Oms on their errands glide,\n954\tWin feet that make NO sound upon the hoors.\n955\tWa meet them at the doorway, on the sIair,\n956\tAlong the passages they come and 60,\n957\tImpalpaole Impressions on Inc air.\n958\t\n959\t\n960\t4 eeuse of something moving 10 and fro.\n961\tThere are more guests al table than The hosta\n962\tInvited; the illuminated hall\n963\tIs thronged with quiet inotfensvc enos's'\n964\t\n965\t\n966\tAz quie, rs the pictures on the wall\n967\tThe stranger at my Preside Gamer see\n968\t\n969\t\n970\tThe forms see nor hear the forms neal:\n971\tHe bu, perceiyes what lie; while unto me\n972\t\n973\t\n974\tAll ,hat has been is visible and clear\n975\tWe have no title.deeds to house or lands:\n976\tSwners and occupants Of earlier dates,\n977\tp,o1 graves forgotten stretch their dusty hands,\n978\tAnd hold In norlmain still their old estates.\n979\tThe spirit world around this world OF sense\n980\t\n981\t\n982\tFloats like an atmosphere, and everywhere\n983\tWatts through these earthly msts and vapurs dense\n984\tA vital breath of more ethereal air.\n985\tOur little lives are kept in equipoise\n986\t\n987\t\n988\tBy opposite attractions and desires;\n989\tThis struggle of the instinct that enjoys,\n990\t\n991\t\n992\tAud the more noble instinct that asPHss'\n993\tThe perturba,ons, the perpe.ua Jr\n994\t\n995\t\n996\tOF earthly wants and aspirations n'sn'\n997\tCome from the influence Of that unseen s'af,-\n998\tThat undiscovered planet 10 our sky.\n999\tAnd as tho ,noon from some dark gate OF cloud,\n1000\tThrowa Ger ,he sea floating bridge OF light,\n1001\tAc,o.s whose trembling planks our fancies crowd,\n1002\tIn The realm of MYSTERY and night;\n1003\tSo from the world OF spirits there descends\n1004\t\n1005\t\n1006\tA bridge Of light, connecting II with this,\n1007\tDer whose unsteady floor. that sways and oends,\n1008\tWander our thoughts above the dark abyss.\n1009\t\n1010\t=== 5_1904-05-20_p1_sn8607