been famous for her absence of\n1104\tgood looks. ieg\n1105\t\n1106\t\n1107\tAfter dinner we adjourned to the bar\n1108\ton's hail.\" where the men informally\n1109\tsmoked we drank our coffee. This\n1110\troom is of noble proportions; 10 feet long.\n1111\twith its curiously wrought roof of Span\n1112\tish chestnut nearly 40 feet above the\n1113\tstone pavement A series of stained glass\n1114\twindows of much beauty, in which the\n1115\tarmorial bearings of the Howards, Fitz-\n1116\talans and Montgomerys are emblazoned,\n1117\tfill one side, and opposite hang ancestral\n1118\tportraits and trophies of the chase. A\n1119\tfire blaZed on the great hearth. under Its\n1120\thood of chiseled stone. Palms and rugs,\n1121\thanging lamps and luxurious couches.\n1122\tserved to soften and modernize the some\n1123\twhat grim splendor of the room, whose\n1124\t\n1125\t\n1126\t\n1127\t\n1128\t\n1129\tseemed rather a fitting background for\n1130\trushlights and armored figures.\n1131\t\n1132\t\n1133\tThe next morning / was awakened by\n1134\tthe chapel bell ringing for mathis, and\n1135\thurried down to find the other guests and\n1136\tservants already in their places. The\n1137\tchapel is about half the size of Grace\n1138\tChurch in New York, and contains one of\n1139\tthe finest organs in England. The morn-\n1140\ting sunshine streamed through the open\n1141\tNuTtV, .It. f,It fh.\n1142\tdropping. pathetic Christ, exquisitely\n1143\twrought from bit of wood of the Span\n1144\tish armada. After few simple words\n1145\tfrom Father Francis and a benediction\n1146\ton the coming day, We went out between\n1147\tthe rows of standing servants, quite\n1148\tready for our breakfast which awaited us\n1149\tin the morning room\" This is u small\n1150\tapartment hung in gay chintzes, with long\n1151\twindows opening on the lawn. According\n1152\tito the English custom, there We're no ser\n1153\twants present to mar The freedom of the\n1154\tmorning meal, at which we all seated our\n1155\tselves regardless of precedence. Great\n1156\t'four-in-hand' chatting dishes stood on the\n1157\tside tables, from which the men served\n1158\tthe women, and in the center of the table\n1159\tWe're the coffee and tea on silver turn\n1160\ttable, from which each one helped HIM\n1161\tself as his or her taste dictated Plans\n1162\tWe're made for the day, and we gladly ac-\n1163\tceded to the duke invitation 10 sec\n1164\tsomething OF the park. An hour later we\n1165\tfound him on the terrace, where peacocks\n1166\tstrutted to and fro in the sunshine. Roses\n1167\tclambered over the marble balustrade, and\n1168\tbelow stretched lawns as free from leaf\n1169\tor twig as a drawing-room floor. At the\n1170\tfoot of the steps bath chairs awaited us,\n1171\tdrawn by tiny donkeys in gay trapping.\n1172\t\n1173\t\n1174\ta few days after the rain was whirling\n1175\tin sheets against the windows, so 1 wan\n1176\tdered into the library for a quiet hour.\n1177\tThe great room is an imitation of goth\n1178\tic cathedral, with ceilings and columns\n1179\tof mahogany, and is 100 feet long by 90\n1180\twide. AT one end sniral staircase leads\n1181\tTO a gallery, which runs round the room\n1182\thalf way TO the ceiling. the railing OF\n1183\twhich Is of bronze of exquisite work\n1184\tmanship. Ten thousand Volumes line the\n1185\twalls some of which are Of inestimable\n1186\tvalue. 1t was charming to prowl about\n1187\tamong the many curios which fill the\n1188\tcabinets. fancying how the ghosts of\n1189\tmany famous men might be spying at\n1190\t\n1191\t\n1192\t=== 65_1920-02-06_pna_sn83045389_00296029269_1920020601_1230.json\n1193\tRichmond times-dispatch. [volume] (Richmond, Va.) 1914-current · 1920-02-06\n1194\tcrops: None\n1195\theadline: \n1196\t---\n1197\tnews from Russia is as clear, convincing\n1198\tana informing as il it came via ouija board\n1199\t"}]},"uuid":"c5afad2a-d1d6-4237-8328-4bf6ceff6485","timestamp":"2026-10-31T08:27:59.417Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/c1/d07.txt","content":"=== 5_1913-05-09_p3_sn89052133_00383341188_1913050901_0148.json\nThe Springfield herald. · 1913-05-09\ncrops: None\nheadline: Searching for Hidden viii.\n---\nThe romantic story of hidden will\nis arousing keen interest in the neigh\nborhood of Bradley Fold, little vii\nlage three miles from Bolton. Lanca-\nshire, England. Search for the mis-\nsing document is being made by a\nwoman from Gunnersbury, London.\nShe believes that II she succeeds in\nher quest, she will become entitled to\na valuable estate. The search was\nprompted originally by statements\nmade by a medium at spiritualistic\nseance The will is supposed to be\nconcealed In wall sunk In a shed\nwhich has been bricked up for many\nyears. 1t formed cellar to an old\nhouse. The London searcher's ances.\ntor, John Bradley. who married his\nhousekeeper, was known to possess\ngreat wealth. No will could be found\nafter his death.\n\n=== 30_1872-01-10_p1_sn87052143_00294556540_1872011001_0005.json\nLexington weekly intelligencer. (Lexington, Mo.) 1871-1887 · 1872-01-10\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\n! ASH KIM tTRAIN.-f IL\nmuch too common n custom to turn\ncattle into the fields of winter grain\nat the present time. This is a very\nunadvisablc practice. Feed is short.\ncertainly, ard the stock need all they\ncan get. Bat what occurs to the\ngrain: a considerable portion is\npulled up by the roots and dcvoured.\n\n\nAnother portion is DESTROYED by the\npoaching ofthc ground, and the filling\nOf the holes made bv Tie feet of tl,e\ncattle with water when the winter\nraiis come. Then the needed bro\nlection the heritage affords TO the\nroots is wanting, and the rop cannst\nwithstand the severe frosts. from\nwhich il is no longer sheltered. The\nresult Is a short crop, for which the\nspring rains are blamed, or the heavy\nshowers in May or June, which knock\nof the blossom, or the hot sun which\nblasts it. or the nudge which sucks\nits juices, and many OTHER things ; all\nOf w%hich,arc probably innocent, and\nthe real blame attaches TO the farmer\nwho allowed his cows To ent Off the\nfall growth. 1t is very rarely indeed\nthat grain needs to be eaten off: and\nIf such should be the case, calves.\nsheep or pigs, well ringed, are better\nthnn cows or heavy stock.-[Hearfh\nand Home.\n\n\nEREAKFAsT ROLLS, Pour sufa.\nGlen. quantity boiling water on one\npint cornmeai to make a thick cruel\nthen lei it stand until nearly cold\nthen add salt aud one large spoonful\nof yeas. mix II wheat -four until it il\nIt'll dough ; kncad II well, aud then\nlet il stand in q warm place for seven\nor eight hours 10 rise: when hight\nbake into rolls, aud bake one hour.\n\n=== 5_1885-02-02_pna_sn84022871_00202194412_1885020201_0226.json\nOrleans County monitor. [volume] (Barton, Vt.) 1872-1953 · 1885-02-02\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n\nHr All notices ofdeaths inserted free. Obituary\nnotices and poetry Jfveeemtsperline.\n---\nIn Craftsbury, Jan. 25, Candace Mason, aged 81\nyears.\n\n\nIn Morgan, Jan. 20, of congestion of the lungs,\nAbbie Lord, wife of Arthur Daggett, aged IS yrs.\nIn Holland, Jan. %, L. C. Heath, aged az years.\nIn Sutton, Jan. 2Sd, of cancer, Jane, wife of Luz\nTheir Gray, aged So years, months, 81 days.\nShe was the aAly daughter of the late Luther\nRice. Was horn in Sutton, and respected in\nchildhood by all who knew her. In early life she\nlearned to love the Saviour and baptized by\nRev. j. Woodman, united with the Freewel Bap-\nfist church in South Barton. In her last sickness\neven when disease doing its last work. she re-\nioiced and praised God that her change had come.\nIn westnore, Jan. IS, Rawson 8. Heat. aged, %\nyears, mos, 20 days.\n\n\nThere is a hcme Wseye friends shall meet,\n\n\nAID 2syer, never part again;\n\n\nAnd those who loved on earth, repeat\n\n\nThe VOWS they pledged in sorrow then.\n\n\nThat spirit-land shall ever bloom,\n\n\nGrief from its cline be ever driven,\n\n\nImmortal joys pervade that home\n\n\nThat spiritand, that home is Heaven\n\n=== 26_1899-04-25_p1_sn87068097_00383340299_1899042501_0462.json\nThe Jersey City news. (Jersey City [N.J.]) 1889-1906 · 1899-04-25\ncrops: None\nheadline: TABlEAUx PHANTASM MA\n---\nJohn w. Sherman's tableaux phantasma\nproduced last night at Columbia Hall,\nocean and Gator avenues, before a large\nand appreciative Audience. The tableaux\nand light effects are beautiful and well\nworth seeing. The tableaux were ar\nranged by Miss Marie G. Orthen.\n\n=== 17_1900-10-19_pna_sn89058013_00296020333_1900101901_0359.json\nThe Camden chronicle. (Camden, Tenn.) 1890-current · 1900-10-19\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nTHE main part Of Hon. John d.\nMcCaIPs speech has been knocked\nOut by Treasurer Craig, who denies\nthat he furnished the Republican\ncandidate any figures, and states\ntbattho deductions made by him\nare not correct. How are the peo-\npie to vote when candidate is SO\nreckless Of the truth as Mr.. Craig's\nstatement proves MIR. Mccall to be?\nParis Press.\n\n=== 9_1888-09-25_pna_sn83025121_00211108939_1888092501_0173.json\nThe Hawaiian gazette. [volume] (Honolulu [Oahu, Hawaii]) 1865-1918 · 1888-09-25\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nTHE \"Aki Claim mot to use the\n\n\nharsher term OF pre-revolution times\n\n\nhas a second time gone indelibly upon\nthe page of Hawaiian history Every\n\n\npatriotic heart must fain have wished\n\n\nthat there was an end to that disagreea-\n\n\nBILE matter being Haunted before the\n\n\neyes OF the world, when His Majesty\n\n\nmade solemn engagement with dele\n\n\nCATION OF the people to see that justice\n\n\nwas done without stint in the matter.\n\n\n1t must therefore have caused q\n\n\ngeneral sensation OF pain through\nout the community when, few\n\n\nmonths ago, it was whispered\n\n\nabout that the King through his\nagents-appointed primarily so that the\nstipulated justice should be executed\nhad taken the responsibility OF repudi-\nsting the obligation OF kingly honor.\nThis position adopted by His Majesty\ncompelled the devisee in trust OF the\ntestator-claimant to seek redress for the\nheirs OF the partly self-wronged man in\nthe Courts of the Kingdom. Thus has\ncome the second PRONUNCIATION of the\nnations shame through the public hear\ning and records of the cause The re-\nsuit OF the suit is to be found in Judge\nPreston's lucid and closely-reasoned de-\ncision published in full in this issue. 1t\nis that the claim having been publicly\nacknowledged and arrangements to\nwhich the King was freely party hav-\ning been made for its liquidation,\nit has passed out of the province\nwhere it might be outlawed\nas part OF an illegal transaction.\nMoreover and here it will be OBJ\nserved that the royal privilege of i'm\nmunity from all imputation of wrong\ndoing proves two-edged sword-it is\nimpossible that any matter or thing per-\ntanning to the Kings person, or estate,\nor dominion, can be considered on any\nbasis OF recognizing that anything illegal\nor wrong can be done by the Sovereign\nunder our Constitution. The decision\njust rendered is not necessarily final,\nyet it is surely improbable that an\nappeal will be taken to the full bench.\nJustice Preston has woven a seemingly\nweb OF fact, law and equity\nin front OF his conclusions, and, if pos\nsibly, there are loopholes through which\nto escape the essential points in His\nHonor's reasoning, it would be subject\nOF universal regret if the unsavory sub\nrelating to the pecuniary\nissue should once more have to be par\nAIDED before the nation and the world.\n\n=== 39_1909-03-27_p10_sn83045433_100493299_1909032701_0329.json\nThe Washington herald. · 1909-03-27\ncrops: None\nheadline: SERVICES TO-MORROw\n\nSPIRITUALISM.\n---\nMESSAGES FROM THE SPIRIT WORLD. Al\nfred II. Terry VIII hold a meeting SUNDAY even\ning n O'Clock. Lecture followed hy Spirit mes\nsageS and psschcaetric readings 81 Schmidts\nHan SIC sth st. NV\n\n\nDIR. J. NJ. TEMPLE. BUSINESS AND TEST\nmedium viii hold grand test meeting for spiritual\nrnessar-s Sunday. Tuesday. ard Friday az s D. m.\nReadings dsiy. so 9Th 81. NV\n\n\nMRS. NOBLE HOLDS SPIRITUAL MEETINGS\nevery sunday Tuesday. and Friday nights az\nO'Clock. Private readings daiiy. l12s llth S. nE\n\n=== 3_1898-03-05_p12_sn85066387_00175037676_1898030501_0072.json\nThe San Francisco call. · 1898-03-05\ncrops: None\nheadline: Refugee Took a Purse Supported by a Strong\n\n Company.\n\nCharley Thorpe and Tod Sloan Divided the Riding\n\n HOnOPS.\n\nGold Scratch Won the Two Year Old Event at Long Odds-Ostler Joe\n\n First in the Handlcap.\n---\nFog was again strong factor at h. eleside yesterday. One event only was decided with the spectators enjoying an unobstructed view of the horses. The favorites met with three reverses, and it is more than likely that in one race at least there were some big do- ings\" Tod Sloan piloted three and Charley Thorpe two winners.\n\n The opening mile run for maidens was only a gallop for Twinkle Twink, which ran in Don Cameron's colors and won eased up to a walk, after going to the post a 4 to choice in the betting In a drive Durward took the place from Whirlwind.\n\n Johnny Campbell finally started the speedy Punter in the six fur- long selling dash that followed, and the wise ones grabbed the even money that was offered about the lilly. It took all of Tod Sloan skill in the saddle to land her winner, a head before the second choice, Prompto Moringa, as usual, finished in the show\n\n The stable, it was said, did not play Gold Scratch to win the four furlong scramble for year-olds At any rate, the gray youngster got the best of a struggling start, and at odds of F to 1 finished over lenStn III front of Primavera, the second choice. Ach, the Corrigan entry, an II to 10 favorite, was beaten for the show by Mossbrae.\n\n Fog undoubtedly proved a strong factor in the victory of Refugee. In the betting on the mile selling event Willie Sinks cripple opened at odds of to l. His price heightened until 20 was reached, when he was played down to and TO- lie won by less than a length from the favorite. Key del was evidently a \"shoo in,\" as two or three of the horses which finished be- hind him are capable of running a much faster mile Of the other start CTS Fashion Plate was probably the only one that was not a lobster and she was dead last time out It was passed over like many of the other dis graceful seances of the present racing season.\n\n A big consignment of coin forced the odds of Caliente down from to to to In the betting on the six furlong selling affair following, and be became lost in the fog. Hurly Burly, the Cor rigan entry, a to 1 chance in the ring, won handily from Bonita, a 12 to 1 shot Chihuahua, another rank out sider was third.\n\n The black horse, Ostler Joe, was fa- vorite for the mile and a sixteenth handicap. which, like most of the others at this track. was a pronounced frost, and he scored without difficulty from Glorian, at one time as good as 20 to in the betting. The second choice, Greyhurst, quit like dog, ending up in the far rear.\n\n=== 21_1897-12-09_p1_sn84026355_00211100242_1897120901_0567.json\nThe Scranton tribune. [volume] (Scranton, Pa.) 1891-1910 · 1897-12-09\ncrops: None\nheadline: Gen. Goblin Calls n Mee,i,,\n---\nHarrisburg, Dec. S.-General Goblin.\ncommander-in-Ghief of the Grand Ar\nmy of the Republic, has called a\nmeeting Of the executive committee of\nthe national council at Cincinnati, De-\ncember 10. The date for the next ma\n\n=== 21_1922-04-27_p1_sn86063952_00295864850_1922042701_0828.json\nThe Guthrie daily leader. [volume] (Guthrie, Okla.) 1893-1996 · 1922-04-27\ncrops: None\nheadline: Bold\n\n\nBandits\n\n\nat Tulsa\n---\nTulsa, Apfn \"''nih ~\nthan = hieek o. ihe station VII\"\n. night ~s'shmsn sleeping IN Inc\nrear 81 the building an unhno\"n\n\n\nburglar 81 s.\" o'clock 'h's\nmorning limm'ed OH the swinging\ndoors IN the icyer OF = do\"h'\ntown fheatre' rr'e1 open me uoer\n10 the Office OF \"i''.Hm Sn''n'\nproprietor; knocked ihr e\"muih,\na''.h OH 'he o'k >a'' s\"a esson ,oa\n\n=== 8_1905-06-22_p6_sn84020358_00271744456_1905062201_1353.json\nThe daily morning journal and courier. · 1905-06-22\ncrops: None\nheadline: COMPOUNCE sPIRITUALIsTs.\n---\nPast Year Prosperous One for the\n\n Society-Election of Officers and a\n\n Seance.\n\n Bristol, June 21-At the annual meet ing of the Compounce Spiritualists' association, which was held at the lake to-day, the. following officers were elected:\n\n President-T. B. Kenyon, of New Ha- ven.\n\n Vice-president-Mrs. J. S. Storrs, of Hartforr.\n\n Secretary-Mrs. J. E. Dillon, of Hart ford.\n\n Delegate to the national convention Mrs J. s. storrs;s alternate, Mrs. J. E. Dillon. h. There were about 300 in attendanle.\n\n=== 30_1906-11-13_p8_sn85042462_00175047414_1906111301_0233.json\nLos Angeles herald. · 1906-11-13\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nish girl. the sorrowing widow and the\ncallow youth, answer this question,\nfor l cannot.\n\n\nWorse than the old. old query, why\nis a cow\" it has proven.\n\n\nSunday night, in company with Mr..\nHazel, l went to a meeting of the\nTruth Seekers society. / sat there and\nhad the pleasure of listening TO re-\nmarks the reverse of complimentary\nabout The Herald. myself and Mr.. Ha-\neel for being so unkind as to abuse\nthe poor fake mediums who are trying\nso hard to make a living by fooling\npeople who can i'll afford to be fooled.\n\n\nUndoubtedly the speakers in that\nmeeting had read The Herald expose\nof the takers and they had even taken\nthe trouble to visit the exhibit of spir-\nitualistic paraphernalia in The Herald\nwindow, for they commented upon 1t\nfreely. Unfortunately, however, the\nguide, he who is supposed to see that\nno Objectionable\" characters enter the\nsacred precincts of the circle. had not\ntaken the precaution to look upon Mr..\nHazeIs picture. for we passed the door\nwithout a challenge.\n\n\nAgain, Why is a guide?\n\n\nIconocIasts Not Recognized\n\n\nThe spirits that pervade ''the third\ndimension of space\" according to the\nmediums. are supposed to be cognizant\nof everything going on in the room\nThese spirits should have warned the\nmedium that there were aliens\" in\nthe room, but alas, they were remiss in\ntheir duties, and we were permitted to\nsit unnoticed and listen to our arraign-\nment To sit there with our ears burn\ning to a scarlet while women told us\nwhat deep dyed villains we are for tell\ning the uninitiated how the faker fools\nthe unwary, the charlatan swindles the\nunfortunate.\n\n\nPart of all they said about us we\nmissed We entered Mammoth hall,\nwhere the meeting was held, after\nmost of the neophytes, believers and\nfrauds had taken their seats and set\ntied themselves for q night of marvel\nGus relevations.\n\n\nA man with a Vandyke beard and\nmelancholy expression was talking.\n\n\nIn high pitched, strident voice, rem\niniscent of Little Effie\" of Elsie\nReynolds creation, he was telling his\nfriends that The Herald should be con-\ndemned for roasting spiritualism be-\ncause spiritualism is in the minority.\n\n\nWe suppressed our desire to make\nspeech to the effect that The Herald\ndoes not condemn spiritpalism, but\nonly those imposterS who pull the heart\n\nOffering a vulgar substitute for that\nwhich is most sacred to- man.\n\n\nWhen the speaker had finished his\ncondemnation of The Herald, the as\nsembIy joined In a song, and then\nmedium sang and played under in\nspiration.\n\n\nMusic Fright\n\n\noh, that song! If there were any\nspirits floating around in the atmos-\nxr,ha troll\nbegan to sing in one key and play in\nanother it would be safe to wager\nthey all departed at the second bar.\n\n\nIf such is inspiration, may heaven\nhereafter deliver us from it.\n\n\nIf such is harmony, we might pray\nthat we will always sound the discord.\n\n\nThe inspired musician\" was not the\ngreatest attraction.\n\n\nShe was only prologue to the main\nshow of the evening.\n\n\nThe medium requested that the lights\nover her head be turned out, and then\nshe closed her eyes. Her two diamond\nearrings glistened in the half light and\nher voice, pleasant voice, was stilled\nfor a moment while she allowed her\nspirit control to take possession of her.\n\n\nThis occupied but a moment as she\nannounced that the conditions were\nvery harmonious. You see, the guides\nhad not told her that there were any\nObjectionable persons in the room.\n\n\nEverything is very pleasant and ev-\nerything is in harmony, so 1 think we\nwill be able to give some very good\nmessages this evening\"\" said the me-\ndunn.\n\n\nters of the audience, old truth seek\ners'' who have been there year in and\nyear out and whose lives have long\nbeen ancient history to the mediums.\n\n\nWe hoped for a message but we were\ndoomed to disappointment, for the me-\ndunn had nothing for US.\n\n\nMessages\" came freely to others,\nbut they were the usual commonplace\npromises and commands.\n\n\nGo Away Disappointed\n\n\nThe woman asked if there were any\nwealthy gentleman in the audience,\nbut there was no one who evidently\nfelt himself so qualified, or if so he\nwas too baShful to answer. A women\narose. Perhaps you mean me-\" she\nsaid.\n\n\nThat's right; do mean you, Sister\n-\" calling her by name.\n\n\nI'm about five months two men will\ncome to you to get you to sign a con-\ntract. One of them will be an old\nman and another a young man. Be\ncareful of the young man and do not\ntrust him too much. Do not sign the\ncontract for two months, then every\nthing will come out all right\"\n\n\nTo one woman the medium brought\nson who told her that by the first\nof May everything would be lovely.\nThe flowers would be blooming, the\nbirds singing and she would have\npeace of mind. The spirit is evidently\nfamiliar with California in spring. or a\nmessage so definite could never have\nbeen given.\n\n\nTo mother she brought the spirit\nOF little girl and the child came with\nbouquet of flowers in her arms and a\nmessage of hope and love. Told her\nthat she was doing everything she\ncould to help her and asked the mother\nTO send out her best thoughts to the\nspirits tht-t the spirits might be helped\n1t is good rule, for 1t works both\nways. The spirits help the mortals and\nthe mortals help the spirits.\n\n\nFinally, disappointed that we had\npaid 10 cents each and had received nc\nmessages, we left.\n\n\nThe Herald spook editor was show\ncred with requests for knowledge on\nvarious points yesterday and some OF\nthem appear I'm this issue.\n\n\nMANY LETTERS COME\n\n\ntn Tue cuo0H rnIrnu\n\n=== 3_1909-09-07_p1_sn89053204_00393344283_1909090701_0143.json\nAmericus times-recorder. [volume] (Americus, Ga.) 1891-current · 1909-09-07\ncrops: None\nheadline: GHOST OF BOB FULTON\n\n\nSEEN INsNEW YORK\n\nCIermOnt Has Trial Trip\n\n\nOn Hudson.\n---\nNew York, Sept Cone hundred\nyears was bridged when the very\nmuch materialized ghost of Robert\nFulton's steamboat, Clermont, whirl-\ned her wheels in the waters of New\nYork bay. AS great modern craft\npassed her she seemed a pigmy of the\n| river, but they footed their whistles in\n| respectful salutation, as a small boy\ntakes off his cap to his granddad, even\nthough the old gentleman is creaky\nin the joints and sadly behind the\ntimes.\n\n\nThe new Clermont is nearly ready\nto take her place in the Hudson-Ful-\nton celebration, and her builders, the\nStaten Island Shipbuilding Company\ngave her a trial spin yesterday\nThrough the Kill van Kull as far\nup as New Brighton she ran against\nthe tide at a speed of almost six\nmiles an hour. That she exceeded\nslightly the rate of her old self was\ndue to the better condition of her en-\ngines She returned with equal ease,\nand when she was again at her pier\nw. J. Davidson, president of the com\npany expressed thorough satisfaction\nwith the result of the test\n\n\nAs the Clermont moved along UNI\nder her own steam she attracted much\nattention along the Staten Island and\nJersey shores. Low lying, and only\nlao feet Ions, she. contrdsted oddly\nwith the larger vessels along the\nshores.\n\n\nThe first things that struck the eye\nwere the unprotected paddle wheels,\nthrowing the foam in great masses\nbehind. The stern overhung on each\nside and the bow was sharp In the\n\n\ngntre the big boiler, with it's diminu-\n\n=== 6_1925-08-07_p2_sn96060765_00383341929_1925080701_0576.json\nThe Winslow mail. · 1925-08-07\ncrops: None\nheadline: VAST GHOSTS OR\nPLAIN K. K. ks\n\n\nASK THE OLD MAN\n---\nJack O'Brien tells this one about\none of the hot nights at Needles not\nlong ago.\n\n\n1t was about midnight, and sud\ndenly from one of the rooms of the\nhotel at which he was stopping,\nthere came terrifying scream,\nwhich was followed by another\nmore terrifying, and still another.\nThen the screaming ceased, but\nwas succeeded by a series of\nweird means and low sobs\n\n\nNow, Mr.. O'Brien says, in Needles\nthat night the inmates of the hotel\nwere sleeping just about as nature\nmade them, and even then it was\nplenty warm enough.\n\n\nNevertheless in moments sine\nafter the screaming started the\ndoor of every room opened and\nas many as twenty men ran out\ninto the hall wrapping sheets\naround them as they went, with\nMr.. O'Brien and his sheet included\nin the procession.\n\n\n''All we lacked were the pillow\ncases.\" Mr.. O'Brien said, \"and the\nKu Kluxers wouldn't have been in\nit,\"\n\n\nOne after another they tried to\nopen the door whence came the\nlow means and walls. The door\nwas locked and their manipulations\nwere in vain. Then one Of the\nsheet-clad members was detailed\nto go outside and break in through\nthe window He did so and then\nopened the door for all to enter\n\n\nOne by one they filed into the\nroom and surrounded the bed,\nwith the sheets wrapped closely\naround them There before them\nlay an old man who was saying:\n\"Oh, my daughter! oh, my daugh\nteri\" while he writhed and moaned\nas if in great pain.\n\n\nwhy, the poor old fellow has a\nnightmare\" one fellow said from\nbeneath his sheet to all the other\nsheets around him. Another re-\ncruit stepped forward and tapped\nthe old gentleman on the forehead.\n\n\nThe old man slowly opened his\neyes and reached out his arms:\nooh, my dau-!\"\n\n\nThen he suddenly blinked. And\nthe whole congregation of sheets\nhad to jump on him at once to\nkeep from landing outside the win\ndow in one leap.\n\n\nAS soon as explanations could be\nmade the old man was left alone.\nAS might be expected. no more\nwailing was heard from the room\nthat night The poor old fellow\nprobably didn't get far enough\nasleep again to have even a symp-\ntom of a nightmare\n\n\nBut take Mr.. O'Brien's word for\nit, it was a fine parade.\n\n=== 13_1897-07-17_pna_sn83045462_00280655144_1897071701_0585.json\nEvening star. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1854-1972 · 1897-07-17\ncrops: None\nheadline: CORRECT SUMNER ATTIRE\n\nProper Articles of Dress for Men During the\n\n\nHeated Term.\n\nSuits VIII De Blue-Shirts and Neck\nwear Will Not BE sLoud--Russct\n\n\nShoes Continue I'm Favor.\n\nFro m Harper's Bazaar.\n---\nrftu bahar\n\n\nSummer was so long in coming that 1t\nseemed as IL tweeds and overcoats would\nbe the fashion we'll into July. Tailors and\nhaberdashers and matters were in despair.\nBolts Of cloths were uncut, and the show\nwindows were filled with shirt materials,\nsummer ties and straw hats, but orders\nwere few. Now that summer has set in\nand the watering places are in the glory OF\ntheir season. one can begin to judge what\nmen are wearing-quite different thing\n! from that which the shopkeepers would\nlike them to wear\n\n\nBlue Suits Arc in Favor\nThere has been a reaction in favor of the\nblue serge suit. This year they are most\npopular. In cut there is much variation\nThe sack coat is either four button or three\nbutton. with a roll collar made very much\nlike the Tuxedo or dinner jacket. Again\nthe double-breasted REFER of either rough\ncheviot or smooth blue cloth is very much\nworn. With white duck trousers this coat\ngives man nautical air, which Is quite\n! Fetching at the seaside. Rough cheviots\nmake excellent and very smart sack SUITS\nand some men having their coats in\nthis material-the favorite colors being blue\nand black-made into short three-buttoned\ngetaways. The blues are worn as business\nor morning suits, and the black coats seem\n~ the question afternoon dress IN\nspring and summer. and to prove formida-\n!, bic rivals to the frock, which is not an\nagreeable garment in the warm season.\nA material for sack or business suits\nThlch mas come into great favor IS a thin\nrough cheviot of very dark blue or even\nlack, with stripes of lighter blue These\nsuits resemble somewhat the older-fash-\nToned ones, still seen in cheap clothiers'\nwindows. of black with thin white stripes\nThe new material, however, is heavier and\nfor stout and short men the pattern is use\nful In making them at least look taller and\nthinner.\n\n\nShirts Are Not \"BriIIfant.\"\n\n\nIn shirts the very brilliant hues have\nbeen put aside, and the blues, the lavenders\nand not too vivid pinks are the most worn\nSolid colors have the preference over pat\nterms, although white stripes are still ia\nvorites Even for the warmest weather linen\nis more in form than either Madras or pom\ngee SILK. One sees fewer brown shirts\nworn, and the semi-neglige, with great\nmother-of-pearl buttons, has been reiegated\n10 the bargain counters. So active has\nbeen the competition among the manufac-\nturerS that today very excellent ready\nmade shirts are being turned out and Of\nTered for sale at very low prices. These\nare well made, with narrow cuffs and two\nTO three button holes-either number now\nbeing fashionable. Colored shirts with de-\ntached cuffs will never be in the wardrobe\nOF any well-dreSsei man. Detached collars\nare permitted, but false\" cuffs never. 1t\nIs needless to state that all .colored shirts,\nexcept iannelS, have cuffs of the same\nmaterial, and white collars. The collar in\nvogue this summer is the ali-around turned\ndown. There are numerous variations of\nthese. many of which are excellent In\nbuying or having collars made, be careful\nas to the quality of the linen, and see that\n1t Is of medium thickness. Do not have\nthe wings SLOPE but that they are\nalmost rectangular. The straight standing\ncollar Is worn with evening and semi-even-\ning dress, and its height is undiminished.\nCollars with turned down wings at the col\nlar button, otherwise standing, are not as\nhigh, and for men with short necks are\ncomfortable and seemly.\n\n\nThe hannel shirts especially those Of tar\ntan and plaid patterns, has come into\nfashion Tor travelling and midsummer ex-\nIRENE neglige. One must have with these\nwhite linen collars and cuffs. The collars\nare usually detached and in this one in\nstance, where there is some uncertainty as\nTO good laundry work, the detached cuff\nmay be permitted. There is a raiscn detre\nfor it.\n\n\nNo Change in Neckwear.\n\n\nIn ties there Is absolutely little change in\nfashion, shape, or in effects. One may\nwear those OF last summer, If yet fresh,\nard be quite up to date. Shopkeapers are\npushing the hunting Ascot or stock-the\nlatter a misnomer-and have christened it\nthe golf and bicycle tie. The Madras\nand liEen Ascots attached to a white Mad\nras band, to button around the neck, like\na Roman collar, are certainly neat, and\nwith neglige shirt have good effect. but\nthey lack GOODNESS. The club tie, square,\nan inch in width, to be adjusted by ones\nself in a plain neat bow, will remain the\nfavorite neckwear of the season. Foulards\nin the Old inevitable dark blues, reds,\ngreens and black, with the familiar polka\ncot or diamond, crescent, crescent and\nstar, leaf, or flower patterns univer-\nsally worn. The designs numerous, and\nthe combinations all artistic and in excel\nlent taste. Cotton linen and Ma iras lies\nVIII also be worn, but the foulards arc al\nways i'll good form, and it is difficult TO find\na substitute for them. There are a few\nnovelties in ties. One is in pattern only-a\ndark blue, green, or biaci: ground. with\nlarge poika-dots of Two contrasting sha les.\nThus white and light blue on a darker\nground Again the London ha5er,lashars\nhave sent to this country large consign-\nnients of yiolet-purple and brick-lust red,\nbut there are not many bold spirits who\nhave ventured to adopt them.\n\n\nWith the Tuxedo. which will be worn n\ngreat deal this summer, especially at hotel\nana watering-place hops, the black silk or\nsatin ties are smaller, and quite square in\nshape. Although supposed authority has\nbeen quoted to support the theory. it will\nnever be good form TO wear a white waist\ncoat and white tie with Tuxedo. Some\nfearful sins against the canons of good\ntaste and form have been committed re-\n\n=== 3_1853-12-06_pna_sn84024735_00415664345_1853120601_0050.json\nRichmond enquirer. [volume] (Richmond, Va.) 1815-1867 · 1853-12-06\ncrops: None\nheadline: POLITICAL.\n\nPHANTOMS.\n---\n:, h,usas wherein men have lived and died,\n\n\nA. hnunt.J houses Through the npen tu\"rs\nThe harmless phan,Oms on their errands glide,\nWin feet that make NO sound upon the hoors.\nWa meet them at the doorway, on the sIair,\nAlong the passages they come and 60,\nImpalpaole Impressions on Inc air.\n\n\n4 eeuse of something moving 10 and fro.\nThere are more guests al table than The hosta\nInvited; the illuminated hall\nIs thronged with quiet inotfensvc enos's'\n\n\nAz quie, rs the pictures on the wall\nThe stranger at my Preside Gamer see\n\n\nThe forms see nor hear the forms neal:\nHe bu, perceiyes what lie; while unto me\n\n\nAll ,hat has been is visible and clear\nWe have no title.deeds to house or lands:\nSwners and occupants Of earlier dates,\np,o1 graves forgotten stretch their dusty hands,\nAnd hold In norlmain still their old estates.\nThe spirit world around this world OF sense\n\n\nFloats like an atmosphere, and everywhere\nWatts through these earthly msts and vapurs dense\nA vital breath of more ethereal air.\nOur little lives are kept in equipoise\n\n\nBy opposite attractions and desires;\nThis struggle of the instinct that enjoys,\n\n\nAud the more noble instinct that asPHss'\nThe perturba,ons, the perpe.ua Jr\n\n\nOF earthly wants and aspirations n'sn'\nCome from the influence Of that unseen s'af,-\nThat undiscovered planet 10 our sky.\nAnd as tho ,noon from some dark gate OF cloud,\nThrowa Ger ,he sea floating bridge OF light,\nAc,o.s whose trembling planks our fancies crowd,\nIn The realm of MYSTERY and night;\nSo from the world OF spirits there descends\n\n\nA bridge Of light, connecting II with this,\nDer whose unsteady floor. that sways and oends,\nWander our thoughts above the dark abyss.\n\n=== 5_1904-05-20_p1_sn86076224_00332890682_1904052001_0450.json\nThe daily silver state. [volume] (Winnemucca, Nev.) 1903-1907 · 1904-05-20\ncrops: None\nheadline: Haunted by His Crimes\n---\nSeattle. May ia young man Civ\nhg his name as Randolph Preston and\nhis home as San Francisco was picked\nup bv the police this morning. AT\nheadquarters Prt ston broke down COM\n.''e:oly and said that he been baunted\nfor two weeks by the phantom OF\nhis crime. The he told how he had\nembezZled a considerable sum OF\nmoney. His explanation on the point\nrs to the person or persons from which\nhe took the money was not clear, SO\nthe police have telegraphed to San\nFrancisco for further particulars.\n\n\nPreston said that he had not eaten a\noit of food for week and when taken\n% a cell he fell into a sleep of ex-\nhaustion. He looks worn out\n\n=== 15_1869-01-09_p3_sn82014899_00211105483_1869010901_0571.json\nThe weekly Arizona miner. · 1869-01-09\ncrops: None\nheadline: Chr ~ 9a(infr.\n\nPlanchette--What is Itf\n---\niA\"Ohette, ana thlnks he has 