sh powdered hair and silk stock\nings. moved with deft3 dignity. II was\na merry meal, full of stories and In\nteresting anecdote. The duke called our\nattention to the large stained glass win\ndow at the end, in which his great-par-\nents' portraits represent Solomon and the\nqueen of Sheba at a banquet. His grace\nquantity remarked that it was hardly Hat\ntering to the queen of Sheba, as his ances-\ntress had been famous for her absence of\ngood looks. ieg\n\n\nAfter dinner we adjourned to the bar\non's hail.\" where the men informally\nsmoked we drank our coffee. This\nroom is of noble proportions; 10 feet long.\nwith its curiously wrought roof of Span\nish chestnut nearly 40 feet above the\nstone pavement A series of stained glass\nwindows of much beauty, in which the\narmorial bearings of the Howards, Fitz-\nalans and Montgomerys are emblazoned,\nfill one side, and opposite hang ancestral\nportraits and trophies of the chase. A\nfire blaZed on the great hearth. under Its\nhood of chiseled stone. Palms and rugs,\nhanging lamps and luxurious couches.\nserved to soften and modernize the some\nwhat grim splendor of the room, whose\n\n\n\n\n\nseemed rather a fitting background for\nrushlights and armored figures.\n\n\nThe next morning / was awakened by\nthe chapel bell ringing for mathis, and\nhurried down to find the other guests and\nservants already in their places. The\nchapel is about half the size of Grace\nChurch in New York, and contains one of\nthe finest organs in England. The morn-\ning sunshine streamed through the open\nNuTtV, .It. f,It fh.\ndropping. pathetic Christ, exquisitely\nwrought from bit of wood of the Span\nish armada. After few simple words\nfrom Father Francis and a benediction\non the coming day, We went out between\nthe rows of standing servants, quite\nready for our breakfast which awaited us\nin the morning room\" This is u small\napartment hung in gay chintzes, with long\nwindows opening on the lawn. According\nito the English custom, there We're no ser\nwants present to mar The freedom of the\nmorning meal, at which we all seated our\nselves regardless of precedence. Great\n'four-in-hand' chatting dishes stood on the\nside tables, from which the men served\nthe women, and in the center of the table\nWe're the coffee and tea on silver turn\ntable, from which each one helped HIM\nself as his or her taste dictated Plans\nWe're made for the day, and we gladly ac-\nceded to the duke invitation 10 sec\nsomething OF the park. An hour later we\nfound him on the terrace, where peacocks\nstrutted to and fro in the sunshine. Roses\nclambered over the marble balustrade, and\nbelow stretched lawns as free from leaf\nor twig as a drawing-room floor. At the\nfoot of the steps bath chairs awaited us,\ndrawn by tiny donkeys in gay trapping.\n\n\na few days after the rain was whirling\nin sheets against the windows, so 1 wan\ndered into the library for a quiet hour.\nThe great room is an imitation of goth\nic cathedral, with ceilings and columns\nof mahogany, and is 100 feet long by 90\nwide. AT one end sniral staircase leads\nTO a gallery, which runs round the room\nhalf way TO the ceiling. the railing OF\nwhich Is of bronze of exquisite work\nmanship. Ten thousand Volumes line the\nwalls some of which are Of inestimable\nvalue. 1t was charming to prowl about\namong the many curios which fill the\ncabinets. fancying how the ghosts of\nmany famous men might be spying at\n\n\n=== 65_1920-02-06_pna_sn83045389_00296029269_1920020601_1230.json\nRichmond times-dispatch. [volume] (Richmond, Va.) 1914-current · 1920-02-06\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nnews from Russia is as clear, convincing\nana informing as il it came via ouija board\n","numLines":1199,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1199}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f75a7182-e0a8-4d71-932b-197b5334f02b","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"other"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Clau