ssesses rare artistic merit\n189\tand has been greatly admired.\n190\t\n191\t\n192\tMiss Maggie Gaule, the celebrated plat\n193\tform clairvoyant and test medium, is daily\n194\tgiving remarkable demonstrations of her\n195\tsubtle gift. She frequently staggers the\n196\tnewcomer by giving him accurate descrip-\n197\ttion Of his business, his home surroundings,\n198\tand ends with word picture of several\n199\tdeparted members OF his family, declares\n200\tthey are near him and usually repeats to\n201\thim loving message of greeting which she\n202\thears clairaudently. 1t is usual that the\n203\tnewcomer is little disturbed by the pub\n204\tlicity of the test, and his face, as he rises\n205\tto acknowledge the same, is a comic study\n206\tof amazement, perplexity and incredulity.\n207\tMiss Gaule is general favo-ite among the\n208\tSpiritualists. Size is as light-hearted and\n209\tunconservative as a child, and is never so\n210\thappy as when surrounded, as she is sure\n211\tto be, by group of admiring friends\n212\t\n213\t\n214\tw, W,\n215\t\n216\t=== 13_1892-08-29_p1_sn87068097_00383340767_1892082901_0202.json\n217\tThe Jersey City news. (Jersey City [N.J.]) 1889-1906 · 1892-08-29\n218\tcrops: None\n219\theadline: CHOLERA SPECTRE.\n220\tEfforts to Keep the Dread\n221\ted Scourge Out of Jer-\n222\t\n223\t\n224\tsey City.\n225\tCENTRES OF INFECTION FOUND.\n226\tWork of the Sanitary Police\n227\tOpen Sewers, Impure Water\n228\t\n229\t\n230\tand Vile Garbage\n231\t\n232\t\n233\tHeaps\n234\t\n235\tTHE DISEASE In EUROPE.\n236\t\n237\tGlasgow Steamers Stop Carrying\n238\tImmigrants-The Scare\n239\t\n240\t\n241\tin England.\n242\t---\n243\tWhether cholera reaches this city or\n244\tnot. the sanitary condition of the place\n245\twill be greatly improved. THE NEWS\n246\tstated Saturday that fourteen policemen\n247\thad been detailed to aid the Health In\n248\tspector in making an examination into\n249\tthe sanitary condition of the city, and the\n250\twork they are doing is good. Today these\n251\tmen made report. Two are attached to\n252\teach precinct and they report to their\n253\tCaptains, who, in turn, see that the\n254\tHealth Inspector is kept in formed of what\n255\tis discovered.\n256\t\n257\t\n258\tThe policemen found nineteen disease\n259\tbreeding places in thu First precinct In\n260\tthe Third precinct four pestilential places\n261\twere found and reported, and in the\n262\tFourth precinct a like number of disease\n263\tbreeders was found. Sixteen such places\n264\twere reported in The Sixth precinct, and\n265\tin the Seventh four were discovered.\n266\t\n267\t\n268\tGLARING EVILS.\n269\t\n270\t\n271\tThis by no means concludes the re-\n272\tsuit of the Investigations of these men,\n273\tand much larger report is anticipated\n274\ttomorrow. Such fragrant violations of\n275\tall sanitary rules as the Railroad avenue\n276\tsewer, Mill Greek, open sewers. garbage\n277\tdumping Eand unwholesome Passaic\n278\twater, will probably be specially meu-\n279\ttoned when the Health Inspector receives\n280\tthe report.\n281\t\n282\t\n283\tInspector Benjamin said today chat\n284\tfumigation would be used in all suspic-\n285\tious localities, and that everything that\n286\the could do to prepare the city in case of\n287\tan invasion of cholera would be done.\n288\tMr.. Benjamin is by no means frightened,\n289\tand while admitting thar it is possible\n290\tfor cholera to pass the quarantine station\n291\the does not believe it probable that such\n292\twill happen.\n293\t\n294\t\n295\t''The City is not a clean one,\" said the\n296\tInspector, \"and it's drinking water is\n297\tas bad as anyone could imagine, but do\n298\tnot believe the scourget will reach US. II\n299\tit should get foothold it would have\n300\tgood foundation and no one knows what\n301\tthe result might be. But as said before\n302\tthe health authorities at Quarantine are\n303\tso unusually cautious that l do not be-\n304\tlieve cholera will reach heren\n305\t\n306\t=== 4