sbands, love your wives. !.\n749\t\n750\t\n751\tl. Never find fault with her haforo\n752\tothers.\n753\t\n754\t\n755\t>. Per contra, remember the counsel\n756\tOf the good book: \"Her husband shall\n757\tpraise her in the gate\" that is, before\n758\tfolks.\n759\t\n760\t\n761\t}. Bear all the burdens for her; even\n762\tthen she'll bear more than you do in\n763\tspite Of you.\n764\t\n765\t\n766\t4. If you want her to submit to your\n767\tjudgment, never ask her to submit to\n768\tyour selfishness.\n769\t\n770\t\n771\t5. A woman's life is made up Of little\n772\tthings. Make her life happy by little\n773\tcourtesies.\n774\t\n775\t\n776\t6. Love isawife's wages. Don't scrimp\n777\tin your pay. Christian at Hork.\n778\t\n779\t=== 19_1889-07-31_pna_sn87056600_00202191502_1889073101_0264.json\n780\tThe Indiana State sentinel. [volume] (Indianapolis) 1868-1895 · 1889-07-31\n781\tcrops: None\n782\theadline: \n783\t---\n784\t/\n785\tfamilies of long, unbroken line, and gives\n786\tnotice by pitiful lamentation, often accom-\n787\tpanied by the wringing and clapping of\n788\t| hands, OF approaching death. BY those\n789\t| who believe in her In Ireland to-day, she\n790\t! is sometimes regarded as a spirit from\n791\t| purgatOrial realms, released from pen\n792\tnance when this pathetic mission is ac-\n793\t! complished; by some she is thought to be\n794\ta direct envoy from the spirit world; but\n795\t! by all she is believed to be the temporary\n796\t/ personation of some deceased member of\n797\t| the family TO which her grievOus message\n798\tis brought She is never trifed with,\n799\t! startled or vexed, for if this would hap\n800\t| pen the banshee would never again honor\n801\t! the same family, at least for a generation\n802\t| OF time, with its tenderly plaintive pres\n803\tence.\n804\t\n805\t\n806\tAll Irish FAIRIES are capable Of confer\n807\t/ ring sure benefits and their power for\n808\t| Furnishing evil is boundless. Conse-\n809\t| quently they are folk commanding more\n810\t| than ordinary respect; and they are uni\n811\tversally spoken of either in the tender\n812\tor friendly manner of ''our neighbors,\"\n813\tor with that delicate Difference easily\n814\t! recognizable in the appellation of ''the\n815\tgood people\" Ghosts, phantoms and\n816\tdemons form a goodly part OF the fairy\n817\tphantasmagoria of Ireland. One of the\n818\tmost hideous of these, and yet fellow of\n819\tinfinite drOllery, is the dullaghan. He is\n820\tgenerally found with his head under his\n821\tarm, IN his pocket, or where there are\n822\tnumber of them together, flinging it mer\n823\trily at some other dullaghan, or engaged\n824\twith it in games of football; and | fancy\n825\tWashington Irving had got an ink\n826\tling OF Irish dullaghans before\n827\the penned his inimitable tales\n828\tOF 'Ichabod Crane\" and rip Van\n829\tWrinkle\" ! found them curiously occu-\n830\tpied in the southwest of Ireland. The\n831\tKerry fishermen claim that once each\n832\tyear, at a certain time of the tide, ship\n833\tmanned with a headless crew sails thrice\n834\taround Nalentia island IN search of a for\n835\tmer pirate captain, who lost his head and\n836\tship in that channel in an engagement\n837\twith Queen Aune's forces. Ballindollag-\n838\than, in county Roscommon, was a noted\n839\thaunt of these odd creatures, and was so\n840\tnamed in their honor. Drumarraght. in\n841\tcounty Fermanagh, is \"the place of spec\n842\ttiers, \"arraght\" being Irish Tor an appari-\n843\tton. Then there are shirtless COLLINS;\n844\tfor Tough Gillaganleny, the name of sew\n845\teral tiny lakes in Ireland, means nothing\n846\tmore nor less than \"the lake of the shirt\n847\tless fellows\" Tobertasha is a coffin\n848\tshaped well. near Kilnamona, in Glare\n849\tand the Specification of its Celtic name,\n850\t'Tobar-a' -taise,\" is the \"well of the\n851\tghost kIlleennagallive Irish, C,dli,-nG-\n852\tBdeqlbb) in Tipperary, is ''the little church\n853\tOF the phantoms\" Again there are fairies\n854\twho do nothing but play at conan, or\n855\thurley. and the fairy fort OF Lisfarbegna-\n856\tgommaun, near Corrofn, in Clare, is sim\n857\tply \"the tort OF the little hurlers.' The\n858\tdancing fairies gave Lissarinka its name\n859\tthe fort OF the dancing. Certain old\n860\tforts or raths in the south of ireland\n861\tcalled LissakeOle, mean the fort Of the\n862\tfairy music While Lisnateedy, from\n863\tfe1d. a whistle, Is ''the fort of the whstling\n864\tfairies\" The POoka is wonderful sprite\n865\tOF merriment and cunning. In fact, he is\n866\tthe Irish (pUca) Puck. He whisks through\n867\tcountless legendary tales. a ripple of\n868\tlaughter and CLEAN OF light; and scores\n869\tOF places in Ireland whose names bear his\n870\town, or some of its many notifications,\n871\tattest his universal popularity.\n872\t\n873\t\n874\tBut of all Irish FAIRIES the most exasper-\n875\tatingly impish practical joker is the lepre-\n876\tchaun. He has many names In Celtic such\n877\tas luprachaun, luricane, lurrigadsne, cluri-\n878\tcane, luppercadane, lOughryman, and the\n879\tcorrect designation from which all these\n880\tcorrections have come, luchOrpan, from lu,\n881\teverything very small, and corpan,\n882\tdiminutive Of corp, body. Leprechaun\n883\tIs the name now universally bestowed in\n884\tIreland upon this merry little spirit. He\n885\tis still more Familiarity known among the\n886\tpeasantry as ''tbe little imp in green\" To\n887\ta hairs breadth he Is just twelve inches\n888\tin hight He is dressed in a little green\n889\tcoat with long dainty tails, bright scarlet\n890\tvest, the prettiest knee-breeches of\n891\tpurple velvet you ever beheld, with\n892\tgreen silk hose and low pumps\n893\t\n894\t\n895\t\n896\t\n897\t\n898\tmonds or sparkling drops of dew. There\n899\tis a iaunty barrhad or cap on his head,\n900\twith the daintiest of dudheens stuck I'M\n901\tder the band at one side, and a jewel of\n902\temeralds in the form OF a shamrock at the\n903\tother A film OF lace made from rarest\n904\tcobweb Is gathered at his throat, another\n905\tfoamy rift of the same rolls over the edges\n906\tOF his little vest, and he wears a wonderful\n907\tfob of wrought and blazing gold. His eyes\n908\tare no bigger than dolls beads, but they\n909\tare the merriest eyes that ever glistened.\n910\tHis mouth is very large from continuous\n911\tlaughter, and his paunch is something\n912\twonderful to behold, developed into out\n913\trageous proportion from the shaking OF\n914\this sides from uncomfortable merriment;\n915\tall the result OF his Jokes, receptions and\n916\tdiversions\" upon those who endeavor to\n917\tuse him Tor gain Indeed the little fellow\n918\tseems to embody the idea OF an endless\n919\ttorment and scourge TO those who permit\n920\tthemselves to become possessed OF I'M\n921\tmolly greed and avarice.\n922\t\n923\t\n924\tPatient plodding, hard labor, and a\n925\tCHEEKY spirit under deprivation and suf-\n926\tfering, are all worthy and true character\n927\tistics OF the lowly OF Ireland. These are\n928\tsurely more or less rewarded in various\n929\tWAYS by the leprechaun; but woe to the\n930\tsOrdid spirit who endeavors to amass\n931\triches through the agency OF this ''little\n932\timp in green\" His is the power to give\n933\tsudden and great wealth. He has mills\n934\tand mines and store-houses Of treasure\n935\tinnumerable. He is to every Irish man,\n936\twoman and child as the end of the seduc-\n937\ttive rainbow and its kettle of gold. to our\n938\town chlldhood's fancy, with this IN\n939\tportant difference. The leprechaun is an\n940\tentity; an actually. No one dare deny\n941\this possession of all the treasures neces-\n942\tsary to instantly lift one from poverty to\n943\tpower and riches. Thousands of aching\n944\tIrish eyes have Toasted upon his gorgeous\n945\tperson and tantalizing face. Besides, you\n946\thave the evidence of scores of places\n947\tin Ireland being named from his\n948\tknown haunts. Near the old city of\n949\tCong in Mayo, there is the Mullen\n950\tlupraghaun cave, OF \"the leprechaun's\n951\tmill\" Here in former times good\n952\tpeople left their caskeens of corn alright\n953\tfall, and came the next morning to find\n954\tthem full Of meal ground by the obliging\n955\tleprechauns during the night Only the\n956\t~ har dov ocoauda1 Observation-Disclosures,\n957\t\n958\t=== 35_1845-07-17_pna_sn83030213_00206530224_1845071701_0155.json\n959\tNew-York daily tribune. [volume] (New-York [N.Y.]) 1842-1866 · 1845-07-17\n960\tcrops: None\n961\theadline: \n962\t---\n963\tCoIoRrD TEMPERANCE COSVENTION.--on\n964\tr,e.dsv las the great annual meeting Of the Deayan\n965\tcote Tcmperance niOn was held in this city. From 10\n966\tsceock until - delegations continued 10 arrive from\n967\t\n968\t\n969\trrept points accompanied by several bands oil music\n970\tmi hearing numerous tastcuy executed banners Aer\n971\tmarching through the principal streets they proceeded\n972\t40 the COurt House from staging erected In front, me\n973\tConvention was organized by eeeting the Rev Mr. Gar\n974\t40, of Troy President who together with others made\n975\t.bo addresses each speaker being limited to u minutes\n976\tInc Rev Mr. Benan, Of New Haven. Ct. highly intel\n977\tlsept and educated speaker made the most effective ad\n978\tdress lie urged upon his colorcd brethren the neces\n979\t,y and importance Of eeyang their race 10 hieher\n980\tdegree Of intelligence and responsibility by education.\n981\t\n982\t\n983\tbety and good conduct the best and surest means\n984\tObtaining the rights and privileges 10 which the speaker\n985\tconendcd they were entitled\n986\t\n987\t\n988\tThe entire proceedings were conducted with the s'rlct\n989\t20, regard proprety and the appearance OF the dele\n990\tmics who a. far az observed conducted themselves\n991\tthroughout the day with the stricct decorun was ghy\n992\tcredisble 10 them in every respect They numbered.\n993\tacco