BCH,--In A certain\nDutch settlement IN the vicinity Of Com\nmuDipaw, in the State Of New Jersey,\nthe dominic Of the church gave notice\nfrom the desk that those who wished 10\nunite with the church should attend al\nthe parsonage on the following Wednes-\nday afternoon, that he might examine\nthem on religious subjects before being\nreceived as members.\n\n\n\"Hans, a simple, harmless, fellow, with\na soft heart and a head Of the same con-\nsistenee, went and presented himself as\na candidate for church membership; or, as\nhe expressed himself, \" 10 be taken in\nupon the church\"\n\n\nThe dominic, suspicious Of Hans\nknowledge in doctrinal matters, began\nhis inquiries by asking him how many\nGods there were?\n\n\nHands, counting on his finger, said +:\n\n\nThe God Of Abraham, the God of\nIsaac, and the God of Jacob-dat ish\nthree, den dere ish God de Father God\nde Son, and God de Holy Ghost--dat\nmakes six and may be deTe is some more\n/ don't reoollect so we well call Il eight\"\n\n\nHan\" said the dominic, \"you will\nnever do; you must 90 back and learn\nyour chalechism.'\n\n\nSO Hans, crest-fallen, and somewhat\nindignant set OF on his way back. Hav-\ning nearly reached home, he met his old\nAcquaintance, Conrad, On his way TO be\nexamined, and asked him If he was GO\ning to - taking in upon the church\nO1i,ad saying that he was, Hans told\nhim 10 stop aud he would let him know\nwhether he wGP'd pass an examination.\n\n\n\"Now,\" says RsGS, \"tell me how many\nGods there are p\n\n\n\"One,\" replied Conrad\n\n\n\"Then,\" said Hans, ''yoG may just\nturn back, you win never pass told\nthe dominic there wsrs eight and Is Na.\nHa saFgfsi\n\n=== 12_1923-03-18_p1_sn84026749_00222253962_1923031801_0449.json\nThe Washington times. · 1923-03-18\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\n/\" HE most powerful incentive\nto those who have gone into\n\n\nthe white shadowland-into\nthat transition which mortal ma\nteriaIists call death-to return to\ngive signs ar communications IL\nthat OF sentimental attachment,\n4. O., the love interest Of the fam\nfly group.\n\n\nThis does not essentially mean\nblood relationship. Indeed, within\nmy experience I have observed\never since I first began expert\nmenting with my late husband,\nJohn Fay, twenty years ago. that\nthe most satisfactory and most\nauthentic results were obtained\nby parents and by children and\nby husbands and wives.\n\n\nI mean to say that II 10 ever\n90 much easier for q wife to re-\noeive signs and communication\nfrom a husband who has gone on,\nthan It IL for business partners\nor friends or distant relatives to\nachieve similar successes by sim\nliar methods.\n\n\nThis experiment, the family\nseance, which I offer this week,\nis perhaps the most frequently\nsatisfactory in actual results be-\ncause Of the strong sentimental\ntie which the darkened tomb. the\ncold grave, the fire of the Gre\nmatorium leaves disaffected and\nas strong as in mortal life.\n\n\nPARTICIPANTS LIMITED.\n\n\nThe family seance should have\nno participants beyond parents,\nchildren, brother or sister, hus-\nband or wife.\n\n\nAS have frequently stated in\nprevious articles Of autospiritisnn.\nthe presences are reluctant to\nmake themselves known because\nOf the fear OF startling or fright\nening those on earth with whom\nthey would communicate.\n\n\nAgain, there IL a something\nwhich the Divine Providence does\nnot mean mortal man to know.\nwhich interferes with q certain\nAVAILABILITY In accomplishing COM\n\n=== 52_1892-08-14_pna_sn84024546_0023728219A_1892081401_0171.json\nPittsburg dispatch. [volume] (Pittsburg [Pa.]) 1880-1923 · 1892-08-14\ncrops: None\nheadline: A Spirit Picture OF Merit.\n---\n\n\n\nThe portrait of beautiful lady, purport-\ning to have been painted by Titan through\nthe mediumship of DR.. Henry Rogers, of\nNew York City, was also exhibited and\nreused considerable discussion. The pict-\nure certainly possesses rare artistic merit\nand has been greatly admired.\n\n\nMiss Maggie Gaule, the celebrated plat\nform clairvoyant and test medium, is daily\ngiving remarkable demonstrations of her\nsubtle gift. She frequently staggers the\nnewcomer by giving him accurate descrip-\ntion Of his business, his home surroundings,\nand ends with word picture of several\ndeparted members OF his family, declares\nthey are near him and usually repeats to\nhim loving message of greeting which she\nhears clairaudently. 1t is usual that the\nnewcomer is little disturbed by the pub\nlicity of the test, and his face, as he rises\nto acknowledge the same, is a comic study\nof amazement, perplexity and incredulity.\nMiss Gaule is general favo-ite among the\nSpiritualists. Size is as light-hearted and\nunconservative as a child, and is never so\nhappy as when surrounded, as she is sure\nto be, by group of admiring friends\n\n\nw, W,\n\n=== 13_1892-08-29_p1_sn87068097_00383340767_1892082901_0202.json\nThe Jersey City news. (Jersey City [N.J.]) 1889-1906 · 1892-08-29\ncrops: None\nheadline: CHOLERA SPECTRE.\nEfforts to Keep the Dread\ned Scourge Out of Jer-\n\n\nsey City.\nCENTRES OF INFECTION FOUND.\nWork of the Sanitary Police\nOpen Sewers, Impure Water\n\n\nand Vile Garbage\n\n\nHeaps\n\nTHE DISEASE In EUROPE.\n\nGlasgow Steamers Stop Carrying\nImmigrants-The Scare\n\n\nin England.\n---\nWhether cholera reaches this city or\nnot. the sanitary condition of the place\nwill be greatly improved. THE NEWS\nstated Saturday that fourteen policemen\nhad been detailed to aid the Health In\nspector in making an examination into\nthe sanitary condition of the city, and the\nwork they are doing is good. Today these\nmen made report. Two are attached to\neach precinct and they report to their\nCaptains, who, in turn, see that the\nHealth Inspector is kept in formed of what\nis discovered.\n\n\nThe policemen found nineteen disease\nbreeding places in thu First precinct In\nthe Third precinct four pestilential places\nwere found and reported, and in the\nFourth precinct a like number of disease\nbreeders was found. Sixteen such places\nwere reported in The Sixth precinct, and\nin the Seventh four were discovered.\n\n\nGLARING EVILS.\n\n\nThis by no means concludes the re-\nsuit of the Investigations of these men,\nand much larger report is anticipated\ntomorrow. Such fragrant violations of\nall sanitary rules as the Railroad avenue\nsewer, Mill Greek, open sewers. garbage\ndumping Eand unwholesome Passaic\nwater, will probably be specially meu-\ntoned when the Health Inspector receives\nthe report.\n\n\nInspector Benjamin said today chat\nfumigation would be used in all suspic-\nious localities, and that everything that\nhe could do to prepare the city in case of\nan invasion of cholera would be done.\nMr.. Benjamin is by no means frightened,\nand while admitting thar it is possible\nfor cholera to pass the quarantine station\nhe does not believe it probable that such\nwill happen.\n\n\n''The City is not a clean one,\" said the\nInspector, \"and it's drinking water is\nas bad as anyone could imagine, but do\nnot believe the scourget will reach US. II\nit should get foothold it would have\ngood foundation and no one knows what\nthe result might be. But as said before\nthe health authorities at Quarantine are\nso unusually cautious that l do not be-\nlieve cholera will reach heren\n\n=== 45_1877-03-18_p1_sn84031492_no_reel_1877031801_0002.json\nNone · 1877-03-18\ncrops: None\nheadline: That Is to Be the Defense in the\n\n\nPike Case.\n\nHe Has DWelt in Hospitals and Asy-\n\n\nlums Innumerable.\n\nMrs. p. Talks Freely About the Gre.\n\n\nligio.Philosophical - Building.\n\nHer idea Of Pike and Of Spiritualism\n\n\n---Mediumistic frauds.\n---\n~ acL WHICH nc Claims was their Legitimate resi-\nHe seems to resent the indignity put upon\n\n\nin tncarcerating him. and, when speaking Of\n\n\nforlorn condition, looks bitterly upon the nee\n\n\nsity for dcpriving him Of his liberty.\nmr. \"This is not the place, nor am l situated\nhe way, to show any independence ~ he said t,\nH. TRIBUNE reporter yesterday. \"Of course t\nw. world condemns me; but you have befriend\nha me, and while care but little what the wor\n~ thinks Of my action, will trust You with t\n\n\nhandspring and the motive. Have you got u\n\n\npapers?'''\n\n\nHe had the day before instructed the report\n\n\nto secure his private papers and see that no o.\n\n\nhad access to them.\n\n\n''Ycs, here they are.''\n\n\ntsI sec you published Geneyieve's confession\n\n\nam glad Of that, for it Is better that ever\n\n\nthing should be known now. And now that\n\n\nam more composed than when saw you yeste\n\n\nday, can SAY that I know that confession to\n\n\ntruc.'\n\n\n''Were You accurate In the statements 70\n\n\nmade to me yesterday?\"\n\n\n\"Accurate, yes; coherent, no. should hay\n\n\nliked to hare seen the statement before 1t Wa\nI published, and made it a little more con\nI ncctcd.''\n\n\n: \"Was there any more to tcll!\"\n\n\n\"Nothing moro. All could remember VA\n| there, but 1t was not in the shape that coult\nhave put it in had I been in better menta\ncondition. However, it is Ill true, and stand\n\n\nhere ready to swear to it at any moment Have\nyou seen Gcneyieve?'\n\n\n\"Not to-day. Shall I tell her anything from\n\n\nyou!''\n\n\nII don't see why she should be imprisoned.\n\n\nShe had nothing to do with this thing.n\n\n\nMrs. Ell told me yesterday that she would\n\n\nbail Geneyieve if bail would be taken.''\n\n\n\"She is a good friend to Gencyieve, and\n\n\nhope they will take bail for her. Will they Let\n! me keep my papers here in ny celia\"\n~ have satisfied the Sheriff that they contain\nnothing out Of the way, and he consents to my\ndelivering them to you.\"\n\n\n\"Did you read Gcnevieye's confession\n| through! What do you think Of its''\n\"DID YoU FORCE IT FROM herr\"\n\"Poor Geneyicve. l did't have to force it\nfrom her. 1 would not believe it for long\nstatement By and by.''\n\n\n''Shal1 you give anything in addition to what\nyou gave me yesterday !\n\n\n40; nothing beyond that will swear to\nto what you hare published, and give that as\nmy statement\"\n\n\nlie took hold of the grated door and looked\nsteadily at the reporter.\n\n\nYou got me lawyer:\" /\n\n\nYou published my statement as I gaye it!\"\n\n\n\"You published Gencvlevc's confession as I |\nwrote itl''\n\n\n\"les.''\n\n\nII am grateful to you for what you have\ndone shan't forget your\n\n\nH turned away from the door and buried his |\nface in Ills hanls.\n\n\nPike is q sensitive man. and proud man, and\nbe kccnly feels his position. Ever si,.ce be be-\ncame satisfied of his wifc's intdclity he has\nbeen completely broken up. He has brooicd\nover it and }amented until he scarcely knew\nwhat be did, and now that his disgrace has be-\ncome common town-talk he has been pretty\nwell upset\n\n\nAS AN INDICATION OF P1KE's PRIDE\nhis friends tell story of time when he was\nhard up some time ago His wife and lady\nfriend went to the Relief and Aid Society for\nsrtion was made to keep the fact\nIII lin. An agent A''''s Of the s'a socfefn aem\nvent to the house, and subsequently to Mr.\n5nt1er, now Pixies counsel. and then gave Mrs\n,ike $40, circumstance which Pike viii now\nearn for the first Time.\n\n\nMRS. PIKE\npent greater portion Of the day in converse !\nlon with her counsel and TRIBUNE reporter. !\nis far as the defense is concerned, she seems to\nave better head than her husband lie\neasons on the improperly OF locking him up, :\nnd theories generally on the inutility Of :\nrying man who manifestly ought not be !\nonyicted. Genevieve, on the contrary. takes l.\npractical view of the matter, and having first\natisi1ed herself and counsel that she can prove 6\n} surrounding circumstances and people who | !\nere present ut the last conversation between\nerself aud husband, that she knew nothing Of | -,\nprojected shooting scrape, she has turned her y\nhole attention to the yanlzing Of the unfor- - -\nmate Pike out Of danger aud durancc.\n- What wil