n are capitalIy invaded. and the\ngfeatea Of the mof precious and eiiabIifhed Il\nDerties of Eugliihmen utterly deRroyed And\nwhereas, the parliament Of Great. Britain BY va\nrious fa,utes and acts, have unrighteoufly def.\nt'oyed our trade. denied and preciuded u, from\nthe letting up and carrying on m.uufaeture,\nh;ghly beneficial to the inhabitants of ,heie tar\nIitories, rearieted and prevented our lawful n\n:eFconrie and commerce with other Hases nd\nkingdoms. have ALTO made laws and inflitu,iou,\nouchins life and limb, 10 diiherifon Of the an\n:ient common law of the land and mo cover,\nhave, in thefe lAter times, robbed and p,under.\nd the honea and laborious ;oh.h;,.,,. e IL.\n\n=== 9_1888-11-02_pna_sn99067997_0041562508A_1888110201_0041.json\nWessington Springs herald. (Wessington Springs, Aurora County, Dakota [S.D.]) 1883-1891 · 1888-11-02\ncrops: None\nheadline: IN THE VINEYARD.\n\nA Short Sermon for Husbands\nWhich Many Of Then,\n\n\nNeed.\n\nGIFT OF THE HOLY GHOST\n\n4 Boy Who Had the Moral Couraoe n\nPray Where There Was Opposi.\n\n\nton to 1t.\n\n4 scs,,.os 70 Husbands.\n---\nText: Husbands, love your wives. !.\n\n\nl. Never find fault with her haforo\nothers.\n\n\n>. Per contra, remember the counsel\nOf the good book: \"Her husband shall\npraise her in the gate\" that is, before\nfolks.\n\n\n}. Bear all the burdens for her; even\nthen she'll bear more than you do in\nspite Of you.\n\n\n4. If you want her to submit to your\njudgment, never ask her to submit to\nyour selfishness.\n\n\n5. A woman's life is made up Of little\nthings. Make her life happy by little\ncourtesies.\n\n\n6. Love isawife's wages. Don't scrimp\nin your pay. Christian at Hork.\n\n=== 19_1889-07-31_pna_sn87056600_00202191502_1889073101_0264.json\nThe Indiana State sentinel. [volume] (Indianapolis) 1868-1895 · 1889-07-31\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\n/\nfamilies of long, unbroken line, and gives\nnotice by pitiful lamentation, often accom-\npanied by the wringing and clapping of\n| hands, OF approaching death. BY those\n| who believe in her In Ireland to-day, she\n! is sometimes regarded as a spirit from\n| purgatOrial realms, released from pen\nnance when this pathetic mission is ac-\n! complished; by some she is thought to be\na direct envoy from the spirit world; but\n! by all she is believed to be the temporary\n/ personation of some deceased member of\n| the family TO which her grievOus message\nis brought She is never trifed with,\n! startled or vexed, for if this would hap\n| pen the banshee would never again honor\n! the same family, at least for a generation\n| OF time, with its tenderly plaintive pres\nence.\n\n\nAll Irish FAIRIES are capable Of confer\n/ ring sure benefits and their power for\n| Furnishing evil is boundless. Conse-\n| quently they are folk commanding more\n| than ordinary respect; and they are uni\nversally spoken of either in the tender\nor friendly manner of ''our neighbors,\"\nor with that delicate Difference easily\n! recognizable in the appellation of ''the\ngood people\" Ghosts, phantoms and\ndemons form a goodly part OF the fairy\nphantasmagoria of Ireland. One of the\nmost hideous of these, and yet fellow of\ninfinite drOllery, is the dullaghan. He is\ngenerally found with his head under his\narm, IN his pocket, or where there are\nnumber of them together, flinging it mer\nrily at some other dullaghan, or engaged\nwith it in games of football; and | fancy\nWashington Irving had got an ink\nling OF Irish dullaghans before\nhe penned his inimitable tales\nOF 'Ichabod Crane\" and rip Van\nWrinkle\" ! found them curiously occu-\npied in the southwest of Ireland. The\nKerry fishermen claim that once each\nyear, at a certain time of the tide, ship\nmanned with a headless crew sails thrice\naround Nalentia island IN search of a for\nmer pirate captain, who lost his head and\nship in that channel in an engagement\nwith Queen Aune's forces. Ballindollag-\nhan, in county Roscommon, was a noted\nhaunt of these odd creatures, and was so\nnamed in their honor. Drumarraght. in\ncounty Fermanagh, is \"the place of spec\ntiers, \"arraght\" being Irish Tor an appari-\nton. Then there are shirtless COLLINS;\nfor Tough Gillaganleny, the name of sew\neral tiny lakes in Ireland, means nothing\nmore nor less than \"the lake of the shirt\nless fellows\" Tobertasha is a coffin\nshaped well. near Kilnamona, in Glare\nand the Specification of its Celtic name,\n'Tobar-a' -taise,\" is the \"well of the\nghost kIlleennagallive Irish, C,dli,-nG-\nBdeqlbb) in Tipperary, is ''the little church\nOF the phantoms\" Again there are fairies\nwho do nothing but play at conan, or\nhurley. and the fairy fort OF Lisfarbegna-\ngommaun, near Corrofn, in Clare, is sim\nply \"the tort OF the little hurlers.' The\ndancing fairies gave Lissarinka its name\nthe fort OF the dancing. Certain old\nforts or raths in the south of ireland\ncalled LissakeOle, mean the fort Of the\nfairy music While Lisnateedy, from\nfe1d. a whistle, Is ''the fort of the whstling\nfairies\" The POoka is wonderful sprite\nOF merriment and cunning. In fact, he is\nthe Irish (pUca) Puck. He whisks through\ncountless legendary tales. a ripple of\nlaughter and CLEAN OF light; and scores\nOF places in Ireland whose names bear his\nown, or some of its many notifications,\nattest his universal popularity.\n\n\nBut of all Irish FAIRIES the most exasper-\natingly impish practical joker is the lepre-\nchaun. He has many names In Celtic such\nas luprachaun, luricane, lurrigadsne, cluri-\ncane, luppercadane, lOughryman, and the\ncorrect designation from which all these\ncorrections have come, luchOrpan, from lu,\neverything very small, and corpan,\ndiminutive Of corp, body. Leprechaun\nIs the name now universally bestowed in\nIreland upon this merry little spirit. He\nis still more Familiarity known among the\npeasantry as ''tbe little imp in green\" To\na hairs breadth he Is just twelve inches\nin hight He is dressed in a little green\ncoat with long dainty tails, bright scarlet\nvest, the prettiest knee-breeches of\npurple velvet you ever beheld, with\ngreen silk hose and low pumps\n\n\n\n\n\nmonds or sparkling drops of dew. There\nis a iaunty barrhad or cap on his head,\nwith the daintiest of dudheens stuck I'M\nder the band at one side, and a jewel of\nemeralds in the form OF a shamrock at the\nother A film OF lace made from rarest\ncobweb Is gathered at his throat, another\nfoamy rift of the same rolls over the edges\nOF his little vest, and he wears a wonderful\nfob of wrought and blazing gold. His eyes\nare no bigger than dolls beads, but they\nare the merriest eyes that ever glistened.\nHis mouth is very large from continuous\nlaughter, and his paunch is something\nwonderful to behold, developed into out\nrageous proportion from the shaking OF\nhis sides from uncomfortable merriment;\nall the result OF his Jokes, receptions and\ndiversions\" upon those who endeavor to\nuse him Tor gain Indeed the little fellow\nseems to embody the idea OF an endless\ntorment and scourge TO those who permit\nthemselves to become possessed OF I'M\nmolly greed and avarice.\n\n\nPatient plodding, hard labor, and a\nCHEEKY spirit under deprivation and suf-\nfering, are all worthy and true character\nistics OF the lowly OF Ireland. These are\nsurely more or less rewarded in various\nWAYS by the leprechaun; but woe to the\nsOrdid spirit who endeavors to amass\nriches through the agency OF this ''little\nimp in green\" His is the power to give\nsudden and great wealth. He has mills\nand mines and store-houses Of treasure\ninnumerable. He is to every Irish man,\nwoman and child as the end of the seduc-\ntive rainbow and its kettle of gold. to our\nown chlldhood's fancy, with this IN\nportant difference. The leprechaun is an\nentity; an actually. No one dare deny\nhis possession of all the treasures neces-\nsary to instantly lift one from poverty to\npower and riches. Thousands of aching\nIrish eyes have Toasted upon his gorgeous\nperson and tantalizing face. Besides, you\nhave the evidence of scores of places\nin Ireland being named from his\nknown haunts. Near the old city of\nCong in Mayo, there is the Mullen\nlupraghaun cave, OF \"the leprechaun's\nmill\" Here in former times good\npeople left their caskeens of corn alright\nfall, and came the next morning to find\nthem full Of meal ground by the obliging\nleprechauns during the night Only the\n~ har dov ocoauda1 Observation-Disclosures,\n\n=== 35_1845-07-17_pna_sn83030213_00206530224_1845071701_0155.json\nNew-York daily tribune. [volume] (New-York [N.Y.]) 1842-1866 · 1845-07-17\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nCoIoRrD TEMPERANCE COSVENTION.--on\nr,e.dsv las the great annual meeting Of the Deayan\ncote Tcmperance niOn was held in this city. From 10\nsceock until - delegations continued 10 arrive from\n\n\nrrept points accompanied by several bands oil music\nmi hearing numerous tastcuy executed banners Aer\nmarching through the principal streets they proceeded\n40 the COurt House from staging erected In front, me\nConvention was organized by eeeting the Rev Mr. Gar\n40, of Troy President who together with others made\n.bo addresses each speaker being limited to u minutes\nInc Rev Mr. Benan, Of New Haven. Ct. highly intel\nlsept and educated speaker made the most effective ad\ndress lie urged upon his colorcd brethren the neces\n,y and importance Of eeyang their race 10 hieher\ndegree Of intelligence and responsibility by education.\n\n\nbety and good conduct the best and surest means\nObtaining the rights and privileges 10 which the speaker\nconendcd they were entitled\n\n\nThe entire proceedings were conducted with the s'rlct\n20, regard proprety and the appearance OF the dele\nmics who a. far az observed conducted themselves\nthroughout the day with the stricct decorun was ghy\ncredisble 10 them in every respect They numbered.\naccording TO our estimation about 5000 and out Of this\nimmense throng did not discover but one single case\nOf intoxcstion. The Temperance cause has probably\ndone more for the elevation and Improvement OF the col\nored population of this country than any other class\nBut few wears since great number OF TINS class were\nwsowing in the most disgusting and polluted haunts Of\nin,emuerance and wee Now with few exceptions they\nhave become steady and industrious citizens and are he\ncsmpC daily more intelligent and useful.\n\n\nThe next Convention in July, lslb, was appointed 10\nhe held al Poughkccpsie Hudson Rcpub.\n","numLines":1007,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1007}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"687edaed-7ece-4fe8-9215-2f0fb049468a","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"other"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2c-reader","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
